Information as a Factor
\par
In Congressional Politics
\par
There are competing claims about the role of information and expertise in
\par
congressional politics, and empirically-based definitions and categorizations differ.
\par
Much of the empirical literature suggests that features of congressional structure shape
\par
the value of information in the policy process, but the implications of this institutional
\par
approach have received little attention. Recent formal models are addressing this issue
\par
by examining how such institutional arrangements as amendment rules might affect the
\par
acquisition and use of information. Twelve propositions about information from the
\par
empirical and the theoretical literatures on Congress have a common message and pro-
\par
vide grounds for comparison of the two traditions.
\par
Observers of Congress offer differing views of the role that pol-
\par
icy expertise and substantive information play in congressional poli-
\par
tics. What might be called the Baconian position, from Bacon's claim
\par
that knowledge is power, sees information as one of the chief factors in
\par
legislative outcomes. In this view expertise confers authority on politi-
\par
cal actors.' Fenno (1973) shows that specialization, which facilitates
\par
the development of expertise, increases the power of individual mem-
\par
bers of Congress to pursue their political goals, whether the goal is
\par
earning reelection, making public policy, or achieving professional
\par
influence and status. This well-known finding corroborates an obser-
\par
vation by Senator Lee Metcalf in the 1974 budget reform debate:
\par
"Information is the name of the game in budget control."2 Being an
\par
expert and having access to information help one get one's way, espe-
\par
cially in an era in which so many policy issues have technical content.
\par
On the other hand, many have noted that substantive or tech-
\par
nical information and expertise often appear to have limited value. In
\par
this view, technical judgment is rarely the primary factor in congres-
\par
sional decisions, even those involving science or technology, and the
\par
best informed do not always prevail over less informed political com-
\par
petitors, whether in congressional floor debate or in the local town
\par
meeting. Critics often note that when politicians pay attention to
\par
