\par
experts at all, they seem to take great care to choose only those who
\par
agree with them.
\par
During the 1970s, when reform efforts provided Congress
\par
with new internal sources of expertise and information, it became
\par
clear that the political context of policy analysis and advice greatly
\par
affected its value to legislators. John Gibbons, director of the congres-
\par
sional Office of Technology Assessment (OTA), acknowledges that
\par
facts are rarely at issue in policy debates (Gibbons 1988). By the mid-
\par
1970s, many of the structural changes of this period-the new OTA
\par
and Congressional Budget Office (CBO), the expanded General
\par
Accounting Office (GAO), the expanded and renamed Congressional
\par
Research Service (CRS), and larger staffs-had contributed to Con-
\par
gress's overall information capacity, but their impact on politics varied
\par
in ways not always connected to the factual quality or quantity of the
\par
information they provided.
\par
Both of these views offer reasonable observations about the
\par
relationship of information to political outcomes, yet they fall quite
\par
short of explaining that relationship. What factors connect the availa-
\par
bility of information about policy to its actual use and its influence on
\par
what Congress does?
\par
Literature on congressional politics has only recently shed
\par
much light on this problem, in part because, as a topic of research,
\par
information has been defined and treated differently at different
\par
times, with little integration of effort. Some authors have focused on
\par
the internal characteristics and categorizations of information, some
\par
on its sources, and others on its role in the strategic considerations of
\par
political actors.
\par
The major division in this literature is between positive the-
\par
ory and traditional, empirically-oriented studies of Congress. As is
\par
often the case in political science, first appearances suggest that these
\par
traditions offer divergent conclusions. The empirical literature
\par
describes the importance of information to committee structure, dis-
\par
tinguishes among types of information, and debates the relevance of
\par
information and policy expertise to congressional politics. At best,
\par
though, information has been of only marginal interest to authors in
\par
this tradition.
\par
In positive theory, explicit assumptions about information
\par
have always been central, although only recently have models been
\par
developed in which information itself is a focal point of the analysis.
\par
These models examine how committee organization, rules of proce-
\par
dure, and uncertainty about outcomes are related to members' incen-
\par
tives to obtain or divulge information about policy.
\par
