\par
In this article I establish links between these two branches of the
\par
literature and identify general propositions that might be subject to the-
\par
oretical or empirical testing. My main claim is that, despite their differ-
\par
ences, positive theory and empirical studies share at least one message
\par
that institutional arrangements shape the relevance of expertise to polit-
\par
ical outcomes. Whether knowledge is power or whether it is irrelevant to
\par
political outcomes depends in part on features of the institution. Both
\par
empirical studies and formal models of Congress suggest that commit
\par
tee composition and norms, legislative rules, and the organization of
\par
authority and jurisdiction affect the relevance of information to the pol-
\par
icy process. This focus on institutions turns attention away from the
\par
content of information, the individual abilities and motives of mem
\par
bers, or specific policy questions and toward the organization of Con
\par
gress itself as shaping the role of information in politics.
\par
The first section of the article discusses the empirical litera-
\par
ture. Its treatment of information is summarized in seven proposi-
\par
tions. The second section examines the study of information in
\par
positive theory and is summarized in five additional propositions. A
\par
final section offers implications and some comparisons of the tw
\par
approaches.
\par
Information in the Empirical Study of Congress
\par
In its treatment of information, the traditional literature on
\par
Congress can be usefully subdivided into three parts: (1) the general
\par
literature on elections, committees, norms, and so forth, which make
\par
almost no explicit mention of information as a factor in politics; (2)
\par
studies of congressional reform, which introduce a debate over the re
\par
evance of technical information and policy analysis to congressional
\par
politics; and (3) analyses of decision making, which provide defin
\par
tions and classifications for information and its uses.
\par
Consider first the study of congressional committees from the
\par
general literature. Many topics that have served as focal points in this
\par
field, such as specialization and decentralization, concern expertise
\par
and the possession of information, despite the fact that authors gener-
\par
ally do not present them in this context.
\par
For example, in his classic study of committees, Fenno (1973)
\par
introduces several information concepts that remain relevant to
\par
research on Congress and information. The norm of specialization
\par
that underlies the committee system represents a division of labor
\par
with respect to technical information. Fenno observes that this divi-
\par
sion of labor produces differing levels of expertise among committees.
\par
