Bruce Bimber
\par
He found that the House Education Committee rarely relied on well-
\par
developed expertise-unlike Appropriations, where individual exper-
\par
tise and mastery of information were much more highly rewarded and
\par
respected. What made expertise valuable on Appropriations was not
\par
so much the subject matter in question but the fact that "members
\par
defer to subcommittee members" and that the "full committee mem-
\par
bers don't try to pose as experts" (Fenno 1973, 103). Fenno's study sug-
\par
gests that this endogenous system of deference and reciprocity is a
\par
necessary if not a sufficient criterion for specialization. Without it, the
\par
development of technical expertise has few political rewards. In
\par
Fenno's work, whether knowledge is power depends on the value sys-
\par
tem and norms of committee members. Fenno provides one reason
\par
why the value of information appears to vary over time and from com-
\par
mittee to committee.
\par
Studies of elections, incumbency, and constituency-member
\par
relationships also provide lessons about the role of information in
\par
Congress. Many of the traditional themes in this genre-such as
\par
explaining why electoral competitiveness has declined and modelling
\par
how members represent their districts-are predicated on voters hav-
\par
ing low levels of information about candidates or representatives and
\par
vice versa. Miller and Stokes's (1963) venerable study of constituency
\par
influence starts with the assumption that constituents are relatively
\par
uninformed about the behavior of their representatives, although the
\par
authors do not dwell on this point. Similarly, they assume that mem-
\par
bers of Congress have little information about the preferences of their
\par
constituents. Under these conditions of incomplete or inadequate
\par
information, the question of how members actually represent their
\par
constituencies becomes very important.3
\par
In the debate over how to explain the incumbency effect,
\par
Mayhew (1974) argues that incumbents have an information advan-
\par
tage over their challengers. Incumbents can generally deliver more
\par
information about themselves to the otherwise uninformed electorate
\par
than can challengers, in part because of their ready access to the media
\par
and their franking privilege. Ferejohn (1977) and others have dis-
\par
counted the importance of this information advantage in electoral suc-
\par
cess. Mayhew (1974) and Miller and Stokes (1963) illustrate how many
\par
important questions of political strategy would be eliminated under
\par
the improbable circumstances of candidates and voters being fully
\par
informed about one another.
\par
Whereas examples such as these from the general literature on
\par
Congress demonstrate the nexus between structure and strategy on the
\par
one hand and information on the other, studies of congressional reform
