\par
focus more explicitly on information in politics. For a brief period, the
\par
role of information in congressional outcomes became the subject of a
\par
debate. As Sundquist (1981) and others have documented, a notable
\par
component of the 1970s reform movement in Congress called for bring-
\par
ing the information capacity and sophistication of Congress closer to
\par
that of the executive branch. These concerns arose in the 1960s and
\par
reached a critical point during Congress's clashes with President Nixon
\par
after 1970. Richard Cohen, writing in the National Journal in 1973,
\par
articulated this view of the problem as an "information gap." Congress's
\par
poor ability to acquire and assess "objective information" on its own
\par
made it too dependent upon the executive branch and restricted its
\par
political power. Rep. Charles Mosher argued this point in the debate
\par
over the creation of OTA: "We in the Congress are constantly
\par
outmanned and outgunned by the expertise of the executive agencies."4
\par
Even after the establishment of OTA, Alton Frye received the encour-
\par
agement of Senate Majority Leader Mike Mansfield and Minority
\par
Leader Hugh Scott when he called for the creation of an Institute for
\par
Congress to provide expert policy analysis (Cohen 1973). By 1978 sev-
\par
eral congressional commissions had studied the state of information
\par
and policy advice within Congress.
\par
During this period, political scientists divided over the impor-
\par
tance of information and expertise to the work of Congress. Some
\par
agreed with reformers that Congress needed better information.
\par
Rieselbach (1977) saw the information issue as a legitimate compo-
\par
nent of congressional reform and even advocated computerization to
\par
help with the problem. Davidson and Oleszek (1977) included among
\par
10 main criticisms of Congress its lack of modern technology for mak-
\par
ing informed policy judgments and its insufficient staff and other
\par
resources. These statements supported reformers' calls for better con-
\par
gressional capacity to handle information.
\par
Others disagreed. Some observers claimed that more so-called
\par
"objective information" alone would not improve Congress's policy-
\par
making capacity. Jones (1976) argued that members need institutional
\par
support primarily for their political judgement and position taking
\par
rather than for acquiring improved technical information. He stated
\par
that members of Congress "are unlikely to invite objective analysis
\par
that may not support their perceptions of what is needed in the district
\par
or the state to get them re-elected" and argued that information is use-
\par
ful only in supporting decisions already made rather than in arriving at
\par
decisions (Jones 1976). He suggested that "for many Congressmen the
\par
purpose of policy analysis is to provide evidence for what their politi-
\par
cal judgement tells them is correct."
\par
