\par
Bruce Bimber
\par
Schick (1976) joined Jones in discounting the value of techni-
\par
cal information. In his view, the problem facing Congress was "not one
\par
of staffing or of shortage of information, but of the institutional char-
\par
acter of Congress." Schneier (1970) also argued that Congress was not
\par
facing an information problem but, on the contrary, enjoyed an abun-
\par
dance of information. He argued that the "information problem is
\par
[actually] a problem of political values, of deciding who gets what,
\par
when and how." He labelled the issue a question of "functional" rather
\par
than "substantial" rationality; Congress coordinates action only with
\par
reference to definite goals and does not, as reformers seemed to
\par
assume, seek intelligent information in an objective, goal-free sense.
\par
Schneier also pointed out that, when certain information-
\par
gathering activities become centralized, the autonomy of congres-
\par
sional policy subsystems may erode; evidence suggests that certain
\par
members of Congress felt the same way. In the OTA debate, some
\par
argued that a new body of experts available to anyone in Congress
\par
would threaten the power of committees by allowing challenges to
\par
their authority on technical matters. Changes to the OTA organic legis-
\par
lation before its passage reflected this concern (Caspar 1981). For sim-
\par
ilar reasons, the new CBO was the subject of congressional criticism in
\par
its first years under director Alice Rivlin. CBO's visibility and inde-
\par
pendent relationship with the media generated conflict with the bud-
\par
get committees, especially in the House, who found their authority
\par
and policy-making prerogatives undermined by a new publicly recog-
\par
nized body of budget experts (Schick 1981).5
\par
To a certain extent, both sides in the debate were eventually
\par
vindicated. Reformers could point to the OTA and CBO which,
\par
despite their rocky starts, were eventually judged modestly successful
\par
in arming Congress with better information and expertise. On the
\par
other hand, those agreeing with Schick and Jones could point out that
\par
more information had not changed the policy process, nor did the
\par
availability of expertise guarantee its use by members. The reform
\par
debate illustrated that the problems of using expertise and policy anal-
\par
ysis in Congress were connected to the larger question of how repre-
\par
sentative institutions make well-informed decisions on complex
\par
matters-a task often considered more suitable to policy making in
\par
centralized executive organizations. The reform debate also suggested
\par
that the value members place on information could depend on rela-
\par
tions of power inside and outside Congress. In the early 1970s many
\par
external observers had wanted a reformed and informed Congress in
\par
order to make it more "intelligent" and to improve the quality of its
\par
policy. But on Capitol Hill itself, the call for more information
\par
