\par
stemmed as much from a desire for control over the power to make
\par
policy as from a desire to improve its content. CBO, OTA, and
\par
increased access to information appeared to be weapons in Congress's
\par
institutional battle with the executive branch rather than academic
\par
exercises in knowledge.
\par
In studies of congressional decision making appearing at the
\par
end of the 1970s, the focus shifted from information and power toward
\par
the more prosaic problems of defining and categorizing information,
\par
identifying its sources, and showing how it was distributed within Con-
\par
gress. In many ways this work has remained marginal to the study of
\par
Congress, illustrating the difficulty authors have had in relating analy-
\par
ses of information to the more central questions in congressional stud-
\par
ies. Zwier (1979) sets the stage for subsequent work by showing that
\par
"specialist" and "nonspecialist" legislators rely upon different sources
\par
for obtaining the information they use in making decisions. Others
\par
writing on this topic, such as Bradley (1980) and Webber (1979), dis-
\par
tinguish two types of information: technical information about the
\par
content of policy and strategic or political information about the pol-
\par
icy process, other political actors, and so forth. A number of authors
\par
employed this or similar distinctions in subsequent work, without
\par
much theoretical development of the idea.
\par
In one model of congressional process that relies on informa-
\par
tion categorizations, Sabatier and Whiteman (1985) describe how
\par
information flows into the Congress and reaches the voting member.
\par
They propose that different types of information follow different paths
\par
in Congress. In their two-stage model both political and policy infor-
\par
mation flows from executive agencies and interest groups to specialist
\par
legislators and from them reaches nonspecialists. A three-stage model
\par
adds staffs as an important step in the information flow.
\par
Probably the most significant piece in the decision-making lit-
\par
erature is Kingdon's study of voting decisions (1981). By his own
\par
description, the book is largely a study of information flow and
\par
searches. Like the literature on congressional elections, Kingdon's
\par
study assumes that constituents and members of Congress are poorly
\par
informed about each other. As a result, members are important voting
\par
influences on one another, since other members seem to be in a posi-
\par
tion to assess constituent responses to proposed policies.
\par
Kingdon's main contribution to the study of information is his
\par
description of the incentive for legislators to conduct information
\par
searches. He observes that members of Congress generally do not find
\par
extensive searches productive. By relying instead upon simple deci-
\par
sion rules, they avoid the impracticality or wastefulness of detailed
