\par
involve questions of the basic distribution of power in the House."
\par
Like Bradley, Cooper and MacKenzie distinguish among several cate-
\par
gories of information: concrete or factual information about circum-
\par
stances and conditions, empirical knowledge about means-ends
\par
relationships, knowledge of broad modes of analysis, and information
\par
about attitudes and preferences.6 But Cooper and MacKenzie do not
\par
use these distinctions to analytical advantage or link them back to
\par
their observation about the importance of information to the distribu-
\par
tion of power. They only claim that "overlapping mixes of objective
\par
and subjective factors" derive from these categories and contribute to
\par
political outcomes.
\par
Whiteman's (1985) study of decision making in committees
\par
shows how an analysis of information can be linked to other factors in
\par
the congressional policy process. He analyzes different uses for policy
\par
analysis and substantive information, categorizing information uses
\par
rather than types of information. His categories are substantive uses,
\par
elaborative uses, and strategic uses of information. Whiteman
\par
defines strategic use, the most common, as information used as a tool
\par
for supporting and advocating existing positions. He finds that sub-
\par
