How do these disparate contributions from the empirical liter-
\par
ature on Congress help us understand the role of information in the
\par
legislative process? They suggest that access to and use of information
\par
is connected to institutional form and of course to political strategy.
\par
Yet they do little to answer questions about what this connection is or
\par
under what circumstances substantive information and expertise may
\par
be important to congressional policy making. To organize and high-
\par
