\par
1. Reciprocity and deference between members are necessary for
\par
committees to acquire expertise and information. Thus, the value
\par
of information can depend on institutional norms (Fenno).
\par
2. Members avoid extensive searches for policy information and rely
\par
on simple decision rules when possible, especially where such rules
\par
involve a clear connection between policy and constituency prefer-
\par
ences (Kingdon).
\par
3. Incomplete information on the part of constituents provides an
\par
electoral advantage for incumbents (Mayhew) and creates uncer-
