\par
substantive, with strategic use being the most common. The more
\par
conflict associated with an issue, the more likely is strategic rather
\par
than substantive or elaborative use of information (Whiteman).
\par
Information in Positive Theory
\par
Positive theory attempts to explain how political actors
\par
respond to information and use it to act on goals. In formal models of
\par
congressional politics, positive theorists include assumptions about
\par
people's ability to acquire and use information. Attacks on such
\par
assumptions are among the most common criticisms of rational
\par
choice-from Herbert Simon's (1957, 1976) objection to utility maxi-
\par
mization to contemporary developmental critiques of the ahistorical-
\par
ness of formal theory. At issue is what actors know, what motivates
\par
them, and how these factors produce action. More recent work in posi-
\par
tive theory has relaxed some of the unrealistic assumptions about
\par
information present in early models of voting and legislatures. The
\par
role of information in politics has been treated more explicitly in this
\par
literature than in the empirical studies of Congress described above,
\par
yet only recently have formal political theorists made information
\par
itself the subject of analysis.
\par
Positive theory is distinguished from empirical analyses by its
\par
explicit use of well-defined information properties or characteristics,
\par
common by including cost, distribution, and certainty. Here cost refers
\par
to the effort required to acquire pertinent information and distribution
\par
to who has and does not have information. Certainty is a property not
\par
of information but of the outcome of decisions based on information:
\par
gaining more complete information reduces the uncertainty of deci-
\par
sion makers regarding these outcomes.
\par
