\par
These properties are illustrated nicely by information eco-
\par
nomics, in many ways a sibling of positive theory that emerged in the
\par
1970s. Employing von Neumann-Morganstern utility maximization
\par
and Bayesian updating from probability theory, information econom-
\par
ics models the value to consumers of information about markets. The
\par
model posits some distribution of prices in a given market, with the ex
\par
ante expected price to a potential buyer being the mean price. That is,
\par
if the buyer simply accepts the price of the first seller encountered, he
\par
or she expects to pay the mean price of goods for sale in that market.
\par
But the buyer may first discover the prices of some number of sellers.
\par
By choosing to discover more prices-to acquire information about
\par
the market-the buyer decreases his or her expected price by locating
\par
sellers with prices below the mean. Information provides a lower ex
\par
post price.
\par
Hershlifer (1973) points out that the singular value of infor-
\par
mation in this model is that it reduces uncertainty-at some cost and
\par
subject to diminishing marginal returns-and thereby changes the
\par
expected value of an outcome. Value here is determined by the change
\par
in the expected price and is a function of the amount of uncertainty the
\par
buyer initially has about prices. The model implicitly assumes that
\par
price information is not universally shared among all sellers and buy-
\par
ers, that it is not evenly distributed. (Buyers change this distribution
\par
by learning about prices, while sellers can change it by advertising
\par
their prices.) This model also assumes that gaining information is
\par
costly to the buyer, the cost being usually in the form of time.
\par
While market transactions are not directly analogous to the
\par
legislative process, the properties at the heart of the information eco-
\par
nomics paradigm are reflected in formal theories of Congress.
\par
Principal-agent theory is one example. Principal-agent problems arise
\par
because agents have information about their own actions which princi-
\par
pals do not have (a form of asymmetric distribution) or are more cer-
\par
tain of the outcomes of decisions. For principals to acquire this
\par
information is difficult and expensive, so they seek institutional
\par
means to facilitate gathering it or to avoid the need for it (the cost)
\par
altogether.7
\par
In their discussion of administrative procedure, McCubbins,
\par
Noll, and Weingast (1987) also illustrate the role of information cost
\par
and distribution in principal-agent models. They suggest that when
\par
Congress imposes administrative controls such as those in the Admin-
\par
istrative Procedures Act and the National Environmental Protection
\par
Act, it is responding to a type of information asymmetry. Congress is
\par
at a substantial informational disadvantage with respect to the
\par
