\par
Congress responds by imposing administrative procedures on the
\par
bureaucracy that require it to obtain and disseminate information and
\par
to enfranchise certain public groups in its decision processes. This
\par
structure tends to bias outcomes in Congress's favor without costly
\par
monitoring and information discovery. Pursuing a similar topic,
\par
McCubbins and Page (1987) model congressional delegation as a cost-
\par
benefit tradeoff between the costs of agency and the benefits of policy-
\par
making efficiency, focusing on the effects of imperfect information
\par
and imperfect instruments of control.
\par
But if they differ in the explicitness with which they model the
\par
role of information, positive theory and empirical studies still share
\par
common ground. Both distinguish among types of information. In
\par
their discussion of agency theory, McCubbins and Page (1987) point
\par
out that information asymmetries between agents and Congress take
\par
two forms: asymmetries in information about the activities of the
\par
agent and in information about how these activities affect outcomes.
\par
McCubbins and Page claim that these two types of asymmetry and the
\par
degree of conflict in the policy issue are predictors of levels of delega-
\par
tion from Congress to its agents. Increasing either the information
\par
asymmetry or the degree of conflict makes Congress more likely to del-
\par
