\par
more confining procedures.
\par
Miller and Moe (1986) also note that the fundamental
\par
principal-agent problem exists independently of technical information
\par
and expertise but that these factors can be added to the picture. They
\par
observe that the commonly discussed problems of monitoring, adverse
\par
selection, and shirking (a form of moral hazard) become especially
\par
problematic when "the information asymmetry includes technical
\par
information in the form of special expertise on the part of the agent."
\par
How the presence and use of technical information and exper-
\par
tise in Congress can be better understood has been explored in a series
\par
of related models by Austen-Smith and Riker (1987; 1990), Austen-
\par
Smith (1990a, 1990b, 1990c), Gilligan and Krehbiel (1987, 1989,
\par
1990), and Krehbiel (1988, 1991). Austen-Smith and Riker (1987,
\par
1990) model legislators' incentives to reveal private information to
\par
each other. Their asymmetric information model, applied to a water
\par
fluoridation policy decision, provides a theoretical connection
\par
between technical information and strategic information. Austen-
\par
Smith and Riker presuppose that "legislation is rarely, if ever, an end
\par
in itself." When legislators make decisions, they are not merely pro-
\par
ducing some preferred immediate legislative outcome, namely the
