The following theoretical propositions about information
\par
summarize these findings in positive theory and can be added to the
\par
previous seven from the empirical literature.
\par
8. The net benefits from acquiring information depend upon the cost
\par
of acquiring it, its distribution, and its ability to decrease uncer-
\par
tainty (Information economics).
\par
9. Open amendment rules undermine the incentives for committee
\par
specialization (that is, for information acquisition and revela-
\par
tion). Closed rules should therefore be more strongly associated
\par
with well-informed bills from specialized committees (Austen-
\par
