\par
likely Congress will be to delegate responsibility for decisions to
\par
agencies (McCubbins and Page).
\par
11. Given an asymmetry in information between Congress and an
\par
agency, the greater the cost to Congress of monitoring and discov-
\par
ering information that overcomes the asymmetry, the more likely
\par
Congress will be to impose procedural requirements on the agency
\par
that require it to acquire and reveal information on its own
\par
(McCubbins, Noll, and Weingast).
\par
12. The greater the uncertainty in the policy environment, the greater
\par
the net informational gains from committee specialization. On
\par
the other hand, the greater the disparity in preferences between
\par
committee and parent body, the lower the net gains (Gilligan and
\par
Krehbiel).
