Implications
\par
Positive theory has produced a number of findings that relate
\par
institutional arrangements to the value of information. These findings
\par
are broadly consistent with many findings of the traditional, empirical
\par
literature. Gilligan and Krehbiel (1987) show how amendment rules
\par
might affect the incorporation of expertise in policy. McCubbins, Noll,
\par
and Weingast (1987) and McCubbins and Page (1987) show how
\par
administrative procedures can represent responses to information
\par
problems. These findings extend empirical observations by authors
\par
such as Fenno, who shows that structure in the form of norms and spe-
\par
cialization affect the value of expertise and facilitate the acquisition of
\par
information by the legislature. This formal work, along with that of
\par
