\par
Austen-Smith and Riker (1987, 1990), explores the common reform-
\par
era concern over the information content of legislation and coincides
\par
with observations by Kingdon, Schick, Jones, and Schneier that mem-
\par
bers' institutional goals limit their desire for objective analysis. It may
\par
not be surprising that these theoretical developments are broadly con-
\par
sistent with earlier empirical findings and concerns; formal models
\par
began to incorporate institutional detail largely to reconcile the predic-
\par
tions of early theories of voting with empirical observations of legisla-
\par
tures (Shepsle 1985).
\par
These models also reinterpret incomplete information in for-
\par
mal theory so that its meaning is consistent with the meaning of infor-
\par
mation in the empirical literature. "Incomplete information" had
\par
typically referred to lack of information about the preferences of oth-
\par
ers for policy choices; the reinterpretation, perhaps more appropri-
\par
ately called "incomplete technical information," refers to lack of
\par
information about the relationship of policy to consequences.8 Obtain-
\par
ing technical information can change an individual's induced prefer-
\par
ences for policy; obtaining information in the traditional sense
\par
changes an individual's beliefs about the policy preferences of others.
\par
The new usage may help bridge empirical and theoretical concerns.
\par
The formal theorists' approach to information, as Gilligan
\par
and Krehbiel point out, departs from Mayhew's influential view of leg-
\par
islators as primarily position takers. These models assume that mem-
\par
bers are concerned with the consequences of policy and choose
\par
legislation on the basis of beliefs about its effects. In this view, constit-
\par
uents judge their representatives more by how outcomes have affected
\par
them than by mere position taking. Never mind what my representa-
\par
tive says, are interest rates really down? has the nuclear power plant
\par
operated safely as promised? did the public works project produce
\par
jobs and reduce traffic congestion? This seems a more optimistic diag-
\par
nosis of the accountability and responsiveness of representative
\par
government.
\par
By acknowledging that members of Congress can know what
\par
outcomes their constituents desire but be uncertain what policies will
\par
produce the right outcomes, this new emphasis may enable positive
\par
theory to speak directly to traditional concerns about the usefulness
\par
and relevance of policy analysis and expert advice in the legislature.
\par
Accomplishing this task will require that the theory be verified
\par
by historical and factual evidence and that it be expanded in several
\par
ways.9 For example, the information legislators receive is often pre-
\par
sented publicly rather than privately, whereas current models assume
\par
private acquisition and control over information. When a congres-
