\textbf{S H A R E D D E C I S I O N - M A K I N G IN THE M E D I C A L}
\par
ENCOUNTER: WHAT DOES IT MEAN? (OR IT TAKES AT
\par
LEAST TWO TO TANGO)
\par
CATHY CHARLES, ''2"3. A M I R A M G A F N V '3 and TIM W H E L A N 3'4'5
\par
'Department of Clinical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, McMaster University, Hamilton, Ontario,
\par
Canada, "Centre for Health Economics and Policy Analysis, McMaster University, Hamilton, Ontario,
\par
Canada, 3Supportive Cancer Care Research Unit, McMaster University, Hamilton, Ontario, Canada,
\par
'OCTRF--Hamilton Regional Cancer Centre, Hamilton, Ontario, Canada and 5Department of
\par
Medicine, McMaster University, Hamilton, Ontario, Canada
\par
Abstract--Shared decision-making is increasingly advocated as an ideal model of treatment decision-
\par
making in the medical encounter. To date, the concept has been rather poorly and loosely defined. This
\par
paper attempts to provide greater conceptual clarity about shared treatment decision-making, identify
\par
some key characteristics of this model, and d scuss measurement issues. The particular decision-making
\par
context that we focus on is potentially life threatening illnesses, where there are important decisions to
\par
be made at key points in the disease process, and several treatment options exist with different possible
\par
outcomes and substantial uncertainty. We suggest as key characteristics of shared decision-making (1)
\par
that at least two participants--physician and patient be involved; (2) that both parties share infor-
\par
mation; (3) that both parties take steps to build a consensus about the preferred treatment; and (4) that
\par
an agreement is reached on the treatment to implement. Some challenges to measuring shared decision-
\par
making are discussed as well as potential benefits of a shared decision-making model for both phys-
\par
icians and patients. Copyright \copyright{} 1997 Elsevier Science Ltd
\par
\emph{Key words--shared }treatment decision-making, physician/patient communication
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Shared decision-making is increasingly advocated as
\par
an ideal model of treatment decision-making in the
\par
medical encounter (Veatch, 1972; Brody, 1980;
\par
Quill, 1983; Brock and Wartman, 1990; Gray ei' \emph{al.,}
\par
1990; Emanuel and Emanuel, 1992; Levine \emph{et al.,}
\par
1992; Deber, 1994). Yet, it is by no means clear
\par
what shared decision-making really means or the
\par
criteria by which to judge what falls within or out-
\par
side the boundaries of this model. In order to be
\par
able to evaluate the merits and limitations of a
\par
shared decision-making model it is first necessary to
\par
be clear about what the model is. To date, the con-
\par
cept of shared decision-making has been rather
\par
poorly and loosely defined. This leads to cons:tier-
\par
able confusion because the same conceptual label
\par
can be used to subsume different underlying philos-
\par
ophies and principles of physician-patient inter-
\par
action. Our goal in this paper is to provide greater
\par
conceptual clarity in thinking about shared
\par
decision-making, to identify some basic character-
\par
istics of this model and to discuss measurement
\par
issues.
\par
Improved conceptualization of shared deci,,;ion-
\par
making would have several benefits. It would make
\par
more explicit what advocacy of shared deci,,don-
\par
*Author for correspondence.
\par
making means, allow for easier recognition when it
\par
does occur, and perhaps facilitate its practice by
\par
physicians and patients who have a preference for
\par
this model of joint decision-making. In so far as
\par
shared decision-making has been linked with posi-
\par
tive patient outcomes (e.g. satisfaction and improve-
\par
ments in functional status), clarification of this
\par
model is clinically relevant (Egbert \emph{et al., }1964;
\par
Schulman, 1979; Greenfield \emph{et al., }1985, 1988;
\par
Brody \emph{et al., }1989; Wennberg, 1990; Mahler and
\par
Kulik, 1990; Lerman \emph{et al., }1990). Greater concep-
\par
tual clarity could also guide research in this area by
\par
providing clearer direction on the types of infor-
\par
mation to be collected as relevant examples of
\par
shared and non-shared decision-making.
\par
The increased and fairly new interest in shared
\par
decision-making derives from a number of different
\par
factors. For example, informed consent, now ethi-
\par
cally and legally ensconced as a patient right, seems
\par
to imply at least a minimum of shared decision-
\par
making in the form of patient consent to treatment
\par
prior to any intervention (Sutherland \emph{et al., }1989).
\par
Moreover, the principle of "informed choice", ie.
\par
disclosure of treatment alternatives rather than
\par
merely informed consent has been endorsed at sev-
\par
eral government levels in Canada and the United
\par
States (Evans, 1987; Greene, 1992; Ontario Ministry
\par
of Health, 1994; Nayfield \emph{et al., }1994).
\par
681682 Cathy Charles \emph{et al.}
\par
Interest in shared decision-making also has its
\par
origins in the consumer rights movement. Here,
\par
concern with patient participation in treatment
\par
decision-making has moved well beyond informed
\par
consent to include broader principles of patient
\par
autonomy, control, and patient challenge to phys-
\par
ician authority (Haug and Lavin, 1983; Ende \emph{et al.,}
\par
1989; Charles and DeMaio, 1993; Llewellyn-
\par
Thomas, 1992, 1995). Shared decision-making is
\par
seen as a mechanism to decrease the informational
\par
and power asymmetry between doctors and patients
\par
by increasing patients' information, sense of auton-
\par
omy and/or control over treatment decisions that
\par
affect their well-being (Eddy, 1990; Ryan, 1992;
\par
Emanuel and Emanuel, 1992).
\par
A final factor is the changing nature of medical
\par
practice. During the last 20 to 30 years, there has
\par
been a dramatic shift away from acute care to
\par
chronic care and caregivers often manage illnesses
\par
or combinations of illnesses rather than cure dis-
\par
ease. For such patients, sickness is not just a tem-
\par
porary status; rather, long-term and chronic illness
\par
may become a permanent part of their identity and
\par
status. In such cases, the physician patient relation-
\par
ship is potentially a long-term one. The plethora of
\par
new drugs available requires that physicians work
\par
closely with such patients to develop the optimum
\par
pharmacological solution, a process that takes time,
\par
continuous monitoring and adjustment of medi-
\par
cation types and levels. This process is likely to
\par
work best if both patients and physicians have a
\par
role in managing the illness and medication regi-
\par
mens.
\par
For certain diseases, such as cancer, which are
\par
both potentially life-threatening and widely preva-
\par
lent, there are key treatment decision points which
\par
may occur only once and arise early on in the
\par
course of the disease which have major conse-
\par
quences for the patient. Women with early stage
\par
breast cancer, for example, may be faced with the
\par
decision to have a lumpectomy versus mastectomy,
\par
and, following surgery, whether to have adjuvant
\par
chemotherapy and/or radiation. These are decisions
\par
which cannot be delayed without potentially serious
\par
implications for the health of the patient. Here
\par
shared decision-making becomes particularly im-
\par
portant to address in the medical encounter, first,
\par
because several treatment options exist with differ-
\par
ent possible outcomes, and substantial uncertainty.
\par
Second, there is often no clear-cut right or wrong
\par
answer. Third, treatments will vary in their impact
\par
on the patient's physical and psychological well-
\par
being (Pierce, 1993). It is this latter type of treat-
\par
ment decision-making context which is our focus in
\par
this paper and we use early stage breast cancer as
\par
an example of a potentially life threatening disease.
\par
We recognize that there are different types of
\par
treatment decision-making contexts (e.g. emergency
\par
treatment, long-term monitoring of medications in
\par
the treatment of chronic disorders such as hyperten-
\par
sion, palliative care) and that different models of
\par
treatment decision-making may be more or less
\par
appropriate or feasible in specific contexts. To dis-
\par
cuss issues of shared decision-making in all these
\par
would be an enormous task; hence, we choose the
\par
specific decision-making context as described above
\par
as our focus for this paper.
\par
This focus is at the micro as opposed to the
\par
macro level of analysis where, clinically, there are
\par
several treatment options available and the choice
\par
of the best treatment for a particular patient
\par
requires value judgements on the part of the patient
\par
and physician. Our paper does not address macro
\par
level economic constraints, i.e. where policy makers
\par
have decided that for certain medical conditions,
\par
there will be a limited number of treatment options
\par
available through public or third party insurers.
\par
Also, we limit the discussion of decision-making at
\par
the micro level to competent patients.
\par
\textbf{MODELS OF TREATMENT DECISION-MAKING}
\par
Shared decision-making is only one among sev-
\par
eral treatment decision-making models discussed in
\par
the literature (Veatch, 1972; Thomasma, 1983;
\par
Emanuel and Emanuel, 1992; Levine \emph{et al., }1992;
\par
Roter and Hall, 1992; Mooney and Ryan, 1993;
\par
Deber, 1994). Prominent among these are the pater-
\par
nalistic model, the informed decision-making model
\par
and the professional-as-agent model. Analysis of the
\par
prototype depictions of each of these models reveals
\par
some overlap in specific characteristics of each. In
\par
addition, many of these characteristics, such as in-
\par
formation sharing, involve gradations rather than
\par
absolutes. In this paper, we want to clarify the cen-
\par
tral elements of the latter 3 models as they are por-
\par
trayed in the literature, and argue that each falls
\par
short of depicting a model of shared treatment
\par
decision-making. We then identify what we see as
\par
necessary components of a model of shared
\par
decision-making, in particular, the exchange of both
\par
information and treatment preferences by both
\par
physician and patient and agreement by both parties
\par
on the treatment to implement.
\par
On first glance, it seems easiest to differentiate the
\par
paternalistic model from shared decision-making
\par
because the former explicitly assumes a passive role
\par
for the patient in the treatment decision-making
\par
process. An early formulation of this model was
\par
Parsons' conceptualization of the sick role. Parsons
\par
argued that the sick role carried with it certain
\par
rights and obligations for patients (Parsons, 1951).
\par
Persons granted (by physicians) the (temporary)
\par
sick role status, for example, were excused from
\par
other role-related activities such as those of family
\par
and work, but they also had an obligation to try to
\par
get well, to seek expert help, and to comply with
\par
the medical regimen (Parsons, 1951). This model
\par
clearly placed the patient in a passive, dependent
\par
role \emph{vis-a-vis }the physician as expert.Shared treatment decision-making: what does it mean? In more recent depictions of the paternalistic
\par
model, the physician is seen as dominating the
\par
medical encounter and using his skills to diagnose
\par
and recommend tests and treatments for the patient.
\par
In the extreme case, "the physician authoritatively
\par
informs the patient when the intervention will be in-
\par
itiated" (Emanuel and Emanuel, 1992). In the less
\par
extreme, the physician will give the patient sele,zted
\par
information and will encourage the patient to \textasciitilde 'on-
\par
sent to what the physician considers best (Ema\textasciitilde auel
\par
and Emanuel, 1992). The role of physician depicted
\par
in this model is guardian of the patient's best irLter-
\par
est. The physician does what he thinks is best for
\par
the patient, without eliciting the latter's preferences.
\par
Patient involvement (if there is any) is limited to
\par
providing consent to the treatment advocated by
\par
the physician (Emanuel and Emanuel, 1992).
\par
Most would agree that this is not a model of
\par
shared decision-making in any sense. Efforts to for-
\par
mulate alternative treatment decision-making
\par
models have arisen, in part, in reaction to the per-
\par
ceived prevalence of the paternalistic approach
\par
(Levine \emph{et al., }1992; Deber, 1994) which is viewed
\par
by many as inappropriate for many current treat-
\par
ment decision-making contexts. The extent to which
\par
this approach is currently practised by physicians is
\par
an empirical question; in emergency situations, for
\par
example, it may still be widely accepted and might,
\par
in practice, be the only feasible model for the task.
\par
Both the informed and the physician-as-agent
\par
decision-making models derive from a recognition
\par
of informational asymmetry between patient and
\par
physician (Levine \emph{et al., }1992). As Hurley \emph{et al.}
\par
(1992) note: "The crux of the information prolclem
\par
is that while the health care provider possesses bet-
\par
ter knowledge regarding the expected effective\textasciitilde aess
\par
of health care in improving health status, the indi-
\par
vidual knows best how improvements in health sta-
\par
tus affect his or her well-being" (p. 4). Techr,ical
\par
knowledge resides in one party to the interaction--
\par
the physician, while preferences reside in the
\par
other--the patient. Yet both types of information
\par
need to be combined if effective care that lead:\textasciitilde{} to
\par
health status improvements valued by patients is to
\par
be provided (Hurley \emph{et al., }1992; Levine \emph{et al.,}
\par
1992). In the informed model this is accomplished
\par
by increasing the patient's knowledge of the possible
\par
risks of alternative therapeutic options and their
\par
clinical effectiveness, so that patients can r\textasciitilde ake
\par
decisions that reflect both their preferences and the
\par
best scientific knowledge available (Hurley \emph{et al.,}
\par
1992).
\par
The informed decision-making model incorpor-
\par
ates the idea of information sharing (primarily E\textasciitilde om
\par
physician to patient); but we would argue that in-
\par
formation sharing does not necessarily lead to a
\par
sharing of the treatment decision-making process.
\par
In fact, since the informed patient has overcome the
\par
problem of information deficit, presumably she is
\par
now enabled to make the treatment decision on her
\par
683
\par
own. Theoretically, in this model, an informed
\par
patient no longer needs to share the treatment
\par
decision-making process because she now possesses
\par
both components (information and preferences)
\par
viewed as essential to the task (Levine \emph{et al., }1992).
\par
In this model, treatment decision-making control is
\par
clearly seen to be vested in the patient (Eddy, 1990).
\par
The physician's role is limited to that of infor-
\par
mation exchange, communicating the needed techni-
\par
cal or scientific knowledge to the patient (Williams,
\par
1988; Mooney and Ryan, 1993). As noted by
\par
Emanuel and Emanuel (1992), the physician \textasciitilde is pro-
\par
scribed from giving a treatment recommendation
\par
for fear of imposing his or her will on the patient
\par
and thereby competing for the decision-making con-
\par
trol that has been given to the patient" (p. 2225). In
\par
other words, the physician's treatment preferences
\par
for the patient do not enter into the decision-mak-
\par
ing process.
\par
If the paternalistic model leaves the patient out-
\par
side the decision-making process, the informed
\par
model leaves the physician outside by limiting the
\par
role of the physician to one of information transfer.
\par
In the extreme case, information transfer can be
\par
done without the presence of any health care
\par
worker, for example, by the patient viewing an
\par
interactive video. We argue that unless both patient
\par
and physician share treatment preferences, a shared
\par
treatment decision-making process did not occur,
\par
no matter how much information may have been
\par
exchanged by either party.
\par
The informed model is premised on the assump-
\par
tion that information is an enabling strategy,
\par
"empowering" the patient to become a more auton-
\par
omous decision maker. Research has shown, how-
\par
ever, that while patients typically express high
\par
preferences for information about their illness and
\par
its treatment (Cassileth \emph{et al., }1980; Strull \emph{et al.,}
\par
1984; Beisecker, 1988; Blanchard \emph{et al., }1988; Ende
\par
\emph{et al., }1989; Lerman \emph{et al., }1990; Beisecker and
\par
Beisecker, 1990; Waterworth and Luker. 1990;
\par
Silverstein \emph{et al., }1991; Biley, 1992; Deber, 1994),
\par
their preferences for participation in treatment
\par
decision-making are much more diversely distribu-
\par
ted (Vertinsky \emph{et al., }1974; Strull \emph{et al., }1984;
\par
Pendleton and House, 1984; Ende \emph{et al., }1989;
\par
Beisecker and Beisecker, 1990; Silverstein \emph{et al.,}
\par
1991; Degner and Sloan, 1992; Ryan, 1992; Hack \emph{et}
\par
\emph{al., }1994). In other words, patients want infor-
\par
mation about their medical condition and treatment
\par
options without necessarily being responsible for
\par
making treatment decisions (Ende \emph{et al., }1989;
\par
Beisecker and Beisecker, 1990; Ryan, 1992).
\par
An informed patient may prefer to make the
\par
decision herself (or be required to do so by the
\par
physician), to share the decision-making process, or
\par
to delegate this responsibility to the physician. In
\par
Scenario 1 in the Appendix, for example, the phys-
\par
ician clearly wants the patient to make the treat-
\par
ment decision. The patient, while claiming to be684 Cathy Charles \emph{et al.}
\par
well informed, nonetheless prefers that the physician
\par
decide. Many patients faced with a serious illness,
\par
substantial uncertainty as to the outcome, and a
\par
time pressure to make a treatment decision among
\par
several competing alternatives, feel extreme psycho-
\par
logical and/or physiological vulnerability, which
\par
may make it difficult for them to participate in
\par
treatment decision-making no matter how well
\par
informed they may feel (Gray \emph{et al., }1990; Ryan,
\par
1992). Also, as this scenario points out. before
\par
patients can decide whether or not to share in
\par
decision-making, they must be offered the choice of
\par
participation by their physician (a point also made
\par
in a recent article by Ong \emph{et al.). }Other patients
\par
may wish to participate but lack a systematic way
\par
of structuring the decision-making process. In this
\par
case, efforts to promote shared decision-making
\par
may well require interventions that not only provide
\par
patients with information but also with a way of
\par
thinking about treatment decision-making that helps
\par
them focus on key issues and evaluate relevant
\par
options.
\par
One such intervention is the treatment decision
\par
aid, ranging in type from high cost interactive
\par
videos to low cost decision boards. Several decision
\par
boards have recently been developed and tested
\par
with women with breast cancer (Levine \emph{et al., }1992;
\par
Whelan \emph{et al., }1995). They provide the patient with
\par
detailed information about her treatment choices,
\par
outcomes, the probability of these outcomes and
\par
quality of life associated with each outcome.
\par
Treatment decision aids are a form of educational
\par
intervention. But they are also aids to treatment
\par
decision-making in that they provide a way of struc-
\par
turing the decision-making process, and breaking it
\par
down into a number of specific and sequential steps.
\par
As Llewellyn-Thomas (1995) notes: "A distinguish-
\par
ing feature of a decision aid is the inclusion of exer-
\par
cises designed to promote clarification of the
\par
patient's values regarding what is at stake and what
\par
it is that he or she is trying to achieve as a result of
\par
treatment" (p. 104). Decision aids are intended to
\par
provide information and to promote "self help'" in
\par
the treatment decision-making process which
\par
enables the patient to more actively participate in
\par
this process, if this is her preference.
\par
It seems fair to say that physicians such as medi-
\par
cal oncologists who develop, test and use treatment
\par
decision-making aids are those who are already mo-
\par
tivated to share treatment decision-making, since a
\par
primary goal of the aids is to help elicit patients'
\par
*In the agency model, the physician is usually depicted as
\par
acting as the patient's agent, but this is not always the
\par
case. Williams, for example, defines a perfect agency re-
\par
lationship as one in which the physician gives the
\par
patient all the information the patient needs and the
\par
patient then makes the decision (Williams, 1988).
\par
Hence, the agency and informed models are also con-
\par
fused in the literature.
\par
treatment preferences. Physicians adopting the
\par
paternalistic approach are unlikely to use such aids
\par
precisely because they can help overcome traditional
\par
professional dominance over interactions in the
\par
medical encounter (Freidson, 1970; West, 1984; Hill
\par
Beu\pounds{} 1989; Waitzkin, 1991). Whelan, for example,
\par
recently reported (1995) that 97\% of women with
\par
breast cancer who were assigned to a group in
\par
which the physician used a treatment decision board
\par
with information about the risks and benefits of
\par
breast irradiation following lumpectomy felt that
\par
they were offered a treatment choice compared with
\par
70\% of women in the no decision board group. To
\par
return to our main point, however, an informed
\par
patient who perceives that she has choices may still
\par
prefer not to make the treatment decision.
\par
The professional-as-agent model is the flip side of
\par
the informed patient model of treatment decision-
\par
making. Its goal is also to resolve the informational
\par
asymmetry between physician and patient. But here,
\par
"the professional-as-agent assumes responsibility for
\par
directing the health care utilization of the patient-
\par
...as an agent trying to choose what the patient
\par
would have chosen, had she been as well-informed
\par
as the professional" (Evans, 1984, p. 75). In other
\par
words, in this model, the physician makes the treat-
\par
ment decision, either assuming that he knows, or
\par
having elicited the patient's preferences for future
\par
health states, lifestyle choices etc. Both components
\par
(information and preferences) then reside in the
\par
physician, rather than the patient, and the former
\par
becomes the sole decision-maker.* Legally phys-
\par
icians cannot implement a treatment without at
\par
least eliciting patient consent. In practice, there may
\par
still be some physicians who assume that they know
\par
the patient's treatment preference, and act on this
\par
without first explicitly testing this assumption. Such
\par
cases are not likely to attain public visibility unless
\par
the patient subsequently launches a legal suit, claim-
\par
ing violation of patient rights to informed consent.
\par
While there have been critiques of the physician-
\par
as-agent model (Evans, 1984; Mooney and Ryan,
\par
1993), our interest is not to take sides in this debate.
\par
Rather we argue that this model is also not necess-
\par
arily one of shared decision-making. In the purest
\par
form of this model, the physician makes the treat-
\par
ment decision as if he had the same preferences as
\par
the patient; decision-making is again portrayed as a
\par
one-sided process. By definition, in this model the
\par
physician's treatment preferences do not count (are
\par
excluded). The only treatment preferences that mat-
\par
ter are those of the patient.
\par
In summary, several models of treatment
\par
decision-making have been developed, partially in
\par
reaction to the paternalistic model. A closer examin-
\par
ation reveals that none of these explicitly describes
\par
a process in which both physicians and patients
\par
necessarily share in decision-making, no matter how
\par
much information they share. The notion that infor-
\par
mation sharing and treatment decision-making areShared treatment decision-making: what does it mean? 685
\par
two separate goals in the medical encounter is
\par
beginning to be recognized in the literature on
\par
doctor-patient communication (Ong \emph{et al., }1995). In
\par
the next section we argue that for shared treatment
\par
decision-making to occur, there needs to be a two-
\par
way exchange not only of information but also of
\par
treatment preferences.
\par
\textbf{CHARACTERISTICS OF SHARED }DECISION-MAKING
\par
In order to specify what falls or does not fall
\par
within the boundaries of shared decision-making, it
\par
is necessary to try to identify some of its key
\par
characteristics. Characteristics are signals of identifi-
\par
cation. The characteristics we identify can be
\par
thought of as minimum or necessary criteria for
\par
classifying a physician-patient decision-making
\par
interaction as shared decision-making, i.e. necessary
\par
but not always sufficient. There may well be other
\par
characteristics that are important that we have not
\par
included. It is also important to recognize that
\par
many of these characteristics are continuous rather
\par
than dichotomous variables in recognition of the
\par
fact that shared decision-making involves gradations
\par
rather than absolutes.
\par
\emph{Shared decision-making involves at least two parlici-}
\par
\emph{pants the physician and patient}
\par
The first characteristic of shared treatment
\par
decision-making is that it involves at least two par-
\par
ticipants--a clinician, who in many cases will be a
\par
physician, and a patient. This seems self-evident; as
\par
noted above, if only one person makes the decision,
\par
the process is not shared.
\par
Frequently treatment decision-making involves
\par
more than one patient and one physician (or other
\par
health care professional) in a single or sequential
\par
medical encounter. For example, research on phys-
\par
ician and elderly patient medical encounters increas-
\par
ingly focuses on triad rather than dyad relationsl\textasciitilde ips
\par
in recognitions that many elderly patients bring a
\par
relative or friend to the physician's office (Rosow,
\par
1981; Coe and Prendergast, 1985; Adelman \emph{et al.,}
\par
1987; Haug and Ory, 1987; Beisecker, 1988; Haug,
\par
1994). Both conceptual and research interest iaas
\par
focused on the types of coalitions that can occur in
\par
such encounters. What this literature highlights is
\par
that family members or friends can play a variety of
\par
different roles within (or outside) the medical
\par
encounter relating to the patient's illness, treatment
\par
selection and management.
\par
Roles which relate specifically to the treatment
\par
decision-making process can include, for example:
\par
(1) information gatherer, recorder or interpreter; (2)
\par
coach, e.g. prompting the patient to ask the phys-
\par
ician certain questions; (3) advisor, e.g. advising the
\par
patient which treatment option to select; (4) nego-
\par
tiator, e.g. advocating on the patient's behalf
\par
regarding the timing or place of treatment or the
\par
patient priority in receiving treatment; (5) caretaker,
\par
e.g. supporting and or reinforcing the patient's
\par
treatment decision. The involvement of family mem-
\par
bers in treatment decision-making may be particu-
\par
larly important with serious illnesses because of the
\par
stress engendered by the diagnosis, the uncertain
\par
outcome, and the potentially major impact of the
\par
illness trajectory and treatment management on
\par
other family members. These issues have received
\par
little attention in the shared treatment decision-
\par
making literature which focuses almost exclusively
\par
on the dyad relationship of physician and patient in
\par
the medical encounter.
\par
When patients bring a relative or friend to a
\par
physician visit, the range and complexity of the
\par
interactional dynamics is automatically increased. In
\par
addition, the introduction of a third person enables
\par
the formation of coalitions. Coalitions (e.g. between
\par
the physician versus the patient and family member)
\par
may occur over such things as how much and what
\par
type of information is given by the physician, what
\par
is the best treatment and how and when to im-
\par
plement it. Studies of triad interactions between
\par
elderly patients, a family member and the physician
\par
have revealed the formation of numerous (and
\par
different) coalitions over numerous (and different)
\par
issues in a single medical encounter. However, the
\par
frequency and pattern of coalition formation is
\par
likely to vary depending on the treatment decision-
\par
making context. The triad relationship of elderly
\par
patient, physician and family member discussing
\par
treatment options for the long-term management of
\par
a chronic illness is different from the situation of a
\par
post-surgery woman with breast cancer who needs
\par
to make an important treatment decision under
\par
tight time constraints. Here, we hypothesize that
\par
fewer coalitions will be likely to occur because all
\par
parties will be motivated to reach consensus quickly
\par
on the treatment to implement so as to afford the
\par
best chance of recovery.
\par
Also not well recognized in the literature on
\par
shared treatment decision-making is the fact that
\par
several physicians often participate in this process
\par
with a single patient. A breast cancer patient, for
\par
example, may have a family physician, surgeon,
\par
radiation oncologist, and medical oncologist, all of
\par
whom may have specific treatment preferences for
\par
the patient. For example, the surgeon may suggest a
\par
post-surgery treatment to the patient which she then
\par
has in mind when subsequently visiting the medical
\par
or radiation oncologist. If the latter presents a
\par
different treatment option, the result may be
\par
increased uncertainty and confusion for the patient.
\par
One study of patients in a cardiology unit reported
\par
that, not infrequently, five or more physicians were
\par
"making treatment decisions" about the patient's
\par
care (Lidz \emph{et al., }1985, p. 248).
\par
These examples suggest that limiting the concep-
\par
tualization of shared decision-making to a phys-
\par
ician-patient dyad may not, in many cases, reflect
\par
the current realities of clinical practice, where other686 Cathy Charles \emph{et al.}
\par
participants may well be involved. It misses
\par
altogether the important role that the patient's
\par
friends or family may play and the case of incompe-
\par
tent or seriously ill patients who require third par-
\par
ties to act on their behalf. There is almost no
\par
discussion in the shared decision-making literature
\par
on the implications of these situations for conceptu-
\par
alization and measurement of shared decision-mak-
\par
ing. Hence, we emphasize that a shared decision-
\par
making process requires at least two participants
\par
(i.e. it takes two to tango) but may often involve
\par
more than two.
\par
\emph{Both parties (physicians and patients) take steps to}
\par
\emph{participate in the process of treatment decision-mak-}
\par
\emph{ing}
\par
Much attention has been focused on exploring
\par
patient preferences for participation in treatment
\par
decision-making. Over the years, several scales have
\par
been developed, with preferences for participation
\par
often conceptualized as a single continuum of the
\par
amount of participation the patient prefers (e.g.
\par
from none, to shared participation, to complete
\par
patient control or autonomy (Strull \emph{et al., }1984;
\par
Beisecker, 1988; Brody \emph{et al., }1989; Ende \emph{et al.,}
\par
1989; Beisecker and Beisecker, 1990)). The terms
\par
control and autonomy are usually not defined; nor
\par
is the term participation. To some, (Strull \emph{et al.,}
\par
1984) participation seems to incorporate the idea of
\par
sharing on an equal basis, but the specifics of what
\par
this really means in terms of input by both phys-
\par
ician and patient is left unclear. Presumably, the
\par
complete patient control end of the continuum cor-
\par
responds to the pure type informed model where
\par
the patient is the sole decision-maker. An implicit
\par
assumption behind much of this research seems to
\par
be that if patients express preferences, they will act
\par
in accordance with these preferences. Preferences
\par
become a proxy for behavioural intent, predictive of
\par
future behaviour.
\par
Empirical research suggests that the link between
\par
patient preferences for participation in treatment
\par
decision-making and actual participation is not that
\par
strong. Patient preferences for information do not
\par
necessarily translate into information seeking beha-
\par
viour; nor do patients who express preferences for
\par
some form of shared decision-making necessarily
\par
act on these in the medical encounter (Haug and
\par
Lavin, 1983; Beisecker, 1988; Beisecker and
\par
Beisecker, 1990; Ryan, 1992). Patient preferences
\par
*Alternatively, it is possible that some patients preferring
\par
an autonomous decision-making role are constrained
\par
to share the process with their physician. In our cul-
\par
ture, physicians have the authority to act as gate-
\par
keepers to health care. They control medical
\par
knowledge, technology, access to treatment and even
\par
norms of behaviour in the medical encounter (Ryan,
\par
1992). At a minimum, the patient needs the physician's
\par
consent to her treatment choice in order to obtain
\par
most services.
\par
for shared decision-making do not seem sufficient to
\par
make it happen in reality*.
\par
If the majority of patients say that they have a
\par
preference for information about their illness and
\par
potential treatment options, while a much smaller
\par
number express preferences to participate in treat-
\par
ment decision-making, this raises some interesting
\par
questions. First, why do patients want information
\par
if it is not to be used by them instrumentally to
\par
help them make a treatment decision? We discuss
\par
this issue in the next section. Second, why do
\par
patients say that they do not want to participate in
\par
treatment decision-making and what does this pre-
\par
ference really mean? We think that the latter is an
\par
under researched area and one that deserves much
\par
more attention because there are a variety of differ-
\par
ent possible explanations which have different impli-
\par
cations for determining when a shared treatment
\par
decision-making process is appropriate.
\par
First, a stated preference (e.g. a patient's stated
\par
preference not to participate in treatment decision-
\par
making) may reflect an underlying and salient per-
\par
sonality characteristic. In this case, introducing a
\par
decision aid to help the patient structure the treat-
\par
ment decision-making process is unlikely to have
\par
the desired effect, because the patient simply is not
\par
motivated to take an active role in the process and
\par
prefers that the physician decide.
\par
For other individuals, a stated preference not to
\par
participate in treatment decision-making may reflect
\par
a situationally specific response. For example, a
\par
woman faced with the diagnosis of early stage
\par
breast cancer may not want to take an active role in
\par
treatment decision-making because of perceived in-
\par
formation or skill deficits. Here, an intervention
\par
such as a decision aid can provide information and
\par
help the patient structure the decision-making pro-
\par
cess. The intervention could, in fact, help to pro-
\par
mote a more active treatment role preference by the
\par
patient.
\par
In still other instances patients may express a pre-
\par
terence for a passive role in treatment decision-mak-
\par
ing because they have learned through previous
\par
interactions that a more active stance is not well
\par
received by providers. For shared decision-making
\par
to occur, there needs to be complementary role ex-
\par
pectations and behaviour between physician and
\par
patient around this issue. No matter how much the
\par
patient wants to participate, if the physician is not
\par
willing, then shared decision-making will not occur.
\par
Similarly, if the physician is willing but the patient
\par
is not, then the process will not be shared. It is in
\par
this sense that we emphasize that "it takes at least
\par
two to tango".
\par
Finally, preferences for a passive role in treatment
\par
decision-making may reflect, in some instances, a
\par
cohort effect. For example, research suggests that
\par
elderly patients often prefer a more passive role in
\par
treatment decision-making than younger patients
\par
(Coe and Prendergast, 1985; Haug, 1994). This isShared treatment decision-making: what does it mean?
\par
687
\par
likely to change over time as the generation of baby
\par
boomers with higher education levels and highel ex-
\par
pectations encounter the health care system.
\par
In summary, the patient preferences literature
\par
provides only a partial answer to the question of
\par
what role patients want to play in treatment
\par
decision-making because it fails to consider that
\par
preferences may be situationally determined, and
\par
hence, subject to change. Moreover, by limiting the
\par
conceptualization and measurement of prefere\textasciitilde aces
\par
to only the patient side of the equation, it misses
\par
the important interactional dynamic with the plays-
\par
ician which is crucial to making shared decision-
\par
making happen in reality*. By and large, physicians
\par
set the norms of interaction in the medical encoun-
\par
ter. If the physician is not motivated to share
\par
decision-making, the patient cannot force this to
\par
happen. Her only option is to seek out another
\par
physician whose expectations about how the
\par
decision-making process should occur is similar to
\par
hers.
\par
If preferences are not enough, then what steps, do
\par
physicians and patients need to take in order to
\par
share in the treatment decision-making process? We
\par
suggest that for the physician it means first, es\textasciitilde :ab-
\par
lishing a conducive atmosphere so that the patient
\par
feels that her views about various treatment options
\par
are valued and needed (Brody, 1980). Second, it
\par
means eliciting patient preferences so that treatment
\par
options discussed are compatible with the patient's
\par
lifestyle and values. Third, it means transferring
\par
technical information to the patient on treatment
\par
options, risks and their probable benefits in as
\par
unbiased, clear and simple a way as is possible.
\par
Fourth, physician participation would also include
\par
helping the patient conceptualize the weighing pro-
\par
cess of risks versus benefits, and asking patients
\par
questions in order to ensure that the causal assump-
\par
tions (information) underlying their treatment pre-
\par
ferences are based on fact and not misconception.
\par
Finally, shared decision-making would also
\par
involve the physician in sharing his treatment :rec-
\par
ommendation with the patient, and/or affirming the
\par
patient's treatment preference. The physician would
\par
need to be careful, however, not to impose his
\par
values about the best treatment onto the patient.
\par
There may always be a danger in a shared
\par
decision-making process that the physician's values
\par
will influence the patient, even if this is not his
\par
intent. Many patients have been socialized to think
\par
that the physician knows best, that they lack the
\par
expertise to make the treatment decision, that a
\par
trusting relationship with the physician means trust-
\par
*This research does, however, demonstrate heterogeneity
\par
in patient preferences for participation in treatment
\par
decision-making. This in turn suggests that any atte\textasciitilde aapt
\par
to define a single normative model of treatment
\par
decision-making which both physicians and patients
\par
"ought" to follow might not fit with empirical reality.
\par
ing his judgement about the most appropriate treat-
\par
ment, or that agreeing with the physician will result
\par
in better or more personal care.
\par
For the patient, shared decision-making means
\par
that she must be willing to engage in the decision-
\par
making process, that is, to take responsibility for
\par
disclosing preferences, asking questions, weighing
\par
and evaluating treatment alternatives and formulat-
\par
ing a treatment preference. This is a problem sol-
\par
ving task that goes beyond information transfer.
\par
Scenario 2 in the Appendix depicts such a shared
\par
decision-making process. Both patient and physician
\par
discuss and evaluate treatment options and together
\par
they build a consensus. We suggest that the test of
\par
a shared decision-making process is if both parties
\par
adopt the complementary roles outlined above, and
\par
if both parties are satisfied with their level of invol-
\par
vement.
\par
\emph{Information sharing is a prerequisite to shared}
\par
\emph{decision-making}
\par
In the typical case, information sharing is a prere-
\par
quisite for shared decision-making. At minimum,
\par
the physician needs to lay out treatment alternatives
\par
and their potential consequences for the patient in
\par
order to obtain informed consent. Without such in-
\par
formation, there might be nothing for the patient to
\par
evaluate and deliberate about. As noted earlier,
\par
patients may also bring information obtained
\par
through other means to the encounter. Both
\par
patients and physicians bring both information and
\par
values; it is not simply a question of physicians
\par
bringing knowledge and patients bringing values.
\par
As a practical problem, it is often not clear what
\par
type and amount of information patients want from
\par
physicians, or why they want it. Patients may want
\par
more information than physicians think is needed
\par
instrumentally to help distinguish the pros and cons
\par
of various treatment options. Similarly, the types of
\par
information physicians desire from patients is not
\par
always clear. Is it the patient's full illness narrative
\par
or only those elements of the patient's story that the
\par
physician thinks relevant to suggesting treatment
\par
options?
\par
The physician's primary professional obligation is
\par
to apply expertise in the treatment of patients. Time
\par
constraints, the potentially rapid course of many
\par
diseases, and financial incentives all operate to
\par
encourage physicians to complete the decision-mak-
\par
ing process as quickly as possible. In this situation
\par
it is likely that patient information is sought by the
\par
physician primarily for its instrumental use in iden-
\par
tifying treatment options that are compatible with
\par
patient values.
\par
The value of information about treatment
\par
alternatives, and their risks and benefits for the
\par
patient, is less well understood (Haug and Lavin,
\par
1981; Beisecker and Beisecker, 1990; Ryan, 1992).
\par
As noted earlier, studies indicate that many patients
\par
have high preferences for information about their688 Cathy Charles \emph{et al.}
\par
illness and its management but do not engage in in-
\par
formation seeking behaviours or in treatment
\par
decision-making. The value of information, from
\par
the patient's perspective, does not appear to lie (or
\par
lie solely) in its potential use as an aid to decision-
\par
making. More important may be the psychological
\par
reassurance or reduced uncertainty which infor-
\par
mation is thought to provide at a time of great
\par
stress and vulnerability (Mooney and Ryan, 1993).
\par
More research is needed in order to more comple-
\par
tely understand the value of information from the
\par
patient's perspective.
\par
Research evidence also suggests that when phys-
\par
icians infer patient preferences for information and
\par
for participation in treatment decision-making, they
\par
often fail to get them right (Strull \emph{et al., }1984;
\par
Waitzkin, 1984; Ryan, 1992). If the medical inter-
\par
action is to reflect patient role preferences, these
\par
need to be made explicit by the patient either on
\par
her own initiative or elicited by the physician as
\par
part of the information exchange.
\par
making (paternalistic and informed) but it remains
\par
a necessary prerequisite for shared decision-making.
\par
However, mutual acceptance does not always in-
\par
dicate a shared decision-making process. A paterna-
\par
listic process of decision-making, for example, can
\par
result in a shared decision. How do we know when
\par
mutual agreement on the decision to implement is
\par
reached? An explicit and verbal acknowledgement
\par
provides one potential indication and can be ascer-
\par
tained through observation. Sometimes, the agree-
\par
ment may be implicit, and signified by a
\par
behavioural intent by the patient to return for treat-
\par
ment or by the physician to book a future treatment
\par
appointment. A verbal agreement at one point in
\par
time may not hold over time. In Scenario 4 in the
\par
Appendix, it appears that an agreement has been
\par
reached at the end of the medical encounter but,
\par
subsequently, the patient changed her mind and did
\par
not return for treatment.
\par
\emph{A treatment decision is made and both parties agree}
\par
\emph{to the decision}
\par
Shared decision-making is usually depicted, either
\par
implicitly or explicitly as a type of decision-making
\par
process. But shared treatment decision-making can
\par
also refer to an outcome, i.e. a shared or agreed
\par
upon decision. The shared process relates to the
\par
roles that patients and physicians play and involves
\par
complementary role expectations and behaviour.
\par
Agreement between physician and patient about the
\par
treatment decision is one possible outcome of this
\par
process; others include no decision or disagreement
\par
as to the preferred treatment. In Scenario 3, for
\par
example, both the physician and the patient reveal
\par
their preferences about treatment but they cannot
\par
reach an agreement.
\par
The test of a shared decision (as distinct from the
\par
decision-making process) is if both parties agree on
\par
the treatment option. This does not mean that both
\par
parties are necessarily convinced that this is the best
\par
possible treatment for this patient, but rather that
\par
both endorse it as the treatment to implement. The
\par
physician may feel, for example, that the patient
\par
would really be better off with another treatment
\par
but agrees to endorse the patient's choice as part of
\par
a negotiated agreement in which the patient's views
\par
count. Through mutual acceptance, both parties
\par
share responsibility for the final decision.
\par
This is an important characteristic and helps to
\par
distinguish shared decision-making from other
\par
models of decision-making. In the extreme case of
\par
the paternalistic and informed models, decision-
\par
making and ultimate responsibility for the decision
\par
are clearly vested with the physician or the patient
\par
respectively, and whether the opposite party accepts
\par
the decision is not relevant. Mutual acceptance may
\par
or may not occur with other models of decision-
\par
SOME MEASUREMENT \textbar SSUES
\par
If there is confusion about what shared treatment
\par
decision-making means conceptually, then there is
\par
bound to be difficulty in measuring it empirically
\par
because of disagreement over what one is looking
\par
for as defining characteristics. We have described
\par
our ideas about this above. Even so, major meth-
\par
odological issues remain. For example some of the
\par
defining characteristics of a shared decision-making
\par
model overlap with those of other models and some
\par
characteristics may be present in varying degrees.
\par
Both these conditions make it difficult to establish
\par
clear boundaries or thresholds of what is inside ver-
\par
sus outside the shared treatment decision-making
\par
box. This is particularly true for the measurement
\par
of a patient's level of participation in treatment
\par
decision-making. For example, if a patient simply
\par
agrees to a treatment decision suggested by the
\par
physician, does this mean she participated in the
\par
treatment process? We do not think so, but the
\par
issue still remains, to what extent does the patient
\par
need to reveal and discuss treatment preferences in
\par
order for her interaction to be defined as one of
\par
shared decision-making? Also, a given medical
\par
encounter may start as one model of interaction,
\par
but evolve into something else as the encounter
\par
unfolds. This may make it difficult to precisely clas-
\par
sify any given medical encounter as falling within
\par
one model or another.
\par
Empirically measuring if and how patients delib-
\par
erate over treatment choices and the process they
\par
use to arrive at a decision is no easy task.
\par
Observation techniques are frequently used to
\par
measure physician-patient communication more
\par
generally, using different interaction analysis sys-
\par
tems. Many of these have been recently summarized
\par
in an article by Ong \emph{et al. }(1995). These systems areShared treatment decision-making: what does it mean? designed to identify and quantify salient features
\par
of physician-patient communication. Observation
\par
methods alone, however, have some limitations
\par
when it comes to measuring decision-making pro-
\par
cesses that occur "in the patient's head" and may
\par
not be made explicit either through verbal or non-
\par
verbal behaviour. From what we observe, we would
\par
need to make attributions about the patient's imen-
\par
tions and internal decision-making processes. For
\par
example, in Scenario 1, is the patient's questior to
\par
the physician a form of information gathering so
\par
that she can better evaluate treatment options or is
\par
she trying to get the physician to make the decision
\par
for her? Does her ultimate agreement with the phy-
\par
sician's recommendation reflect a thoughtful con-
\par
sideration of the alternatives, deference to physician
\par
authority, or reluctance to engage in the decision-
\par
making process? The same observation could y:eld
\par
several different stories or interpretations.
\par
Similarly, structured questionnaires that have
\par
used a Likert type scale to measure patient prefer-
\par
ences for or actual participation in treatment
\par
decision-making in previous studies also have limi-
\par
tations. They simplify and structure the measure-
\par
ment process to such a degree that little information
\par
is gleaned about the dynamics of shared decision-
\par
making or the interactional process involved.
\par
Moreover, as noted earlier, it is often not clear how
\par
to interpret different preferences.
\par
We think that an important area for future
\par
research is a more in-depth exploration of why
\par
patients hold different treatment preferences, and
\par
what meanings patients ascribe to these preferences.
\par
Qualitative methods such as illness narratives or
\par
semi-structured interviews seem well suited to lhis
\par
task for several reasons. First, they allow for an in-
\par
depth exploration and, hopefully, increased under-
\par
standing of a complex process: how patients think
\par
about decision-making in general and treatment
\par
decision-making in particular. Second, they allow
\par
more freedom for the patient to structure the dis-
\par
course of the interview so that the information cap-
\par
tured reflects patients' perspectives rather than
\par
researchers' preconceived measurement categor:es.
\par
Third, with qualitative interviews it is also possible
\par
to pay much more attention to social, cultural and
\par
illness contexts which may influence patients' views
\par
about appropriate roles in treatment decision-mak-
\par
ing. Such interviews may also be used to generate
\par
information about what patients perceive are bar-
\par
riers to shared decision-making and the kinds of
\par
interventions they suggest (if any) that would help
\par
them to adopt a more active treatment decision-
\par
making role.
\par
In making the above recommendation we do not
\par
mean to suggest that qualitative methods are 1:he
\par
only appropriate ones for studying issues related to
\par
shared decision-making. For many issues, a combi-
\par
nation of qualitative and quantitative methods r\textasciitilde ay
\par
well be appropriate. The particular methods
\par
689
\par
adopted should be those most useful in generating
\par
the best information about the specific question of
\par
interest.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
Because shared decision-making is increasingly
\par
advocated as an ideal treatment decision-making
\par
process, one might be tempted to try to identify
\par
explicit behaviours prescribing how to engage in
\par
this process. The ability to define specific behaviours
\par
holds appeal because it would provide a form of
\par
instructional guide, but we think it is also proble-
\par
matic. There is no single route to shared decision-
\par
making; some of the characteristics we describe can
\par
be met by a variety of different behaviours. Also,
\par
the physician-patient relationship is a personal, and
\par
often trusting relationship. To develop a standar-
\par
dized checklist of concrete and invariant steps for
\par
shared decision-making would not fit this decision-
\par
making context where the preferences of both phys-
\par
icians and patients can vary widely and change over
\par
time. While a checklist approach may resonate with
\par
many physicians in terms of a clinical framework of
\par
decision-making (Sackett \emph{et al., }1985), it may not
\par
resonate with patients' models of decision-making
\par
or constructions of their illness experience. Finally,
\par
shared decision-making is in some sense a matter of
\par
perception. Similar objective states of shared
\par
decision-making (if these could be defined) might
\par
well be both perceived and valued differently by
\par
different patients.
\par
Perhaps more important than prescribing specific
\par
behaviours would be to agree on certain fundamen-
\par
tal principles around treatment decision-making
\par
for example, that patients' preferences for partici-
\par
pation be elicited and acknowledged, that patients
\par
be given choices as to how the decision-making pro-
\par
cess will proceed, and that their choices be respected
\par
and adhered to in the physician's behaviour.
\par
Herein lies one of the benefits of a shared
\par
decision-making approach. It offers a potential
\par
middle choice of treatment decision-making models
\par
between two polar extremes. On the one hand, the
\par
paternalistic model is characterized by physician
\par
dominance of the decision-making process. On the
\par
other hand, the informed decision-making model
\par
seems to limit the role of the physician to one of
\par
transferring information, enhancing the patient's
\par
ability to engage in autonomous decision-making
\par
with ultimate control but also responsibility for the
\par
treatment choice. Empirical research demonstrates
\par
that many patients, for whatever reasons, prefer not
\par
to assume full decision-making control. But many
\par
may also not like the idea of no say at all. Shared
\par
decision-making offers an intermediate alternative
\par
for both physician and patient. For the patient it
\par
offers some say without total responsibility, and for
\par
the physician, an opportunity to go beyond a role690 Cathy Charles \emph{et al.}
\par
of transferring information to also participate in,
\par
but not dominate, the decision-making process.
