  pages = {187--203},
  publisher = {Springer},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Risk Analysis, Vol. 19, No. 2, 1999
\par
Combining Probability Distributions From Experts in
\par
Risk Analysis
\par
Robert T. Clemen1,2 and Robert L. Winkler1
\par
This paper concerns the combination of experts' probability distributions in risk analysis,
\par
discussing a variety of combination methods and attempting to highlight the important
\par
conceptual and practical issues to be considered in designing a combination process in
\par
practice. The role of experts is important because their judgments can provide valuable
\par
information, particularly in view of the limited availability of ``hard data'' regarding many
\par
important uncertainties in risk analysis. Because uncertainties are represented in terms of
\par
probability distributions in probabilistic risk analysis (PRA), we consider expert information
\par
in terms of probability distributions. The motivation for the use of multiple experts is
\par
simply the desire to obtain as much information as possible. Combining experts' probability
\par
distributions summarizes the accumulated information for risk analysts and decision-makers.
\par
Procedures for combining probability distributions are often compartmentalized as mathe-
\par
matical aggregation methods or behavioral approaches, and we discuss both categories.
\par
However, an overall aggregation process could involve both mathematical and behavioral
\par
aspects, and no single process is best in all circumstances. An understanding of the pros and
\par
cons of different methods and the key issues to consider is valuable in the design of a
\par
combination process for a specific PRA. The output, a ``combined probability distribution,''
\par
can ideally be viewed as representing a summary of the current state of expert opinion
\par
regarding the uncertainty of interest.
\par
KEY WORDS: Combining probabilities; expert judgment; probability assessment.
\par
1. INTRODUCTION
\par
Expert judgments can provide useful informa-
\par
tion for forecasting, making decisions, and assessing
\par
risks. Such judgments have been used informally for
\par
many years. More formally, consulting several ex-
\par
perts when considering forecasting or risk-assessment
\par
problems has become increasingly commonplace in
\par
the post-World War II era. Cooke (1991) reviews
\par
many of the developments over the years as attempts
\par
have been made to use expert judgments in various
\par
settings. Application areas have been diverse, includ-
\par
1 Fuqua School of Business, Duke University, Durham, NC
\par
27708-0120.
\par
2 To whom all correspondence should be addressed.
\par
187
\par
ing nuclear engineering, aerospace, various types of
\par
forecasting (economic, technological, meteorologi-
\par
cal, and snow avalanches, to name a few), military
\par
intelligence, seismic risk, and environmental risk
\par
from toxic chemicals.
\par
In this paper we consider the problem of using
\par
multiple experts. Wu, Apostolakis, and Okrent (1990,
\par
p. 170) state that ``In PRA, an important issue related
\par
to knowledge representation under uncertainty is the
\par
resolution of conflicting information or opinions.''
\par
Although we discuss judgments of various kinds, in-
\par
cluding forecasts, estimates, and probability assess-
\par
ments, our primary focus is on the aggregation and
\par
use of subjectively assessed probability distributions.
\par
The paper does not pretend to give a comprehensive
\par
view of the topic of group judgments; the accumu-
\par
0272-4332/99/0400-0187\$16.00/1  1999 Society for Risk Analysis188 Clemen and Winkler
\par
lated knowledge in this field springs from many disci-
\par
plines, including statistics, psychology, economics,
\par
engineering, risk analysis, and decision theory. Our
\par
intent is to highlight the key issues involved in com-
\par
bining experts' probability distributions and to dis-
\par
cuss a variety of combining methods.
\par
Because the focus in this paper is on the combi-
\par
nation of experts' probability distributions, we do
\par
not discuss the process by which such probability
\par
distributions might be elicited from individual ex-
\par
perts. For general discussions of risk analysis, the
\par
reader is directed to basic sources (see Merkhofer
\par
(1987), Mosleh, Bier, \& Apostolakis (1987), Bonano
\par
et al. (1990), Keeney \& von Winterfeldt (1989, 1991),
\par
Morgan \& Henrion (1990), Hora (1992), and Ot-
\par
way \& von Winterfeldt (1992)). We believe that the
\par
elicitation protocol should be designed and con-
\par
ducted by a risk assessment team comprising individ-
\par
uals knowledgeable about both the substantive issues
\par
of interest and probability elicitation.
\par
The mathematical and behavioral approaches
\par
we discuss in this paper assume that the experts
\par
have ironed out differences in definitions and that
\par
they all agree on exactly what is to be forecast or
\par
assessed. Practicing risk analysts know that these
\par
are strong assumptions and that much effort may
\par
be required in order to reach such agreement.
\par
Discussions of protocol development and use, such
\par
as those referenced in the previous paragraph, typi-
\par
cally emphasize the importance of familiarizing the
\par
experts with the major issues in the problem at
\par
hand so that the experts will have a common
\par
understanding of the problem. Bonduelle (1987)
\par
specifically addresses the matter of resolving defini-
\par
tional disagreements among experts as an integral
\par
part of the aggregation problem.
\par
Even though reasonable experts may agree on
\par
the definitions of relevant variables, it is, of course,
\par
still possible for them to disagree about probabilities
\par
for those variables. Such disagreement may arise
\par
for a multitude of reasons, ranging from different
\par
analytical methods to differing information sets or
\par
different philosophical approaches. Indeed, if they
\par
never disagreed there would be no point in con-
\par
sulting more than one expert. Morgan and Keith
\par
(1995, p. 468) note that the results of expert elic-
\par
itations related to climate change ``reveal a rich
\par
diversity of expert opinion.'' Consulting multiple
\par
experts may be viewed as a subjective version of
\par
increasing the sample size in an experiment. Because
\par
subjective information is often viewed as being
\par
``softer'' than ``hard scientific data,'' it seems particu-
\par
larly appropriate to consult multiple experts in an
\par
attempt to beef up the information base.
\par
These motivations are reasonable; the funda-
\par
mental principle that underlies the use of multiple
\par
experts is that a set of experts can provide more
\par
information than a single expert. Although it is
\par
sometimes reasonable to provide a decision maker
\par
with only the individual experts' probability distribu-
\par
tions, the range of which can be studied using
\par
sensitivity analysis, it is often necessary to combine
\par
the distributions into a single one. In many cases,
\par
for example, a single distribution is needed for
\par
input into a larger model; if that model requires
\par
distributions for many variables, a full-blown sensi-
\par
tivity analysis may not be feasible.
\par
Combination, or aggregation, procedures are
\par
often dichotomized into mathematical and behav-
\par
ioral approaches, although in practice aggregation
\par
might involve some aspects of each. Mathematical
\par
aggregation methods consist of processes or analyti-
\par
cal models that operate on the individual probability
\par
distributions to produce a single ``combined'' proba-
\par
bility distribution. For example, we might just take
\par
the averages of probabilities from multiple experts.
\par
Reviews of the literature on mathematical combina-
\par
tion of probability distributions include Winkler
\par
(1968), French (1985), Genest and Zidek (1986),
\par
and Cooke (1991); Clemen (1989) reviews the
\par
broader area of combining forecasts (see also Bunn,
\par
1988). Mathematical aggregation methods range
\par
from simple summary measures such as arithmetic
\par
or geometric means of probabilities to procedures
\par
based on axiomatic approaches or on various models
\par
of the information-aggregation process requiring
\par
inputs regarding characteristics such as the quality
\par
of and dependence among the experts' probabilities.
\par
In contrast, behavioral aggregation approaches
\par
attempt to generate agreement among the experts
\par
by having them interact in some way. This interac-
\par
tion may be face-to-face or may involve exchanges
\par
of information without direct contact. Behavioral
\par
approaches consider the quality of individual expert
\par
judgments and dependence among such judgments
\par
implicitly rather than explicitly. As information is
\par
shared, it is anticipated that better arguments and
\par
information will be more important in influencing
\par
the group and that redundant information will
\par
be discounted.
\par
In Sections 2 and 3, we discuss mathematical
\par
and behavioral methods, respectively, for combining
\par
experts' probability distributions. Some empirical
\par
results regarding these approaches are presentedCombining Probability Distributions 189
\par
in Section 4, and in Section 5 we summarize our
\par
views on the key issues in the combination of
\par
experts' probability distributions in risk analysis.
\par
2. MATHEMATICAL COMBINATION
\par
METHODS
\par
2.1. Axiomatic Approaches
\par
Early work on mathematical aggregation of
\par
probabilities focused on axiom-based aggregation
\par
formulas. In these studies, the strategy was to postu-
\par
late certain properties that the combined distribution
\par
should follow and then derive the functional form of
\par
the combined distribution. French (1985) and Genest
\par
and Zidek (1986) provide critical reviews of this liter-
\par
ature, and our summary here draws heavily on
\par
these sources.
\par
An appealing approach to the aggregation of
\par
probability distributions is the linear opinion pool,
\par
so named by Stone (1961), and dating back to Laplace
\par
(Bacharach, 1979):
\par
