\par
of dependence in the context of the normal model.
\par
Schmittlein, Kim, and Morrison (1990) develop pro-
\par
cedures to decide whether to use weights based on the
\par
covariance matrix or to use equal weights. Similarly,
\par
Chandrasekharan, Moriarty, and Wright (1994) pro-
\par
pose methods for investigating the stability of weights
\par
and deciding whether to eliminate some experts from
\par
the combination.
\par
Although the normal model has been useful, it
\par
has some shortcomings. In particular, one must find
\par
a way to fit the expert judgments into the normal
\par
framework. If the experts have provided distributions
\par
that are unimodal and roughly symmetric, this is gen-
\par
erally not a problem. Otherwise, some sort of trans-
\par
formation is required. The covariance matrix typi-
\par
cally is estimated from data; Winkler (1981) derives
\par
a formal Bayesian model when is viewed as an
\par
uncertain parameter. Assessing subjectively is pos-
\par
sible; Gokhale and Press (1982) and Clemen and
\par
Reilly (1999) discuss the assessment of correlation
\par
coefficients via a number of different probability
\par
transformations. Finally, the posterior distribution is
\par
always a normal distribution, and typically is a com-
\par
promise. For example, suppose two experts give
\par
