\par
Another variant on the normal model is a Bayes-
\par
ian hierarchical model (Lipscomb, Parmigiani, \&
\par
Hasselblad, 1998) that allows for differential random
\par
bias on the part of the experts by assuming that each
\par
expert's error mean in the normal model can be
\par
viewed as a random draw from a second-order distri-
\par
bution on the error means. Dependence among ex-
\par
perts' probabilities arises through the common
\par
second-order distribution. Hierarchical models
\par
generally result in an effective shrinkage of individual
\par
means to the overall mean and tend to provide robust
\par
estimates. Because they involve another layer of un-
\par
certainty, they can be complex, particularly when the
\par
parameters are all viewed as unknown and requiring
\par
prior distributions.
\par
Mendel and Sheridan (1989) develop a Bayesian
\par
model (also discussed in some detail in Cooke, 1991)
\par
that allows for the aggregation of probability distribu-
\par
tions that are not necessarily normal. Assume that
\par
each expert always provides m fractiles of his or her
\par
distribution. For each expert, this defines m 1 bins
\par
into which the actual outcome could fall. With n
\par
experts, an (m 1)n array represents all of the possi-
\par
ble joint outcomes that could conceivably occur. Each
\par
cell in this array represents the actual outcome falling
\par
into a specific bin for expert 1, a bin for expert 2,
\par
and so on for all n experts. Data on previous assess-
\par
ments and outcomes provides information about the
\par
likelihood of an outcome falling in each bin. The
\par
model incorporates this information in a Bayesian
\par
way, updating the distribution on the parameters rep-
\par
resenting the probability for each bin as new informa-
\par
tion is obtained.
\par
When the experts provide a set of distributions
\par
for a new variable, , there are specific bins (at most
\par
nm 1) into which the actual realization of may
\par
fall, owing to the way the experts' fractiles interleave.
\par
To find the aggregated probability distribution for ,
\par
the distribution is normalized over the possible bins
\par
into which the actual may fall.
\par
Mendel and Sheridan's model conveniently com-
\par
bines issues of both individual calibration and depen-
\par
dence, and it does so in capturing information about
\par
the occurrences of actual outcomes in the (m 1)n
\par
array. In fact, this approach is perhaps most appropri-
\par
ately termed joint calibration, because it produces
\par
probability distributions that are based on a multivar-
\par
iate version of the traditional single-expert calibra-
\par
tion approach. The notion of joint calibration is also
\par
discussed by Clemen (1986), who notes that Morris'sCombining Probability Distributions 193
\par
Bayesian aggregation paradigm can be viewed as a
\par
joint-calibration exercise. In addition, Clemen (1985)
\par
and Clemen and Murphy (1986) use a joint-calibra-
\par
tion procedure similar in spirit to Mendel and Sheri-
\par
dan to study the combination of meteorological fore-
\par
casts of precipitation.
\par
Many of the methods above require the estima-
\par
tion of parameters of the likelihood function, and an
\par
often unspoken assumption is that past data will be
\par
used for such estimation. Although data-based meth-
\par
ods might be viewed as an important part of the
\par
scientific process, it is often the case in risk analysis
\par
that past data are not available. In such situations,
\par
the decision-maker or the risk-assessment team can
\par
subjectively estimate the parameters (or assess prior
\par
distributions for the parameters). This can be a diffi-
\par
cult task, however; consider the assessment of the
\par
elements of the covariance matrix in the normal
\par
model or of the probabilities associated with each
\par
cell in the (m 1)n array in Mendel and Sheri-
\par
dan's model.
\par
Some effort has been directed toward the devel-
\par
opment of Bayesian aggregation methods that are
\par
suitable for the use of subjective judgment in de-
\par
termining the likelihood function. Clemen and Win-
\par
kler (1993), for example, present a process for subjec-
\par
tively combining point estimates from experts; the
\par
approach is based on the sequential assessment of
\par
conditional distributions among the experts' fore-
\par
casts, where the conditioning is specified in an influ-
\par
ence diagram. Formal attention has also been given
\par
to Bayesian models for situations in which the experts
\par
provide only partial specifications of their probability
\par
distributions (e.g., moments, fractiles) or the decision
\par
maker is similarly unable to specify the likelihood
\par
function fully (Genest \& Schervish, 1985; West, 1992;
\par
West \& Crosse, 1992; Gelfand, Mallick, \& Dey, 1995).
\par
Bunn (1975) develops a model that considers only
\par
which expert performs best on any given occasion
\par
and uses a Bayesian approach to update weights in
\par
a combination rule based on past performance.
