\par
In this section, we have discussed a number of
\par
mathematical methods for combining experts' proba-
\par
bility distributions. A number of important issues
\par
should be kept in mind when comparing these ap-
\par
proaches and choosing an approach for a given appli-
\par
cation. These issues include the type of information
\par
that is available (e.g., whether full probability distri-
\par
butions are given by the experts or just some partial
\par
specifications of these distributions); the individuals
\par
performing the aggregation of probabilities (e.g., the
\par
risk assessment team, a single decision-maker or ana-
\par
lyst, or some other set of individuals); the degree
\par
of modeling to be undertaken (assessment of the
\par
likelihood function, consideration of the quality of
\par
the experts' judgments); the form of the combination
\par
rule (which could follow directly from modeling or
\par
could be taken as a primitive, such as a weighted
\par
average); the specification of parameters of the com-
\par
bination rule (e.g., the weights); and the consideration
\par
of simple vs. complex rules (e.g., simple averages
\par
versus more complex models). The empirical results194 Clemen and Winkler
\par
in Section 4 will shed some light on some of these
\par
questions, and we will discuss the issues further in
\par
Section 6.
\par
3. BEHAVIORAL APPROACHES
\par
Behavioral combination approaches require ex-
\par
perts to interact in some fashion. Some possibilities
\par
include face-to-face group meetings, interaction by
\par
computer, or sharing of information in other ways.
\par
The group may assess probabilities or forecasts, or
\par
simply discuss relevant issues and ideas with only
\par
informal judgmental assessment. Emphasis is some-
\par
times placed on attempting to reach agreement, or
\par
consensus, within the group of experts; at other times
\par
it is simply placed on sharing of information and
\par
having the experts learn from each other. The degree
\par
to which the risk-assessment team is involved in struc-
\par
turing and facilitating the expert interaction can vary.
\par
Specific procedures (e.g., Delphi, Nominal Group
\par
Technique) can be used, or the risk-assessment team
\par
can design the interaction process to suit a particu-
\par
lar application.
\par
The simplest behavioral approach is to assemble
\par
the experts and assign them the task of generating
\par
a ``group'' probability distribution. Discussion and
\par
debate can be wide-ranging, presumably resulting in
\par
a thorough sharing of individual information. Ideally,
\par
such sharing of information leads to a consensus, and
\par
under certain circumstances agreement ``should'' be
\par
reached (e.g., Aumann, 1976). In practice, though,
\par
experts often do not agree. Any group probabilities
\par
that arise from the discussion may require the experts
\par
to negotiate and compromise in some way. In this
\par
sense, the so-called consensus may not reflect any
\par
one expert's thoughts perfectly. Instead, it is a state-
\par
ment that each expert is willing to accept for the sake
\par
of the group appearing to speak with one voice.
\par
Group interaction can suffer from many prob-
\par
lems (e.g., see Janis \& Mann, 1977). Some individuals
\par
may tend to dominate the discussions, new ideas may
\par
be discouraged, or the group may ignore important
\par
information. The phenomenon of group polarization
\par
may occur, in which a group tends to adopt a position
\par
more extreme than its information warrants (Plous,
\par
1993). Hogarth (1977) points out, however, that inter-
\par
action processes need not be dysfunctional. Having
\par
experienced analysts serve as facilitators for the group
\par
can improve the process. For example, decision con-
\par
ferencing (Phillips, 1984, 1987) is a particular ap-
\par
proach to structuring group discussion for decision-
\par
making and group probability assessment. The role of
\par
facilitators is very important in decision conferencing,
\par
and Phillips and Phillips (1990) claim that the main
\par
role of a facilitator should be to control the process
\par
and structure of the group interaction (thereby trying
\par
to head off dysfunctional aspects of interaction), not
\par
to contribute substantively to the content of the dis-
\par
cussion.
\par
One of the oldest approaches to structuring
\par
group judgments, the Delphi method, requires indi-
\par
rect interaction (Dalkey, 1969; Linstone \& Turoff,
\par
1975; Parente \textasciiacute{} \& Anderson-Parente \textasciiacute, 1987). Although
\par
different variations exist, experts typically make indi-
\par
vidual judgments (in our case, assessments of proba-
\par
bility distributions), after which judgments are shared
\par
anonymously. Each expert may then revise his or
\par
her probabilities, and the process may be repeated.
\par
Ideally, the experts would agree after a few rounds,
\par
but this rarely happens. At the end of the Delphi
\par
rounds, the experts' final probability distributions
\par
typically are combined mathematically.
\par
The Nominal Group Technique (Delbecq, Van
\par
de Ven, \& Gustafson, 1975) is a related behavioral ag-
\par
gregation approach. Experts first assess their proba-
\par
bility distributions individually and then present those
\par
distributions to other group members. Group discus-
\par
sion follows with the assistance of a facilitator, after
\par
which the experts may revise their probabilities. As
\par
with Delphi, the final probability distributions may re-
\par
quire mathematical aggregation. Lock (1987) pro-
\par
poses a similar behavioral approach that stresses care-
\par
ful task definition and advocacy of alternative
\par
perspectives.
\par
A recently proposed aggregation method de-
\par
scribed byKaplan (1990)is designed toaccount explic-
\par
itly for the information available to the group. In
\par
Kaplan's approach, a facilitator/analyst first leads the
\par
experts through a discussion of the available informa-
\par
tion. The objective of this discussion is to determine
\par
the ``consensus body of evidence'' for the variable of
\par
interest. When consensus is reached regarding the rel-
\par
evant information, the analyst proposes a probability
\par
distribution, conditioned on the consensus body of ev-
\par
idence. At this point, the analyst must obtain assur-
\par
ance from the experts that the evidence has been inter-
\par
preted correctly in arriving at the probability
\par
distribution. Because different experts may interpret
\par
the evidence in different ways, the ``group judgment''
\par
may differ from the individual experts' judgments and
\par
may result from something like a negotiation process
\par
among the experts.
\par
Our discussion in this section has been brief, notCombining Probability Distributions 195
\par
because behavioral combination approaches are not
\par
important but because they are, for the most part, not
\par
a set of fixed procedures. Delphi, the Nominal Group
\par
Technique, decision conferencing, and Kaplan's ap-
\par
proach are exceptions. There are, however, a number
\par
of important issues relating to behavioral combina-
\par
tion: the type of interaction (e.g., face-to-face, via com-
\par
puter, anonymous); the nature of the interaction (e.g.,
\par
sharing information on relevant issues, sharing proba-
\par
bilities, trying to assess ``group probabilities''); the
\par
possibility of individual reassessment after interac-
\par
tion; and the role of the assessment team (e.g., as facili-
\par
tators). Although the discussion of behavioral ap-
\par
proaches is brief, the empirical evidence regarding
\par
behavioral combination and related topics such as
\par
group decision making is more extensive, as we shall
\par
see in Section 4.
