4. EMPIRICAL EVIDENCE
\par
The various combining techniques discussed in
\par
Sections 2 and 3 all have some intuitive appeal, and
\par
some have a strong theoretical basis given that certain
\par
assumptions are satisfied. The proof, of course, is in
\par
the pudding. How do the methods perform in prac-
\par
tice? Do the combination methods lead to ``im-
\par
proved'' probability distributions? Do some appear
\par
to perform better than others? Some evidence avail-
\par
able from experimentation, analysis of various data
\par
sets, and actual applications, including work on com-
\par
bining forecasts that is relevant to combining proba-
\par
bilities, is reviewed in this section.
\par
4.1. Mathematical Versus Intuitive Aggregation
\par
Before comparing different combination meth-
\par
ods, we should step back and ask whether one should
\par
bother with formal aggregation methods. Perhaps it
\par
suffices for the decision-maker or the risk-assessment
\par
team to look at the individual experts' probability
\par
distributions and to aggregate them intuitively, di-
\par
rectly assessing a probability distribution in light of
\par
the experts' information.
\par
Hogarth (1987, Ch. 3) discusses the difficulty
\par
humans have in combining information from differ-
\par
ent data sources. Although his discussion covers the
\par
use of all kinds of information, his arguments apply
\par
to the aggregation of expert opinion. Among other
\par
phenomena, Hogarth shows how individuals tend to
\par
ignore dependence among information sources, and
\par
4.2. Comparisons Among Mathematical Methods
\par
Some evidence is available regarding the relative
\par
performance of various mathematical aggregation
\par
methods. In an early study, Stae \textasciidieresis l von Holstein (1972)
\par
studied averages of probabilities relating to stock
\par
market prices. Most of the averages performed simi-
\par
larly, with weights based on rankings of past perfor-
\par
mance slightly better than the rest.
\par
Seaver (1978) evaluated simple and weighted
\par
averages of individual probabilities. The perfor-
\par
mance of the different combining methods was simi-
\par
lar, and Seaver's conclusion was that simple combina-
\par
tion procedures, such as an equally weighted average,
\par
produces combined probabilities that perform as well
\par
as those from more complex aggregation models. Fer-
\par
rell (1985) reached the same conclusion in his review
\par
of mathematical aggregation methods, and Clemen
\par
and Winkler (1987) reported similar results in aggre-
\par
gating precipitation probability forecasts.
\par
In a follow-up study, Clemen and Winkler (1990)
\par
studied the combination of precipitation forecasts us-
\par
ing a wider variety of mathematical methods. One
\par
of the more complex methods that was able to ac-
\par
count for dependence among the forecasts performed
\par
best. Although a simple average was not explicitly
\par
considered, a weighted average that resulted in
\par
weights for the two forecasts that were not widely
\par
different performed almost as well as the more com-
\par
plex scheme.196 Clemen and Winkler
\par
Winkler and Poses (1993) report on the combi-
\par
nation of experts' probabilities in a medical setting.
\par
For each patient in an intensive care unit, four indi-
\par
viduals (an intern, a critical care fellow, a critical
\par
care attending, and a primary attending physician)
\par
assessed probabilities of survival. All possible combi-
\par
nations (simple averages) of these four probabilities
\par
were evaluated. The best combination turned out to
\par
be an average of probabilities from the two physicians
\par
who were simultaneously the most experienced and
\par
the least similar, with one being an expert in critical
\par
care and the other having the most knowledge about
\par
the individual patient.
\par
All of these results are consistent with the gen-
\par
eral message that has been derived from the vast
\par
empirical literature on the combination of point fore-
\par
casts. That message is that, in general, simpler aggre-
\par
gation methods perform better than more complex
\par
methods. Clemen (1989) discusses this literature. In
\par
some of these studies, taking into account the quality
\par
of the expert information, especially regarding rela-
\par
tive precision of forecasts, turns out to be valuable.
\par
The above studies focus on the combination of
\par
point forecasts or event probabilities, and mathemati-
\par
cal methods studied have been either averages or
\par
something more complex in which combination
\par
weights were based on past data. Does the result
\par
that simpler methods work better than more complex
\par
methods carry over to the aggregation of probability
\par
distributions, especially when the quality of the ex-
\par
pert opinions must be judged subjectively? Little spe-
\par
cific evidence appears to be available on this topic.
\par
Clemen, Jones, and Winkler (1996) reported that
\par
Winkler's (1981) normal model and the more com-
\par
plex conditional-distributions model (Clemen \&
\par
Winkler, 1993) performed at about the same level.
\par
Even though little evidence exists on the perfor-
\par
mance of combination methods for probability distri-
\par
butions, do other kinds of evidence exist that might
\par
shed light on the performance of the different kinds
\par
of methods? The answer is a qualified ``yes.'' The
\par
Bayesian approach to aggregating expert probabili-
\par
ties can be viewed as an exercise in decomposition.
\par
Rather than a decision maker holistically assessing a
\par
probability distribution in light of expert reports, the
\par
Bayesian approach decomposes the problem into one
\par
of making assessments about expert judgments that
\par
are then recomposed via Bayes Theorem. Thus, for
\par
prospective evidence on the performance of Bayesian
\par
approaches, we appeal to the growing literature on
\par
the value of decomposing probability judgments into
\par
smaller and more manageable assessment tasks. Rav-
\par
inder, Kleinmuntz, and Dyer (1988) provide a theo-
\par
retical argument for the superiority of decomposed
\par
assessments. Wright, Saunders, and Ayton (1988),
\par
though, found little difference between holistic and
\par
decomposed probability assessments; on the other
\par
hand, Hora, Dodd, and Hora (1993) provide empiri-
\par
cal support for decomposition in probability assess-
\par
ment. With regard to decomposition and the assess-
\par
ment of point estimates, Armstrong, Denniston, and
\par
Gordon (1975) and MacGregor, Lichtenstein, and
\par
Slovic (1988) found that decomposition was valuable
\par
in improving the accuracy of those estimates. Morgan
\par
and Henrion (1990) review the empirical support for
\par
decomposition in probability assessment, and Bunn
\par
and Wright (1991) do the same for forecasting tasks
\par
in general. A tentative conclusion is that, for situa-
\par
tions in which the aggregation must be made on the
\par
basis of subjective judgments, appropriate decompo-
\par
sition of those judgments into reasonable tasks may
\par
lead to better performance. Moreover, decomposi-
\par
tion enables the use of different sets of experts for
\par
different variables, thereby matching expertise to as-
\par
sessment task.
\par
4.3. Behavioral Approaches
\par
Perhaps the best known results from the behav-
\par
ioral group--judgment literature is group polariza-
\par
tion, the tendency of groups to adopt more extreme
\par
positions than would individual members. ``Group-
\par
think'' (Janis, 1982) is an extreme example of this
\par
phenomenon. According to Plous (1993), hundreds
\par
of studies of group polarization have been performed
\par
over the years, with the consistent conclusion that
\par
after discussion, a group will typically advocate a
\par
riskier course of action than they would if acting
\par
individually or without discussion.
\par
The results on group polarization would appear
\par
to suggest caution when using behavioral combina-
\par
tion methods. However, it is important to realize that
\par
the results on group polarization apply primarily to
\par
unstructured group discussions. It has been shown
\par
that group polarization can be deterred by such mea-
\par
sures as delaying commitment of the group, spreading
\par
power among members, seeking additional informa-
\par
tion, and encouraging conflict among members (see
\par
Park, 1990). In the context of group probabilities, the
\par
polarization results indicate that groups might tend
\par
to be overconfident about their conclusions. Sniezek
\par
(1992) reviews work on confidence assessment in
\par
group decision-making and concludes that groups areCombining Probability Distributions 197
\par
more confident than individuals and that they appear
\par
to be overconfident. In an experimental study of
\par
group decision-making behavior, Innami (1994) finds
\par
that the quality of group decisions increases to the
\par
extent that group members exchange facts and rea-
\par
sons (a ``reasoning'' orientation) and decreases to
\par
the extent that group members stick to their positions
\par
(a ``positional'' orientation), and that an intervention
\par
that emphasizes a knowledge-based logical discussion
\par
and consensual resolution of conflicts improves the
\par
quality of group decisions.
\par
Experimental conclusions with respect to group
\par
judgment have been mixed. For example, a few stud-
\par
ies on group-level judgment suggest caution. Myers
\par
and Lamm (1975) report evidence that face-to-face
\par
interaction in groups working on probability judg-
\par
ments may lead to social pressures that are unrelated
\par
to group members' knowledge and abilities. Gustaf-
\par
son et al. (1973), Fischer (1975), Gough (1975), and
\par
Seaver (1978) found in their experiments that interac-
\par
tion of any kind among experts led to increased over-
\par
confidence and hence poorer calibration of group
\par
probability judgments. More recently, Argote, Sea-
\par
bright, and Dyer (1986) found that the representa-
\par
tiveness heuristic is used more by groups than by
\par
individuals, presumably leading to more biases (e.g.,
\par
overconfidence) related to this heuristic. In a related
\par
study, Tindale, Sheffey, and Filkins (1990) concluded
\par
that groups committed the conjunction fallacy more
\par
often than individuals. All of these results are gener-
\par
ally consistent with the notion of group polarization.
\par
A number of studies have examined the accuracy
\par
of group judgments. In a review, Hastie (1986) con-
\par
siders quantity estimation (comparable to point fore-
\par
casting), problem solving, and answering almanac
\par
questions, and Gigone and Hastie (1997) update this
\par
review by considering relevant work that has ap-
\par
peared in the intervening time. In general, groups
\par
tend to perform better than the average individual,
\par
but the best individual in a group often outperforms
\par
the group as a whole. Looking only at quantity esti-
\par
mation (most pertinent for risk-assessment studies),
\par
the conclusion is that groups are only slightly (one-
\par
eighth of a standard deviation) more accurate than
\par
individuals on average. More recently, Sniezek and
\par
Henry (1989, 1990) have produced experimental evi-
\par
dence that the group's advantage in quantitative esti-
\par
mation may be somewhat greater than was reported
\par
by Hastie.
\par
Another review by Hill (1982), examining 50
\par
years of research on decision-making, reported simi-
\par
lar results. She found that group performance is typi-
\par
cally better than the average group member, but not
\par
as good as the best member. Einhorn, Hogarth, and
\par
Klempner (1977) and Uecker (1982) report experi-
\par
ments in which groups performed at the level of the
\par
best individual member. Both cases used students as
\par
subjects, and both allowed unlimited exchange of
\par
information but required that the group reach a con-
\par
sensus.
\par
A related finding is that group accuracy often
\par
depends on the rules used by the group to arrive at
\par
a single judgment. Sniezek (1989) compared five
\par
types of group-aggregation methods. Four were be-
\par
havioral-aggregation methods, and the fifth was a
\par
simple average of individual judgments. All four of
\par
the behavioral methods were more accurate than the
\par
average, but of those four, the best results by far
\par
were obtained by the ``dictator'' rule, in which the
\par
group selects on the basis of discussion a single indi-
\par
vidual whose opinion the group adopts. In an interest-
\par
ing twist, though, the chosen spokesperson always
\par
modified his or her opinion to be closer to the group
\par
average, thereby decreasing group accuracy slightly.
\par
In this case, the subjects again were college students,
\par
and the task was sales forecasting.
\par
One of Sniezek's five group techniques was Del-
\par
phi, and her results were similar to earlier studies on
\par
the accuracy of Delphi (e.g., Dalkey, 1969; Dalkey \&
\par
Brown, 1971). These studies showed that forecasts of
\par
individuals converged and that the Delphi technique
\par
performed slightly better than a similar procedure
\par
with face-to-face open interaction groups. In a study
\par
of bankers, Brockhoff (1975) found the same results
\par
for almanac questions, but face-to-face interaction
\par
provided better forecasts for economic forecasting.
\par
More recent work on Delphi has led to mixed results;
\par
Hastie (1986) and Parente \textasciiacute{} and Anderson-Parente \textasciiacute{}
\par
(1987) review this literature. The conclusion appears
\par
to be that Delphi has no clear advantage over other
\par
behavioral combination methods.
\par
4.4. Mathematical Versus Behavioral Aggregation
\par
Most of the research comparing mathematical
\par
and behavioral aggregation has focused on compari-
\par
sons with a simple average of forecasts or probabili-
\par
ties rather than with more complicated mathematical
\par
combination methods. Results from these compari-
\par
sons have been mixed. For example, for forecasting
\par
college students' grade-point averages, Rohrbaugh
\par
(1979) found that behavioral aggregation worked bet-
\par
ter than taking simple averages of individual group198 Clemen and Winkler
\par
members' forecasts. Hastie (1986), Hill (1982), and
\par
Sniezek (1989) reached similar conclusions, as de-
\par
scribed above. However, Lawrence, Edmundson, and
\par
O'Connor (1986) reported that mathematical combi-
\par
nation improved on the behavioral combination of
\par
forecasts. In Flores and White's (1989) experiment,
\par
mathematical and behavioral combinations per-
\par
formed at approximately the same level. Goodman
\par
(1972) asked college students to assess likelihood ra-
\par
tios in groups and individually; the behavioral combi-
\par
nation showed slight improvement over the mechani-
\par
cal combination.
\par
Seaver (1978) asked student subjects to assess
\par
discrete and continuous probability distributions for
\par
almanac questions. Several different conditions were
\par
used: individual assessment, Delphi, Nominal Group
\par
Technique, free-form discussion, and two other ap-
\par
proaches that structured information sharing and dis-
\par
cussion. Both simple and weighted averages of the
\par
individual probabilities were also calculated. The
\par
conclusion was that interaction among the assessors
\par
did not improve on the performance of the aggre-
\par
gated probabilities, although the subjects did feel
\par
more satisfied with the behavioral aggregation re-
\par
sults.
\par
Reagan-Cirincione (1994) used an intensive
\par
group process intervention involving cognitive feed-
\par
back and a computerized group support system for a
\par
quantity estimation task. The results show this to be
\par
the only study reviewed by Gigone and Hastie (1997)
\par
for which group judgments are more accurate than a
\par
mathematical average of the individual judgments. In
\par
general, Gigone and Hastie (1997) conclude that the
\par
evidence indicates that a simple average of individual
\par
judgments tends to outperform group judgments.
\par
Moreover, they discuss ways in which groups might
\par
improve over mathematical combinations and con-
\par
clude that ``there is a limited collection of judgment
\par
tasks in which groups have a legitimate opportunity to
\par
outperform individual judgments'' (p. 162).
\par
5. CONCLUSION
\par
We have reviewed a variety of methods for com-
\par
bining probability distributions in risk analysis. The
\par
empirical results reviewed in Section 4 suggest that
\par
mathematical aggregation outperforms intuitive ag-
\par
gregation and that mathematical and behavioral ap-
\par
proaches tend to be similar in performance, with
\par
mathematical rules having a slight edge. A compari-
\par
son of behavioral approaches yields no clear-cut con-
\par
clusions. As for mathematical combination methods,
\par
simple combination rules (e.g., a simple average) tend
\par
to perform quite well. More complex rules sometimes
\par
outperform the simple rules, but they can be some-
\par
what sensitive, leading to poor performance in some
\par
instances. All of these conclusions should be qualified
\par
by noting that they represent tendencies over a series
\par
of different empirical studies, generally conducted in
\par
an experimental setting as opposed to occurring in
\par
the context of a real-world risk analysis. These studies
\par
do not, unfortunately, directly assess the precise issue
\par
that needs to be addressed. For the purpose of the
\par
typical risk analysis in which probability distributions
\par
are to be combined, but limited past data are avail-
\par
able, the real question is, ``What is the best way to
\par
combine the judgments?'' Thus, although we should
\par
pay careful attention to available empirical results
\par
and learn from them, we should think hard about
\par
their generalizability to realistic risk-analysis applica-
\par
tions.
\par
Both the mathematical combination of probabil-
\par
ities with some modeling and the use of interaction
\par
among the experts have some intuitive appeal. It is
\par
somewhat disappointing, therefore, to see that mod-
\par
eling and behavioral approaches often provide results
\par
inferior to simple combination rules. We feel a bit
\par
like the investor who would like to believe that some
\par
careful analysis of the stock market and some tips
\par
from the ``pros'' should lead to high returns but finds
\par
that buying a mutual fund which just represents a
\par
stock market index such as the S \& P 500 would
\par
yield better returns. On the other hand, we should
\par
remember that the simple combination rules do per-
\par
form quite well, indicating that the use of multiple
\par
experts and the combination of probabilities from
\par
these experts can be beneficial. One message that
\par
comes from the work on the combination of probabil-
\par
ities is that, at a minimum, it is worthwhile to consult
\par
multiple experts and combine their probabilities.
\par
Another message is that further work is needed
\par
on the development and evaluation of combination
\par
methods. The challenge is to find modeling proce-
\par
dures or behavioral approaches (or processes involv-
\par
ing both modeling aspects and behavioral aspects)
\par
that perform well enough to justify the extra cost
\par
and effort that is associated with serious modeling
\par
or expert interaction. On the behavioral side, Davis
\par
(1992, p. 34) states: ``The engineering of increases in
\par
decision performance while maintaining [the advan-
\par
tages of group decision making] is a proper challenge
\par
for fundamental theory and research in applied psy-
\par
chology.'' Gigone and Hastie (1997, p. 166) echo thisCombining Probability Distributions 199
\par
in their concluding comments: ``The quality of group
\par
decisions and judgments is an essential ingredient in
\par
democratic institutions and societies, but research
\par
on group judgment accuracy is stagnant. . . . Better
\par
methods and analyses will help behavioral scientists,
\par
engineers, and policymakers to design and select
\par
group decision-making procedures that will increase
\par
efficiency, justice, and social welfare.''
\par
Regarding mathematical combining procedures,
\par
we believe that simple rules will always play an im-
\par
portant role, because of their ease of use, robust
\par
performance, and defensibility in public policy set-
\par
tings where judgments about the quality of different
\par
experts are eschewed. Simple rules do not, however,
\par
allow explicit consideration of factors such as over-
\par
confidence and dependence among experts. A model-
\par
ing challenge is to develop mathematical approaches
\par
that facilitate modeling and assessment, are robust
\par
(e.g., avoid extreme situations such as highly negative
\par
weights), and lead to improved performance. Chhib-
\par
ber, Apostolakis, and Okrent (1994, p. 102) write as
\par
follows: ``The Bayesian aggregation tool is demon-
\par
strably powerful, but it is not well understood . . .
\par
Further studies to understand its behavior . . . need
\par
to be undertaken to realize its full potential.'' In
\par
principle, the Bayesian approach allows for careful
\par
control in adjusting for the quality of expert distribu-
\par
tions, including overconfidence and dependence. It is
\par
also worth noting that the normal and copula models
\par
permit the symmetric treatment of the experts, in
\par
which case simple combining rules fall out of these
\par
models as special cases.
\par
More generally, the process of combining proba-
\par
bility distributions in risk analysis may well involve
\par
both mathematical and behavioral aspects and should
\par
be considered in the context of the overall process
\par
for obtaining and utilizing expert judgment in a given
\par
application (for discussions of this process, see the
\par
references in Section 1). Important issues to consider
\par
include the following.
\par
\ding{108} Selection of experts. Having dealt with the
\par
combination of probabilities from experts in
\par
this paper, we see natural implications for the
\par
selection of experts whose probabilities are to
\par
be combined. Experts who are very similar
\par
(in modeling style, philosophy, access to data,
\par
etc.) tend to provide redundant information,
\par
and the high level of dependence means not
\par
only minimal gains from aggregation but also
\par
difficulties with some modeling approaches
\par
due to multicollinearity and the extreme (very
\par
large positive or negative) weights that can
\par
result. Thus, heterogeneity among experts is
\par
highly desirable. In terms of the number of
\par
experts, Makridakis and Winkler (1983) and
\par
Clemen and Winkler (1985) demonstrate the
\par
diminishing marginal returns associated with
\par
large numbers of experts. Their analyses, fur-
\par
ther supported by Ferrell (1985), suggest using
\par
three to five experts.
\par
\ding{108} Flexibility and process design. We believe that
\par
there is no single, all-purpose combining rule
\par
or combining process that should be used in all
\par
situations. Rather, the design of the combining
\par
process (as part of the overall expert judgment
\par
process) should depend on the details of each
\par
individual situation. This process design, con-
\par
ducted by the decision-maker (or decision-
\par
making body) in conjunction with the risk as-
\par
sessment team, should take into account fac-
\par
tors such as the nature and importance of the
\par
uncertainties, the availability of appropriate
\par
experts, past evidence available about the ex-
\par
perts and about the quantities of interest, the
\par
degree of uncertainty about quantities of inter-
\par
est, the degree of disagreement among the ex-
\par
perts, the costs of bringing experts together,
\par
and a variety of other factors. We believe that
\par
a carefully structured and documented process
\par
is appropriate.
\par
\ding{108} The role of modeling versus rules. Risk analysis
\par
applications involving combination have often
\par
used simple combination rules, usually a sim-
\par
ple average. Such simple rules are valuable
\par
benchmarks, but careful consideration should
\par
be given to modeling in order to include, in a
\par
formal fashion, factors such as the quality of
\par
the judgments from individual experts and the
\par
dependence among experts. One possible sce-
\par
nario is that the experts are judged to be ex-
\par
changeable and their probabilities should be
\par
treated symmetrically, but this should be a
\par
conscious choice on the part of the risk assess-
\par
ment team. The degree of modeling will vary
\par
from case to case, ranging from fairly simple
\par
modeling (e.g., unequal weights based on judg-
\par
ments of relative precision) to more detailed
\par
modeling (e.g., building a full copula model).
\par
When little information is available about the
\par
relative quality of and dependence among the
\par
experts' probabilities, a simple rule such as a
\par
simple average is recommended.
\par
\ding{108} The role of interaction. This aspect of the com-200 Clemen and Winkler
\par
bination process will also vary from case to
\par
case, depending on such factors as the per-
\par
ceived desirability of exchanging information
\par
and the ease with which such information can
\par
be exchanged. Evidence to date does not pro-
\par
vide strong support for benefits of interaction
\par
in the actual aggregation process, yet it has
\par
considerable intuitive appeal and has been
\par
used in risk analysis applications (e.g., EPRI,
\par
1986; Hora \& Iman, 1989; Winkler et al., 1995).
\par
We feel that the jury is still out on the impact
\par
of interaction on the quality of the resulting
\par
combined probabilities and that any benefits
\par
are most likely to come from exchanges of
\par
information (possibly including individual ex-
\par
perts' probability distributions) as opposed to
\par
forced consensus through group probability
\par
assessments. This implies that mathematical
\par
combination will still be needed after interac-
\par
tion and individual probability assessment or
\par
reassessment. Also, it is important that the
\par
interaction process by carefully structured
\par
with extensive facilitation (preferably by the
\par
risk assessment team). We emphasize that our
\par
conclusions here relate specifically to the ag-
\par
gregation process; we believe that interaction
\par
is valuable in ironing out differences in defini-
\par
tions and assumptions, clarifying what is to be
\par
forecast or assessed, and exchanging infor-
\par
mation.
\par
\ding{108} The role of sensitivity analysis. It is helpful to
\par
conduct a sensitivity analysis to investigate the
\par
variation in the combined probabilities as pa-
\par
rameters of combining models are varied. This
\par
can help in decisions regarding the scope of
\par
the modeling effort. A related note is that re-
\par
porting the individual experts' probabilities as
\par
well as any combined probabilities provides
\par
useful information about the range of opinions
\par
in a given case as well as about the likely sensi-
\par
tivity of combined probabilities to different
\par
combining procedures.
\par
\ding{108} Role of the risk-assessment team. As should
\par
be clear from the above discussion, the risk-
\par
assessment team plays a very important role
\par
in the combination of probability distributions
\par
as well as in all other aspects of the expert
\par
judgment process. With respect to mathemati-
\par
cal combination, the team should perform any
\par
modeling, make any assessments of expert
\par
quality that are needed in the modeling pro-
\par
cess, and choose the combination rule(s) to
\par
be used. On the behavioral side, they should
\par
structure any interaction and serve as facilita-
\par
tors. In general, they are responsible for the
\par
design, elicitation, and analysis aspects of the
\par
combination process.
\par
In summary, the combination of experts' proba-
\par
bility distributions in risk analysis is valuable for en-
\par
capsulating the accumulated information for risk ana-
\par
lysts and decision-makers and providing the current
\par
state of expert opinion regarding important uncer-
\par
tainties. Normatively and empirically, combining can
\par
lead to improvements in the quality of probabilities.
\par
More research is needed on the potential benefits of
\par
different modeling approaches and the development
\par
of mathematical combination rules. Likewise, contin-
\par
ued research can lead to a better understanding of the
\par
cognitive and social psychology of group judgments,
\par
with the goal of further developing useful behavioral
\par
aggregation procedures. The ability to use wisely in
\par
practice both mathematical and behavioral aggrega-
\par
tion methods, whether separately or in tandem, can
\par
contribute greatly to the practice of risk analysis.
