\par
centrations at least cost differ systematically, with policies based on benefit-cost
\par
analysis generally requiring larger near-term reductions in GHG emissions.{$\ast$}
\par
The systematic effect of the evaluation endpoint on the assessed merits of alter-
\par
native policies is examined here using a simple analytic model and the magnitude
\par
of the effect is evaluated using a numerical integrated-assessment model. The intu-
\par
ition is straightforward: the present value of abatement costs is likely to be reduced
\par
by deferring emission reductions to the future, due to discounting future costs to
\par
reflect the opportunity cost of capital, anticipated improvements in abatement tech-
\par
nologies, avoidance of premature depreciation of existing physical and human capi-
\par
tal, and the existence of oceanic and other sinks that remove greenhouse gases from
\par
the atmosphere (Wigley et al., 1996). Under a stabilization objective, emissions
\par
are harmful only to the extent that they interfere with achieving the stabilization
\par
goal. Within limits, higher near-term emissions can be offset by greater subsequent
\par
emission reductions. In contrast, both near-term and future damages from climate
\par
change are accounted for under a benefit-cost objective. Near-term damages from
\par
climate change cannot be prevented by future emission reductions (although the
\par
monetary value of near-term damages may be compensated by decreasing future
\par
damages). In short, near-term and future abatement costs are counted under both
\par
objectives, but near-term consequences of emissions are counted only under the
\par
benefit-cost objective.
\par
Alternative evaluation endpoints and criteria for choosing among them are dis-
\par
cussed in Section 2. The result that optimal near-term emission reductions are
\par
larger under a benefit-cost than a least-cost stabilization objective is established
\par
using an analytic model in Section 3. The magnitude of the difference in near-term
\par
emission paths for a variety of stabilization levels and climate-damage functions
\par
is evaluated using an integrated assessment model in Section 4. Conclusions are
\par
presented in Section 5.
\par
\textbf{2. Endpoints for Policy Evaluation}
\par
The Framework Convention on Climate Change calls for `stabilization of green-
\par
house-gas concentrations . . . at a level that would prevent dangerous anthropogenic
\par
interference with the climate system'. Although an appropriate level has not been
\par
identified, emission paths that stabilize carbon dioxide concentrations at several
\par
levels have been constructed (Schimel et al., 1995; Wigley et al., 1996).\dag{} An
\par
economic-welfare perspective suggests an alternative goal, maximizing the differ-
\par
ence between the benefits of reducing climate change and the costs of abatement.
\par
This approach has been widely used in integrated-assessment modeling (Cline,
