for their sector or region. Wynne [84] documents a case
\par
in the United Kingdom, where local stakeholders (farmers)
\par
felt their social identity was threatened by scientists' de-
\par
nial of local knowledge. The IPCC has also recognized
\par
(a) the need for procedural fairness, so that all parties have
\par
equal opportunities to participate, and (b) the link between
\par
domestic and international processes, since addressing cli-
\par
mate change may require responses at different levels of
\par
society and governance [7].
\par
\emph{2.3. Regional scale integration}
\par
Scale is an important component of the integration chal-
\par
lenge. IAMs tend to be global in scale, and by neces-
\par
sity, a considerable degree of aggregation is required. As
\par
a result, some regional scale environmental and societal
\par
processes can be missed (e.g., direct and indirect link-
\par
ages between climate and (a) forest fires, (b) landslides in
\par
permafrost regions, (c) non-market values, (d) subsistence
\par
lifestyles, (e) transboundary flows of goods, etc.). The re-
\par
gional scale s--s can therefore be an important complement
\par
to IAMs by providing the modeller with a better foundation
\par
for any aggregation assumptions that need to be made in
\par
the global scale modeling context, especially assumptions
\par
related to impacts and adaptation.
\par
There is a need, therefore, to bring regional stakehold-
\par
ers into the assessment process at its earliest stage. Pol-
\par
icy analyses cannot be framed in terms of preparing tools
\par
which a benevolent dictator (the boss) would use, since
\par
this would encourage the omission of heterogeneous as-
\par
pects [40]. Global and national scale actions are dependent
\par
on achieving consensus of many diverse regional scale ac-
\par
tors. The entry of new ideas into the policy agenda is
\par
facilitated by processes that allow for discussion of a wide
\par
variety of perspectives, and this is best met through partic-
\par
ipation of all interested parties in research priority setting,
\par
conduct, communication and application. The integration
\par
approach should acknowledge the pluralistic nature of po-
\par
litical systems that use science and the status of scientific
\par
information as one among several inputs into the policy
\par
process [71].
\par
\emph{2.4. Linkage with sustainable development}
\par
Although there is broad consensus on a number of issues
\par
related to climate change, there will continue to be uncer-
\par
tainties in various components. IA must cope with such
\par
uncertainties. Since we do not know the real range of pos-284 \emph{S.J. Cohen / Scientist--stakeholder collaboration in integrated assessment of climate change}
\par
sible outcomes or \emph{changes in risk}, impact assessment often
\par
depends on scenarios as the platform for providing infor-
\par
mation that would be ``useable'' by decision makers. These
\par
scenarios cover periods of 50 years or more, and this poses
\par
significant problems for analyses based on economic effi-
\par
ciency (e.g., use of cost--benefit models, discounting, etc.).
\par
Howarth and Monahan [35] argue that intergenerational eq-
\par
uity, a criterion of sustainable development, is a platform
\par
that would allow stakeholders' views on social objectives to
\par
be brought into the analysis as a complement of economic
\par
models.
\par
The concept of sustainable development requires a
\par
recognition of the interconnected nature of environment and
\par
development questions. The present--future, ``North--South''
\par
and rich--poor dimensions are difficult for researchers to
\par
come to grips with. These are policy issues articulated
\par
by stakeholders, who are looking for information to help
\par
them make decisions in a rapidly changing world. In con-
\par
trast, climate change began as a science issue and is now
\par
expanding as a policy issue as its human dimensions be-
\par
come recognized. Climate change, however, is still per-
\par
ceived primarily as an environmental problem, a risk with
\par
thresholds and probabilities that can be precisely defined.
\par
Robinson [64] points out that (1) risk analysis represents
\par
only the technical dimension of a much more complex set
\par
of decision-making problems, and (2) decision makers are
\par
more likely to choose a scenario or forecast that best bal-
\par
ances various policy purposes. Robinson concludes that
\par
decision making will be improved by the mutual learning
\par
among scientists and stakeholders that would occur when
\par
science is done in a policy context.
\par
zie Basin Impact Study, described below, made a similar
\par
attempt.
\par
By necessity, the regional s--s collaborative would in-
\par
volve the recruitment of a wide range of interested parties,
\par
some of whom would want to bring other agendas to the
\par
study. Most researchers and stakeholders would be part-
\par
time participants, and their locations would probably be
\par
widely scattered. Those who are not directly funded by the
\par
project would participate on a voluntary basis. This group
\par
might be difficult to manage and co-ordinate, unless finan-
\par
cial resources could be made available to engage these indi-
\par
viduals directly as consultants or ``members of the board''.
\par
It may also be difficult to attract regional stakeholders if the
\par
IA is initiated and managed from outside the study area.
\par
There are several considerations of a technical nature
\par
that also need to be considered. First, time and resource
\par
constraints may create a dependence on existing data sets
\par
generated outside the IA. These may be difficult to adapt
\par
to the analytical tools being considered for the IA. Second,
\par
the wide range of available Geographic Information Sys-
\par
tems (GIS) has led to problems in data transfer between
\par
systems. A common GIS platform might be appropriate
\par
for a collaborative, but this would need to be decided upon
\par
at the earliest possible time. This could affect selection of
\par
integration exercises for the IA.
\par
Increased stakeholder involvement in IA provides sub-
\par
stantial advantages, but it is a difficult process. Rothman
\par
and Robinson [68] propose a conceptual framework for IA
\par
that recognizes multiple base cases and scenarios, expand-
\par
ing the scope of issues which an IA of climate change
\par
should consider, and accounting for the qualitative dimen-
\par
sions of the interaction of natural and human systems.
\par
\emph{2.5. Advantages and constraints of the regional s--s}
\par
\emph{collaborative}
\par
The regional s--s collaborative represents an opportunity
\par
for stakeholders to acquire some ownership of the IA. It
\par
encourages the development of interdisciplinary approaches
\par
to research, and provides common ground for linking sci-
\par
entific expertise (``What if'') with stakeholders' knowledge
\par
(``So what'' and ``What should be done''). There is also
\par
the potential for the creation of new data bases and s--s
\par
networks that would have value long after the completion
\par
of the IA. Most important for analytical purposes, this ap-
\par
proach is more likely to be able to describe the \emph{indirect}
\par
implications of climate change, which may turn out to be
\par
more costly than direct impacts. This could provide a valu-
\par
able source of information for global scale IAM efforts.
\par
Within the various types of IA efforts listed in table 1,
\par
the regional s--s collaborative could draw most easily from
\par
the following: analogue scenarios, education, expert judg-
\par
ment, decision support models, testing of instruments, and
\par
themes and places. In practice, however, few of these ef-
\par
forts have been conducted with stakeholders as collabora-
\par
tors throughout the research process. The New Zealand
\par
case [50] provided an important precedent with its effort to
\par
engage the Maori in a climate change study. The Macken-
\par
\textbf{3. An example of a scientist--stakeholder collaborative}
\par
\textbf{on climate change: Mackenzie Basin Impact Study}
\par
\textbf{(MBIS)}
\par
The Mackenzie Basin Impact Study (MBIS) was a cli-
\par
mate change impact assessment focusing on Northwest
\par
Canada [16--21]. It was designed to be a scientist--stake-
\par
holder collaborative, with 30 research activities on various
\par
topics ranging from permafrost and water levels to forest
\par
economics and community response to floods. Details on
\par
results are provided elsewhere [20,21]. The primary focus
\par
here is on the MBIS integration framework (figure 1). In
\par
this disaggregated approach, scenarios were used as inputs
\par
to first-order and second-order impact assessments, includ-
\par
ing permafrost and ecosystem studies. The socio-economic
\par
component used results from these assessments, as well as
\par
the climate scenario information where appropriate.
\par
Included in this framework were several integration
\par
modeling exercises:
\par
(a) resource accounting, multiregional input--output mod-
\par
eling and community surveys of the non-wage econ-
\par
omy of an aboriginal community [44];\emph{S.J. Cohen / Scientist--stakeholder collaboration in integrated assessment of climate change }285
\par
Figure 1. Research framework for MBIS.
\par
(b) multiobjective model focusing on scenarios of chang-
\par
ing land utilization [36]; and
\par
(c) land assessment framework (ILAF) with goal program-
\par
ming and Analytic Hierarchy Process [88].
\par
As part of the collaborative, integration was also attempted
\par
through information exchange while study components
\par
were in progress. This was particularly important for the
\par
socio-economic studies (table 2). Both sectoral and inte-
\par
grated modeling exercises used information from various
\par
MBIS activities, as well as other studies and data for their
\par
computer-based mathematical models.
\par
The objective was to provide an impact assessment for
\par
the region, rather than on individual sectors. Regional
\par
questions may be closer to stakeholders' frame of refer-
\par
ence, since choices and decisions often involve tradeoffs
\par
with other interests. Consider the following cases being
\par
addressed within the MBIS [21]:
\par
\emph{3.1. Changing water levels}
\par
The Mackenzie Basin is a very large high latitude water-
\par
shed, 1.8 million km2 in area (figure 2). It includes several
\par
large lakes and rivers, freshwater and coastal deltas, and
\par
extensive wetlands and peatlands with many small, shal-
\par
low lakes. One sub-area of considerable interest is the
\par
Peace--Athabasca delta, which includes habitat for fish and
\par
migratory waterfowl.
\par
Within global warming scenarios derived from climate
\par
model outputs, it appears that annual runoff in much of
\par
the basin may decrease. Seasonal fluctuations would re-
\par
main, including spring peak levels at or near observed,
\par
but fall/winter minimum river and lake levels would be
\par
lower [41]. Ice cover on the Peace River would be re-
\par
duced [4].
\par
Although water use in this region is modest, in-stream
\par
flow requirements for ecological purposes are very impor-
\par
tant for fish, birds and other wildlife. The Peace--Athabasca
\par
delta has recently experienced low water levels and an
\par
extensive period of reduced flood frequencies important
\par
for the survival of the wetlands, and concerns have been
\par
expressed regarding the viability of habitat for fish and
\par
wildlife. This concern resulted in several major research
\par
initiatives, including the Peace--Athabasca Delta Technical
\par
Studies (PAD). PAD includes experiments at creating arti-
\par
ficial ice dams in order to induce flooding for ecological
\par
purposes [59].
\par
PAD and other efforts were initiated because of the as-
\par
sumption that the delta's water level problems were caused
\par
by logging and the operations of a hydroelectric facility lo-
\par
cated upstream. The region's climate was not considered to
\par
be a direct factor. This view, however, may be changing,
\par
as the current warming trend in the region becomes more
\par
noticeable [73].
\par
Would stakeholder response to changing water levels be
\par
different if they believed that the cause was natural climatic
\par
variability rather than a hydroelectric facility? What if the
\par
cause was ``global warming''? What if it was a combination
\par
of all three? If this scenario was to lead to any changes in
\par
water management, what would the financial implications
\par
be?
\par
MBIS identified these questions, but no definitive answer
\par
was provided. Tools are available to analyze the economic
\par
aspects of water resources, but these have not necessar-
\par
ily been developed for all watersheds, including this one.
\par
There is new Canadian legislation being considered to fa-
\par
cilitate interjurisdictional cooperation in water management
\par
in this basin, and this was discussed during the MBIS Fi-
\par
nal Workshop [48]. This would be a policy discussion that
\par
could benefit from the development of a basin management
\par
model, but that is not typically a part of IAMs.
\par
Alcamo et al. [2] have noted that IAMs need to address
\par
water issues, as well as other multiobjective concerns (for-
\par
est sustainability, environmental security, etc.). Perhaps a
\par
focus on basin specific policy instruments, such as water
\par
management agreements, would be a way to characterize
\par
this issue for large basins that could be represented in global
\par
scale models.
\par
\emph{3.2. Changing land capabilities}
\par
Agriculture in the MBIS region is confined to the south-
\par
ern third due to the relatively short growing season. There
\par
are potential sites farther north, but these are limited by
\par
cold temperatures. A warming scenario could enable small
\par
grains to be cultivated on an additional 10 million hectares
\par
of land, but this would require expanded irrigation services
\par
in order to be economically viable [10].
\par
This land, however, is currently being used for other pur-
\par
poses, primarily for forestry and wildlife habitat. The latter
\par
is particularly important to native communities, which de-
\par
pend on them for food, and for manufacturing clothing and
\par
other articles made of skins and furs. Much of this activity
\par
is part of the traditional lifestyles of the region's aborigi-
\par
nal peoples, and is recognized as a ``non-wage'' economy.
\par
There is aboriginal participation in the ``wage'' economy,286 \emph{S.J. Cohen / Scientist--stakeholder collaboration in integrated assessment of climate change}
