\par
be a change in land capability does not necessarily mean
\par
that land conversion would immediately follow. Such deci-
\par
sions would depend on stakeholders' views being resolved
\par
through the process of policy making in the region, and this
\par
needs to be recognized in the IA, whether or not an IAM
\par
is used.
\par
As with the previous case, this is a multiobjective issue
\par
with multiple stakeholders. This is why it is important to
\par
broaden IA to account for different biases and perspectives.
\par
Van Asselt and Rotmans [6] suggest a modeling approach
\par
in which different kinds of actors (individualist, hierarchist,
\par
egalitarian) are represented within the IAM process, but this
\par
should be seen as complementary to other activities which
\par
enable scientists and stakeholders to share information.
\par
\emph{3.3. Changing fire weather}
\par
Fire is a normal component of the boreal ecosystem,
\par
and within Canada's Northwest Territories, around 1 million
\par
hectares burn in an average year. In 1994, there were 630
\par
fires, covering approximately 3 million hectares. That year
\par
was around 1--1.5\textopenbullet C above average, with below average
\par
rainfall during the growing season. By contrast, a 4--5\textopenbullet C
\par
increase is described within global warming scenarios for
\par
the MBIS region derived from climate model simulations.
\par
Territorial fire fighting resources were severely tested in
\par
1994, and almost 25\% of the fires were not fought. The
\par
costs for some fires reached CAN\$1.2 million each, com-
\par
pared with a historic maximum of CAN\$0.5 million. 1995
\par
was also a bad year. Several communities had to be evacu-
\par
ated, and there were problems in Northern Alberta as well.
\par
For the region as a whole, fire suppression and control ex-
\par
penditures have increased almost 50\% in constant dollars
\par
between the 1971--1989 and 1990--1994 periods. This is
\par
happening because of increased fire incidence as well as
\par
the growing importance of the forest industry [67].
\par
In a climate change scenario, years like 1994 and 1995
\par
could become more common. The ``average'' fire season\emph{S.J. Cohen / Scientist--stakeholder collaboration in integrated assessment of climate change }287
\par
would include a higher Fire Weather Index, resulting in in-
\par
creased potential area burned. What effect would this have
\par
on fire management? This question cannot be considered in
\par
isolation from land management objectives, since these ob-
\par
jectives would be used to set priorities for selection of fires
\par
to fight. Community protection would always be a high
\par
priority, but what about wildlife migration routes? What
\par
if commercial agriculture, tourism and forestry expand into
\par
the region? The side effects of increased fire frequency
\par
may offset increased tree productivity in a warmer climate.
\par
Average tree age would decline in this scenario, resulting
\par
in decreased yields [33].
\par
Just because there could be increased fire potential does
\par
not necessarily mean that more forested land will burn, or
\par
be allowed to burn. As with the case of possible land con-
\par
version, any changes in fire management policy, including
\par
associated financial implications, would depend on changes
\par
in both fire potential, land use and stakeholders' views.
\par
sessments can identify climate-related concerns that may
\par
be hidden at continental or global scales [75,85]. If the
\par
UNFCCC is going to achieve its objective of preventing
\par
``dangerous anthropogenic interference'' of the atmosphere,
\par
a greater effort should be made to incorporate the regional
\par
impacts/adaptation dimension into assessments of climate
\par
change. This will require explicit recognition that for each
\par
unit of land, there are multiple stakeholders with multiple
\par
interests.
\par
\textbf{4. Results of MBIS integration exercises}
\par
The MBIS Final Report [20] provides detailed results of
\par
both sectoral and integration exercises. For this discussion,
\par
results of the integration exercises are summarized, and the
\par
overall disaggregated approach is assessed.
\par
\emph{4.1. Resource accounting with input--output modeling and}
\par
\emph{community survey}
\par
\emph{3.4. Effects of renewable resource impacts on}
\par
\emph{non-renewable resource development}
\par
One interesting aspect that is beginning to emerge is
\par
how decisions regarding responses in the energy industry
\par
may be affected by impacts on renewable resources. In
\par
this scenario, climate change is seen as having a negligi-
\par
ble direct effect on exploration and production operations,
\par
despite sea level rise, sea ice changes, permafrost thaw
\par
and associated landslides [3]. However, if climate change
\par
affects the boreal and tundra ecosystems (including forest
\par
growth, wildlife habitats, fire frequency, etc.), would re-
\par
gional stakeholders alter their views on future energy de-
\par
velopments? This relates to the juxtaposition of the formal
\par
wage and informal non-wage economies in this region. The
\par
latter may be significantly affected by climate change, and
\par
it is not clear at the moment whether this would encourage
\par
or discourage expansion of the wage economy. Aboriginal
\par
people want both [8,60], so the challenge is to design a
\par
response strategy that meets this goal.
\par
If the non-wage economy is at risk, could this lead to
\par
greater restrictions on industrial growth, or would there ac-
\par
tually be an acceleration of industrial development, but at
\par
the expense of a decline in the non-wage economy, and
\par
traditional aboriginal lifestyles? What would happen to
\par
forestry, park management, and other renewable resource
\par
based activities?
\par
Each of these cases includes potential economic and so-
\par
cial costs and benefits which may not be captured by sec-
\par
toral assessments, or by available continental/global scale
\par
IAMs. There are financial and policy implications as well,
\par
which may affect regional and national climate change
\par
strategies (e.g., scenario of land conversion from forest to
\par
cropland).
\par
Climate impacts mechanisms and valuation of such
\par
impacts are poorly understood. Some sectoral and re-
\par
gional sensitivities may be related to factors other than
\par
climate [46]. On the other hand, integrated regional as-
\par
Lonergan et al. [44] developed an economic model and
\par
combined it with a community survey to address potential
\par
implications on the two economies of the region, wage-
\par
based and non-wage (traditional harvesting) activities [43].
\par
This was a multiregional input--output model, which pro-
\par
duced estimates of employment impacts. The model was
\par
applied to a scenario of expansion of oil production in the
\par
Beaufort Sea, based on Anderson et al. [3]. Results from a
\par
case study of the Pedzeh Ki First Nation (Wrigley, NWT)
\par
suggest increased short term wage employment in the com-
\par
munity business and personal services sector, but concerns
\par
were raised regarding potential community disruption if
\par
these employment opportunities would be located outside
\par
the community. Given the changing political situation due
\par
to land claims and other events, implications for aboriginal
\par
lifestyles could not be determined. In addition, this exer-
\par
cise did not include potential impacts on forests, wildlife
\par
and permafrost (see section 4.5).
\par
\emph{4.2. Multi-objective model}
\par
This model optimizes economic, forest cover and other
\par
attributes, subject to a number of constraints, including
\par
wildlife habitat preservation and soil erosion limits [36,37].
\par
Four scenarios were considered: low and high growth in
\par
agriculture and commercial forestry, and low and high re-
\par
placement of forest land by agriculture. The latter two sce-
\par
narios were included in response to the forest yield decline
\par
projected by Hartley and Marshall [33]. In all cases, agri-
\par
culture expansion could occur within constraints imposed
\par
by other resource users, but in the latter two scenarios, the
\par
decline in forestry remains. This model could be tested
\par
with other scenarios.
\par
\emph{4.3. Land Assessment Framework}
\par
The Land Assessment Framework [87,88] was devel-
\par
oped to assess the implications of climate change for288 \emph{S.J. Cohen / Scientist--stakeholder collaboration in integrated assessment of climate change}
\par
achieving regional development goals. The framework in-
\par
cluded the use of remote sensing imagery, GIS, and a goal
\par
programming model. The model minimizes deviation from
\par
defined target levels for agriculture, forestry and other re-
\par
source uses. These targets were based on rankings of land
\par
use goals provided by stakeholders in a survey consisting
\par
of paired comparisons of goals. This survey provided data
\par
for the Analytic Hierarchy Process exercise [88]. Using
\par
impacts results from one of the climate warming scenar-
\par
ios obtained from other MBIS projects, one climate change
\par
scenario was assessed by this model. Results showed that
\par
spruce production goals would not be reached, and soil ero-
\par
sion loss would exceed the target by around 15\% because
\par
of expansion of agriculture.
\par
\emph{4.4. Other cross disciplinary exercises}
\par
Integration was attempted through working as a research
\par
team, with information exchange between participants be-
\par
ing facilitated by the project leader through workshops
\par
and personal contact. All participants used climate warm-
\par
ing scenarios developed for MBIS, though in some cases,
\par
modifications were done (e.g., a different interpolation of
\par
climate data). Table 2 shows some examples of cross-
\par
disciplinary collaboration, including application of (a) the
\par
basin runoff scenario in the levels and flows study [41],
\par
and the tourism study [11]; (b) the forest fire scenario in
\par
the forest yield model [33], forest industry study [67], and
\par
the study on furbearing animals [42]; (c) permafrost in-
\par
formation in the settlement development study to assess
\par
risk [9]; and (d) sea ice and coastal zone scenarios (con-
\par
tributed to MBIS [16,17]) and permafrost scenarios in the
\par
energy study [3].
\par
Integrated modeling exercises were also complemented
\par
by workshops which provided opportunities for scientists
\par
and stakeholders to express their views on how climate
\par
change might affect the region [16,17,19], and to react to
\par
research results [20]. The latter occurred during the MBIS
\par
Final Workshop, which included stakeholders participating
\par
in round table discussions on the ``So what'' and ``What
\par
should be done'' questions.
\par
Most stakeholders at the workshop felt that the im-
\par
pacts scenario represents a different view of the future, and
\par
raises new questions for resource management, planning,
\par
and governance. Their comments were detailed and ani-
\par
mated. What they were reacting to, however, was the in-
\par
formation provided by the physical, biological and socio-
\par
economic studies, and the debate generated by the presence
\par
of a wide range of stakeholder interests. The integrated
\par
modeling exercises did not attract much attention. There
\par
are three possible explanations:
\par
(a) the methods were not well understood by stakeholders
\par
and other MBIS researchers;
\par
(b) some models used only limited amounts of information
\par
from the other MBIS projects; and/or
\par
(c) results from these exercises were similar to what stake-
\par
holders could already get from a qualitative assessment
\par
of the sectoral studies.
\par
This does not mean that the integrated models were not
\par
useful learning exercises. It suggests, however, that their
\par
role in collaborative exercises such as this one is dependent
\par
on communication between modellers and stakeholders, so
\par
that stakeholders feel confident that their values, prefer-
\par
ences and perceptions are included in the analysis [69].
\par
\emph{4.5. Assessment of disaggregated approach}
\par
All of the above exercises were able to utilize some out-
\par
puts from other MBIS and contributed activities. This in-
\par
dicates that integration was at least partially achieved. The
\par
final workshop with stakeholder participation provided an
\par
important forum for exchanging information across disci-
\par
plines and cultures, a shared learning experience about the
\par
\emph{region's sensitivity to climate change}.
\par
There were, however, a number of missed opportunities:
\par
(a) the hydrologic scenario was not completed until late in
\par
the program, too late for many MBIS projects;
\par
(b) the resource accounting study could not include forest-
\par
ry, agriculture, wildlife, or permafrost considerations
\par
due to incompatibility of data;
\par
(c) results from the forest pests study were not available
\par
until after completion of the forest yield study; and
\par
(d) local (traditional) knowledge could not be included in
\par
any of the \emph{quantitative }models.
\par
Most of these reflect difficulties in coordinating the timing
\par
of outputs, and anticipating problems in data transfer, such
\par
as mismatch of scales, differences in data base formats and
\par
measurement units (especially when providing data for eco-
\par
nomic models), and differences in Geographic Information
\par
Systems (GIS).
\par
This set of modeling and non-modeling exercises was
\par
pursued because there was no consensus within the col-
\par
laborative regarding the ``best'' approach to do IA. There
\par
continued to be concerns that current IAMs (as well as
\par
economic models, such as input--output models) were un-
\par
derestimating impact costs, and that the IA had to provide a
\par
more holistic assessment than that which could be produced
\par
by any single IAM. Even if some of the results would be
\par
presented as qualitative narratives, rather than in economic
\par
terms, such information would be valuable to stakeholders,
\par
and ultimately, to the debate about responses to climate
\par
change.
\par
\textbf{5. Three questions for integrated assessment of climate}
\par
\textbf{change}
\par
Impacts research is a complex challenge because it in-
\par
cludes physical, biological and socio-economic aspects.\emph{S.J. Cohen / Scientist--stakeholder collaboration in integrated assessment of climate change }289
\par
Most assessments in the regional/country context have in-
\par
cluded several key economic sectors in a parallel sectoral
\par
assessment format. They have not explicitly looked at
\par
connections between sectors. In principal, IA could do
\par
this, and there are some other case studies which have at-
\par
tempted to use models and other approaches for this pur-
\par
pose [49,65,85]. Rather than relying on a single ``end to
\par
end'' model, these case studies have been disaggregated in
\par
approach [54] and regional in scale, serving to highlight
\par
questions that may be missed by global scale, mitigation
\par
focused, IAMs. The case study of Northwest Canada de-
\par
scribed above is similar.
\par
\emph{5.1. What is the problem with integrated assessment of}
\par
\emph{climate change?}
\par
The main problem with IA of climate change is that it
\par
is difficult to determine economic and social costs of im-
\par
pacts, and the benefits of adaptation measures. This results
\par
in stakeholders focusing on mitigation alone, without recog-
\par
nizing the costs of inaction. Environmentalists and advo-
\par
cates of sustainable development and proactive mitigation
\par
responses have tended to ``leap'' from the initial indicator
\par
of stress (i.e., global temperature increase) to the policy de-
\par
bate over eco-taxes and AIJ. Opponents have done a similar
\par
``leap'' but for opposite reasons. This enables them to focus
\par
their critical attacks on uncertainties associated with tem-
\par
perature projections, rather than facing broader and more
\par
numerous interests associated with potential impacts of \emph{sce-}
\par
\emph{narios }of climate change and other changes (political, eco-
\par
nomic, technological), including lifestyle and policy shifts
\par
required to reduce vulnerabilities and achieve sustainabil-
\par
ity [23].
\par
The ``What should be done'' question of policy response
\par
needs more context than just projections of temperature
\par
changes from climate models. This is why it is impor-
\par
tant to broaden the dimensions of the ``What if'' and ``So
\par
what'' questions. In terms of value of information, we
\par
stand to gain a much larger ``bang for the buck'' by invest-
\par
ing more in the climate impacts component of IA. Nord-
\par
haus and Popp [52] suggest that \emph{better information on cli-}
\par
\emph{mate change damage is actually of higher economic value}
\par
\emph{than similar improvements in information on mitigation, the}
\par
\emph{temperature--CO2 relationship and the carbon cycle.}
\par
\emph{5.2. What if?}
\par
What if the world becomes warmer because of increased
\par
concentrations of greenhouse gases? What would the im-
\par
pacts be for regions and countries?
\par
The desired attributes of an assessment of ``What if''
\par
might be:
\par
(a) impacts described by science in an interdisciplinary
\par
framework,
\par
(b) forward looking, but cognizant of scientific uncertain-
\par
ties,
\par
(c) includes direct and indirect impacts of current climate
\par
and climate change scenarios, including a more holistic
\par
assessment of the costs of inaction,
\par
(d) focuses on places and issues, not disciplines and tools,
\par
and
\par
(e) includes input from stakeholders in planning and re-
\par
search.
\par
The challenges of providing assessments with these at-
\par
tributes include:
\par
(a) raising awareness and understanding among social sci-
\par
entists, who have little experience or training in at-
\par
mospheric issues,
\par
(b) raising awareness and understanding among regional
\par
stakeholders, who focus on regional scale issues and
\par
have little time and few resources to devote to climate
\par
change and other long term global scale issues,
\par
(c) encouraging expanded application of alternative analyt-
\par
ical tools to climate change problems, including tools
\par
from various specialties within economics, particularly
\par
since data needs may not be well defined in this con-
\par
text,
\par
(d) identifying useful approaches for incorporating local
\par
knowledge into the assessment, and
\par
(e) identifying the ``risk capital'' of the IA community that
\par
might attract new partners and shareholders in collab-
\par
orative research.
\par
The latter is more difficult than it may appear. Stakeholders
\par
are naturally suspicious of ``black boxes'' and there needs
\par
to be some way of improving their transparency. Stake-
\par
holders tend to trust local knowledge more than ``science''
\par
because the former has been acquired in order to survive,
\par
rather than as a product of the curious mind. However,
\par
stakeholder knowledge may not be so transparent either, as
\par
there is a tendency for some people to underestimate their
\par
uncertainties for personal reasons, especially if opinions are
\par
given as part of an adversarial process [56].
\par
\emph{5.3. So what/What should be done?}
\par
Does the impacts scenario make a difference to stake-
\par
holders' visions of the future? What potential responses to
\par
this scenario can be identified? Addressing these two ques-
\par
tions in the context of ``What if'' would provide similar
\par
attributes:
\par
(a) defined by stakeholders in a multiobjective framework,
\par
as they react to ``What if'',
\par
(b) forward looking, but cognizant of current policy instru-
\par
ments and tools,
\par
(c) focuses on places and issues, including those that may
\par
represent obstacles to response, and290 \emph{S.J. Cohen / Scientist--stakeholder collaboration in integrated assessment of climate change}
\par
(d) includes input from scientists as part of a new dialogue
\par
with stakeholders.
\par
This represents an important opportunity for both scien-
\par
tist and stakeholder communities to learn from each other.
\par
There are, however, some significant challenges:
\par
(a) how to overcome the ``leap'' of advocates and oppo-
\par
nents of proactive measures;
\par
(b) how to address the ``leap'' of sustainable development
\par
advocates, who tend to view climate change as a symp-
\par
tom of overuse of energy and other resources, and yet
\par
propose alternatives which might increase vulnerabili-
\par
ties to climate change (e.g., increased use of renewable
\par
resources); and
\par
(c) how to define the role(s) of IA in identifying mitigation
\par
and adaptation measures.
\par
The latter should include the generation of various adapta-
\par
tion and mitigation scenarios and description of their ben-
\par
efits and costs, compared with the costs of inaction.
\par
\emph{5.4. IA as a process}
\par
IA is an evolving, non-linear process which can identify
\par
problems, raise questions, propose solutions, and encour-
\par
age cross-sectoral learning [79]. Such a process requires
\par
analytical tools, but more than that, it is also dependent on
\par
the communication skills of its participants. If stakeholders
\par
and their knowledge are to be drawn into this process, IA
\par
must become less of a ``black box'' and more human. This
\par
relates both to the use of IAMs and to the personal net-
\par
works developed by IA participants during an IA exercise.
\par
Networking outside of one's peer group is a time consum-
\par
ing process which generally occurs behind the scenes. It
\par
is not ``model development'' in the traditional sense, and
\par
scientists rarely get the opportunity or the recognition for
\par
such activities.
\par
IA is therefore about two kinds of activities: (a) con-
\par
struction or modification of analytical tools (IAMs, other
\par
models, non-model approaches) and (b) development of im-
\par
pact research frameworks and networks. IAM construction
\par
and IA of regions and countries have not generally involved
\par
stakeholder participation, but they have always developed
\par
research networks. In contrast to the development of IAMs,
\par
regional and national IAs of climate change impacts have
\par
really been framework and network builders, rather than
\par
model builders, and would seek out existing analytical tools
\par
that could be applied to their particular cases.
\par
\textbf{6. Reflections and lessons}
\par
The cases from the Mackenzie Basin Impact Study de-
\par
scribed in this essay illustrate how climate change may have
\par
indirect but profound impacts on land cover, regional re-
\par
source exploitation, and consequently, national strategies
\par
for climate change adaptation and mitigation. The MBIS
\par
exercise is now completed. There are many gaps and
\par
methodological challenges that still remain, but important
\par
lessons are being learned, and new questions are being
\par
identified. These new questions have come to light be-
\par
cause of collaboration with stakeholders outside of IAM
\par
activities, but within the study's overall integration frame-
\par
work. This framework is composed of modeling and non-
\par
modeling approaches, which draw on sectoral studies and
\par
local scientific and stakeholder knowledge. It is difficult to
\par
conceive how an IAM, by itself, could have described these
\par
complex linkages between climate and climate-sensitive re-
\par
sources.
\par
Pursuing a ``bottom up'' disaggregated approach to IA is
\par
a significant organizational challenge [25]. The MBIS ex-
\par
perience includes both successful collaboration and missed
\par
opportunities. More funding would have helped, of course,
\par
but the process could be improved:
\par
(a) Selection of integration exercises requires additional
\par
criteria before acceptance by the assessment's review
\par
committee, including explicit specifications of data
\par
needs and commitments to assist in communication be-
\par
tween study participants.
\par
(b) A common platform for GIS should be established be-
\par
fore selection of other projects. Compatibility with this
\par
platform should be one of the criteria for acceptance
\par
into the assessment. This could involve setting up a
\par
Home Page on the World Wide Web, but this option
\par
would require additional resources (as part of the sec-
\par
retariat's function ?), and there would be questions of
\par
access, confidentiality and security.
\par
(c) First-order impact studies should be completed early
\par
so that higher order projects have more time to apply
\par
the results.
\par
(d) A full-time secretariat is needed to facilitate co-
\par
ordination, communication and archiving of data.
\par
At the MBIS Final Workshop, held in May 1996, regional
\par
stakeholders were asked if the climate change impact sce-
\par
narios made a difference to their visions of the future. Most
\par
of them said ``yes'' or ``yes in the long term''. Their views
\par
on possible responses to the scenarios included reactive
\par
and proactive adaptation, as well as intervention in pol-
\par
icy debates at other levels (i.e., national, international). In
\par
response to questions about the challenges of improving
\par
communications between scientists and stakeholders, there
\par
were some specific proposals [20], including:
\par
(a) production of ``plain language'' reports,
\par
(b) establishment of a regionally-based structure to serve as
\par
an information source on global environmental issues,
\par
such as climate change,
\par
(c) support for community based monitoring programs,
\par
and
\par
(d) encouragement of efforts to facilitate collaboration be-
\par
tween research projects initiated in different regions.\emph{S.J. Cohen / Scientist--stakeholder collaboration in integrated assessment of climate change }291
\par
A collaborative IA effort, with stakeholder input, is an ap-
\par
proach that can bring a complex issue to the level of de-
\par
cision makers. Qualitative narratives complement quan-
\par
titative modeling activities and provide a richness that
\par
would be difficult to simulate in a computer. Each ac-
\par
tivity provides its own insight, and the presence of var-
\par
ious integration exercises serves to provide balance be-
\par
tween transparent but subjective local knowledge, and
\par
less transparent but potentially innovative ``black boxes''.
\par
Unfortunately, these black boxes cover only a limited
\par
set of issues, leaving out such things as (a) sustain-
\par
ability of aboriginal lifestyles, (b) long term viability
\par
of resource management agreements, and (c) implica-
\par
tions for sustainability of economic development activi-
\par
ties.
\par
IA could do more if it does not constrain itself exclu-
\par
sively to the development of IAMs. If IA in general, and
\par
IAMs in particular, are going to become attractive to a
\par
broad range of decision makers, they cannot be done in
\par
isolation from them. Debate about climate change should
\par
not simply involve stakeholders in mitigation (i.e., limita-
\par
tion of greenhouse gas emissions). There are many more
\par
actors that could become involved, and they should not
\par
be left out. In the accelerating rush towards building the
\par
``perfect'' IAM, perhaps some effort should be devoted to
\par
improving our ability to ``walk''. This walk, or new para-
\par
digm for IA, would include: (a) consideration of regional
\par
impacts and adaptation, (b) linkage with existing resource
\par
management instruments and policies, (c) identification of
\par
indirect impacts when the focus is on places, rather than
\par
sectors, and (d) incorporation of local knowledge into the
\par
analysis.
\par
The effect of a change of climate on a region is more
\par
than the sum of changes to trees, crops and rivers. People,
\par
through their various public and private activities, will re-
\par
spond to the combined effects of climate change on land
\par
and water resources. These responses will be tempered and
\par
shaded by the choices government officials, community res-
\par
idents and industry leaders make in response to other issues
\par
such as the demands of the global economy, traditional
\par
lifestyles and political realities. Computer-based models
\par
are one way to bring together or integrate many parts of
\par
the whole, but these models are limited in their abilities to
\par
describe how regions and people relate to climate change
\par
and other stresses. The experience of MBIS suggests that an
\par
integrated assessment requires a partnership of stakehold-
\par
ers and scientists, operating at regional and global scales,
\par
in which visions are shared and respected, and information
\par
is freely exchanged.
\par
In order to counteract the strong tendency to treat IAs
\par
and IAMs as if they were equivalent [68], perhaps it is
\par
appropriate to spell out a ``guiding principle'' for IA of
\par
climate change. The following is offered as a starting point
\par
for discussion:
\par
\emph{Integrated assessment of climate change seeks to pro-}
\par
\emph{vide information on all aspects of this issue, including en-}
\par
\emph{vironmental, economic and social. The framework for as-}
\par
\emph{sessment should be based on a set of concerns shared by}
\par
\emph{researchers and stakeholders}:
\par
(1) \emph{impacts of emissions on climate};
\par
(2) \emph{direct and indirect impacts of climate change scenarios}
\par
\emph{on countries and regions};
\par
(3) \emph{assessment of adaptation options}; \emph{and}
\par
(4) \emph{assessment of abatement }(\emph{mitigation}) \emph{options, includ-}
\par
\emph{ing associated interactions and feedbacks}.
\par
\emph{This framework should be holistic, incorporating the use of}
\par
(a) \emph{integrated assessment models},
\par
(b) \emph{other analytical methods, and}
\par
(c) \emph{knowledge of researchers and stakeholders from sec-}
\par
\emph{tors and locations of interest}.
