Given the value of y, no other information (e.g., about the values of x or z) is relevant for
\par
determining response probability P. In this example, y could be defined as the \emph{internal}
\par
\emph{dose }on which P depends. How well P can be predicted depends only on how well y can\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
be estimated, and not on the rest of the information in the time series x or the parameter
\par
vector z.
\par
Defining internal dose as the minimum information that is sufficient to derive or
\par
calculate P provides a concept that can be extended to large risk models. To do so, we
\par
introduce a mathematical abstraction of the structure of a model called its \emph{derivation}
\par
\emph{graph.} This conceptual apparatus and closely related ideas and extensions have been used
\par
by many modelers using various terms, such as influence diagrams (Schachter, 1990),
\par
causal graphs (Pearl, 1998; Galles and Pearl, 1997), Bayesian networks (Pearl,1988,
\par
Jensen 1996), and structured modeling graphs (Geoffrion, 1996). A \emph{derivation graph} for a
\par
risk model is a directed graph, which we denote by G = (V, E), showing how P is derived
\par
from other quantities. In such a graph, V is a set of vertices or "nodes", representing the
\par
quantities (parameters or variables) of the model. E is a set of directed edges (``arrows'')
\par
between pairs of nodes (i.e., E is an asymmetric binary relation, E {$\subset$} V x V). Unless
\par
otherwise stated, G is assumed to be acyclic, i.e., it has no directed cycles (Schachter,
\par
1990). The interpretation of the graph is as follows:
\par
(i) \emph{The value of any quantity v in V is determined by the values of the quantities}
\par
\emph{that point into it,} i.e., that have directed edges ending at v, if there are any. Thus,
\par
one can associate with each node a formula or algorithm that determines its value
\par
from the values of its inputs. From this perspective, the graph structure reflects the
\par
order in which values of quantities can be calculated and propagated (Shenoy,
\par
1992). In a non-deterministic model, the conditional probability density function
\par
(pdf) for the value of a quantity is determined by the values of the quantities that
\par
point into it. If all quantities are scalars (rather than time series, spatial density
\par
functions, or other mathematical objects) then the derivation graph is called an
\par
\emph{influence diagram }(Schachter, 1990).
\par
(ii) Nodes with only outward pointing edges constitute the model's \emph{input quantities.}
\par
Nodes with only inward pointing edges are \emph{derived quantities. }They constitute
\par
outputs from the model. \emph{Intermediate nodes,} having both inward and outward
\par
arrows, are also outputs, in that their values are determined by the input quantities.
\par
The administered dose variable x is an input variable and P is a derived output
\par
quantity.\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
Although not required by this directed acyclic graph (``dag'') formalism, it will often
\par
be convenient to interpret derivation graphs as showing \emph{causal dependency }relations
\par
among quantities\emph{,} as defined by Iwasaki and Simon, 1994. These relations ``are based on
\par
the notion of \emph{manipulability} of a variable by \emph{intervention }into the system. When we say
\par
that `variable B depends on variable A', which we will write as
\par
A {$\rightarrow$} B,
\par
we mean that there exists some mechanism that determines the value of B from that of A,
\par
whether it be a physical law, an actual device, or some other kind of communication path
\par
between the two variables. Thus, everything being equal, a change in A will necessarily
\par
result in a change in B.'' In our dag formulation, a change in A does not \emph{necessarily}
\par
change the value of B, since the relation between them may be stochastic or may be such
\par
that B has the same value for several different values of A. For example, if A has caused
\par
an irreversible change in B, then a further change in A may not change the value of B
\par
further. Nonetheless, interpretations of dags in which changes in input variables
\par
propagate among the other variables in a model along the arrows linking them are useful
\par
for many purposes in risk analysis, as well as in other areas of applied science and
\par
engineering. As noted by Iwasaki and Simon (\emph{ibid,} p. 146) ``The method for determining
\par
causality based on propagation of disturbances is generally consistent with, but somewhat
\par
less general than, the more formal method of causal ordering'' developed in their paper.
\par
This method, drawing on earlier econometric work on structural equation modeling, is
\par
based on dependency relations among variables in systems of structural equations
\par
(Bentler, 1992). It orders variables in such a way that earlier ones determine the values of
\par
later ones, i.e., the values of later ones can be derived from the values of earlier ones.
\par
Thus, causal orderings of variables in models can provide an interpretation of, and
\par
motivation for, derivation graphs.
\par
To construct an internal dose quantity in a risk model with derivation graph G,
\par
define Y as a subset of quantities with the following properties:
\par
(i) \emph{Conditional Independence: }The value of P is \emph{conditionally independent} of the
\par
values of all other quantities given the values of the quantities in Y. Thus, learning
\par
the value of any other quantities (i.e., in V - Y) would not change P's conditional
\par
pdf. For example, fP(P \textbar{} Y, v) = fP(P \textbar{} Y), for any v in V - Y, where fP denotes the\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
probability density function of P and "\textbar " means "given the information" or
\par
``conditioned on''.
\par
(ii) \emph{Sufficiency: }The values in Y suffice to determine the conditional pdf of P (or
\par
the value of P, in a deterministic model). This property implies, for example, that
\par
there are no explanatory variables in V that affect P but that do not affect the
\par
variables in Y.
\par
(iii) \emph{Minimality: }No proper (smaller) subset of Y has properties (i) and (ii).
\par
The information in Y is minimally sufficient to determine uniquely, via the risk model, the
\par
value or conditional pdf of the response probability P.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Definition: }}\emph{In a risk model with derivation graph G = (V, E) having response}
\par
\emph{probability P as a derived output, a quantity is a measure of internal dose if and only if it}
\par
\emph{satisfies properties (i) through (iii).}
\par
According to this definition, a set Y satisfying properties (i) through (iii) is a measure of
\par
internal dose if and only if it contains a single quantity. The adequacy of a proposed
\par
internal dose measure y can be assessed statistically, if y can be measured or calculated, by
\par
testing the hypothesis that P is statistically independent of x (and other inputs), given the
\par
value of y. In a deterministic model, the conditional pdf of P is a single probability number
\par
when the values of the input quantities are uniquely specified. Thus, deterministic risk
\par
models are included as special cases.
\par
Internal dose measures may not be unique, even in models where they exist. For
\par
example, if y is an internal dose measure, then so is any monotonic transformation of y.
\par
The next section introduces units of measurement and measurement scales to eliminate
\par
this ambiguity. In addition, consider the following simple model, and assume that there is
\par
a one-to-one, deterministic relation between x and y:
\par
x {$\rightarrow$} y {$\rightarrow$} P
\par
Then either x or y can be used as a measure of internal dose. However, y is usually
\par
determined by the values of several other quantities (e.g., administered dose and metabolic
\par
and pharmacokinetic parameters), and its value does not uniquely determine theirs. In\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
general, if there are multiple measures of internal dose in a risk model, we prefer the one
\par
that is closest to P in the derivation graph (excluding P itself).
\par
\textbf{2. EXISTENCE AND ALGEBRAIC FORM OF INTERNAL DOSE MEASURES}
\par
Given a deterministic risk model P = f(x ; z), when does there exist a derived scalar
\par
quantity (an "internal dose")
\par
y = g(x, z)
\par
such that y uniquely determines the value of P? defined by the conditional probability density function
\par
More generally, in a stochastic risk model
\par
fP(p \textbar{} x, z),
\par
we wish to determine when there exists a scalar quantity y = g(x, z) such that
\par
fP(p \textbar{} x, z) = fP(p \textbar{} y) = fP(p \textbar{} x, z, y).
\par
That is, P depends on x and z only through the scalar y. If such a y exists, then it is
\par
important to determine the algebraic form of the function g(.) by which it is calculated
\par
from x and z.
\par
The questions of existence and algebraic form can be addressed constructively in
\par
the important special case in which all input quantities are physical quantities measured on
\par
ratio scales, i.e., scales that are unique up to choice of a dimensionally consistent system
\par
of measurement units. For example, concentration, measured in mass of chemical per unit
\par
volume of inhaled air, is a ratio scale variable. The ratio of any two concentrations is
\par
independent of the choice of measurement units, e.g., mg/liter, ounces per cubic foot, etc.
\par
Suppose that P is determined by a set of primary physical quantities, each measured on a
\par
ratio scale. Examples of such primary quantities might include the concentration and
\par
duration of an inhalation exposure (if the exposure time series is completely specified by
\par
these two quantities), the mass of the exposed animal, its respiratory volume, breathing
\par
rate, and so forth. Let y be a secondary quantity, also measured on a ratio scale, derived
\par
from the primary quantities by some algebraic combining function. Thus, if the primary
\par
quantities are summarized in a vector (x, z), where x contains the exposure information\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
(e.g., the concentration and duration of exposure) and z contains all other primary
\par
quantities (covariates) needed to determine y, then y is assumed to be derived from (x, z)
\par
via some formula
\par
y = g(x, z).
\par
So far, all that has been added to the initial formulation is that y and the components of x
\par
and z are all measured on ratio scales, in a dimensionally consistent system of units. If the
\par
mapping defined by g is well-behaved (e.g., once-differentiable), this assumption of ratio
\par
scale measurements suffices to establish the algebraic form of the combining function, g(.).
\par
The product law of dimensional analysis states that \emph{"The measurement of a secondary}
\par
\emph{quantity is expressible as a product of powers of measurements of primary quantities"}
\par
(Lin and Segal, 1988, p. 206; Bhaskar and Nigam, 1990, p. 106). Thus, if x has m
\par
components and z has n components, then the formula for y is:
\par
y = C(x1)a1(x2)a2...(xm)am(z1)b1(z2)b2...(zn)bn
\par
where C is a constant (usually set equal to 1, which fixes the measurement scale of y) and
\par
the exponents are constants to be determined. Intuitively plausible formulas such as
\par
y = {$\beta$}A(x)/BW = {$\beta$}(concentration)(duration of exposure)(body weight)-1
\par
are special cases of the product law.
\par
The product law is not directly applicable to risk models since the output P, being
\par
a probability, is not measured on a ratio scale. However, there is a one-to-one
\par
correspondence between probabilities and the arrival rates of (adverse) events in a Poisson
\par
process, operated for one period of time, that would produce one or more arrivals with
\par
the given probabilities. Namely, if p is a probability number, then the derived quantity
\par
r = -ln(1 - p)
\par
is the average arrival rate (measured in expected number of arrivals per unit time, a ratio
\par
scale) that would produce at least one arrival in unit time with probability p. This
\par
transformation of the probability axis provides for ratio-scale measurement of event
\par
likelihoods. Therefore, the product law applies to r, and any risk model that uniquely\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
derives P [and hence the quantity -ln(1 - P)] from primary ratio-scale quantities (x, z) must
\par
have the product form:
\par
-ln(1 - P) = C(x1)a1(x2)a2...(xm)am(z1)b1(z2)b2...(zn)bn.
\par
Inverting yields the final formula for P:
\par
P = 1 - exp[-C(x1)a1(x2)a2...(xm)am(z1)b1(z2)b2...(zn)bn].
\par
This establishes the following result.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Proposition 1: }}\emph{Under the conditions of the product law of dimensional analysis, if the}
\par
\emph{response probability P is completely and minimally determined by (i.e., derived from)}
\par
\emph{one or more ratio-scale input quantities, then a numerical measure of internal dose}
\par
\emph{exists. It is given by a product of powers of these input quantities. If this internal dose}
\par
\emph{measure is denoted by y, then the form of the dose-response relation is}
\par
\emph{P = 1 - exp(-Cy).}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Corollary: }}\emph{Suppose that the response probability of an animal exposed to dose x at time}
\par
\emph{0 (e.g., via injection) depends only on x and on the length of the follow-up period, say, T.}
\par
\emph{Assume that x is measured on a ratio scale (e.g., mg of chemical/kg of animal's body}
\par
\emph{weight). Then the time-dose-response function follows a Weibull survival model, i.e., it is}
\par
\emph{of the form:}
\par
\emph{P = 1 - exp(-CxaTb),}
\par
\emph{where a, b, and C are constants.}
\par
Generalizations to more complicated dosing scenarios follow immediately. For example, if
\par
dosing is sustained for D days, and if P is determined by D as well as x (now measured in
\par
mg/kg/day, for example) and T, then the dose-response model would become
\par
P = 1 - exp(-CxaTbDc),\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
where the constants C, a, b, and c must be determined, e.g., by nonlinear regression
\par
applied to sample data for P, x, T, and D.
\par
In summary, dimensional analysis provides useful partial answers to the questions
\par
of whether an internal dose measure exists (it does if P is determined by a set of physically
\par
measurable, ratio-scale input quantities) and the form that the internal dose quantity must
\par
take (a product of powers of the primary input quantities). These results share the usual
\par
limitations of dimensional analysis. Specifically, they do not identify the group of primary
\par
input quantities needed to form a complete risk model for calculating P. (Indeed, the set
\par
of quantities that are treated as primary is typically unique only up to algebraic
\par
combinations that preserve group structure; see Doucet and Sloep, 1992). Such causal
\par
knowledge must come from biological or other considerations . Bhaskar and Nigam
\par
(1990) discuss in detail for simple physical models the interplay between the mathematical
\par
constraints implied by dimensional analysis and the qualitative knowledge of system
\par
dynamics and of the interdependencies among variables needed to deduce specific
\par
formulas describing input-output behaviors.
\par
\textbf{3. UNCERTAINTY ANALYSIS OF COMPLEX RISK MODELS}
\par
That P may depend on a large number of input quantities only through a single
\par
intermediate quantity raises the possibility that the value of P has only a limited sensitivity
\par
to uncertainties in the values of model inputs. Input uncertainties may be represented in a
\par
derivation graph such as Figure 1a.
\par
x {$\rightarrow$} P \textleftarrow{} z {$\rightarrow$} z* \textleftarrow{} e
\par
\textdownarrow{}
\par
n(x) \textleftarrow{} N(x)
\par
\textbf{\emph{Figure 1a: }}\emph{A Model with Imperfectly Measured Covariates}
\par
In this and subsequent derivation graphs, quantities that can be observed, including
\par
derived quantities that are deterministic functions of observed quantities, are underlined.
\par
Theoretical constructs or quantities that can not be observed are indicated in plain text.
\par
Thus, in this diagram, the dose x and the empirical quantal response at that dose,
\par
n(x)/N(x), are each observable. [Notice that this is a nondeterministic model: P and N(x)
\par
do not uniquely determine n(x), but they do uniquely determine the binomial pdf of n(x).]\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
Similarly, the covariate (and/or parameter) vector z can not be observed without error.
\par
What can be observed instead is a quantity z*, which depends on both the true (but
\par
unknown) value of z and also on an unobserved source of uncertainty, e, interpreted as
\par
noise or as measurement error. In simple statistical models, the relation between z* and z
\par
is often assumed to be
\par
z* = z + e,
\par
where all three quantities are vectors of the same dimension. Another possibility is that z*
\par
is a function of z (for example, z* might contain the aggregate quantity of total
\par
metabolites produced whereas z could be a higher-dimensional vector that reports the
\par
amounts formed of each specific metabolite). In general, z* contains less information than
\par
z (in a sense soon to be made precise), either because it is contaminated with exogenous
\par
error e or because it is a function of z, or both.
\par
A question of practical interest is whether z* can contain so much uncertainty that
\par
risk estimates of P (given x) will be less uncertain if they ignore z* than if they include it in
\par
estimating P. This type of question arises in discussions of the practical value to policy
\par
makers of incorporating information from PBPK and other complex "biologically-based"
\par
risk models. In such a model, there are typically many more uncertain quantities that must
\par
be estimated from limited data than in simpler (e.g., purely statistical) risk models. On the
\par
other hand, there may be additional measurements that can be taken (e.g., on the
\par
approximate values of partition coefficients or cytotoxic potencies) that can help to
\par
validate the complex model and raise confidence in its predictions. Which effect
\par
dominates? Is uncertainty about the relation between x and P increased or decreased by
\par
using a more complex model with partial but imperfect (perhaps highly uncertain)
\par
estimates of the additional parameters? These questions are addressed next.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Influence Diagram Models with Observations and Uncertain Inputs}}
\par
A clear answer can be given if uncertainty and information are formalized as
\par
entropies of probability density functions and if derivation graphs are specialized to be
\par
influence diagrams. Recall that an influence diagram is a derivation graph in which all
\par
quantities are scalars or finite-dimensional vectors. The joint pdf for values of model
\par
quantities is represented by storing at each derived node its conditional pdf, specifying the
\par
conditional probability density for each of its possible values, given each possible\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
combination of values for its inputs. Marginal pdfs are stored at input nodes. Any joint
\par
pdf may be expressed (or ``factored'') in terms of marginal and conditional pdfs for the
\par
input and output variables, respectively (Schachter, 1990). For practical computational
\par
purposes, each variable is typically assumed to have a finite number of possible distinct
\par
values (e.g., continuous quantities are rounded to a few significant digits). We will
\par
assume discrete variables except where otherwise stated (e.g., Proposition 3).
\par
To illustrate the main ideas before stating general results, consider the schematic
\par
representation of uncertainty in a model with a PBPK component, shown in Figure 1b.
\par
measurement error = ey {$\rightarrow$} y* c = cytotoxic/pharmacodynamic parameters
\par
{$\uparrow$} \textdownarrow{}
\par
administered dose = x {$\rightarrow$} y {$\rightarrow$} P {$\rightarrow$} [n(x)/N(x)] \textleftarrow{} N(x) = number of animals exposed to x
\par
{$\uparrow$}
\par
PBPK parameters = b {$\rightarrow$} b* \textleftarrow{} eb = measurement error in b
\par
\textbf{\emph{Figure 1b: }}\emph{A More Complex Derivation Graph with Uncertainties}
\par
In this model, administered dose (x) is assumed to be described by a vector of numerical
\par
parameters, such as concentration, hours per day, days per week, and number of weeks of
\par
dosing. These quantities and some pharmacokinetic parameters (b) uniquely determine a
\par
vector of internal metabolite doses (y) (e.g., described as a vector of AUCs of metabolites
\par
in specific physiological compartments). In contrast to Section 2, it is not important here
\par
whether y is a scalar or a vector, so long as it contains all the information from x and b
\par
that is relevant for predicting P. The pharmacokinetic parameters are not directly
\par
observable, but some function of y can be measured with error (y* denotes the measured
\par
quantity and ey the measurement error), while some function of the parameters b can also
\par
be measured with error (b* and eb, respectively). Finally, P is uniquely determined from y
\par
and from the values of some pharmacodynamic parameters (c), which may include cell
\par
kinetics and cytotoxic potency parameters, mutagenic potency parameters, and so forth.
\par
In the diagram, it is assumed that the values of these quantities can not be measured, even
\par
with error. At best, only their prior joint frequency distribution is available for purposes of
\par
risk assessment. As usual, the unobservable P and the experiment design variable N(x),
\par
i.e., the sample size at dose level x, uniquely determine the pdf of n(x), the number of
\par
animals with tumors at dose level x, and hence the pdf of the observed quantal response,
\par
n(x)/N(x).\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
In such an influence diagram model, information from observed quantities can be
\par
propagated throughout the diagram to obtain posterior (conditioned) pdfs for quantities of
\par
interest -- in this case, the value of P corresponding to any specified value of x. Several
\par
practical computational algorithms are available to accomplish such "fusion and
\par
propagation of evidence"; see, for example, Laurtitzen and Spiegelhalter (1988), Pearl
\par
(1988), Schachter (1990), Shenoy (1992). The result is the one that would be obtained by
\par
conditioning the full prior joint pdf for all model quantities on all observations using Bayes'
\par
rule, and then extracting [by integrating over all other quantities, i.e., "marginalizing out"
\par
(Shenoy, 1992)] the pdf for P. However, influence diagram algorithms are often practical
\par
for applied work and are available in commercial decision analysis software packages such
\par
as HUGIN™ (Jensen, 1996) as well as in a variety of academic and research software. By
\par
contrast, straightforward Bayesian computation of the posterior pdf by direct application
\par
of Bayes' rule is often computationally unmanageable because it requires multivariate
\par
integration or summation over a combinatorially explosive number of possible joint values
\par
of model quantities. Henceforth, we assume that Bayesian conditioning of unobserved
\par
output quantities (including P) on the values of all observed quantities can be carried out
\par
using readily available influence diagram algorithms.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Comparing Derivation Graph Models}}
\par
Figure 1b seems more complex than Figure 1a. This intuition can be sharpened by
\par
defining the following comparative binary relations between derivation graphs.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Definition:}}\emph{ Let G and H be two derivation graphs. Then}
\par
\emph{(i) G is an extension of H if H is a subgraph of G and G contains input or output nodes}
\par
\emph{not in H.}
\par
\emph{(ii) G is an elaboration of H if G has all the same input and output nodes as H, but also}
\par
\emph{contains additional intermediate nodes.}
\par
\emph{(iii) G is an expansion of H if G is an elaboration or an extension of H, or both.}
\par
\emph{Conversely, if G is an expansion of H, then H is a contraction of G.}
\par
\emph{(iv) G is a reduction of H if G is a contraction of H and G and H have the same}
\par
\emph{observable quantities.}\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
\emph{(v) G is more informed than H if the observable quantities of H are a proper subset of}
\par
\emph{the observable quantities of G.}
\par
With this terminology, refinements of the notion of comparative complexity between
\par
models can be made. The following relation is useful in discussing whether greater model
\par
complexity increases or reduces uncertainty about its predictions.
\par
\emph{(vi) G is at least as structurally complex as H if G is a more informed expansion of H}
\par
\emph{or if G is an expansion of H and G and H have the same observed quantities.}
\par
To illustrate these definitions, note that
\par
(i) x {$\rightarrow$} y {$\rightarrow$} P is a more-informed extension of x {$\rightarrow$} y {$\rightarrow$} P
\par
\textdownarrow{}
\par
y* \textleftarrow{} ey
\par
(ii) x {$\rightarrow$} P is a reduction of x {$\rightarrow$} y {$\rightarrow$} P; neither is more informed.
\par
If G and H are two derivation graph models for the risk variable P [or for its observable
\par
output, n(x)/N(x)] and if G is at least as structurally complex as H, then which model is
\par
better able to predict P? Proposition 3 below answers this question.
