\textbf{\emph{Reducing Derivation Graph Models}}
\par
This section introduces a concept of \emph{model reduction,} motivated in part by the use
\par
of reduced-form models in econometrics to replace causally interpretable structural
\par
equations and to improve estimation (Judge \emph{et al.,} 1985). It is useful for analyzing the
\par
information about risk (P) provided by a risk model and a set of observations on some of
\par
its quantities.
\par
An unreduced model typically contains quantities that are not essential for
\par
determining (or constraining) the values of its observable quantities. Reducing a
\par
derivation graph model requires one to compose (via function composition) the input-
\par
output mappings of its nodes, so that the effects on the model's observable quantities that
\par
would be transmitted by the deleted nodes are still represented in the reduced model. For
\par
example, if the diagram\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
x {$\rightarrow$} y {$\rightarrow$} P
\par
corresponds to the deterministic algebraic model
\par
y = g(x), P = f(y),
\par
then the reduced diagram
\par
x {$\rightarrow$} P
\par
would correspond to the \emph{reduced model}
\par
P = f(g(x)) = (f o g)(x),
\par
where o denotes function composition. (In practice, the response probability P is not
\par
observable, so this is only an illustration.) If the mappings from x to y and from y to P
\par
were nondeterministic, specified by conditional pdfs, then they could still be composed to
\par
yield the pdf of P conditioned on x. For example, suppose that Pr(y \textbar{} x) and Pr(P \textbar{} y) are
\par
defined by the following matrices (probabilistic mappings):
\par
Pr(y \textbar{} x): x = 0 x = 1 Pr(P \textbar{} y): y = 0 y = 1
\par
y = 0 1 0.5 P = 0 1 0.2
\par
y = 1 0 0.5 P = 1 0 0.8
\par
The composition of these mappings is the matrix product Pr(P \textbar{} x) = Pr(P \textbar{} y)Pr(y \textbar{} x):
\par
Pr(P \textbar{} x): x = 0 x = 1
\par
P = 0 1 0.6
\par
P = 1 0 0.4
\par
Given a probability distribution column vector for the input x, say p (with non-negative
\par
components summing to 1), the column vector Pr(P \textbar{} x)p gives the corresponding
\par
probability distribution for P. This process of replacing unobserved intermediate
\par
quantities (such as y) with direct mappings from observable inputs to observable outputs
\par
will be called \emph{model reduction. }A model is said to be \emph{fully reduced} if no further
\par
reductions are possible, i.e., if it contains only observable quantities for its intermediate
\par
and output nodes.\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
Reducing a model usually yields a smaller set of \emph{reduced parameters} (consisting of
\par
algebraic combinations of the parameters in the original model) that still fully determine
\par
the input-output mappings and the other constraints among observable quantities implied
\par
by the original model. For example, in the model [P = f(y), y = g(x)], if the original model
\par
equations are
\par
g(x) = a + bx and f(y) = c + dy,
\par
then the four parameters a through d in the original model (referred to as the "structural"
\par
model parameters in econometrics) may be combined to form the reduced model (Judge \emph{et}
\par
\emph{al}., 1985):
\par
P = a' + b'x
\par
having the two reduced parameters
\par
b' = bd and a' = (c + ad).
\par
Notice that only the reduced parameters can be estimated from the observable quantities x
\par
and P. The four original parameters are not uniquely identifiable from the observed
\par
quantities, no matter how many observations are available.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Entropy Inequalities}}
\par
Model reduction prunes away quantities that do not affect observable outcomes,
\par
even if they have clear, perhaps conceptually important, theoretical or biological
\par
interpretations. Uncertainties about those quantities are superfluous, in that resolving
\par
them will not improve ability to predict risk (or other observable output quantities) from
\par
the input quantities. In general, uncertainty about reduced parameters is less than
\par
uncertainty about their constituent (structural) parameters.
\par
To make this claim precise in the context of influence diagram models, a
\par
quantitative measure of the "uncertainty" about a discrete random variable is needed.
\par
Recall that the \emph{entropy, }measured in bits, of a discrete random variable X is defined as\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
ent(X) = -{$\Sigma$}xp(x)log2 p(x),
\par
where x ranges over all possible values of x and p(x) = Pr(X = x) is the probability density
\par
assigned to value x (Cover and Thomas, 1991). If -log2 p(x) = log2[1/p(x)] is interpreted
\par
as the amount of information gained when it is learned that X has value x, then ent(X) may
\par
be interpreted as the expected amount of information that will be gained by learning the
\par
value of X. It also gives a lower bound on the average number of binary questions with
\par
equally likely answers (each question asking whether the value of X belongs to a specified
\par
subset of values) required to determine the value of X when an optimal questioning
\par
strategy is used (Cover and Thomas, 1991). Notice that this number depends only on the
\par
number of distinct values of X and their probabilities, but not on their magnitudes or on
\par
whether they are closely spaced. For many purposes, ent(X) is a uniquely satisfactory
\par
measure of the uncertainty about a discrete random variable (Buckley, 1985). It is
\par
maximized when all of the possible values of X are equally likely (i.e., no information is
\par
available to help determine the correct value) and is equal to zero when the value of the
\par
random variable X is known (probability = 1). The next two results use entropy as a
\par
quantitative measure of uncertainty about model quantities.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Proposition 2:}}\emph{ Let b be a vector of quantities (parameters or variables) in an influence}
\par
\emph{diagram model. Let r(b) be the vector of reduced quantities (algebraic combinations or}
\par
\emph{other functions of b) obtained by reducing the model. Then the uncertainty about r(b) is}
\par
\emph{less than or equal to the uncertainty about b, i.e.,}
\par
\emph{ent[r(b)] }{$\leq$}\emph{ ent(b).}
\par
\emph{Proof:} This inequality follows from the following general result in information theory. If
\par
X is any discrete random variable, then the entropy of any function of X is less than or
\par
equal to the entropy of X (Cover and Thomas, 1991, p. 43).
\par
Intuitively, the value of r(b) may fail to distinguish between two or more values of b (i.e.,
\par
r(.) may be many-to-one), so that less information is needed to uniquely determine the
\par
value of r(b) than to uniquely determine the value of b.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Corollary: }}\emph{If response probability P depends on a vector of input quantities, b, only}
\par
\emph{through an internal dose quantity that is a scalar function of them, say, y = g(b), then}\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
\emph{ent(P) }{$\leq$}\emph{ ent(y) }{$\leq$}\emph{ ent(b).}
\par
This corollary gives specific meaning to the claim that uncertainty about internal dose is
\par
less than or equal to uncertainty about the input quantities (e.g., administered dose and
\par
physiological parameters) used to derive it.
\par
An objection to entropy as a measure of uncertainty about a random variable is
\par
that its value does not depend on the range of possible values, but only on the number of
\par
distinct values and their probabilities. Entropy is often interpreted as a measure of the
\par
"surprise" that one might feel on learning the specific value of a random variable, but it
\par
does not try to express whether the surprise reflects a value near or far from the expected
\par
value. Yet, intuitively, a random variable that is uniformly distributed between 0 and 100
\par
is "more uncertain" than one that is uniformly distributed between 0 and 0.01 because the
\par
range of its possible values is larger. (Note, however, that this intuition implies that
\par
"uncertainty" is not invariant under changes of scale; see e.g., Goodman, 1987.) By
\par
dropping the restriction to discrete random variables, this element of "uncertainty" can
\par
also be incorporated into a quantitative measure. Namely, for a continuous random
\par
variable, the \emph{differential entropy} is defined as
\par
ent(X) = - {$\int$}p(x)log2p(x)dx
\par
where p(x) is the probability density of x and the integral is taken over the entire set of
\par
possible values of X. For a continuous random variable X,
\par
ent(aX) = ent(X) + log2\textbar a\textbar,
\par
where \textbar a\textbar{} is the absolute value of a (or of the determinant of a, if X is a vector and a is a
\par
matrix). Thus, scaling up the range of X increases differential entropy (\textbar a\textbar{} {$>$} 1), while
\par
scaling the range of X down reduces differential entropy (\textbar a\textbar{} {$<$} 1). Interpretively, ent(X) is
\par
related to the logarithm of the volume of the support set (the set of values with positive
\par
probability measure) for X (Cover and Thomas, 1991, p. 226). For a scalar random
\par
variable uniformly distributed over an interval of length L, 2ent(X) = L.\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
The next result applies to models with probabilistic mappings, including
\par
deterministic models as a special case. It also holds for continuous as well as for discrete
\par
random variables, with ent(X) being interpreted as differential entropy if X is continuous.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Proposition 3 (Entropy Inequality): }}\emph{Let G and H be two influence diagram models for}
\par
\emph{P, and suppose that G is at least as structurally complex as H. Let ent(P \textbar{} G) denote the}
\par
\emph{entropy of the pdf of P after conditioning on all observable quantities in G, and similarly}
\par
\emph{for ent(P \textbar{} H). Then}
\par
\emph{ent(P \textbar{} G) }{$\leq$}\emph{ ent(P \textbar{} H),}
\par
\emph{with equality if and only if P is statistically independent of the observations in G, given}
\par
\emph{the observations in H.}
\par
\emph{Proof: }For any two random variables X and Y, ent(X \textbar{} Y) {$\leq$} ent(X), with equality if and
\par
only if X and Y are independent (i.e., "conditioning reduces entropy") (Cover and
\par
Thomas, 1991, Theorem 16.1.5, p. 483). Let X = (P \textbar{} H), i.e., it is the random variable P
\par
with its pdf conditioned on all observations in H. Let Y denote any observations in G that
\par
are not in H. These interpretations give Proposition 3. (If Y is empty, then G and H
\par
contain the same information and equality holds in the proposition.)
\par
Proposition 3 implies that, if one influence diagram model for P is more complex than
\par
another, in that it contains more observable quantities, then the more complex model will
\par
produce lower-entropy (less uncertain) predictions of P after conditioning on all
\par
observations. On the other hand, if one model for P contains the same observations as
\par
another but includes more derived, non-observable quantities, these do not increase
\par
uncertainty about P. They may be eliminated via model reduction without affecting
\par
calculations of the pdf of P. Thus, a structurally simpler model, in which some
\par
observations are ignored in order to reduce model complexity, does \emph{not} lead to better
\par
(more certain) risk estimates. The extra complexity is worthwhile as long as it carries with
\par
it an opportunity to incorporate more relevant observations into estimation of the dose-
\par
response relation.
\par
That an influence diagram model with more observed quantities should always lead
\par
to conclusions about P that are at least as certain as those from a less informed model is
\par
intuitively appealing. The practical importance of this result is that it implies that \emph{observed}\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
\emph{quantities that are relevant for estimating P should be included in the estimation process},
\par
even if they contain very large measurement errors. Doing so can never make the estimate
\par
of P worse (i.e., make its posterior pdf, after conditioning on observed quantities, have
\par
higher entropy) than it would be if some observations were ignored. This is in contrast to
\par
the recommendations from econometric procedures that seek to minimize mean square
\par
prediction error. The squared error loss criterion conventionally adopted in econometrics
\par
often prescribes that a subset of regressors should be used in preference to the complete
\par
set of regressors (e.g., Judge \emph{et al.,} 1985, p. 866). Including independent variables that
\par
are measured with sufficiently great error variance can increase the error sum of squares
\par
for the estimated regression coefficients. However, to reduce uncertainty about the
\par
dependent variable, P, as measured by the entropy of its posterior distribution, all
\par
independent variables should be included, unless P is conditionally independent of some of
\par
them, given the rest. In this case, the variables that are conditionally irrelevant for
\par
predicting P may be dropped without affecting the posterior distribution of P (Galles and
\par
Perl, 1997).
\par
As an example, in Figure 1b, suppose that P is estimated either by its full posterior
\par
pdf after conditioning on observed information, or by some functional of this posterior pdf
\par
(e.g., its mean, median, or mode). Then conditioning on the observed value of b*, which
\par
is an error-prone estimate of the true but unknown parameter vector b, will always reduce
\par
the entropy of P, no matter how broad is the distribution of the error term eb, so long as b
\par
and b* are not statistically independent.
\par
\textbf{4. COMPATIBILITY WITH DECISION-ANALYTIC MODELS FOR THE}
\par
\textbf{VALUE OF INFORMATION}
\par
Influence diagrams for risk management applications typically include decision
\par
variables, i.e., quantities whose values are set by a risk manager or policy maker.
\par
Uncertainty about model quantities may then be viewed as important only to the extent
\par
that it jeopardizes good decision-making. To formalize and quantify this concept of
\par
\emph{decision-relevant uncertainty,} let \emph{A} denote a set of possible alternative actions or
\par
decisions (e.g., occupational exposure standards for exposure to a chemical) and let \emph{B}
\par
denote the set of possible parameter vectors for an influence diagram model of risk.
\par
Introduce a loss function (a real-valued bivariate function \emph{L: A x B }{$\rightarrow$}\emph{ R}) defined as
\par
follows:\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
\emph{L(a, b)} = loss from decision or action \emph{a} when the parameters of the risk model are
\par
\emph{b}, {$\forall$}\emph{ a }{$\in$}\emph{ A, }{$\forall$}\emph{ b }{$\in$}\emph{ B.}
\par
The number \emph{L(a, b)} indicates the undesirability of the outcome in which policy decision \emph{a}
\par
is implemented when the true dose-response relation corresponds to parameter vector \emph{b}.
\par
Under most normative theories of decision making, loss is measured on a difference scale
\par
(unique up to choice of origin and scale) that may be normalized to run from 0 for the best
\par
outcome to 1 for the least desirable outcome. Equivalently, the \emph{(a, b)} pairs may be
\par
assigned utilities scaled to run from 0 for the least-preferred pair to 1 for the most-
\par
preferred pair. Since decisions that maximize expected utility minimize expected loss,
\par
either formulation may be used (DeGroot 1970, p. 123).
\par
Given a set of observations, suppose that a decision from \emph{A} is made that minimizes
\par
expected loss with respect to the posterior pdf of \emph{b}. The procedure is that, first, the
\par
impact of the observations on the probable value of\emph{ b} is computed, perhaps using
\par
commercial influence diagram software. The result is a posterior pdf for \emph{b}. This pdf
\par
induces a unique pdf for \emph{L(a, b)} for each risk management decision or act, \emph{a }{$\in$}\emph{ A.} A
\par
\emph{minimum expected loss} or \emph{maximum expected utility} decision rule chooses \emph{a} so that the
\par
induced pdf of \emph{L} has the smallest mean value among all pdfs that can be obtained by
\par
selecting acts in\emph{ A }(DeGroot 1970). Such an \emph{a} is also called a \emph{Bayes action} (Shao, 1999,
\par
p. 195). The \emph{value of information} can be measured by the reduction in the expected loss,
\par
i.e., in the expected value of \emph{L}, when a Bayes action is taken after using the information to
\par
compute the pdfs of \emph{L} for different acts in A (Laffont, 1990, Chapter 4). The question
\par
then arises of whether risk models with greater structural complexity lead to better
\par
decisions, that is, to Bayes actions with smaller expected losses.
\par
A principle results of modern decision theory gives an affirmative answer. Under
\par
most normative models of how decisions "ought" to made, including models that justify
\par
Bayes action (maximum expected utility) decision-making,\emph{ the expected value of}
\par
\emph{information is nonnegative}. Conditioning the pdf of \emph{b} on more information before
\par
choosing an action leads to expected losses that are never greater than those based on less
\par
information.
\par
The intellectual framework of modern normative decision theory, within which this
\par
result is formalized and proved, is large and sophisticated, defying brief summary. See
\par
Quiggin (1993) for an excellent review of both ``standard'' (subjective expected utility, or\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
``SEU'') theories and some alternatives, including controversial ones in which the value of
\par
information need not be positive. Without attempting to recapitulate the SEU framework,
\par
we note that most widely accepted theories of "rational" behavior imply that a rational
\par
decision maker will prefer to condition on all available relevant information, no matter
\par
how imperfect, because doing so is expected to reduce decision loss. This conclusion --
\par
that information has non-negative value -- holds regardless of the decision maker's specific
\par
loss function or utility function. Like the entropy inequalities, this information value
\par
inequality implies that even poor observations, in which observed values only weakly
\par
associated with the true values, will not lead to worse risk management decisions.
\par
Applied to the influence diagram setting, this result may be stated as follows.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Proposition 4 (Expected-Loss Inequality): }}\emph{Let G and H be two influence diagram}
\par
\emph{models with the same uncertain quantities b and decision variables a. Suppose that G is}
\par
\emph{at least as structurally complex as H. Let Eb\textbar G[L(a, b)] denote the expected loss}
\par
\emph{associated with the pdf of b after conditioning on all observable quantities in G,}
\par
\emph{assuming that a Bayes action is selected, and define Eb\textbar H[L(a, b)] similarly. Then, under}
\par
\emph{the usual assumptions of subjective expected utility (SEU) theory (Quiggin, 1993),}
\par
\emph{Eb\textbar G[L(a, b)] }{$\leq$}\emph{ Eb\textbar H[L(a, b)].}
\par
\emph{Thus, the expected loss from an optimal decision made using model G is less than or}
\par
\emph{equal to the expected loss from an optimal decision made using model H.}
\par
\emph{Proof:} Laffont (1990), Section 4.2, Theorem 1, gives the following result. Information structure 1
\par
is finer than information structure 2 if and only if, for \emph{any} prior probability distribution and for \emph{any}
\par
utility function, the expected utility of a Bayes action conditioned on information structure 1 is at
\par
least as great as the expected utility of a Bayes action conditioned on information structure 2. An
\par
\emph{information structure} is defined as a function mapping "states of nature" (which we interpret as
\par
specifying the values of all quantities in a model) into a set of observations. One information
\par
structure is \emph{finer than} another if the set of states that could have generated an observation in the
\par
first is a subset of the set of states that could have generated the corresponding observation in the
\par
second, for all possible pairs of observations from the two information structures. Letting the first
\par
information structure correspond to the set of observations in G and the second correspond to the
\par
set of observations in H establishes our Proposition 4.
\par
For expected utility decision-makers, increased structural complexity in a risk
\par
model not only leads to more certain inferences about risk (Proposition 3), but also to\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
decisions with lower (or equal) expected losses. truly better-informed than decisions based on H.
\par
In this sense, decisions based on G are
\par
As an important application, let\emph{ A} be the set of possible unit risk estimates for a
\par
carcinogen, so that a "decision" now refers to making (or choosing) a unit risk estimate.
\par
Interpret \emph{b} as the true unit risk, defined as the slope of the dose-response curve as dose
\par
approaches zero. Then \emph{L(a, b)} is the loss associated with estimating the unit risk estimate
\par
as \emph{a} when its true value is \emph{b.} If \emph{a} is chosen to minimize expected loss, then conditioning
\par
the estimate on more observations will reduce (or, if the information is irrelevant to \emph{b},
\par
leave unchanged) the expected loss. This is true for \emph{any }loss function. It is true even if
\par
the observations incorporated into the estimate (via conditioning) are very noisy or very
\par
imperfect surrogates for the biological quantities that determine \emph{b}. Therefore, a complex
\par
model that incorporates more relevant observations and measurements will lead to better
\par
(or to unchanged) risk management decisions, as evaluated by the expected loss criterion,
\par
than a simpler model that ignores the additional information.
