E(Loss \textbar{} data) = (v/x2) + wE(n2 \textbar{} data) = (v/x2) + wN2E(P2 \textbar{} data),
\par
where
\par
E(P2 \textbar{} data) = 1 - E[exp(-\emph{bq}x2) \textbar{} data]\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
Choosing x2 to minimize the expected loss is the same as choosing it to minimize the simpler
\par
expression
\par
(1/x2) + kE(P2 \textbar{} data)
\par
where k {$>$} 0 is defined as k = (wN2/v). All relevant value trade-offs in this example can be
\par
reduced to selection of a value for k.
\par
The term E(P2 \textbar{} data) may be quantified via Bayes' rule if a prior joint pdf for the
\par
reduced parameter \emph{bq} is specified. (Note that it is impossible to estimate \emph{b} and \emph{q} separately
\par
from the data. Therefore, assigning each a pdf to each independently and then sampling
\par
their values would introduce spurious uncertainty into the model. Using the reduced
\par
parameter \emph{bq} avoids this difficulty.) A perhaps more natural, but logically equivalent,
\par
alternative is to define a prior pdf for P1 and update it by conditioning on the data. The
\par
posterior pdf for P1 thus obtained induces a unique corresponding posterior pdf for \emph{bq}, since
\par
the two quantities are related by the transformation
\par
\emph{bq} = -ln(1 - P1)/x1.
\par
For example, suppose that the prior pdf for P1 is the maximum-entropy prior, i.e., the
\par
uniform density between 0 and 1. Then, after observing the data, the posterior pdf for P1
\par
has a beta-distribution with parameters (n1 + 1) and (N1 - n1 + 1). Its mean value is:
\par
E(P1 \textbar{} data) = (n1 + 1) / (N1 + 2) = 11/1002
\par
(DeGroot, 1970, p. 160). Note that the observation of zero responses when dose = 0 has
\par
no effect on inferences in this model, since it is implied by the node formulas. If the correct
\par
node formula for determining P were uncertain, however, then the zero-response
\par
observation could help to select among alternative formulas. The influence diagram
\par
framework is useful for addressing such \emph{model selection} issues as well as data uncertainty
\par
issues.
\par
The posterior pdf for P1 implies a posterior pdf for P2, for any choice of the decision
\par
variable x2. Namely, the formula
\par
P2 = 1 - exp(-\emph{bq}x2) = 1 - exp[ln(1 - P1)(x2/x1)],\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
transforms the beta pdf for P1 into a pdf for P2 by a change of variables.
\par
To minimize the scaled expected loss function
\par
(1/x2) + kE(P2 \textbar{} data)
\par
by appropriate choice of x2, it is necessary to be able to compute the expected value of P2
\par
for each choice of x2. This can be done conveniently using influence diagram tools such as
\par
the \emph{Analytica}™ software, originally developed at Carnegie-Melon University and now sold
\par
by Lumina Decision Systems, Inc. of Palo Alto. Using this software, and assuming that k =
\par
1000, the example can quickly be solved for the optimal value of x2. The Bayesian action is
\par
found to be x2 = 3 ppm-years, with expected loss 0.67. Making the standard too relaxed or
\par
too stringent by a factor of 10 (i.e., setting x2 = 30 ppm-years or x2 = 0.3 ppm-years,
\par
respectively) increases the expected loss 5-fold in each case, to about 3.36, and shifts the
\par
cumulative loss distribution function rightward.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Discussion}}
\par
The Bayesian decision-analytic approach requires a prior pdf for uncertain
\par
quantities as an input. Its defenders have developed several responses to the criticism that
\par
the need to specify a prior pdf injects undesirable subjectivity into the analysis. One
\par
defense is that use of subjective prior information is not necessarily undesirable. A
\par
second, pragmatic defense is that, when there are adequate data, the posterior pdf is not
\par
sensitive to the specific prior selected. Finally, there have been numerous attempts to
\par
cope with ``vague priors'', e.g., by using sets of prior pdfs, by relaxing the requirement that
\par
prior beliefs be represented by additive probability measures (as in super-additive
\par
Dempster-Shafer belief functions), by selecting maximum-entropy priors as defaults, and
\par
so forth (Dubois \emph{et al.,} 1992). Powerful "coherence" arguments support the use of
\par
conventional Bayesian prior pdfs -- for example, on the grounds that beliefs can be updated
\par
in a dynamically consistent fashion only if they are capable of being represented by a
\par
Bayesian prior pdf (Epstein and Breton, 1993). In any case, use of Propositions 3 and 4
\par
does not require resolution of the debate over selection and use of priors. If a\emph{ }joint pdf for
\par
model inputs is available \emph{in principle,} whether or not it is specified subjectively, the
\par
entropy and expected-loss inequalities will hold.\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
\par
\textbf{5. CONCLUSIONS}
\par
This paper has the following two main practical implications for risk analysts
\par
building and using complex risk models:
\par
1. \emph{Always use model reduction }to eliminate spurious uncertainties, prior to model
\par
parameter estimation and quantitative uncertainty analysis. For example,
\par
Monte-Carlo analyses that randomly sample values for all the original input
\par
quantities in a model, instead of only for reduced input quantities, may produce
\par
unnecessarily large uncertainties in the outputs.
\par
2. \emph{Always include model complexities that allow additional relevant (although}
\par
\emph{possibly very noisy or imperfect) observations to be incorporated} into the
\par
estimation of input-output relations, including dose-response relations. Doing
\par
so reduces or leaves unchanged both the uncertainty in model predictions and
\par
the expected loss from decisions based on the model.
\par
Concern that adding details and complexity to risk models will reduce their value by
\par
impairing their ability to obtain accurate risk estimates may sometimes be justified in a
\par
traditional multivariate regression setting, based on sum of squared error loss functions.
\par
But this concern is not justified in the Bayesian risk estimation framework we have
\par
discussed. Provided that model reduction is used to eliminate ``spurious'' complexities,
\par
which we have defined as those not grounded in observations, greater model complexity
\par
enables more certain risk estimates and better decisions.\copyright{} COX ASSOCIATES, 1999. 503 Franklin St., Denver, CO, 80218. Ph 303-388-1778; Fax --303-388-0609. tony@cox-associates.com
