\par
Establishing research priorities and developing
\par
and evaluating alternative policy options in the
\par
domain of climate change are activities that
\par
require a broad look across all the elements of
\par
the climate problem. Such broad integrated
\par
assessment should involve different analytical
\par
approaches in different parts of the problem.
\par
However, appropriate computer model frame-
\par
works, into which a variety of specific alternative
\par
results and submodels can be inserted, can pro-
\par
vide convenient vehicles for putting the pieces
\par
together. Such a framework is described. A
\par
simple version is used to illustrate how such
\par
frameworks can be used to perform uncertainty
\par
analysis, and how, together with expert judg-
\par
ment, they might be used in setting research
\par
priorities.
\par
Keywords: Climate change; Models; Uncertainty analysis
\par
Climate scientists have known for many years that
\par
human activities could have significant impacts on
\par
the climate. 1 The last several years have seen this
\par
possibility emerge as a focus of serious national and
\par
international debate in public policy circles. 2 From a
\par
scientific perspective climate change poses many
\par
challenging problems. From a public policy perspec-
\par
tive three questions are central. First, compared
\par
with all the other issues that affect human societies,
\par
how important is climate change likely to be to the
\par
various concerned parties? Second, what can these
\par
concerned parties do to mitigate or adapt to the
\par
effects of climate change? And third, what are the
\par
key uncertainties which cloud our answers to these
\par
questions and how rapidly, and at what cost, might
\par
these uncertainties be reduced through research?
\par
It is not possible to arrive at informed answers to
\par
these policy questions by focusing only on selected
\par
details. A broad integrated assessment, that spans
\par
The authors are with the Department of Engineering and
\par
Public Policy, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, PA
\par
15213, USA.
\par
all important aspects of the climate problem, is
\par
required) An interdisciplinary team of faculty and
\par
doctoral students in the Department of Engineering
\par
and Public Policy at Carnegie Mellon University is
\par
engaged in an on-going programme of such inte-
\par
grated assessment. The first, simple integrated mod-
\par
el developed in this programme used scenario analy-
\par
sis and demonstrated that, at least for the next
\par
decade, the prior judgment that decision makers
\par
bring to the problem, and the decision rules they
\par
employ (eg expected value versus minimizing max-
\par
imum loss), could be far more important in control-
\par
ling the policy conclusions that they reach than the
\par
results of scientific discoveries over that period. 4
\par
This paper describes a second model framework,
\par
termed ICAM-1 for 'integrated climate assessment
\par
model, version 1', which moves beyond simple scen-
\par
arios to support a much wider range of analysis. This
\par
model framework includes significant feedback be-
\par
tween the various elements of the problem,
\par
incorporates and propagates process and parameter
\par
uncertainty through the analysis explicitly, and pro-
\par
vides the ability readily to make basic structural
\par
modifications (eg to reflect new science or pose new
\par
policy questions). This is in contrast to the IMAGE
\par
model developed by Rotmans et al 5 and its much
\par
more comprehensive progeny ESCAPE, which do
\par
include feedback but do not support uncertainty
\par
analysis or structural adjustments. PAGE, being
\par
developed by Hope, is designed like ICAM-1 to
\par
explicitly represent and propagate uncertainties. 6
\par
PAGE includes feedback between the natural re-
\par
lease of CO2 and CH4 and climate change but does
\par
not include macroeconomic feedback such as abate-
\par
ment expenditure lowering economic growth rates.
\par
ICAM-1 is being developed to address four ques-
\par
tions:
\par
{$\bullet$} Given our current level of knowledge, can we
\par
differentiate between different climate policies in
\par
terms of outcomes that matter?
\par
{$\bullet$} Which uncertainties in the climate problem most
\par
contribute to uncertainties in our ability to esti-
\par
mate outcomes that matter?
