Structure of framework
\par
The overall structure of the ICAM-1 framework is
\par
shown in Figure 1. Each of the boxes with rounded
\par
corners represents a submodel. Starting at the right,
\par
the submodel labelled 'economic processes' is in-
\par
tended to capture such processes as population
\par
growth, economic growth, energy consumption and
\par
technological change. These socioeconomic proces-
\par
ses result in changes in various geophysical loadings
\par
which take the form of changes in patterns of land
\par
use and in emissions of radiatively important sub-
\par
stances such as greenhouse gases and particulates.
\par
The latter are represented by the box labelled 'emis-
\par
sions'. In the submodel labelled 'geophysical proces-
\par
ses' these emissions, together with atmospheric
\par
chemistry and loss processes, result in some new
\par
concentration of radiatively active gases and particu-
\par
lates, which leads to an alteration in the earth's
\par
radiative balance, which in turn leads to a change in
\par
climate. If this climate change is significant it will
\par
result in various physical and ecological changes.
\par
Some of these, such as losses to human habitat due
\par
to storms and sea level rise, losses to the managed
\par
environment (agriculture, fisheries and forestry etc)
\par
and losses to the unmanaged environment (including
\par
species and habitat loss), are observed and valued by
\par
people. The economic consequences of these losses
\par
are fed back into the economic processes submodel.
\par
There are three important ways in which human
\par
activities can be modified to respond to the issue of
\par
climate change. First, current human activities can
\par
be altered in a way that will reduce or eliminate their
\par
effects on climate (eg stop burning fossil fuels). We
\par
call such alternatives 'abatement'. Second, climate
\par
change can be allowed to occur, and human activi-
\par
ties can then be modified to accommodate to the
\par
resulting changes (build dikes, change crop mix,
\par
relocate agriculture etc). We call this 'adaptation'.
\par
Finally, the earth/atmosphere system can be inten-
\par
tionally modified to change its properties (eg in-
\par
crease the albedo). We call this 'geoengineering'.
\par
Abatement and geoengineering are included expli-
\par
citly as policy options in Figure 1. Adaptation, and
\par
its associated costs, are incorporated within the
\par
description of impacts, since if climate change occurs
\par
some adaptation is inevitable.
\par
The idea of an assessment framework is that the
\par
structure and assumptions of the subelements, and
\par
i n d e e d even the relationship b e t w e e n those
\par
subelements, is not fixed. Rather it is the central
\par
focus of on-going analysis. Various alternative sub-
\par
models may be substituted, and their implications
\par
explored. These may be as simple as a few variables
\par
210 ENERGY POLICY March 1993or they may involve reduced form or response
\par
surface descriptions of very complex models. De-
\par
pending on the questions being posed, different
\par
combinations of submodels may prove to be most
\par
useful. Modern non-procedural software environ-
\par
ments facilitate such flexibility.
\par
Besides flexibility, ease of modification and
\par
maintenance of multiple alternative versions, there
\par
are several other attributes that a model framework
\par
for integrated assessment of the climate problem
\par
should possess. Because uncertainty is so important,
\par
it should be easy to represent uncertain quantities as
\par
probability distributions, propagate probabilistic
\par
values through the models and perform various
\par
kinds of deterministic and stochastic sensitivity and
\par
uncertainty analysis. In order to keep track of what
\par
is going on and to be able to focus easily on those
\par
interactions and results that are of greatest import-
\par
ance, the software environment must be able to
\par
conveniently display both deterministic and probabi-
\par
listic results. Comprehension of the model structure
\par
and results should be enhanced through visualization
\par
tools.
\par
All of these features can be found in Demos (for
\par
Decision Modelling System), a general software
\par
environment for policy modelling developed in the
\par
Department of Engineering and Public Policy at
\par
Carnegie Mellon by Henrion and coworkers over the
\par
past decade. 7 ICAM-1 is being developed in Demos.
\par
It provides a simple illustration of the climate change
\par
assessment framework employing a particular mix of
\par
subelements and structure.
\par
The Demos environment operates through dia-
\par
grams that represent the functional dependencies
\par
among variables. These are a modified version of
\par
what decision analysis and artificial intelligence term
\par
influence diagrams. Thus, Figure 1 is not just a
\par
conceptual illustration. It is the top level display
\par
from the current Demos implementation of ICAM-
\par
1. If we were to click on any of the bold ovals, the
\par
system would display the influence diagrams for the
\par
associated submodels, most of which in turn contain
\par
one or more additional submodels. Finally, at the
\par
bottom, reside the variables and the mathematical
\par
relationships that define the model. This is illus-
\par
trated in Figure 2, where we see that the emissions
\par
submodel contains models of CH4 as well as other
\par
radiatively important trace substances, and that the
\par
C H 4 model contains 11 source terms each of which is
\par
described by a probability distribution and a time
\par
trend due to feedbacks and interaction with the
\par
various driving forces. The panel in the lower left of
\par
Figure 2 shows the confidence bands of emissions
\par
from rice paddies.
