\par
Of course, it is not necessary that all costs (and
\par
benefits) be converted to a single metric. One could
\par
develop multiattribute utility functions that repre-
\par
sent the values of key actors, or carry out analyses
\par
focused on meeting specific goals for important
\par
variables (eg maintain biodiversity at some specified
\par
level).
\par
Illustrating the framework
\par
In our simple illustration, ICAM-1 has been con-
\par
figured to simulate economic and climate change for
\par
two global regions (high latitude/developed and low
\par
latitude/developing) using 25 year time steps over
\par
the period 1975 to 2100. The differentiation between
\par
high and low latitudes makes it possible to examine
\par
the gross differences in the magnitude of climate
\par
change, as well as different economic circumstances
\par
and availability of resources needed to adapt to a
\par
changed climate. While a relatively simple model,
\par
this formulation contains over 230 variables, a quar-
\par
ter of which are uncertain.
\par
Economic processes
\par
We assume a simple model of economic processes in
\par
which (uncertain) population and labour productiv-
\par
ity are the determinants of economic growth rate.
\par
The net emissions of greenhouse gases are then
\par
computed from the level of economic activity and
\par
assumed changes in energy efficiency. For the future
\par
a response surface for the Edmonds-Reilly model of
\par
the global energy economy is being prepared for use
\par
in ICAM-1 and similar surfaces or reduced form
\par
models may be developed for some of the other
\par
economic models being examined by the Energy
\par
Modeling Forum. 8
\par
212 E N E R G Y P O L I C Y March 1993Projecting macrovariables such as population,
\par
labour productivity and technological performance
\par
for a period as long as the interval from the Amer-
\par
ican Civil War to today is clearly an uncertain
\par
proposition! In the current illustration, we have
\par
assumed a reasonably benign future in which, while
\par
parameter values are treated as uncertain, popula-
\par
tion growth stabilizes at zero growth and 0.5\%
\par
growth per annum by 2075 for the high latitude and
\par
low latitude regions respectively. Throughout the
\par
projection period labour productivity and energy
\par
efficiency continue to improve. Sensitivity studies
\par
that we have carried out show that the course of
\par
these macrovariables can profoundly affect conclu-
\par
sions about policy alternatives. In future analyses it
\par
will be important systematically to explore less ben-
\par
ign futures in which, for example, much of the
\par
developing world sinks into permanent poverty and
\par
overpopulation, or in which international conflict,
\par
pandemics or sustained economic collapse cause
\par
major disruptions. It will also be important to ex-
\par
plore futures in which dramatic technological prog-
\par
ress, such as development of the technology to
\par
support a low-cost solar powered hydrogen eco-
\par
nomy, occurs with unexpected rapidity. Such consid-
\par
erations, external to the main elements of the cli-
\par
mate problem, can completely dominate the policy
\par
responses that one selects. Since the future is uncer-
\par
tain, it is important to search for policy options that
\par
are likely to be robust in the face of a wide range of
\par
possible futures.
\par
Emissions
\par
The illustration includes emissions of CO2, CH4,
\par
N20 and sulphate aerosols. Natural emissions con-
\par
tinue at historic levels. Anthropogenic emissions are
\par
tied to energy consumption, and an uncertain auton-
\par
omous energy-efficiency index. Other radiatively
\par
important gases will be added in the future. When
\par
the associations are better understood, feedbacks
\par
between anthropogenic activities and feedbacks due
\par
to a changed climate can be included. For example,
\par
draining wetlands reduces emissions of methane.
\par
A warmer future may release methane from the
\par
steppes and tundra in the circumpolar regions.
\par
Geophysical processes
\par
In the geophysical process submodel, the change in
\par
the atmospheric concentration of greenhouse gases
\par
and sulphate aerosols is translated into a change in
\par
the radiative forcing of the atmosphere, based on the
\par
radiative forcing equations summarized in the scien-
\par
tific assessment report of the Intergovernmental
\par
Panel on Climate Change (IPCC). 9
\par
A model framework for integrated studies of the climate problem
\par
In our illustration, only uncertainty in the impact
\par
of the biogeochemical cycle on the atmospheric
\par
lifetime of various greenhouse gases, and direct and
\par
indirect radiative properties of aerosol sulphates is
\par
included. The atmospheric concentrations of these
\par
substances are calculated on the basis of a lifetime,
\par
randomly selected from a range that reflects current
\par
conventional wisdom (eg the atmospheric lifetime
\par
for CH4 is uniformly distributed between 8 and 12
\par
years). The next level of sophistication will involve
\par
the inclusion of a more detailed model of the carbon
\par
cycle.
\par
There is, in general, less uncertainty about the
\par
conversion of atmospheric concentration of
\par
radiatively important substances into changes in
\par
radiative forcing, than in the response of the climate
\par
system to changed radiative forcing. In the IPCC
\par
report, a likely range of 1.5{$^\circ$}C to 4.5{$^\circ$}C is reported as
\par
the equilibrium globally averaged temperature rise
\par
for a scenario equivalent to a doubling of atmospher-
\par
ic CO2 concentration (radiative forcing of 4 W/m2).
\par
Our illustration does not include a model that
\par
predicts climate changes (AT, \&precipitation,
\par
Agrowing season etc). Such changes can be explicitly
\par
incorporated in the future when models become able
\par
to produce useful estimates. In order to represent
\par
climate prediction uncertainty, however, we normal-
\par
ized the range of model prediction uncertainty and
\par
calculated an effective climate by multiplying that
\par
figure by the absolute change in radiative forcing.
\par
This metric retains the units of W/m 2 and is used to
\par
estimate the losses due to climate change.
\par
Impacts of climate change
\par
For the current illustration, we and a colleague,
\par
Lester Lave, have produced subjective probability
\par
distributions that describe the market and non-
\par
market costs that we believe might be associated
\par
with the climate change resulting from various
\par
amounts and rates of change in radiative forcing.
\par
Clearly these should be viewed as illustrative. At this
\par
stage, attention should be focused on the approach
\par
and methods that we are illustrating and not on
\par
specific details of model outputs or conclusions.
\par
The most important feature distinguishing market
\par
and non-market goods and services is that the major-
\par
ity of the former are produced. If climate change
\par
affects production of goods and services, we may
\par
adjust our demand by substitution of an alternative,
\par
or by converting to a less climate susceptible produc-
\par
tion process. Thus, the market can be expected to
\par
lead to an adaptation to climate impacts, and reduc-
\par
tion of losses due to changed climate through time.
\par
Through time, economic and population growth
\par
ENERGY POLICY March 1993 213A model framework for integrated studies of the climate problem
\par
are also likely to influence the production of goods
\par
and services. If these goods are produced from
\par
plentiful resources, and improvements in production
\par
technologies continue, it is likely that the cost of
\par
these goods and services will decrease. Many of the
\par
goods and services in the market fall into this
\par
category. Consequently, the cost of a unit of goods
\par
and services in the market is not likely to rise in the
\par
absence of climate change.
\par
In contrast, goods and services not traded in the
\par
market are predominantly of natural origin. The
\par
response of natural systems to climate change is also
\par
to adapt. However, many people place a value on
\par
natural systems as they are. Hence, adaptation away
\par
from status quo is seen by many as costly. Furth-
\par
ermore, over time economic and population growth
\par
are likely to increase the pressure on natural re-
\par
sources, which we know to be limited in supply,
\par
driving up their value. Thus, not only will the value
\par
of non-market goods and services probably increase
\par
through time, but any climate change induced
\par
adaptation seems likely to cumulatively increase the
\par
subjective value of losses.
\par
We used these heuristics as aids in constructing
\par
the surfaces that describe our subjective estimates of
\par
the mean economic impacts associated with climate
\par
change. In the market impacts part of the impacts
\par
submodel, changes in the level of atmospheric radia-
\par
tive forcing are transformed into fractional losses of
\par
GDP. Considerable uncertainty surrounds the
\par
estimations of these impacts, but it is generally
\par
agreed that the faster the rate of change in radiative
\par
forcing, or the greater its magnitude, the higher the
\par
level of impact. Furthermore, the impacts associated
\par
with a given level of radiative forcing (RF) dimin-
\par
ishes with time as adaptive measures are endoge-
\par
nously invoked by the market. Thus, the economic
\par
loss response surfaces have been defined using the
\par
independent parameters ARF, as the proxy measure
\par
for the level of climate change, and the rate of
\par
climate change, defined as W/m 2 pa. The locus of
\par
the mean impact associated with changes in radiative
\par
forcing defines the surfaces presented in Figures 3a
\par
and 3b. In these figures, the uncertainty about the
\par
surfaces is illustrated by displaying as the probability
\par
density functions for the year 2050, at a rate of
\par
climate change given by 0.5 W/m 2 pa.
\par
Similar surfaces that describe our subjective esti-
\par
mate of the mean impact of climate change on
\par
non-market goods and services are shown in Figures
\par
3c and 3d. In this case, the independent parameters
\par
are the level of climate change and time because
\par
changes in natural habitats are likely to be cumula-
\par
tive. The locus of the expected damages is deter-
\par
mined by the level of climate change, by the length
\par
of time since climate parameters diverged from their
\par
historic values, and by the financial ability of the
\par
public to exercise their willingness to pay to preserve
\par
nature in its undisturbed state. Taking a given level
\par
of climate change, and moving through time, we
\par
observe that the damage level rises to level off. The
\par
initial lower bound is determined by the ability of
\par
the public to allocate resources from their disposable
\par
income to preservation of nature. Under this for-
\par
mulation in the case of low latitude regions the
\par
ability to exercise the willingness to pay for preserva-
\par
tion of nature cannot be exercised in 1975 when
\par
average per capita incomes were lower than
\par
US\$1000 (the threshold income for the high latitude
\par
regions is set at US\$17 000). As the averaged per
\par
capita income increases, the ability to devote re-
\par
sources to the preservation of nature also rises. The
\par
upper bound is determined by our assumption that,
\par
on average, no more than 6\% of GDP could be
\par
devoted to this goal.
\par
We recognize that some have fundamental objec-
\par
tions to a willingness to pay formulation, preferring
\par
a rights based formulation. Others object to a wil-
\par
lingness to pay formulation that explicitly separates
\par
the developed and developing world on the basis of
\par
budget constraints and the ability to exercise prefer-
\par
ences. The formulation adopted here is for illustra-
\par
tion. Future analysis of alternative formulations will
\par
be important because the range in results may well
\par
dominate many scientific uncertainties.
\par
Abatement
\par
Two quite different strategies have been adopted in
\par
the literature to estimate the costs of abatement.
\par
The bottom-up engineering approach suggests that
\par
the costs of abatement are small, perhaps even
\par
negative at low control levels. Top-down economic
\par
models assume that control at any level has positive
\par
costs. In the current illustration we have combined
\par
results obtained by various economics models exer-
\par
cised in the Energy Modeling Forum 12 with the
\par
engineering estimates reported by a committee of
\par
the National Academy of Sciences, and included
\par
uncertainty that spans the full range of estimates. 10
\par
We have assumed the availability of a non-fossil
\par
backstop technology, whose marginal cost is lognor-
\par
mally distributed with a geometric mean of US\$20
\par
per million BTUs (roughly five times the current
\par
world price of oil) and a geometric standard devia-
\par
tion of 1.5. This technology is assumed to be avail-
\par
able from 2025 and provides an upper bound on the
\par
cost of controlling CO2 emissions.
