Uncertainties contributing to ambiguity
\par
We can explore the contribution of uncertainty in
\par
individual model variables to the uncertainty in our
\par
ability to estimate total costs by computing the rank
\par
order correlation between the two. The various rank
\par
correlations for the optimal policy choice in each
\par
region are shown in Table 3. For the current model
\par
configuration and assumptions, and assuming no
\par
geoengineering, we can draw four conclusions:
\par
{$\bullet$} the significance of the uncertain parameters
\par
varies by policy and region;
\par
{$\bullet$} the uncertainty in the cost of abatement plays a
\par
relatively minor role in the determination of
\par
outcome uncertainties;
\par
{$\bullet$} market loss uncertainties, especially in the low
\par
latitude region, are a significant contributor to
\par
uncertainty in outcomes; and
\par
{$\bullet$} calculation of the rank correlation coefficients
\par
using a lower discount rate leads to energy effi-
\par
ciency growing to be a key uncertainty.
\par
As noted previously, our example analysis has
\par
assumed a rather benign socioeconomic future. If
\par
instead we assume that labour productivity and
\par
energy efficiency do not grow beyond today's levels
\par
and that population growth continues at current
\par
rates, then consumer surplus is reduced dramatical-
\par
ly. While the gross measure of discounted GDP loss
\par
