\par
To illustrate the way in which the integrated
\par
model can be used to assess the value of research,
\par
consider uncertainty about climate sensitivity to
\par
changed radiative forcing. In our current illustra-
\par
tion, we have used the same expected level and
\par
range of climate sensitivity as that reported in the
\par
IPCC scientific assessment (likely value of 2.5{$^\circ$}C,
\par
and range of 1.5 to 4.5{$^\circ$}C for an increase in radiative
\par
forcing of 4 W/m2). Suppose that we are prepared to
\par
make policy choices on the basis of expected present
\par
value (any other decision rule could be substituted),
\par
then we run the ICAM-1 model with the present
\par
uncertain value for the climate sensitivity and obtain
\par
estimates of the expected present value of each of
\par
our policy options. Now assume that we mount a
\par
research programme designed to improve our under-
\par
standing of climate sensitivity. We consider a set of
\par
possible values which the mean and variances (\textasciitilde t,o)
\par
of our estimate might assume after the research is
\par
complete. We can rerun the ICAM model for each
\par
of these.
\par
In our example, we have postulated three levels of
\par
climate sensitivity, the present estimate, one half,
\par
and twice the present estimate. We have also
\par
assumed two levels of uncertainty about the ex-
\par
pected climate sensitivity, one equal to the standard
\par
deviation of present estimates, the other an order of
\par
magnitude smaller. The results are displayed in
\par
Figure 5 as a function of the first two moments of the
\par
probability distribution that describes uncertainty in
\par
climate sensitivity. While policy C is optimal with
\par
current assumptions, a lower climate sensitivity
\par
would result in policy A being optimal. If climate
\par
sensitivity increases, policy C remains optimal;
\par
however, the absolute magnitude of potential losses
\par
due to climate change grows larger. Finally, reduced
\par
uncertainty in climate sensitivity does not alter the
\par
policy choice, but the level of losses are reduced and
\par
the differences among various policies grow less
\par
significant.
\par
Results from future research may lead one to
\par
continue with a policy that had earlier been selected
\par
as optimal (perhaps with a change, 6, in its expected
\par
cost) or may result in a change of policy. If the
\par
ENERGY POLICY March 1993 219A model framework for integrated studies of the climate problem
\par
preferred policy changes, expenditures which have
\par
been made in pursuit of the former policy may or
\par
may not contribute to achievement of the latter.
\par
Such considerations are very important in trying to
\par
identify policy choices that are likely to be robust in
\par
the future. Whether or not the policy changes, if
\par
research reveals that the expected cost of the prefer-
\par
red policy has declined from an expected value of D
\par
to D - 6 as a result of the new knowledge, society
\par
has 6 more disposable income to support consump-
\par
tion or investment than it had expected to have. If
\par
research reveals that the expected cost of the opti-
\par
mum policy goes up from D to D + 6, then society
\par
must reallocate its other expenditures and invest-
\par
ments to secure the additional resources 6.
\par
How should we place a value on knowing, earlier
\par
than we might otherwise have known, whether we
\par
are pursuing the right policy and how much more or
\par
less it will cost than we had thought? In simplest
\par
terms the value of learning ahead of time that the
\par
optimal policy will cost more than we thought it
\par
would is effectively the present value of the social
\par
sinking fund that would be required to cover the
\par
debt of 6 that we would otherwise incur. The value
\par
of avoiding such indebtedness clearly depends upon
\par
a variety of factors, including beliefs about future
\par
economic and technological growth. While simple in
\par
principle, the arguments that we have sketched
\par
become very complicated as soon as the cost and
\par
benefit streams are distributed over time and
\par
accrued to different actors within the society.
\par
To keep the illustration simple, suppose that we
\par
are performing an analysis for a single rational social
\par
decision maker (we could do it for any actor, and in
\par
some cases would expect to get very different results
\par
depending on which actor we chose). 2{$^\circ$} Assume that
\par
we have obtained from the research community
\par
subjective judgments of probabilities (P\textasciitilde,.o, Y\textasciitilde P,,o =
\par
1.0) of the likelihood of ending up at each of the
\par
alternative points (\textasciitilde t,o) in Figure 4 after the prop-
\par
osed programme of research has been completed.
\par
The expected value of the research programme can
\par
then be computed as:
\par
