ending up with each of the possible outcomes (\textasciitilde,o).
\par
This expected cost can be compared with the cost of
\par
the proposed research programme. For example, in
\par
the illustration shown in Figure 5, if (despite our
\par
prior distribution) a research planner assumes that
\par
each of the six probability densities for climate
\par
sensitivity are equally likely, and that unexpected
\par
savings should be valued at 10\% and avoided indebt-
\par
edness at 15\% of the principal, then the expected
\par
value of the proposed research programme on cli-
\par
mate sensitivity would be approximately 0.3\% of
\par
total GDP in the high latitude region. Calculations
\par
of this sort performed with more elaborated research
\par
programmes and integrated models should prove
\par
useful as an aide in setting future research priorities.
\par
Conclusions
\par
In order to reduce the current uncertainties about
\par
climate change, the USA has embarked on a large
\par
(more than a billion dollars a year) national research
\par
programme. While much of this effort involves the
\par
relabelling of existing federal activities which have
\par
modest relevance to the climate problem, the re-
\par
sources being invested in activities of direct rel-
\par
evance to the climate problem are significant and are
\par
likely to grow. We have proposed an integrated
\par
assessment framework and illustrated how such a
\par
framework might be used as aid in evaluating
\par
alternative policies for responding to climate change
\par
and in allocating resources to directed research on
\par
climate change.
\par
In this specific illustration we find that the choice
\par
of decision rule plays a key role in the selection of
\par
mitigation policies, that given a decision rule, uncer-
\par
tainty in key variables can make it difficult or
\par
impossible to differentiate between the outcome of
\par
alternative policies and that the model parameters
\par
that contribute the most uncertainty to outcomes
\par
depend on the choice of policy, the discount rate and
\par
the geographical region being considered. We have
\par
illustrated how a combination of an integrated
\par
assessment model and the expert subjective judg-
\par
ments of researchers might be used as an aid in
\par
identifying areas of directed research that are likely
\par
to have particular value to future policy makers.
\par
In the future, elaborated versions of integrated
\par
models like the one described here should prove
\par
useful in developing and evaluating climate change
\par
policy options and in establishing priorities for
\par
directed research.
