\par
Experts in cancer biology/toxicology, pharmacokinetics, and dose-response modeling were iden-
\par
tified by a panel of science-policy specialists. In a workshop, experts reviewed the chloroform
\par
data, received training in probability elicitation, and constructed a consensual probability tree
\par
based on biological theories of cancer causation. Distributions of carcinogenic risk were developed
\par
based on the probability tree. chloroform data, judgmental probabilities provided by the experts
\par
and classical statistical techniques. Risk distributions varied considerably between experts, with
\par
some predicting essentially no risk from 100 ppb chloroform in drinking water while others have
\par
at least some probability on risks generally considered of regulatory significance. Estimated human
\par
risk was much lower when extrapolating from liver tumors in animals than from kidney tumors.
\par
Issues of scientific disagreement leading to different risk distributions between experts are discussed
\par
The resulting risk distributions are compared to standard EPA risk calculations for the same
\par
exposure scenario as well as to the expert judgments of epidemiologists about cancer risks of
\par
chlorinated drinking water. Issues in combining expert judgments are discussed, and several al-
\par
ternative methods are presented. Strengths and weaknesses of the distributional approach are
