\par
legislation through the National Envuonmental Policy \textbf{Act (NESA) }in \textbf{1970 }mirschl et al.
\par
1993; Pal and Rajappa, \textbf{19931. }In Canada, the Environmental Assessment and Review
\par
Process (EARP) \textbf{was }estabiished in 1974 to enforce standardized guidelines for \textbf{carrying }out
\par
impact assessments of federally proposed development projects \textbf{F A R O , 19781. }This
\par
process \textbf{was }strengthened through the Environmental Assessment Review Process Guidelines
\par
\textbf{Order by }the \textbf{federal }cabinet in 1984. Further actions were taken \textbf{by }the Canadian Parliament
\par
in 1993 to establish legislation that requires al1 development proposals seelring federal fimding
\par
support or Canadian government approvals to undergo an EIA. This trend towards
\par
establishment of legislation for protecting the envuonment proves that there is general
\par
consensus \textbf{regarding }the progressive deterioration of natural resources. This \textbf{in tum implies}
\par
that there is a need for appropriate tools for implementing sustainability principles into the
\par
decision \textbf{making }process so that decision rnakers \textbf{can }meet \textbf{increasingly }s a g e n t govemment
\par
regulations for minimizing adverse impacts of development projects. \"invariably, there is an
\par
implicit requirement to consider impacts of a development project coliectively in order to
\par
determine which, if \textbf{any, }of the alternatives satisfies ceplatory guidelines.
\par
Various methods of coalescing impacts ofdevelopment plans have been discussed. Pal
\par
