\par
The revenibility framework \textbf{is }proposed as a combined \textbf{theoretical }and \textbf{analytical }tool for
\par
implementing reversibility as one of \textbf{many viable }sustahabiiity \textbf{criteria in the }decision \textbf{mak\`uig}
\par
process. The integration of \textbf{theoretical aspects }of sustauiability into \textbf{the }fmework \textbf{has}
\par
increased its validity \textbf{and effectiveness }for application as a tool for sustainable project
\par
selection. Therefore, \textbf{the }fmework is an appropriate tool for \textbf{implementing }reversibility \textbf{as}
\par
\textbf{one }of \textbf{many }possible criteria for achieving sustainable project selection. In addition, \textbf{\emph{the}}
\par
pro posed framework \textbf{has }provided an \textbf{ex }panded view of development projects, \textbf{the}
\par
construction of electrical transmission and distribution \textbf{lines }in particular, that involve use of
\par
natural ecological resources and affect social and economic factors.
\par
Environmental impact statements \textbf{(EIS). }discussed in Chapter \textbf{1. can be }used as a
\par
reference point for evaluating the efficacy of the proposed reversibility framework.
\par
Environmental impact statements, which summarize the findings of the process of
\par
environmental impact assessments \textbf{(HA). }contain a great amount of information about
\par
potential social, ecological and economic impacts. This information is presented in a varieiy
\par
of formais of text and tables which are acceptable and follow regulated govemment
\par
guidelines. \textbf{As }indicated in Chapter \textbf{1. the }most significant attempt at establishing and
\par
