http://www.jstor.org\textbf{A Tabu Search Heuristic for the Vehicle}
\par
\textbf{Routing Problem}
\par
\textbf{Michel Gendreau * Alain Hertz * Gilbert Laporte}
\par
\textbf{Centre de recherche sur les transports Universite' de Mon tre'al, C.P. 6128, succursale A,}
\par
\textbf{Montre'al, Que'bec, Canada H3C 3J7}
\par
\textbf{De'partement de mathermatiques, Ecole Polytechnique Fe'de'rale de Lausanne,}
\par
\textbf{Ecublens, CH-1015 Lausanne, Switzerland}
\par
\textbf{T he purpose of this paper is to describe TABUROUTE, a new tabu search heuristic for the}
\par
\textbf{vehicle routing problem with capacity and route length restrictions. The algorithm considers}
\par
\textbf{a sequence of adjacent solutions obtained by repeatedly removing a vertex from its current}
\par
\textbf{route and reinserting it into another route. This is done by means of a generalized insertion}
\par
\textbf{procedure previously developed by the authors. During the course of the algorithm, infeasible}
\par
\textbf{solutions are allowed. Numerical tests on a set of benchmark problems indicate that tabu search}
\par
\textbf{outperforms the best existing heuristics, and TABUROUTE often produces the best known}
\par
\textbf{solutions.}
\par
\textbf{(Vehicle Routing Problem; Tabu Search; Generalized Insertion )}
\par
\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
\textbf{The purpose of this paper is to present TABUROUTE,}
\par
\textbf{a new heuristic for the following version of the Vehicle}
\par
\textbf{Routing Problem (VRP). Let G = (V, A) be a directed}
\par
\textbf{graph where V = \textbraceleft{} vo, v1, . . . , vl \textbraceright{} is a vertex set, and}
\par
\textbf{A = \textbraceleft (vi, vj): i = j]\textbraceright{} is an arc set. Vertex v0 denotes a}
\par
\textbf{depot at which m identical vehicles are based, and the}
\par
\textbf{remaining vertices of V represent c ities. The value of}
\par
\textbf{m is either fixed at some constant, or bounded above}
\par
\textbf{by In. With every arc (vi, vj) is associated a nonnegative}
\par
\textbf{distance cij. (For the sake of simplicity, the terms "dis-}
\par
\textbf{tances," "travel times," and "travel costs" will be used}
\par
\textbf{interchangeably.) The VRP consists of designing a set}
\par
\textbf{of least cost vehicle routes in such a way that}
\par
\textbf{(a) every route starts and ends at the depot;}
\par
\textbf{(b) every city of V \textbackslash{} \textbraceleft{} v0 \textbraceright{} is visited exactly once by}
\par
\textbf{exactly one vehicle, and}
\par
\textbf{(c) some side constraints are satisfied.}
\par
\textbf{We consider the following side constraints:}
\par
\textbf{(d) With every city is associated a nonnegative de-}
\par
\textbf{mand qi (q0 = 0). The total demand of any vehicle route}
\par
\textbf{may not exceed the vehicle capacity Q.}
\par
\textbf{(e) Every city vi requires a service time bi (60 = 0).}
\par
\textbf{The total length of any route (travel plus service times)}
\par
\textbf{may not exceed a preset bound L.}
\par
\textbf{In our version of the problem, vehicles bear no fixed}
\par
\textbf{cost, and their number is a decision variable.}
\par
\textbf{The VRP lies at the heart of distribution management}
\par
\textbf{and has been extensively studied over the last three}
\par
\textbf{decades or so. (See the surveys by Christofides, Min-}
\par
\textbf{gozzi, and Toth 1979, Bodin, Golden, Assad, and Ball}
\par
\textbf{1983, Christofides 1985, Laporte and Nobert 1987,}
\par
\textbf{Golden and Assad 1988, and Laporte 1992). The VRP}
\par
\textbf{is a hard combinatorial problem, and to this day only}
\par
\textbf{relatively small VRP instances can be solved to opti-}
\par
\textbf{mality. Interesting exceptions are the problems solved}
\par
\textbf{to optimality by Fisher (1989), using minimum k-trees.}
\par
\textbf{We are mostly interested here in heuristic algorithms.}
\par
\textbf{Extending the scheme proposed by Christofides (1985),}
\par
\textbf{these algorithms can be broadly classified into four}
\par
\textbf{types: (1) Constructive algorithms (see, e.g., Clarke and}
\par
\textbf{Wright 1964, Mole and Jameson 1976, Desrochers and}
\par
\textbf{Verhoog 1989, Altinkemer and Gavish 1991); (2) Two-}
\par
\textbf{phase algorithms (see, e.g., Gillett and Miller 1974,}
\par
\textbf{1276 MANAGEMENT SCIENCE/Vo1. 40, No. 10, October 1994 0025-1909/94/4010/1276\$01 .25}
\par
\textbf{Copyright C 1994, The Institute of Management SciencesGENDREAU, HERTZ, AND LAPORTE}
\par
\textbf{Tabu Search Heuristic}
\par
\textbf{Christofides, Mingozzi, and Toth 1979, Fisher and Jaik-}
\par
\textbf{umar 1981, Toth 1984); (3) Incomplete optimization al-}
\par
\textbf{gorithms (see, e.g., Christofides, Mingozzi, and Toth}
\par
\textbf{1979); (4) Improvement methods (see, e.g., Stewart and}
\par
\textbf{Golden 1984; Harche and Raghavan 1991).}
\par
\textbf{Metaheuristics such as simulated annealing and tabu}
\par
\textbf{search can be viewed as improvement methods. These}
\par
\textbf{are search schemes in which successive neighbors of a}
\par
\textbf{solution are examined, and the objective is allowed to}
\par
\textbf{deteriorate in order to avoid local minima. As a rule,}
\par
\textbf{these algorithms are designed to be open-ended and}
\par
\textbf{their running time, which can sometimes be quite large,}
\par
\textbf{is not a polynomial function of the size of the input}
\par
\textbf{data. Using an analogy with a material annealing pro-}
\par
\textbf{cess used in mechanics (Metropolis et al. 1953, Kirk-}
\par
\textbf{patrick, Gelatt, and Vecchi 1983), simulated annealing}
\par
\textbf{ensures that the probability of attaining a worse solution}
\par
\textbf{tends to zero as the number of iterations grows. Such}
\par
\textbf{a method was applied to the VRP by Osman (1991,}
\par
\textbf{1993). Tabu search was proposed by Glover (1977) (see}
\par
\textbf{Glover 1989, 1990 and Glover, Taillard, and de Werra}
\par
\textbf{1993 for recent overviews). Here, successive "neigh-}
\par
\textbf{bors" of a solution are examined and the best is selected.}
\par
\textbf{To prevent cycling, solutions that were recently ex-}
\par
\textbf{amined are forbidden and inserted in a constantly up-}
\par
\textbf{dated tabu list. We are aware of a number of VRP al-}
\par
\textbf{gorithms based on this approach. One of the first at-}
\par
\textbf{tempts to apply tabu search to the VRP is due to Willard}
\par
\textbf{(1989). Here, the problem is first transformed into a}
\par
\textbf{TSP by replication of the depot, and the search is re-}
\par
\textbf{stricted to neighbor solutions that can be reached by}
\par
\textbf{means of 2-opt or 3-opt interchanges while satisfying}
\par
\textbf{the VRP constraints. In Pureza and Franca (1991), the}
\par
\textbf{search proceeds from one solution to the next by swap-}
\par
\textbf{ping vertices between two routes. Osman (1991, 1993)}
\par
\textbf{uses a combination of 2-opt moves, vertex reassign-}
\par
\textbf{ments to different routes, and vertex interchanges be-}
\par
\textbf{tween routes. Another algorithm was developed by Se-}
\par
\textbf{met and Taillard (1993) for the solution of a real-life}
\par
\textbf{VRP containing several features, and different from the}
\par
\textbf{version considered in this paper. Here the basic tabu}
\par
\textbf{move consists of moving a city from its current route}
\par
\textbf{into an alternative route. Finally, Taillard (1992) par-}
\par
\textbf{titions the vertex set into clusters separately through}
\par
\textbf{vertex moves from one route to another. Clusters are}
\par
\textbf{updated throughout the algorithm. Note that in all these}
\par
\textbf{algorithms, a feasible solution is never allowed to be-}
\par
\textbf{come infeasible with respect to side constraints.}
\par
