\textbf{Step 2c of SEARCH contains a diversification strategy.}
\par
\textbf{Following Glover (1989), vertices that have been moved}
\par
\textbf{frequently are penalized by adding to the objective}
\par
\textbf{function of the candidate solution a term proportional}
\par
\textbf{to the absolute frequency of movement of the vertex v}
\par
\textbf{currently being moved. Taillard (1992) suggests using}
\par
\textbf{a constant equal to the product of three factors: (a)}
\par
\textbf{ZXmax, a factor equal to the absolute difference value be-}
\par
\textbf{tween two successive values of the objective function,}
\par
\textbf{(b) the square root of the neighborhood size (shown}
\par
\textbf{later to be proportional to the number mn of routes), (c)}
\par
\textbf{a scaling factor g equal to 0.01 in our implementation.}
\par
\textbf{As a rule, using too large a value of g lessens the like-}
\par
\textbf{lihood of obtaining a good solution. Too low a value}
\par
\textbf{does not produce the desired diversification effect be-}
\par
\textbf{cause the algorithm does not move away from the cur-}
\par
\textbf{rent solution. Post-optimality tests show that the al-}
\par
\textbf{gorithm is quite insensitive to g as long as it remains in}
\par
\textbf{the interval [0.005, 0.02].}
