\par
Other endpoints that have been proposed include the maximum change or rate of
\par
change of global annual mean surface temperature, which is frequently used as an
\par
index of climate change (Krause et al., 1989; Rijsberman and Swart, 1990; Wirth
\par
and Lashof, 1990; Hammitt et al., 1992); the magnitude of sea-level rise (Rotmans,
\par
1990; Wigley and Raper, 1992); and the GHG emission rate. As a control variable,
\par
GHG emissions are a natural focus of the policy debate (WMO, 1988; Framework
\par
Convention on Climate Change); as an endpoint, emissions are not relevant except
\par
as they may be viewed as a proxy for the rate of change of atmospheric concen-
\par
trations. The Global Warming Potential (GWP), an index developed for comparing
\par
the effects of emitting different GHGs, uses discounted or cumulative radiative
\par
forcing through a specified horizon as the evaluation endpoint (Houghton et al.,
\par
1990; Lashof and Ahuja, 1990), while an alternative, the Economic Damage Index
\par
(EDI), uses the present value of incremental economic welfare loss as its endpoint
\par
(Eckaus, 1992; Reilly and Richards, 1993; Schmalensee, 1993; Kandlikar, 1995;
\par
Hammitt et al., 1996).450 JAMES K. HAMMITT
\par
These alternative evaluation endpoints correspond to different stages in the
\par
causal chain linking anthropogenic GHG emissions to climate-change damages, il-
\par
lustrated in Figure 1 (not all of the important linkages can be represented as part of a
\par
linear chain, but the metaphor captures the most important aspects of the problem).
\par
The causal chain extends from anthropogenic emissions to atmospheric concentra-
\par
tions of anthropogenic GHGs (e.g., CO2, N2O, CH4, CFCs), other radiatively active
\par
gases whose concentrations are influenced by anthropogenic emissions (e.g., O3),
\par
and other atmospheric constituents (e.g., sulfate aerosols). Changes in atmospheric
\par
composition in turn influence radiative forcing, clouds, water vapor, atmospheric
\par
circulation, and other factors. These atmospheric changes influence global climate,
\par
including regional and temporal patterns of temperature, precipitation, and wind.
\par
Changes in climate may produce a wide range of impacts, including effects on the
\par
composition and growth of unmanaged ecosystems, agriculture, other economic
\par
activities, human health, and sea level. Changes in atmospheric composition also
\par
produce direct impacts, such as carbon dioxide fertilization of plants. (There are
\par
also interactions among the impact categories; e.g., sea level change affects unman-
\par
aged ecosystems and land availability.) These impacts influence human welfare by
\par
altering economic opportunity sets, e.g., through changes in environmental condi-
\par
tions, agricultural productivity, and biodiversity. The welfare losses associated with
\par
climate impacts depend on human preferences and opportunities for adaptation.
\par
The damages from climate change are the monetary value of direct losses and
\par
induced expenditures on adaptation (benefits of climate change are represented as
\par
negative damages).
\par
In choosing an endpoint for evaluating the merits of alternative abatement poli-
\par
cies, there is a tradeoff between relevance of the endpoint and the confidence
\par
with which it can be forecast. (For ongoing evaluation of the effectiveness of an
\par
abatement policy, the accuracy with which the current value of the endpoint can
\par
be monitored is also important.) For evaluating climate policies, endpoints farther
\par
down the causal chain (Figure 1) are most relevant but least confidently quantified.
\par
Relative to the other endpoints, GHG emissions are most confidently projected, al-
\par
though they are influenced by uncertainties about future population, magnitude and
\par
composition of economic growth, technological innovation, and human consump-
\par
tion patterns (Leggett et al., 1992). Projections of successive endpoints depend on
\par
the preceding endpoint and on additional, uncertain factors: atmospheric compo-
\par
sition is sensitive to uncertainties about emissions, the global carbon cycle, and
\par
atmospheric chemistry; climate to uncertainties about atmospheric concentrations
\par
and climate-feedback processes; impacts to uncertainties about global and regional
\par
climate, natural and human adaptation, and the structure of future economic ac-
\par
tivity; and damages to human preferences over economic activity and changes in
\par
ecosystems. The relevance of the endpoints to description of future environmental
\par
conditions and to decision makers varies inversely with stage in the causal chain:
\par
GHG emissions and concentrations are of little or no interest as endpoints; impacts
\par
of climate change and damages are of great interest. (Emissions are of interest asEVALUATION ENDPOINTS AND CLIMATE POLICY 451
\par
a control variable, and concentrations are of interest for monitoring the effects of
\par
abatement policies.)
\par
The tradeoff between uncertainty and relevance of endpoints is ubiquitous in en-
\par
vironmental policy. Environmental and health risks are often evaluated by defining
\par
an `acceptable-risk' level without assessing preferences over possible end states.
\par
In the United States, for example, chemicals that may cause cancer are often reg-
\par
ulated so as to reduce the predicted excess-cancer incidence to one-per-million
\par
lifetimes without regard to the type of cancer, its lethality, age of onset, or ef-
\par
ficacy of treatment (Rosenthal et al., 1992). In evaluating policies toward CFCs
\par
and stratospheric-ozone depletion, total column ozone loss (an idealized annual
\par
global average) was the standard endpoint used from the emergence of the issue
\par
in the mid-1970s through negotiation of the Montreal Protocol (the international
\par
agreement restricting CFC emissions) in the mid-1980s. This endpoint was relied
\par
on even though more relevant endpoints (e.g., skin-cancer incidence, the monetary
\par
value of increased cancer risk) were sometimes evaluated as well; indeed, the U.S.
\par
Regulatory Impact Analysis for the Montreal Protocol quantified the monetary
\par
benefits of controlling CFC emissions (EPA, 1987). (EPA did not quantify all the
\par
benefits, since it was able to show that health benefits alone justified the costs of
\par
regulation.{$\ast$})
\par
The primary reliance on total column ozone depletion reflected an implicit bal-
\par
ancing of relevance and uncertainty. Subsequent to the discovery of the Antarctic
\par
ozone hole, evaluations have used an endpoint corresponding to an earlier link
\par
in the causal chain, atmospheric chlorine concentration. The shift reflects a per-
\par
ception that total column ozone depletion was neither as relevant nor as accurately
\par
predicted as previously believed, since the discovery of the hole (which was not ex-
\par
plained by contemporary theories) revealed both the great seasonal and geographic
\par
variation in ozone depletion and the weakness of current theories to predict ozone
\par
depletion (Hammitt, 1997). Stratospheric chlorine concentration is not directly rel-
\par
evant to human health or ecosystem impacts but is thought to be a (causal) indicator
\par
of the appearance of the Antarctic ozone hole. The existence of the ozone hole
\par
affects seasonal ultraviolet radiation levels, which in turn have implications for
\par
human health and ecosystems (Smith et al., 1992).
\par
Benefit-cost analysis provides a framework for determining the appropriate
\par
level of abatement when benefits (and costs) can be accurately assessed. When
\par
benefits are highly uncertain, as for global climate change, several approaches
\par
are available for determining an appropriate abatement level. The most direct ap-
\par
proach is to formally characterize the uncertainty about benefits using subjective
\par
probability methods (Raiffa, 1968; Morgan and Henrion, 1990; Cooke, 1991) and
\par
to maximize the expected difference between benefits and costs. Alternatively,
\par
{$\ast$} By comparing alternative control levels, EPA concluded that the degree of emission reduction
\par
required by the Montreal Protocol was approximately the level that maximized benefits minus control
\par
costs. If benefits other than reduced skin cancer had been more comprehensively evaluated, a larger
\par
degree of control might have been justified.452 JAMES K. HAMMITT
\par
a less relevant but more accurately predicted endpoint may be adopted, and an
\par
`acceptable' level of the endpoint somehow determined. The determination of an
\par
`acceptable' level is fundamentally a matter of judgment, and may incorporate
\par
several types of information. Most directly, the judgment may represent a holistic
\par
summary of available information about the uncertain benefits, including judgment
\par
about the relationship between the selected endpoint and the magnitude of salient
\par
consequences. Such a judgment may provide an informal substitute for using sub-
\par
jective probabilities to characterize uncertainty and integrating with respect to the
\par
probabilities to find the expected benefit. Whether a holistic judgment is more or
\par
less accurate than a formal probability analysis is unclear, but a formal analysis has
\par
the advantage of making the use of available knowledge, assumptions, and logic
\par
underlying the conclusion accessible so they can be more readily evaluated.
\par
In some cases, it may be possible to determine the acceptable level by consider-
\par
ation of only the benefits or the costs of control. One frequently-espoused criterion
\par
is based on judgment about the benefits: it selects a level of control such that the
\par
magnitudes of adverse consequences are negligibly small, in the sense that they
\par
would have no practical significance or would be undetectable (against a naturally
\par
variable background or below limits of detection). This negligible-impact crite-
\par
rion is probably an important implicit justification for the one-per-million lifetime
\par
cancer-risk standard often mentioned in the U.S. (Rosenthal et al., 1992, Kelly,
\par
1995). It is not likely to be a useful guide when, as for climate change, the marginal
\par
cost of reducing the hazard to a negligible level is likely to be high.
\par
Alternatively, the acceptable level may be determined using only information
\par
about the costs of control, by selecting the level beyond which the marginal cost of
\par
further control begins to increase sharply. So-called `knee-of-the-curve' analysis
\par
rests on the assumption that the marginal control cost is smaller than the marginal
\par
benefit at low control levels but is higher than the marginal benefit at high control
\par
levels. As illustrated by Figure 2, the approach may work well in cases where
\par
the abatement-cost curve has a sharp bend; in other cases, the point beyond which
\par
marginal control costs are likely to exceed the marginal benefits is not well defined.
\par
In summary, alternatives to formal assessment of marginal benefits and costs
\par
that are likely to provide useful guidance rely on judgment about the uncertain
\par
benefits; alternatives like the negligible-impact criterion and `knee-of-the-curve'
\par
analysis are unlikely to prove useful in difficult cases. Whether and when a holistic-
\par
judgment approach is superior to more formal and disaggregated analysis is uncer-
\par
tain.
\par
