\textbf{5. Conclusions}
\par
The tradeoff between predictability and relevance of the evaluation endpoint is
\par
common in environmental-policy analysis. In climate change, the choice between
\par
the two prominent alternatives -- quantified damages of climate change and limiting
\par
the atmospheric concentration of GHGs to levels that are not dangerous -- leads to
\par
qualitatively different policy implications. Least-cost stabilization of atmospheric
\par
concentrations implies that, if the desired stabilization level is neither too near
\par
nor below the current level, it is optimal to delay significant reductions in GHG
\par
emissions, relative to their uncontrolled level, for a decade or more. A corollary
\par
implication is that the optimal marginal-abatement cost for the next few decades
\par
is nearly zero. In contrast, minimizing the sum of abatement costs and damages,
\par
as suggested by benefit-cost analysis, implies that GHG emissions should be im-
\par
mediately reduced below their uncontrolled, `business as usual' levels; the current
\par
optimal marginal-abatement cost is larger than for least-cost stabilization paths
\par
(except for low stabilization levels). For example, comparing the optimal benefit-
\par
cost path for the base case (T2x =2.5\textopenbullet C, \textmu{} =0.02) with the least-cost paths
\par
for stabilization at 450, 550, 650, and 750 ppmv shows that the optimal emis-
\par
sion reductions (and marginal-abatement costs) are higher under the benefit-cost
\par
endpoint than under the stabilization endpoint until 2002, 2024, 2069, and 2107,
\par
respectively. For larger values of the climate sensitivity, larger damages, damages
\par
that depend on the rate of climate change, and for path-dependent development and
\par
diffusion of abatement technologies, the benefit-cost endpoint implies even more
\par
stringent abatement compared with the stabilization objective.
\par
The dependence of optimal emissions over the next few decades on the choice
\par
between atmospheric stabilization and quantified-benefit endpoints can be under-
\par
stood by comparing the forms of the objective function and constraint. Under
\par
stabilization, higher near-term emissions can be offset by greater subsequent re-
\par
ductions as atmospheric CO2 approaches the stabilization level. As noted in Sec-
\par
tion 1, Wigley et al. (1996) describe several factors that contribute to making
\par
early emission reductions cost-ineffective for stabilization (CO2 sinks, technologi-
\par
cal progress, depreciation of capital stock, and discounting); these can be offset by
\par
the (very uncertain) cost of rapid change from increasing to decreasing emissions
\par
if departure from BAU is delayed too long. If the optimal stabilization level is
\par
sure to exceed 550 ppmv, uncertainty about its value does not affect the choice
\par
{$\ast$} Although they do not remark upon it, Peck and Teisberg (1994) also find the optimal marginal-
\par
abatement cost is larger in the near-term and smaller in the distant future when damages are
\par
proportional to the cubed rate rather than to the cubed level of climate change.EVALUATION ENDPOINTS AND CLIMATE POLICY 465
\par
of near-term emission path as optimal emissions for the next few decades coin-
\par
cide for higher stabilization levels. (Stabilization at 350 ppmv or below requires
\par
rapid emission reductions to return to a level that has already been exceeded, and
\par
least-cost stabilization below 550 ppmv requires departures from BAU by 2010.)
\par
In contrast, the benefit-cost approach incorporates (very uncertain) damages that
\par
occur in the near term as well as the distant future; near-term damages can be
\par
reduced by near-term emission reductions, but not by delayed reductions.
\par
The appropriate stabilization level, the climate sensitivity, and the relationship
\par
between climate change and damages are presently unknown. Consequently, an
\par
appropriate near-term emission path involves hedging, and falls somewhere be-
\par
tween the paths that would be appropriate if the stabilization level or climate
\par
sensitivity and damage function were known (Manne and Richels, 1995; Peck
\par
and Teisberg, 1993). Because the optimal paths for least-cost stabilization at CO2
\par
concentrations of 550 ppmv and above virtually coincide with the BAU emission
\par
path, optimal near-term emissions for stabilizing atmospheric concentrations will
\par
not depart from BAU if the probability that the appropriate stabilization level is
\par
less than about 550 ppmv is negligible. In contrast, the appropriate hedging policy
\par
under the benefit-cost approach, whether damages depend on the magnitude or rate
\par
of climate change, implies some near-term emission reduction from BAU.
\par
These conclusions may be sensitive to factors that have been omitted from
\par
the analysis. Political and institutional factors could make the delayed-reduction
\par
strategy for atmospheric stabilization less attractive, as it may be difficult to cred-
\par
ibly commit to a policy of imposing emission reductions several decades in the
\par
future. Moreover, there may be additional near-term benefits of policies to reduce
\par
GHG emissions, such as improved health due to reduction in airborne particulates
\par
that could accompany changes in fossil-fuel combustion (Burtraw and Toman,
\par
1997). Both benefit-cost and least-cost-stabilization perspectives imply that GHG
\par
emissions will eventually need to decline substantially relative to BAU emissions;
\par
achieving this reduction will require the development and diffusion of innovative
\par
technologies and changes in anticipated human living patterns. An important re-
\par
search issue is how the incentives to develop such changes depend on near-term
\par
policies (Grubb, 1997; Schneider and Goulder, 1997). It is likely that near-term
\par
policies to reduce emissions -- whether market incentives or restrictions on emis-
\par
sions or technologies -- would accelerate the development of changes that will
\par
reduce the cumulative cost of responding to climate change.
