\textbf{1. INTRODUCTION }Risk assessors attempting to use probabilistic approaches to describe uncertainty often find them- selves in a data-sparse situation: available data are only partially relevant to the parameter of interest, so one needs to adjust empirical distributions, use explicit judgmental distributions, or collect new data. In determining whether or not to collect additional data, whether by measurement or elicitation of ex- perts, it is useful to consider the expected value of the information (Raiffa, 1968; National Risk Council, 1996; \textbf{Presidential/Congressional }Commission on ' Department of Health Policy and Management and Center for \textbf{Risk }Analysis, School of Public Health, Harvard University, 718 Huntington Ave., Boston, Massachusetts 02115; e-mail: \textbf{JamesHammitt@Harvard.edu}
\par
Department of Physics, Harvard University, Cambridge, Massa- chusetts 02138 e-mail: \textbf{shlyakhter@physics.harard.edu 135}
\par
Risk Assessment and Risk Management, 1997). The
\par
expected value of information depends on the prior
\par
distribution used to represent current information. If
\par
the prior distribution is too narrow, the calculated
\par
expected value of information will be biased; in cases
\par
of interest to risk analysts, the bias is likely to be
\par
downward. The well-documented tendency toward
\par
overconfidence (Kahneman \textbf{\emph{et al., }}1982; Morgan and
\par
Henrion, 1990), including the neglect of potential
\par
surprise, suggests this bias may be important. This
\par
paper examines the expected value of information
\par
and suggests procedures to guard against overconfi-
\par
dence and underestimation of the expected value
\par
of information.
\par
The word ``uncertainty'' is often used without
\par
formal definition. unless otherwise noted, the term
\par
will be used here in the sense defined by Rothschild
\par
and Stiglitz (l970). Their concept applies to distribu-
\par
tions with equal expected values and can be expressed
