\textbf{2. EVIDENCE AND MODELING}
\par
\textbf{OF OVERCONFIDENCE}
\par
The importance of incorporating surprise into
\par
uncertainty modeling and analysis stems from the
\par
well-documented tendency of both experts and lay
\par
people to underestimate uncertainty in their knowl-
\par
edge of quantitative information. When asked to con-
\par
struct prior distributions for quantities that can be
\par
verified, subject matter experts and lay people often
\par
produce distributions that are far too tight. Typical
\par
results find 55-75\% of true values outside subjective
\par
interquartile ranges and 20-45\% outside central 98\%
\par
confidence regions (Alpert and Raiffa, 1982; Lich-
\par
tenstein \textbf{\emph{et }}al., 1982; Morgan and Henrion, 1990).
\par
Henrion and Fischhoff (1986) examined the
\par
history of measurements of fundamental physical
\par
constants (e.g., the speed of light). New and more
\par
precise measurements are often outside the reported
\par
error bars of the old measurements. To quantify
\par
the extent to which subsequent measurements de-
\par
part from the distributions implied by previous
\par
measurements, Henrion and Fischhoff introduced
\par
the Surprise Index \textbf{(SI), }defined as the fraction of
\par
new results falling more than 2.3 times the (old)
\par
reported standard deviation from the old result. If
\par
new measurements were randomly drawn from a
\par
normal distribution with mean and standard devia-
\par
tion equal to the old values, the SI would be 2\%.
\par
Analysis of physical measurements suggests the \textbf{SI}
\par
varies between about 10 and 40\% (Henrion and
\par
Fischhoff, 1986).
\par
In some fields, experts have been shown to pro-
\par
vide relatively well-calibrated probability judgments.
\par
The classic example is meteorology, where forecasts
\par
of precipitation probabilities and of maximum and
\par
minimum daily temperatures have been shown to be
\par
well calibrated (Murphy and Winkler, 1977). Mete-
\par
orologists benefit from experience in forecasting
\par
probabilities for a large number of similar events and
\par
receiving rapid feedback. More recently, Winkler and
\par
Poses (1993) have shown that physicians' estimates
\par
of patient survival probabilities are reasonably well-
\par
calibrated, even though physicians do not routinely
\par
provide quantitative survival probabilities. In con-
\par
trast, financial analysts have been shown to signifi-
\par
cantly overestimate corporate earnings growth
\par
(Chatfield \textbf{\emph{et }}al., 1989; Dechow and Sloan, 1997). In
\par
one of the few studies evaluating calibration in risk
\par
analysis, Hawkins and Evans (1989) found that indus-
\par
trial hygenists provided reasonably accurate esti-
\par
mates of the mean and 90th percentile of a distribu-
\par
tion of personal exposure to chemical industry
\par
workers, although they substantially overestimated
\par
median exposure.
\par
Shlyakhter (1994) and Shlyakhter \textbf{\emph{et af. }}(1994)
\par
analyzed several large historical datasets of physical
\par
measurements, population, and energy projections.
\par
They concluded that empirical distributions of mea-
