\textbf{3. THE PRIOR DISTRIBUTION INFLUENCES}
\par
\textbf{THE EXPECTED VALUE OF}
\par
\textbf{INFORMATION}
\par
One danger in neglecting the possibility of sur-
\par
prise in developing prior distributions is that the Ex-
\par
pected Value of Information (EVI) calculated using
\par
that prior may be biased downward (Hammitt, 1995).
\par
As shown below, the expected posterior probability
\par
of an event equals the prior probability (e.g., the
\par
probability that perfect information about the carcin-
\par
ogenic risk of specified exposure to a compound will
\par
reveal that the risk exceeds the 95th percentile of its
\par
prior distribution is exactly \textbf{5\%): }Consequently, if
\par
the tails of the prior distribution are too thin to repre-
\par
sent the ``true uncertainty,'' the apparent probability
\par
of learning that the parameter is far from the prior
\par
median will be ``too small.'' The EVI is the integral
\par
over all possible posterior distributions of the oppor-
\par
tunity loss prevented by improved information,
\par
weighted by the probability of that information. If
\par
posterior distributions that lead to a change in deci-
\par
sion are given too little probability, the EVI may be
\par
underestimated. Although there is no general rela-
\par
tion between overconfidence and bias in EVI, in
\par
many risk-analytic problems the bias is likely to be
\par
downward.
