\textbf{5. COMBINING INFORMATION IN A PRIOR}
\par
\textbf{DISTRIBUTION}
\par
\textbf{A }prior distribution may be based on data for a
\par
similar parameter, the informed judgment of the risk
\par
analyst or subject-matter experts, or a combination
\par
of sources. Expert judgments may be encoded infor-
\par
mally or through formal probability-elicitation meth-
\par
ods. In the usual case, it is necessary to combine
\par
information from multiple sources, either to adjust
\par
the empirical distribution of a similar parameter to
\par
account for differences between that parameter and
\par
the one of interest, or to reflect differences among
\par
multiple experts whose judgments have been elicited
\par
using formal procedures. Many procedures for com-
\par
bining information have been proposed but none are
\par
clearly superior (Cooke, 1991; Jacobs, 1995; Clemen
\par
and Winkler, 1997). Moreover, the implications of
\par
alternative procedures when one is concerned to
\par
avoid overconfidence bias associated with the neglect
\par
of potential surprise has received little attention.
\par
In the following subsections, we discuss two main
\par
classes of aggregation procedures, propose a method
\par
to adjust for overconfidence in expert judgments, and
\par
illustrate with an example using expert judgments
\par
about the magnitude of global climate change that
\par
would result from increasing atmospheric carbon di-
\par
oxide.
\par
\textbf{5.1. Theory}
\par
Dependence among experts is both central to
\par
proper combination of expert judgments and difficult
\par
to evaluate. Judgments of multiple experts about a pa-
\par
rameter can be extremely informative when those
\par
judgments are probabilistically independent, condi-
\par
tional on the ``true'' value. If, as is often the case, ex-
\par
perts share much of the knowledge relevant to estimat-
\par
ing a parameter value (e.g., a common scientific
\par
literature), the information contained in the union of
\par
multiple experts' judgments may be little more than
\par
that contained in a single expert's judgment (in effect,
\par
each expert may report an idiosyncratic perception of
\par
\textbf{145}
\par
a consensus). Clemen and Winkler (1985) provide
\par
bounds on the number of independent experts whose
\par
combined information is equivalent to that of a larger
\par
number of dependent experts.
\par
We consider two classes of algorithms for com-
\par
bining distributions across experts: weighted aver-
\par
ages (opinion pools) and Bayesian combinations
\par
(Cooke, 1991; Jacobs, 1995; Clemen and Winkler,
\par
1997). The weighted-average approach is simple, in-
\par
tuitively appealing, and can generate a wide range
\par
of combination rules. Bayesian approaches are moti-
\par
vated by treating each expert's judgment as data to
\par
be used in updating a prior distribution. A difficulty
\par
is that Bayesian methods require specifying the likeli-
\par
hood function or distribution of expert judgments
\par
conditional on the value of the uncertain parameter
\par
of interest. The limited available evidence on relative
\par
performance of alternative combination methods
\par
suggests that simple averages often perform nearly as
\par
well as the theoretically superior Bayesian methods
\par
(Clemen and Winkler, 1997).
\par
A weighted average provides a transparent
\par
mechanism for representing unequal degrees of ex-
\par
pertise. The weights can be assessed by the participat-
\par
ing experts themselves or by others; they may also
\par
be used as sampling weights to represent the propor-
\par
tion of the universe of relevant experts with similar
\par
views. A weighted average of the experts' probability
\par
density functions (a linear opinion pool) can be moti-
\par
vated by the assumption that one of the experts'
\par
distributions is ``correct,'' but it is not known which
\par
one; the weights represent the relative probability
\par
that each expert's distribution is ``correct.''
\par
The simple weighted average can be generalized
\par
to provide a broad set of combination rules with
\par
potentially attractive properties, although it does not
\par
allow for convenient representation of dependence
\par
among experts' judgments. Following Cooke (1991),
\par
consider the case in which \textbf{\emph{m }}experts provide subjec-
\par
tive probabilities for each of \textbf{\emph{n }}events; let \textbf{\emph{pij }}denote
\par
expert \textbf{\emph{i's }}probability of event \textbf{\emph{j . }}A set of weights \textbf{\textbraceleft wi\textbraceright}
\par
is assigned to account for differences in expertise or
\par
credibility among experts. Define the elementary r-
\par
norm-weighted mean
\par
and the r-norm probability
