\par
For all values of r, the r-norm probability has the
\par
``zero-preservation property'': if all experts assign
\par
probability zero to event \emph{j, }then \emph{Pr(j) }= 0. For r =
\par
0, if any expert assigns probability zero to event \emph{j,}
\par
then \emph{Po(j) }= 0 (Cooke, 1991).
\par
An alternative approach to combining expert
\par
judgments is based on Bayes' rule. Beginning with a
\par
(possibly diffuse) prior distribution, the analyst treats
\par
each expert's distribution as new information and
\par
updates his prior using Bayes' rule. The details of
\par
the updating depend on the analyst's assessment of
\par
the likelihood function. The likelihood represents the
\par
probability that each expert will give the judgment
\par
he provides as a function of the underlying state of
\par
nature, and consequently incorporates information
\par
about the relative quality of experts' judgments (e.g.,
\par
bias and overconfidence) as well as dependencies
\par
among the experts. The likelihood function provides
\par
a much more discriminating approach to characteriz-
\par
ing the quality of an expert's judgment than does the
\par
single weight permitted by the weighted-average ap-
\par
proaches.
\par
Several models for the likelihood have been ex-
\par
amined in the literature (Cooke, 1991; Jacobs, 1995;
\par
Clemen and Winkler, 1997). The geometric-mean
\par
combination \emph{Po }can be derived from a model that
\par
treats each expert's assessment of a parameter as
\par
equal to the true parameter value multiplied by a
\par
random error (Mosleh and Apostolakis, 1986;
\par
Cooke, 1991).
\par
Jouini and Clemen (1996) proposed a copula-
\par
based approach to combining distributions. \textbf{A }copula
\par
is a mathematical function that can be used to repre-
\par
sent probabilistic dependence when coupling mar-
\par
ginal probability distributions (the experts' judg-
\par
ments) into a multivariate distribution (the joint
\par
likelihood of the experts' judgments). This approach
\par
provides a flexible method for representing depen-
\par
dence among experts without restricting the form of
\par
the experts' distributions. In the following subsection,
\par
we describe the copula-based approach and use it to
\par
combine expert distributions.
\par
Two properties that have been advocated for
\par
rules to combine expert distributions are the ``mar-
\par
ginalization'' and ``external Bayesianity'' properties.
\par
Each property requires that the distribution obtained
\par
by combining expert judgments be invariant to speci-
\par
fied procedural choices. However, most reasonable
\par
combination methods violate one if not both of
\par
these properties.
\par
Marginalization requires that the combined
\par
probability of an event A be independent of whether
\par
the analyst combines the experts' assessed probabili-
\par
ties of A or \textbf{sums }the combination of the experts'
\par
assessed probabilities of \textbf{s }{$>$} 1 mutually exclusive
\par
and collectively exhaustive subsets of \textbf{A. }The r-norm
\par
probability satisfies marginalization only for r = 1.
\par
Cooke (1991) argues that marginalization is a
\par
normatively important property, offering the follow-
\par
ing example: Two (equally credible) experts both
\par
assign probability 0.8 to the event that an \textbf{old }flash-
\par
light will not work. Using the geometric-mean combi-
\par
nation \emph{Po, }the combined probability that the flashlight
\par
will not work is also 0.8. Assuming only two possible
\par
(mutually exclusive) failure modes, dead battery and
\par
corroded contacts, the probability of failure based on
\par
combining the assessed probabilities of each failure
\par
mode need not equal 0.8 if the experts disagree about
\par
the relative probabilities of the failure modes. If the
\par
experts assign probabilities (0.7, 0.1) and (0.1, 0.7),
\par
respectively, to the two failure modes, the combined
\par
probability that the flashlight will not work is \textbf{0.73}
\par
(the probabilities of dead battery and of corroded
\par
contacts are each [0.1 - 0.7]1/2- that of no failure is
\par
[0.2 - \textbf{0.2]1/2). }Because of the zero-preservation prop-
\par
erty, if the experts assign probabilities (0.8, 0) and
\par
(0,0.8) to the failure modes, the combined aggregate
\par
probability that the flashlight will not work is zero,
\par
as the combined probability of each failure mode is
\par
zero (even though the experts agree that the marginal
\par
probability is 0.8).
\par
In response to Cooke, one might argue that the
\par
experts' disagreement about failure modes provides
\par
important information about the credibility of each
\par
expert's probability that the flashlight will not work.
\par
Strong disagreement about the probability of the in-
