\par
The external Bayesianity property applies to the
\par
case where one wishes to update a distribution when
\par
new data (or additional experts' judgments) are ob-
\par
tained. It requires that the same posterior distribution
\par
result whether one updates the prior distribution ob-
\par
tained by combining the experts' prior distributions,
\par
or updates each expert's distribution and then com-
\par
bines the resulting posterior distributions. Of the \textbf{\emph{r-}}
\par
norm probabilities, only \emph{Po }is externally Bayesian
\par
(Cooke, 1991). Lindley (1985, 1988) argues that this
\par
property is also not reasonably required of a proce-
\par
dure, but again if the combination rule is not exter-
\par
nally Bayesian the analyst will need to justify the
\par
selected method for updating a combined distri-
\par
bution.
\par
Acknowledging the possibilities of expert over-
\par
confidence and surprise suggests evaluating the ef-
\par
fects of combination rules on tails and other subsets
\par
of the parameter domain assigned low probability.
\par
As already noted, the geometric-mean combination
\par
\emph{Po }as well as other Bayesian combinations exhibit an
\par
extreme form of the zero-preservation property: if
\par
any expert assigns probability zero to some range of
\par
parameter values, the combined distribution does so
\par
as well. If experts' distributions are represented as
\par
triangular, uniform, or other forms that assign posi-
\par
tive probability to only a finite interval, the interval
\par
to which no expert assigns zero probability may be
\par
small or nonexistent. This possibility represents a
\par
limitation of the Bayesian approaches but also high-
\par
lights the danger in assigning zero probability to
\par
events that are not ``impossible.'' We caution analysts
\par
to avoid assigning zero probability to parameter val-
\par
ues that are not logically outside the bounds of the
\par
parameter (e.g., mass concentration ratios may be
\par
assigned zero probability outside the interval \textbf{[O- }11).
\par
\textbf{147}
\par
In addition, to reflect possible neglect of surprise
\par
events, it may be useful to extend the tails of experts'
\par
distributions as discussed in Sect. 2. (The results of
\par
extending the tails of experts' distributions before
\par
combining them and extending the tails of the com-
\par
bined distribution will not, in general, coincide.) The
\par
linear opinion pool may be preferred to a Bayesian
\par
combination because it represents greater uncer-
\par
tainty in the sense that its support (the set of parame-
\par
ter values assigned positive probability) is a superset
\par
of the support of a Bayesian combination. This fol-
\par
lows because Bayesian combinations assign zero
\par
probability to parameter values for which any experts
\par
give zero probability, but the linear opinion pool
\par
assigns zero probability only to parameter values for
\par
which all experts give zero probability.
