\textbf{5.2. Example: Climate Sensitivity}
\par
Morgan and Keith (1995) elicited probability
\par
distributions for a number of parameters related to
\par
global climate change from 16 recognized experts in
\par
climate science. The elicitations were conducted us-
\par
ing an extensive, formal process including pre-testing
\par
the elicitation instrument, advance distribution of
\par
background materials to experts, and day-long inter-
\par
views. For illustration, we consider the experts' judg-
\par
ments for a central parameter in assessing the risk
\par
of climate change, the ``climate sensitivity'' \textbf{\emph{ATh, }}de-
\par
fined as the equilibrium increase in global annual
\par
mean surface temperature that would result from a
\par
doubling of atmospheric carbon dioxide concentra-
\par
tion from its pre-industrial level.
\par
Morgan and Keith (1995) caution that it may
\par
not be appropriate to combine distributions across
\par
experts and that the diversity among the experts'
\par
distributions itself conveys important information
\par
about the degree of consensus in the expert commu-
\par
nity. In decision making, it may be better to analyze
\par
the problem using each expert's distribution individu-
\par
ally to determine whether differences among experts'
\par
judgments are important to the decision and whether
\par
decisions that are robust to differences among ex-
\par
perts' judgments can be identified (Morgan and Hen-
\par
