\par
Probabilistic independence appears to be an untena-
\par
ble assumption in this case, since most of the experts
\par
have participated in developing the IPCC evaluations
\par
and all are familiar with (and contributors to) the
\par
literature summarized by IPCC. Even when a rather
\par
high degree of dependence is assumed, however, the
\par
combination of 16 or 17 expert judgments yields a
\par
much narrower range than most of the experts pro-
\par
vided individually, reflecting the statistical power of
\par
multiple observations (even when the observations
\par
are not independent). The broad uncertainty interval
\par
produced by the linear opinion pool is only consistent
\par
with the Bayesian approach if an extraordinarily high
\par
degree of dependence is assumed.
\par
Several alternative approaches to obtaining a
\par
distribution for climate sensitivity exist. For example,
\par
uncertainty about climate sensitivity can be examined
\par
by estimating \textbf{\emph{ATk }}from multiple runs of general cir-
\par
culation and other models representing some of the
\par
processes through which \textbf{C02 }concentrations affect
\par
global climate. If one elicited judgmental multivariate
\par
distributions about the appropriate values of parame-
\par
ters to be used in such models and then derived the
\par
uncertainty in climate sensitivity by propagating
\par
these parameter uncertainties through a set of mod-
\par
els, the result would differ from the results obtained
\par
by combining the distributions of climate sensitivity
\par
produced by the modekg These procedures would
\par
yield different results, even if the combination rule
\par
satisfied the marginalization property, because the
\par
climate models are nonlinear in most parameters.
\par
The expert judgments for AT, are based in part on the results
\par
of such model simulations.
\par
Alternatively, expert judgments could be character-
\par
ized in terms of a climate-feedback parameter (Wig-
\par
ley and Schlesinger, 1985). The feedback parameter
\par
is proportional to the reciprocal of \textbf{\emph{ATk; }}if the experts'
\par
distributions for \textbf{\emph{ATk }}were transformed to distribu-
\par
tions for the feedback parameter, combined, then
\par
transformed to yield a distribution for \textbf{\emph{ATkr }}the re-
\par
sults would also differ from the results of directly
\par
combining the expert distributions for \textbf{\emph{ATk.}}
\par
The appropriate level of detail at which to obtain
\par
expert judgment is unknown (and surely depends on
\par
context). There is some evidence supporting the view
\par
that experts should provide judgmental estimates
\par
about rather disaggregated components of a problem
\par
that can be aggregated using an appropriate model
\par
(Clemen and Winkler, 1997).
\par
\textbf{6. CONCLUSION}
\par
The assessed expected value of information de-
\par
pends on the prior distribution used to represent cur-
\par
rent information. Although exceptions can be readily
\par
generated, it is typically the case in risk assessment
\par
that the narrower the prior distribution, the smaller
\par
the assessed expected value of information. The well-
\par
documented tendency of individuals to be overcon-
\par
fident in summarizing their information, including
\par
particularly the tendency to underestimate the proba-
\par
bility of surprise, can lead to large underestimates of
\par
the expected value of information. Such underesti-
\par
mates may be reduced by adopting prior distributions
\par
designed to mitigate the effect of neglecting potential
\par
surprise, such as a long-tailed compound distribution
\par
15396924, 1999, 1, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1539-6924.1999.tb00395.x by Universit\"ats-U Landesbibliothek Bonn, Wiley Online Library on [02/05/2023]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License\textbf{Information and the Probability of Surprise }calibrated to previous experience in the relevant
\par
domain.
\par
In addition to uncertainty about one or more
\par
parameter values, the expected value of information
\par
depends on other characteristics of the problem, in-
\par
cluding the available decision options, research op-
\par
portunities, and the resulting payoffs. Important fac-
\par
tors may be heuristically represented as the
\par
informativeness of available research opportunities,
\par
the promise that research can be sufficiently persua-
\par
sive to result in a decision that differs from the one
\par
that would be made in the absence of the research,
\par
and the relevance of uncertainty about a parameter
\par
to determination of which of the available decisions
\par
is superior.
\par
In attempting to incorporate all the relevant evi-
\par
dence, analysts will often wish to combine information
\par
from multiple sources, including expert judgment. Nu-
\par
merous procedures for combining information across
\par
experts and other sources have been proposed but
\par
there is no consensus regarding the best procedure or
\par
even the relevant attributes for making that determi-
\par
nation. The most appropriate procedure is surely de-
\par
pendent on the context of the problem and the state
\par
of the scientific theory, modeling, and data available
\par
for estimating important quantities.
\par
The two prominent, simple procedures exam-
\par
ined, Bayesian combination (using a copula to repre-
\par
sent dependence among experts) and linear opinion
\par
pool, yield substantially different results and differ in
\par
their sensitivity to details of the experts' distributions.
\par
The linear opinion pool is less sensitive to individual
\par
distributions, and its results appear to be inconsistent
\par
with the theoretically justified Bayesian approach ex-
\par
cept under the extreme assumption of perfect depen-
\par
dence among experts. A potential weakness of the
\par
Bayesian approach is its extreme sensitivity to ex-
\par
perts who assign zero probability to events that other
\par
experts view as possible; this limitation can be readily
\par
mitigated by extending the tails of expert distribu-
\par
tions to reflect possible overconfidence.
\par
Recognizing the possibility of surprise and ade-
\par
quately incorporating it in a risk or uncertainty analy-
\par
sis remains a challenge. The expected value of infor-
\par
mation is likely to be more sensitive to the probability
\par
of surprising outcomes than is the optimal decision
\par
under uncertainty, which suggests that research deci-
\par
sions in particular may benefit from thoughtful analy-
\par
sis of the characteristics of potential surprises. Analy-
\par
sis of possible surprises is likely to remain highly
\par
imperfect, however. By definition, potential surprises
\par
will remain elusive.
