\par
Risk analysis often depends on complex, computer-based models to describe links between policies
\par
(e.g., required emission-control equipment) and consequences (e.g., probabilities of adverse health
\par
effects). Appropriate specification of many model aspects is uncertain, including details of the
\par
model structure; transport, reaction-rate, and other parameters; and application-specific inputs such
\par
as pollutant-release rates. Because these uncertainties preclude calculation of the precise conse-
\par
quences of a policy, it is important to characterize the plausible range of effects. In principle, a
\par
probability distribution function for the effects can be constructed using Monte Carlo analysis, but
\par
the combinatorics of multiple uncertainties and the often high cost of model runs quickly exhaust
\par
available resources. This paper presents and applies a method to choose sets of input conditions
\par
(scenarios) that efficiently represent knowledge about the joint probability distribution of inputs.
\par
A simple score function approximately relating inputs to a policy-relevant output-in this case,
\par
globally averaged stratospheric ozone depletion-is developed. The probability density function
\par
for the score-function value is analytically derived from a subjective joint probability density for
\par
the inputs. Scenarios are defined by selected quantiles of the score function. Using this method,
\par
scenarios can be systematically selected in terms of the approximate probability distribution func-
\par
tion for the output of concern, and probability intervals \textbf{for }the joint effect of the inputs can be
\par
readily constructed.
\par
\textbf{KEY WORDS: }Sensitivity analysis; scenarios; modeling; ozone depletion; uncertainty.
\par
\textbf{1. INTRODUCTION}
\par
Risk analysis attempts to incorporate available
\par
knowledge about the biological, chemical, physical, and
\par
economic processes that link public policies to environ-
\par
mental release of pollutants (or other factors) and ulti-
\par
mately to human health and ecosystem consequences. In
\par
evaluating the health risk associated with environmental
\par
release of an industrial pollutant, for example, risk analysis
\par
must consider: the quantities released; transport, chem-
\par
ical, and physical transformations; human-exposure
\par
pathways; exposed populations; physiological and met-
\par
abolic processes; and dose-response relationships.
\par
