\par
emission-control or other policy necessarily depend on
\par
a large number of assumptions about parameter values
\par
and model structure. Even the most elaborate computer
\par
models rely on mathematical idealizations of the real
\par
processes. Consequently, risk analysis cannot describe
\par
the precise consequences of a policy; it should seek in-
