stead to characterize the distribution of plausible con-
\par
sequences and their likelihoods.
\par
Thorough sensitivity analysis is impeded by the
\par
``curse of dimensionality.'' The space of all possible
\par
parameter values and model structures is typically of
\par
high dimension.2 Analysts must choose which regions
\par
to examine with available resources. The time and ex-
\par
pense required to run complex models may further limit
\par
the number of runs that can be undertaken. Conse-
\par
quently, methods to choose alternative scenarios (sets of
\par
input parameters) that maximize the information per model
\par
run are of value.3
