CFC-113 and Halon use are expected to grow more
\par
rapidly than that of other PODs; CFC-12 use is expected
\par
to decline relative to the world economy, although not
\par
in absolute quantity. This distinction reflects a typical
\par
pattern of chemical use: relatively recently marketed
\par
``specialty'' chemicals, produced in limited quantities,
\par
grow rapidly \textbf{as }they are adopted in applications \emph{to }which
\par
they are well suited. Older chemicals, produced in larger
\par
quantities, may not grow as quickly because they have
\par
already been adopted in the applications to which they
\par
are best suited. Standard deviations are similar for all of
\par
the PODs except CFC-113 and the Halons. CFC-113 use
\par
is comparatively uncertain because of possible regula-
\par
tions affecting waste-solvent disposal and substantial
\par
substitutability with other solvents. Halon use is espe-
\par
cially uncertain because these compounds are relatively
\par
new and additional applications may be devised.
