  pages = {90--105},
  publisher = {Schattauer GmbH},
  doi = {10.1055/s-0038-1634867},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Toward Normative Expert Systems: Part I
\par
The Pathfinder Project
\par
David E. Heckerman
\par
Departments of Computer Science and Pathology
\par
University of Southern California
\par
HMR 204, 2025 Zonal Ave
\par
Los Angeles, CA 90033
\par
{$<$}dheck@sumex-aim.stanford.edu{$>$}
\par
Eric J. Horvitz
\par
Palo Alto Laboratory
\par
Rockwell International Science Center
\par
444 High Street
\par
Palo Alto, California 94301
\par
{$<$}horvitz@sumex-aim.stanford.edu{$>$}
\par
Bharat N. Nathwani
\par
Department of Pathology
\par
University of Southern California
\par
HMR 204, 2025 Zonal Ave
\par
Los Angeles, CA 90033
\par
To appear in Methods of Information in Medicine, 1992
\par
1Abstract
\par
Pathfinder is an expert system that assists surgical pathologists with the diagnosis of
\par
lymph-node diseases. The program is one of a growing number of normative expert
\par
systems that use probability and decisiontheory to acquire, represent, manipulate, and
\par
explain uncertain medical knowledge. In this article, we describe Pathfinder and our
\par
research in uncertain-reasoning paradigms that was stimulated by the development of
\par
the program. We discuss limitations with early decision-theoretic methods for reason-
\par
ing under uncertainty and our initial attempts to use non-decision-theoretic methods.
\par
Then, we describe experimental and theoretical results that directed us to return to
\par
reasoning methods based in probability and decision theory.
\par
Keywords: expert systems, decision making, diagnosis, probability theory, decision
\par
theory, artificial intelligence, pathology
\par
21 Introduction
\par
Decision-theoretic or normative expert systems have the potential to provide better decision
\par
support than do traditional expert systems in problem areas or domains where the accurate
\par
management of uncertainty is important. This potential for improvement arises because
\par
people, including experts, make mistakes when they make decisions under uncertainty. That
\par
is, people often deviate from the rules of decision theory, which provides a set of compelling
\par
principles or desiderata for how people should behave when reasoning or making decisions
\par
under uncertainty. Decision theory includes the rules of probability and the principle that a
\par
person should always choose the alternative that maximizes his expected utility.
\par
Traditional expert systems provide decision support by mimicking the recommendations
\par
of experts. They do so by managing uncertainty with non-decision-theoretic methods. Such
\par
systems are valuable, because they provide important information to a nonexpert who is
\par
confronted with a confusing decision, and because they offer reminders to users who may be
\par
stressed or fatigued. Nonetheless, they tend to duplicate the errors made by experts.
\par
In contrast, normative expert systems use decision theory to manage uncertainty. The
\par
word ``normative'' comes from decision analysts and cognitive psychologists who emphasize
\par
the importance of distinguishing between normative behavior, which is what we do when
\par
we follow the desiderata of decision theory, and descriptive behavior, which is what we do
\par
when unaided by these desiderata. By encoding expert knowledge in a decision-theoretic
\par
framework, we can reduce errors in reasoning, and thereby build expert systems that offer
\par
recommendations of higher quality.
\par
In this article, we describe Pathfinder, a normative expert system that assists surgical
\par
pathologists with the diagnosis of lymph-node diseases [1, 2]. The Pathfinder project began
\par
in 1983 as a joint project among researchers at Stanford University (David Heckerman, Eric
\par
Horvitz, and Larry Fagan) and the University of Southern California (Bharat Nathwani---the
\par
primary pathology expert---and Keung-Chi Ng) [3]. Currently, a commercial derivative of
\par
Pathfinder, called Intellipath, is being used by practicing pathologists and by pathologists
\par
in training as an educational tool [4].
\par
Also in this article, we discuss the importance of the proper management of uncertainty
\par
for diagnosis of lymph-node diseases; and we discuss our research in uncertain-reasoning
\par
paradigms that was stimulated by the development of Pathfinder. In particular, we examine
\par
practical limitations with early decision-theoretic methods for reasoning under uncertainty
\par
and our initial attempts to overcome these limitations through the use of non-decision-
\par
theoretic reasoning paradigms. Then, we describe experiments with these non-decision-
\par
theoretic approaches as well as theoretical analyses that directed us to return to a method-
\par
ology based in probability and decision theory. In the companion to this article, we describe
\par
the decision-theoretic representations that we developed to make practical the construction
\par
of a normative version of Pathfinder.
\par
2 Diagnosis in Surgical Pathology and Pathfinder
\par
Surgical pathologists perform diagnosis primarily by examining sections of tissue microscop-
\par
ically. Sometimes, pathologists also incorporate clinical, radiology, and laboratory informa-
\par
3tion, and examine tissue with expensive tests derived from immunology, microbiology, and
\par
cell-kinetics research. Based on this information, the pathologist provides a diagnosis to the
\par
surgeons and oncologists who participate in the patient's treatment. That is, the pathologist
\par
tells these physicians, ``the patient has disease x.''
\par
The well-being of patients depends strongly on the accuracy of the pathologist's diag-
\par
nosis. In the case of lymph-node diagnosis, for example, let us suppose that the patient
\par
has Hodgkin's disease, a malignant disease, but that the pathologist makes a diagnosis of
\par
mononucleosis, a benign disease that can resemble Hodgkin's disease. In this situation, the
\par
patient's chance of death is significantly greater than it would have been had the diagno-
\par
sis been correct, because he does not receive immediate treatment for his malignancy. In
\par
contrast, let us suppose that the patient has mononucleosis, and that the pathologist makes
\par
a diagnosis of Hodgkin's disease. In this case, the patient likely will undergo expensive,
\par
painful, and debilitating treatment, to be ``cured,'' only because he never had the malignant
\par
disease in the first place.
\par
A general pathologist performs diagnosis on tissue sections from all parts of the body.
\par
When a general pathologist has difficulty with diagnosis, he frequently refers the case to a
\par
subspecialist, who has expertise in the diagnosis of a particular tissue type. This referral
\par
process usually incurs both a delay in diagnosis and an extra cost. Sometimes, the delay
\par
in diagnosis is unacceptable, and the pathologist cannot refer the case to a subspecialist.
\par
For example, surgeons often rely on pathologists for the timely diagnosis of disease in frozen
\par
tissue taken from patients under anesthesia [5, 6].
\par
The subspecialty of lymph-node diagnosis is one of the most difficult areas in surgical
\par
pathology [7, 8, 9, 10]. For example, one multisite oncology study analyzed almost 9000
\par
cases of malignant lymphoma. The study found that although experts show agreement
\par
with one another, the diagnoses rendered by general pathologists for certain diseases had
\par
to be changed by expert lymph-node pathologists in as many as 65 percent of the cases
\par
[10]. Our goal in building Pathfinder is to close the wide gap between the quality of lymph-
\par
node diagnoses made by general pathologists and those made by subspecialists. We hope to
\par
increase the accuracy of in-house pathology diagnoses, to reduce the frequency of referrals,
\par
and to assist pathologists with intraoperative diagnosis when there is insufficient time for
\par
expert consultation.
\par
Pathologists have difficulty with diagnosis for two reasons. First, they may misrecognize
\par
or fail to recognize microscopic features. Second, they may combine evidence inaccurately
\par
to form a diagnosis. The second problem arises because the pathologist must consider an
\par
enormous number of features and diseases, and because the relationships among diseases
\par
and features are uncertain. Most of Pathfinder research has concentrated on the evidence-
\par
combination problem. That is, we have worked to develop an expert system that can help
\par
pathologists cope with the many uncertain relationships in the diagnosis of lymph-node
\par
pathology. Indeed, Pathfinder reasons about more than 60 diseases that can invade the
\par
lymph node (25 benign diseases, 9 Hodgkin's lymphomas, 18 non-Hodgkin's lymphomas, and
\par
10 metastatic diseases), using more than 130 microscopic, clinical, laboratory, immunologic,
\par
and molecular-biologic features. Similarly, in this article, we concentrate on the problem of
\par
managing uncertainty in large domains. Nevertheless, as we mention in Section 9, we also
\par
have addressed the feature-recognition problem.
\par
43 A Pathfinder Dialog
\par
In rendering a diagnosis, a pathologist (1) identifies and quantifies features; (2) constructs a
\par
differential diagnosis, a set of diseases consistent with the observations; and (3) decides what
\par
additional features to evaluate and what costly tests to employ to narrow the differential
\par
diagnosis. He repeats these steps until he has observed all useful features. This procedure is
\par
called the hypothetico-deductiveapproach [11, 12, 13, 14]. Cognitive psychologists have found
\par
that physicians frequently employ this approach in performing clinical diagnosis [12, 14].
\par
Pathfinder uses this same method, summarized in Figure 1, to assist pathologists with
\par
their task of diagnosis. Associated with each feature are two or more mutually exclusive and
\par
exhaustive instances. For example, the feature NECROSIS is associated with the instances
\par
ABSENT, PRESENT, and PROMINENT.The Pathfinder system allows a user to report instances
\par
for one or more salient features of a lymph-node section. Given these feature--instance pairs,
\par
the system displays a differential diagnosis ordered by likelihood of diseases. In response
\par
to a query from the user, Pathfinder recommends a set of features that are the most cost
\par
effective for narrowing the differential diagnosis. The pathologist can answer one or more
\par
of the recommended questions. This process continues until the differential diagnosis is a
\par
single disease, there are no additional tests or questions, or a pathologist determines that
\par
the informational benefits are not worth the costs of further observations or tests.
\par
The operation of the latest version of Pathfinder is illustrated by the set of screen photos
\par
in Figure 2. Figure 2(a) shows the initial Pathfinder screen. The FEATURE CATEGORY
\par
window displays the categories of features that are known to the system, the OBSERVED
\par
FEATURES window displays feature--instance pairs that will be observed by the pathologist,
\par
and the DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS window displays the list of possible diseases and their
\par
probabilities. The probabilities in Figure 2(a) are the prior probabilities of disease---the
\par
probabilities for disease given only that a patient's lymph node has been removed and is
\par
being examined.
\par
If the user selects (double-clicks) the feature category SPHERICAL FEATURES, then
\par
Pathfinder displays a list of features for that category. To enter a particular feature, the
\par
user double-clicks on that feature, and then selects one of the mutually exclusive and ex-
\par
haustive instances for that feature. For example, Figure 2(b) shows what happens when the
\par
user selects the feature F \% AREA (percent area of the lymph-node section that is occupied
\par
by follicles). In the figure, a third window appears that lists the instances for this feature:
\par
NA (not applicable), 1--10\%, 11--50\%, 51--75\%, 76--90\%, and {$>$}90\%. Figure 2(c) shows the result
\par
of selecting the last instance for this feature. In particular, the feature--instance F \% AREA:
\par
{$>$}90\% appears in the middle column, and the differential diagnosis is revised, based on this
\par
observation.
\par
As we mentioned, the user can continue to enter any number of features of his own
\par
selection. Figure 2(d) shows the Pathfinder screen after the user has reported that follicles
\par
are in a back-to-back arrangement and show prominent polarity. Alternatively, the user
\par
can ask the program to recommend additional features for observation. Figure 2(e) shows
\par
that the most cost-effective feature to evaluate, given the current differential diagnosis, is
\par
monocytoid cells. If the user observes that monocytoid cells are prominent, then we obtain
\par
the differential diagnosis in Figure 2(f). In this case, the four features in the middle column
\par
have narrowed the differential diagnosis to a single disease: the early phase of AIDS.
\par
