\par
Figure 1: Hypothetico-deductive reasoning in Pathfinder. First, the pathologist reports
\par
instances of salient features to the system. The system then constructs a differential
\par
diagnosis---a list of hypotheses that are consistent with the observations, and an assignment
\par
of likelihood to each such hypothesis. Next, the system analyzes the current differential di-
\par
agnosis to identify the most useful features for the pathologist to observe. The process cycles
\par
until the differential diagnosis is narrowed to a single disease, there are no additional tests
\par
or questions, or the pathologist determines that the informational benefits are not worth the
\par
costs of further observations or tests.
