84 Decision-Theoretic Computations in Pathfinder
\par
Both an early version and the latest version of Pathfinder employ decision-theoretic compu-
\par
tations to assist pathologists with diagnosis. In this section, we examine these computations.
\par
In particular, we examine how Pathfinder (1) uses probabilistic inference to generate a dif-
\par
ferential diagnosis, (2) uses decision theory to recommend a diagnosis, and (3) uses decision
\par
theory to recommend features for observation. First, however, let us discuss some funda-
\par
mentals of probability and decision theory.
\par
4.1 Probability Theory
\par
Probability theory has roots, more than 3 centuries ago, in the work of Bernoulli, Laplace,
\par
Fermat, and Pascal [15]. The theory describes how to infer the probability of one event from
\par
the probability of related events. The prevalent conception of the probability of some event
\par
xis that it is a measure of the frequency with which xoccurs, when we repeat many times
\par
an experiment that has x as a possible outcome. A more general notion, however, is that
\par
the probability of x represents the degree of belief held by a person that the event x will
\par
occur in a single experiment. If a person assigns a probability of 1 to x, then he believes
\par
with certainty that xwill occur. If he assigns a probability of 0 to x, then he believes with
\par
certainty that xwill not happen. If he assigns a probability of between 0 and 1 to x, then
\par
he is to some degree unsure about whether or not xwill occur.
\par
The interpretation of a probability as a frequency in a series of repeated experiments
\par
traditionally is referred to as the objective or frequentist interpretation. In contrast, the
\par
interpretation of a probability as a degree of belief is called the subjective or Bayesian in-
\par
terpretation, in honor of the Reverend Thomas Bayes, a scientist from the mid-1700s who
\par
helped to pioneer the theory of probabilistic inference [16, 15]. Both interpretations follow
\par
the same set of mathematical rules.
\par
In the Bayesian interpretation, a probability or belief will always depend on the state
\par
of knowledge of the person who provides that probability. For example, if we were to give
\par
someone a coin, he would likely assign a probability of 1/2 to the event that the coin would
\par
show heads on the next toss. If, however, we convinced that person that the coin was
\par
weighted in favor of heads, he would assign a higher probability to the event. Thus, we write
\par
the probability of xas p(x\textbar{$\xi$}), which is read as the probability of xgiven {$\xi$}. The symbol {$\xi$}
\par
represents the state of knowledge or background knowledge of the person who provides the
\par
probability.
\par
The conception of probability as a measure of personal belief is central to research on
\par
the use of probability and decision theory for representing and reasoning with expert knowl-
\par
edge in computer-based reasoning systems. There is usually no alternative to acquiring from
\par
experts the bulk of probabilistic information used in an expert system. For example, there
\par
are more than 14 thousand probabilities in the latest version of Pathfinder; and some of
\par
these probabilities are on the order of 10-6
\par
. Thus, performing the experiments necessary
\par
to determine objective probabilities for Pathfinder would entail much time and great ex-
\par
pense. Fortunately, when experimental data is available, the Bayesian approach provides a
\par
mechanism for expert systems to update their probabilities, given this data [17, 18, 19].
\par
94.2 Decision Theory and Utility Assessment
\par
Decision theory extends the Bayesian interpretation of probability theory, and prescribes how
\par
a decision maker should choose among a set of alternativesor actions, given his utilityor pref-
\par
erence for each possible outcome and his belief that each outcome will occur. In particular,
\par
decision theory includes the rules of probability theory and the maximum-expected-utility
\par
(MEU) principle, which states that a decision maker should always choose the alternative
\par
that maximizes his expected utility [20].
\par
Utility assessment is nontrivial and is the subject of many debates. In this article, we
\par
mention only a few important points concerning utility assessment for Pathfinder. The
\par
interested reader should consult more general discussions by Keeney and Raiffa [21], McNeil
\par
et al. [22], and Howard [23].
\par
For each disease pair (dj,dk) in Pathfinder, we encode the utility u(dj,dk), which sum-
\par
marizes the preferences of the decision maker for the situation in which a patient has disease
\par
dj, but is diagnosed as having disease dk. Factors that influence such preferences include
\par
the length of the patient's expected life, the pain associated with treatment and with the
\par
disease itself, the psychological trauma to the patient and his family, and the monetary cost
\par
associated with treatment and with disability.
\par
An important consideration in the assessment of these (and any other) utilities is: Who
\par
is the decision maker? From our perspective, a pathologist is only a provider of information.
\par
Thus, the u(dj,dk) in the utility model of a computer-based diagnostic system should reflect
\par
the patient's preferences. For example, consider the situation where a pathologist believes,
\par
after reviewing a case, that the probability of the benign infection mononucleosis is 0.9,
\par
and that the probability of Hodgkin's disease is 0.1. Should the patient be treated for
\par
Hodgkin's disease now, or should he wait for more definitive diagnostic signs to develop?
\par
As we discussed, delaying treatment of Hodgkin's disease decreases the chances of long-term
\par
survival if the patient has this condition. On the other hand, the treatment for Hodgkin's
\par
disease is highly invasive. The decision about therapy will depend on how the patient
\par
feels about the alternative outcomes. Different patients may have dramatically different
\par
preferences.
\par
As we discuss in Sections 4.4 and 4.5, differences in patient preferences can in principle
\par
affect recommendations made by an expert system for diagnosis. Thus, utility assessment
\par
poses a fundamental problem to any researcher who wants to develop such expert systems.
\par
Specifically, whenever a patient case is processed by an expert system, the system or a
\par
decision analyst should assess the utilities of that patient and provide these utilities to the
\par
system. Such utility assessment would be extremely time consuming and expensive. As
\par
we see in Sections 4.4 and 4.5, however, only Pathfinder's diagnostic recommendations and
\par
not its recommendations for evidence gathering are sensitive to patient utilities. Thus,
\par
by allowing Pathfinder to make only evidence-gathering recommendations, we render the
\par
program's recommendations insensitive to patient utilities. We can therefore encode in
\par
Pathfinder the utilities u(dj,dk) from one representative patient.
\par
To construct Pathfinder's utility model, we assessed the utilities of Bharat Nathwani,
\par
the primary Pathfinder expert. We found it relatively easy to assess his utilities, because he
\par
was familiar with the ramifications of many specific correct and incorrect diagnoses.
\par
Another important consideration in utility assessment is the wide range of severities as-
\par
10sociated with outcomes. For example, if a patient has a viral infection and is incorrectly
\par
diagnosed as having cat-scratch disease---a disease caused by an organism that is killed with
\par
antibiotics---the consequences are not severe. In fact, the only non-negligible consequence
\par
is that the patient will take antibiotics unnecessarily for several weeks. If a patient has
\par
Hodgkin's disease and is incorrectly diagnosed as having mononucleosis, however, the con-
\par
sequences are often lethal.
\par
It is important for us to measure preferences across such a wide range, because sometimes
\par
we must balance a large chance of a small loss with a small chance of a large loss. For
\par
example, even though the probability that a patient has syphilis is small---say, 0.001---
\par
treatment with antibiotics may be appropriate, because the patient may prefer the harmful
\par
effects of antibiotics to the small chance of the harmful effects of untreated disease.
\par
Early attempts to assess preferences for both minor and major outcomes in the same unit
\par
of measurement were fraught with paradoxes. For example, in a linear willingness-to-pay
\par
approach, a decision maker might be asked, ``How much would you have to be paid to accept
\par
a one in ten-thousand chance of death?'' If the decision maker answered, say, \$1000, then
\par
the approach would dictate absurdly that he would be willing to be killed for \$10 million.
\par
Howard (1980) constructed an approach that avoids many of the paradoxes of earlier
\par
models. Like several of its predecessors, the model determines what an individual is willing
\par
to pay to avoid a given chance of death, and what he is willing to be paid to assume a given
\par
chance of death. Also, like many of its predecessors, Howard's model shows that, for small
\par
risks of death (typically, p {$<$} 0.001), the amount someone is willing to pay to avoid, or is
\par
willing to be paid to assume, such a risk is linear in p. That is, for small risks of death,
\par
an individual acts as would an expected-value decision maker with a finite value of life,
\par
called the small-risk value of life. For significant risks of death, however, the model deviates
\par
strongly from linearity. For example, the model shows that there is a maximum probability
\par
of death, beyond which an individual will accept no amount of money to risk that chance of
\par
death. Most people find this result to be intuitive.1
\par
To use this model, we first determined Bharat Nathwani's small-risk value of life. When
\par
asked what dollar amount he would be willing to pay to avoid chances of death ranging from
\par
1 in 20 to 1 in 1000, he was consistent with the linear model to within a factor of 2, with a
\par
median small-risk value of life equal to \$20 million (in 1988 dollars). To make the application
\par
of the model more convenient, we used Howard's definition of a micromort: one--in--1-million
\par
chance of death [24]. In these units, the Pathfinder expert's small-risk value of life was \$20
\par
per micromort.2
\par
Given this small-risk value of life, we could then measure his preferences for major and
\par
minor outcomes in a common unit: 1 minus the probability of immediate, painless death that
\par
he was willing to accept to avoid a given outcome and to be once again healthy. In particular,
\par
we assessed his preferences for minor outcomes with willingness-to-pay questions, such as
\par
``How much would you be willing to pay to avoid taking antibiotics for two weeks?'' We then
\par
translated these answers, via the linearity result of Howard's model, to units of probability
\par
1The result makes several assumptions, such as the decision maker is not suicidal and is not concerned
\par
about how his legacy will affect other people.
\par
2In general, the micromort is a useful unit of measurement, because it helps to emphasize that the linear
\par
relationship between risk of death and willingness to pay holds for only small probabilities of death.
\par
11of death. For example, an answer of \$100 translated to a utility of
\par
1- 5 micromorts = 1- 0.000005 = 0.999995
\par
We assessed his preferences for major outcomes directly in units of probability of death. For
\par
example, he imagined that he had---say---Hodgkin's disease, and that he had been misdiag-
\par
nosed as having mononucleosis. He then imagined that there was a magic pill that would rid
\par
him of this disease with probability 1- p, but would kill him, immediately and painlessly,
\par
with probability p. He then provided the value of pthat made him indifferent between his
\par
current situation and the situation in which he takes the pill. The utility of this outcome is
\par
1- p.
