4.3 Construction of a Differential Diagnosis
\par
First, let us examine the problem of differential diagnosis in general. Let mand ndenote the
\par
number of diseases and features in a medical domain, respectively. Also, let d1,d2,...,dm
\par
denote the disease entities. For the moment, let us suppose that each disease dj may be
\par
present or absent. Let Dk denote some instance of diseases. That is, Dk denotes some
\par
assignment of present or absent to each of the diseases d1,d2,...,dm. Further, let f1, f2,
\par
..., fn denote the features in the domain, and let ij denote the observed instance for the
\par
jth feature.
\par
Now imagine that a user of a probabilistic expert system for this domain has observed
\par
instances for qfeatures. To simplify the notation, let us renumber the nfeatures so that the
\par
user has observed instances for the first q features. Typically, the user will want to know
\par
the probability of each disease instance, given the observations f1i1, f2i2,..., fqiq. This
\par
quantity for disease instance Dk is known as the posterior probability of Dk, and is denoted
\par
p(Dk\textbar f1i1,f2i2,...,fqiq,{$\xi$}) (1)
\par
Thus, the number of probabilities of interest is exponential both in the number of observed
\par
features and in the number of diseases.
\par
In principle, an expert could assess directly these posterior probabilities. Aside from
\par
the intractable nature of this task, however, most physicians are more comfortable assessing
\par
probabilities in the opposite direction. That is, they are more comfortable assessing the
\par
probabilities that the set of observations f1i1, f2i2,..., fqiq will appear given a particular
\par
disease instance Dk, denoted
\par
p(f1i1,f2i2,...,fqiq\textbar Dk,{$\xi$}) (2)
\par
Using Bayes' theorem, the expert system can compute from these probabilities and the prior
\par
probability of disease instances p(Dk\textbar{$\xi$}) the desired posterior probabilities
\par
p(Dk\textbar f1i1,f2i2,...,fqiq,{$\xi$}) =
\par
p(f1i1,f2i2,...,fqiq\textbar Dk,{$\xi$}) p(Dk\textbar{$\xi$})
\par
PDl p(f1i1,f2i2,...,fqiq\textbar Dl,{$\xi$}) p(Dl\textbar{$\xi$}) (3)
\par
where the sum over Dl runs over all disease instances. Unfortunately, this approach to the
\par
