\par
12p(f1i1,f2i2,...,fqiq\textbar Dk,{$\xi$}) is exponential both in the number of diseases and in the number
\par
of features.
\par
To manage the complexity of the general case, researchers who built the first proba-
\par
bilistic expert systems made two assumptions. First, they supposed that all findings were
\par
conditionally independent, given any disease instance. That is, they assumed that, if the
\par
true disease state of the patient was known, then the likelihood of seeing any observation
\par
fkik did not depend on observations made about any other features. Thus,
\par
p(fjij\textbar Dk,f1i1,...,fj-1ij-1,fj+1ij+1,...,fqiq,{$\xi$}) = p(fjij\textbar Dk,{$\xi$}) (4)
\par
Given this assumption, it follows from the rules of probability [25] that
\par
p(f1i1,f2i2,...,fqiq\textbar Dk,{$\xi$}) = p(f1i1\textbar Dk,{$\xi$}) p(f2i2\textbar Dk,{$\xi$}){$\cdot$} {$\cdot$} {$\cdot$} p(fqiq\textbar Dk,{$\xi$}) (5)
\par
Second, these researchers supposed that the traditional disease entities were mutually ex-
\par
clusive and exhaustive. That is, they assumed that each disease instance corresponded to a
\par
situation where only one disease was present.
\par
Given these two assumptions, the expert system can compute the posterior probabilities
\par
of disease from the tractable computation
\par
