\par
computation. We call any model that employs these two assumptions a simple-Bayes model.
\par
Ledley and Lusted proposed this model for medical diagnosis in 1959 [26].
\par
In the domain of lymph-node pathology, the assumption that diseases are mutually ex-
\par
clusive is appropriate, because co-occurring diseases almost always appear in different lymph
\par
nodes or in different regions of the same lymph node. Also, the large scope of Pathfinder
\par
makes reasonable the assumption that the set of diseases is exhaustive. The assumption of
\par
global conditional independence, however, is inaccurate. For example, given certain diseases,
\par
finding that follicles are abundant in the tissue section increases greatly the chances that
\par
sinuses in the interfollicular areas will be partially or completely destroyed. Nonetheless, to
\par
simplify our task, we used the simple-Bayes model to construct the first probabilistic version
\par
of Pathfinder. Later, after developing several graphical representation languages that we
\par
describe in the companion to this article, we encoded successfully the conditional noninde-
\par
pendence or conditional dependence among the features in the domain. We shall return to
\par
this discussion in Section 8.
