4.4 Recommendation of a Diagnosis
\par
As we mentioned, a diagnosis is a statement of the form: ``The patient has disease x.''
\par
Sometimes, as we saw in the patient case in Section 3, the posterior probability of one
\par
disease will equal 1 and the posterior probability of all other diseases will equal 0. In this
\par
case, making a diagnosis is not a decision. Rather, the diagnosis is a consequence of the
\par
rules of logic. In most cases, however, observations usually do not narrow the differential
\par
13diagnosis to a single disease. In these situations, making a diagnosis is a decision: an
\par
irrevocable allocation of resources under uncertainty.
\par
Using the MEU principle, the system can determine a diagnosis from the probabilities
\par
of disease and the utilities u(dj,dk). Let {$\varphi$}denote the set of feature--instance pairs f1i1, f2i2,
\par
..., fqiq that we have observed thus far. First, for each diagnosis dk, the system computes
\par
eu(dk\textbar{$\varphi$}), the expected utility of that diagnosis given observations {$\varphi$}, using the formula
\par
eu(dk\textbar{$\varphi$}) = X
\par
