\par
To complete the determination, the system selects the optimal diagnosis, denoted dx({$\varphi$}),
\par
using the equation
\par
dx({$\varphi$}) = argmaxdk [eu(dk\textbar{$\varphi$})] (8)
\par
where the function argmaxdk [{$\cdot$}] returns the disease that maximizes its argument.
\par
We do not allow Pathfinder to recommend diagnoses, because we have observed that such
\par
recommendations are somewhat sensitive to the utility model. That is, when we change the
\par
utilities in the model from values appropriate for one patient to values appropriate for another
\par
patient, the program's recommendations can change significantly. By preventing Pathfinder
\par
from recommending diagnoses, we hope to encourage a change in the way pathologists and
\par
care-providing physicians communicate. In the short term, we hope that pathologists will
\par
begin to express clearly---in the language of probability---uncertainty associated with their
\par
observations. In the long term, we hope that each physician who is associated with the care
\par
of a patient---including the primary physician, the pathologist, the radiologist, the surgeon,
\par
the oncologist, and the radiotherapist---and the patient himself will communicate in decision-
\par
theoretic terms to determine the best treatment for that patient. Such communication could
\par
take place via a shared decision model embodied in an expanded version of Pathfinder.
