ish significantly the diagnostic accuracy of their program that assists physicians with the
\par
diagnosis of congenital heart disease [27]. In addition, although we have not yet conducted
\par
a similar experiment with Pathfinder, our expert almost always has been impressed by the
\par
questions generated by the myopic approximation.
\par
As we mentioned in previous sections, Pathfinder's diagnostic recommendations are sen-
\par
sitive to the utility model, and we therefore do not allow Pathfinder to make such recom-
\par
mendations. Fortunately, however, we have found in an informal study that Pathfinder's
\par
recommendations for evidence gathering are insensitive to the model. In particular, we have
\par
found that Pathfinder's recommendations often are similar to those made by a second version
\par
of the program in which u(dj,dk) is equal to 1 when both dj and dk are benign diseases,
\par
u(dj,dk) is equal to 1 when both dj and dk are malignant diseases, and u(dj,dk) is equal
\par
to 0 otherwise. Consequently, we allow Pathfinder to make recommendations for evidence
\par
gathering.
\par
The observation that recommendations for evidence gathering are less sensitive to the
\par
utility model than are diagnostic recommendations may be due to the fact that more factors
\par
contribute to the computation of net value of information than to the computation of the
\par
diagnosis. That is, only the probabilities of diseases and the utilities u(dj,dk) contribute to
\par
the computation of the diagnosis, whereas these factors, the information content of a feature,
\par
and the cost of a feature contribute to the computation of net value of information.
\par
5 Alternative Reasoning Methodologies
\par
The first medical expert systems employed computations based in probability theory. In
\par
particular, throughout the 1960s and early 1970s, medical expert systems used the simple-
\par
Bayes model to construct differential diagnoses. These systems included Warner's system
\par
for the diagnosis of heart disease [30], Gorry's program for the management of acute renal
\par
failure, and deDombal's system for the diagnosis of acute abdominal pain [31].
\par
Evaluations of most of these early systems showed that the programs performed well.
\par
In fact, the diagnoses rendered by several of them were more accurate than were those
\par
made by experienced physicians [31]. Nonetheless, in the early 1970s, researchers began to
\par
criticize these systems. They noted that the domains of these programs were small and did
\par
not reflect realistic clinical situations. Furthermore, researchers argued that errors due to
\par
the erroneous assumptions of the simple-Bayes model would become unacceptable as the
\par
domains of these systems were expanded [32, 33]. One group of investigators showed that
\par
16the diagnostic accuracy of an expert system based on the simple-Bayes model deteriorated
\par
significantly as the number of features in the system increased. These investigators traced
\par
the degradation in performance to violations of the conditional-independence assumptions
\par
in the simple-Bayes model [34]. Another group of researchers showed that the assumption
\par
of global conditional independence could be unrealistic in small domains as well [35].
\par
In the early 1970s, perceptions of the inadequacy of the early decision-theoretic systems
\par
led to the development of alternative methods for reasoning under uncertainty [36, 37, 32].
\par
Many of these developments occurred in the field of Artificial Intelligence in Medicine. Some
\par
of the alternative methods were ad hoc mechanisms, designed as custom-tailored techniques
\par
for particular domains or systems. These approaches included the MYCIN certainty-factor
\par
model and the QMR scoring scheme. Other methods were developed as alternative theoret-
\par
ical formalisms, such as the Dempster--Shafer theory of evidence and fuzzy decision theory.
\par
In the first year of Pathfinder research, we appreciated the limitations of the simple-Bayes
\par
model, and believed that the use of this model would significantly impair the performance
\par
of Pathfinder. Consequently, we examined several alternative reasoning methodologies. In
\par
this section, we introduce these approaches. In the following two sections, we describe our
\par
empirical and theoretical analyses of these methodologies in the context of Pathfinder.
\par
A well-known ad hoc method for managing uncertainty is the certainty-factor (CF)
\par
model [38]. Shortliffe and Buchanan designed the model to augment the rule-based approach
\par
to reasoning for MYCIN, a program for the diagnosis and treatment of bacteremias and
\par
meningitis [33]. In using the model, an expert attaches a certainty factor to each if--then
\par
rule. The certainty factor represents the expert's change of the belief in the consequent of
\par
the rule, given the antecedent of the rule. In particular, a CF between 0 and 1 means that
\par
the expert's belief in a consequent increases if the antecedent is true, whereas a CF between
\par
-1 and 0 means that the expert's belief decreases. In a rule base, the consequent of one
\par
rule may serve as the antecedent of another rule. In addition, two or more rules may share
\par
the same antecedent or consequent. As a result, a rule base forms an inference network: a
\par
directed graph in which an arc from proposition ato proposition bcorresponds to the rule
\par
``if athen b.'' The CF model prescribes a method for propagating certainty factors through
\par
such a network. That is, given an observation of an antecedent in the network, we can
\par
use CF-model formulas to compute the effective certainty factor for any consequent in the
\par
network that is a descendent of that antecedent. Although the CF model was designed for
\par
MYCIN, the model has found many applications in other domains. Today, the model is the
\par
most popular method for managing uncertainty in rule-based systems.
\par
Quick Medical Reference or QMR (formerly Internist-1) uses another ad hoc method for
\par
managing uncertainty [39, 40]. The QMR project, now in its eighteenth year, assists internists
\par
with the diagnosis of more than 600 diseases, through the consideration of approximately
\par
4000 manifestations or features of disease. In QMR, each feature has two instances; in
\par
particular, a feature is either absent or present. More important, each disease can be either
\par
absent or present. Thus, QMR can address cases in which more than one disease is present.
\par
The ad hoc scoring scheme employs two measures to represent the degree of association
\par
between a feature and a disease: an evoking strength and a frequency. The evoking strength
\par
for a given feature--disease pair represents the degree to which the presence of the disease
\par
causes that feature to be present. The frequency for a given feature--disease pair represents
\par
how often that feature is present in patients who have the disease. In addition, the scheme
\par
17represents the import of each feature, which is inversely proportional to the likelihood that
\par
an insignificant disease (such as the common cold) can cause the feature to be present. Given
\par
an assignment of present and absent to a subset of features, QMR uses evoking strengths,
\par
frequencies, and imports to assign a score to each disease. QMR then displays diseases in
\par
order of descending score. Like early decision-theoretic systems, QMR uses a hypothetico-
\par
deductive approach to reasoning. In particular, the system contains ad hoc algorithms for
\par
generating useful recommendations for additional evidence gathering based on the current
\par
differential diagnosis and on evoking strengths, frequencies, and imports.
\par
A more theoretical alternative to probabilistic reasoning was developed by Dempster
\par
and extended by Shafer [41, 42]. The approach, now called the Dempster--Shafer theory of
\par
evidence, was motivated by theoretical objections to the decision-theoretic approach [43].
\par
Nonetheless, many artificial-intelligence (AI) researchers adopted special cases of the ap-
\par
proach to avoid the perceived computational intractability of decision theory [44, 45]. Cur-
\par
rently, the theory has many interpretations [42, 46, 47, 48]. One of the most popular inter-
\par
pretations is that given in Shafer's original text. In this interpretation, an expert assesses
\par
the degree of support that a piece of evidence lends to hypotheses in the frame of discern-
\par
ment: a set of mutually exclusive and exhaustive hypotheses. He does so for every piece of
\par
evidence that may be observed. A combination rule can then be used to compute the degree
\par
of support that multiple pieces of evidence lend to hypotheses in the frame of discernment.
\par
In the interpretation, an expert assesses degrees of support for a single piece of evidence by
\par
constructing a basic probability assignment over the frame. That is, he assigns a mass, rang-
\par
ing from 0 to 1, to each subset of hypotheses in the frame. The mass for a particular piece
\par
of evidence and a subset of hypotheses represents the degree of support that the evidence
\par
lends to the subset. Like the CF model and the QMR scoring scheme, the Dempster--Shafer
\par
theory manipulates measures of change in belief. In Section 7.1, we examine the relationship
\par
among these methodologies.
\par
Another theoretical approach for managing uncertainty is fuzzy decision theory [49].
\par
The theory addresses the presence of ambiguous terms such as ``large'' and ``tall'' in the
\par
specification of decision problems. Fuzzy decision theorists do not object to the use of
\par
probability theory or decision theory when events are defined precisely. They argue, however,
\par
that it is desirable to reason in situations where there is imprecision in the definition of events
\par
in addition to uncertainty about their occurrence. An example of a fuzzy decision problem
\par
is as follows:
\par
An urn contains many balls of various sizes, of which several are large. To draw
\par
a ball, you must pay a small sum of money. If you draw a large ball, however,
\par
you will win a valuable prize. Should you draw the ball?
\par
6 Empirical Study of Reasoning Methods
\par
During the first year of Pathfinder research, we experimented with the reasoning method-
\par
ologies described in the previous section. In this section, we examine the results of those
\par
experiments.
\par
186.1 Rule-Based Reasoning
\par
The first version of Pathfinder was a rule-based system that employed propositional logic
\par
for reasoning. After informally evaluating the system, we discovered two related problems.
\par
First, the program did not take into account the uncertainty associated with the relationships
\par
between observations and diseases. This deficiency of the program became apparent to us
\par
almost immediately. Indeed, as we have mentioned, proper management of uncertainty
\par
is crucial to accurate diagnosis in the domain of lymph-node pathology. We might have
\par
addressed this concern with the use of the CF model. We found, however, another problem
\par
with the rule-based approach, which forced us to abandon the methodology. In particular,
\par
our expert was frustrated by the system, because it asked many questions that were irrelevant
\par
to discriminating the diseases on the current differential diagnosis. This behavior was a
\par
result of the fact that the rule-based methodology generated recommendations for additional
\par
observations based on a fixed traversal through the rule base.
\par
As a result of our informal evaluations, we searched for a more flexible approach to the
\par
overall control of diagnostic reasoning. We discovered literature describing the hypothetico-
\par
deductive approach and systems such as QMR and Gorry's diagnostic program that im-
\par
plemented the approach. We decided to construct a new version of Pathfinder modeled
\par
after QMR. Nonetheless, we were not satisfied with the scoring scheme of QMR because it
\par
had no theoretical foundation; and we searched for a more principled method for managing
\par
uncertainty.
\par
6.2 Fuzzy Reasoning
\par
We considered fuzzy decision theory as a possible reasoning methodology for Pathfinder,
\par
but quickly rejected its use. We did so, because we found that neither general pathologists
\par
nor experts in hematopathology agreed on the meanings of fuzzy descriptions of feature--
\par
instance pairs such as ``mild capsule thickening,'' ``rare Sternberg-Reed cells,'' or ``prominent
\par
necrosis.'' For example, one expert stated that Sternberg-Reed cells were ``rare'' when there
\par
were one to five of these cells in any 4-square-centimeter section of a lymph node. Another
\par
expert stated that these cells were ``rare'' when there were one to ten of these cells in
\par
any 4-square-centimeter section. We did not believe that fuzzy decision theory---a scheme
\par
devised by researchers who were unfamiliar with the domain of lymph-node pathology---nor
\par
any other mechanism would provide meaningful inferences, if we continued to employ these
\par
fuzzy feature-instance descriptions.
\par
Instead, we asked the four hematopathology experts---Drs. Costan Berard, Jerome
\par
Burke, Ronald Dorfman, and Bharat Nathwani---to clarify the meanings of the descrip-
\par
tions that they were using. Although, as we have just discussed, the experts' interpretations
\par
did not coincide initially, the experts did not find it difficult to construct unambiguous inter-
\par
pretations for each feature instance. The experts handled disagreements in a manner similar
\par
to that used by coauthors of a manuscript who are faced with a disagreement. That is, when
\par
their initial interpretations of a feature instance did not coincide, each pathologist put forth
\par
an argument for the merits of his interpretation of that feature instance. Then, in most
\par
cases, the four experts accepted unanimously one interpretation. When the experts could
\par
not agree, the primary author of the system (Bharat Nathwani) selected the interpretation.
\par
196.3 The Dempster--Shafer Theory of Evidence
\par
Next, we examined the Dempster--Shafer theory of evidence. We discovered that the theory
\par
could be interpreted as a methodology for combining measures of change in belief, as could
\par
the CF model and QMR scoring scheme. We were attracted to the methodology, however,
\par
because it appeared to be a more principled approach to uncertainty management. Conse-
\par
quently, we constructed the second version of Pathfinder, using the Dempster--Shafer theory.
\par
In particular, we implemented a special case of the theory described by Barnett [44]. In this
\par
approach, the expert assigned nonzero masses only to (1) singleton subsets of the frame of
\par
discernment and (2) the entire frame of discernment. We refer to this simplified approach
\par
as the Dempster--Shafer--Barnett model.
\par
We then evaluated informally this version of Pathfinder by allowing the expert to exercise
\par
the system with real and imaginary cases. The expert was satisfied with the diagnostic
\par
accuracy of the system.
\par
At this time, probability theory was low on our list as a method for combining evi-
\par
dence to build a differential diagnosis, because of the limitations of the simple-Bayes model.
\par
Nevertheless, we were interested in experimenting with probabilistic reasoning, given the pio-
\par
neering work of Ledley, Lusted, Gorry, Barnett, and others. We re-examined the measures of
\par
uncertainty that we had assessed from our expert, and realized that these measures could be
\par
interpreted in probabilistic terms. We implemented the simple-Bayes model in Pathfinder,
\par
without assessing additional measures of uncertainty.
\par
5
\par
We then compared the performance of the Dempster--Shafer--Barnett and simple-Bayes
\par
versions of Pathfinder. Without informing the expert, we switched the scoring scheme of
\par
Pathfinder from the Dempster--Shafer--Barnett approach to the simple-Bayes model. To our
\par
surprise, after running several cases with the probabilistic scheme, the expert exclaimed
\par
excitedly that the diagnostic accuracy of the program had improved significantly.
\par
Several years later, in a formal study, we compared the diagnostic accuracy of the
\par
Dempster--Shafer--Barnett, simple-Bayes, and CF models in the domain of lymph-node pathol-
\par
ogy. We verified our informal observation that the simple-Bayes model provided greater
\par
diagnostic accuracy (i.e., greater agreement with the expert) than did the Dempster--Shafer--
\par
Barnett model. We also found that the simple-Bayes model provided greater diagnostic
\par
accuracy than did the CF model [50].
\par
7 Theoretical Study of Reasoning Methods
\par
Surprised with the dominance of the simple-Bayes model over the alternative methods, we
\par
investigated the relationship between probability theory and alternative reasoning strategies
\par
over the next two years. We also studied the theoretical justifications for probabilistic and
\par
decision-theoretic reasoning.
\par
