These theoretical results helped us to understand the dominance of the simple-Bayes
\par
model over the nonprobabilistic alternatives. In particular, the other approaches did not
\par
avoid the assumptions of conditional independence of the simple-Bayes model; they merely
\par
obscured the assumptions. In fact, these approaches assumed that evidence was conditionally
\par
independent, given each disease and given the negation of each disease. When there are
\par
more than two mutually exclusive and exhaustive diseases in a domain, theses conditional
\par
independence assumptions are stronger than are the assumptions in the simple-Bayes model
\par
[58, 51].
\par
We can understand the limitations of the alternative scoring schemes at a more intuitive
\par
level. Let us consider rule-based inference, in particular. The first rule-based inference
\par
schemes used the rules of logic. As a result, these schemes enjoyed a property known as
\par
modularity. That is, given the logical rule ``if a then b,'' and given that a is true, we can
\par
assert that bis true no matter how we established that ais true, and no matter what else
\par
we know to be true. For example, given the rule
\par
if l1 and l2 are parallel lines then l1 and l2 do not intersect
\par
we can assert that l1 and l2 do not intersect once we know that l1 and l2 are parallel lines.
\par
This assertion satisfies the property of modularity: The assertion depends on neither how
\par
we came to know that l1 and l2 are parallel, nor what else we know.
\par
The CF model is an extension of the rules of logic that imposes this same principle of
\par
modularity on inferences. For example, given the rule
\par
if PERITONITIS then APPENDICITIS, CF= 0.7
\par
and given that a patient has peritonitis, the CF model allows us to increase the likelihood
\par
that the patient has appendicitis by the amount corresponding to a CF of 0.7, no matter how
\par
we establish that peritonitis is present. Given the correspondences described in the first part
\par
of this section, we see that the odds--likelihood updating scheme, the QMR scoring scheme,
\par
and the Dempster--Shafer--Barnett model also incorporate the property of modularity. We
\par
shall refer to these methods collectively as modular belief updating schemes.
\par
Unfortunately, these schemes in reality do not satisfy the property of modularity. Contin-
\par
uing our example, suppose the patient has vaginal bleeding. This fact increases the likelihood
\par
that she has a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, and thus increases the likelihood that she has
\par
peritonitis. The chances that the patient has an appendicitis decreases, however, because the
\par
presence of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy can account for the presence of peritonitis. Overall,
\par
we have that the likelihood of peritonitis increases, whereas the likelihood of appendicitis
\par
decreases. The modular rule linking peritonitis with appendicitis is inconsistent with these
\par
relationships.
\par
6This work led to the construction of a probabilistic version of QMR, called QMR-DT [54, 55, 56, 57].
\par
22In general, logical relationships represent complete models of interaction. In contrast,
\par
uncertain relationships encode invisible interactions. We summarize these hidden interac-
\par
tions with numerical measures, such as a certainty factor or likelihood ratio. In the process of
\par
such a summarization, we lose information about the detailed categorical interaction. There-
\par
fore, when we try to combine uncertain information, unexpected (nonmodular) interactions
\par
may occur. We should not expect that any modular belief updating scheme will be able to
\par
handle such subtle interactions.
\par
7.2 A Practical Problem with Nonprobabilistic Methods
\par
In continuing to explore the difference between probabilistic and nonprobabilistic alterna-
\par
tives, we encountered a practical limitation associated with the use of modular belief updat-
\par
ing schemes. Specifically, these schemes require that we assess the strength of an uncertain
\par
relationship in the direction in which it is used. That is, we must specify the change in belief
\par
of an unobservable hypothesis, given an observable piece of evidence. Unfortunately, experts
\par
often are more comfortable quantifying uncertain relationships in the direction opposite to
\par
that in which they are used [59].
\par
In particular, Kahneman and Tversky have shown that people usually are more comfort-
\par
able when they assess the likelihood of an effect given a cause rather than when they assess
\par
the likelihood of a cause given an effect. For example, expert physicians prefer to assess
\par
the likelihood of a finding, given a disease, rather than the likelihood (or belief update) of
\par
a disease, given a finding [60]. Henrion attributes this phenomenon to the nature of causal-
\par
ity. In particular, he notes that a predictive probability (the likelihood of a finding, given a
\par
disease) reflects a stable property of that disease. In contrast, a diagnostic probability (the
\par
likelihood of a disease, given a finding) depends on the incidence rates of that disease and of
\par
other diseases that may cause the finding. Thus, predictive probabilities are a more useful
\par
and parsimonious way to represent uncertain relationships---at least in medical domains (see
\par
[61], pages 252--3). The developers of QMR make a similar observation [39].
\par
Unfortunately, effects are usually the observable pieces of evidence, and causes are the
\par
sought-after hypotheses. Thus, in using a modular belief updating scheme, we force experts
\par
to provide judgments of uncertainty in a direction that is more cognitively challenging. We
\par
thereby promote errors in assessment. In contrast, when we use probability theory to manage
\par
uncertainty, we can assess the strength of an uncertain relationship in one direction, and then
\par
reverse the relationship using Bayes' theorem, when the need arises.
\par
7.3 CompellingPrinciplesfor Uncertainty Management andDe-
\par
cision Making
\par
After identifying limitations of non-decision-theoretic approaches for uncertain reasoning,
\par
we explored theoretical advantages of the decision-theoretic approach. Perhaps the most
\par
significant advantage we discovered was the fact that the rules of probability and the MEU
\par
principle follow from compelling principles, and that people often violate these principles
\par
when unaided by decision-theoretic systems.
\par
For example, Ramsey and deFinetti showed that anyone who does not follow the rules
\par
of probability theory would be willing to accept a ``Dutch book'': a combination of bets
\par
23leading to a guaranteed loss of money under any circumstances [62, 63, 64]. In contrast,
\par
Cox developed a set of desiderata about fundamental properties of a measure of belief unre-
\par
lated to betting behavior that also imply the rules of probability [65, 25]. In addition, von
\par
Neumann and Morgenstern constructed a remarkable proof of the MEU principle [20]. They
\par
developed five compelling principles or desiderata that every decision maker should follow,
\par
and demonstrated that these desiderata imply the MEU principle. One desideratum is the
\par
principle of transitivity, which states that if a decision maker prefers outcome Ato outcome
\par
B, and prefers outcome Bto outcome C, then he must prefer outcome Ato outcome C. To
\par
see that this desideratum is compelling, let us suppose that a decision maker's preferences
\par
are not transitive. In particular, suppose he prefers Ato B, B to C, and C to A. Because
\par
he prefers Cto A, he should be willing to exchange Afor Cand a small payment. Similarly,
\par
this person should be willing to exchange C for B, and B for A. Thus, we can extract
\par
payments from him, and yet leave him with the same outcome. Repeating this procedure,
\par
called a money pump, we can extract an arbitrarily large payment from this person. Decision
\par
theorists and cognitive psychologists have devised justifications for each of von Neumann and
\par
Morgenstern's desiderata [20, 66, 67].
\par
Psychological studies have demonstrated that, in the real world, human decision makers
\par
exhibit a set of stereotypical deviations or biases from the desiderata of decision theory
\par
[68, 69]. From the perspective of decision theory, we can view these deviations as mistakes.
\par
For example, people sometimes have nontransitive preferences, and thus are vulnerable to a
\par
money pump. Also, physicians often forget to consider the prior probability of disease when
\par
making a diagnosis. As we mentioned in the introduction, we use the adjectives ``normative''
\par
and ``descriptive'' to emphasize the differences between decision making consistent with the
\par
rules of decision theory and decision making in the real world.
\par
In medicine, descriptive errors in decision making are particularly dangerous, given the
\par
high stakes associated with some decisions. Such errors easily may lead to needless expen-
\par
ditures, pain and suffering, or loss of life. Fortunately, normative expert systems can help
\par
physicians to avoid such errors. For example, systems that encode explicitly the prior proba-
\par
bilities of diseases will likely improve the decisions made by physicians who would otherwise
\par
forget to consider this important information.
\par
Nonetheless, several researchers have argued that we should employ descriptive rather
\par
than normative methods for decision making in expert systems. In particular, investiga-
\par
tors have stated that decision-theoretic methods lack the expressiveness needed to encode
\par
expertise or to describe intelligent behavior [70, 37, 71, 43]. Indeed, several of these inves-
\par
tigators have argued that the CF model, fuzzy decision theory, and the Dempster--Shafer
\par
theory of evidence may be more appropriate than is decision theory for uncertainty man-
\par
agement, because these non-decision-theoretic methods are possibly more descriptive than is
\par
decision theory [71, 43, 72]. To our knowledge, however, there are no psychological studies
\par
that support these assertions. In fact, our experiment comparing the diagnostic accuracy of
\par
the simple-Bayes, CF, and Dempster--Shafer--Barnett models (see Section 6.3) demonstrated
\par
that, among these approaches, the simple-Bayes model is the most descriptive method for
\par
managing uncertainty.
\par
248 A Return to Decision Theory: Practical Consider-
\par
ations
\par
Once we understood the limitations of non-decision-theoretic methods and the theoretical
\par
benefits of decision theory for managing uncertainty, we still faced the practical limitations of
\par
probability and decision theory. In particular, we were concerned that it would be unfeasible
\par
to relax the conditional independence assumptions of the simple-Bayes model for Pathfinder.
\par
Nonetheless, we speculated that adding the most salient conditional dependencies would not
\par
lead to a combinatorial explosion. Indeed, we conjectured that experts themselves could
\par
not appreciate all the subtle conditional dependencies that may exist among the features
\par
in large medical domains. To manage the complexity of their domain, these experts must
\par
be reasoning under many assumptions of conditional independence. We believed that, if we
\par
could capture these assumptions made by the experts, then we could produce normative
\par
expert systems that perform at least as well as do experts.
\par
Our work to achieve these goals in the domain of lymph-node pathology was successful.
\par
Over the next three years, we developed graphical knowledge representations that allowed
\par
us to capture the important conditional dependencies in this domain in a reasonable amount
\par
of time. In a formal evaluation, we demonstrated that the diagnostic accuracy of the new
\par
version of Pathfinder was at least as good as that of the Pathfinder expert [73]. In the
\par
companion to this article, we describe these new knowledge representations in detail.
\par
9 Pathfinder in Clinical Practice
\par
Several years after the Pathfinder project was initiated, we re-engineered the program on
\par
MS-DOS computers, and made the system available commercially. The system, named
\par
Intellipath, consists of a normative expert system for lymph-node diagnosis and a set of
\par
supportive informational tools including an analog videodisc library of images, text infor-
\par
mation on diseases and microscopic features, references to the literature, and an automated
\par
report-generator.
\par
In 1988, the American Society of Clinical Pathologists began selling the Intellipath pro-
\par
gram to practicing pathologists and pathologists in training in North America. The program
\par
was a commercial success, and we constructed similar systems for other types of human tis-
\par
sue. Currently, approximately 200 pathologists are using the program, and systems for
\par
breast, bone, larynx, skin, small intestine, stomach, thymus, and urinary bladder pathology
\par
and for lung and thyroid cytology are available.
\par
In Section 2, we mentioned that pathologists have difficulty with diagnosis because they
\par
are unable to combine evidence accurately and because they misrecognize or fail to recognize
\par
microscopic features. Most of Pathfinder research addresses the first problem; the videodisc
\par
component of Intellipath, however, addresses the second problem. In particular, when a user
\par
has trouble recognizing a feature, he can ask Intellipath to display images of that feature.
\par
These images illustrate both typical and atypical presentations of the feature.
\par
Recently, we have integrated the latest version of the Pathfinder expert system with the
\par
Intellipath platform, including the videodisc for lymph-node pathology. We will evaluate
\par
this program, called Pathfinder II, in clinical trials funded by the National Cancer Institute.
\par
25In these studies, we shall determine whether pathologists who use the system perform better
\par
than do those pathologists who do not have access to the system. We shall quantify separately
\par
improvements in pathologists' ability to combine evidence and improvements in their ability
\par
to recognize features. In addition, we shall determine whether pathologists can recognize
\par
situations in which they need assistance from an expert system or human expert.
\par
10 Conclusions
\par
We are not alone in the investigation of probabilistic and decision-theoretic methods for
\par
uncertain reasoning in medical expert systems. Several other recent research projects have
\par
explored normative reasoning in medical expert systems, including the Nestor system for
\par
diagnosis of endocrinology disorders [74], the Glasgow Dyspepsia expert system for assisting
\par
in gastroenterology diagnosis [75], the Neurex system for diagnosis of neurological disorders
\par
[76], the Medas system for assisting physicians in emergency medicine [77], the Munin sys-
\par
tem for diagnosis of muscular disorders [78], and the Sleep Consultant system for diagnosis
\par
of sleep disorders [79]. Furthermore, over the last five years, a dedicated community of re-
\par
searchers addressing the management of uncertainty in reasoning systems has evolved. The
\par
proceedings of the main conference of this group of researchers, the Conference on Uncer-
\par
tainty in Artificial Intelligence, is a collection of the latest theoretical and empirical research
\par
on this topic [80, 81, 82, 83, 84].
\par
We believe that the development of normative expert systems will lead to improvements
\par
in the capture and delivery of expert knowledge. This view is supported by our successes with
\par
Pathfinder. We hope that our experiences will inspire other medical-informatics investigators
\par
to develop normative expert systems for medicine.
