\par
ideas to the development of a new generation
\par
of expert systems that treat uncertainty, pref-
\par
erences, and decision making on a more prin-
\par
cipled basis. In particular, we concentrate on
\par
the use of influence diagrams and belief nets
\par
and their role in representation, knowledge
\par
engineering, tractable inference, and explana-
\par
tion. Although we believe that decision
\par
theory and decision analysis can also make
\par
valuable contributions to broader issues in AI,
\par
including planning, reasoning under con-
\par
strained resources, autonomous agents, and a
\par
variety of other topics, these subjects are
\par
beyond the scope of this article. Our focus
\par
here is specifically on their contributions to
\par
knowledge-based expert systems.
\par
Foundations of Decision
\par
Analysis
\par
The foundations of probability theory extend
\par
at least as far back as the seventeenth century
\par
in the works of Pascal, Bernoulli, and Fermat.
\par
Probability is a language for expressing uncer-
\par
tainty about propositions and quantities in
\par
terms of degrees of belief. Decision theory
\par
Articles
\par
extends this language to express preferences
\par
among possible future states of the world and,
\par
hence, among alternative actions that might
\par
lead to them. Probability and decision theory
\par
provide a set of principles for rational infer-
\par
ence and decision making under uncertainty.
\par
By themselves, however, these mathematical
\par
theories are insufficient to address real prob-
\par
lems. Decision analysis is the art and science of
\par
applying these ideas to provide practical help
\par
for decision making in the real world.
\par
We start by introducing the essential ideas
\par
of decision theory and decision analysis. We
\par
will not detail the axioms of decision theory
\par
or present decision analysis techniques, such
\par
as decision trees. Many excellent texts present
\par
this material (Howard and Matheson 1984;
\par
Raiffa 1968). Here, we focus on the essential
\par
underlying ideas and rationale, which are
\par
often misunderstood even among those who
\par
have mastered the technical material.
\par
Subjective Probability
\par
A probability, of course, is simply a number
\par
expressing the chance that a proposition is
\par
true or that some event occurred, with a value
\par
in the range from 0 (certainly false) to
\par
1 (certainly true). A subjective probability is a
\par
probability expressing a person's degree of
\par
belief in the proposition or occurrence of an
\par
event based on the person's current informa-
\par
tion. This emphasis on probability as a personal
\par
belief depending on available information
\par
contrasts with the propensity and frequency
\par
views of probability as something existing
\par
outside any observer. In the propensity view,
\par
probability is a physical property of a device,
\par
for example, the tendency of a particular coin
\par
to come up heads. In the frequency view, prob-
\par
ability is a property of a population of similar
\par
events, for example, the fraction of heads in a
\par
long sequence of coin tosses.
\par
A subjectivist might start with some prior
\par
belief about the fairness of the coin, perhaps
\par
based on experience with other coins, and
\par
then update this belief using Bayes's rule as
\par
data become available from experimental
\par
tosses. After many coin tosses, the belief of
\par
the subjectivist will, in general, converge to
\par
the observed frequency as the data over-
\par
whelm the prior belief. Thus in the long run,
\par
the subjectivist and the frequentist will tend
\par
to agree about a probability. The key distinc-
\par
tion is that the subjectivist is willing to assign
\par
probabilities to events that are not members
\par
of any obvious repeatable sequence, for exam-
\par
ple, the discovery of room temperature super-
\par
conductivity before the year 2000, but the
\par
frequentist is not. Almost all real-world prob-
\par
Decisions
\par
underlie any
\par
action that a
\par
problem solver
\par
might take\dots{}
\par
WINTER 1991 65Articles
\par
The appeal of subjective probability is that it is applicable whether there is little
\par
or much data available.
\par
lems of interest to decision analysts and
\par
expert system builders involve at least some
\par
uncertain events or quantities for which
\par
empirical data are unavailable or too expen-
\par
sive to collect; so, they must resort to the use
\par
of expert opinion. The appeal of subjective
\par
probability is that it is applicable whether
\par
there is little or much data available.
\par
Many people find the subjectivist (also
\par
known as Bayesian or personalist) view of
\par
probability natural. However, a few find the
\par
use of probability a stumbling block, often
\par
because the distinction between subjective
\par
and classical views is not made clear, as it
\par
rarely is in introductory courses on probabili-
\par
ty and statistics. An indication of this confu-
\par
sion is the common misapprehension that
\par
probabilistic methods are only applicable
\par
when large amounts of data are available.
\par
Subjective probabilities are often used to
\par
encode expert knowledge in domains where
\par
little or no direct empirical data are available.
\par
However, if and when data do become avail-
\par
able, Bayesian reasoning provides a consistent
\par
framework to combine data with judgment to
\par
update beliefs and refine knowledge.
\par
If a probability depends on who is assessing
\par
it, then it does not make sense to talk about
\par
``the'' probability. Therefore, the subjectivist
\par
talks about ``your'' probability or ``expert A's''
\par
probability. To draw attention to the fact that
\par
the probability is based or conditioned on the
\par
information available to the assessor, it is
\par
often explicitly specified. P(X \textbar{} s) is used to
\par
notate the probability of a proposition or
\par
event X conditioned on s, the assessor's prior
\par
state of information or background knowl-
\par
edge. If the assessor gets a new piece of evi-
\par
dence E, the revised probability of X is
\par
written P(X \textbar{} E, s), where the comma denotes
\par
the conjunction of evidence E and prior
\par
knowledge s. We often call P(X \textbar{} s) the prior
\par
probability, that is prior to observing E, and
\par
P(X \textbar{} E, s) the posterior probability.
\par
Qualitative Structuring and
\par
Conditional Independence
\par
Probability is best known as a way to quantify
\par
uncertain beliefs, but at least as important is
\par
its role in providing a principled basis for
\par
qualitative encoding of belief structures. This
\par
qualitative role is based on the notion of con-
\par
ditional independence. Event A is judged
\par
independent of event B conditional on back-
\par
ground knowledge s if knowing the value of
\par
B does not affect the probability of A:
\par
P(A \textbar{} s) = P(A \textbar{} B, s) .
\par
Conditional independence is symmetric, so
\par
the previous equation is equivalent to stating
\par
that B is conditionally independent of A:
\par
P(B \textbar{} s)= P(B \textbar{} A, s) .
\par
Conditional independence formalizes the
\par
qualitative notion that A and B are irrelevant
\par
to each other. Conditional independence
\par
and, conversely, dependence, provide the
\par
basis for expressing the qualitative structure
\par
in graphic form as a belief network or influ-
\par
ence diagram. Practicing decision analysts,
\par
like knowledge engineers building convention-
\par
al expert systems, often claim that designing
\par
the qualitative structure is far more important
\par
than precision in the numeric parameters. In
\par
Knowledge Representations from Decision
\par
Analysis and in Knowledge Engineering and
\par
Decision Analysis, we discuss structuring
\par
techniques using belief nets and influence
\par
diagrams as well as methods for eliciting
\par
numeric probabilities.
\par
Decision Theory
\par
Decision theory is based on the axioms of
\par
probability and utility. Where probability
\par
theory provides a framework for coherent
\par
representation of uncertain beliefs, utility
\par
theory adds a set of principles for consistency
\par
among beliefs, preferences, and decisions. A
\par
decision is an irrevocable allocation of
\par
resources under the control of the decision
\par
maker. Preferences describe a decision maker's
\par
relative ordering of the desirability of possible
\par
states of the world. The key result of utility
\par
theory is that, given fundamental properties
\par
of belief and action, there exists a scalar func-
\par
tion describing preferences for uncertain out-
\par
comes. Utility theory also provides ways to
\par
express attitudes toward uncertainty about
\par
outcome values, such as risk aversion. The
\par
valuation of an outcome can be based on the
\par
traditional attributes of money and time as
\par
well as on other dimensions of value, includ-
\par
66 AI MAGAZINEing pleasure, pain, life-years, and computa-
\par
tional effort. Multiattribute utility theory pro-
\par
vides ways to combine all these elements to
\par
produce a single scalar utility to represent the
\par
relative desirability of any certain outcome.
\par
Based on the axioms of decision theory, it is
\par
relatively easy to show that one should select
\par
the decision that maximizes the expected
\par
utility over a set of decisions with uncertain
\par
outcomes.
\par
Normative versus Descriptive Theories
\par
The axioms of probability and decision theory
\par
are fairly simple and intuitive. Many but not
\par
all who examine them find them compelling
\par
as principles for rational action under uncer-
\par
tainty. The theory is often referred to as nor-
\par
mative, in the sense that it provides a set of
\par
criteria for consistency among beliefs, prefer-
\par
ences, and choices that it claims should be
\par
adhered to by a rational decision maker.
\par
Given a set of beliefs and preferences, the
\par
theory prescribes which decisions should be
\par
chosen, namely, those that maximize expect-
\par
ed utility.
\par
Decision theory is not proposed as a descrip-
\par
tive theory; it does not purport to provide
\par
a good description of how people actually
\par
behave when making choices under uncer-
\par
tainty. Indeed, it has provoked a large body of
\par
empirical research examining the differences
\par
between how decision theory suggests we
\par
ought to behave and how we actually do
\par
(Kahneman, Slovic, and Tversky 1982). These
\par
psychological studies have found qualitative
\par
similarities between human intuitive judgment
\par
under uncertainty and the prescriptions of
\par
decision theory, but they have also demon-
\par
strated pervasive and consistent biases and
\par
inconsistencies. These differences seem to
\par
result from various mental heuristics that we
\par
use to render complex reasoning tasks
\par
amenable to our cognitive capacities.
\par
Proponents of alternative schemes for
\par
uncertain reasoning used in expert systems,
\par
such as certainty factors and fuzzy set theory,
\par
have sometimes used these findings from psy-
\par
chology to justify using these formalisms on
\par
the grounds that they can be better models of
\par
human reasoning than probability and deci-
\par
sion theory. However, virtually no empirical
\par
evidence shows that these alternative schemes
\par
provide better psychological models. In any
\par
case, for decision analysis, the goal is to
\par
improve human reasoning rather than repli-
\par
cate it. The observed biases and inconsisten-
\par
cies in unaided human reasoning are central
\par
to the justification for using normative aids,
\par
just as our limited capacity for mental arith-
\par
Articles
\par
metic is the reason we find electronic calcula-
\par
tors so useful.
\par
Divide and Conquer
\par
Decision analysis does not aim to avoid sub-
\par
jective judgments. That would be impossible.
\par
Rather, its strategy is divide and conquer: It
\par
replaces complex subjective judgments about
\par
which decisions are best with simpler subjec-
\par
tive judgments about the probabilities of
\par
component events and relative preferences
\par
for elements of possible outcomes. The psy-
\par
chological literature tells us what kinds of
\par
judgments people find simplest and how to
\par
minimize cognitive biases in obtaining them.
\par
The components of the decomposed model
\par
are then reassembled to obtain recommenda-
\par
tions about the complex decisions implied by
\par
the simpler judgments. Decision theory justi-
\par
fies the technical operations involved in the
\par
reassembly, such as applying Bayes's rule and
\par
selecting choices to maximize expected utility.
\par
Thus, decision analysis does not seek to elimi-
\par
nate human judgment but, rather, to simplify
\par
and clarify it.
\par
The Focusing of Attention
\par
Real-world situations have unlimited com-
\par
plexity in terms of the number of conceivable
\par
actions, states of the world, and eventual out-
\par
comes. Models are necessarily incomplete and
\par
uncertain. The key issue in modeling,
\par
whether for decision analysis or knowledge
\par
engineering using other representations, is
\par
the focus of attention: how to identify what
\par
matters and ignore the rest. Decision analysis
\par
provides a variety of techniques for focusing
\par
attention by performing sensitivity analysis
\par
to help identify those uncertainties and
\par
assumptions that could have a significant
\par
effect on the conclusions. The focus on deci-
\par
sions is of particular importance: The ques-
\par
tion about a possible model elaboration is not
\par
simply, Might it be relevant? but instead, Is it
\par
likely to change the resulting decision recom-
\par
mendations? This method turns out to be an
\par
extremely effective way to guide the model-
\par
ing process and pare away the inessential.
\par
Resources can then be directed to modeling
\par
and analyzing the most sensitive aspects of
\par
the problem.
\par
Insight, Not Numbers
\par
A decision analyst that simply presents a deci-
\par
sion maker with the numeric expected utility
\par
of each decision strategy, along with the
\par
injunction to choose the highest expected
\par
utility, is unlikely to be effective. Practicing
\par
WINTER 1991 67Articles
\par
The question
\par
\dots is not,
\par
Might this be
\par
relevant? but
\par
instead, Is it
\par
likely to
\par
change the
\par
resulting
\par
decision\dots{}
\par
decision analysts, like builders of expert sys-
\par
tems for decision support, discovered early
\par
that the most important product of the anal-
\par
ysis is not the numbers or even the recom-
\par
mended decision but the improved insights
\par
for the decision makers. These insights come
\par
from understanding why one decision is rec-
\par
ommended over another and which assump-
\par
tions and uncertainties are most critical to
\par
this conclusion. The process is generally fos-
\par
tered by the close involvement of decision
\par
makers in the modeling and analysis process.
\par
Without a basic understanding of the analy-
\par
sis, decision makers are unlikely to accept the
\par
results as a trustworthy basis for action. This
\par
perspective emphasizes the importance of
\par
clear explanations of the model assumptions
\par
and analysis if they are to be used and useful.
\par
The Practice of Decision Analysis
\par
Just as success in building conventional
\par
expert systems requires a great deal more
\par
than understanding the relevant AI research,
\par
decision analysis involves a great deal more
\par
than decision theory. Among other things, it
\par
includes techniques for structuring problems,
\par
encoding probabilities and utilities, comput-
\par
ing implications, analyzing sensitivities, and
\par
explaining results to highlight insights, as we
\par
discuss later. Decision analysis emerged in the
\par
1960s from the recognition that probability
\par
and decision theory could be applied to real-
\par
world decision problems (Howard and Math-
\par
eson 1984; Raiffa 1968; von Winterfeldt and
\par
Edwards 1986). Over the last 20 years, it has
\par
grown into an established professional disci-
\par
pline. A number of commercial consulting
\par
and research firms perform decision analyses
\par
in business, government, and medicine. The
\par
number of professional decision analysts is
\par
comparable to the number of professionals
\par
building expert systems for real-world problems.
\par
Many large corporations routinely apply deci-
\par
sion analysis to scheduling, capital expan-
\par
sion, and research and development decisions.
\par
The emphasis has been on assisting people
\par
and organizations faced with high stakes and
\par
complex resource-allocation problems.
\par
Early Bayesian Expert Systems
\par
By expert system, we mean a reasoning
\par
system that performs at a level comparable to
\par
or better than a human expert does within a
\par
specified domain. It is useful to classify tasks
\par
for which expert systems have been con-
\par
structed as analytic or synthetic. In systems
\par
dedicated to analytic tasks, a set of alternatives
\par
such as possible diagnoses or decisions is
\par
68 AI MAGAZINE
\par
either explicitly enumerated or relatively easy
\par
to enumerate; the central task is the valua-
\par
tion of the alternatives. With synthetic tasks,
\par
the space of alternatives (for example, the set
\par
of possible configurations or plans) can be
\par
extremely large, and the main problem is
\par
constructing one or more feasible options.
\par
Analytic tasks include prediction, classifica-
\par
tion, diagnosis, and decision making about a
\par
limited set of options. Synthetic tasks include
\par
the generation of alternatives, design, config-
\par
uration, and planning.
\par
The area of AI in which decision theory has
\par
had the most obvious influence is analytic
\par
expert systems, particularly diagnostic sys-
\par
tems. Probability and decision analysis pro-
\par
vide an appealing approach to analytic tasks
\par
because of the central role of inference and
\par
decision making under uncertainty. Conse-
\par
quently, we focus here on expert systems for
\par
analytic tasks. Decision theory can also be rel-
\par
evant to synthetic tasks, where probabilistic
\par
or preference value functions can guide
\par
search among large numbers of options. The
\par
pioneering work in analytic expert systems
\par
involved medical applications, although
\par
much recent work has addressed fault diagno-
\par
sis in electronic components and mechanical
\par
devices (de Kleer 1991; Genesereth 1984).
\par
In general, three kinds of tasks are involved.
\par
The first task is diagnosis: How can we infer
\par
the most probable causes of observed prob-
\par
lems (for example, diseases or machine faults)
\par
given a set of evidence (for example, symptoms,
\par
patient characteristics, operating conditions,
\par
or test results)? The second task is informa-
\par
tion acquisition: What additional informa-
\par
tion or tests should we request? This choice
\par
involves weighing the benefits of achieving a
\par
possibly more accurate diagnosis against the
\par
costs of obtaining the information. The third
\par
task is making treatment decisions: What can
\par
we do to fix or treat the problem?
\par
The earliest work on diagnostic expert sys-
\par
tems used explicitly Bayesian and decision-
\par
analytic approaches (Gorry and Barnett 1968;
\par
Ledley and Lusted 1959). The general Bayesian
\par
formulation for diagnostic inference is as fol-
\par
lows: Suppose we are considering a set H of n
\par
possible hypotheses,
\par
H = \textbraceleft h1, h2, \dots{} hn\textbraceright{} ,
\par
and a set F of m findings,
\par
F = \textbraceleft f1, f2, ... fm\textbraceright{} .
\par
In a medical application, the hypotheses
\par
are possible diseases, and the findings can
\par
include patient history, physical signs, symp-
\par
toms, and laboratory results. We assume for
\par
the simplicity of the presentation that all
\par
hypotheses and pieces of evidence are two-
\par
valued, logical variables, each either true orfalse. A diagnosis or explanation D is a subset
\par
of H. It is a set of hypotheses believed to be
\par
present, implying all others are absent. We
\par
represent initial beliefs about the prevalence
\par
of the diseases as a prior probability distribu-
\par
tion over all diagnoses P(D \textbar{} s) for each
\par
diagnosis D, conditioned on the expert's
\par
knowledge s. Suppose E is observed evidence
\par
that some findings are present, others absent,
\par
and the rest unobserved. Knowledge about
\par
the uncertain relationships between diagnosis
\par
and evidence is represented as the conditional
\par
probability distribution P(E \textbar{} D, s). We can
\par
apply Bayes's theorem to compute the poste-
\par
rior probability of each diagnosis after observ-
\par
ing evidence E:
\par
P(D \textbar{} E, s) = P(E \textbar{} D,s) P(D \textbar{} s)
\par
