\par
This most general formulation is complex
\par
both to assess and to compute. Because a
\par
patient can have more than one disease out
\par
of n possible diseases, the number of possible
\par
diagnoses (that is, disease combinations) is 2n
\par
.
\par
Thus, the number of independent parameters
\par
necessary to specify the complete prior distri-
\par
bution is 2n - 1. For m pieces of evidence, the
\par
general conditional distribution has 2m - 1
\par
independent parameters given each hypothe-
\par
sis, requiring the specification of 2n (2m - 1)
\par
total independent parameters for all diagnoses.
\par
Clearly, this approach becomes impractical
\par
for more than two or three hypotheses and
\par
pieces of evidence without some kind of sim-
\par
plification.
\par
Two simplifying assumptions were often
\par
made: First (A1) is that the hypotheses in H
\par
are mutually exclusive and collectively
\par
exhaustive; for example, each patient has no
\par
more than one disease. Second (A2) is that
\par
there is conditional independence of evidence;
\par
that is, given any diagnosis, the occurrence of
\par
any piece of evidence fi of the component
\par
hypotheses is independent of the occurrence
\par
of any other piece of evidence fj:
\par
P(fi \textbar{} s) = P(fi \textbar{} fj, s) .
\par
