\par
and Barnett 1968), acute abdominal pain (de
\par
Dombal et al. 1972), and surgical pathology
\par
(Heckerman et al. 1991). Despite the apparent
\par
simplicity of the assumptions, some of these
\par
systems performed at or above the level of
\par
experts. For example, the system of de
\par
Dombal and his colleagues (1974) averaged
\par
over 90-percent correct diagnoses of acute
\par
abdominal pain such as appendicitis, whereas
\par
expert physicians were averaging 65 percent
\par
to 80 percent.
\par
Despite the success of this simple Bayesian
\par
scheme in several of these early applications,
\par
enthusiasm for this approach began to fade in
\par
the early 1970s. One reason might have been
\par
the poor user interfaces of many early sys-
\par
tems and the general lack of attention to inte-
\par
grating systems with the habits and
\par
environment of the diagnostic practitioner.
\par
An important lesson from this experience is
\par
that superior diagnostic performance alone is
\par
not sufficient for acceptance.
\par
A second and more often-cited reason is the
\par
restrictiveness of the assumptions of mutual
\par
exclusivity and conditional independence.
\par
This scheme is sometimes termed ``Idiot's
\par
Bayes.'' More generally, critics have pointed
\par
out the limited expressiveness of this formu-
\par
lation and the apparent mismatch between
