\par
AI Approaches to Expert Systems
\par
Perhaps the decisive blow to early Bayesian
\par
schemes was the appearance of an appealing
\par
alternative approach using logical and rule-
\par
based representations derived from AI. This
\par
approach focused more on the representation
\par
and use of large amounts of expert knowl-
\par
edge and less on questions of normative opti-
\par
mality. Many researchers in this area had had
\par
little exposure to, or interest in, probability
\par
and decision theory.
\par
A key feature of the new expert system
\par
paradigm was the application of the produc-
\par
tion-rule architecture to real-world diagnosis.
\par
The appeal of production rules lay in their
\par
apparent capacity to represent expert knowl-
\par
edge in a flexible declarative and modular
\par
form (Buchanan and Shortliffe 1984). The
\par
production rule has the form of logical impli-
\par
cation: To handle the uncertainty in real-world
\par
diagnosis, investigators simply extend the
\par
production-rule representation to allow inter-
\par
mediate degrees of truth between true and
\par
false for both propositions and for the appli-
\par
cability of each rule. The two best-known
\par
approaches that represent uncertainty as an
\par
extension of deterministic rule-based expert
\par
systems are MYCIN (Buchanan and Shortliffe
\par
1984) and PROSPECTOR (Duda, Gaschnig, and
\par
Hart 1979).
\par
MYCIN, the expert system to aid physicians
\par
in the diagnosis of bacterial infections, intro-
\par
duced the certainty factor, a number represent-
\par
ing the degree of confirmation (between 0
\par
and 1) or disconfirmation (between 0 and -1)
\par
of each proposition or rule. The basic MYCIN
\par
scheme was made available for other applica-
\par
tions as EMYCIN and it is used in several com-
\par
mercially available expert system shells.
\par
PROSPECTOR was constructed to aid geologists
\par
in the identification of commercial mineral
\par
deposits. PROSPECTOR uses probabilities to rep-
\par
resent degrees of belief in propositions and
\par
quantities related to likelihood ratios to
\par
quantify rule strengths, although its updating
\par
rules are not exactly consistent with a coher-
\par
ent probabilistic interpretation.
\par
The developers of both MYCIN and PROSPECTOR
\par
originally intended their schemes as approxi-
\par
mations to the probabilistic ideal, which they
\par
saw as unattainable for the reasons we dis-
\par
cussed. Recent theoretical and experimental
\par
work examined these and other heuristic
\par
schemes for uncertain reasoning with produc-
\par
tion rules and found a number of inherent
\par
problems related to assumptions about priors,
\par
irreversibility, and the myth of modularity.
\par
A common objection to probabilistic
\par
approaches is the difficulty of assessing prior
\par
probabilities, degrees of belief in hypotheses
\par
before evidence is available. Empirical data
\par
are often hard to obtain, and subjective judg-
\par
ments can be unreliable. MYCIN (although not
\par
PROSPECTOR) appears to evade this problem by
\par
not requiring prior beliefs. Contrary to many
\par
popular interpretations, the certainty factor
\par
was originally intended to represent an
\par
update or change in belief induced by the
\par
evidence, not an absolute degree of belief
\par
(such as a probability) (Heckerman 1986;
\par
Heckerman and Horvitz 1987). Thus, it aggre-
\par
gates the overall change in belief given the
\par
evidence without having to explicitly repre-
\par
sent the prior or posterior belief in each
\par
hypothesis.
\par
When MYCIN suggests a treatment for an
\par
infection, it effectively uses the certainty fac-
\par
tors for the diseases as a proxy for their rela-
\par
tive probability. Because it avoids explicit
\par
reference to priors or prevalence rates, it is, in
\par
effect, treating all infections as having equal
\par
prior probabilities. In fact, diseases often
\par
differ in prevalence rates by many orders of
\par
magnitude, and although physicians might
\par
find them difficult to precisely quantify, they
\par
usually have approximate knowledge about
\par
them. In addition, even approximate priors
\par
can have a substantial effect on diagnosis and
\par
treatment. For example, the fairly prevalent
\par
mononucleosis and relatively rare Hodgkin's
\par
disease can appear similar in a lymph node
\par
70 AI MAGAZINEbiopsy; the differences in the prior probabilities
\par
can be essential in diagnosis and treatment.
\par
Given that experts have even a rough knowl-
\par
edge about priors, it seems important to
\par
explicitly represent this knowledge.
\par
An inherent problem of rule-based repre-
\par
sentations is their irreversibility for uncertain
\par
inference. Rules for diagnosis are generally
\par
specified in the direction from possible evi-
\par
dence to the hypotheses. This approach sup-
\par
ports diagnostic reasoning, but it is hard to
\par
reverse the direction to support predictive
\par
inference, for example, to predict the likely
\par
effects of a given disease. (Note that the issue
\par
here is the flow of evidence, not the flow of
\par
control, so the applicability of forward or
\par
backward chaining is irrelevant.) In many
\par
cases, it seems easier to assess uncertain
\par
dependence in the causal direction, for exam-
\par
ple, the propensity of a given disease to cause
\par
a symptom, than in the diagnostic direction,
\par
the degree to which the symptom suggests
\par
the presence of the disease. Causal dependen-
\par
cies are often more invariant over different
\par
situations because they reflect basic properties
\par
of the mechanisms, whereas diagnostic
\par
dependencies depend on the prevalence of
\par
alternative possible explanations of the effect.
\par
Hence, it is often desirable to encode expert
\par
knowledge about causal dependencies but
\par
reverse the direction for diagnostic inference
\par
(Shachter and Heckerman 1987). This is pre-
\par
cisely what Bayes's theorem does for coherent
\par
probabilistic representations, but it is generally
\par
difficult to do with rule-based representations.
\par
It is also hard to support intercausal inference
\par
(Henrion 1987; Wellman and Henrion 1991),
\par
that is, increasing belief in one possible cause
\par
of an observed effect because of new evidence
\par
against another cause (and conversely). For
\par
example, given that an incipient cold and an
\par
allergy attack are both possible causes of
\par
sneezing, and given a person is sneezing, the
\par
observation of an allergen (for example, a cat)
\par
should reduce belief in the cold (figure 2).
\par
Although one can add special-purpose rules
\par
to achieve this effect, this approach defeats
\par
the goal of having the rules encode only
\par
domain knowledge and not general knowl-
\par
edge about how to perform inference. Again,
\par
this kind of inference arises naturally in prob-
\par
abilistic representations but is awkward and
\par
impossible to do in general for rule-based
\par
schemes. Rules primarily support reasoning in
\par
the direction from condition to action: An
\par
ideal knowledge representation is isotropic in
\par
that it encodes knowledge in whatever way is
\par
most natural but supports reasoning in any
\par
direction required: predictive, diagnostic, or
\par
intercausal.
