\par
An often-cited advantage of the rule-based
\par
representation scheme is the ability to add or
\par
remove rules from a knowledge base without
\par
modifying other rules (Davis 1983). This
\par
property has been referred to as modularity.
\par
The modularity of rules in a logical production
\par
system is a consequence of the monotonicity
\par
of logic: Once asserted, the truth of a propo-
\par
sition cannot be changed by other facts.
\par
Unfortunately, it has been shown that this
\par
property does not carry over in any straight-
\par
forward manner to uncertain reasoning with
\par
rules. Uncertain beliefs are intrinsically less
\par
modular than beliefs held with certainty, fre-
\par
quently making the rule-based calculi ineffi-
\par
cient for reasoning with uncertainty
\par
(Heckerman and Horvitz 1987). The tradi-
\par
tional assumption of modularity in rule-
\par
based approaches for reasoning under
\par
uncertainty has implications that had not pre-
\par
viously been appreciated.
\par
Thus, like the early probabilistic systems,
\par
rule-based methods impose strong restric-
\par
tions on the kinds of dependence that can
\par
effectively be represented. Unlike the explicit
\par
assumptions of the simplified probabilistic
\par
systems, the restrictive assumptions in the
\par
heuristic approaches have been less apparent.
\par
The implicit nature of the assumptions in
\par
rule-based systems has tended to promote a
\par
dangerous ``myth of modularity'': Rule-based
\par
approaches, like the simple probabilistic
\par
approaches, do not have the expressiveness
\par
necessary to coherently represent the rela-
\par
tionships among uncertain beliefs.
