Knowledge Representations
\par
from Decision Analysis
\par
As we saw, there has been justified criticism
\par
of the restrictive assumptions of both the
\par
simplified Bayesian scheme and the heuristic
\par
rule-based approaches to uncertain reasoning.
\par
Some have been led to believe that the
\par
assumptions of mutual exclusivity and condi-
\par
tional independence of the Idiot's Bayes's
\par
scheme are essential to any Bayesian scheme.
\par
However, this belief is a misconception. In
\par
the last decade or so, much richer knowledge
\par
representations have been explored, still
\par
based in a principled way on probability and
\par
decision theory but capable of expressing a
\par
wider range of both qualitative and quantita-
\par
tive knowledge in a flexible and tractable
\par
manner. Much of this work has centered on
\par
the use of acyclic-directed graphs to repre-
\par
sent uncertain relationships, including belief
\par
networks and influence diagrams. These rep-
\par
resentations facilitate the assessment of
\par
coherent prior distributions and make it
\par
easier for knowledge engineers and experts to
\par
express and understand more general kinds of
\par
dependence and independence assumptions.
\par
Influence Diagrams
\par
Influence diagrams are a graphic knowledge
\par
representation language to display decision
\par
problems, including decision variables, state
\par
variables, and preference or value variables
\par
and their relationships (Howard and Mathe-
\par
son 1981). As well as having a rigorous
\par
formal interpretation, they have a perspicu-
\par
ous qualitative structure that facilitates
\par
knowledge acquisition and communication.
\par
Influence diagrams offer an important com-
\par
plement to more traditional representations,
\par
such as decision trees and tables of joint
\par
probability distributions and outcome values
\par
for each action and state (Raiffa 1968). Unlike
\par
these models, influence diagrams provide an
\par
explicit representation of probabilistic depen-
\par
dence and independence in a manner accessi-
\par
ble to both human and computer.
\par
The influence diagram is an acyclic-direct-
\par
ed graph. The nodes represent propositions or
\par
quantities of interest, including decision vari-
\par
ables, states of the world, and preference
\par
values. The arcs represent influence or rele-
\par
vance, that is, probabilistic or deterministic
\par
relationships between the variables. An influ-
\par
ence diagram for a medical decision problem
\par
is shown in figure 3. The diagram encodes a
\par
decision problem about whether to undergo
\par
coronary artery bypass graft surgery. The
\par
danger in this situation is the risk of myocar-
\par
dial infarction, that is, heart attack.
\par
