\par
of dependence that can be represented effectively.
\par
The example demonstrates the four differ-
\par
ent kinds of nodes in an influence diagram. A
\par
decision node, depicted as a rectangle, repre-
\par
sents a set of possible alternative actions
\par
available to a decision maker. Decisions are
\par
the control variables or policy variables under
\par
the direct control of a decision-making agent.
\par
In the example, the angiogram test node rep-
\par
resents the decision of whether to perform an
\par
artery-imaging procedure that provides infor-
\par
mation about the extent of coronary artery
\par
disease in the patient. The heart surgery node
\par
is the decision about whether to undergo a
\par
coronary bypass operation.
\par
The arcs into a decision node are informa-
\par
tional arcs. They indicate what information is
\par
available, that is, the values of uncertain vari-
\par
ables or previous decisions that will be known
\par
at the time the decision is made. The diagram
\par
indicates that when he/she makes the surgery
\par
decision, the decision maker will know
\par
whether the patient has chest pain and the
\par
outcome of the angiogram test if it was per-
\par
formed.
\par
Chance nodes represent states of the world
\par
that are uncertain. They are depicted by cir-
\par
cles or ovals. Uncertain belief about a chance
\par
node is specified as a probability distribution
\par
conditioned on the outcomes of its predeces-
\par
sor nodes. For example, the probability distri-
\par
bution over the values of life-years (years of
\par
life remaining to the patient) depends on
\par
whether the patient has a heart attack and
\par
whether heart surgery was performed because
\par
there is a risk of death from the surgery itself.
\par
A deterministic node is depicted by a double
\par
circle and represents a state of the world that
\par
is a deterministic function of its predecessor
\par
nodes. In the example, the cost is simply the
\par
sum (a deterministic function) of the mone-
\par
tary expenses of the angiogram test, the surgi-
\par
cal procedure, and the hospitalization
\par
following a heart attack. Note that we can
\par
actually be uncertain about a deterministic
\par
node because its predecessors are uncertain.
\par
In this case, we are uncertain about the cost
\par
because the heart attack node is uncertain,
\par
even though it depends deterministically on
\par
these predecessors.
\par
Finally, the value node is depicted as a dia-
\par
mond and represents the preferences or utilities
\par
of a decision maker for alternative outcomes.
\par
Generally, each influence diagram has only
\par
one value node. Its predecessors indicate
\par
those outcomes or attributes that are included
\par
in the evaluation of a choice or plan. For the
\par
heart disease example, the attributes are life
\par
quality, life-years, and cost. This multiattribute
\par
utility function expresses trade-offs among
\par
these attributes for an individual patient as
\par
well as attitudes toward risk and time.
\par
Any variable can be represented by a con-
\par
tinuous scalar or a set of discrete values. It is
\par
usual for the value node to be continuous.
\par
Some variables can be inherently discrete,
\par
such as the heart surgery decision. It is either
\par
performed or not, with no intermediate possi-
\par
bilities. In other cases, the variable can be
\par
inherently continuous but treated as discrete
\par
for representational and computational con-
\par
venience. For example, in response to a par-
\par
ticular level of exertion, the node chest pain
\par
has the values none, mild discomfort, and
\par
crushing sensation. It is important that the
\par
set of outcomes for each variable be defined
\par
unambiguously. They must be mutually
\par
exclusive and exhaustive in the sense of cov-
\par
ering all possible values.
\par
Belief Networks
\par
Much of the research on representation and
\par
inference with these graphic representations
\par
has focused on specializations of influence
\par
diagrams that contain only chance nodes
\par
(Cooper 1984; Kim and Pearl 1983; Lemmer
\par
1983; Pearl and Verma 1987). These special-
\par
ized representations exclusively express prob-
\par
abilistic relationships among states of the
\par
world without explicit consideration of deci-
\par
sions and values. Several different terms have
\par
been used for these representations, including
\par
causal probability networks and Bayesian nets
\par
(Pearl 1988). We use belief networks, which
\par
seems to be the most popular.
\par
Three Levels of Representation
\par
The representation of a decision problem can
\par
be seen at three levels of specification: relation,
\par
function, and number (Howard and Matheson
\par
1981). We can define a model at each level
\par
without defining information at more specific
