ic and synergistic influences between vari-
\par
ables in qualitative probabilistic terms and
\par
presents methods of qualitative probabilistic
\par
reasoning based on them.
\par
Finally, the level of number quantifies the
\par
numeric values in the functions and condi-
\par
tional distributions. For example, at the level
\par
of number, we might specify that P(chest pain
\par
= mild discomfort \textbar{} coronary artery disease = 1
\par
vessel) = 0.25. Chance nodes without prede-
\par
cessors can be specified at the level of number
\par
with marginal (prior) probability distributions.
\par
Conditional Independence
\par
As we mentioned previously, at root, probabilis-
\par
tic independence is a qualitative relationship
\par
among variables. It captures the intuitive
\par
notion of irrelevance. A belief network
\par
expresses independence graphically. The
\par
arrows or arcs---or, more precisely, the lack of
\par
arcs between variables---express probabilistic
\par
independence. Several kinds of independence
\par
74 AI MAGAZINE
\par
are illustrated in figure 4. Source variables (that
\par
is, those variables with no predecessors) are
\par
marginally independent. Where two variables
\par
have one or more common parents but no
\par
arc between them, they are conditionally
\par
independent of each other given their
\par
common parent(s). Finally, a variable is con-
\par
ditionally independent of its indirect prede-
\par
cessors given all the variable's immediate
\par
predecessors (that is, those nodes from which
\par
it directly receives an arc).
\par
The influence diagram, or belief net, provides
\par
knowledge engineers the flexibility to specify
\par
and reason about dependencies---or, more
\par
important, independencies---at a purely qual-
\par
itative level before progressing to the level of
\par
function or number. Thus, they can capture
\par
expert beliefs in their full richness (or sim-
\par
plicity as the case might be) without arbitrary
\par
restrictions. The representation provides
\par
explicit control over modularity assumptions.
\par
The independencies in an influence diagram
\par
are a formal expression of the locality of
\par
effect among variables. The effects of one
\par
variable on a distant variable can only propa-
\par
gate along the influence arcs. More precisely,
\par
a variable is screened from the effects of dis-
\par
tant variables (is conditionally independent
\par
of them) given its Markov blanket, that is, its
\par
direct predecessors, direct successors, and the
\par
direct predecessors of these successors (that
\par
is, parents, children, and spouses). In figure 4,
\par
the Markov blanket of y is \textbraceleft v, x\textbraceright, and the
\par
Markov blanket of w is \textbraceleft u, v, x\textbraceright.
\par
One way to look at a belief net is as a
\par
decomposition of the joint-probability distri-
\par
bution over all its variables. The joint proba-
\par
bility is equal to the product of the probability
\par
for each variable conditional on its predeces-
\par
sors (if any). The belief net in figure 4 speci-
\par
fies the following decomposition:
\par
P(u,v,w,x,y \textbar{} s) = P(y \textbar{} v,x, s) P(v \textbar{} u,w, s)
\par
P(x \textbar{} w, s) P(w \textbar{} s) P(u \textbar{} s) . (1)
\par
If we assess the probability distribution for
\par
each variable conditional on its predecessors,
\par
provided this network has no directed cycles,
\par
the product of the variables is guaranteed to
\par
be a coherent joint distribution. That is, the
\par
joint will be complete, having no unspecified
\par
parameters, and consistent, containing no
\par
conflicts. In this way, the belief network pro-
\par
vides a simple solution to the problem that
\par
was unsolved in PROSPECTOR and related sys-
\par
tems, namely, how to assign probabilities to
\par
variables and links without creating incoher-
\par
ence.
\par
We can now more carefully interpret the
\par
heart surgery influence diagram (figure 3) in
\par
terms of conditional independence. In the
\par
diagram, the primary expression of condition-al independence involves coronary artery dis-
\par
ease and its effects. The diagram asserts that
\par
the probabilities over the values of chest pain
\par
(both current and future), angiogram test,
\par
and myocardial infarction are all dependent
\par
on the value of coronary artery disease. Fur-
\par
thermore, given knowledge of coronary artery
\par
disease, the effects of the disease are condi-
\par
tionally independent of one another. Once
\par
we know the precise extent of a patient's
\par
heart disease, then the presence of chest pain
\par
does not change our belief that he/she might
\par
have a heart attack at some time in the future.
\par
The knowledge of coronary artery disease as
\par
the causal agent tells us all the information
\par
available about the interaction of its effects.
\par
Knowledge Engineering and
\par
Decision Analysis
\par
Knowledge engineering is the process by which
\par
knowledge is elicited from experts in the
\par
domain, structured, encoded, installed, and
\par
refined in expert systems. Although this term
\par
has not traditionally been used in the decision
\par
analysis field, the work of a decision analyst
\par
in constructing a decision model is funda-
\par
mentally similar to the activities of a knowl-
\par
edge engineer. Both the knowledge engineer
\par
and the decision analyst work with a decision
\par
maker or a domain expert to create a formal
\par
representation of aspects of his/her knowledge,
\par
often supplemented with data from other
\par
sources, such as texts and policy documents.
\par
In conventional expert systems, the knowl-
\par
edge engineer typically uses rule-based and
\par
object-based representations coupled with
\par
some type of deductive inference method.
\par
The decision analyst uses influence diagrams
\par
and decision trees to express qualitative knowl-
\par
edge about the situation. Influence diagrams
\par
have proved particularly useful for expressing
\par
qualitative judgments about uncertain depen-
\par
dence and independence relations in a way
\par
that is intuitive but also with a principled
\par
probabilistic basis. The qualitative diagram is
\par
then used as a framework for assessing quan-
\par
titative probabilistic dependencies. Decision
\par
analysts differ in putting more emphasis on
\par
decisions and building an explicit quantita-
\par
tive preference or utility model, which is only
\par
implicit or absent in rule-based representa-
\par
tions (Henrion and Cooley 1987; Langlotz et
\par
al. 1986). Like knowledge engineers for expert
\par
systems, practicing decision analysts have
\par
developed and refined a battery of practical
\par
techniques for eliciting and quantifying
\par
knowledge and beliefs.
\par
The Encoding of Probabilities
\par
How to obtain the necessary probabilities is
\par
frequently a major concern of those contem-
\par
plating decision analysis for the first time. For
\par
some events, there can be relevant empirical
\par
data to guide probability assessment, but for
\par
many real problems, most or all probabilities
\par
will need to be obtained from expert judg-
\par
ment. Indeed, even where data are available,
\par
it is a matter of expert judgment about how
\par
relevant they are and whether adjustments
\par
are needed to fit the situation at hand. To
\par
the strict subjectivist, one should be able to
\par
express one's belief in any proposition as a
\par
single probability number no matter how
\par
little or much one knows about it. This is a
\par
consequence of the axioms of decision theory,
\par
such as the ability to order the relative proba-
\par
bility of any set of events. The objection
\par
What if I don't know the probability? loses
\par
force once one realizes that probability is not
\par
a physical characteristic, such as mass or
\par
length, that one is trying to estimate; it is just
\par
a way to express one's degree of knowledge or
\par
ignorance about the proposition. Nonetheless,
\par
expressing one's knowledge in terms of proba-
\par
bilities is often a demanding task.
\par
However, decision analysts have developed
\par
a variety of techniques to make it as easy as
\par
possible, even for assessors who have little
\par
technical understanding of probability (Morgan
\par
and Henrion 1990; Spetzler and Stael von
\par
Holstein 1975). Methods are available for
\par
assessing discrete-event probabilities and con-
\par
tinuous probability distributions. The simplest
\par
methods require the assessor to make only
\par
qualitative judgments about which is more
\par
probable, the event of interest or some refer-
\par
ence event of agreed probability. The probabil-
\par
ity wheel is a popular method for providing a
\par
reference event. It is a simple graphic device
\par
consisting of a disk with a colored sector
\par
whose angle visually represents the probability
\par
of the reference event. According to whether
\par
the probability of the event is judged greater
\par
or lesser than the relative size of the sector, its
\par
angle is adjusted larger or smaller until the
\par
expert is indifferent. Thus, a probability can
\par
be obtained without explicitly mentioning a
\par
number. As they gain experience with proba-
\par
bility assessment, many experts find they
\par
prefer directly giving numeric probabilities.
\par
For extremely low or high probabilities, tech-
\par
niques that use odds or log-odds scales have
\par
been shown to be useful (von Winterfeldt and
\par
Edwards 1986).
\par
An extensive literature on human judgment
\par
has identified cognitive biases and mental
\par
heuristics that tend to distort human judg-
\par
ments about uncertain events (Kahneman,
