\par
Prototypical Influences
\par
To fully express the influence on a binary
\par
variable E dependent on n binary hypotheses
\par
H1, H2, \dots{} Hn requires a conditional probability
\par
distribution with 2n independent parameters.
\par
However, in practice, influences can often be
\par
specified by a prototypical function incorpo-
\par
rating independencies that greatly simplify
\par
the assessment. A common example of a pro-
\par
totypical influence is the noisy-Or gate, which
\par
is a probabilistic generalization of a standard
\par
Boolean Or (figure 5). With a Boolean Or, the
\par
occurrence of any single one of the input
\par
events is sufficient to cause the output event.
\par
In a noisy-Or, each input has some probabili-
\par
ty of being sufficient to cause the output. The
\par
processes that prevent the signal from being
\par
sufficient are independent of each other. This
\par
structure is useful in representing many causal
\par
relationships, for example, where several dif-
\par
ferent faults can each cause the same device
\par
failure mode, or several different diseases can
\par
each cause a common symptom.
\par
The noisy-Or relationship requires the spec-
\par
ification of only one parameter for each input,
\par
the probability of the effect given only that
\par
input is present, pi = P(E \textbar{} Hi only, s). The
\par
probability of the effect given any combina-
\par
tion of input can simply be derived from the
\par
individual parameters:
\par
P(E \textbar{} H1, H2, ... Hn, s) = 1 - (1 - p1) (1 - p2) \dots{}
\par
(1 - pn) .
\par
Thus, the complete conditional distribution
\par
requires the specification of only n parame-
\par
ters (Good 1950; Pearl 1986b).
\par
It is often useful to also introduce a leak,
\par
that is, the probability that the effect can
\par
occur in the absence of any cause explicitly
\par
represented in the knowledge base. The leak
\par
constitutes a kind of residual ``all others'' cat-
\par
egory, representing what is not explicit in the
\par
knowledge base and substantially mitigating
\par
the effects of the closed-world assumption.
\par
For example, in the QMR-BN knowledge base
\par
for internal medicine, each finding or symp-
\par
tom is, on average, associated with about 80
\par
diseases (Shwe et al. 1991). The leaky noisy-
\par
Or assumption allows it to be encoded by a
\par
single causal strength for each disease-finding
\par
pair (about 40,000 such pairs exist in the
\par
knowledge base). Each of the approximately
\par
4,000 findings also has a leak probability, rep-
\par
resenting the chance the finding is observed
\par
because of an unmodeled cause or a false pos-
\par
itive test result.Researchers are seeking techniques for
\par
explicitly acquiring and representing other
\par
forms of independence. Heckerman (1990)
\par
describes the use of partitions that can sub-
\par
stantially reduce the effort to assess the dis-
\par
ease-finding links. For each finding, its
\par
associated diseases are classified into groups
\par
that have similar causal strengths. This strength
\par
need only be assessed then once for many dis-
\par
eases. The approach, and the related similarity
\par
networks, are efficient because experts find it
\par
much easier to judge whether two probability
\par
distributions are similar than to assess the dis-
\par
tributions themselves.
\par
Value Structuring and Utility Modeling
\par
The modeling of human preferences in the
\par
form of utility functions is a major concern
\par
for decision analysts. In important decisions,
\par
there are often several objectives that conflict,
\par
for example, in the heart surgery case, life
\par
duration, life quality, pain, and monetary
\par
cost. Decision theorists have developed the
\par
multiattribute utility theory as a way to model
\par
human preferences in such situations (Keeney
\par
and Raiffa 1976; von Winterfeldt and Edwards
\par
1986). The most important stage in preference
\par
modeling is generally the first in which these
\par
objectives or attributes are identified. They are
\par
often organized into a value tree or attribute
\par
hierarchy. It is important not to omit key
\par
attributes and avoid attributes that overlap.
\par
For each attribute, a value function can be
\par
assessed that maps from the quantity of inter-
\par
est, for example, dollars of income or years of
\par
life expectancy, into a value scale whose
\par
increments are equal in significance to the
\par
decision maker. A function must be assessed
\par
then that combines the values for each attribute
\par
into a single utility number to compare alter-
\par
native options. Depending on whether the
\par
attributes are judged independent or whether
\par
there are interactions among them, this can
\par
be a simple additive model or something
\par
more complex. Again, a variety of elicitation
\par
techniques are available to discover the
\par
model form and assess weights or trade-offs
\par
between the attributes (Keeney and Raiffa
\par
1976; von Winterfeldt and Edwards 1986).
\par
Several researchers have examined computer
\par
aids for value structuring and preference
\par
modeling (Holtzman 1988; Klein 1990; and
\par
Wellman 1985).
\par
Utility functions also represent attitudes to
\par
uncertainty or risk. For example, a risk-averse
\par
person will prefer a certain prize of a \$500 to a
\par
50-percent chance of \$1000 and a 50-percent
\par
chance of 0. A variety of elicitation techniques
\par
have been developed to help decision makers
\par
Articles
\par
assess their risk attitudes by asking their rela-
\par
tive preferences among gambles or lotteries
\par
with varying probabilities and outcomes
\par
(Howard 1970; Keeney and Raiffa 1976; von
\par
Winterfeldt and Edwards 1986). Assessment
\par
protocols generally involve making judgments
\par
in a variety of different ways to provide cross-
\par
checks.
\par
Model Refinement and Sensitivity
\par
Analysis
\par
As we noted in Early Bayesian Expert Systems,
\par
no model is complete: It is necessarily a sim-
\par
plification of the knowledge, beliefs, and pref-
\par
erences of the expert or decision maker, which
\par
are themselves a simplification of reality. It is
\par
a compromise between simplicity for ease of
\par
knowledge engineering and computational
\par
tractability and completeness for maximum
\par
precision. During model construction, we can
\par
elaborate it and simplify it as we explore
\par
trade-offs between accuracy and tractability.
\par
We refer to this process as completeness modu-
\par
lation (Horvitz 1987). This process is also used
\par
to some extent by knowledge engineers, but
\par
decision analysts have particular perspectives
\par
and tools to help them.
\par
In particular, the decision analysts use a
\par
variety of sensitivity analysis methods to
\par
examine the importance of various model
\par
parameters and structures, including specific
\par
probabilities, risk tolerance (expressing risk
\par
attitude), and multiattribute trade-off weights
\par
(Howard 1968). In judging the sensitivity of
\par
the model to a particular parameter or model
\par
feature, the question is not just, Could it
\par
affect model predictions? but more specifically,
\par
Could it affect the predicted expected utility
\par
in such a way as to change the recommended
\par
decision? This decision-oriented perspective
\par
provides a stronger measure of importance
\par
that allows pruning many more parameters as
\par
unimportant.
\par
A well-known decision-oriented sensitivity
\par
measure is the expected value of perfect informa-
\par
tion (EVPI), which is the increase in expected
\par
value from improving the decision should the
\par
true value of an uncertain quantity become
\par
known. Another such measure is the expected
\par
value of including uncertainty (EVIU), which
\par
assesses the importance of the explicit repre-
\par
sentation of uncertainty in a variable
\par
(Morgan and Henrion 1990, chapter 12).
\par
Because the probabilistic representation of a
\par
variable exacts costs in elicitation, representa-
\par
tion, and inference, it is desirable to include
\par
only those uncertainties that matter. A third
\par
decision-oriented measure is the expected
\par
