\par
value of computation (EVC) (Horvitz, Cooper,
\par
and Heckerman 1989). Where additional
\par
computation gives improved precision, but at
\par
additional cost, the EVC can help decide how
\par
much computation is worthwhile.
\par
To date, few people have investigated the
\par
automation of sensitivity analysis for proba-
\par
bilistic reasoning. A promising area in this
\par
regard is the development of better error the-
\par
ories, improving our ability to predict the
\par
effects of various kinds of errors and simplifi-
\par
cations in input in different model classes.
\par
For example, the effects of errors in the
\par
assessment of conditional probabilities can
\par
only be attenuated in predictive inference but
\par
can be amplified in their effect on posterior
\par
probabilities in diagnostic inference (Henrion
\par
1988a). General theory of this type could lead
\par
to more intelligent automated sensitivity aids
\par
to guide knowledge engineering in the most
\par
profitable directions.
\par
Computer Support for
\par
Problem Structuring
\par
Conventionally, decision analysis has been
\par
applied to specific decision problems, where
\par
expert systems are designed to support a
\par
whole class of decision problems within a
\par
domain. Of course, the cost in constructing it
\par
can be amortized over a large number of
\par
applications. The usefulness of decision-
\par
focused sensitivity analysis for guiding model
\par
construction in decision analysis points to a
\par
fundamental difference in goals between con-
\par
ventional decision analysis and knowledge-
\par
based expert systems. Decision-focused
\par
sensitivity analysis seems to work well in cut-
\par
ting a model down for a specific decision
\par
problem. However, when constructing a
\par
knowledge base for a wide class of decisions,
\par
determining what might be relevant is much
\par
harder. Almost any hypothesis or evidence
\par
might potentially be of importance to support
\par
decision making for, say, internal medicine. A
\par
vast knowledge base is necessary because
\par
little can be ruled out a priori as irrelevant.
\par
Given a large knowledge base of potentially
\par
relevant information, can we automate the
\par
task of constructing a tractable model focused
\par
on a particular case? Holtzman (1988)
\par
approached this problem by encoding the
\par
expertise of the decision analyst as a set of
\par
rules for constructing influence diagrams
\par
within a particular domain. Holtzman's pro-
\par
totype, RACHEL, addresses decision counseling
\par
on problems of infertility. It contains deter-
\par
ministic rules that construct and analyze an
\par
influence diagram that responds to the specif-
\par
ic information, preferences, and situation of a
\par
particular case.
\par
Breese (1990) takes a different approach to
\par
this problem. His knowledge base represents
\par
specific and general facts about variables and
\par
their probabilistic relations. His system, ALTER
\par
ID, constructs a belief net or influence diagram
\par
from components in the knowledge base
\par
using a refinement process that is analogous
\par
to the human decision analyst. ALTER ID adds
\par
variables to the model in stepwise fashion
\par
and uses deterministic sensitivity analysis to
\par
guide the direction and extent of the refine-
\par
ments. These approaches raise the more gen-
\par
eral question of what kind of structures are
\par
appropriate for representing uncertain knowl-
\par
edge for decision making if belief nets and
\par
influence diagrams are not the best primitive
\par
representation.
\par
Reasoning with Belief Networks
\par
and Influence Diagrams
\par
Suppose we observe the actual value of one or
\par
more variables in a belief network or influence
\par
diagram, which might have implications for
\par
other chance variables whose probabilities
\par
will change as a result. A variety of reasoning
\par
types are possible. For example, in figure 3,
\par
knowledge that a patient has coronary artery
\par
disease allows us to infer predictively (that is,
\par
in the direction of the influence arcs, general-
\par
ly from cause to effect) the changed probabil-
\par
ity that he/she will suffer future chest pain or
\par
a heart attack. Conversely, given that the
\par
patient has a specified level of chest pain and
\par
angiogram test outcome, we can infer diag-
\par
nostically (that is, in the reverse direction,
\par
from effect to cause) the probability that the
\par
patient has coronary artery disease. Given
\par
evidence about an effect, for example, sneez-
\par
ing in figure 2, the independent evidence
\par
78 AI MAGAZINEeliminating one cause, allergy, allows us to
\par
reason intercausally that the other possible
\par
cause, a cold, is more likely.
\par
We can also calculate the expected value or
\par
utility of alternative decisions, thus obtaining
\par
recommendations for which a decision is
\par
preferable. These decisions can include prima-
\par
ry decisions, for example, whether heart
\par
surgery is recommended given current knowl-
\par
edge, and information-acquisition decisions,
\par
for example, whether doing an angiogram
\par
test is worthwhile given its costs and the
\par
chance it might improve the primary decision.
\par
We can also do a variety of sensitivity analyses,
\par
answering such questions as, How probable
\par
must coronary artery disease be for surgery to
\par
be worthwhile? or for a given probability of
\par
coronary artery disease, What trade-off
\par
between life quality (freedom from pain) and
\par
life-years of survival would make surgery
\par
worthwhile?
\par
Exact Inference in Belief Networks
\par
Conceptually, the simplest way to perform
\par
inference and compute the probability of an
\par
outcome that is conditional on any observa-
\par
tions is simply to generate the joint distribu-
\par
