Of course, the snag with this approach is
\par
that for n variables, there are 2n scenarios.
\par
Thus, computational effort is exponential in
\par
the number of variables and is infeasible for
\par
more than a dozen or so variables. The key to
\par
computational efficiency for inference in
\par
belief networks is the exploitation of the spec-
\par
ified independence relations to avoid having
\par
to explicitly calculate the full joint distribu-
\par
tion. Most of these decompose the network
\par
into smaller pieces. A variety of methods have
\par
been developed, each focusing on particular
\par
families of belief network topology. The sim-
\par
plest method is the simplified Bayes's scheme,
\par
which we discussed earlier and is illustrated
\par
in figure 1. The next simplest applies when
\par
