\par
Figure 6: A Singly Connected Belief Network, or Polytree.
\par
the network is singly connected, that is, a poly-
\par
tree with no more than one path between any
\par
pair of nodes, as illustrated in figure 6 (Kim
\par
and Pearl 1983). Both these algorithms are
\par
linear in the number of network variables.
\par
Unfortunately, most real networks are mul-
\par
tiply connected; so, more complex methods
\par
are required. A variety of approaches have
\par
been explored (Lauritzen and Spiegelhalter
\par
1988; Pearl 1986a; and Shachter 1986) whose
\par
efficiency varies according to the network's
\par
characteristics. All these approaches can have
\par
problems if the network contains many inter-
\par
secting loops (ignoring directionality), as
\par
illustrated in figure 7. Cooper (1991) shows
\par
that the general problem of exact probabilis-
\par
tic inference in a belief network is NP-hard;
\par
so, we should not expect to find an exact
\par
method that is computationally efficient for
\par
arbitrary networks. Nevertheless, exact meth-
\par
ods have proved practical for sizable multiply
\par
connected networks. For example, the HUGIN
\par
system can perform diagnostic inference in
\par
under five seconds on the MUNIN network for
\par
neuromuscular disorders, containing about
\par
1000 chance nodes with up to 7 states each
\par
(Andreassen et al. 1987).
\par
Approximate Inference in
\par
Belief Networks
\par
Despite these successes, there remain networks
\par
such as the QMR-BN belief network (Shwe et al.
\par
1990) with about 4500 nodes with many
\par
intersecting loops, an arrangement that gener-
\par
ally appears intractable for exact methods.
\par
Concern about the tractability of exact meth-
\par
ods has provoked research into approximate
\par
WINTER 1991 79Articles
\par
Figure 7. A Multiply Connected Belief Network
\par
methods. Most approximate methods achieve
\par
their efficiency by considering only a small
\par
subset of the 2n scenarios and estimating or
\par
bounding the probabilities of events from
\par
this subset. These methods include random
\par
sampling schemes, bounded conditioning,
\par
and search schemes (Henrion 1990b).
\par
Random sampling, or simulation-based
\par
approaches, generates a random sample of
\par
network instantiations and estimates resulting
\par
probabilities from the sample. In logic sam-
\par
pling (Henrion 1988b), each instantiation is
\par
generated following the belief network arcs,
\par
selecting a value for each variable using the
\par
probability conditional on its instantiated
\par
parents. Logic sampling for predictive infer-
\par
ence is a version of Monte Carlo simulation,
\par
applied to discrete variables. For purely pre-
\par
dictive inference, that is in the direction of
\par
the influence arrows, such random sampling
\par
methods are efficient, with computational
\par
effort linear in the number of uncertain vari-
\par
ables and quadratic in the level of precision
\par
required. These methods work equally well
\par
for continuous or discrete variables. For
\par
purely continuous variables there are also a
\par
variety of analytic methods of error propaga-
\par
tion available (see chapter 8 of Morgan and
\par
Henrion [1990] for a review).
\par
For diagnostic reasoning, the complexity of
\par
pure logic sampling is exponential in the
\par
number of observed variables because all
\par
instantiations inconsistent with observed evi-
\par
dence are simply dropped from the sample.
\par
Likelihood weighting (Shachter and Peot 1990)
\par
mitigates this problem. Instead of instantiat-
\par
ing the observed variables, it computes the
\par
joint likelihood that is conditional for each
\par
80 AI MAGAZINE
\par
scenario, using this number to weight the
\par
scenario in the sample. A virtue of logic sam-
\par
pling and derived approaches is that succes-
\par
sive samples are independent, so convergence
\par
is guaranteed, and the precision of estimated
\par
probabilities can be estimated by standard
\par
statistical methods.
\par
An alternative simulation-based approach
\par
is stochastic, or Markov, sampling (Pearl
\par
1987). In this case, variables are instantiated
\par
in random sequence, using the probability
\par
conditional on their entire Markov blanket.
\par
This approach neatly avoids problems from
\par
observed variables, which are simply clamped
\par
to their observed value. However, unlike logic
\par
sampling, successive instantiations are not
\par
independent, and the process can suffer from
\par
convergence problems when the network
\par
contains near-deterministic influences (Chin
\par
and Cooper 1987).
\par
Cutset conditioning (Pearl 1988) involves
\par
instantiating variables in the cutset of a net-
\par
work to render the remaining network singly
\par
connected, thus amenable to the efficient
\par
polytree method. Bounded conditioning
\par
improves the efficiency of complete cutset
\par
conditioning by examining the most proba-
\par
ble instantiations of the cutset from which it
\par
computes bounds on the posterior probabili-
\par
ty (Horvitz, Suermondt, and Cooper 1989).
\par
A different class of approximate methods
\par
uses heuristic search of the space of possible
\par
scenarios to find the hypotheses that best
\par
explain the observed findings (Cooper 1984;
\par
Henrion 1990a; Peng and Reggia 1987; de
\par
Kleer 1991). Because a small fraction of the
\par
hypotheses often accounts for the bulk of the
\par
probability, this approach can be efficient.
\par
Admissibility heuristics can guarantee finding
\par
the most probable hypotheses (or n most
\par
probable hypotheses). The most powerful
\par
heuristics depend on specific assumptions,
\par
such as the bipartite network with noisy-
\par
Or--combining functions. Versions of these
\par
search methods can compute bounds on the
\par
probabilities of the hypotheses. These meth-
\par
ods seem promising. For example, the TOP N
\par
algorithm was applied to the QMR-BN network,
\par
and can find the most probable multiple-dis-
\par
ease hypotheses in seconds (Henrion 1991).
\par
All these approximate algorithms are flexible
\par
or anytime algorithms, that is they can be halted
\par
at any time to give an answer, and additional
\par
computation continually improves results.
\par
Sampling algorithms decrease the variance of
\par
their estimates as the sample size increases.
\par
Bounded conditioning and search-based
\par
methods, such as TOP N, monotonically narrow
\par
the bounds on the probability of the hypoth-
\par
esis as the search continues. Such flexibility isvaluable when there is uncertainty about
\par
deadlines or the cost of delaying a decision,
\par
and can be shown to yield increases in the
\par
expected value of reasoning (Horvitz 1988).
\par
Given a model of delay costs, metelevel deci-
\par
sion analysis can be applied to decide when
\par
to stop computing and act (Horvitz, Cooper,
\par
and Heckerman 1989; Horvitz and Rutledge
\par
1991).
\par
Explanation
\par
Although decision analysts have long under-
\par
stood the importance of explaining decision
\par
analysis, as encapsulated in the slogan
\par
``insight not numbers,'' AI researchers have
\par
frequently criticized probabilistic and deci-
\par
sion-theoretic reasoning as inherently diffi-
\par
cult to explain (Davis 1982; Politser 1984;
\par
Szolovits 1982). Teach and Shortliffe (1981)
\par
identified the ability of an expert system to
\par
explain its reasoning strategies and results as
\par
key to its acceptance by users. Automating
\par
the generation of explanations has long been
\par
a focus of attention in expert system research
\par
(Shortliffe 1984; Winograd 1971; Swartout
\par
1983; Wallis and Shortliffe 1982). More recently,
\par
the development of decision analysis--based
\par
systems has led to increased interest in auto-
\par
mated methods to generate explanations of
\par
probabilistic and decision-analytic reasoning.
\par
Evidence Weights
\par
One approach to explaining probabilistic
\par
inference is to examine the individual impact
\par
of each piece of evidence on the overall con-
\par
clusion. A classic technique is the evidence
\par
weight, that is, the logarithm of the likelihood
\par
ratio, of a hypothesis H with respect to each
\par
piece of evidence, ei:
\par
P(ei \textbar{} H, s)
\par
(P(ei \textbar{} H, s) ).
\par
W(H, ei) = Log
\par
Weights of evidence have the useful proper-
\par
ty of being additive provided the pieces of
\par
evidence, ei, are conditionally independent.
\par
That is, the evidence weight for combined
\par
evidence is just the sum of the weights of the
\par
components:
\par
W(H, e1 \& e2 \& \dots{} en) = W(H, e1) + W(H, e2)
\par
+ ... + W(H, en) .
\par
The convenience of weights of evidence for
\par
performing and explaining probabilistic infer-
\par
ence has an ancient and venerable history,
\par
having first been pointed out by Peirce (1956)
\par
in 1878 and rediscovered by Turing (Good
\par
1950) and Minsky and Selfridge (1961),
\par
among others. Several probabilistic expert sys-
\par
tems for medical diagnosis have made use of
\par
Articles
\par
likelihood ratios and weights of evidence for
\par
explaining the importance of evidence to
\par
hypotheses (Ben-Bassat et al. 1980; Hecker-
\par
man, Horvitz, and Nathwani 1991; Reggia
\par
and Perricone 1985; Spiegelhalter and Knill-
\par
Jones 1984). The additivity of evidence
\par
weights has led to perspicuous explanations
\par
using evidence ledger sheets, which sum the
\par
weights for and subtract the weights against a
\par
hypothesis (Spiegelhalter and Knill-Jones 1984).
\par
The Modulation of Completeness and
\par
Abstraction
\par
A good explanation should be as simple as
\par
possible, consistent with communicating the
\par
essential information. One approach to creat-
\par
ing simpler explanations of probabilistic diag-
\par
nosis is to control the level of abstraction to
\par
suit the user's needs. For example, PATHFINDER
\par
can generate explanations at the level of evi-
\par
dence for and against general classes of dis-
\par
ease, such as inflammatory, infectious, and
\par
malignant diseases, rather than about each
\par
individual disease in the classes (Horvitz et al.
\par
1989). In PATHFINDER and related work by Ben-
\par
Bassat and Teeni (1984), the level of abstrac-
\par
tion can be controlled with heuristic
\par
abstraction hierarchies, classifying diseases
\par
into groups that depend on the diagnostic
\par
problem at hand. Generating a good explana-
\par
tion involves trade-offs between various goal
\par
attributes, particularly simplicity and com-
\par
pleteness. The use of multiattribute utility
\par
models supports metareasoning about these
\par
trade-offs to construct more effective explana-
\par
tions (Horvitz 1987; McLaughlin 1987).
\par
Qualitative Explanation
\par
Because people generally seem to find qualita-
\par
tive descriptions easier to understand than
\par
quantitative results, several projects have
\par
sought to explain probabilistic and decision-
\par
analytic reasoning in qualitative terms. One
\par
approach has been to map numeric probabili-
\par
ties into phrases such as ``unlikely'' or ``almost
\par
certain'' or value outcomes into phrases such
\par
as ``slight risk'' or ``severe danger.'' Users often
\par
find this approach appealing both for encod-
\par
ing and explanation. Elsaesser (1988) uses a
\par
related approach for the qualitative explana-
\par
tion of Bayesian updating. Sember and Zuker-
\par
man (1989) provide a linguistic explanation
\par
of reasoning between neighbors in a belief
\par
network. Klein (1990) demonstrates how to
\par
generate qualitative explanations of choices
\par
based on hierarchical additive multiattribute
\par
utility models. This approach has also been
\par
used to explain Bayesian updates; for exam-
\par
