\par
For decision analysis, the goal is to improve human reasoning
\par
rather than to replicate it.
\par
ple, ``Observation of A makes B a great deal
\par
more likely'' (Elsaesser and Henrion 1990).
\par
The literature on the numeric interpretation
\par
of probability phrases is considerable, show-
\par
ing a degree of consistency but significant
\par
context effects that can cause misinterpreta-
\par
tions (Wallsten et al. 1986).
\par
A related approach focuses on the relative
\par
sizes of probabilities, values, or expected utili-
\par
ties. Langlotz, Shortliffe, and Fagan (1986b)
\par
constructed a system called QXQ that explains
\par
why one decision has higher expected value
\par
than another using qualitative comparisons
\par
of the probabilities and values of the most
\par
important possible outcomes of each decision.
\par
Henrion and Druzdzel (1990) developed a
\par
scenario-based scheme in which posterior
\par
probabilities are explained in terms of the rel-
\par
ative plausibility of alternative scenarios, or
\par
deterministic stories, that can explain the
\par
observed findings. Explanations are simplified
\par
by considering only the most likely scenarios.
\par
A third qualitative approach uses qualitative
\par
probabilistic networks (QPNs). QPNs are belief
\par
nets and influence diagrams in which influ-
\par
ences are represented as purely qualitative;
\par
for example, observing A increases the proba-
\par
bility of B (Wellman 1988b). QPNs have been
\par
used as the basis for explaining probabilistic
\par
reasoning by qualitative belief propagation,
\par
tracing the directions of the impacts of evi-
\par
dence through a network (Henrion and
\par
Druzdzel 1990; Wellman and Henrion 1991).
\par
Comparing Decision Analysis
\par
and Rule-Based Expert Systems
\par
There has been a common perception that
\par
even if commonly used calculi for uncertain
\par
reasoning have deficiencies, it does not really
\par
make much practical difference what method
\par
you use. This perception is partly based on an
\par
early experiment with MYCIN that found its
\par
conclusions were fairly robust with respect
\par
to the granularity of the certainty factors
\par
(Buchanan and Shortliffe 1984). However, as
\par
the MYCIN authors originally warned, it is dan-
\par
gerous to overgeneralize from these results.
\par
An empirical comparison of certainty factors
\par
with probabilistic inference in a simple case
\par
showed that the certainty factor--based infer-
\par
ence underresponded to the true diagnostici-
\par
ty of the evidence by a factor of 2 and in 25
\par
percent of the cases actually responded in the
\par
wrong direction (Wise 1986). However, MYCIN
\par
itself was a forgiving application in which
\par
this behavior did not have serious results on
\par
recommended treatments.
\par
Comparisons of Uncertainty Calculi
\par
Experimental comparisons of certainty factor
\par
and other rule-based schemes with probabilis-
\par
tic inference have shown that the differences
\par
can be significant under other circumstances.
\par
A comparison of alternative formulations of
\par
PATHFINDER found that the Bayesian version
\par
significantly outperformed formulations
\par
using certainty factors and Dempster-Shafer
\par
belief functions (Heckerman 1988). A system-
\par
atic comparison of six common uncertainty
\par
calculi with a variety of small- and medium-
\par
sized rule sets found that the difference
\par
between schemes depended strongly on the
\par
situation (Wise 1986). When the evidence is
\par
strong and consistent, most schemes perform
\par
well. However, if the evidence is weak or con-
\par
flicting, heuristic schemes can perform poorly
\par
relative to Bayesian schemes, sometimes
\par
doing no better than random guessing.
\par
Differences in Knowledge Engineering
\par
The studies just mentioned compared the
\par
performance of different uncertain reasoning
\par
schemes within the same basic structure. In
\par
practice, the decision-analytic approach to
\par
knowledge engineering can focus on different
\par
aspects of a domain and result in a signifi-
\par
cantly different structure than rule-based and
\par
other expert system paradigms. The resulting
\par
structural differences can cause greater differ-
\par
ences in performance than the differences in
\par
inference mechanism. To investigate differ-
\par
ences in the knowledge engineering process,
\par
Henrion and Cooley (1987) examined the
\par
construction of two diagnostic systems for
\par
the same problem, namely, the diagnosis and
\par
the treatment of disorders of apple orchards.
\par
The systems were based on decision analysis
\par
and a rule-based paradigm, respectively. Part
\par
of the inference network for the rule-based
\par
system is shown in figure 8a, and the corre-
\par
