\par
The most obvious difference between the
\par
models is that the influence diagram contains
\par
an explicit model of the orchardist's costs and
\par
preferences (the diamond node) as a function
\par
of the potential costs and benefits of fungicide
\par
treatment. Where the rule-based system
\par
simply asserted that treatment was recom-
\par
mended if a fungus infection was suspected,
\par
the decision model explicitly weighed the
\par
costs and probabilities. The influence diagram
\par
being somewhat larger took somewhat more
\par
effort to build and quantify. However, the
\par
decision-analytic model could cover a wider
\par
range of cases and types of inference.
\par
The Perfect versus the Fallible Expert
\par
The difference in the treatment of discrepan-
\par
cies between system performance and the
\par
expert's expectation illustrates a fundamental
\par
difference between the two paradigms. The
\par
rule-based system was tuned so that its behav-
\par
ior coincided as closely as possible with the
\par
expert's judgments. Thus, the expert was the
\par
``gold standard'' for performance. In contrast,
\par
when a result of the decision analysis (that
\par
the fungicide treatment was barely worth-
\par
while in a particular case) ran counter to the
\par
expert's intuition, the decision analyst
\par
explored with the expert the possibility that
\par
the result inferred by normative methods
\par
from the expert's more strongly held opinions
\par
might be correct. Indeed, it turned out that
\par
the analysis of this apparent discrepancy led
\par
the expert to a new insight about the nature
\par
of the problem, and he changed his intu-
\par
itions. This experience illustrates the way in
