the subjective probabilities and costs estimat-
\par
ed by expert mechanics, performed about 20
\par
percent better in terms of expected cost than
\par
the test sequences suggested by the same
\par
mechanics. This improvement in perfor-
\par
mance was robust to substantial errors in the
\par
assessed costs and probabilities. This study is
\par
another empirical indication of how norma-
\par
tive inference methods can result in signifi-
\par
cant improvement over unaided human
\par
expert judgment.
\par
Recent Applications
\par
No matter how great the theoretical appeal of
\par
a set of principles of rationality, most practi-
\par
tioners that build knowledge-based systems
\par
are unlikely to be convinced that the approach
\par
is worth trying in the absence of substantial
\par
successful applications. Although some aspects
\par
of the technology, including knowledge engi-
\par
neering tools, inference algorithms, and
\par
explanation techniques for large networks,
\par
have yet to reach full maturity, a few signifi-
\par
cant applications are already in practical use,
\par
with several more on the way to completion.
\par
Of course, decision analysis has been applied
\par
to practical decision making for over 20 years
\par
in business, engineering, medicine, and other
\par
domains. In many cases, the product has been
\par
a computer-based tool to support decisions.
\par
However, these tools have typically focused
\par
on a narrowly specified class of decisions for
\par
which only a limited number of uncertain
\par
variables (a few tens at most) are used. Our
\par
focus here is on systems that can legitimately
\par
be termed knowledge based, that is, systems
\par
that incorporate belief networks of significant
\par
size. Most such systems to date aim at diag-
\par
nosis in medicine or engineered systems. We
\par
will describe the PATHFINDER and INTELLIPATH
\par
project, and briefly mention several others.
\par
The PATHFINDER project has used a probabilis-
\par
tic and decision analytic approach to develop
