\par
The academic project has given rise to a com-
\par
mercial system, INTELLIPATH, which provides
\par
diagnostic support for surgical pathologists in
\par
several domains of pathology. Over 250
\par
INTELLIPATH systems have been sold worldwide,
\par
and it represents one of the few commercially
\par
successful applications of knowledge- based
\par
technology for medical diagnosis. The history
\par
of PATHFINDER and INTELLIPATH provides a
\par
number of interesting lessons.
\par
Surgical pathologists make diagnoses based
\par
on the observation of features in a section of
\par
tissue under the microscope. The diagnosis of
\par
diseases from lymph node biopsies is one of
\par
the most difficult tasks of surgical pathology.
\par
Although experts show agreement with one
\par
another, diagnoses by community hospital
\par
pathologists have to be changed by expert
\par
hematopathologists in as many as 50 percent
\par
of the cases (Velez-Garcia et al. 1983). It is
\par
critical to differentiate benign from malignant
\par
conditions and to classify precisely malignant
\par
lymphoma so that the most appropriate form
\par
of surgery, radiation therapy, or chemothera-
\par
py can be chosen. For these reasons, improve-
\par
ments in diagnostic performance have the
\par
potential for significant benefits.
\par
Early work on PATHFINDER at the Stanford
\par
University Medical Computer Science Group
\par
explored a variety of nonprobabilistic and
\par
quasiprobabilistic schemes (Heckerman
\par
1988). These schemes included a rule-based
\par
production system using propositional logic,
\par
EMYCIN certainty factors, and Dempster-Shafer
\par
belief functions (Shafer 1976). Finally,
\par
PATHFINDER investigators tried a Bayesian prob-
\par
abilistic scheme, reinterpreting the numeric
\par
beliefs as probabilities and assuming the con-
\par
ditional independence of findings. The switch
\par
to the probabilistic interpretation resulted in
\par
a qualitative improvement in performance,
\par
that is, agreement with expert diagnosticians.
\par
This improvement was immediately apparent
\par
and remarked on by the expert in a blinded
\par
study, where the expert did not know which
\par
representation was being used.
\par
The current PATHFINDER belief network
\par
includes about 30 different types of primary
\par
and secondary malignant hematopoietic dis-
\par
eases of the lymph node and about 30 differ-
\par
ent benign diseases that are easily confused
\par
with malignant lymphomas (Heckerman,
\par
Horvitz, and Nathwani 1991). The diseases
\par
are assumed to be mutually exclusive, an
\par
appropriate assumption in pathology. The
