\par
PATHFINDER performs exact diagnostic infer-
\par
ence in this network in under a second on a
\par
486-based computer.
\par
Although PATHFINDER does not make treatment
\par
recommendations, it does use approximate
\par
expected value of information calculations to
\par
guide the sequencing of questions to those
\par
that will be most valuable in differentiating
\par
among the most probable and important
\par
diagnoses. As a measure of importance, it
\par
uses estimates of the relative costs of misdiag-
\par
nosis, that is, the potential cost of treating for
\par
disease A when disease B is present. Current-
\par
ly, a study funded by the National Cancer
\par
Institute on the clinical efficacy of using the
\par
PATHFINDER belief network is under way at the
\par
University of Southern California.
\par
INTELLIPATH is a commercial system based on
\par
the ideas in PATHFINDER and is distributed by
\par
the American Society of Clinical Pathologists.
\par
It provides a practical diagnostic aid for surgi-
\par
cal pathologists. INTELLIPATH integrates proba-
\par
bilistic reasoning with a set of supportive
\par
tools, including an analog videodisc library of
\par
microscope images to illustrate typical fea-
\par
tures, text information on diseases and
\par
microscopic features, references to the litera-
\par
ture, and an automated report generator.
\par
Hitherto, knowledge bases encoding the
\par
knowledge of teams of expert surgical pathol-
\par
ogists are available for 10 of 40 different
\par
tissue types.
\par
Another significant medical application is
\par
MUNIN, a system for diagnosis of neuromuscu-
\par
lar disorders developed by a team at the Uni-
\par
versity of Aalborg, Denmark (Andreassen et
\par
al. 1987). MUNIN is implemented in HUGIN, a
\par
general tool for construction and inference
\par
on belief networks (Andersen 1989). MUNIN
\par
contains about 1000 chance nodes with as
\par
many as 7 states each and has significant
\par
multiply connected portions. Exact diagnos-
\par
tic inference takes under 5 seconds.
\par
A third medical application, SLEEP CONSULTANT,
\par
diagnoses sleep disorders and related pul-
\par
monary disorders (Nino-Marcia and Shwe
\par
1991). It was developed by Knowledge Indus-
\par
tries (KI), a Palo Alto, California, company
\par
that specializes in the development of proba-
\par
bilistic and decision-analytic systems. Data
\par
are automatically obtained from a digital
\par
polysomnograph monitor that records
\par
dynamic physiological data while the patient
\par
is sleeping (or trying to). In the physician's
\par
office, the data are transferred to the diagnos-
\par
tic system and combined with other data
\par
obtained from patient interviews and records
\par
86 AI MAGAZINEto obtain probabilities for possible disorders.
\par
The system is being distributed by CNS, a
\par
Minneapolis company specializing in sleep
\par
diagnosis equipment.
\par
A number of probabilistic systems are being
\par
developed for the diagnosis of capital equip-
\par
ment. Knowledge Industries has developed a
\par
diagnostic system for a major airline that
\par
deals with jet engine problems (figures 9 and
\par
10). Another KI system is under development
\par
for the Electric Power Research Institute to
\par
diagnose problems with large gas turbines
\par
used by electric utilities. SRI International,
\par
together with Ontario Hydro, a Canadian
\par
power utility, developed GEMS (generator expert
\par
monitoring system), a prototype system for
\par
the diagnosis of electric power generator
\par
problems. This project is interesting in that
\par
it was originally developed using a forward-
\par
chaining rule-based system with certainty
\par
factors. Because of difficulties with this repre-
\par
sentation, the authors, who had no prior
\par
experience with probabilistic schemes, con-
\par
verted it to a belief network representation.
\par
They found this approach a far more natural
\par
and convenient representation of the causal
\par
knowledge of the experts. The resulting net-
\par
work contains about 1000 diagnostic nodes
\par
and 1300 links, primarily noisy-Ors (Klempn-
\par
er, Kornfeld, and Lloyd 1991).
\par
Perhaps the largest Bayesian belief network
\par
currently in existence was developed as part
\par
of the QMR-DT Project (Shwe et al. 1991). QMR
\par
(Quick Medical Reference) is a knowledge
\par
base and diagnostic aid for internal medicine,
\par
a development of the INTERNIST-1 system.
\par
(Miller, Masarie and Myers 1986). QMR con-
\par
tains carefully encoded knowledge of almost
\par
600 diseases, about 4,000 findings (signs,
\par
symptoms, lab results, and so on), and 40,000
\par
links between them. QMR uses a heuristic,
\par
numeric representation of the uncertain rela-
\par
tionships among diseases and findings. The
\par
QMR-DT (for QMR Decision Theory) Project---at
\par
Stanford University, Carnegie Mellon Univer-
\par
sity, and the University of Pittsburgh---has
\par
developed a probabilistic reformulation of
\par
QMR. This reformulated system provides a rig-
\par
orous probabilistic interpretation of the inde-
\par
pendence assumptions and a mapping from
\par
the numeric associations to conditional prob-
\par
abilities. The assumptions in the first version,
\par
QMR-BN (QMR Belief Net) (figure 11), include
\par
marginal independence of diseases given age
\par
and sex of the patient, conditional indepen-
\par
dence of findings, and noisy-Or influences.
\par
The system also includes leak rates, that is,
\par
probabilities that findings will be observed, in
\par
the absence of causal diseases. QMR-BN adds
\par
prior probabilities for diseases that are obtained
\par
Articles
\par
from empirical data on prevalence rates.
\par
The purpose of the QMR-DT Project is to
\par
explore the practicality and value of a coher-
\par
ent probabilistic interpretation compared to
\par
the existing heuristic QMR scheme. It has also
\par
stimulated the development of new inference
\par
algorithms: Because multiple diseases are pos-
\par
sible and because the network is highly multi-
\par
ply connected, standard exact algorithms are
\par
rendered impractical. Random Sampling with
\par
likelihood weighting (Shwe et al. 1991) and
\par
the search-based TOP N algorithm (Henrion
\par
1991) have both proved practical. Initial
\par
results have demonstrated comparable perfor-
\par
mance to the original QMR on a number of
\par
test cases (Middleton et al. 1991).
\par
Conclusions
\par
We introduced the basic ideas of probabilistic
\par
reasoning and decision analysis and reviewed
\par
their application to work on knowledge-based
\par
expert systems. Historically, interest in heuristic
\par
uncertainty calculi and rule-based representa-
\par
tions arose partly in response to the computa-
\par
tional difficulties and restrictive expressiveness
\par
of the early probabilistic expert systems. How-
\par
ever, recent work has revealed that these
\par
heuristic schemes have problems of their
\par
own: restrictive assumptions and inconsisten-
\par
cies. A decision-theoretic perspective makes it
\par
clear that no scheme for reasoning and deci-
\par
sion making under uncertainty can avoid
\par
making assumptions about prior beliefs and
\par
independence, whether these assumptions are
\par
implicit or explicit.
\par
Recognition of the difficulties of the heuris-
\par
tic approaches, coupled with the recent devel-
\par
opment of more efficient and expressive
\par
representations from decision analysis, has
\par
stimulated a renewed interest in probabilistic
\par
and decision-theoretic approaches. In particu-
\par
lar, belief networks and influence diagrams
\par
provide an appealing knowledge representa-
\par
tion that can express uncertain knowledge,
\par
beliefs, and preferences in both qualitative
\par
and quantitative forms in a flexible yet princi-
\par
pled fashion. Among the advantages of these
\par
representations, we have discussed the follow-
\par
ing. Knowledge, whether expert judgment or
\par
from empirical data, may be expressed in
\par
whichever direction is most natural, general-
\par
ly, but not necessarily in the causal direction.
\par
Probabilistic algorithms use this form to
\par
reason in whichever direction is
\par
required---causal, diagnostic, or intercausal.
\par
Thus, unlike rule-based representations, there
\par
is no essential link between the form of
\par
encoding and the form of reasoning for
\par
WINTER 1991 87Articles
\par
which it is used. The influence diagram also
\par
includes an explicit model of decisions and
\par
the utilities of their possible outcomes. This
\par
modularity maintains separation between
\par
judgments of likelihood and judgments of
\par
preferences. These representations provide
\par
the basis for a rapidly increasing array of
\par
developments.
\par
A number of diagnostic systems based on
\par
probabilistic and decision-analytic ideas have
\par
achieved expert system status in that they
\par
have achieved expert-level performance. Indi-
\par
cations are increasingly positive that these
\par
methods will scale up for large knowledge
\par
bases. A variety of general tools, or shells, for
\par
building and reasoning with belief networks
\par
and influence diagrams are now available
\par
(Andersen 1989; Arnold and Henrion 1990;
\par
Srinivas and Breese 1990).
\par
Current research focuses on the development
\par
of improved tools for knowledge acquisition,
\par
the search for more efficient inference algo-
\par
rithms for complex multiply connected belief
\par
networks and influence diagrams, and more
\par
effective techniques for explaining decision-
\par
analytic reasoning. Some topics that deserve
\par
particular attention for further research
\par
include (1) the extending of the integration
\par
of influence diagrams with the representation
\par
of other kinds of semantic network notions,
\par
including causal relations, inheritance and
\par
abstraction hierarchies; (2) improved approxi-
\par
mate inference algorithms for large networks;
\par
(3) compilation techniques for influence dia-
\par
grams to support rapid inference; (4) a wider
\par
array of prototypical influences to facilitate
\par
knowledge engineering; (5) intelligent sensi-
\par
tivity analysis and more sophisticated knowl-
\par
edge-based aids to guide problem structuring;
\par
and (6) better error theories of the effects of
\par
simplifications and approximations. Of par-
\par
ticular benefit for knowledge encoding,
\par
inference, and explanation would be the
\par
development of hybrid qualitative and quan-
\par
titative approaches, including ranges of prob-
\par
abilities and values and order-of-magnitude
\par
estimates, to allow a wider array of techniques
\par
for handling partial and vague specifications
\par
that are qualitatively consistent with the
\par
principles of decision analysis.
\par
In summary, we believe that the synthesis
\par
of ideas from decision analysis and expert
\par
systems and, more generally, from decision
\par
theory and AI is starting to generate consider-
\par
able mutual benefit. Probability and decision
\par
theory provide a principled basis for automat-
\par
ed reasoning and decision making under
\par
uncertainty. Decision analysis provides practi-
\par
cal ideas and techniques for knowledge engi-
\par
neering. AI provides a variety of computational
\par
88 AI MAGAZINE
\par
and representational techniques to help auto-
\par
mate various aspects of decision analysis and,
\par
thus, render it more widely available. This
\par
interaction between decision analysis and
\par
expert systems has already produced some
\par
important contributions to both reasoning
\par
theory and knowledge representation and the
\par
development of a new generation of practical
\par
technology. In the long run, we anticipate
\par
the emergence of a combined enterprise in
\par
which the current separation between the
\par
two technologies disappears.
