@article{hidyClimateAlterationGlobal1994,
  title = {Climate Alteration a Global Issue for the Electric Power Industry in the 21st Century},
  author = {Hidy, G.M. and Spencer, D.F.},
  year = 1994,
  journal = {Energy Policy},
  volume = {22},
  number = {12},
  pages = {NaN-NaN},
  doi = {10.1016/0301-4215(94)90016-7},
  langid = {english},
  note = {\emph{Energy Policy }1994 22 (12) 1005-1027
\par
\textbf{Climate alteration}
\par
\textbf{A global issue for the electric power industry in}
\par
\textbf{the 21st century}
\par
\textbf{G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
\emph{Development of global electric generation and distribu-}
\par
\emph{tion systems will be central to mankind's economic and}
\par
\emph{social well being. However, electricity is likely to be}
\par
\emph{produced mainly from fossil fuel combustion for the}
\par
\emph{foreseeable future. As a consequence, this global energy}
\par
\emph{sector will contribute to growing carbon dioxide emis-}
\par
\emph{sions, with a potential for climate alteration. Concern}
\par
\emph{for potentially adverse climate effects has led to consid-}
\par
\emph{eration of strategies for managing GHG emissions. With}
\par
\emph{present technologies, extreme reduction strategies will}
\par
\emph{be costly and may create significant stress on the pro-}
\par
\emph{jected worm economic and energy systems.}
\par
\emph{Technological options have been evaluated to a modest}
\par
\emph{degree. These range from conventional emission control}
\par
\emph{approaches to geo-engineering opportunities and bio-}
\par
\emph{spheric carbon management. To be practical, these}
\par
\emph{options need to be developed further and demonstrated}
\par
\emph{to properly assess their operational characteristics.}
\par
\emph{Keywords: }Climate; Electricity; Mitigation
\par
As humanity enters the 21st century, electricity contin-
\par
ues to demonstrate its societal value and versatility as a
\par
major energy source. It is virtually impossible to envi-
\par
sion industrialization and societal well being without
\par
electrification. While electricity has proven itself to be
\par
an enormous benefit, \textbf{there are }byproducts of its produc-
\par
tion and distribution that have undesirable effects on the
\par
environment. Most of these derive from the combustion
\par
of fossil carbonaceous fuels converting thermal energy
\par
to electrical energy. Power plant combustion of coal, oil
\par
and natural gas emits air pollutants such as nitrogen
\par
oxides (NO and NO2), and, for some fuels, sulfur diox-
\par
ide (SO2), as well as traces of heavy metal
\par
contaminants, and organic pyrolysis byproducts. In
\par
addition, fossil fuel combustion produces carbon diox-
\par
The authors are with the Electric Power Research Institute
\par
Palo Alto, CA 94303, USA
\par
ide (CO2), a naturally occurring gas that has important
\par
properties of radiant energy absorption. Unabated emis-
\par
sions per unit of energy consumed of these constituents
\par
are generally largest from direct coal combustion.
\par
At least in the first half of the 21st century, fossil fuels
\par
will remain plentiful, so that it is expected that they will
\par
be used extensively throughout the world as a principal
\par
source of electrical energy production. Even though
\par
other energy sources such as hydroelectric, other 'renew-
\par
ables' and nuclear are available, they are generally
\par
expected to be minor worldwide components, at least
\par
until the mid-21 st century.l World society will thus nec-
\par
essarily need to assess the projected growth in electricity
\par
consumption in the light of the potential for growing pro-
\par
duction of combustion gases, and a declining inventory
\par
of known fossil fuel reserves. The trade offs between the
\par
accommodation of population growth and economic
\par
growth with electricity use and environmental stress will
\par
become increasingly important throughout the world.
\par
The objective of this paper is to review the global
\par
issues associated with expected increases in gaseous
\par
emissions, particularly carbon dioxide, from increased
\par
electricity production, indicating the nature and signifi-
\par
cance of the issue. We emphasize a methodology
\par
integrating information on environmental issues with
\par
social and economic factors to develop informed inter-
\par
national policies. The importance of prioritizing societal
\par
benefits from electrification as opposed to environmen-
\par
tal stress is considered in balancing a societal future.
\par
The paper summarizes the technological choices avail-
\par
able worldwide that could minimize the environmental
\par
impact of increasing electricity production.
\par
Most stresses on the environment are localized
\par
around a pollutant source where relatively high levels of
\par
atmospheric contaminants are found. However, in recent
\par
years potentially adverse environmental effects have
\par
also been identified over large regions, or even globally.
\par
A global scale pollution phenomenon has appeared in
\par
the observed increase in atmospheric CO 2 concentration
\par
with an increase in fossil fuel combustion.
\par
0301-4215/94/12 1005-23 \copyright{} 1994 Butterworth-Heinemann Ltd 1005\emph{Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
The atmospheric build up of gases like CO 2 would
\par
have little environmental significance if it were not for
\par
the fact that they are capable of absorbing thermal radi-
\par
ation. CO 2 is essentially transparent to the wavelengths
\par
of solar radiation entering the earth's atmosphere, but
\par
absorbs (infrared) wavelengths of emitted or outgoing
\par
radiation. At natural levels, water vapor, CO 2 and other
\par
radiatively active or greenhouse gases (GHGs) insure
\par
that the earth exists at a moderate surface temperature.
\par
However, added CO 2 will result in increased energy
\par
being captured by the earth's atmosphere. This energy
\par
may either warm the atmosphere at the earth's surface,
\par
or result in increased energy exchange between the
\par
atmosphere and oceans, probably altering climate on
\par
both regional and global levels. Warming is of great
\par
concern because projections suggest that at recent rapid
\par
rates of CO 2 increase in the atmosphere, warming to
\par
temperatures not seen for many thousands of years on
\par
earth could occur by the mid-to-late 21st century.
\par
While recognizing the beneficial aspects of climate
\par
warming, some scientists have hypothesized that
\par
important disturbances in both managed and natural
\par
ecosystems could result from climate alteration. Some
\par
believe that these changes could have serious economic
\par
consequences, and could affect the sustainability of the
\par
earth systems. 2
\par
Increased energy exchange between the atmosphere
\par
and oceans also may alter the climate. Formation of
\par
large-scale thermal gradients with strong convection
\par
cells can result in an increased frequency and strength of
\par
major storms, eg typhoons or hurricanes, monsoons, and
\par
tornadoes. Changes in atmosphere ocean thermal struc-
\par
ture may shift the regions where these intense storms are
\par
present. Intensification of convective mixing could also
\par
obscure the expected atmospheric temperature rise for a
\par
significant period, making it particularly important that
\par
variables other than temperature, such as strengths or
\par
intensity of large-scale pressure systems, be analyzed as
\par
the atmospheric CO 2 concentration increases.
\par
There are other GHGs that are linked with electricity
\par
production and use. These include methane (CH4) - nat-
\par
ural gas from fuel production and transport - nitrous
\par
oxide (N20), a trace nitrogen oxide in fossil fuel com-
\par
bustion, and chlorofluorohydrocarbons (CFCs), as used
\par
in refrigerants. Thus, the global character of the climate
\par
alteration is influenced by several gases released from
\par
production or use of electricity. Since 03 is also a radia-
\par
tively active gas, its potential growth in atmospheric
\par
concentrations from NO or NO 2 emissions is indirectly
\par
linked with electricity.
\par
For the purposes of discussion, climate alteration
\par
from atmospheric GHG accumulation is believed to be
\par
the principal global environmental issue facing the
\par
electricity industry today. We have noted that several
\par
1006 GHGs may be of importance. Policy makers and the
\par
electric utilities have concentrated on CO 2 as their prin-
\par
cipal concern, given the volume of these emissions. In
\par
addition, the growing use of natural gas domestically
\par
and liquefied natural gas (LNG) overseas for electricity
\par
production may result in increased emissions of CH 4,
\par
which has a much greater global warming potential
\par
(GWP) than CO 2. A very recent evaluation of CH 4
\par
losses associated with LNG imports by Japan and
\par
France indicate the potential significance of this trend)
\par
Recent study of CFCs suggests that their GWP may be
\par
weak, in that their energy absorption in the lower atmo-
\par
sphere seems to be offset by their cooling action in the
\par
upper atmosphere.
\par
\textbf{Integrating framework for risk analysis}
\par
Analysis of trade offs associated with energy use and
\par
environmental stress is well addressed through a concep-
\par
tual or analytical framework integrating natural systems
\par
with human activity. 4 A schematic diagram of such an
\par
integrating framework is shown in Figure 1. The natural
\par
linkages between the earth's atmosphere, land, oceans
\par
and ice are shown with the sun. Processes in these ele-
\par
ments create climate, which in turn affects human and
\par
natural ecosystems. The linkage with the human system
\par
is shown mainly on the right side, with key components
\par
being human values and preferences, coupled through
\par
the gross domestic product (GDP) with economic activ-
\par
ities and human institutional response. The natural cycle
\par
components are shown on the left side of Figure 1. The
\par
influence of people on the natural cycle derives from
\par
greenhouse gases and land use.
\par
To begin with, the apparent conflict between environ-
\par
mental preservation and human aspirations is seen in the
\par
values and preferences element of Figure 1. Some have
\par
advocated that the drive toward economic parity
\par
between the developed and developing world will be a
\par
dominant consideration for nations in the next century.
\par
Economic development will be driven to a great extent
\par
by international access to low cost, reliable energy
\par
sources, particularly electricity.
\par
Human values and preferences essentially direct the
\par
flow of sociopolitical functions into the economic activ-
\par
ity and human response element. From this, greenhouse
\par
gases enter the natural biogeochemical system along
\par
with disturbances in land use (and terrestrial biosphere).
\par
These 'forcings' create alterations in natural climate
\par
interactions which create environmental effects. The
\par
identification or perception of the potential significance
\par
of these effects then finds its way back to human values
\par
and preferences and consequently modulates economic
\par
activity. Through weighing human values and prefer-
\par
ences by a social-political progression, choices are made
