\textbf{Weighing the benefits of electrification}
\par
While much as been said about potential environmental
\par
risks of climate alteration, little has been said about
\par
potential benefits, or national trade offs accounting for
\par
human welfare and increased energy use, especially
\par
electricity.
\par
On a per capita basis, about three-fourths of the
\par
world's population consumes only one-fourth of the
\par
world's energy. 5 Only about 30\% of the world's popula-
\par
tion has other than minimal access to electricity supply
\par
today, and this group has the largest annual population
\par
growth rates. Even without population growth, there will
\par
be enormous increased demand in the developing world
\par
for electrification to improve the opportunity for human
\par
well being.
\par
Key factors benefiting directly from increased
\par
electrification include:
\par
{$\bullet$} improved overall economic base (national, family);
\par
{$\bullet$} improved public health management through protec-
\par
tion of drinking water and sewage treatment;
\par
{$\bullet$} local refrigeration (village, home) for food preserva-
\par
tion;
\par
{$\bullet$} access to high technology health care (diagnostic and
\par
operative);
\par
{$\bullet$} diversity of industrial infrastructure, robotics, com-
\par
puters;
\par
{$\bullet$} enhanced communications and data availability; and
\par
{$\bullet$} enhanced educational programs.
\par
The expected improvements in each of these categories
\par
can be assessed by such variables as: growth in GDP;
\par
decreased infant mortality and expected lifetime
\par
increases; dietary variations and fish, fowl and meat
\par
preservation; development of capital and opportunities
\par
for new industrial enterprises; increased financial mar-
\par
kets and currency value; and the average level of
\par
education of developing nations and the quantity of pro-
\par
fessionals.
\par
While establishing a clear basis for estimating the
\par
integrated human benefits of electrification will have
\par
substantial uncertainties, this foreseen energy preference
\par
provides a very specific basis for weighing environmen-
\par
tal risks against the benefits of electrification.
\par
\textbf{Economic activity and energy growth}
\par
\emph{Electricity and energy supply}
\par
Given the assumption that energy and electrification will
\par
be central to fulfilling national economic aspirations,
\par
what guidance to decision making can derive from a risk
\par
\emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12 }1007\emph{Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
analysis incorporating the elements in Figure 1?
\par
Projection of future economic activity and energy
\par
demand is an essential ingredient to the analysis.
\par
The future of the international energy market is predi-
\par
cated on its growth in line with growth in population,
\par
and in the economic well being of that population, as
\par
measured, for example, by the annual GDP change.
\par
Historically, growth in electricity production has fol-
\par
lowed closely economic growth, as sometimes measured
\par
by GDP. 6 This pattern has been tempered to an extent in
\par
the developed nations by changes in technology that
\par
have reduced substantially electricity use per capita.
\par
Growth in electricity use and production is projected
\par
through the early 21st century to be quite modest in the
\par
developed world. In fact, many of the Organization for
\par
Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) coun-
\par
tries have over capacity in production. Very substantial
\par
growth in electricity production is projected for the
\par
developing world in the next century that will parallel
\par
the predicted economic growth historically established
\par
in the developed world. This growth also will be further
\par
fueled by substantial population growth in these countries.
\par
Projections of expansion in energy requirements and
\par
electricity production suggest that large-scale curtail-
\par
ment of fossil fuel use in the developed nations will
\par
accomplish relatively little to constrain GHG emissions
\par
in the next century, since the growth of energy use in
\par
developing countries is far larger than that projected for
\par
the developed world. 7 Of the three largest electricity
\par
producers in the developing world - India, China and
\par
Brazil - the two former will rely mainly on coal, and the
\par
latter will continue to rely heavily on hydroelectric
\par
power. For most other nations, coal, oil and natural gas
\par
represent major fuel components. 8 This type of fuel mix
\par
is expected to be accessible to the developing world well
\par
into the 21st century, as shown in Figure 2. Thus inter-
\par
nationally, coal, oil and natural gas, or liquefied natural
\par
gas (LNG) are likely to be the fuels of choice for genera-
\par
tion sources well into the mid-21st century. Fossil fuels
\par
will probably not be supplanted by renewables or by
\par
nuclear power generation. The use of other energy sup-
\par
plies, including biomass and renewables, is predicted to
\par
grow, but they are expected to continue to play a minor
\par
role relative to fossil fuels. If this is indeed the case, then
\par
substantial effort needs to be focused on providing tech-
\par
nology options that will manage atmospheric CO 2 levels
\par
in the light of the projected continued use of these fossil
\par
resources.
\par
The extent and severity of a GHG emission problem
\par
is likely to be dictated by an important combination of
\par
factors. These are: (1) the amount of fossil carbon fuel
\par
available; (2) the rate of consumption of fossil carbon
