\par
\emph{Projection of carbon emissions}
\par
Many scenarios have reported future carbon emissions,
\par
and with evaluated world energy requirements. 9 These
\par
projections generally give similar growth in carbon
\par
emissions through the first third to half of the 21st cen-
\par
tury. They assume growth in energy production that is
\par
moderated by increased use of technology for improved
\par
energy efficiency, rising fuel prices and a mix of genera-
\par
tion or mitigation technologies to minimize world CO 2
\par
emissions. Projections suggest a doubling from 1990
\par
annual emissions (approximately 6 Gt C pa) by 2050,
\par
and a quadrupling of 1990 annual emissions by the 22nd
\par
century.
\par
Energy growth and carbon emissions can also be pro-
\par
jected simply from the annual growth rate increase
\par
embodied in recent history. Such a projection assumes
\par
an unconstrained world generation technology and fuel
\par
mix which would be static at least through mid-century,
\par
and that new major steps to abate CO 2 emissions will
\par
not be taken. Improved efficiency of energy use is
\par
accounted for in the modest growth rate change for the
\par
developed countries, but much less so for developing
\par
countries, as indicated for three scenarios in Table 1 and
\par
Figure 3. These are similar to assumptions built into the
\par
Manne and Richels model estimates. \textasciitilde{$^\circ$} However, the
\par
compounding is not moderated by factors such as tech-
\par
nology development and use, fuel prices or other price
\par
constraints, as in the Manne and Richels \textasciitilde 1 work and the
\par
