\par
and Manne and Richels, use a world energy projection
\par
that focuses on a 'backstop' technology, which super-
\par
sedes conventional fossil fuel use, such as advanced
\par
nuclear power. Instead, the higher rates of emission in
\par
Figure 3 are dominated by the compounding of growth
\par
in emissions from the developing world based on GDP
\par
of about 3.5\% per year, excluding price constraints and
\par
the continuing strong dependence on fossil fuels.
\par
Figure 3 also includes the IPCC high and low projec-
\par
