\par
\textbf{Carbon emissions and environmental stress}
\par
Given the high probability that atmospheric CO 2 levels
\par
will continue to increase, the next step is to assess poten-
\par
tial alterations in climate, and the potential consequent
\par
environmental implications of such changes. The state
\par
of knowledge in these areas is summarized using ex-
\par
amples to illustrate the diversity of results emerging
\par
from scientific studies. The examples indicate that
\par
today's knowledge is generally too uncertain for politi-
\par
cal decisions to constrain the world energy supply solely
\par
in favor of CO 2 emission reductions.
\par
Using different types of climate prediction models,
\par
scientists have estimated that a doubling of atmospheric
\par
CO 2 concentrations from \emph{pre-industrial }levels could
\par
warm the Earth's surface air temperature by 1.5 to
\par
4.5 {$^\circ$} C. \textasciitilde 5 The recent IPCC estimates suggest that doub-
\par
ling of atmospheric CO 2 concentrations may take place
\par
by mid- to late 21 st century. Estimates of atmospheric
\par
concentrations of CO 2 are included for the unconstrained
\par
compounding scenarios in Table 1, based on a (current)
\par
CO 2 airborne fraction of 0.57. These projected high CO 2
\par
emission rates would result in projected atmospheric
\par
CO 2 concentrations far larger than 600 ppm by the end
\par
of the century. Keller and Goldstein estimate that, as the
\par
buffering capacity of the oceans is altered during the
\par
next century, the airborne fraction may be closer to 1.0
\par
toward the end of the period, resulting in even higher
\par
CO 2 concentrations than shown in Table 1.16 If
\par
1010 \emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12}atmospheric CO 2 levels run into the 1000 ppm range,
\par
global surface air temperature increase might run well
\par
beyond the 1.5 to 4.5{$^\circ$}C range.
\par
With recent progress in knowledge about climate
\par
forcing, factors have emerged that tend to moderate
\par
warming. For example, calculations estimating the nega-
\par
tive forcing from tropospheric aerosols suggest that their
\par
cooling effect could substantially moderate the GHG
\par
effect. \textasciitilde 7 Changes in aerosol concentrations and their
\par
chemistry also could change cloud reflectivity, by affect-
\par
ing hydrometeor size distributions, adding to the
\par
negative forcing. These modifications to theoretical
\par
models will continue for the foreseeable future, making
\par
current projections of global surface temperature rise
\par
quite uncertain.
\par
Paleoclimatological records have shown that the
\par
earth's past has recorded major shifts in climate, accom-
\par
panied by changes in sea level and evolutionary
\par
responses from natural ecosystems, as well as, more
\par
recently, patterns of human life. Atmospheric levels of
\par
carbon dioxide are estimated to have been 10-20 times
\par
present levels in the Ordovician period (500 million
\par
years ago) and perhaps 10 times present levels as
\par
'recently' as the Cretaceous period (100 million years
\par
ago). \textasciitilde 8 There are two very important conclusions from
\par
these results, namely that the Earth did not show a 'run-
\par
away' greenhouse gas response at very high CO 2 levels;
\par
and that temperature rises of 6-12{$^\circ$}C have been esti-
\par
mated for the Cretaceous 'warming' period.
\par
Extrapolation of this prehistory into future effects of
\par
carbon dioxide concentration increases has suggested
\par
that disruptions could be experienced from global warm-
\par
ing, especially in human economies that are
\par
agriculturally dominated, rely on a maritime environ-
\par
ment or exist in drought stressed regimes. Within the
\par
current projections of climate alterations, such changes
\par
appear to be manageable. Other severe global-scale dis-
\par
turbances, such as those discussed above, or modified
\par
ocean thermal current shifts that reinforce large-scale
\par
climate shifts, also could produce serious instabilities in
\par
the climate, t9 No direct evidence of such a disturbance
\par
has as yet come from mathematical modeling\textasciitilde{} although
\par
meaningful atmosphere--ocean coupled models are now
\par
being developed.
\par
Reducing uncertainties in prediction of climate change,
\par
as well as environmental effects, appears to be critical to
\par
insightful decision making. While some believe that the
\par
results from global scale models offer a sufficient frame-
\par
work for defining climate change, 2{$^\circ$} others are less
\par
sanguine about climate warming predictions, or other
\par
global or regional climate alterations. Even with satellite
\par
data, accurate short-range, regional weather predictions
\par
are limited to three to four days. The debate about the reli-
\par
ability of modeled climate change continues. 21
\par
\emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12 Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
While correspondence between model results and
\par
observations is a conventional standard for evaluation,
\par
little has been done to compare climate models with his-
\par
torically observed patterns of climate change. Coarse
\par
qualitative similarities between GCM climate predic-
\par
tions and atmospheric behavior have been reported.
\par
Very few detailed intercomparisons are available.
\par
However, there are a number of reported inconsistencies
\par
between model estimates and observed atmospheric
\par
conditions. Observations of hemispheric differences in
\par
surface temperature history between the northern and
\par
southern hemispheres were problematic in model predic-
\par
tions until atmosphere-ocean coupled calculations were
\par
made. These inferred the importance of the oceanic
\par
influence on hemispheric temperature history. Models
\par
also predict important latitudinal differences in average
\par
surface temperature increase with increasing atmo-
\par
spheric GHG concentration. For example, models indi-
\par
cate that the Arctic regions should exhibit stronger
\par
warming than the middle and low latitude regions when
\par
carbon dioxide concentrations reach twice pre-anthropo-
\par
genic levels ie about 560 ppm. Lindzen noted that
\par
records of temperature observations do not support this
\par
differentiation. 22
\par
Recently Kahl \emph{et al }reported an analysis of a 30 year
\par
record of Arctic temperature observations from ice
\par
islands and other locations. 23 These data do not display
\par
any significant warming between the 1956 and 1986
\par
time period. However, climate model calculations for
\par
temperature rise with atmospheric CO 2 level increases
\par
corresponding to this period do not indicate a tempera-
\par
ture increase that could be detected in the variability of
\par
the observations.
\par
The example of Arctic temperature intercomparisons
\par
between models and observations illustrate well the
\par
inconclusiveness of such efforts. If we attempt to
\par
extrapolate GCM calculations from a 500 km grid scale
\par
to regional conditions within such a grid, the resulting
\par
comparisons show that GCMs cannot even yield reliable
\par
predictions of local conditions. This is especially
\par
important for risk assessment since local or regional
\par
conditions are of principal interest in these instances.
\par
The discussion of climate model predictions identifies
\par
very large uncertainties in these predictions, even though
\par
the models give important and useful insight about the
\par
forcing of climate alteration from human activities.
\par
However, to estimate the risk of future changes, we note
\par
that the large uncertainties in carbon emission predictions
\par
are compounded substantially by uncertainties in the cli-
\par
mate model projection. This uncertainty correspondingly
\par
continues in a chain as an analyst moves to estimates of
\par
effects of climate alteration.
\par
A chain of broadening uncertainty in the occurrence
\par
of and effects associated with climate alteration begins
\par
1011\emph{Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
with the predictions of global atmospheric change over
\par
the next century, and is compounded at each step by
\par
great uncertainties in predicted regional weather sensi-
\par
tivity, ocean level and current disturbance, human
\par
system disruption, and ecosystem responses etc. \emph{If any-}
\par
\emph{thing, the uncertainties in the entire chain today are}
\par
\emph{considerably larger than those perceived a decade ago.}
\par
Even the observational data on which current damage
\par
estimates are based need careful scrutiny. As an ex-
\par
ample, sea level is likely to be the most straightforward
\par
measure of impact on human settlement and coastal eco-
\par
systems. Recent careful analysis of historical records
\par
indicates that the sea level change depends critically on
\par
the stability of the bottom level (rising or sinking).
\par
Evaluation of data for geophysical confounding has indi-
\par
cated only a few records can concisely reveal apparent
\par
sea level rise. 24 Those that do suggest a rise of only
\par
1.8 mm/pa. A rise in sea level at the current rate would
\par
result in a cumulative rise of about 20 cm (less than 8 in)
\par
in 2100.
\par
Wigley and Raper have predicted a rise of 20--50 cm
\par
by 2100 with new estimated levels of climate warm-
\par
ing. 25 This is about twice or more than the current rate,
\par
but substantially smaller than the 1990 IPCC estimate,
\par
though the 1992 IPCC assessment recognized smaller
\par
potential for sea level change than their earlier esti-
\par
mate. 26
\par
Perhaps most uncertain are quantitative estimates of
\par
the effects of climate alteration on human well being,
\par
and managed or natural ecosystems. Effects on humans
\par
will vary greatly depending on societal economic devel-
\par
opment. As noted above, for example, people may
\par
experience minimal stress from alteration in surface
\par
temperature. However, severe or persistent drought, or
\par
changes in weather and severe storm patterns could seri-
\par
ously disrupt human activities.
\par
Natural ecosystems are expected to diverge in their
\par
responses to climate warming. 27 In North America, for
\par
example, exploratory study of species of vegetation by
\par
the Nature Conservancy suggests that species with a
\par
broad climatic range today will probably be resilient to
\par
temperature shifts predicted in the next century. 28
\par
However, certain species with narrow climate tolerance,
\par
like the Pacific Coast redwoods, are likely to experience
\par
significant stress. Some ecologists believe that the
\par
effects of climate alteration together with other stress
\par
will affect biodiversity. The ultimate capability to man-
\par
age ecosystems and preserve natural systems will
\par
depend on maintaining a broad range of viable species
\par
through protection of today's gene pool. A measure of
\par
vulnerability of biodiversity has not been determined.
\par
Analytical studies by region outside North America have
\par
not been reported, but are essential to advance knowl-
\par
edge of ecological effects of climate change.
\par
Coarse worldwide studies of economic or environ-
\par
mental stress brought about by climate alteration may be
\par
misleading. What really counts is information on a local
\par
or regional scale, the regime where human and other
\par
ecosystems are specifically affected. An early attempt to
\par
estimate environmental economic impacts on a regional
\par
scale has been reported for the US Midwest. 29 Their
\par
results focus on a US agricultural economy in Missouri,
\par
Iowa, Nebraska and Kansas (MINK). Environmental
\par
impact was estimated using the 1930s' dust bowl condi-
\par
tions as an analog to an altered, warmer Midwest. The
\par
MINK methodology added insight into climate change
\par
and adaptation in an agricultural economy. While the
\par
approach was useful as a first picture of a community
\par
response to change, it fell short of generating regional
\par
damage functions for the economic- effects of climate
\par
alteration over a range of climate change expectations.
\par
The examples cited above and many others discussed
\par
in state of science assessments illustrate that the early
\par
warnings about the potential for and the environmental
\par
effects of climate alteration require attention, but that the
\par
knowledge available is very limited. 3{$^\circ$}
\par
\textbf{Technological options for the future}
\par
So far, we have traced components of the interactions in
\par
Figure 1 from a forcing of human activities to an uncer-
\par
tain response of natural systems. To complete the
\par
interactive human response cycle, knowledge about car-
\par
bon management options is needed. The following
\par
sections summarize the technological options at hand
\par
that could be developed to minimize human intervention
\par
in the carbon cycle. The alternative is to focus on issues
\par
of adaptation if a carbon management strategy for the
\par
electricity sector is not forthcoming on an international
\par
scale.
\par
\emph{Deployment of improved generation technology}
\par
Management of CO 2 emissions from power production
\par
will depend on expeditiously introducing new technol-
\par
ogy worldwide. Particular attention needs to be given to
\par
high thermal efficiency, minimum pollution release and
\par
cost effectiveness. The developed world has different
\par
criteria for technologies from developing nations. In the
\par
former, all these elements weigh in choices, while
\par
today's requirements in developing countries focus
\par
strongly on \emph{least initial cost }choices, particularly least
\par
first cost ie capital. In addition, many developing nations
\par
require simplicity in construction, maintenance and
\par
operations. Since many such countries have only limited
\par
transmission and distribution coverage and capacity,
\par
forms of low cost and geographically distributed genera-
\par
tion are critical.
\par
\emph{1012 Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12}Where natural gas or distillate oil is available, low
\par
cost generation favors aeroderivative gas and industrial
\par
turbines to meet the first three criteria above. This tech-
\par
nology also meets the latter two criteria reasonably well.
\par
Where residual oil or coal is the primary fuel, the
\par
simple, low cost technology is the conventional steam
\par
driven plant without emission controls. However, in the
\par
future these plants are expected to require emission con-
\par
trols with their associated added costs, and added
\par
maintenance requirements. For the developing world
\par
conventional steam plants will likely remain high prior-
\par
ity because of the wealth of experience in their reliable
\par
operation, and in the short term will most probably have
\par
minimal pollution control systems.
\par
Advanced fossil fuel technologies like integrated coal
\par
gasification coupled with either a combustion turbine
\par
combined cycle or fuel cells and fluidized bed combust-
\par
ers will probably become the key choices for developed
\par
nations in the second decade of the 21st century. Even
\par
though these choices offer important improvements in
\par
efficiency and pollution management, their initial pene-
\par
tration into the marketplace may be slow because of
\par
installation factors, relatively high first costs and opera-
\par
tional inexperience. There is a strong need to build first
\par
of a kind (FOAK) units early in the 21st century to mini-
\par
mize impediments to further use.
\par
Combined cycle plants using gas turbines and fuel
\par
cells with natural gas will achieve improvements in con-
\par
version efficiencies of over 50\% in the near future.
\par
Efficiency improvements are also projected for coal pro-
\par
cessing using gasification and advanced combined cycle
\par
or fuel cell technologies from approximately 34\% to
\par
45\% with the most efficient systems. Both of these tech-
\par
nologies offer additional important advantages over
\par
current pulverized coal, in that they provide for larger
\par
reductions in other pollutants such as SO 2 and NO\textasciitilde, as
\par
well as CO 2. While these technologies are being demon-
\par
strated in various countries, their introduction to the
\par
generation inventory is likely to be slow over the next
\par
20 years because of critical constraints and pressure to
\par
use existing plants as long as possible. However, appli-
\par
cation of these advanced systems in developing
\par
countries could substantially reduce the expected emis-
\par
sions from these economies. 3\textasciitilde{}
\par
There are significant but limited opportunities at the
\par
margin of generation needs for renewable technologies,
\par
including biomass combustion and solar or wind power.
\par
Biomass energy use can be effected through direct com-
\par
bustion, through an intermediate stage of gasification or
\par
through liquid fuel production (eg methanol or ethanol).
\par
Increased fuel processing with its inefficiencies is a
\par
trade off with the thermal efficiency of the combustion
\par
system. The inherent limitation to biomass use is the
\par
large agri- or silviculture area required to generate signi-
\par
\emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12 Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
ficant amounts of electricity. Solar or wind power is
\par
particularly useful for geographically distributed
\par
systems, but suffers from being intermittently opera-
\par
tional. A companion energy storage system, like
\par
batteries, is required for such technologies to ensure
\par
system reliability. This adds both to their deployment
\par
and operational costs. While solar power remains very
\par
expensive relative to wind power (in optimal locations)
\par
and conventional fossil fueled generation, cost may not
\par
be critical in remote areas which must have energy
\par
available to support economic development.
\par
Nuclear power generation still offers the promise of a
\par
safe, clean pollutant free electricity source. With known
\par
uranium reserves, nuclear power generation could ulti-
\par
mately generate a significant fraction of world electricity
\par
needs in the 21st century. However, the issues of nuclear
\par
weapons proliferation, construction and operation costs,
\par
accidental releases of radioactivity, and radioactive
\par
waste disposal have all created negative sociopolitical
\par
views towards this technology.
\par
Opportunities for new hydroelectric generation on a
\par
large scale appear to be relatively limited since most of
\par
the large river systems are already dammed. While
\par
hydroelectric generation is a well established and opera-
\par
tionally inexpensive technology, large-scale dam
\par
placement may create side effects leading to carbon
\par
emissions. As forests and grasslands are inundated by
\par
rising waters, decay of submerged vegetation may emit
\par
large quantities of CH 4 and CO 2.
\par
The choices for electricity generation technology are
\par
many, and they can be selected creatively to match
\par
regional fuel resources and worker skills. There is an
\par
expectation for marginal shifts in choice favoring mini-
\par
mization of CO 2 emissions. However, as noted earlier,
\par
world resources of primary energy and financial
\par
resources severely constrain shifts away from reliable,
\par
inexpensive fossil fuel technology.
\par
\emph{The efficiency factor}
\par
From a global standpoint, curtailment of emissions from
\par
electrical utilities has focused mainly on CO 2 reduction,
\par
although management of production, transport and end-
\par
use applications of methane may also become more
\par
significant in the coming decades, as well. CO 2 reduc-
\par
tions, especially in light of our projected growth in
\par
carbon emissions, can be facilitated in two ways; first,
\par
by using less fossil fuel based energy and, second, by
\par
direct reduction in emissions per energy unit produced. 32
\par
These steps seem very simple at first glance. They
\par
involve in the first group a societal effort to reduce
\par
energy consumption per capita through conservation
\par
measures and improved efficiency of machines. In the
\par
second case, measures are involved to improve the
\par
\emph{1013Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
efficiency of converting fossil fuels to electricity or
\par
electricity and other coproducts, or substituting other
\par
primary energy sources to fuel power plants.
\par
For the developed world, the IPCC has projected
\par
energy savings on the order of 1\% per year. This rate is
\par
based largely on the experience of the 1970s and 1980s,
\par
but the sustainability of this level for several decades is
\par
questionable without substantial incentives from
\par
national energy policies. 33 For electrical end-use
\par
devices, there is considerable controversy about the
\par
energy savings that can be gained from widespread use
\par
of more efficient electrical technologies. Substantial
\par
gains from efficiency improvements have been identified
\par
with lighting, variable speed motors, refrigeration and
\par
building heating. In the immediate future, these gains
\par
would apply primarily to the developed world. The
\par
potential savings from the electrical sector depends on
\par
usage habits by nation. Relatively speaking, the USA
\par
has a high electric energy consumption per capita,
\par
12 000 kWh per capita annually. This is compared with
\par
a worldwide average of 1500kWh per capita, and
\par
approximately 800 kWh per capita in developing coun-
\par
tries. 34 For the USA, estimates of savings vary even
\par
among investigatorsY however, most experts would
\par
project a technical potential for cumulative end-use
\par
electrical energy efficiency improvement of 25--40\%
\par
over the next 25-50 years. This improvement potential
\par
is consistent with an expected 1\% per annum carbon
\par
growth rate for developed countries, and is reflected in
\par
our carbon emission rate estimates for the OECD
\par
countries.
\par
Adoption of efficient electrical technologies can not
\par
only replace old equipment used today, but also can add
\par
to improved, environmentally sensitive choices for new
\par
uses. A recent study suggests that many end-use electri-
\par
cal technologies reduce total emissions compared with
\par
fossil fuel alternatives, depending on the regional varia-
\par
tion in fuel mix, emission type and energy end-use
\par
efficiency. 36 Examples include those shown in Figure 4.
\par
From such examples, widespread electrification, in prin-
\par
ciple, can offer major opportunities for effective national
\par
contributions to GHG and other pollutant reduction.
\par
The US technical potential for energy savings is not
\par
necessarily translatable to other countries. The interna-
\par
tional savings to be accrued are problematic, and depend
\par
on existing and future energy use per capita, particularly
\par
if practices are extended to the developing world. Even
\par
in the developed world, Japan, for example, has recently
\par
seen significant increased energy growth as the Japanese
\par
consumer is introduced to modern electrical appliances
\par
and conveniences.
\par
In summary, projected increases in electricity end-use
\par
efficiency and substitution of electricity for other end-
\par
use fuels, natural gas, in the USA, could lead to
\par
1014 substantial overall reductions in CO 2 emissions. These
\par
so-called 'wiser and wider' uses of electricity are fore-
\par
cast to have the potential to reduce CO 2 emissions by
\par
nearly 700 Mt by 2010, or approximately 13\% of US
\par
present CO 2 emissions. 37 It is therefore imperative that
\par
the benefits of electric power be evaluated from the gen-
\par
erating plant through the final end-use to assess the
\par
contributions to GHG emission reductions.
\par
\emph{Atmospheric carbon management}
\par
Aspirations for worldwide economic growth with envi-
\par
ronmental sensitivity may well motivate technology
\par
applications for carbon management that focus on emis-
\par
sion controls at one extreme and stabilization of
\par
atmospheric carbon level at the other. A variety of
\par
approaches have been proposed already that would
\par
introduce large-scale operations to preserve options for
\par
use of carbonaceous fuels in the future. These potential
\par
solutions need to be active for a period of one to two
\par
centuries at least, giving humanity time to evolve away
\par
from a fossil carbon-based energy system. They can be
\par
either permanent, sequestering CO 2 for greater than 200
\par
years; or temporary, sequestering CO 2 or suppressing
\par
atmospheric heat absorption in our decadal periods,
\par
accumulating to more than two centuries.
\par
\emph{Conventional C02 recovery technology. }The feasibility
\par
of CO 2 removal from power plant effluents by an
\par
absorption technology has been explored. 38 The CO 2
\par
absorption technologies rely on amine solutions as the
\par
scrubbing fluid. Once the CO 2 is concentrated by a sep-
\par
aration scheme (stripper or membrane), the CO 2 must be
\par
sequestered or recycled.
\par
The primary approach considered for control of CO 2
\par
effluents would be storage in abandoned deep under-
\par
ground wells or aquifers, or pumping the gas through
\par
pipelines with final disposition in the deep ocean.
\par
Potentially, CO 2 can be used for some practical purpose;
\par
however, use is limited by the volume that can be
\par
accommodated. The CO z could be partially converted to
\par
methane or ethylene, by photochemical reactions. Such a
\par
process has been developed, using an aqueous phase
\par
process involving photoelectrochemical reactions cata-
\par
lyzed by semiconductors. 39 This process is an inorganic
\par
analogy to photosynthesis, and appears to be too ineffi-
\par
cient in CO z conversion to be useful for large volumes
\par
of gas. Only a relatively small fraction of the expected
\par
CO 2 emissions of 10 Gt C pa in the mid-21st century
\par
could be eliminated by these methods.
\par
On the other hand, deep ocean sequestration of CO 2
\par
has a number of interesting features. Permanent seques-
\par
tration of CO 2 would inject liquid, gaseous or solid CO 2
\par
hydrate below depths of 1000 m in the ocean. It appears
\par
\emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12}that the minimum energy approach will depend on form-
\par
ing a solid nCO2mH20 material (a clathrate compound),
\par
with n varying from 5.4 to 8.0 in a water matrix of
\par
(m=)46 molecules. These compounds form most easily
\par
at high pressure ({$>$}40 atm) and low temperature (0 {$^\circ$} C)
\par
equivalent to approximately 1000 m in the ocean. The
\par
clathrates have a specific gravity greater than sea water;
\par
thus, the solids would sink after injection and either dis-
\par
solve in the deep ocean waters or reach the ocean floor
\par
and remain in the sediments. The ocean floor already
\par
contains both CO 2 and methane clathrates, so the ocean
\par
floor would be 'minimally' disturbed. However, there
\par
are still many unanswered questions about the impact of
\par
such sediments on the ecology of the deep ocean floor
\par
and whether the clathrates would redissolve in the deep
\par
ocean waters.
\par
Stegen \emph{et al }have shown that injection of CO 2 below
\par
1000 m depths in the ocean greatly decreases the maxi-
\par
\emph{Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
mum atmospheric CO 2 concentration. 4{$^\circ$} Recently,
\par
further work of Cole \emph{et al }indicates that the ocean
\par
system may be capable of absorbing 1500 to 4000 Gt of
\par
additional carbon. 41 This range of oceanic CO 2 seques-
\par
tration potential covers our estimates of projected
\par
additional fossil fuel use throughout the 21 st century and
\par
into the 22nd century. The ocean thus appears to be the
\par
most capable and desirable repository for future fossil
\par
fuel carbon.
\par
In assessing the overall benefits of sequestering CO 2
\par
in the oceans, the energy requirements for CO 2 recovery
\par
combined with pumping, liquefying or solidifying, and
\par
injecting CO 2 to depths of 100 m or more results in very
\par
high estimated emission control costs for fossil fueled
\par
plants, effectively adding US\$100 to 300 per ton of car-
\par
bon sequestered or adding 50\% to 150\% to the
\par
electricity cost. 42
\par
