engineering systems for sequestering CO 2 may result.
\par
\emph{Biospheric management. The }second group of measures
\par
involve mitigation of CO 2 build up by taking advantage
\par
of the carbon cycle. Much has been said about the desir-
\par
ability of afforestation of abandoned agricultural lands,
\par
and reforestation in areas denuded of trees. However,
\par
few studies have appeared describing the practical
\par
aspects of terrestrial carbon accumulation through silvi-
\par
culture, Two studies provide insight into the amount of
\par
carbon that could be stored in forest management world-
\par
wide. 44 These important results are summarized in Table
\par
2. Based on the world land area available for forestation,
\par
0.1-1.0 GtC pa could be removed from the atmosphere.
\par
Its long-term removal, however, is contingent on har-
\par
vesting mature trees whose growth is slowed with
\par
maturity, and sequestration of the harvest as lumber for
\par
buildings or other 'storage' in such a way that the timber
\par
will not decay to form gaseous carbon emissions from
\par
the soil. Even at the levels of carbon sequestration pro-
\par
jected, forest management can apparently only realize a
\par
small fraction of the annual carbon emissions in the next
\par
century.
\par
Additional insight about the role of afforestation in
\par
managing the carbon cycle can be obtained from carbon
\par
cycle models. For example, Keller and Goldstein have
\par
used the GLOCO model. 45 They found that reforestation
\par
of abandoned agricultural land in the mid-latitudes was
\par
most effective for carbon storage relative to tropical and
\par
boreal forests. Further, the model added insight about
\par
the time scale for forests to absorb carbon on a large
\par
scale. The atmospheric CO 2 reduction begins to be
\par
effective for high land use only after 20-40 years of tree
\par
growth. This relatively slow change in atmospheric C O 2
\par
concentration indicates a temporal 'inefficiency' in the
\par
mitigation strategy relative to conventional emission
\par
control and deep ocean sequestration.
\par
A second terrestrial mitigation strategy involves the
\par
cultivation of, and carbon storage in, halophyte plants,
\par
that thrive in saline water irrigation. There are very large
\par
arid regions of the world where fresh water irrigation is
\par
unavailable, but brackish water or sea water is readily
\par
accessible for irrigation. Glenn \emph{et al }have reported that,
\par
if the arid lands were used for halophyte agriculture, as
\par
much as 0.1-1.0 Gt C pa could be removed from the
\par
atmosphere, a level essentially similar to that for silvi-
\par
culture. 46
\par
A test of halophyte production by sea water irrigation
\par
in Porto Penasco, Mexico has shown that rapid growing
\par
species can be cultivated, with long-term carbon storage
\par
in the soil, without decay. Some halophyte species also
\par
appear to be useful for food supply (production of a soy-
\par
bean-like oil, and for animal food). The plants also may
\par
be useful as a biomass fuel in areas where trees or other
\par
vegetation may not flourish.
\par
The costs of halophyte production are listed in Table
\par
2; they are similar to afforestation.
\par
The idea of accelerating growth of phytoplankton in
\par
the ocean derives from the fact that there are very large
\par
quantities of microorganisms at the ocean surface that
\par
sequester large quantities of CO z during photosynthesis.
\par
Assuming that conutrients like nitrogen and phosphor-
\par
ous are available, phytoplankton growth (and carbon
\par
assimilation) can be stimtdated by adding iron to the sur-
\par
face water. 47 Significant atmospheric carbon removal is
\par
projected (approximately 1.0 Gt C pa), for example, in
\par
plankton rich, iron deficient mid- to high latitude south-
\par
em oceans with iron dispersal over open oceans of 18
\par
million square miles. 48 The cost of this undertaking
\par
would be the expense of purchase, transport and disper-
\par
sal of large components of soluble iron salt over the
