\par
stimulate higher yields of ocean fisheries. Finally, the
\par
amount of iron necessary is unclear because of high
\par
winds prevalent in these oceans. Dispersive forces indi-
\par
cate that the iron may move primarily horizontally rather
\par
than enter the water column, where it would stimulate
\par
plankton growth.
\par
Another alternative is to take advantage of the expan-
\par
sive open ocean surface available for biological carbon
\par
sequestration. Macro-algal farming of a few million
\par
square miles could absorb CO 2 in the same range as
\par
emissions (Table 2). 50 Some preliminary work has
\par
begun to conceptually design marine seaweed farms on
\par
the scale envisioned for Gt C pa removal. Individual
\par
farm units would be \textasciitilde 1000 acre structures clustered to
\par
process approximately 25 000 to 50 000 wet tonnes of
\par
harvested algae per day. Some 2500 such systems would
\par
be necessary to achieve one GtC pa C removal potential.
\par
An ocean farm system concept is shown in Figure 5. The
\par
structure would be negatively buoyant with support from
\par
a series of buoys. This structure would then have tethers
\par
located on 10-20 foot spacing to serve as holdfasts for
\par
the macro-algae (seaweed). Each system would cover an
\par
area of approximately 100 000 acres in the open ocean
\par
away from commercial shipping lanes. The seaweed
\par
would be harvested by ships that would process the bio-
\par
mass in digesters to produce methane for fuel, or
\par
chemical synthesis. Inorganic solids retrieved from these
\par
digesters could be pumped back through the seaweed
\par
farm, and/or the nutrient rich deep waters could pumped
\par
to the surface to suppress nutrient depletion. The CO 2
\par
produced from the anaerobic digesters is partially recy-
\par
cled to the macro-algal farms to increase productivity,
\par
and the bulk of the CO 2 is formed into clathrates and
\par
deposited in the deep ocean. While the removal of CO 2
\par
by marine phytosphere management could realize
\par
7-10 Gt C pa, estimated costs of this practice are in the
\par
same range as terrestrial mitigation opportunities ie
\par
US\$150-300 tonne C sequestered, although the value of
\par
a range of products, including fuels, chemicals,
\par
\emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12 Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
enhanced fisheries etc could partially offset these costs.
\par
The practical aspects of such an enterprise are prodig-
\par
ious, although China produces over one million wet
\par
tonnes of algae annually as food supplements.
\par
One fundamental assumption underlying large-scale
\par
CO 2 sequestration in the ocean is the ocean's capacity to
\par
accept more carbon without suffering unacceptable per-
\par
turbations. Recent analyses by Cole \emph{et al }indicate that
\par
the maximum acceptable addition of carbon to the deep
\par
oceans may be 2 to 5.2 atmospheric carbon masses. This
\par
is just about equal to the estimated amounts of economi-
\par
cally recoverable global fossil fuel inventory. Further
\par
understanding of this sequestration potential is critical to
\par
a search for a practical overall CO 2 mitigation strategy.
\par
Comparative examination of cohventional emission
\par
control technology and both terrestrial and oceanic bio-
\par
spheric mitigation prospects suggests comparable, high
\par
costs of carbon removal and sequestrations. To be prac-
\par
tical in adding small incremental costs to electric energy,
\par
these carbon constraint practices have to demonstrate
\par
reductions in control cost of 1/2 to 1/6th of those deter-
\par
mined to date. The potential limitations of geophysical
\par
carbon storage, and the limited land area for afforesta-
\par
tion or halophyte cultivation, suggest limited
\par
opportunities for atmospheric CO 2 management from
\par
terrestrial systems, even if costs were not a considera-
\par
tion. Ocean sequestration provides the largest available
\par
reservoir for fossil carbon.
\par
\emph{Geo-engineering. }The use of global scale techniques to
\par
modify atmospheric processes has been explored as a
\par
third mitigation concept. 52 Perhaps the most exotic idea
\par
for reducing the effect of GHGs calls for launching a
\par
giant reflector in space. This would consist of a giant
\par
orbiting mirror, fabricated from aluminum foil
\par
0.1-0.5 \textasciitilde m thick. Without making the shield so large as
\par
to modulate sunlight as seen from the earth, the structure
\par
could be as large as 1000 km in radius to reduce solar
\par
flux by 1.4\%. Very preliminary design suggests that a
\par
solar reflector of about 2000km radius could be
\par
launched and remain stable with active controls to adjust
\par
for the solar radiation pressure of sunlight. The cost esti-
\par
mate for the project indicates placement at a cost of
\par
about US\$10 trillion or about US\$10 per tonne carbon
\par
equivalent removed (4000Gt CO 2 mitigates a 1\%
\par
change in solar constant). However, the technology is
\par
high risk for workability, and may be far more expen-
\par
sive in practice with the needed developmental work,
\par
and actual delivered orbital payload costs. Considerable
\par
evaluation work would be needed to examine the atmos-
\par
pheric dynamic and ecological consequences of a giant
\par
shadow traveling across the earth's surface.
\par
The introduction of aerosol particles into the upper
\par
atmosphere - the stratosphere - effectively modifies the
