\par
direct cost of this approach of about US\$40 billion per
\par
I 0 1 8 \emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12}year. Such estimates incorrectly use the notion of resi-
\par
dence time, which is actually a half-life for exponential
\par
decay of atmospheric concentrations. Noting such a
\par
decay relationship, much larger quantities of dust would
\par
be needed to maintain a stable haze layer, implying that
\par
the reported costs are far too low.
\par
Addition of stratospheric particle layers would
\par
involve the risk of acidifying the upper atmosphere more
\par
than present and modifying atmospheric chemical pro-
\par
cesses on a massive scale. Furthermore, the strong acid
\par
present would tend to settle in time into the troposphere
\par
where it would contribute to enhanced acid deposition
\par
on a worldwide scale. The benefit gained in moderating
\par
the GHG effect may not be worth that risk. Further,
\par
large jet aircraft have an operating ceiling below a mini-
\par
mum altitude chosen for injection. Thus, achievement of
\par
a stable haze layer might be problematic using aircraft.
\par
Artillery projectile launches are hypothetical since they
\par
have not been attempted as stratospheric launch
\par
vehicles.
\par
Geo-engineering ideas have aided in stimulating sci-
\par
entists; however, their practicality needs to be evaluated
\par
at scales applicable to the entire earth. Further, consider-
\par
able effort is needed to analyze the engineering
\par
processes carefully for undesirable environmental risks.
\par
While the early, crude cost estimates may make geo-
\par
engineering appear to be attractive, these costs, to be
\par
credible, need to be defined by actual pilot experiments
\par
and very thorough systems and logistical analyses, as
\par
well as environmental risks analysis.
\par
On a reported cost basis, surveys of the CO 2 mitiga-
\par
tion opportunities tend to favor the space or high altitude
\par
solids injection methods identified to date to modify the
\par
earth's albedo. To be technically credible, a substantial
\par
improvement in engineering knowledge of space shields
\par
or particle layers is needed to estimate costs that are
\par
technically comparable with those developed for other
\par
options. As with all the global scale 'solutions', careful
\par
environmental impact analyses of modifying atmo-
\par
sphere--ocean-biosphere processes need to be carried out
\par
before these options are considered practical measures.
\par
\emph{Prospects for global emissions reduction}
\par
From the considerations above, the \emph{technically feasible}
\par
range of CO 2 emission management can be estimated
\par
crudely and compared with emissions growth. For the
\par
purposes of comparison, the global carbon emissions by
\par
2050 are expected to be in the range of 15-20 Gt C pa,
\par
as compared with present day levels of about 6 Gt C pa.
\par
We will use the mid-range level of unabated carbon
\par
emissions of 20 Gt C pa shown in Figure 3 to assure the
\par
benefits of various mitigation approaches. Our assess-
\par
ment of emerging energy use trends suggests that
\par
