\par
\emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12 }1021\emph{Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
reforestation and plantations; halophyte production for
\par
sequestration in semi-arid regions; continental shelf and
\par
open ocean macroalgal systems; and Antartic Ocean phy-
\par
toplankton stimulation. As is clear from Figure 8,
\par
terrestrial biomass production is limited to approximately
\par
3 Gt C annually, while macro-algal systems have a poten-
\par
tial of 2-8 Gt C annually, if a few percent of the ocean
\par
were used. Phytoplankton stimulation requires fertiliza-
\par
tion of nearly 15\% of the open ocean, with only a Gt C
\par
annual sequestration potential.
\par
The net costs of terrestrial production and use of bio-
\par
mass is expected to be much less than that for ocean
\par
systems. Macro-algal system costs must be reduced by
\par
factors of two to four to be economically competitive
\par
based on present system costs of approximately US\$300
\par
to 400 per tonne of C (net cost of US\$200 to US\$300
\par
per tonne of C). Lower costs have been estimated for
\par
advanced, high productivity systems, but they are pres-
\par
ently very speculative.
\par
It should be recognized that biomass extraction of car-
\par
bon from the atmosphere could cost 1.6 to 1.75 times the
\par
cost of stack gas scrubbing and still be competitive,
\par
since natural processes remove approximately 40\% of
\par
the carbon entering the atmosphere, assuming the carbon
\par
airborne fraction remains at 0.57. In addition, these
\par
systems extract all carbon in the atmosphere from distri-
\par
buted sources, as well as point sources such as power
\par
plants. This may become an important aspect in defining
\par
future cost-effective mitigation approaches. Further, bio-
\par
mass carbon substitution for fossil fuels would extend
\par
the lifetime of the world fossil fuel reserves and may
\par
ultimately become the carbon fuel supply as reserves are
\par
depleted.
\par
Introduction of improvements in end-use and genera-
\par
tion efficiency would provide technically feasible,
\par
relatively low cost opportunities for managing a signific-
\par
ant part of unabated carbon emissions in the next
\par
century. Nevertheless, the rough estimates above
\par
illustrate that full management of emissions will require
\par
high cost options, potentially including CO 2 removal and
\par
sequestering technology or biological macro-fixation.
\par
The importance of marine storage and processing
\par
options should not be ignored. In view of the large
\par
uncertainties in both the environmental risk in climate
\par
alteration, and in CO 2 emission management, it would
\par
be prudent at this time to pursue aggressively the devel-
\par
opment of the management options in all cost ranges.
\par
\textbf{Adding information for decision making}
\par
The preceding sections have summarized certain knowl-
\par
edge emerging from studies of different components in
\par
the integrating framework of Figure 1. Extraction of
\par
information from synthesizing component knowledge
\par
1022 comes from models. Models have been developed that
\par
describe the socio-economic linkages on the right side of
\par
Figure 1. And highly simplified models have attempted
\par
to integrate all of the elements shown in Figure 1. In this
\par
last section, we summarize some results from these inte-
\par
grating studies that seek to inform decision makers
\par
about evaluating objectively risks of climate alteration,
\par
direction for research, and the social costs versus bene-
\par
fits expected from carbon emission management.
\par
\emph{Refining estimates of risk}
\par
Recognition of the large uncertainties in projecting the
\par
effects of increases in atmospheric GHG content has led
\par
to large investments and R\&D in several countries.
\par
Much of the enhanced effort is devoted to advancing the
\par
knowledge about atmosphere-ocean-biosphere interac-
\par
tions. These endeavours are advancing a description of
\par
these interactions, but are not necessarily well focused
\par
on narrowing the scientific uncertainties in answers to
\par
questions posed by decision makers. 57 Questions that
\par
will define a 21st century international electric energy
\par
policy need to address a blend of environmental issues
\par
with socio-economic benefits and issues on a political
\par
scale not attempted in the past. 58 The value of trade offs
\par
between economic well being through electrification and
\par
environmental protection vary widely by nation.
\par
Integrating this into a more or less uniform policy of
\par
international cooperation will have to account for such
\par
trade offs for decades.
\par
Most risk assessments have been measured in terms
\par
of the developed world, which has had economic
\par
reserves to relieve both local and regional environmental
\par
stress. The GHG issue clearly involves decisions derived
\par
from values and preferences of both the developing and
\par
developed world. The economic stakeholding, as well as
\par
priorities for public well being versus sustainability, can
\par
differ markedly in these groups of countries.
\par
To facilitate efficient acquisition of information
\par
blending international science, technology and socioeco-
\par
nomics, the decision framework in Figure 1 is useful.
\par
These analyses are made less qualitative and subjective
\par
by adapting for the assessment a series of interlocked
\par
analytical models describing process components of
\par
interacting phenomena. Previous attempts to develop
\par
integrating frameworks, eg for acid rain, have met with
\par
only partial acceptance. 59 This experience, however,
\par
provides important guidelines for an integrated assess-
\par
ment of the climate change issue.
\par
Early review of information needs 6{$^\circ$} based on applica-
\par
tion of an assessment concept, indicates that advancing
\par
the understanding of global change will require substan-
\par
tial added investment in regional scale climate
\par
alteration, and environmental effects studies, as well as
\par
\emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12}socioeconomic studies. This need is particularly acute
\par
for Pacific Rim nations, Africa and India. Perspective is
\par
vitally needed to estimate potential positive effects of
\par
climate alteration, as well as negative effects. To date,
\par
accounting for positive or beneficial effects of climate
\par
change have not been attempted.
\par
The potential for insight from integrated assessment,
\par
exemplified in Figure 1, has been demonstrated in 'zero
\par
order' models, as reported, for example, by Nordhaus,
\par
and Peck and Teisberg. 61 Nordhaus's results centered on
\par
economic evaluation of the USA. He notes that the parts
\par
of the economy most vulnerable to climate alteration are
\par
agriculture and transport. The former only accounts for a
\par
few percent of the economy. With this in mind, he pro-
\par
jects only a minimal environmental benefit to the USA
\par
for the cost of a CO 2 emission reduction through a car-
\par
bon constrained energy system. Nordhaus notes that the
\par
blend of business sectors vulnerable to climate change
\par
may be much larger in developing countries, which are
\par
highly dependent on adequate food production through
\par
agriculture.
\par
The Peck and Teisberg 62 analyses have noted that
\par
optimal worldwide CO 2 emission reductions depend
\par
strongly on a significant non-linearity in the economic
\par
damage versus climate change relationship. Although
\par
we would expect such non-linear characteristics, they
\par
have not been demonstrated, except in a coarse way.
\par
One example is reported for sea level rise. 63
\par
Other damage functions are likely to increase non-
\par
linearly with climate change. Assumptions suggest a
\par
non-linear relation greater than quadratic in tempera-
\par
ture rise is necessary to force significant damage
\par
from climate in economic terms. The Peck and
\par
Teisberg calculations suggested that added scientific
\par
information would substantially improve the decisions
\par
on emissions management; key priority information is
\par
required from improvements in climate predictions,
\par
and environmental impact (estimating damage
\par
functions), and costs of adaptation to climate
\par
warming.
\par
Damage functions also should be cast in parameters
\par
other than temperature rise. For example, damages aris-
\par
ing from more frequent or stronger storm systems, such
\par
as that in Hawaii and Florida in 1992, are probably non-
\par
linear with energy retention in the atmosphere-ocean
\par
system. To date, information has not been available
\par
to approach damage estimation with this level of
\par
sophistication.
\par
\emph{Economic" insight}
\par
A combination of superficially consistent predictions of
\par
future warming, combined with articulation of scientific
\par
concerns for the impact of global climate alteration, has
\par
\emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12 Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
led politicians to seek international commitment to cur-
\par
tail GHG emissions (eg The International Climate
\par
Convention). Stabilization or management of GHG
\par
emissions to 1990 levels beyond 2000 could cost world
\par
society hundreds of billions to trillions of dollars in the
\par
coming decades, with the potential for significant dis-
\par
ruption to the economies of nations as well as energy
\par
production and use in world economic development.
\par
According to one projection, the consequences of CO 2
\par
emission stabilization would be most strongly felt in the
\par
developing world, including Eastern Europe, the CIS
\par
and Chinese economies. 64 These economies are pres-
\par
ently least able to absorb an expensive program for
\par
global environmental protection. The economic conse-
\par
quences of seeking 'insurance' against an environmental
\par
liability from GHG are large enough for the energy
\par
industry and governments to require substantially
\par
improved information about both the environmental
\par
effects of global climate change and a future with a car-
\par
bon-constrained electrical resource.
\par
The macroeconomic calculations of Manne and
\par
Richels indicate CO 2 emissions reductions are sensitive
\par
to the quality and timing of economic information and
\par
technology development. 65 Their analysis indicates that
\par
there could be large pay offs associated with reducing
\par
uncertainties in the world economic system and technol-
\par
ogy options. At the same time, they found that the value
\par
of information was sensitive to the confidence in its
\par
timeliness and reliability. Small increases in accuracy
\par
could yield substantial dividends in decision making and
\par
reduced total costs of mitigation.
\par
A reduction strategy is sensitive to futures in new sup-
\par
ply and conservation technologies. An optimistic
\par
scenario would include abundant low cost, carbon free
\par
supply alternatives and highly efficient, low cost, cus-
\par
tomer use technologies. However, for the pessimist,
\par
there could be a heavy dependence on carbon intensive
\par
synthetic fuels and less self-disciplined (autonomous)
\par
conservation. The latter is more difficult to adapt to a
\par
carbon constraint. As a pessimistic decision maker, it
\par
makes sense to shift some of the burden of emissions
\par
abatement from the future to the present generation. An
\par
optimistic outlook shows confidence that future genera-
\par
tions can productively adapt to a carbon constraint, and
\par
there is minimal pressure for immediate, disruptive cut-
\par
backs.
\par
Peck and Teisberg 66 have investigated the sensitivity
\par
of economic uncertainties in developing a carbon con-
\par
strained energy pricing strategy that would affect
\par
strongly the international electrical energy economy.
\par
Their calculations employed the CETA cost-benefit
\par
model to explore an optimum coal use strategy minimiz-
\par
ing atmospheric CO 2 build up into the 22nd century. The
\par
model includes specified uncertainties such as growth in
\par
1023\emph{Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
labor and (macro-) economic discounting from 1990, as
\par
well as the non-linearity of economic damage with
\par
global wanning. In the CETA model, fuel use is pro-
\par
jected to be qualitatively similar to the minimum rise in
\par
Figure 3 in the early 21 st century, but shifts increasingly
\par
to non-fossil fuels and synthetic fuels (coal gas) in mid-
\par
century.
\par
Their exercise suggested that environmental costs of
\par
CO 2 emissions will be a key factor in future develop-
\par
ment of world coal resources. They found that the
\par
marginal environmental damages are sensitive to some
\par
benefit related parameters in 2030, and very sensitive to
\par
benefit related parameters in 2100. A carbon emission
\par
pattern optimized for minimizing energy cost and envi-
\par
ronmental damage is relatively insensitive to most
\par
parameters in 2030, but very sensitive to many parame-
\par
ters in 2100. These are illustrated in Table 3.
\par
Peck and Teisberg have examined information value
\par
in optimization of international policy for minimizing
\par
risk from climate alteration. 67 They have assumed for
\par
optimization that emission reductions would take place
\par
in those regions where the most inexpensive CO 2 reduc-
\par
tions could be purchased. If an optimal policy is
\par
projected with today's level of scientific uncertainty,
\par
their CETA cost-benefit model analysis suggests the
\par
economic value of information is large enough to justify
\par
current research efforts. If an optimal policy is adopted,
\par
the calculations suggest that ample time is available to
\par
plan and execute a well designed research program
\par
to resolve uncertainties. Added information would
\par
substantially improve the decisions on emissions
\par
management.
\par
Key priority information is required from improve-
\par
ments in climate predictions and environmental impact
\par
(estimating damage functions), and cost of adaptation to
\par
climate warming. However, Peck and Teisberg note that
\par
if the political processes select a potentially suboptimal
\par
process today, and this choice could be modified by
\par
early resolution of uncertainties, the urgency of the reso-
\par
lution is dramatically increased. 68 Thus, indications are
\par
strong that industry and the public should insure that
\par
global electric energy and environmental policy is well
\par
served by advancing the technology of management of
\par
socioeconomic and climate systems.
\par
Management of worldwide needs for electricity
\par
generation, powered by fossil fuels and CO 2 emission
\par
stabilization, may be a difficult and expensive task to
\par
organize. Some preliminary ideas have emerged in anal-
\par
ysis of international trade opportunities that should be
\par
noted.
\par
Manne and Richels have used their Global 2100 eco-
\par
nomic model to explore how management of the timing
\par
by nation of carbon constraints might minimize the cost
\par
of carbon emission stabilization in the next century. 69
\par
Stabilization for the analysis is assumed to involve a
\par
worldwide 20\% reduction in 1990 carbon emission
\par
levels. Further, they assumed that developed nations sta-
\par
bilize emissions at a 1990 level, while developing
\par
countries are allowed to double their emissions. To facil-
\par
itate a reduced worldwide emissions level, their
\par
calculations indicate that a 'cost' of constraining emis-
\par
sions would be imposed on fossil fuel use that would
\par
rise from 0 at 1990 levels to approximately US\$250 per
\par
tonne C in 2050. This represents a cost of an alternative
\par
carbon constrained strategy that would substantially
\par
increase the cost of energy production, including
