\par
fied as a part of fossil fuel use strategies.
\par
Instead of direct cash transfers for carbon constrained
\par
energy management, it is possible that energy technol-
\par
ogy exchange will be a currency for international
\par
programs. Introduction of an international emission trad-
\par
ing business would reduce the cost of energy
\par
development in the developing countries in the early
\par
years, while adding substantially to the cost of carbon
\par
fuel use in the developed world until the early to mid-
\par
21st century. Thus, the developed world would
\par
accelerate its efforts to adjust to carbon constraints early
\par
in the 21st century, leaving the adjustment costs to the
\par
developing world until later.
\par
The calculated cumulative savings in energy costs,
\par
including the fees for carbon emission stabilization,
\par
would be a few percent for the OECD countries, and
\par
partially developed nations such as the former USSR
\par
and China, but several percent for the rest of the world.
\par
However, a few percent reduction in annual GDP could
\par
product significant short-term economic or employment
\par
dislocations.
\par
The idea of adding an 'externality' cost to energy to
\par
cover the expense of environmental stress from pollution
\par
has attracted the interest of the US government and state
\par
utility commissions as a means of rationalizing the con-
\par
sumer cost of energy against some sustainable energy
\par
future. This concept has also attracted the interest of the
\par
international community as a means of developing inter-
\par
national parity for energy consumption.
\par
Costs for (GHG) environmental protection, as well as
\par
the future of coal based electricity production, depend
\par
on global uncertainties. The international industry is
\par
likely to be drawn into the global climate change issue
\par
more and more in the next decade. Its strategy to address
\par
this issue will be enjoined with national and local strate-
\par
gies for environmental protection. Thus, the
\par
participation of the electric power industry will be
\par
important in studies to reduce scientific uncertainties and
\par
to expand technical options for GHG emission stabiliza-
\par
tion. As an active partner, the electricity industry can
\par
insure the use of the most energy efficient and cost-
\par
effective technologies as its key contribution to the
\par
growing well being of the word population.
\par
\emph{Energy Policy 1994 Volume 22 Number 12 Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}
\par
\textbf{Conclusions}
\par
In recent years, electric utilities have encountered a vari-
\par
ety of environmental issues associated with production
\par
and delivery of electricity. Most of these have an inter-
\par
national common denominator, but are not 'global' in
\par
character. However, electricity production does contrib-
\par
ute to atmospheric GHG accumulation, which is
\par
believed to force global scale climate alteration. Most
\par
analysts, to date, have gauged the significance of this
\par
effect by global mean temperature rise. At the current
\par
rate of GHG rise, the earth's surface temperature could
\par
rise between 1.5-4.5 {$^\circ$}C by the mid- to late 21st century,
\par
assuming a doubling of the pre-industrial atmospheric
\par
CO 2 (equivalent) level.
\par
The significance of climate alteration to environmen-
\par
tal stress is very uncertain; projections are largely
\par
anecdotal from historical experience. Little knowledge is
\par
available to quantify the risk of climate alteration to
\par
humans, or managed and natural ecosystems. Analysis
\par
of the risk of climate alteration for decision making has
\par
to involve evaluation of interactions between natural
\par
biogeochemicai processes, climate response and human
\par
activities. Such analyses can be facilitated using an inte-
\par
grating framework.
\par
The solution to the issue of climate alteration is intim-
\par
ately linked with world energy development and use to
\par
improve the standard of living for over three-fourths of
\par
the world population. Electrification of developing coun-
\par
tries in the 21st century is the principal ingredient to
\par
achieve this improved standard of living, health care
\par
improvement etc. In these nations, fossil fuels are pro-
\par
jected to be the principal, low cost energy sources. If
\par
this is the case, GHG management will need to be
\par
implemented, keeping in mind the demands for eco-
\par
nomic development using fossil fuels, especially coal.
\par
Considering depletion of fossil fuels, combined with a
\par
long-duration response time of climate forcing, the GHG
\par
issue appears to be most critical in the next 100 years.
\par
As insurance against possible effects of climate altera-
\par
tion, steps must be taken to maximize the benefits to be
\par
obtained from the use of fossil fuels, from production
\par
through end use. Electrification, combined with the use
\par
of improved high efficiency generation and electrical
\par
devices offer the most important, optimal strategy for
\par
managing efficient energy growth. However, even using
\par
the best measures, it appears that these measures alone
\par
will be inadequate to meet global GHG management
\par
objectives in the long term.
\par
Curtailment of CO 2 emissions from electricity pro-
\par
duction can be done in theory using conventional
\par
removal processes and CO 2 sequestration by deep well
\par
storage or deep ocean sequestration. Alternatives in-
\par
clude geo-engineering options, and biospheric process
\par
1025\textbf{\emph{Climate alteration: G M Hidy and D F Spencer}}
\par
\textbf{manipulation. Most options identified to date will cost}
\par
\textbf{US\$100-\$300 per tonne C sequestered. This expense is}
\par
\textbf{about a factor of two to six too large to be acceptable as}
\par
\textbf{a practical expense for society to consider at this time.}
\par
\textbf{Until the risk of climate alteration is better defined in}
\par
\textbf{terms of national and international interests and social}
\par
\textbf{values, decision makers will be faced with difficult}
\par
\textbf{choices about the degree of investment in reduction of}
\par
\textbf{these risks. Further development of practical options for}
\par
\textbf{GHG management are crucial to informed societal deci-}
\par
\textbf{sions on long-term environmentally sensitive electric}
\par
\textbf{energy growth.}
\par
\textbf{An international global assessment and mitigation}
\par
\textbf{strategy, properly weighing costs and benefits, is essen-}
\par
\textbf{tial to ensure that adequate controls are available, if}
\par
\textbf{necessary.}
