\par
Bayesian Methods for Analysing Climate Change and Water
\par
Resource Uncertainties
\par
Benjamin F. Hobbs{$\ast$}
\par
Department of Systems, Control and Industrial Engineering, 610 Crawford Hall,
\par
Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH 44106, U.S.A.
\par
The purpose of this paper is to outline the advantages of the Bayesian
\par
approach for analysing uncertainties involving climate change, emphasizing the
\par
study of the risks such changes pose to water resources systems. Bayesian
\par
analysis has the advantage of basing inference and decisions on a coherent and
\par
normatively appealing theoretical framework. Furthermore, it can incorporate
\par
diverse sources of information, including subjective opinions, historical
\par
observations and model outputs. The paper summarizes the basic assumptions
\par
and procedures of Bayesian analysis. Summaries of applications to detection of
\par
climate change, estimation of climate model parameters, and wetlands
\par
management under climatic uncertainty illustrate the potential of the Bayesian
\par
methodology. Criticisms of the approach are summarized. It is concluded that
\par
in comparison with alternative paradigms for analysing uncertainty, such as
\par
fuzzy sets and Dempster--Shafer reasoning, Bayesian analysis is practical,
\par
theoretically sound, and relatively easy to understand.
\par
 1997 Academic Press Limited
\par
Keywords: climate change, global warming, water resources, Bayesian analysis,
\par
decision-making, risk analysis, wetlands, Great Lakes.
\par
1. Introduction
\par
The object of climate change research is to dispel uncertainty concerning the likelihood,
\par
magnitude and impacts of climate change. Bayesian analysis is a practical and theo-
\par
retically appropriate tool for making inferences about climate change and for making
\par
decisions based on those inferences. It can help to address questions such as:
\par
{$\bullet$} Given present evidence, has the climate already become significantly warmer?
\par
{$\bullet$} When might we know if greenhouse gas-forced climate change is really occurring?
\par
{$\bullet$} Given the uncertainties inherent in global circulation models (GCMs), what range
\par
of mesoscale impacts can be expected in the future?
\par
{$\bullet$} Should we commit resources now for preventing or adapting to anticipated
\par
climate change, or should we wait to obtain additional information?
\par
{$\ast$} Current address: Dept. of Geography and Environmental Engineering, The Johns Hopkins University,
\par
313 Ames Hall, 3400 No. Charles St., Baltimore, MD 21218.
\par
0301--4797/97/010053+20 \$25.00/0/ev960116  1997 Academic Press Limited54
\par
Bayesian analysis of climate change risks
\par
The purpose of this paper is to summarize the case for Bayesian analysis of climate
\par
change uncertainties. Other papers in this special issue present alternative risk analysis
\par
methods. Here I address the following questions:
\par
{$\bullet$} What can Bayesian analysis do?
\par
{$\bullet$} What does Bayesian analysis assume?
\par
{$\bullet$} How does Bayesian analysis do what it does?
\par
{$\bullet$} How might Bayesian analysis be useful for analysing climate change?
\par
{$\bullet$} What criticisms have been made of Bayesian analysis, and what are the responses
\par
to those criticisms?
\par
This paper can only touch upon a few aspects of the rich theory that underlies
\par
Bayesian analysis. Only a handful of the numerous applications of the approach to
\par
climate and weather analysis, hydrology and water resources planning can be mentioned.
\par
More thorough introductions to Bayesian analysis and its applications are available
\par
elsewhere (e.g. Raiffa, 1968; Berger, 1985; Gatsonis et al., 1992). For an introductory
\par
discussion of the use of Bayesian analysis for water resources decision making under
\par
climate change, see Fiering and Rogers (1991).
\par
2. What can Bayesian analysis do?
\par
Three features of Bayesian analysis are appealing to practitioners. First are the useful
\par
outputs it provides:
\par
{$\bullet$} Inferences. These include hypothesis tests; expected values for model parameters;
\par
credible intervals for those parameters (analogous to, but not exactly the same
\par
as, the confidence intervals of classical statistics); and projections and credible
\par
intervals for future system behavior. Bayesian analysis can derive these inferences
\par
from diverse sources of information, such as historical observations, expert
\par
opinions and model simulations. In contrast, classical statistics and information
\par
theory can only use historical frequency data and often assume that the system
\par
being observed is stationary. Such ``frequentist'' approaches underlie signal-to-
\par
noise ratios and other statistical tests for climate change (Wigley and Barnett,
\par
1990; Schneider, 1994). However, they have little value for projecting the future
\par
course of climate change because of their exclusive reliance on historical ob-
\par
servations.
\par
{$\bullet$} Optimal strategies. Given the uncertainties facing us and the information that is
\par
available, Bayesian analysis can be used to identify good decisions. Here, a good
\par
decision is defined as one that maximizes the expected value of one or more
\par
performance indices.
\par
{$\bullet$} Value of information. Bayesian analysis can be used to trace how additional
\par
information would alter the decision we would make and improve the expected
\par
performance of those decisions. This enables us to quantify the value of that
\par
information in economic terms or whatever performance indices one wishes. The
\par
``expected value of perfect information'' tells us the most we should be willing to
\par
pay for perfect knowledge of the future. Thus, it is a useful measure of the cost
\par
of uncertainty. The ``expected value of imperfect information'' quantifies the value
\par
of the imperfect information we might obtain from actual studies. That value
\par
can then be compared with the cost of doing such studies. This cost might include
\par
the benefits that are foregone if we put off a decision until the information
\par
becomes available.B. F. Hobbs 55
\par
{$\bullet$} Robustness. Bayesian analysis provides a systematic set of procedures for under-
\par
standing the sensitivity of our results to the assumptions we make, and the
\par
economic consequences of that sensitivity.
\par
These products are of obvious utility to decision-makers and climate-change sci-
\par
entists. A second feature of Bayesian analysis makes these products even more valuable.
\par
This feature is the comprehensive framework that Bayesian analysis represents, and
\par
the normatively attractive assumptions that underlie that framework. Alternative
\par
paradigms, such as fuzzy set theory, lack this advantage. The virtue of comprehensiveness
\par
is its coherent encompassing of both inference and decision-making. Since the importance
\par
of information cannot be assessed without understanding its impacts on decisions, this
\par
is appealing. By ``normatively valid assumptions'', I mean that the axioms that underpin
\par
Bayesian analysis are ones that most rational people would agree ought to be adhered
\par
to (even if, in fact, human frailties prevent us from doing so). The comprehensive
\par
framework implies that if we believe that we should accept those assumptions, then we
\par
should be willing to accept the conclusions derived from Bayesian analysis---or at least
\par
be inclined to consider them carefully.
\par
The third and final appealing feature of Bayesian analysis is that its procedures are,
\par
in most circumstances, practical and relatively simple compared to other approaches
\par
(e.g. Dempster--Shafer reasoning; see Caselton and Luo, 1992), and based on familiar
\par
notions of probability. This is an important advantage to the practitioner who must
\par
not only obtain results at a reasonable cost, but also be able to explain and justify
\par
them to others.
\par
3. What does Bayesian analysis assume?
\par
What assumptions must we accept in order to obtain these benefits of Bayesian analysis?
\par
Berger (1985) notes that researchers have developed several dozen alternatives sets of
\par
axioms as a basis for Bayesian analysis. The four major assumptions I outline below
\par
are loosely based on those of Raiffa (1968), and are similar to other developments.
\par
