\par
Equation (3), the third property of subjective probabilities, can be rearranged to
\par
yield Bayes' law:
\par
P(A B)=P(A)[P(B A)/P(B)] (4)
\par
That is, the probability of A given that B has occurred (the ``posterior'' probability of
\par
A), equals the ``prior'' probability P(A) multiplied by the ratio of (a) the likelihood of
\par
observing B given that A will occur (P(B A)) to (b) the overall or ``marginal'' probability
\par
of B(P(B)). This disarmingly simple equation is the foundation of Bayesian inference.
\par
4. How does Bayesian analysis do what it does?
\par
Bayesian analysis produces the outputs described in Section 2 in four steps:
\par
1. Specify problem structure.
\par
2. Quantify inputs.
\par
3. Perform the analysis.
\par
4. Examine robustness of the results to changes in assumptions.
\par
Each step is discussed below.
\par
4.1.   
\par
This consists of describing the options that are available to the decision-maker (resource
\par
commitments, information gathering); when choices from among those options can be
\par
made; and when information is obtained that reduces uncertainty. Decision trees and
\par
influence diagrams are two useful ways of portraying problem structure (Clemens,
\par
1991); here, trees are used. Figure 1 shows a tree that will be referred to again later in58
\par
