\par
the paper. It represents the general structure of several types of decisions involving
\par
management of the Laurentian Great Lakes of North America (Hobbs et al., 1996).
\par
As for any decision tree, the one in Figure 1 has three major elements.
\par
{$\bullet$} Decision nodes, represented as squares. Here, the options are to make an irreversible
\par
commitment of resources, or to put off the decision. Examples of such com-
\par
mitments might be the construction of shore protective works or a control
\par
structure at the outlet of Lake Erie, a decision to develop a wetland, or the
\par
signing of an agreement to export water out of the basin.
\par
{$\bullet$} Chance nodes, represented as circles. I show three types of uncertainties. The first
\par
includes lake-related variables, such as levels or temperatures, which fluctuate
\par
randomly and which also have an uncertain trend due to the possibility of climate
\par
change. Socio-economic variables, such as values of shoreline property, make up
\par
the second category. The third group includes climate variables, which provide
\par
information that can be used to update the decision-makers' beliefs concerning
\par
the likelihood and magnitude of climate change. These may overlap with the lake
\par
variables. Probabilities, usually containing some subjective element, are associated
\par
with each branch on a chance code.
\par
{$\bullet$} outcomes, which I show as benefits and costs ``B\&C'' that accrue over time. In
\par
general, there may be several incommensurable types of benefits and costs, which
\par
means that the decision problem is a multicriteria one.
\par
Decision nodes can be omitted in pure inference problems, such as the testing of
\par
hypotheses, estimation of model parameters, or making of predictions.
\par
4.2.  
\par
Once the structure of the problem has been outlined in the form of a tree or influence
\par
diagram, then numerical inputs are needed: probabilities and outcomes. First, some
\par
more notation:
\par
: state of nature, which, at least initially, is unknown. An example would
\par
be 1 which might represent the state ``the global mean temperature
\par
will be warm by between 0{$\cdot$}25 and 0{$\cdot$}5{$^\circ$}C per decade over the next
\par
century''.
\par
z: information, usually in the form of an observation of some random
\par
process (e.g. next year's global mean temperature) or a prediction by an
\par
expert or model. Obtaining information alters subjective probabilities of
