\par
Now the necessary inputs can be summarized as follows:
\par
P( ): prior probabilities of the state of nature. These should be assessed based
\par
on the best information now available. If information is unavailable or
\par
ambiguous, then so-called ``non-informative priors'', which attempt to
\par
avoid favoring some states of nature over others, are appropriate.
\par
Priors might also be based on past historical observations, numerical
\par
simulations and expert judgment.
\par
P(z ): likelihoods, which describe the probability of obtaining evidence z
\par
given that the state of nature is actually . For instance, if the climate
\par
is actually warming by an average of 0{$\cdot$}25--0{$\cdot$}5{$^\circ$}/decade (i.e. the stateB. F. Hobbs 59
\par
of nature ), what is the likelihood that the observed global mean
\par
temperature next year will be, say, between X{$^\circ$} and Y{$^\circ$} (i.e. the
\par
observation z)? The values of P(z ) are frequently determined by
\par
models that, given an assumed set of parameters (the state of nature
\par
), can generate probability distributions P(z ). Expert judgment can
\par
also be used to assess likelihoods.
\par
In addition, if we are not merely going to make inferences about the state of nature,
\par
but are also going to model the decision-making process, then the following additional
\par
information is required:
\par
a: x: U(x): options (resource commitments, information gathering) available at
\par
each decision node.
\par
measure(s) of performance. In general, x might be a vector, en-
\par
compassing a range of monetary and non-monetary criteria.
\par
a utility function translating the measures of performance into a single
\par
index of worth. Several U(x)'s might be defined, each representing a
\par
different interest group's point of view.
\par
4.3.   
\par
There are two basic types of Bayesian analysis: posterior and preposterior. The purpose
\par
of posterior analysis is to update the probabilities of the state of nature after information
\par
z is obtained, yielding the posterior probabilities P( z). Bayes' Law (4) is used to
\par
accomplish this. If the represent different hypotheses, then the posterior probabilities
\par
can be used to ``test'' which hypothesis is most credible. For example, 1 might represent
\par
the hypothesis that ``the climate at location X has not warmed by more than 0{$\cdot$}25{$^\circ$} in
\par
the last 40 years'', while 2 might instead be ``the climate has warmed by more than
\par
0{$\cdot$}25{$^\circ$}''. Their posterior probabilities, given the evidence z (the actual temperature record)
\par
and the prior probabilities (say, equal), show which hypothesis is more likely.
\par
If is instead a continuous variable, such as the parameter of a flood frequency
\par
distribution, then posteriors P( z) can be used to calculate several useful statistics.
\par
These include the most likely value of (the mode or ``maximum likelihood'' estimate),
\par
its expected value (which might differ greatly from the mode), and credible intervals.
\par
A X \% credible interval for is defined as a domain D such that the posterior probability
\par
of falling in that domain is X \%:
\par
