\par
An example of the calculation of these statistics is given later in this paper.
\par
Posterior analysis focuses on inference. Preposterior analysis, in contrast, emphasizes
\par
decision-making. First, considering the distribution of possible outcomes z and ,
\par
preposterior analysis calculates the optimal immediate action a---the so-called ``Bayes
\par
decision''. More generally, the optimal strategy is defined, i.e. the option at each decision
\par
node that maximizes the expected net benefits from that point on. This is accomplished
\par
by the ``folding back'' method, also called stochastic dynamic programming. Basically,
\par
the following calculation is made for each decision node, starting at the last period:
\par
choose the action yielding the highest expected benefit. Because this calculation will60
\par
