\par
have already been done for all subsequent decision nodes, the procedure automatically
\par
takes into account what decisions might be made later in response to further information.
\par
Preposterior analysis also produces a second important product: the value of further
\par
information. The value of perfect information, which gauges the cost of uncertainty, is
\par
obtained as follows. It equals the difference between the expected net benefits of the
\par
Bayes decision, and the expected net benefits if the future could be perfectly forecast---i.e.
\par
if there was no uncertainty. More important for decision-making, preposterior analysis
\par
can also assess the value of imperfect information. Say that an experiment, modeling
\par
exercise or other research project would yield information z about the present or future
\par
climate. If that information is optimally used to update prior distributions via Bayes'
\par
law, then having that information available will usually improve the expected net
\par
benefits of the optimal strategy, which in general, will depend on what information is
\par
obtained. The expected amount of that improvement is the value of that information.
\par
If net benefits are measured using multiple criteria, then the value of information itself
\par
will have multiple dimensions (e.g. expected reduction of cost, expected reduction of
\par
environmental impact, etc.).
\par
4.4.  
\par
Robustness analysis concerns the question of the dependency of the results on the
\par
assumed P( ), P(z ), and U(x). Such analysis is important because psychologists have
\par
shown that assessments of subjective probabilities and utilities can be unreliable and
\par
subject to predictable biases (Fischhoff et al., 1979; Kahneman et al., 1982).
\par
Although critics and users of Bayesian analysis both emphasize the importance of
\par
prior distributions, likelihoods and utility functions can also significantly alter the
\par
conclusions of an analysis. Uncertainties in P(z ) are sometimes called modelling and
\par
representativeness uncertainties, when the P(z ) result from some model (such as a
\par
log-normal flood frequency model) that might either be the wrong model or that might
\par
be in error because reality is inherently more complex than any model we might
\par
realistically formulate (Bernier, 1991).
\par
There are several approaches to robustness analysis (Berger, 1985). The first is
\par
traditional sensitivity analysis, in which different assumptions are tested, and variations
\par
in the results are noted. In general, if the prior P( ) is ``sharp'' (i.e. high for a few
\par
values of , and low for the rest), then the conclusions will be relatively insensitive to
\par
P(z ). That is, evidence will not alter prior beliefs greatly. On the other hand, a sharp
\par
P(z ) usually implies that the results are relatively insensitive to P( ). There evidence
\par
matters more than previous beliefs.
\par
In the second approach to robustness analysis, the analyst might attempt to minimize
\par
the effect of subjectivity by adopting inherently ``robust'' prior distributions. These are
\par
``flat'' or ``non-informative'' priors, discriminating little or not at all among alternative
\par
values of . In many cases, use of such priors causes Bayesian analysis to give the
\par
same results as traditional statistical methods (maximum likelihood estimators, classic
\par
statistical tests) and information theory (Tsao et al., 1993). However, if credible prior
\par
information is possessed that does indeed discriminate among the possible , then it
\par
should be used. Throwing such information away by using a flat prior will, in general,
\par
yield inefficient and biased inferences (e.g. parameter estimates) and decisions that fail
\par
to maximize expected net benefits (Krzysztofowicz, 1983).
\par
The third approach to robustness analysis calculates the regret that could result
\par
from using the ``wrong'' inputs. Say that there are two alternative sets of assumptionsB. F. Hobbs 61
\par
concerning P( ), P(z ), and U(x): \textbraceleft P1( ), P1(z ), U1(x)\textbraceright{} and \textbraceleft P2( ), P2(z ), U2(x)\textbraceright.
\par
We start by calculating the optimal action a for each set of inputs: a1 and a2. ``Regret''
\par
is the foregone expected net benefits resulting from assuming that the first set of inputs
\par
is correct (and thus implementing its action a1) when the other set is the right one
\par
instead. This results from a failure to choose the true optimal action. If a1 and a2 are
\par
very similar, or at least yield similar expected net benefits, then this regret will be
\par
relatively small. But if the possible regret is large, then alternative assumptions make
\par
a practical difference. Additional attention should then be paid to the question of which
\par
set of inputs is truly more representative of the decision-makers' knowledge and values.
\par
A fourth approach to robustness analysis is maximum likelihood analysis. It uses
\par
observations of z to determine which P( ) and P(z ) seem most consistent with the
\par
evidence. For instance, say that the observed annual floods in the last three years were
\par
12 000, 18 000, and 9000 m3/sec, respectively. Then, a prior distribution that assigns
\par
zero probability to a mean annual flood of over 10 000 m3/s would seem to be less
\par
credible than one that allows such a possibility. A metric of credibility in this case is
\par
P(z)= P(z )P( ), the overall (marginal) likelihood of the evidence (Berger, 1985).
\par
In the above case, this metric is likely to be much smaller for the prior that excludes
\par
the possibility of a mean value over 10 000 m3/sec.
\par
An example of a simple but insightful robustness analysis of the global warming
\par
problem is that presented by Lave and Dowlatabadi (1993). They examined three types
\par
of uncertainties: the relationship between emissions of greenhouse gases and global
\par
climate change; the resulting effects on humans and the environment; and the costs of
\par
reducing greenhouse gas emissions. They evaluated three hypothetical greenhouse gas
\par
reduction strategies under these uncertainties. The robustness of the results were
\par
analysed by considering the sensitivity of the decisions to the inputs, and the regret
\par
that results if incorrect inputs are used.
\par
5. How might Bayesian analysis be useful for analysing climate change?
\par
Bayesian analysis can use evidence to make and update climate change predictions and
\par
estimates of model parameters. Bayesian methods can also be used to define optimal
\par
natural resource management strategies, given the prospect of possible climate change.
\par
Three illustrations are summarized here of the use of Bayesian analysis to analyse
\par
climate change. The first two focus on inference: what models and parameters are most
\par
consistent with evidence? One of the examples examines annual flows in the Senegal
\par
River of West Africa, and the other studies sea level rise. The third example concerns
\par
optimal management of lacustrine wetlands under climatic uncertainty.
\par
