5.2.           
\par
(  , 1992)
\par
In this study, a formal method was developed for using evidence to update probability
\par
distributions for a complex model's parameters, which were treated as the unknown
\par
state of nature. The model and the probability distributions of its parameters can then
\par
be used to make predictions that reflect the remaining uncertainty. The approach was
\par
then applied to the problem of projecting sea level rise. It can also be used to improve
\par
models of other impacts of climate change, including hydrologic effects, and to
\par
systematically update those models as additional information is obtained. The steps of
\par
the method are as follows:
\par
1. Define a prior probability distribution over the parameters of the model (in
\par
their case, the parameters of a model of sea level rise). A prior distribution could
\par
also be provided over alternative models, in which different models are assigned
\par
different levels of credibility [in the spirit of Ellis (1988) who considers the
\par
implications for SO2 control of several alternative models to project acid de-
\par
position].
\par
2. Sample I times by a Monte Carlo procedure. Each realization i, i=1,...,I is
\par
then used to obtain a projection Mi( i) of one or more variables of interest (here,
\par
future sea levels). Each projection Mi( i) is assumed to be equally likely (i.e.
\par
having a prior P(Mi( i))=1/I).
\par
3. The variables to be observed, or evidence, are designated as z (in this case,
\par
observed changes in sea level at different locations). A likelihood function
\par
P(z Mi( i)) is derived, based on likely sources of observational error.
\par
4. z is observed, and then Bayes' law is used to update the posterior distribution
\par
of model projections P(Mi( i)z). These can then be translated back into posterior
\par
distributions for the model parameters P( z).
\par
5. Step 2 can then be returned to, this time sampling from P( z) rather than P( ).
\par
Then, as new evidence is obtained, the distribution can be further updated by
\par
repeating Step 4.
\par
To illustrate the application of this procedure, Patwardhan and Small's (1992) application
\par
to sea level rise is summarized below.
\par
The model used by them was a simple one-dimensional upwelling-diffusion model
\par
of the ocean that simulates the effect of thermal expansion upon sea levels. Within
\par
are four parameters that were modeled as being uncertain:
\par
{$\bullet$} temperature sensitivity (equilibrium surface temperature increase for doubled
\par
CO2,
\par
{$^\circ$}C)
\par
{$\bullet$} vertical diffusion coefficient for heat transfer (cm2/sec)
\par
{$\bullet$} ocean upwelling velocity (m/year)
\par
{$\bullet$} ocean mixed layer depth (m)
\par
In addition, the uncertain state of nature included growth rates for atmospheric
\par
concentrations of four greenhouse gases, which are also treated as being uncertain.64
\par
Bayesian analysis of climate change risks
\par
The probability distributions for each of these parameters and variables are assumed
\par
to be uniform and independent.
\par
The output Mi( i) of the model is a projection of mean sea level for a given range
\par
of years for an assumed state of nature i. By sampling the parameters from the
\par
assumed distribution of and then calculating one projection for each sample, a range
\par
of predictions Mi( i) for sea levels can be obtained. For instance, their model projects
\par
a mean sea level change of about 0{$\cdot$}75 m by the year 2100, with a 90\% credible interval
\par
of 0{$\cdot$}2 m to 1{$\cdot$}3 m. As actual sea levels and gas concentrations are observed over time,
\par
this evidence can be used to update the parameters of the model, resulting in (presumably,
\par
but not necessarily) less uncertainty. However, z consists of local measurements of sea
\par
level, which can differ from global means because of factors such as isostatic adjustment
\par
and changes in long-term wind patterns. These factors can be considered in the
\par
derivation of likelihood functions P(z Mi( i)).
\par
To illustrate how this updating process can work, Patwardhan and Small (1992)
\par
first used observed sea levels from 1900 to 1980 as z. As a result of applying their
\par
Monte Carlo Bayesian procedure, they refined the distributions of the four model
\par
parameters. The largest change was in the temperature sensitivity, whose 90\% credible
\par
interval shrunk from [0{$\cdot$}69,4{$\cdot$}3] to [0{$\cdot$}96,4{$\cdot$}3]. As another illustration of the method, they
\par
demonstrated how the method would have been applied sequentially over the period
\par
1900--1980 as additional evidence accumulated. When year 2000 sea levels were forecast
\par
using just evidence obtained through the year 1900, the 90\% credible interval for the
\par
rise relative to 1900 levels was [0{$\cdot$}13 m, 0{$\cdot$}75 m]. As evidence accumulated through the
\par
century, this interval shrunk until by 1980 it was [0{$\cdot$}10 m, 0{$\cdot$}25 m].
