\par
(, 1994)      
\par
( ET AL., 1994)
\par
This example integrates the problems of inference and decision-making in a Bayesian
\par
framework. The problem Krzysztofowicz (1994) addresses can be simply stated, as
\par
follows. There is uncertainty concerning the future climate; the state of nature
\par
represents possible future climates. A decision must be made either to commit resources
\par
(e.g. invest in a flood control project) or wait for further information z that can be
\par
used to revise prior probabilities of the various climates. (In addition, operating decisions
\par
that are not irreversible commitments can also be made, but I disregard that complication
\par
here.) This commitment of resources is called a ``stopping''. Waiting can be worthwhile
\par
if the net benefits of the resource commitment depend on climate. Waiting can result
\par
in, ultimately, a better resource commitment (e.g. design of a hydraulic structure), but
\par
there are likely to be opportunity costs in the meanwhile---no benefits are received from
\par
a structure that is not built.
\par
Krzysztofowicz (1994) assumes that the evidence Zt at time t of climate change has
\par
a likelihood function of the form P(zi  ,zt-1). That is, given that the actual climate is
\par
, zt is a first order (autoregressive) Markov process. Given this likelihood function,
\par
and the prior probabilities of , Bayes' law (4) can be applied to the evidence, resulting
\par
in an update of the decision-makers' beliefs concerning climate change. Krzysztofowicz
\par
(1994) presents two important results for this problem under the assumption that there
\par
exists a utility function of the type I discussed in Section 3. The first is that an
\par
optimal ``stopping-control'' strategy can, in theory, be obtained by stochastic dynamic
\par
programming. This strategy describes the best decision now (whether or not commitB. F. Hobbs 65
\par
resources, and how to control the system), and the best decisions in the future under
\par
all possible realizations of the evidence zt. The second is that the value of improved
\par
forecasts (information) can be calculated.
\par
This general framework is relevant to many problems involving the management
\par
of the Great Lakes of North America. Below, I summarize an application to wetlands
\par
management as an illustration (Hobbs et al., 1994). Other applications have been made
\par
to decisions concerning construction of breakwaters at Presque Isle, PA and regulation
\par
of the outflows of Lake Erie (Hobbs et al., 1996).
\par
An increase in greenhouse gas concentrations equivalent to a doubling of CO2
\par
concentration could drop levels of Lake Erie by 1 to 2 m (Croley, 1990). This decrease
\par
would have an enormous effect on coastal wetlands. Many existing wetlands would be
\par
deprived of the periodic inundation that they require, while new wetlands would be
\par
created from former lake bottom.
\par
To illustrate the application of Figure 1's decision framework, I consider an existing
\par
wetland that would disappear if lake levels drop that far. I assume that this wetland is
\par
situated so that the land would be valuable for commercial development.
\par
The elements of this problem are:
\par
a: decisions whether to allow commercial development a1 or to preserve the
\par
wetland (``wait'') a2. I disregard the possibility of maintaining the wetland
\par
by diking and pumping by assuming that its expense could not be justified
\par
if lake levels drop. This is because diking would cut off access to the lake,
\par
thereby destroying the wetland's value for aquatic habitat.
\par
: states of nature indicating whether lake levels are stationary around the
\par
historical mean ( 1) or whether they vary around a mean trend that decreases
\par
linearly over 40 years to a level 1{$\cdot$}5 m below the historical mean ( 2). My
\par
prior probabilities are P( 1)=P( 2)=0{$\cdot$}5.
\par
zt: evidence obtained in year t concerning whether lake levels are indeed
\par
permanently dropping because of climate change. For simplicity, I assume
\par
that yearly mean lake levels are the only such evidence, although in reality
\par
evidence can be obtained from many sources. Observations of these levels
\par
are used in Bayes' law to update the state of nature probabilities. I assume
\par
that lake levels are a first-order autoregressive process.
\par
x: outcomes, including benefits of development, if allowed, and permanent loss
\par
of wetlands. I assume, for illustration purposes, that the wetland has a high
\par
(but unspecified) ecological and social value if lake levels do not drop
\par
permanently ( 1). However, because the wetland would degrade in quality
\par
and other wetlands would be created if levels fall permanently ( 2), I assume
\par
that the wetland would have relatively little ecological value if 2 is realized.
\par
The particular performance measures I use are:
\par
x1: worth of commercial development, equal to the expected present value
\par
of the land for commercial purposes at the date of development. An
\par
expected value is calculated because it is uncertain when, if ever,
\par
commercial development would occur.
\par
x2: loss of wetlands, equal to the probability that a valuable wetland is
\par
eventually developed (i.e. that both commercial development and 2
\par
occur). In a sense, this is the long run ecological cost that could result
\par
from making a wrong decision.66
