This problem fits into the optimal stopping-control framework because:
\par
1. Commercial development is assumed to be irreversible.
\par
2. Delaying development would allow managers to obtain better information on
\par
whether lake levels are going to drop permanently, via Bayes' law.
\par
3. The benefit of not developing the wetland depends on whether lake levels
\par
permanently decrease.
\par
4. If development is delayed, economic benefits will be foregone in the meanwhile.
\par
The heart of the problem is thus whether to develop now, and run the risk that a
\par
valuable wetland will be destroyed, or to wait, and thereby give up economic benefits
\par
in the interim.
\par
One possible approach to solving this problem is to assume a monetary value for
\par
the worth of wetlands, and then combine that value with the economic value of
\par
commercial development. An alternative approach is multicriteria analysis. The focus
\par
of my multicriteria analysis is on generating tradeoffs among competing criteria x1
\par
(development benefits) and x2 (risk of ecological loss due to making the wrong decision).
\par
Different strategies will result in different levels of performance of each objective, as
\par
illustrated in Figure 3. The commercial development axis has been normalized so that
\par
``1'' represents the maximum possible economic value of development (resulting from
\par
development immediately at t=0). The points on the lower left represent conservative
\par
strategies that prohibit development unless it is almost certain that lake levels will drop;
\par
as a result, there is little chance of developing a valuable wetland but also a relatively
\par
low present worth of development. The points on the upper right represent more
\par
aggressive strategies that allow development even if climate change is highly uncertain.
\par
Consequently, the probability of mistakenly developing a valuable wetland is higher,
\par
but so are the development benefits.
\par
The points in Figure 3 are generated as follows. Rather than discretizing probability
\par
distributions of lake levels and then solving the resulting (enormous) tree by backwards
\par
dynamic programming, I opted to define a range of simple policies and then stimulateB. F. Hobbs 67
\par
their performance using Monte Carlo simulation. The policies are of the form: If in
\par
year t the posterior probability of decline in lake levels Pt( 2) exceeds a threshold T, then
\par
develop the wetland. Otherwise, wait. A low threshold T (e.g. T=0{$\cdot$}7 or less) yields the
\par
upper right points in the figure. In those cases, there are high development benefits x1
\par
because development is likely to occur relatively soon, but there is also a high probability
\par
x2 of mistakenly developing a valuable wetland. In contrast, a high threshold (T=0{$\cdot$}9
\par
or more) results in the lower left points. Such a cautious policy lowers the probability
\par
of mistaken development, but also diminishes the present worth of development benefits.
\par
There may be (indeed, are likely to be) better strategies that lie below and to the right
\par
of this curve; they might be discovered either by simulating more complex policies or
\par
by backwards dynamic programming.
\par
The exact form of the tradeoff curve depends on the assumed interest rate and the
\par
prior probabilities. If the interest rate is zero, then the curve consists of a straight line
\par
connecting the points (x1=0, x2=0) (development never occurs because the threshold
\par
is zero) and (x1=1, x2=0{$\cdot$}5) (development immediately occurs because of a high
\par
threshold, thereby resulting in a high risk of losing a valuable wetland). A change in
\par
the prior probability of climate change P( 2) scales the tradeoff curve up or down in
\par
rough proportion to that probability.
\par
When managers are presented with a curve such as Figure 3, they can decide what
\par
trade-off between development benefits and risk of wetland loss is acceptable. The
\par
decision depends on the relative weight given to wetlands and commercial development.
\par
6. Criticisms of Bayesian analysis
\par
The above applications illustrate some potential uses of Bayesian analysis for inference
\par
and decision-making. However, critics direct four types of criticisms at Bayesian theory
\par
and applications. I discuss each in turn, along with some of the rebuttals offered by
\par
advocates of Bayesian analysis.
\par
6.1.   
\par
The idea that subjective judgments of prior probabilities and likelihood functions
\par
can---and indeed should---influence the outcome of an inference process is abhorrent
\par
to many scientists. The debate between ``frequentists'' and Bayesians is now an ancient
\par
one, dating back more than a century (Poirier, 1988; Rust et al., 1988). Frequentists
\par
argue that inference should be based on observed data, and that alone; subjectivity
\par
prevents us from viewing that data objectively and can introduce bias.
\par
One reply Bayesians make to this criticism is that subjectivity is inevitable, and is
\par
merely swept under the rug by frequentists. For example, many standard statistical
\par
tests assume a priori that all parameter values are equally likely, even though, for
\par
example, physical principles might dictate non-negativity. The Bayesian approach
\par
instead makes subjective assumptions explicit, where they can be scrutinized and
\par
analysed for robustness.
\par
Subjectivity's inevitability is apparent in the effort to detect whether global warming
\par
is occurring. There has been, for example, a statistically significant warming trend
\par
during the 20th century. However, whether or not such evidence ``confirms'' a greenhouse
\par
hypothesis depends on the assumptions that are made about:
\par
{$\bullet$} other possible hypotheses (e.g. whether the possibility of sun spot-driven climate
\par
change is considered) and68
\par
Bayesian analysis of climate change risks
\par
{$\bullet$} the degree of natural variation, or ``noise'', that might mask a climate warming
\par
``signal'' (such as the random shifts in the means of regional temperature and
\par
precipitation that occur on a multi-decade time scale; Slivitsky and Mathier,
\par
1994).
\par
These can be viewed as assumptions concerning prior distributions and likelihood
\par
functions---assumptions that Bayesians make explicit and can be subject to robustness
\par
analysis.
\par
Another reply made by Bayesians is that it is demonstrably suboptimal to ignore
\par
prior beliefs when making decisions. That is, decision-makers will, in the long run,
\par
realize lower net benefits (utility) if they fail to combine prior beliefs and evidence
\par
according to Bayes' law. To just consider past direct observations of a phenomenon
\par
when making inferences (a key aspect of the frequentist approach) ignores potentially
\par
very valuable information based upon physical reasoning (models) and export opinion.
\par
Krzysztofowicz (1983), for instance, shows how an intentional disregarding of such
\par
information yields lower expected net benefits for a decision-maker who must use
\par
weather forecasts to make decisions about resource allocations (in his case, crop
\par
watering).
\par
6.2.       
\par
There is ample empirical evidence that people act contrary to the assumptions of
\par
Bayesian analysis. They fail to update prior beliefs using Bayes' Law, and they have
\par
great difficulty specifying utility functions because their preferences are incoherent and
\par
inconsistent (Kahneman et al., 1982).
\par
A Bayesians' response to this criticism might be ``so what?'' The point of Bayesian
\par
analysis is to improve upon unaided human judgment, not to imitate it. Bayes' Law is
\par
not meant to be a psychological theory that can be used to predict behavior; rather it
\par
is supposed to be a guide to making more rational, consistent, and defensible decisions.
\par
Bayesian analysis is the only integrated approach to inference and decision making
\par
that is fully consistent with a set of assumptions that have normative appeal.
\par
6.3.       '   
\par
Critics of Bayesian analysis argue that ``uncertainty'' encompasses several distinct
\par
concepts, and that subjective probabilities fail to do them justice. For instance, linguistic
\par
imprecision is one form of uncertainty. What is ``warm''? What is a ``significant''
\par
environmental impact? Another form of uncertainty results from ignorance. As an
\par
example, scientists do not know whether higher CO2 levels will encourage further
\par
accumulation of biomass (and thus sequestering of CO2) in climax forests. Imprecision
\par
and ignorance are not the same phenomena as randomness, such as is the situation
\par
with random hydrologic variables for which probability distributions have been es-
\par
timated (e.g. the annual discharge for a river with 100 years of flow data). To boil
\par
down these various dimensions of uncertainty into a single number---a subjective
\par
probability---strikes many people as distortion and oversimplification (e.g. Tonn and
\par
Schaffhauser, 1994).
\par
Fuzzy-set theory has been offered as an alternative in the case where uncertainty is
\par
due to the imprecision of language (Zadeh, 1983; Duckstein, 1994a). It represents
\par
degrees of, for instance, ``warmness'' or ``significance'' by fuzzy set membership functionsB. F. Hobbs 69
\par
whose values can range from 0 (definitely not warm or not significant) to 1 (definitely
\par
warm or significant). Although they use the same scale as probabilities, fuzzy set
\par
membership functions are subjected to different manipulation procedures. Duckstein
\par
(1994b) and Nachtnebel and Duckstein (1994) compare the practical implications of
\par
fuzzy set and Bayesian approaches.
\par
When instead ignorance is the problem, Dempster--Shafer (D--S) reasoning has been
\par
proposed as a generalization of Bayesian analysis (e.g. Caselton and Luo, 1992). Instead
\par
of assessing a precise point probability for each individual event, the D--S method
\par
characterizes uncertainty by assigning, in effect, upper and lower bounds upon the
\par
probability of an event. These bounds are called ``belief functions''. It is argued that
\par
such bounds are a reasonable characterization of the state of knowledge: if the
\par
probability distribution is known, then the bounds are very tight, but if there are few
\par
data, then the bounds can be loose. As evidence is obtained, these bounds are updated.
\par
To the criticism that subjective probabilities are an over-simplification, a conservative
\par
Bayesian might reply as follows. Under reasonable assumptions (Section 3), it can be
\par
proven that subjective probability is all that is needed for rational inference and decision-
\par
making. The main requirement is that the decision-maker can respond meaningfully to
\par
the reference lottery, which permits reduction of the many dimensions of uncertainty
\par
into one. To develop a new, more complex calculus of uncertainty, such as fuzzy sets
\par
or D--S reasoning, violates Occam's razor: that a model or theory should be no more
\par
complicated than necessary to accomplish its goal (Lindley, 1987). Moreover, if the
\par
object is to avoid over-precision resulting from assigning a single probability, it seems
\par
counterproductive to replace a single precise number (probability) with two such
\par
numbers (upper and lower bounds).
\par
Regarding the issue of linguistic imprecision, some risk analysts argue that im-
\par
precision is the result of laziness. They assert that definition of appropriate quantitative
\par
scales will, in most cases, get rid of the problem (Morgan and Henrion, 1990).
\par
Another reply to the criticism that Bayesian probabilities do not adequately char-
\par
acterize knowledge is that carefully controlled psychological studies fail to conclude
\par
that fuzzy set or D--S representations of uncertainty are any more valid than subjective
\par
probability. Indeed, there is some evidence to the contrary (Oden, 1977; Morgan and
\par
Henrion, 1990).
\par
A final reply is that non-Bayesian decision procedures are often ad hoc in nature.
\par
For instance, fuzzy set-based decision procedures aggregate different criteria using
\par
formula whose assumptions concerning the structure of people's preferences are unclear
\par
and never verified in practice.
\par
Dowlatabadi and Morgan (1993) also criticize the ability of Bayesian probabilities
\par
to represent people's knowledge, although for a different reason. They argue that
\par
Bayesian value of information studies tend to mischaracterize the value of additional
\par
research because analysts do not anticipate the ``surprises'' that research can reveal.
\par
They therefore recommend that stimulation approaches should be used in climate
\par
change studies to characterize the range of uncertainty in terms of probability dis-
\par
tributions, without engaging in Bayesian preposterior analysis. The retort of a Bayesian
\par
advocate might be that the methodology itself is not unsound; rather, people's prior
\par
distributions have much too little variance, consistent with the overconfidence bias
\par
identified by Kahneman et al. (1982). The Bayesian's solution would be to train people
\par
to be better calibrated assessors of subjective probabilities, which would in general
\par
result in priors that allow for a wider range of possible outcomes.70
\par
Bayesian analysis of climate change risks
\par
6.4.     
\par
Bayesian analysis can indeed be complicated. This is especially so if multiple information
\par
sources with correlated errors are to be considered; i.e. if evidence z is a vector whose
\par
elements have likelihood functions that are not conditionally independent:
\par
P(z1,z2  ){$\neq$}P(z1  )P(z2  ) (6)
\par
It is generally difficult to generate joint likelihood distributions, and the resulting
\par
complications can be involved.
\par
Conditional dependence of likelihoods is likely to be a problem when applying
\par
Bayesian analysis to climate change. For example, the results of different GCMs, which
\par
could be treated as separate zi, are likely to have correlated errors because the models
\par
use more or less similar simplifications of meteorologic and hydrologic process and
\par
rely on many of the same data sources. Consequently, the models are likely to have
\par
similar biases and cannot be considered to be conditionally independent sources of
\par
evidence.
\par
This complication has been one reason why the Bayesian approach has not been
\par
widely used in studies attempting to detect climate warming, or to estimate the effect
\par
of climate change on hydrologic systems (Duckstein, 1994a). It is also the major reason
\par
why most developers of expert systems for medical and other applications do not adopt
\par
the full Bayesian philosophy (de Mantaras, 1990). Practical expert systems have used
\par
either approximations to Bayesian reasoning that assume, e.g. independence of distinct
\par
sources of evidence, or have used non-Bayesian methods, such as D--S reasoning (again,
\par
assuming independence).
\par
One possible reply to this criticism is that a correct analysis must sometimes be
\par
complicated because the problems themselves are complex. Rival procedures, if they
\par
are simpler, take a risk by assuming important aspects of the problem away---meaning
\par
that they might yield incorrect inferences or suboptimal decisions. And, in many cases,
\par
rival procedures are actually more complex (Lindley, 1987).
\par
Nonetheless, practitioners of the Bayesian approach admit that complexity is a
\par
problem, and are working hard at developing practical methods for more difficult
\par
problems (e.g. Varis and Kuikka, 1996). Simplified solution procedures based on
\par
Bayesian concepts may prove as or more accurate and optimal than non-Bayesian
\par
methods, which make their own simplifications while, at the same time, adding un-
\par
necessary complications. An example of such a simplified procedure was presented in
\par
Section 5.3. There, a simulation method was used instead of backwards dynamic
\par
programming to identify strategies for managing the development of wetlands under
\par
climate uncertainty.
\par
7. Conclusions
\par
Bayesian analysis is an attractive approach for analysing climate uncertainties for at
\par
least three reasons.
\par
{$\bullet$} It provides useful outputs: inferences and recommendations for decisions that
\par
are consistent with those inferences.
\par
{$\bullet$} It is based on a comprehensive and coherent framework rooted in normatively
\par
appealing assumptions.B. F. Hobbs 71
\par
{$\bullet$} Compared to alternative paradigms, its concepts are familiar and, as the case
\par
studies show, its methods are often practical.
