@article{kadvanyTamingChanceRisk1996,
  title = {Taming Chance: Risk and the Quantification of Uncertainty},
  shorttitle = {Taming Chance},
  author = {Kadvany, John},
  year = 1996,
  journal = {Policy Sciences},
  volume = {29},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--27},
  publisher = {Springer},
  doi = {10.1007/BF00141477},
  langid = {english},
  note = {\emph{Policy Sciences }29: 1-27, 1996.
\par
9 1996 \emph{KluwerAcademic Publishers. Printed in the Netherlands.}
\par
\textbf{Taming chance: Risk and the quantification of uncertainty}
\par
J O H N KADVANY
\par
\emph{Applied Decision Analysis, Inc., 2710 Sand Hill Road, Menlo Park, CA 94025, U.S.A.}
\par
\textbf{Abstract. }The quantification of uncertainty through probability is central to definitions of risk
\par
used in environmental poficy analysis. This essay explores the translation of unquantified uncer-
\par
tainty into probability and the expression of allied philosophical problems in the practice of
\par
environmental risk and decision analysis. First we look at means used in science for handling
\par
uncertainty associated with some maior risks and which are not well represented through prob-
\par
ability. 'Saving the science' without quantified probability is addressed through the role of
\par
probabilistic events in risk analysis, suggesting the need to expand the scope of risk analysis to
\par
include some types of unquantified reasoning about adverse events. Next we look at uses of sub-
\par
jective probability and decision analysis to overcome problems of unquantified uncertainty in
\par
science, where we argue that a constructive conception of probability judgments, based in the
\par
foundations of decision analysis, provides \textbf{the }most useful approach for such methods. A theme
\par
throughout is the role of intellectual control implicit in our efforts 'to tame change' through the
\par
representation of uncertainty through probability.
\par
\textbf{I. Defining risk}
\par
Though alternative definitions of risk are adopted by both individual analysts
\par
and institutions, a single definition sufficient for most applications is that of
\par
risk as \emph{adverse consequences under uncertaing'. }This definition, which I will
\par
call \emph{relational risk, }succinctly identifies the dependent categories of \emph{value,}
\par
\emph{events }(including \emph{actions), }and \emph{knowledge }which jointly characterize par-
\par
ticular instances or classes of risk as a complex relation between humans and
\par
nature. Depending on preferred methods for dealing with uncertainty, theo-
\par
ries of human action, ideologies of value, and accounts of the social or natural
\par
worlds, specialized definitions can be used in place of relational risk. The best
\par
known is a special case of relational risk, namely \emph{quantified risk: }adverse con-
\par
sequences \emph{plus }a probability assignment over events. 1
\par
This is essentially the definition adopted by the U.S. Environmental Pro-
\par
tection Agency (EPA), the National Academy of Sciences (NAS), and many
\par
others. Other definitions may also be found, such as the Council on Environ-
\par
mental Ouality's (CEQ) \emph{'possibility }of suffering harm from a hazard,' (Cohrs-
\par
sen and Covello, 1989: p. 1) which attempts not to enforce the mathematical
\par
representation of uncertainty from the start, though an interpretation of 'pos-
\par
sibility' significantly different from probability is not provided by them. For
\par
some, there are no alternatives to probabilistic uncertainty. One risk analyst
\par
asserts that to be '[mathematically] coherent ... is synonymous with being
\par
objective,' and that 'probability theory is still the only rational way that is
\par
available to us for handling uncertainty' (Apostolakis, 1990: pp. 1359,1364). 2 Taken fiterally, this comment implies that there was no rationality, nor
\par
rational means of dealing with uncertainty, before, say, the seventeenth cen-
\par
tury, when probability was invented: Aristarchus, Archimedes, Copernicus,
\par
Galileo, Tycho Brahe were 'irrational' insofar as their scientific research con-
\par
tained significant uncertainties. This example is extreme, but points up the
\par
curious consequence that, taken seriously, the EPA/NAS definition implies
\par
that risk, or dangers under uncertainty, either did not exist, or could not be
\par
rigourly thought about, for most of human history.
\par
The examples of risk definitions illustrate two issues to be addressed by
\par
this essay, and two ways in which uncertainty plays a fundamental role in the
\par
quantification of dangers or pollution. The CEQ definition shows that it's not
\par
obvious what is meant by quantified versus unquantified risk, and what rela-
\par
tionships may exist be between the two. Second, as indicated by the accusa-
\par
tion of 'irrationality; the value of risk analysis for some depends on whether,
\par
or to what extent, you can represent uncertainff through probability. The
\par
relationship between quantified and unquantified uncertainty, the 'taming of
\par
chance; 3 so to speak, apparently concerns the legitimacy of risk analysis as a
\par
science, and the role of science in environmental policy analysis.
\par
But what does the requirement of quantified uncertainty amount to? Does
\par
it somehow 'follow from' the idea of risk analysis as science? Or is it the case
\par
that the quantification of uncertainty places certain \emph{demands }on science? If
\par
that is the case, can the demands always be met?
\par
To answer these questions, I'll argue in Section 2 that \emph{extensionality }mean-
\par
hag the provisional characterization of dangerous or poliuting \emph{events }in what
\par
are intended to be largely description-independent and intentionless terms, is
\par
more fundamental to risk anlaysis than the probabilistic quantification of
\par
uncertainty over such events. That is, probability models of any kind assume a
\par
'universe' (sometimes called a 'sample space') of mathematical events to
\par
which probabilities are assigned, and no events means no probability, though
\par
there still may be uncertainty. Also, \emph{provisional }means that such characteriza-
\par
tions are fallible and theory-laden through the theories defining them; there
\par
are no privileged 'Events; only 'events.' Section 2 summarizes why the con-
\par
cept of risk provides no substitutes for this general methodological require-
\par
ment. Section 3 then illustrates how extensional events are defined, discov-
\par
ered, or otherwise constituted through scientific research, while quantifica-
\par
tion of uncertainty associated \textasciitilde vith such events may lag considerably or not be
\par
forthcoming from science at all. The availability of such events is the 'demand'
\par
referred to above. The examples in Section 3 summarize aspects of this pro-
\par
cess of event-creation, so to speak, for three important environmental risks
\par
(nuclear winter, global warming, electromagnetic field exposure) and shows
\par
that science is not always completely successful in saying just \emph{what risk is. }The
\par
consequence is that limiting risk analysis for policy purposes to quantified
\par
risk, therefore, at the least confuses the role of science in policy, and at worst
\par
effectively eliminates valuable scientific research from decision-making by
\par
agencies or the public. Not that probabilistic quantification of uncertaintyshould be 'given up, whatever that might mean. We all want estimates of HIV
\par
diffusion rates, the incidence of heart disease among various populations, and
\par
the frequency of automobile accidents as a function of time of day and blood-
\par
alcohol levels. The point is that the role of uncertainty in science and our
\par
understanding of dangers can go significantly beyond the standard methodo-
\par
logical assumptions of risk analysis.
\par
Section 4 then takes up subjective (or 'Bayesian') probability theory and
\par
decision-analytic approaches to chancc as a surrogate for unquantified scien-
\par
tific uncertainty in environmental policy. Such approaches to the quantifica-
\par
tion of uncertainty are widely used in environmental policy as an alternative
\par
to probabilistic models created 'naturally' through scientific research. I argue
\par
that applications of subjective probability \emph{may }mistakenly attempt to repre-
\par
sent a social-psychological process, through which quantification is dynami-
\par
cally constructed and not neutrally 'assessed" with a kind of specious objec-
\par
tivity. As summarized in Section 5, taken together, these two explorations into
\par
the contemporary quantification of uncertainty demonstrate two means of
\par
control, or the 'taming of chance, in environmental policy and risk analysis:
\par
either the taming of real unquantified scientific uncertainty, or the tinning of
\par
psychological, social, and political processes needed to make policy decisions
\par
in spite of uncertain knowledge. The terms of my discussion include the logic
\par
of risk and probability and methodological criteria in science, and are intend-
\par
ed to place this argument at the foundations of risk analysis as opposed to
\par
some vaguely removed 'analysis of values, or the sociology of scientific or
\par
policy 'practice.' We can settle our value and institutional differences and
\par
there still will remain fundamental problems in quantifying uncertainty.
\par
\textbf{II. Extensionality in risk analysis}
\par
How might one attempt to \emph{justify }the role of probability in risk analysis, as
\par
opposed to simply \emph{assuming }its central role, as is often done?
\par
A basic goal of risk analysis is to provide means for some type of canonical
\par
representation of risk, or 'risk-in-itself,' whether the risk be attributable to
\par
lung cancer, core melts, atmospheric CO2, automobile accidents, or AIDS.
\par
\emph{We need something to count. }Even if, as analysts, we happen to lack confi-
\par
dence in a risk estimate, we think that 'a risk still exists, however uncertain its
\par
calculation, (Wilson, 1987: p. 45) and a central purpose of risk analysis is to
\par
characterize, causally and quantitatively, what that risk \emph{is. }But while this broad-
\par
brush characterization of risk analysis may seem too uncontroversial or general
\par
to be of importance, it also does not imply that the 'natural' or 'obvious' treat-
\par
ment of uncertainty is through probability. Nor are there alternative 'appeals'
\par
to the role of science in risk analysis which might justify that 'derivation.'
\par
For example, risk is not amenable to the kind of axiomatic analysis pro-
\par
vided for concepts like mass or force, in the sense that risk is not reducible to
\par
a calculus of physical abstractions. Neither is risk a 'natural kind,' like water,fungi, neurons, or stars. That is, 'risk' does not denote a class of objects, sub-
\par
stances, events, or processes falling under a readily identified observational or
\par
functional group which then can be analyzed through observed or theorized
\par
commonalties. Indeed, risk analyses utilize a range of principles associated
\par
with different paradigmatic contexts, such as ecological versus human health
\par
risks, and depend very much on the type of risk at issue. Neither are there
\par
genuine conjectures and refutations, nor crucial experiments, nor scientific
\par
research programs about risk \emph{per se. }There is not, for example, nor will there
\par
ever be, a set of 'risk constants' (10 -6 notwithstanding) a nor 'conservation
\par
laws' as part of the analytical theory of risk. Nor is there a characteristic
\par
explanatory style, such as mechanistic explanations in physics, or functional
\par
explanations in biology, or holistic-mathematical explanations in theoretical
\par
ecology. Rather, the goal of defining 'risk as it is' is achieved through an array
\par
of methodological techniques used to create risk analyses using many kinds of
\par
theories and styles of scientific reasoning. Often, risk analysis adds no funda-
\par
mentally new consequences to the theories is uses; as stated by the NAS, 'In
\par
principle, risk analysis does no more than organize information from substan-
\par
tive disciplines in a way that allows overall estimates of risk to be computed'
\par
(National Research Council, 1989; p. 223). There is also no special category
\par
of \emph{causality }addressed by risk analysis. Causality, for a discipline as broad as
\par
risk analysis, is largely an extra-scientific concept, added ahnost as a socio-
\par
logical honorific to a compelling body of research, say that associated with the
\par
conjecture that 'smoking causes cancer.' The risk analyst Lester Lave, for
\par
example, says that causality is 'a theoretical construct; it cannot be proved
\par
empirically. When scientists speak of proving causality, they are referring to a
\par
consensus among scientists .... Neither laboratory experiments nor epidemi-
\par
ology can "prove" causality' (Lave, 1982: p. 36). 5 \emph{Therefore: the use of proba-}
\par
\emph{bility follows neither from a risk-specific methodology nor a risk-specific con-}
\par
\emph{ception of causality.}
\par
The definition of risk as a \emph{relation, }quantified or not, also has similar con-
\par
sequences for the scope of risk analysis and the role of uncertainty. Environ-
\par
mental risk, for example, is a relation among a hazard source, exposure
\par
pathways, exposed populations or environments, and health or environmen-
\par
tal consequences (Merkhofer and Covello: 1994, p. 2). 6 As may be said to
\par
make the point, a plutonium baseball in outer space poses less risk than does
\par
a can of cleanser in a child's hand. This variable relational structure results in
\par
an almost unlimited variety of causal models, ranging from fault-trees for
\par
nuclear reactors, to pharmacokinetic conveyer mechanisms in the body, to
\par
theories of chemical fate and transport in soils and groundwater. While such
\par
model structures may be organized in taxonomies, the taxonomy is purely a
\par
(valuable) organizational schema which does not provide a further theoretical
\par
basis for risk. That is, there is no essential 'structure' to risk relations, even
\par
\emph{limiting }ourselves to quantified risk. In any instance, the 'right' model of what
\par
a risk is depends on the types of sciences involved, whether the consequences
\par
affect health or the environment, the likely principal causes of the risk, thechoice of risk metric (e.g., indMdual versus population risk), the current level
\par
of scientific knowledge, and heuristics used in various biological, medical, or
\par
physical sciences. While applications of, say, mass, motion, or momentum
\par
have, at least in \emph{relative terms, an }overarching and somewhat 'essentializing'
\par
set of fundamental relations connecting them together, the relational concept
\par
of risk has no such framework, \emph{in principle. }Where science lags, fails, or falls
\par
short for policy analysis, value judgments on risky outcomes, institutional
\par
control, and politics often fill the void, for better or worse. But the void is
\par
there because relational risk provides few critical methodological, explana-
\par
tory, or causal constraints.
\par
To repeat: there are no fundamental constraints on risk analysis which 'lead
\par
to' the quantification of uncertainty via probability., or no more so than science
\par
in general; the quantification of uncertainty through probability is a choice.
\par
What conceptual feature, then, \emph{should }risk analysis appeal to in order to justify
\par
probabitistic analysis, and to distinguish itself from, say, interpretive ethnogra-
\par
phies of risk, or the informal judgments sometimes called risk perceptions?
\par
The only real conceptual requirement on quantified risk analysis, or more
\par
broadly, the non-interpretative analysis of pollutions and dangers, that is con-
\par
sistent with the varieties of risks and their causes is that of \emph{extensionaBty.}
\par
Extensionality means that a theo\textasciitilde{} takes \emph{no }account of how objects or pro-
\par
cesses are described or represented. Human deaths 'are' no more than counts
\par
of individual bodies falling under mortality criteria, and may be equally-well
\par
specified as 'lives lost' or 'lives saved.' Alternative characterizations of human
\par
life should not make a difference to the outcomes studied in a quantified risk
\par
analysis. Properties that \emph{cannot }be described in extensional terms include
\par
voluntariness, controllability, equitability, or dread, i.e., all and more of the
\par
standard 'qualitative' risk factors prominent in risk perception research (e.g.,
\par
Slovic et al., 1990). Whether a risk is voluntary, for example, is a social fact
\par
that cannot be explicated only in extensional terms because the relevant cri-
\par
teria of truth may depend upon the intentional understanding of human agen-
\par
cy or causation in social acts. The non-interpretative (i.e., not involving issues
\par
of meaning and intention) analytical task is to describe the risk to living or-
\par
ganisms but not human beings, or to the environment but not nature. Such
\par
descriptions are typically extensional, while those theories describing inten-
\par
\emph{tional }human relations are typically \emph{intensional, }meaning that the theories \emph{do}
\par
depend on choices for how intentional relations are described. The role of
\par
qualitative risk factors such as voluntariness or equity, for example, may be
\par
created through the language of 'fights; 'obligations" and 'justice; all of which
\par
are manifested through the ways in which a society and its members choose to
\par
describe themselves, their nile-governed acts, and teleological behavior in
\par
general; the categories are not reducible to extensional terms \emph{only. 7}
\par
A risk analyst would likely be unsurprised to hear that she's been speaking
\par
extensionally all her life. Indeed, much of the best-known work on cognitive
\par
judgments under uncertainty may be summarized as saying exactly that com-
\par
mon-sense expressions of quantified uncertainty, at least in 'experimental' set-tings, reflect non-extensional reasoning? \emph{Most importantly, an extensional}
\par
\emph{description of risk outcomes and their causes is the fundamental prerequisite to}
\par
\emph{the use of probability in risk analysis; }that's just how probability is defined,
\par
over mathematical 'events.' This requirement is also \emph{all }that distinguishes risk
\par
analysis, in general, from other risk representations or languages of risk. The
\par
reliance on probability as a measure of uncertainty will be useful only so far
\par
as there are the types of (theory-laden and revisable) events needed for prob-
\par
abilistic reasoning. So to the extent that scientific uncertainty is \emph{exactly about}
\par
the definition or characterization of dangerous events, we may know just
\par
enough to be concerned about a danger, yet not enough to quantify that
\par
uncertainty via probability. That should not mean we ignore or even distort
\par
the science. Rather, we need to extend risk analysis to accommodate such
\par
unquantified scientific uncertainty, which may even be the rule rather than the
\par
exception with regard to many contemporary hazards?
\par
The next section supports this claim using three examples of unquantified
\par
scientific uncertainty which today are not well-represented through any useful
\par
probability distribution, and which illustrate the role of science in 'providing'
\par
or 'not providing' events for risk analysis. The point of these examples will be
\par
that the uncertainty associated with many scientific (e.g., physical, biomedical,
\par
geophysical, etc.) analyses of risk is not usefully cast in probabilistic terms
\par
because of the provisional success science has in defining events suitable for
\par
probabilistic models. But risk analysis cannot avoid extensionality insofar as
\par
probabilistic or other quantification is a goal; and as there is no special foun-
\par
dation otherwise in risk analysis for characterizing 'risk as it is,' the proper
\par
scope of risk analysis, especially for policy purposes, \emph{should }be relational risk
\par
in general and include the unquantified expression of scientific uncertainty.
\par
\textbf{IIl. Unquantified scientific uncertainty}
\par
Quantified risk represents a considerable narrowing of the more general
\par
definition of relational risk. While relational risk has implicitly built into it any
\par
theory of the natural world or human action, and the uncertain knowledge of
\par
both, the restriction in quantified risk to events amenable to probabilistic
\par
modeling, and uncertainty restricted to uncertainty as represented by prob-
\par
ability theory, means that the transition from unquantified risk to quantified
\par
risk may be problematic when the special conditions of mathematical prob-
\par
ability are not met. While quantified risk is the most natural definition for
\par
many risks, and the one almost always adopted by analysts (at least as a goal),
\par
it fails to represent all types of uncertainty found in the scientific analysis of
\par
danger. The three examples following illustrate three 'grades' of scientific
\par
uncertainty and their qualitative features not attributable to value choices,
\par
subjective bias, or 'technical versus cultural' rationality, all of which are typical
\par
'external' explanatory notions applied to communication or policy problems
\par
associated with risk analysis. 1\textasciitilde{} Rather, this qualitative or structural uncertain-ty is largely 'internal' to the science itself and is intrinsic to some of the best
\par
science available to the policy analyst today.
\par
\emph{Example 1. }Climate forecasting for nuclear winter, or the global weather
\par
and climate changes expected to occur following a large-scale nuclear war, is
\par
a relatively simple example of unquantified risk not readily expressed as a
\par
whole through quantified risk (Turco et al., 1990). While there may be a con-
\par
sensus that 'the basic physics of nuclear winter appear to be firmly estab-
\par
lished" and specific uncertainties can be calculated for smoke emission, soot
\par
dispersion, fire ignition, and other consequences of a large-scale nuclear
\par
exchange, there is, 'as in any- complex geophysical problem ... no reliable
\par
quantitative methodology for determining the overall uncertainty, in existing
\par
climate forecasts for nuclear winter.' Nonetheless, 'existing data indicate that,
\par
should substantial urban areas or fuel stocks be exposed to nuclear ignition,
\par
severe enviromnental anomalies - possibly leading to more human casualties
\par
than the direct effects of nuclear war - would be not just a remote possibilky,
\par
but a likely outcome: That is, there is a strong likelihood of climatological
\par
perturbations accompanied by devastating consequences 'unprecedented in
\par
human history; but of such a redoubtable complexity and uniqueness that the
\par
uncertainty associated with the hazard event - at least for one research group
\par
- resists meaning\textasciitilde{} probabilistic description: there's no probability distribu-
\par
tion integrating component uncertainties in a non-trivial or useflll way.
\par
According to one set of researchers, for theoretical (though possibly not prag-
\par
matic) purposes, the \emph{theories }of nuclear winter may not be completely repre-
\par
sented in a quantified risk calculus. This risk analysis contains several prob-
\par
abilistically quantifiable components, but no quantified global assessment,
\par
and nonetheless, a clear pragmatic recommendation on the level of risk by
\par
combining quantified and tmquantified risk. A desired integration of the
\par
quantified climate indicators in an overall distribution, it is believed, has no
\par
credible basis. For these researchers, there is no useful quantitative bridge
\par
between their holistic judgment of danger and several interdependent prob-
\par
abilistic fctors: this is a point at which the quantification of uncertainty stops.
\par
For those agencies or individual strictly defining risk as quantified risk, strict-
\par
ly speaking, no total risk has been identified. At the least, in this case the pos-
\par
sibility of communicating scientific uncertainty is hampered by identifying
\par
risk \emph{only }\textasciitilde dth its quantified representativesJ I
\par
\emph{Example 2. }Global warming of the earth's atmosphere and the attendant
\par
potential for climate and environmental changes on a mass scale is another
\par
risk partaking of relational risk in essential ways, notwithstanding James
\par
Hanseffs polemical and widely reported expression of quantified uncertainty
\par
before Congress that he was '99\%' sure that global warming had occurred
\par
(Schneider, 1989: Chapter 6). Similar to nuclear winter, there is today no
\par
accepted methodolo\textasciitilde{} which integrates results depending on pure nnmerical
\par
simulation, Bayesian models of historical temperature trends, physical-chemi-
\par
cal atmospheric models, and theories of ecological-economic transformation,
\par
to mention just a few modeling techniques found in areas of active research.More cautious advocates of the global warming hypothesis are less sanguine
\par
than Hansen about summarizing an entire research program in global clima-
\par
tology by a single number or probability distribution (Schneider, 1989: pp.
\par
196-197), and offer a number of rationales. 12 For example, we may identify
\par
specific technical problems such as unrealistic models of cloud dynamics, or
\par
inadequate temperature records, as the root of skepticism in climate models;
\par
or, one may question whether asking if the climate year 1987 was, statistically
\par
speaking, '1\% hot" is a valid test of the warming conjecture; or one may ques-
\par
tion specific assumptions implicit in Hansen's claim, such as the statistical
\par
independence of climate years, or suggest correcting for urban bias induced
\par
by warm buildings; or one may propose a competing non-warming research
\par
program based on the assumption of negative feedback loops to the atmos-
\par
phere through ocean coohng.
\par
In the early 1990s this list could be continued at length, and distinguishes
\par
the risk communication on global warming from that of nuclear winter and
\par
many other risks. For global warming, the proliferation of uncertainties
\par
reflects in part the emergence of numerous research programs crossing theo-
\par
retical and technological boundaries, rather than (or at least, in addition to)
\par
the intrinsic complexity of an otherwise well-understood hazard. At the same
\par
time, basic research questions are not reducible to a 'naive falsificationist'
\par
view of scientific progress, in the sense tht the global warming conjecture can
\par
be confirmed or refuted through a crucial experiment or test criterion, speci-
\par
fiable in advance of carrying out the testJ 3 With such a test defined, one might
\par
justify Hansen's '99\% sure' with some rigor, but without an implied test \emph{event,}
\par
there is no probability. More specifically, a key problem in evaluating the risk
\par
of global warming is whether, e.g., warming (if it is occurring) is due signifi-
\par
cantly to carbon emissions from fossil-fuel electric power plants, not whether
\par
warming is occurring per se. But defining a simple 'test' for that conjecture is
\par
not what Hansen's '99\% sure' could ever mean. This requirement for a critical
\par
event is called the 'clairvoyance test'14 in decision analysis, and is precisely
\par
what a policy analyst should look for in probabilistic assertions of uncertainty.
\par
That is, one should be able to 'look into the future' like a clairvoyant and be
\par
able to specify the conditions which, in principle, show whether the prob-
\par
abilistic event has occurred or not. But the clairvoyance test often fails when
\par
applied to scientific research. Rainfall tomorrow in California is OK, tem-
\par
perature change this year in a well-defined locale is also fine, but \emph{global}
\par
\emph{warming caused by excessive carbon emissions, }and many risk hypotheses of
\par
the form 'physical process X causes risk Y' cannot be so construed, as an
\par
unknown causal structure is just what is needed to define relevant \emph{events.}
\par
Indeed, one function of global warming research programs is to \emph{define }just
\par
the tests which will serve as corroborations or refutations of the existence,
\par
mechanisms, and causes of global warming. In other words, Hanselfs famous
\par
risk message implicitly implies the maturing, rather than the beginning, of
\par
research programs whose goal is to find the events making a claim like his
\par
possible, regardless of its numerical meaning. Unlike nuclear winter, absenceof probabilistic quantification for global warming reflects perhaps less the
\par
inherent complexity of greenhouse phenomena than the level of scientific
\par
progress. There are here, then, two distinct types of qualitative scientific
\par
uncertainty.
\par
Rather than there being a single testable conjecture for global warming and
\par
greenhouse effects, there is a group of developing \emph{research programs, }whose
\par
observable or verifiable consequences are steps removed from the core of
\par
competing assumptions driving the programs forward. 15 These research pro-
\par
grams and the competition between them consist of unfinished \emph{series }of theo-
\par
ries developing over time, connected through a network of empirical and
\par
theoretical conjectures, corroborations, anomalies, technical puzzles, outright
\par
inconsistencies, shared and competing notions of physical and quantitative
\par
method, and varying heuristics for moving the programs forward in time and
\par
defending them from attack; all these categories of scientific research pro-
\par
grams are amply illustrated by popular and technical accounts of greenhouse
\par
theory and debates, and serve to organize much of greenhouse risk communi-
\par
cation. The methodological categories of greenhouse research programs are
\par
also used to justify" much of current scientific analysis of global warming, but
\par
these substantive aspects of unquantified risk are not expressed well, ff at all,
\par
through probabilistic quantification, and their roles are not ncccssarily reduc-
\par
ible to the subjectivity or politics of scientific judgment, as often suggested:
\par
the categories listed are categories of theories and representations \emph{used }in
\par
research programs. These are the 'qualitative dimensions' of greenhouse risk
\par
analysis, and concern resolving technical puzzles and theoretical anomalies,
\par
creating theoretical and empirical corroborations, elaborating core assump-
\par
tions into working models, and so on. Such qualitative dimensions exist
\par
throughout modern science, and in the case of global warming, happen to
\par
drive much risk communication.
\par
\emph{Example 3. }The final example of a risk whose scientific uncertain\textasciitilde{} is
\par
poorly represented in probabilistic terms is the potential cancer risk due to
\par
60 hertz, alternating current electromagnetic fields (EMF). These fields are
\par
created by household electrical appliances, by industrial machinery, and by
\par
transmission and distribution lines; EMFs are ubiquitous in electrified coun-
\par
tries world-wide (Nair et al., 1989). Two kinds of methodological issues are
\par
involved in quantifying the health risk, if any, due to EMF exposures. The first
\par
set of issues has to do with the anomalous character of EMF risk compared to
\par
most chemical or environmental risks. For example, biological effects of EMF
\par
on the ability of cells to communicate with one another may occur in highly
\par
non-linear patterns, such as 'windows' of field frequency and intensity,
\par
between which no biological effects do occur. Furthermore, even for windows
\par
in which effects occur, increasing frequency or intensity does not necessarily
\par
lead to stronger effects; at least at the cellular level, it is not apparent that
\par
'more is worse" as is often assumed for better-understood and regulated risks.
\par
It is also not apparent what measure of dose is relevant for epidemiological
\par
studies, among time-averaged, threshold, cumulative, or other EMF exposure10
\par
metrics. For these reasons, the problem of simply defining, much less asses-
\par
sing, EMF health risks in terms of sources, exposure, and consequences
\par
poses technical and theoretical difficulties of the first order, while sufficient
\par
concern has been raised by biological and epidemiological studies to rule out
\par
the categorical denial that EMF could possibly pose any health risk, as was
\par
asserted openly by many until only recentlyJ 6 As with global warming, an
\par
important focus of non-quantitative, scientific risk discourse on EMF is the
\par
structure and progress in EMF research; as for nuclear winter, there are fun-
\par
damental theorctical complexities resisting solution as well.
\par
The second set of issues involved in quantifying EMF health risk consists
\par
in how causal theories may direct the transition from unquantified to quanti-
\par
fied risk. No biophysical mechanism is now known which would account for
\par
EMF health effects, and it is possible that the discovery of an explanatory
\par
mechanism will generate a significant reorganization 17 in cellular biophysics,
\par
meaning a major change in the fundamental assumptions on which cellular
\par
research programs are based, with a scope going well beyond the puzzles pre-
\par
sented by exposures to appliances or power-lines. This situation differs con-
\par
siderably from that of global warming, where there is not an expectation that,
\par
say, some physical conservation law \textasciitilde fill require 'exceptions' to explain green-
\par
house phenomena. For EMF, the shift would consist in the rejection of the
\par
core assumption, widely held until only the last decade, that EMF, as a form
\par
of nonionizing radiation, could have no significant biophysical \emph{(afortiori,}
\par
health) effects because it does not carry sufficient energy either to heat tissue
\par
or break chemical bonds, as Unlike global warming or nuclear winter, the
\par
quantitative assessment of EMF as a public health risk may depend on a radi-
\par
cal and epoch-making reorganization of some scientific and medical knowl-
\par
edge. Hence we have a third level of qualitative uncertainty, beyond analytical
\par
complexity and progress in evolving research programs, corresponding to
\par
epistemie change on the order of replacing or significantly revising core
\par
assumptions of multiple research programs extending beyond EMF research
\par
on its own. This unquantified uncertainty also plays a key role in EMF risk
\par
communication and public policy. For example, consider the statement that
\par
'researchers ... put the chances at between 10\% and 60\% that EMFs will turn
\par
out to have some health effects. '19 Either there is an \emph{event }to which '10\%" and
\par
'60\%' \emph{refer - }in which case somebody knows what 'EMF turns out to have
\par
health effects' should be taken to \emph{m e a n - }or this is just another way of expres-
\par
sing a sociological insight of 'which way this thing is going to go, which is not
\par
the same at all as 'EMF has health effects'; the latter is a natural, not a social,
\par
event.
\par
The moral of these three illustrations is that important practical cases of
\par
unquantified risk may be understood in terms of the qualitative uncertainty
\par
used to represent scientific progress and the passage from unquantified to
\par
quantified risk. As mentioned, this scientific uncertainty is not explicable in
\par
terms of value-laden choices, qualitative-perceived versus quantitative risk,
\par
nor subjective bias. The uncertainty summarized here \emph{just is the process o f}11
\par
\emph{science itself, }and is not especially well-suited to representation through prob-
\par
ability. The thinking is extensional, and that is what a risk analyst can draw
\par
upon; but there is no consistently meaningful quantification of key aspects of
\par
the scientific uncertainty, and the policy analyst waiting for quantification
\par
ignores useful science at her own risk.
\par
In the next section we look at an approach with deep philosophical roots
\par
providing a different approach for the policy-maker to negotiate with unquan-
\par
tiffed uncertainty
\par
\textbf{IV. Ramsey's choice}
\par
The examples of the previous section tacitly assume that scientific credibility
\par
\emph{per se }is a critical measure of value for quantified uncertainty. Alternatively,
\par
policy- and decision-makers faced with environmental and technological
\par
problems have to make decisions, and not 'contribute' to science. The role of
\par
science for policy is to help make good decisions, not to create knowledge for
\par
its own sake. The competition of scientific research programs or the impetus
\par
to reformulate fundamental principles may be driven by imagination tangling
\par
with nature, rather than the need to allocate scarce resources.
\par
The policy-maker then has two options available. One option used often is
\par
to relax research standards so that answers are forthcoming for policy pur-
\par
poses but are not defensible as science \emph{per se. }For example, the work might be
\par
conducted by consultants and published as an agency technical report, but
\par
not be submitted to a process of peer review (Jasanoff, 1990). The character-
\par
istic feature of this approach is to make enough assumptions - whether of
\par
exposure to pesticides, groundwater transport, accidental human interven-
\par
tion, whatever - and assign, by estimation or fiat, numbers such that a 'rea-
\par
sonable' answer is forthcoming for the problem posed. The need for a \emph{type }of
\par
answer is used to back-infer assumptions needed to create it.
\par
U.S. EPA has used such models by adopting 'conservative' parameter esti-
\par
mates when needed, that is, estimates which will almost surely \emph{overestimate}
\par
the ultimate risk, such as estimates for pesticide toxicity, exposure, or species-
\par
sensitivity. Then, if the final calculation of risk is 'low; there will be informal
\par
confidence that the expected use is 'safe,' though a true risk estimate has typi-
\par
cally been lost through the uncoordinated use of 'safety factors' in model
\par
development. Among the difficulties encountered by such an approach is
\par
what happens if the policy estimate is at the borderline of what is deemed
\par
'safe' versus 'unsafe; say a calculation of (to within an order of magnitude) a
\par
10 -6 lifetime cancer risk, often used to define a \emph{de minimus }level of regulatory
\par
concern from pesticide exposure. Then, obviously, a non-organic grower or
\par
pesticide manufacturer may argue that the estimate is 'really' conservative,
\par
and that the pesticide is 'really' safe, while environmentalists may be outraged
\par
when regulators broker an agreement to minimize economic impacts by slow-
\par
ly phasing out a chemical instead of banning it outright. This scenario is12
\par
essentially what occurred in the late 1980s for the growth-enhancer sold
\par
commercially to apple growers as Alar. But when models are allowed to intro-
\par
duce safety factors for conservatism into parameter estimation, it's very diffi-
\par
cult to untangle legitimate assumptions from those invented for policy
\par
'science.'
\par
As another examle, which will be continued below, the Department of
\par
Energy (DOE) has wanted to know the likely" patterns of seismic activity,
\par
among many other physical conditions, to occur in the vicinity of the pro-
\par
posed high-level nuclear waste repository at Yucca Mountain, Nevada \emph{for the}
\par
\emph{next 10,000 years. }The repository is intended as permanent storage for the
\par
nuclear waste which has been accumulating for decades at the nation's nu-
\par
clear power plants. Through the Nuclear Waste Policy Act and its amend-
\par
ments, the federal government has guaranteed that it would develop a reposi-
\par
tory for nuclear waste generated by the nation's utilities using a tax levied on
\par
electric energy production. The 10,000 year value is not arbitrary, but is
\par
defined by environmental regulations administered by the EPA. Part of the
\par
solution for the DOE, in brief, was to extrapolate known geological trends
\par
into the future, which certainly provides \emph{an }answer, but one which has been
\par
severely criticized as geological science: 10,000 year site-specific forecasts in
\par
geology simply lack general scientific credibility (Shrader-Frechette, 1993).
\par
While for DOE the computation apparently had temporary pragmatic value,
\par
the approach leaves much to be desired in intellectual terms. Nonetheless,
\par
along with the Alar example it is illustrative and typical of one means used by
\par
policy-makers to tame scientific uncertainty for public policy. In some cases,
\par
if scientists won't produce estimates on their own, policy-makers or their
\par
proxies will have it done for them.
\par
A second option for the policy-maker is to eschew science as the correct
\par
\emph{ultimate }context for environmental policy. Not that science is to be discarded;
\par
far from it. But the results of science are to be seen specifically as a hand-
\par
maiden to decision-making: scientific facts, theories, research programs, and
\par
conclusions are of interest only insofar as they contribute to, say, selecting a
\par
site for the high-level waste repository; or deciding whcthcr to retrofit miles
\par
of electrical transmission or distribution lines to mitigate potential electro-
\par
magnetic field risk; or allocating more resources for ecological habitat resto-
\par
ration. \emph{Selecting, retrofitting, and restoring are all actions }to be \emph{decided }upon,
\par
and the knowledge one has today, scientific or otherwise, is what one has for
\par
decisions or choices that must be made \emph{now; }the excuse that 'more research is
\par
needed' is off the table. 2\textasciitilde{} Contextualizhlg knowledge with respect to deci-
\par
sions, outcomes, and preferences is the perspective of \emph{decision analysis, }and
\par
our philosophical interest is how the transition occurs between unquantified
\par
uncertainty and the probabilistic judgments required to make a decision
\par
analysis complete.
\par
In this century, it was the logician Frank Ramsey who thought first and
\par
deepest of uncertainty in the context of action or decision. 'Let us" wrote
\par
Ramsey in 1931, 'try to find a method of measuring beliefs as \emph{bases ofpossible}13
\par
\emph{actions.., }our judgment about the strength of our belief is really about \emph{how}
\par
\emph{we should act }in hypothetical circumstances...,' and not by the 'introspection'
\par
of 'belief-feelings' and the like (Ramsey, 1988: p. 29, emphasis added). Thus
\par
began one major branch of modern decision theory and the formalization of
\par
subjective or 'Bayesian' probability theory. Ramsey provided the basic argu-
\par
ments and a set of axioms involving preferences for uncertain choices needed
\par
to show that mathematical probability could be interpreted as representing
\par
'degrees of belief.' Regardless of the quality of our science or other knowledge
\par
base, environmental risk experts, environmentalists, policy-makers, whoever
\par
\textbf{- }could, in principle - quantify their beliefs as Ramsey's probabilities as long
\par
as these numbers were derived from possible choices among variously pre-
\par
ferred outcomes. Since 'choosing' a 'preferred' scientific hypothesis makes
\par
little sense, Ramsey's paradigm is an alternative to scientific practice in the
\par
context of policy and decision-making, zl Ramsey and others of the early
\par
twentieth century assumed that degrees of belief took as their 'objects,' or
\par
what degrees of belief were degrees of, \emph{propositions, }meaning not intuitions or
\par
feelings, but idealized descriptions of outcomes or possible states of the
\par
world which occur under uncertainty. Ramsey not only showed how to inter-
\par
pret probability through preference and decision, he saw that the means for
\par
associating such preferences with individuals - hence 'subjective' probability
\par
\textbf{- }was the language representing such preferences.
\par
A marvelous consequence of Ramsey's approach, later finessed by yon
\par
Neumann and Morgensteru, de Finetti, Savage, and others, is that the rule for
\par
selecting the optimal choice among competing alternatives is simple. One
\par
chooses on the basis of highest mathematical 'expected value" basically, the
\par
sum of numerical values assigned to outcomes weighted by their probabili-
\par
ties. 22 The simplicity and elegance of Ramsey's approach is unsurpassed. His
\par
theory's computational transparency and constructive content make it entire-
\par
ly suitable for practical problems. For example, Ramsey's method for assign-
\par
ing probabilities to propositions, or statements, is to ask for the decision-
\par
maker's (expert's, environmentalist's, etc.) preferences between outcomes:
\par
'Are you indifferent between (A) a sure loss of \$100,00 \emph{versus }(B) a 1/10
\par
chance of losing 10 acres of wetlands, \emph{or }do you prefer one prospect over the
\par
other? '23 An adequate set of propositions or statements expressing, e.g.,
\par
'option B is preferred to option A,' can be used to derive more complex pref-
\par
erences among alternatives associated with uncertain outcomes, say, the pri-
\par
orization of wetland restoration sites given a limiIed budget. Such is the basis
\par
in language for an empirical process of subjective probability assessment. 24
\par
To give a sense of the type of problem that can be addressed through Ram-
\par
sey's paradigm, and subjective probability judgments, consider the choice of
\par
which of five sites to select for the hi\textasciitilde -level nuclear waste repository men-
\par
tioned above, from among Yucca Mountain (a site in volcanic tuff, and the
\par
site tentatively selected), Davis Canyon (a bedded salt site in Utah), Deaf
\par
Smith (a bedded salt site in Texas), Hartford (a basalt site in Washington), and
\par
Richton Dome (a salt dome in Mississippi) (Merkhofer and Keeney, 1987).14
\par
\textbf{The possible siting 'outcomes' are complex, in fact multiple-valued, involving}
\par
\textbf{the aggregation of potential health impacts, environmental impacts, socio-}
\par
\textbf{economic imapcts, and other categoriesY But the basic decision-analytic}
\par
\textbf{approach of valuing outcomes and comparing outcomes weighted by their}
\par
\textbf{probabilities is the same. The decision alternatives have been defined in}
\par
\textbf{advance and the problem is to compre the \emph{relative }benefits and \emph{relative }prob-}
\par
\textbf{abilities of choices, which is all that Ramsey and his successors carl provide.}
\par
\textbf{That is, there's nothing in decision analysis axioms telling you in some \emph{abso-}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{lute }sense whether you've assigned the right probabilities, and obviously the}
\par
\textbf{outcome values or utilities are wholly value-laden, which is admirably explicit}
\par
\textbf{in the decision-analytic approach. All you are told is how to construct a, not}
\par
\textbf{\emph{the, consistent }set of probabilities, and how to calculate optimal choices from}
\par
\textbf{them, assuming Ramsey's axioms. In this case, it turns out that of the five}
\par
\textbf{options, Yucca Mountain is optimal. Hartford was selected first for political}
\par
\textbf{reasons, but the choice was changed later to the Nevada site; a clear improve-}
\par
\textbf{ment according to the analysis, showing its worth, but the site selection proc-}
\par
\textbf{ess was still overwhelmingly politicked, and the utilization of Yucca Moun-}
\par
\textbf{tain is not proceeding as scheduled today (Jacob, 1990).}
\par
\textbf{As mentioned, all that decision analysis can tell you - which is a lot - is how}
\par
\textbf{to make complex judgments under uncertainty \emph{consistent. }For concreteness,}
