What can be said about these numbers as relative or absolute likelihoods is
\par
instructive in terms of the transition from unquantified to quantified risk. The
\par
strength of the decision analysis depends, in part, on the strength of these
\par
numbers. If the numbers are credible in some absolute sense, then that
\par
strength increases; if the numbers are only relatively consistent, the strength
\par
diminishes, but not to zero by any means. Relatively consistency, however, is
\par
often enough for decision-making. For example, Table 1 shows that the
\par
'incomplete sealing of the shafts and the repository' is twice as likely- to occur
\par
at Deaf Smith than at Davis Canyon; and is about \emph{two orders of magnitude}
\par
more likely to occur at Hartford than each of these three sites. More than the
\par
point estimates of likelihoods, these relative comparisons are just what Rmn-
\par
sey was looking for, and are fully in line with the mathematics of subjective
\par
probability. Indeed, when Hartford was selected by Congress as the initial site,
\par
this analysis was used to discredit that alternative, 27 and it is hard to fault this
\par
use of the study. From such relative judgments alone, one can infer compara-
\par
tive benefits, given a fixed set of decision alternatives.
\par
Mathematically and axiomatically, nothing stronger than relative consisten-
\par
cy of judgments and the comparative value of choices can be asserted. But, as
\par
occurs for many descendants of Ramsey, such a soft transition from unquanti-
\par
fled to quantified risk is not enough, as great an improvement as it is, say, over
\par
EPA's 'safety factors.' A claim of the absolute accuracy of such subjective
\par
judgments is desired, in the sense that the experts' judgments are hoped to be
\par
\emph{calibrated }to the \emph{true values. }That is, in contrast to Bruno de Finetti's polemi-
\par
cal assertion that 'probability, does not exist' - i.e., that subjective probability
\par
is completely a matter of self-consistent, personal judgments - in many appli-
\par
cations of subjective probability a claim for such internal consistency is im-
\par
plicitly considered insufficient. Instead, some intimation is made for the abso-
\par
lute correctness of the judgments involved, thus creating a hybrid probability
\par
theory which is half frequentist-objectivist, and half-personalist-subjective,
\par
and, one supposes, is intended to regain some sliver of quasi-scientific objec-
\par
tivity and thereby to tame uncertainty.
\par
In the repository siting case, for example, it is mentioned that psychological
\par
studies 'suggest that professionals with training in assessing probabilities can
\par
conduct this task in a reliable manner,' referring to the process of improving
\par
weathermeffs personal probabilistic forecasts (e.g., the likelihood of rain on a
\par
given day in a town) which, unlike the high-level repository, can be improved
\par
through empirical observation and feedback to forecasters of weather out-
\par
comes: rain, hail, sun, clouds, etc. With a 10,000 year horizon for the events at
\par
of concern, such feedback is not a serious option, and the probabilities may
\par
not be 'updated' from prior distributions to posterior distributions; to finesse
\par
the point, the likelihoods are 'subjective' but not 'Bayesian" Whatever calibra-
\par
tion is possible must be created by ensuring consistency on prior probabili-
\par
ties. But consistency does not imply uniqueness, and so calibration is largely a
\par
hope, though one which may be guided by knowledge of typical heuristics for
\par
judgment under uncertainty, (Kahneman et al., 1982). While that limitation16
\par
has no implications for the relative ranking of sites for a high-level waste
\par
repository, there is a significant limitation on the political force that can be
\par
claimed for the analysis, and the type of political control that might be expect-
\par
ed to ensue through this interpretation of nuclear uncertainty. As far as the
\par
axiomatic theory goes, there is no fundamental basis for calibration, no 'third
\par
way' between the limited, self-consistent judgments of Ramsey, de Finetti, or
\par
Savage, and the frequentist view of traditional statistics. 28
\par
Yet, the Janus-faced figure of calibrated objective-subjective probability
\par
appears throughout the probability assessment literature. For example, a
\par
distinguished review article opens with the assertion that 'probability \emph{en-}
\par
\emph{coding, }the process of \emph{extracting }and quantifying individual judgment about
\par
uncertain quantities, plays an important role in the application of decision
\par
analysis'29; yet 'encoding' has no mathematical basis apart from the classical
\par
sources, nor does the 'extraction' metaphor, which, one presumes, is sup-
\par
posed to make the assessment analogous to the determination of something
\par
like a psychophysical magnitude, like perceived hue or pitch. The metaphors
\par
of extraction and encoding are subtle means of enhancing objectivity and
\par
taming uncertainty in ways not guaranteed by mathematical theory alone.
\par
There is also a large literature on 'bias' and 'debiasing' (Fischhoff, 1982)
\par
which can be taken in a relative or absolute sense. That is, there are means for
\par
eliminating many \emph{relative }biases which tend to create inconsistencies, or,
\par
taken more strongly, there are means for eliminating biases tending to create
\par
deviations from a 'true value" The former is what should be meant while the
\par
latter may often be \emph{allowed }to be meant. In passing from unquantified to
\par
quantified risk it is more desirable, for some, to provide control and tame
\par
chance through a synthetic objectivity, rather than being satisfied with the
\par
relative improvement provided to the decision-making process by being
\par
explicit, by articulating consequences, and by identifying ranges of outcomes
\par
and the tradeoffs associated with them.
\par
Let's see how deeply the metaphors persist. The guidance given to encode
\par
or extract is not always that of enlightened dialogue. One source provides
\par
instruction on how to proceed, somewhat reminiscent of guidance once pro-
\par
vided for eliciting witchcraft accusations, in the case of 'experts who "can't or
\par
won't play the elicitation game" ': that is, scientists who are 'culturally or
\par
esthetically unable to put probabilities on things [sic] "3\textasciitilde{} These scientists need
\par
to be tamed:
\par
Depending on the circumstance of the elicitation there are a number of
\par
ways in which an analyst can 'turn up the heat' on an expert. You can go
\par
through a series of arguments to show that answering the questions will not
\par
violate the expert's scientific integrity. You can point out that some deci-
\par
sions have to be made before all the scientific facts are in hand, and ask if
\par
the expert would really feel better if people with less scientific understand-
\par
ing of the problems than they provided the scientific judgments on which
\par
society bases its decisions. You can try to make it clear that in this frame-17
\par
work, probability is not a statement about the real world but is, rather, a
\par
statement about the expert's belief .... \emph{And you can simply persist until it}
\par
\emph{becomes easier for the expert to cooperate than continue to resist.}
\par
For brevity, let's call this assessment step, conducted with reluctant or intran-
\par
sigent assessees, \emph{inquisition.}
\par
To sort out the difficulties in what is occurring in this somewhat outrageous
\par
passage, it's necessary to get back to what a subjective probability and a prob-
\par
ability assessment are supposed to be, which will return us to the philosophi-
\par
cal focus of our exploration. Frank Ramsey warned that these are not intro-
\par
spective extractions of an internal state, but objective preferences, or desired
\par
choices between alternatives, and that such choices are expressed in terms of
\par
\emph{propositions. }A proposition, unfortunately, is a relatively mystical entity.
\par
Propositions are an abstraction of sentences and have long been eschewed by
\par
logicians, if not by as many analytic philosophersP More straightforwardly,
\par
preferences are expressed through sentences, being simply statements of
\par
preferences, or other judgments involving uncertainty and outcomes. A
\par
probability assessment may be defined then as a consistent set of sentences,
\par
being a reconstructed fragment of discourse, involving relevant decision alter-
\par
natives, outcomes, and uncertainties, and expressing the decision-maker's
\par
preferences. Interestingly, there's nothing in the notion of subjective probabil-
\par
ity and utility that is inherently cognitive; perhaps a better term of art would
\par
be \emph{intersubjective }probability. But over the years that decision analysis has
\par
developed, vague phrases like 'measuring degrees of belief,' which Ramsey
\par
used informall?; other introspective-sounding locutions, or 'extraction, have
\par
unfortunately obfuscated the logical basis underlying assessment. An assess-
\par
ment is, first, \emph{a set of sentences, }and theories of subjective probability are
\par
therefore metatheories about the sentences produced by people who make
\par
assertions involving probability. These metatheories may be purely math-
\par
ematical, cognitive, psychoanalytic, social-psychological, psychophysical,
\par
mystical - take your pick; but none of that is implied in assessment per se. 32
\par
For example, assertions included in an assessment involve probabilities about
\par
events, but typically, these events never directly become part of our experi-
\par
ence, and hence there is not necessarily a psychophysical basis, as often sug-
\par
gested, for a kind of introspected 'hedonic' v a l u e 33 - yet another attempt to
\par
tamc unccrtainty by creating for it a quasi-physical status. Rather, what we
\par
experience primarily is \emph{information }about events, meaning the uncertain
\par
status of events and outcomes. If my decision or preferences depends on
\par
some conjecture or hypothesis H, whether on the state of ecosystems, high-
\par
level waste repositories, or cancer cases, I don't act directly on whether H is
\par
true or false; H must be interpreted to be true or false through its expression
\par
in language. A decision-maker can consider preferences among only those
\par
possibilities expressed in sentences she understands. An assessment tests can-
\par
didate sets of sentences for consistency under Ramse\textasciitilde s (or Savage's, or
\par
whoever's) rules. If inconsistent, the?, may be modified to make them con-18
\par
sistent; that's part of the normative content of decision analysis. Hence typi-
\par
cally, an assessment will take the form of a dialogue which eventuates in a
\par
final set of judgments expressed as sentences involving preferences, uncer-
\par
tainties, and actions. This 'convergent' set, when it exists, may conventionally
\par
be called 'the assessment'; it is neither an 'extraction,' nor an 'encoding" and
\par
its quality is not improved by inquisition.
\par
Once assessment is framed logically, in terms of sentences and sets of (con-
\par
sistent) sentences, or what logicians call a \emph{theory, }one question is immediate:
\par
Is the theory \emph{complete? }That is, is a positive preference between alternatives,
\par
or associated probability judgments, \emph{always }implied by the theory 'held by'
\par
the assessee? Can I always provide a judgment of preference \emph{one way or an-}
\par
\emph{other? }Suppose it is said that, 'I can't choose bctween the S100,000 and the
\par
uncertain prospect on the ecosystem, but I'm not indifferent between them, I
\par
have no preference" In terms of a theory T expressing these preferences, this
\par
is to say that thc theory is incomplete, which is a legitimate fact for a person to
\par
have about their beliefs: for a sentence s couched in the language of the
\par
theory, neither s or \emph{not-s }is contained in T or derivable from T. Our inquisi-
\par
tor's plight above, to wrest a judgment from the recalcitrant assessee, then has
\par
no \emph{logical }foundation, though it may have its pragmatic motivations. It is an
\par
empirical question whether, at the start of a probability assessment, individ-
\par
uals 'have' a theory to be 'extracted.' Indecision does not imply indifference. 34
\par
Stated positively, this means that subjective probabilities have to be construct-
\par
ed, the), don't spring fully-blown, extracted, from anyone's head, and the
\par
preferences we construct may depend on which questions we ask ourselves.
\par
As stated by Glen Sharer, the \emph{false }idea is that
\par
a person's true preferences are well-defined before he deliberates and that
\par
he just needs to ask himself the right question in order to find out this true
\par
preference .... From our constructive viewpoint, we see quite a different
\par
picture. The man [in Shafer's example] does not really have a true prefer-
\par
ence, and he is looking to various arguments ... in an effort to construct
\par
one .... For the constructive view, indecision is the starting point. Before
\par
we start to work constructing preferences, we may be undecided between
\par
all pairs of acts. We may not even have thought of all the possible acts. But
\par
this does not mean we are indifferent. As we construct preferences, we
\par
eliminate some indecision. In the end we may eliminate all indecision; we
\par
may, that is to say, rank all acts in a strict order of preference .... But
\par
Savage has given us no reason why we should feel compelled to carry our
\par
elimination of indecision so far .... Why is it normative to ... rank all acts?
\par
(Sharer, 1988: pp. 197, 204).
\par
The \emph{elimination }of indecision, or its transformation into indifference or
\par
strict preference, is empirical and pragmatic, not logical or mathematical,
\par
while the constructive process of eliminating indecision, through an assess-
\par
ment, may still be cast logical terms: a 'dialogue' is simply a shared set of19
\par
sentences created by the assessee and assessor, and through which a theory is
\par
constructed. \emph{A constructive account of preference and uncertainty therefore is}
\par
\emph{builtinto the foundation, }where 'constructive' refers to the process of enumer-
\par
ating, revising, and potentially converging on an assessment. Constructive
\par
accounts of preference are now finding favor among cognitive researchers
\par
(Gregory et al., 1993) who criticize the essentialist, 'extraction' perspectives
\par
of some economists who may be asked by the government to 'estimate' the
\par
environmental value to the public, for example, of damage associated with
\par
disasters such as the Exxon Valdez Alaska oil spill. Our account shows that
\par
the constructive approach is built into the mathematical foundations. The
\par
construction may involve a single decision-maker or a politically active group.
\par
In the former case 'cognitive' issues may be important, but there is no reason
\par
that political or social-psychological factors should not play roles as well, and
\par
in the latter case they likely will.
\par
There is then, no 'extraction' or 'encoding' as is often expressed; these are
\par
misleading mechanistic, information-theoretic metaphors, and are implicit
\par
attempts to control a process that is open-ended and collaborative. This
\par
philosophical perspective is consistent with what is being discovered by psy-
\par
chologists as requirements for meaningful assessments. There is also no
\par
reason to side with the inquisitor who believes that 'experts' should play the
\par
elicitation game as presented. Structurally, the interview process used to elicit
\par
expert judgments is better compared to psychoanalysis, with an attendant
\par
interest ha the assessment process as an evolving dialogue (Keeney, 1977,
\par
1982). From a communicative perspective, there are fundamental differences
\par
between an interview conducted as psychoanalytic dialogue, in which truth-
\par
claims are as much created as found, and one aimed at objectively 'extracting'
\par
pre-existing values otherwise distorted through eliminable biases, analogous
\par
to 'changing the perspective' on a visual scene. It is a fundamental and per-
\par
haps moral choice whether communication is construed as a meaning-laden
\par
conversation, or the adjustment of a mechanism. It is also the former, not the
\par
latter, which has logical priority.
\par
Let's finally return then to Frank Ramsey and the origins of decision theory,
\par
for the wrong choice occurring there, and leading, I believe, to wayward
\par
opportunities for subjective-objective probability. Ramsey's choice for his
\par
time and place was unavoidable. His theory of language was to 'assume here
\par
Wittgenstein's theory of propositions "35 meaning, roughly, that states of the
\par
world, and preferences among them, may be thought of in terms of idealized
\par
descriptions contained in some unspecified logical calculus. This calculus is
\par
thought to be a fairly pure 'picture' of the world, or a kind of logical isomor-
\par
phism, while its adequacy as an empirical representation of preferences, and
\par
even some of its logical shortcomings, such as indifference versus indecision,
\par
is largely left unquestioned. Ramsey, for all his genius, was perhaps too early
\par
in the evolution of mathematical logic to have a less speculative understand-
\par
ing of his own theory. The difference between what Ramsey assumed, and
\par
what later analysts and cognitive researchers have shown is needed for deci-20
\par
sion analysis practice, is the difference between a static, abstracted view of
\par
language, and a dynamic, constructive view of language, which recognizes that
\par
the objectivity of expression evolves through a temporal dialogue. It is the dif-
\par
ference between logical positivism and a kind of austere ethnography, or the
\par
difference between the early Wittgenstein of the \emph{Tractatus, }which Ramsey
\par
knew well, and the late Wittgenstein of the \emph{Philosophical Investigations, }which
\par
Ramsey would never know having died so young. The contrast may also be
\par
conceived in terms of a view of language as almost a dispensable medium of
\par
expression for mathematized propositions, versus a process requiting the
\par
interaction of individuals developing rules for understanding the world as
\par
they make their way in it. The former philosophy at times also becomes,
\par
through interpretations of 'assessment,' a means of taming the latter. It is
\par
remarkable then that from one of the most formalist branches of modem
\par
philosophy, a radically anti-formalist conclusion should ensue: that making a
\par
formalism itself work requires a constructive, temporal, and social process. It
\par
is not recognizing \emph{that }which leads to mistaken opportunities of control,
\par
extraction, and inqnisition.
\par
\textbf{V. Taming chance}
\par
By the taming of chance 36 I mean the use of probabilistic or statistical
\par
methods to represent uncertainty when occurring largely to the exclusion of
\par
unquantified, less structured, and less codified methods which may only be
\par
implicit in human behavior or scientific practice; typically within some exten-
\par
sive institutional context, such as that of a federal regulatory agency; and
\par
involving detailed procedures for defining and elaborating facts or categories
\par
associated with an uncertain science of pollution or dangers of concern. As
\par
such, the risk representations involving the quantification of uncertainty pro-
\par
vide important means, for better or worse, of environmental control. The
\par
taming of chance is one among many intellectual technologies 37 guiding the
\par
control of society and nature through the processing of uncertainty and inter-
\par
pretations of danger.
\par
Many risk debates are primarily value-driven, in the sense that what we
\par
choose to do, for example concerning the restoration of contaminated haz-
\par
ardous waste sites, is going to depend primarily on our willingness to pay for
\par
certain levels of environmental restoration: the costs are high, and are they
\par
worth it? Uncertainty, along with value, is also at the conceptual center of
\par
risk, and the taming of chance is near the center of risk debates which focus
\par
on uncertainty. When the focus of debate moves to uncertainty, then, the topic
\par
has become that of \emph{control. }From an engineering perspective this should be
\par
clear, as uncertainty and control essentially complement one another. High
\par
uncertainty results from low information content in a message, and both
\par
imply high entropy and lack of control; conversely, when control is possible,
\par
uncertainty, of certain kinds, is reduced. The information-theoretic analogy is21
\par
only metaphorical for most environmental risks, but the relationship between
\par
control and chance is largely the same: we tame chance in order to gain con-
\par
trol.
\par
In this essay, we have explored some aspects of the translation process
\par
between unquantified risk and its quantified representatives. The examples of
\par
unquantified scientific uncertainty in Section 3 demonstrate that problems in
\par
this translation process lead to the conclusion that either: (i) risk analysis has
\par
no use for much of what is known as 'science' because the ways in which
\par
uncertainty is interpreted resist significant quantification; or (ii) risk analysis
\par
has to work within more flexible and less controlled boundaries for science in
\par
providing advice to policy-makers. Option (i) is clearly a mistake, and is not
\par
what often occurs in practice anyway. But a belief against (ii) is canonized in
\par
the narrow definition of quantified risk. I suggest this belief results from a mis-
\par
taken seientistic application of probabilistic methods to science itself, and is a
\par
way of creating more control than may be delivered by scientific knowledge
\par
alone. 3s A fear, I suspect, lurking behind acceptance of option (ii) is the
\par
bogey-man of 'constructive' science. The idea that scientific methodology
\par
itself is in flux, that rules are often made up as we go along, is anathema to late
\par
children of logical positivism. It is somehow believed that the historical les-
\par
sons of contemporary philosophy of science - respected as it often is by prac-
\par
ticing mathematicians and scientists - will lead to some kind of degrading
\par
anarchy. But when you look closely enough at any human activity, intellectual
\par
or otherwise, there's a good deal of anarchy \emph{in the sense that }rules for the ac-
\par
tivity get made up, normalized, criticized, and changed all the time. That is,
\par
it's a constructive process, with no implications one way or the other regard-
\par
ing solipsism, anti-materialism, or vulgar relativism.
\par
Which brings us to our ending point in Section 4 on intersubjective prob-
\par
ability and its constructive assessment. Here too, there is a dependency
\par
between the representation of uncertainty and control: that is, the process
\par
must appear to be non-constructive in order to maintain or leverage certain
\par
types of institutional control. Consider, for comparison, the idea that natural
\par
resource values may, with sufficient methodological skill applied to surveys
\par
and data analysis, be more or less neutrally assessed or 'extracted'; this being
\par
the presumption behind most economic valuations of environmental damage,
\par
including those used in courts. While it is often acknowledged that this valua-
\par
tion is problematic, its behavioral rationale is rarely raised. But there has been
\par
a debate, now proceeding for some years, between behavioral researchers and
\par
economists, the former arguing, largely without rebuttal, that judgments of
\par
environmental value (e.g., of the damage incurred by the Exxon Valdez) are
\par
driven by constructive processes (Fischhoff and Furby, 1988; Fischhoff,
\par
1991). 39 The implicit threat here to control is that the courts and economists
\par
are not in a position to assess the true value of damaged resources. Construc-
\par
tive processes in probabifity assessments, as occur in expert judgment of
\par
uncertainty, similarly makes apparent the taming of chance and limits certain
\par
types of control over environmental risks.22
\par
\textbf{What all this expresses, I believe, are some of the ways in which we \emph{cannot}}
\par
\textbf{indeed tame chance or control uncertainty to the extent we, as a society,}
\par
\textbf{would like. In a sense, that's why the big risks we face, such as global warming}
\par
\textbf{or species extinctions, \emph{are }risks: they are really not yet under control. The}
\par
\textbf{examples of this essay show that, in spite of the undoubted improvements}
\par
\textbf{brought to policy through the risk concept, we have also reached some limits}
\par
\textbf{of usefulness for quantifying uncertainty, and these limits are serving to but-}
\par
\textbf{tress outmoded ideologies of knowledge, representation, quantification, and}
\par
\textbf{judgment. Overcoming these limits means making policy more 'scientific' by}
\par
\textbf{directly participating in scientific debates \emph{without }inappropriate proxies for}
\par
\textbf{uncertainty. It also means engaging in policy making by fully accepting the}
\par
\textbf{constructive, participatory, ultimately open-ended and untamed nature of}
\par
\textbf{judgments under uncertainty.}
