\textbf{I. INTRODUCTION}
\par
\textbf{C\_ONCERNS for the accuracy of adjudication permeate analyses of proce-}
\par
\textbf{dural rules and aspects of substantive law. Yet the value of more accurate}
\par
\textbf{adjudication is largely taken for granted. When this is done, however,}
\par
\textbf{there is no basis for choosing among rules (or for judges to make discre-}
\par
\textbf{tionary judgments when applying them), for it typically is the case that}
\par
\textbf{greater accuracy comes at a cost. Even if precise quantification of various}
\par
\textbf{benefits of accuracy is impossible, decision-making will be enhanced by}
\par
\textbf{understanding why accuracy may be desirable.}
\par
\textbf{This article presents an economic analysis of the value of accuracy in}
\par
\textbf{adjudication. It focuses primarily on three considerations: the effect of}
\par
\textbf{inaccuracy on implementation of substantive legal norms (individuals'}
\par
\textbf{incentives to comply with the law and proper assignment of future rights}
\par
\textbf{and obligations), the administrative costs of inaccuracy and attempts to}
\par
\textbf{reduce it, and costs arising from the imposition of sanctions.}
\par
\textbf{Accuracy is a central concern with regard to a wide range of legal}
\par
\textbf{rules. One might go so far as to say that a large portion of the rules of}
