\par
\textbf{mation concerning damages in adjudication are socially excessive or inad-}
\par
\textbf{equate. Part III addresses accuracy in determining liability. The context exam-}
\par
\textbf{ined is one in which there may be errors: individuals who truly committed}
\par
\textbf{acts causing harm-referred to as the "guilty" for convenience3-may}
\par
\textbf{mistakenly be exonerated (false negatives), and those who actually did}
\par
\textbf{not commit harmful acts-the "innocent"-may mistakenly be sanc-}
\par
\textbf{tioned (false positives). Such errors reduce the law's deterrent effect.}
\par
\textbf{The existence of false negatives means that those who commit harmful}
\par
\textbf{acts are less likely to be sanctioned, while false positives increase ex-}
\par
\textbf{pected sanctions for innocent behavior. Both effects reduce the disincen-}
\par
\textbf{tive to commit harmful acts.}
\par
\textbf{Thus, one benefit of accuracy is that it is a means of increasing deter-}
\par
\textbf{rence. The analysis considers what combination of expenditures to in-}
\par
\textbf{crease accuracy and other methods-raising enforcement effort and in-}
\par
\textbf{creasing sanctions-enhances deterrence in the least costly manner. In}
\par
\textbf{addition, the analysis demonstrates that greater reliance on accuracy}
\par
\textbf{allows a given level of deterrence to be achieved while imposing sanctions}
\par
\textbf{less often, which is beneficial whenever sanctions themselves are socially}
\par
\textbf{costly (as with imprisonment). The analysis also considers the relation-}
\par
\textbf{ship between accuracy, the level of sanctions, and concerns for sanc-}
\par
\textbf{tioning the innocent. The main result is that, when error is greater, an}
\par
\textbf{enforcement policy that relies on higher rather than lower sanctions may}
\par
\textbf{be appropriate.4 Other topics addressed in Part III are how the burden}
\par
\textbf{of proof should be incorporated into the article's analysis and whether}
\par
\textbf{individuals' incentives to present information in adjudication are socially}
\par
\textbf{appropriate.}
\par
\textbf{In Part IV, the analysis shifts to situations in which the purpose of}
\par
\textbf{adjudication is to determine either future behavior (such as in licensing}
\par
\textbf{procedures) or eligibility for future public benefit payments. Because the}
\par
\textbf{analysis of Parts II and III is concerned with incentives for ex ante behav-}
\par
\textbf{ior, it is largely inapplicable in this setting. (When future rights and obliga-}
\par
\textbf{tions are determined as a consequence of prior behavior, both the analy-}
\par
\textbf{sis of Part IV and that of the prior parts will be relevant.) Determining}
\par
