\par
\textbf{with implicit changes in the burden of proof. This part also indicates}
\par
\textbf{how incentives for individuals to pursue their claims tend to be socially}
\par
\textbf{excessive.}
\par
\textbf{Part V examines concerns that fall outside a conventional economic}
\par
\textbf{analysis, particularly those related to the fairness of adjudication. The}
\par
\textbf{discussion suggests that some such concerns, notably the problem of}
\par
\textbf{mistakenly sanctioning the innocent (whether imprisoning the innocent}
\par
\textbf{or requiring individuals not truly liable to make payments to plaintiffs),}
\par
\textbf{are already reflected to a substantial extent in the economic analysis. It}
\par
\textbf{is explained that, if one's concern about mistakenly imposed sanctions}
\par
\textbf{were greater than the economic analysis credits, the implications for legal}
\par
\textbf{rules are not always what they appear to be. In addition, Part V addresses}
\par
\textbf{the extent to which process values have significance independent of ef-}
\par
\textbf{fects on results (as through improving accuracy by providing more infor-}
\par
\textbf{mation to decision-makers). The discussion in this part is incomplete,}
\par
\textbf{in part by design, as the focus of this article is on economic effects,}
\par
\textbf{and in part by necessity, as frequently invoked concerns about fair-}
\par
\textbf{ness are rarely sufficiently specified to allow them to be applied and}
\par
\textbf{assessed.}
\par
\textbf{II. ACCURACY IN THE ASSESSMENT OF DAMAGES}
\par
\textbf{This part considers efforts to increase accuracy in specifying damages}
\par
\textbf{to be awarded in adjudication. The analysis in Section A emphasizes the}
\par
\textbf{relationship between the degree of accuracy that is anticipated in ex post}
\par
\textbf{adjudication and how individuals choose to behave ex ante, in light of}
\par
\textbf{the sanctions they expect to be applied to their conduct. It takes into}
\par
\textbf{account how the accuracy of adjudication influences individuals' incen-}
\par
\textbf{tives to become better informed before they act. In Section B, the analy-}
\par
\textbf{sis is applied to various legal settings. Section C extends the discussion to}
\par
\textbf{address parties' incentives to present information in adjudication (thereby}
\par
\textbf{increasing accuracy).312 THE JOURNAL OF LEGAL STUDIES}
