\textbf{2. The Degree of Ex Post Accuracy and Individuals' Incentives}
\par
\textbf{to Acquire Information Ex Ante}
\par
\textbf{a. How Accuracy Ex Post Induces Individuals to Acquire Information}
\par
\textbf{Ex Ante. The analysis in Subsection 1 assumes that individuals, at the}
\par
\textbf{time they decide how to act, either are or are not informed about an}
\par
\textbf{aspect of the harm they might cause. Often, however, the extent to which}
\par
\textbf{individuals are informed will be a matter of choice. For example, one}
\par
\textbf{who contemplates using dynamite may not know very precisely how dan-}
\par
\textbf{gerous it is but may be able to consult experts who are more familiar}
\par
\textbf{with the extent of the danger from various uses. The more individuals}
\par
\textbf{are willing to spend, the more information they can acquire.'9}
\par
\textbf{The central point of this subsection is that the extent to which individu-}
\par
\textbf{als will choose to become more informed ex ante, when contemplating}
\par
\textbf{how to act, will depend on the degree of accuracy they expect in adjudica-}
\par
\textbf{tion, ex post. An individual will see no value in making an expenditure}
\par
\textbf{to learn whether her act will cause harm of 5, 10, or 15 if she knows that}
\par
\textbf{a court will award 10 in any event.20 Only if she anticipates that the court}
\par
\textbf{will learn the level of harm more precisely, and make damages reflect the}
\par
\textbf{actual harm, will she have an incentive to learn the actual level of harm}
\par
\textbf{ex ante.}
\par
\textbf{18 Relatedly, if the driver knew in advance that substantial harm would be caused, she}
\par
\textbf{would have been more careful. And if she knew that reckless driving in her lifetime would}
\par
\textbf{in fact produce trivial harm-that is, if she knew in advance that she would be extremely}
\par
\textbf{lucky-she would have been less careful. The point is that such differences in outcomes}
\par
\textbf{are often unknown in advance, in which case they do not affect behavior.}
\par
\textbf{19 One can think of the two cases in Subsection 1 as representing extremes with regard}
\par
\textbf{to ex ante information costs. The case in which individuals were assumed to be informed}
\par
\textbf{is similar to one in which information is extremely cheap, while the one in which they are}
\par
\textbf{not informed might be one in which information is prohibitively expensive. (In many in-}
\par
\textbf{stances, of course, predicting future events with high precision may be virtually impossible;}
\par
\textbf{one can think of the information cost as infinite, in which case individuals would never}
\par
\textbf{acquire the information in any event.)}
\par
\textbf{20 If the actor and potential victim are in a contractual relationship, there may be an}
\par
\textbf{incentive with regard to negotiation of price and adjusting behavior. See note 41 infra}
\par
\textbf{(noting the Hadley v. Baxendale problem).ACCURACY IN ADJUDICATION 317}
\par
\textbf{Whether individuals ultimately acquire the information is another mat-}
\par
\textbf{ter. Individuals will acquire information when the benefits of being in-}
\par
\textbf{formed exceed the costs of the information. The benefit of being informed}
\par
\textbf{is that one can better adjust one's behavior in light of actual legal conse-}
\par
\textbf{quences. For example, if there is a probability that one will learn that}
\par
\textbf{actual harm, and thus expected liability, is very high, and one would}
\par
\textbf{choose not to act in that instance, then the benefit of information would}
\par
\textbf{be the difference between the net value of not acting and that of acting}
\par
\textbf{in such an instance, weighted by the probability one expects to learn that}
\par
\textbf{actual harm is very high.21 Similarly, one might learn that harm is lower}
\par
\textbf{than anticipated and in that instance commit an act that one otherwise}
\par
\textbf{would have refrained from doing.}
\par
\textbf{To illustrate, suppose that the use of a toxic substance will cause harm}
\par
\textbf{of 5, 10, or 15, each with equal probability, and that the user does not}
\par
\textbf{know the actual harm that would be caused. If the benefit from using the}
\par
\textbf{substance is 12, it would be used in the absence of information because}
\par
\textbf{the expected liability is 10. If one acquired information on actual harm,}
\par
\textbf{the substance would not be used in the event that harm was 15.22 This}
\par
\textbf{produces a gain of 3-liability of 15 is avoided while the benefit of 12 is}
\par
\textbf{forgone. Because the probability of this outcome is 1/3, the expected}
\par
\textbf{value of information is 1.23 Thus, information would be acquired if and}
\par
\textbf{only if the cost of the information were less than 1.}
\par
\textbf{Information ex ante is valuable only if what is learned will be reflected}
\par
\textbf{in awards ex post. Similarly, as noted in Subsection 1, accuracy ex post}
\par
\textbf{is valuable only if individuals also have accurate information ex ante;}
\par
\textbf{that statement can now be interpreted to include both the case in which}
\par
\textbf{individuals already have the necessary information and that in which they}
\par
\textbf{will be induced to discover it. And individuals will be induced to learn}
\par
\textbf{information ex ante only if their benefits from adjusting behavior exceed}
\par
\textbf{the cost of the information.}
\par
\textbf{21 The value of not acting can be taken to equal zero as a reference point. The value of}
\par
\textbf{acting would be the benefit of the act minus the expected sanction; in this instance, the}
\par
\textbf{value is negative. (If the difference were positive, it would be desirable to act even if one}
\par
\textbf{learned that harm was very high. Then the individual would have had no reason to acquire}
\par
\textbf{the information.)}
\par
