\par
\textbf{b. The Social Value of Accuracy Ex Post. Having established when}
\par
\textbf{accuracy is valuable, it remains necessary to consider whether the value}
\par
\textbf{is worth its cost. In Subsection lb, when individuals simply were as-}
\par
\textbf{sumed to be informed, accuracy ex post was desirable when the benefit of}
\par
\textbf{the improvement in behavior was greater than the cost of more accurate}
\par
\textbf{adjudication ex post. In the present context, the benefit with regard to}
\par
\textbf{behavior must exceed the sum of the cost of accuracy ex post and the}
\par
\textbf{cost of individuals' becoming informed ex ante.24}
\par
\textbf{c. Whether Individuals' Incentives to Acquire Information Ex Ante Are}
\par
\textbf{Excessive or Inadequate.25 It is noted in Subsection a that individuals}
\par
\textbf{acquire information when the anticipated benefits to themselves exceed}
\par
\textbf{its cost. This subsection explores whether, ex ante, individuals will tend}
\par
\textbf{to acquire more or less information than is socially appropriate (taking}
\par
\textbf{as given that adjudication will involve the expenditure of resources to}
\par
\textbf{reach accurate results ex post).}
\par
\textbf{First, consider a benchmark case. Assume that liability is strict, the}
\par
\textbf{only uncertainty concerns damages, and individuals properly estimate}
\par
\textbf{the value of information to themselves.26 Then, if the information con-}
\par
\textbf{cerns how a court will properly assess harm, individuals' incentive to}
\par
\textbf{acquire information will be socially appropriate.}
\par
\textbf{The reason incentives are proper in this simple context relates to famil-}
\par
\textbf{iar properties of a rule of strict liability.27 When individuals acquire in-}
\par
\textbf{formation about the true level of harm, and expected damages equal}
\par
\textbf{expected true harm, they will be induced to behave appropriately. Re-}
\par
\textbf{latedly, the private benefit from their change in behavior will equal the}
\par
\textbf{social benefit. (The private benefit will consist of gains or losses from}
\par
\textbf{modifying one's activity, which are assumed to be social gains or losses}
\par
\textbf{as well. Changes in expected liability payments will equal changes in}
\par
\textbf{expected harm, which is a correct measure of the external social cost of}
\par
\textbf{one's activity.) Finally, individuals' private cost of information involves}
\par
\textbf{real expenditures of resources (usually, time or expenditures to purchase}
\par
\textbf{experts' services), which will thus be a proper measure of the social}
\par
\textbf{cost of information. Therefore, when individuals compare their private}
