\textbf{3. Compensatory Objectives and Risk Aversion}
\par
\textbf{When parties are risk-averse-that is, when uncertain losses are more}
\par
\textbf{detrimental than a certain loss with the same expected value-the level}
\par
\textbf{of actual damage awards will affect their welfare directly, in addition to}
\par
\textbf{affecting their behavior.30 To illustrate this, recall the example in which}
\par
\textbf{actual harm may be 5, 10, or 15, each with equal probability.}
\par
\textbf{Consider first the effect with regard to injurers (defendants), who have}
\par
\textbf{been the focus of the analysis thus far. In the scenario examined here,}
\par
\textbf{inaccuracy corresponds to making defendants pay the average harm}
\par
\textbf{caused by their type of act (10), while accuracy involves each defendant}
\par
\textbf{paying the actual harm caused by her particular act (5, 10, or 15). By}
\par
\textbf{construction, the expected payment (the mean) is the same in both in-}
\par
\textbf{stances (10). But the variance differs: there is no variance if the average}
\par
\textbf{harm is paid by all injurers, while there is positive variance if each injurer}
\par
\textbf{pays the actual harm she causes (the standard deviation is approximately}
\par
\textbf{4.1).31 As a result, greater accuracy in adjudication increases the risk to}
\par
\textbf{which defendants are exposed. On this account, accuracy is less desirable}
\par
\textbf{than otherwise.32 Thus, when individuals are uninformed ex ante, so that}
\par
\textbf{there is no behavioral benefit, accuracy would be undesirable even if the}
\par
\textbf{greater accuracy were free!33}
\par
\textbf{For plaintiffs, the effect would tend to be the opposite. Assume that}
\par
\textbf{plaintiffs' damages compensate for pecuniary losses (or nonpecuniary}
\par
\textbf{losses, such as physical injuries, that are fully restored by expenditures}
\par
\textbf{of money). Then, if they are risk-averse, the optimal amount of compen-}
\par
\textbf{30 The possibility that one may care about accurate compensation independent of con-}
\par
\textbf{cerns for imposing risk is considered in Subsection VA1.}
\par
\textbf{31 More generally, there is positive variance and thus risk in both instances if an injurer's}
\par
\textbf{act causes harm only with a probability, for then when no harm arises, the payment is zero.}
\par
\textbf{Nonetheless, the variance is greater when, in instances where harm occurs, individuals pay}
\par
\textbf{for actual harm rather than the average harm for such instances.}
\par
\textbf{32 A complete analysis would be more complicated because, when defendants are risk-}
\par
