\textbf{C. Parties' Incentives to Present Information in Adjudication}
\par
\textbf{The discussion in Sections A and B largely assumes that the legal sys-}
\par
\textbf{tem-those who promulgate rules or judges who apply them-is able to}
\par
\textbf{choose the degree of accuracy in adjudication. In adversary adjudication,}
\par
\textbf{however, this choice is made largely through a scheme of indirect con-}
\par
\textbf{trols. Legislators and judges do not determine which witnesses will testify}
\par
\textbf{at trial. Rather, they establish a system in which private litigants present}
\par
\textbf{whatever information they believe will further their cause. The quantity}
\par
\textbf{and type of information a fact-finder ultimately considers is thus a product}
\par
\textbf{of procedural and substantive law and the parties' choices. This section}
\par
\textbf{considers whether parties' incentives to present information tend to be}
\par
\textbf{socially appropriate, excessive, or inadequate.86}
\par
\textbf{This question has an important bearing on the design of the legal sys-}
\par
\textbf{tem. If parties' incentives tend to be socially appropriate, there would}
\par
\textbf{be no need to worry greatly about too much or too little accuracy. One}
\par
\textbf{could leave it to the litigants, who would produce the correct level.87 If}
\par
\textbf{has multiple causes, statistical evidence may be the only reliable evidence available in any}
\par
\textbf{event. Relatedly, in statistical studies with large samples, a fairly high error rate in each}
\par
\textbf{observation may be unimportant as long as errors in measurement are unbiased because}
\par
\textbf{errors will tend to cancel. Adjudication of an individuals' right to recover is not usually}
\par
\textbf{conducted under the assumption that error is permissible because it evens out across cases.}
\par
\textbf{See Section C infra.}
