\par
\textbf{harm.89}
\par
\textbf{When will a plaintiff or defendant offer evidence of actual harm? For}
\par
\textbf{concreteness, assume that average harm is 150. Furthermore, assume}
\par
\textbf{that, after the accident, each of the parties learns of the actual harm and}
\par
\textbf{can prove it in adjudication at a cost of 20. Then, whenever actual harm}
\par
\textbf{is less than 130, the defendant will spend 20 to prove actual harm; when-}
\par
\textbf{ever actual harm exceeds 170, the plaintiff will spend 20 to prove harm;}
\par
\textbf{and whenever actual harm is between 130 and 170, neither party will}
\par
\textbf{prove harm, so damages will be 150. (The reasoning is straightforward:}
\par
\textbf{if actual harm were 125, the defendant would rather pay 125 in damages}
\par
\textbf{plus 20 in litigation costs than pay 150 in damages. If actual harm were}
\par
\textbf{175, the plaintiff would rather collect this amount, spending 20 to prove}
\par
\textbf{it-for a net of 155-than collect damages of 150. If actual harm is near}
\par
\textbf{150, neither party gains enough by proving that actual harm differs from}
\par
\textbf{150 to justify the cost of 20.)}
