\textbf{3. Abuse of Power}
\par
\textbf{Additional procedural safeguards are advocated-sometimes implic-}
\par
\textbf{itly-on the ground that they make abuse of power more difficult.268}
\par
\textbf{Thus, for example, if decision-makers are required to state reasons for}
\par
\textbf{their decisions, it may be easier to detect inadequate effort or impermissi-}
\par
\textbf{ble grounds for decisions.269 The benefits of enhanced detection are two-}
\par
\textbf{fold: decision-makers have a greater incentive to behave properly in the}
\par
\textbf{first place if their misbehavior is more likely to be observed; and misbe-}
\par
\textbf{havior that nonetheless occurs may be easier to correct, through appeals}
\par
\textbf{of individual cases, removal of decision-makers, or reforms of the pro-}
\par
\textbf{cess.270 Concerns such as these directly involve problems of accuracy.}
\par
\textbf{When decisions are made haphazardly or on criteria other than those}
\par
\textbf{deemed relevant, more errors will occur.}
\par
\textbf{There is a further connection between accuracy and the abuse of}
\par
\textbf{power. If a system must be structured so that it provides accurate out-}
\par
\textbf{comes, it will tend to be more difficult to manipulate, as by prosecuting}
\par
\textbf{individuals based on fabricated charges. An abusive government may}
\par
\textbf{have the alternative of enacting substantive rules making illegal the be-}
\par
\textbf{havior it finds threatening. Some such enactments are directly regulated}
\par
\textbf{by constitutional provisions, such as the First Amendment, the prohibi-}
\par
\textbf{tions on ex post facto laws and bills of attainder, and limits on the govern-}
\par
\textbf{ment's power to take property. In addition, enactment of offensive sub-}
\par
\textbf{stantive law may be self-limiting because it will be more difficult for a}
\par
\textbf{government to hide the purposes of its actions. Thus, it may be no acci-}
\par
\textbf{dent that so many constitutional protections concerned with controlling}
\par
\textbf{government power involve adjudication and often concern its ac-}
\par
\textbf{curacy.271}
\par
\textbf{268 Although the relationship between procedural protections and abuse of power is not}
\par
\textbf{usually addressed in depth, it is common for advocates of intrinsic process values to offer}
\par
\textbf{references to fears of abuse of power in support. See, for example, Subrin \& Dykstra,}
\par
\textbf{supra note 257, at 456-57, 458-59; Tribe, supra note 203, at 666-67, 718.}
\par
\textbf{269 The problem of effort is a general problem of incentives that arises when agents must}
\par
\textbf{be relied on to implement mandates specified by others-in this context, by the legislature}
\par
\textbf{or executive officials. A more particular worry is that decisions will not merely be occasion-}
\par
\textbf{ally erroneous due to insufficient effort but rather will be infected by prejudice.}
\par
\textbf{270 Redish and Marshall distinguish intrinsic arguments concerning the benefits of reveal-}
\par
\textbf{ing reasons for official decisions: "An official's revelation that he has acted arbitrarily in}
\par
\textbf{dealing with an individual, though perhaps helpful in certain senses to the individual, does}
\par
\textbf{not, in and of itself, treat that individual with dignity. As long as the individual can do}
\par
\textbf{nothing to alter the outcome, the revelation does not alter the morally unacceptable fact of}
\par
\textbf{arbitrary governmental treatment." Redish \& Marshall, supra note 246, at 490.}
\par
\textbf{271 Of course, some constitutional procedural protections may interfere with accuracy,}
\par
\textbf{as when credible evidence is excluded on account of Fourth Amendment violations or when398 THE JOURNAL OF LEGAL STUDIES}
\par
\textbf{Concerns about abuse of power may, however, raise independent is-}
\par
\textbf{sues. First, one may be more concerned with errors that arise, say, due to}
\par
\textbf{racial prejudice than as a result of inevitable imperfection in fact-finding.}
\par
\textbf{Second, problems involving abuse of power may involve a more complex}
\par
\textbf{dynamic. A procedure-say, delegating a decision entirely to an expert-}
\par
\textbf{may normally be both cheap and accurate but, in the wrong hands or in}
\par
\textbf{tense times, may go awry.272 These factors may affect an assessment of}
\par
\textbf{how valuable accuracy is in a given context or the determination of how}
\par
\textbf{accurate various procedures would be in practice,273 and also complicate}
\par
\textbf{the economic analysis.274}
\par
\textbf{Finally, it is useful to observe that concerns for abuse of power may}
\par
\textbf{fit more comfortably than others with the legal context in which disputes}
\par
\textbf{over accuracy have arisen-constitutional challenges concerning proce-}
\par
\textbf{dural due process.275 Some commentators have remarked that a cost-}
\par
\textbf{benefit calculus276 such as that employed in Mathews v. Eldridge is not}
\par
\textbf{the Confrontation Clause leads to excluding the only available evidence of a crime. See,}
\par
\textbf{for example, Smith v. Murray, 477 U.S. 527, 544-46 (1986) (Stevens, J., dissenting). This}
\par
\textbf{suggests that maximizing accuracy is not in all instances the best way to control abuse.}
\par
\textbf{See note 138 supra. The point here is merely that it is often useful toward that end. More-}
\par
\textbf{over, when a procedure that reduces accuracy in a given case is adopted to deter abuse, it}
\par
\textbf{may be that overall accuracy is enhanced in the process. (Nonetheless, some procedures}
\par
\textbf{are advocated on grounds of fairness or dignity that are not claimed to be instrumentally}
\par
\textbf{motivated.)}
\par
\textbf{272 As another example, if a fact-finder is allowed to deviate from scheduled damages to}
\par
\textbf{fine-tune its award, this added discretion may provide more room for abuse.}
\par
\textbf{273 Analyzing the latter requires a positive analysis of government institutions, which}
\par
\textbf{differs entirely from the sort of analysis presented here.}
\par
\textbf{274 If inaccuracy is due to prejudice rather than adjudicators' inability to produce accurate}
\par
\textbf{assessments even while making the best possible inferences from incomplete information,}
\par
\textbf{inaccurate awards would be biased, rather than being correct on average (as was assumed}
\par
\textbf{in much of the analysis in Part II). It is desirable to eliminate systematic bias-whether}
\par
\textbf{produced intentionally or through misunderstanding-regardless of whether actors ex ante}
\par
\textbf{have precise information about the actual harm they will cause. (Those favored by antici-}
\par
\textbf{pated error will be underdeterred, and those disfavored will be overdeterred.) This situation}
\par
\textbf{is like the case analyzed in Subsection IIA4, if one assumes that actors anticipate the error}
\par
\textbf{ex ante. See also Subsections IIAlc and IIB3 and note 9 supra.}
\par
\textbf{275 One also might defend constitutional review of procedural protections on separation of}
\par
\textbf{powers grounds. Thus, if process is insufficient, an agency may be seen as not implementing}
\par
\textbf{Congress' commands. Such an approach would have much in common with the positivist}
\par
\textbf{one suggested in many of the Court's opinions, see, for example, Board of Regents v. Roth,}
\par
\textbf{408 U.S. 564 (1972), and taken to its most extreme form in Justice Rehnquist's plurality}
\par
\textbf{opinion in Arnett v. Kennedy, 416 U.S. 134 (1974) (defining property rights as limited by}
\par
\textbf{the procedure provided), a position rejected by the Court in Cleveland Board of Education}
\par
\textbf{v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532 (1985).}
\par
\textbf{276 It also is not clear that an analysis emphasizing participation, dignity, or other fre-ACCURACY IN ADJUDICATION 399}
\par
\textbf{obviously suited to implementation by appellate courts rather than by}
\par
\textbf{legislative bodies or specialized agencies.277 Moreover, the Due Process}
\par
\textbf{Clause is a component of the Bill of Rights, much of which is designed}
\par
\textbf{to protect individuals against potential government abuse.278 Thus, it is}
\par
\textbf{not necessarily appropriate for the courts' tests of the constitutionality of}
\par
\textbf{procedures to mirror an economic analysis designed to determine which}
\par
\textbf{procedures are socially best.279}
\par
\textbf{VI. CONCLUSION}
\par
\textbf{The economic analysis of accuracy in adjudication suggests that the}
\par
\textbf{effects and value of accuracy depend greatly on the legal setting. Accu-}
\par
\textbf{racy in the assessment of damages is relevant primarily because of how it}
\par
\textbf{affects the precision with which legal rules control behavior. But greater}
\par
\textbf{accuracy ex post, in adjudication, only improves ex ante behavior to the}
\par
\textbf{extent that individuals are knowledgeable, at the time they act, about the}
\par
\textbf{detail an adjudicator later will learn. An important complication is that}
\par
\textbf{the anticipation of greater accuracy in adjudication induces individuals}
\par
\textbf{to become more informed before they act. Greater accuracy is valuable}
\par
\textbf{if it results in a sufficient improvement in behavior to justify its cost in}
\par
\textbf{adjudication as well as the additional costs individuals incur to become}
\par
\textbf{informed about the legal consequences of contemplated acts. It is noted,}
\par
\textbf{however, that individuals' incentives to present information in adjudica-}
\par
\textbf{tion are not closely related to the social value of accuracy; in some in-}
\par
\textbf{stances, there are tendencies for these private incentives to be excessive.}
\par
\textbf{quently expressed concerns is more appropriate. One might believe that courts are experts}
\par
\textbf{in such matters as participation in adjudication, but presumably they are similarly expert}
\par
\textbf{in the accuracy of adjudication. A distinction could, of course, be maintained if "due}
\par
\textbf{process" is interpreted, as a matter of definition, to include concerns for participation but}
\par
\textbf{not concerns for accuracy.}
\par
\textbf{277 See, for example, Mashaw, supra note 183, at 1439, 1450-52; Tribe, supra note 203,}
\par
\textbf{at 718. There are also problems with designing procedures through piecemeal adjudication.}
\par
\textbf{See, for example, note 209 supra.}
\par
\textbf{278 See, for example, Wolff v. McDonnell, 418 U.S. 539, 558 (1974) ("[t]he touchstone}
\par
\textbf{of due process is protection of the individual against arbitrary action of government"); see}
\par
\textbf{Mashaw, supra note 195, at 898.}
\par
\textbf{279 It does not follow that courts should have no concern for an economic analysis or}
\par
\textbf{even that the inquiry into abuse of power is wholly distinct. Rather, it may be, for example,}
\par
\textbf{that courts should limit their attention to concerns for abuse of power and attempt to assure}
\par
\textbf{that procedures adopted are sufficient to avoid significant threats, to the extent that this is}
\par
\textbf{feasible. In some instances, this may involve requiring more or less procedural protection}
\par
\textbf{than would otherwise be prudent; in others it may involve imposing different sorts of}
\par
\textbf{protection.400 THE JOURNAL OF LEGAL STUDIES}
\par
\textbf{Accuracy in determining liability affects deterrence and the costs of}
\par
\textbf{imposing sanctions. With regard to the former, greater accuracy, along}
\par
\textbf{with increased enforcement effort and higher sanctions, is a way to en-}
\par
\textbf{hance deterrence. Increasing accuracy differs in that it allows a given}
\par
\textbf{level of deterrence to be achieved while imposing sanctions less often,}
\par
\textbf{which is an additional benefit when sanctions are socially costly. Increas-}
\par
\textbf{ing accuracy-which reduces false convictions of the innocent and mis-}
\par
\textbf{taken acquittals of the guilty-is distinguished from raising the burden}
\par
\textbf{of proof-which reduces false convictions but increases mistaken acquit-}
\par
\textbf{tals. The discussion emphasizes that accuracy is one of many instruments}
\par
\textbf{of the legal system, and the optimal degree of accuracy cannot be sepa-}
\par
\textbf{rated from how other aspects of the system are designed.}
\par
\textbf{When adjudication determines future rights and obligations, greater}
\par
\textbf{accuracy is valuable directly, as it determines the extent to which future}
\par
\textbf{actions conform to legal norms. (Accuracy may continue to have values}
\par
\textbf{similar to those in other contexts because the anticipation of pronounce-}
\par
\textbf{ments governing future circumstances will influence prior behavior.) The}
\par
\textbf{value of accuracy in this context-which receives the most attention in}
\par
\textbf{discussions of procedural due process with regard to government entitle-}
\par
\textbf{ments-is often misunderstood because of a failure to distinguish the}
\par
\textbf{purposes and effects of accuracy and of changes in the implicit burden}
\par
\textbf{of proof. While this investigation of accuracy is confined to economic consider-}
\par
\textbf{ations, it is observed that the economic analysis incorporates to some}
\par
\textbf{extent many other values thought to be implicated by accuracy, such as}
\par
\textbf{the fairness of over- or undercompensating accident victims or the cost}
\par
\textbf{of mistakenly sanctioning the innocent. Moreover, the discussion sug-}
\par
\textbf{gests that admitting other values may affect the analysis in surprising}
\par
\textbf{ways. For example, placing added weight on a concern for mistakenly}
\par
\textbf{sanctioning the innocent may favor a legal strategy that employs higher}
\par
\textbf{sanctions.}
\par
\textbf{This preliminary study of such a broad subject is inevitably incomplete.}
\par
\textbf{First, because the effects and value of accuracy depend greatly on the}
\par
\textbf{context, further investigation is necessary both to explore contexts not}
\par
\textbf{considered here and to study more carefully those that are examined in}
\par
\textbf{order to determine how generally the analysis applies. Second, the analy-}
\par
\textbf{sis can be applied in a concrete situation only after examining how partic-}
\par
\textbf{ular legal rules affect accuracy and how accuracy will affect individuals'}
\par
\textbf{behavior. Third, this article is confined to economic considerations-}
\par
\textbf{notably, the extent to which behavior and the granting of entitlements}
\par
\textbf{conforms to the law and the various public and private costs of adminis-ACCURACY IN ADJUDICATION 401}
\par
\textbf{tering the law. Some regard this perspective as including most or all of}
\par
\textbf{what is relevant,280 while others believe that significant weight should be}
\par
\textbf{accorded to additional concerns. It is to be hoped that the present inquiry}
\par
\textbf{will serve two purposes: to suggest that the effects and value of accuracy}
\par
\textbf{should not be taken almost entirely for granted, and to outline some}
\par
\textbf{important lines of analysis in a wide range of legal settings.}
\par
\textbf{280 One might think that Jeremy Bentham would be the prototype, although his views on}
