@article{kearneyUnderstandingGlobalChange1994,
  title = {Understanding Global Change: A Cognitive Perspective on Communicating through Stories},
  shorttitle = {Understanding Global Change},
  author = {Kearney, Anne R.},
  year = 1994,
  month = aug,
  journal = {Climatic Change},
  volume = {27},
  number = {4},
  pages = {NaN-NaN},
  issn = {0165-0009, 1573-1480},
  doi = {10.1007/BF01096270},
  urldate = {2024-05-03},
  copyright = {http://www.springer.com/tdm},
  langid = {english},
  note = {\textbf{U N D E R S T A N D I N G}
\par
\textbf{P E R S P E C T I V E S T O R I E S}
\par
\textbf{ON}
\par
\textbf{G L O B A L C H A N G E : C O M M U N I C A T I N G A C O G N I T I V E}
\par
\textbf{T H R O U G H}
\par
A N N E R. K E A R N E Y
\par
\emph{School of Natural Resources and Environment, The University of Michigan, 430 E. University,}
\par
\emph{Dana Bldg., Ann Arbor, M148109-1115, U.S.A.}
\par
Abstract. Human behavior must be changed in order to ameliorate the adverse
\par
effects of global change. However, numerous studies have shown that durable
\par
change is difficult to achieve unless people have an adequate understanding of
\par
the problem and of appropriate behavioral solutions. Creating such an under-
\par
standing has not been easy; the very nature of global change makes effective
\par
communication difficult. This paper proposes that one reason information has
\par
been unsuccessful is that it is not generally structured to take advantage of the
\par
way people process information. The cognitive processes involved in informa-
\par
tion acquisition and the special problems associated with communicating about
\par
global change are discussed. This discussion is used to identify those informa-
\par
tional characteristics that will facilitate the transfer of information about global
\par
change. Stories (also referred to as case-studies or analogies) are suggested as one
\par
structure that encapsulates these characteristics; the role of stories in information
\par
transfer is elaborated upon. Though written from the point of view of presenting
\par
information to the public so that it creates understanding and impacts behavior,
\par
the paper is also relevant for scientists who wish to effectively communicate their
\par
ideas to the media and to researchers in other disciplines.
\par
\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
Information aimed at encouraging conservation behavior and ameliorating envi-
\par
ronmental problems associated with global change has been largely ineffective. The
\par
typical informational approach to these issues that are highly abstract, happening
\par
on a huge scale, and can't be experienced directly has been to provide people with
\par
text that is highly abstract (e.g., providing scientific estimates of average expect-
\par
ed temperature changes due to global warming), huge in scale (e.g., the number
\par
of species lost yearly, the number of forested acres lost daily), and is not directly
\par
related to daily experience (e.g., widening gaps in the ozone layer). Newspaper arti-
\par
cles specializing in series of unrelated, vivid and concrete informational 'nuggets'
\par
(Graves \emph{et aL, }1988) and a doom and gloom tone also fall far short of creating an
\par
understanding of the problems and offering potential solutions. We must do better
\par
than this.
\par
There are two classes of solutions to increasing the effectiveness of text*: one
\par
is to modify the reader's information processing (e.g. by requiring them to use
\par
* Note that text is emphasized here because of its widespread use and relative inexpensiveness.
\par
The principles of effective information transfer, however, apply to other modes as well.
\par
\emph{Climatic Change }27: 419--441, 1994.
\par
(6) 1994 \emph{Kluwer Academic Publishers. Printed in the Netherlands.}420 Anne R. Kearney
\par
outlining strategies or to take a test) so that they can glean more from existing text;
\par
the other is to modify text to fit more closely with the reader's biases in information
\par
processing (Resnick, 1984). The first class of solutions is impractical. Applying
\par
specific strategies to learn from less than optimal text is attentionally intensive; in
\par
a world where demands on our time and attention come from all quarters, there is
\par
little incentive to work so hard at learning. The only viable solution is to create
\par
texts that take advantage of the reader's natural processes and thereby facilitate
\par
learning.
\par
A good story (case-study or analogy) is particularly compatible with the way
\par
people process information; it is one way to effectively communicate information
\par
about global change. Through stories, people can understand the meaning and scope
\par
of global changes and can gain suitable analogies for choosing appropriate actions
\par
(Halford and Sheehan, 1991). The potential of stories in communicating about
\par
global change and in promoting conservation behavior has largely been ignored;
\par
the urgency of these problems demands that it be explored.
\par
This paper draws on cognitive theory to explore those textual characteristics that
\par
increase the transfer of information. First, an overview is given of the important
\par
role of knowledge in decision making and behavior change. Special attention is
\par
given to the difficulties in acquiring knowledge about global change. Next, the
\par
cognitive processes involved in information acquisition are described and some
\par
textual characteristics that facilitate information processing are discussed. Finally,
\par
it is pointed out that given the unique characteristics of global change issues and the
\par
constraints of human information processing, particular informational formats will
\par
be most effective at transmitting information. Stories, or analogies, are suggested
\par
as one such format.
\par
\textbf{2. The Role of Information in Decision Making}
\par
Effective communication about issues of global change is important on several
\par
fronts. First, given the interdisciplinary nature of global change, it is critical that
\par
scientists from disparate disciplines are able to communicate with one another. Sec-
\par
ond, decision makers rely on information from the scientific community to guide
\par
their decisions about policy, strategies, and priorities in issues of global change;
\par
to make effective decisions, they must understand the issues. Third, informational
\par
programs are part of many strategies aimed at encouraging people to change their
\par
behavior in order to reduce global scale environmental degradation. (e.g., There
\par
are governmental informational programs directed at affecting the purchase deci-
\par
sions of consumers; this information includes energy-efficiency labeling of autos
\par
and energy auditing programs (Yates and Aronson, 1984). Other informational
\par
programs are directed at changing daily behaviors such as commuting mode.)
\par
Though few would argue against the importance of information, the problem of
\par
communicating - of transforming information to knowledge - is often disregarded.
\par
In many models of decision making, it is trivialized to the point that information
\par
Climatic Change August 1994\emph{Understanding Global Change }421
\par
is \emph{equated }with understanding (e.g., see Yohe, 1991). These models assume that
\par
information need only be presented and that the individual who receives it will
\par
receive it \emph{as presented. The }individual will then be able to combine the information
\par
with other information to arrive at some decision. In this model, the important
\par
informational variable is simply the amount of information the individual is given;
\par
the more information presented, the better the final decision will be. Such a model
\par
of knowledge acquisition and decision making is psychologically naive (Stem,
\par
1992).
\par
The acquisition of knowledge is not a straight-forward process. Because the
\par
human brain is a system with finite information processing resources operating in
\par
a world of infinite complexity, it is impossible to process and store everything.
\par
Humans are very selective about which stimuli they attend to and what informa-
\par
tion they remember. Information that is uninteresting, confusing, or seemingly-
\par
irrelevant will tend to be ignored altogether. It is also important to note that the
\par
information that does get processed by the brain is rarely stored as received. People
\par
bring their own knowledge, experiences, and biases to every new bit of informa-
\par
tion. Because of this, information is lost, warped, or even added during processing
\par
(Bartlett, 1932). This selectivity is one reason that the mere provision of infor-
\par
mation (e.g. pamphlets, slogans, instructions, uninspiring newspaper articles) has
\par
typically been ineffective at changing behavior (Ester and Winett, 1982; Stern and
\par
Aronson, 1984; Dennis \emph{et al., }1990). With information, what matters is not only
\par
how much is presented, but how it is conveyed (Stem, 1992).
\par
Assuming the information is appropriately stored in the brain, it can then enter
\par
into the decision making process. Again, this process is not as straight-forward as it
\par
may appear. Specific bits of information are not the only components of the decision
\par
making process; the \emph{state }of an individual's knowledge about the topic in question
\par
also plays an important role (Simon, 1992; see Kaplan, 1991, for a thorough
\par
discussion on this position). During decision making, an automatic assessment of
\par
how much knowledge an individual has and how solid that knowledge seems is
\par
made. This assessment is then combined with that person's actual knowledge about
\par
the issue, resulting in a decision.
\par
The importance of this assessment of the state of an individual's knowledge
\par
cannot be overemphasized. People prefer making decisions that put them in situa-
\par
tions where they can use what they know and where they feel they have sufficient
\par
knowledge to deal with unexpected events. Likewise, people dislike, and thus tend
\par
to avoid, decisions that would place them in situations where they have insufficient
\par
knowledge to guide their behavior and where the possibility of confusion is great.
\par
Thus, it is possible to see why people may choose not to adopt a behavior (e.g.
\par
recycle) when they feel they don't have enough information on what the behavior
\par
will entail. This may occur even though all the information they do have clearly
\par
makes adopting the behavior seem like the rational choice. In these cases, forcing
\par
someone to do a behavior (e.g. through the use of fines) may only reinforce the
\par
perception of having inadequate knowledge. In these cases, there can be consid-
\par
Climatic Change August 1994422 Anne R. Kearney
\par
erable repercussions, including negative reactance (Reich and Robertson, 1979;
\par
Brahm and Brahm, 1981), a need to continually increase the external motivation,
\par
and a cessation of the behavior when the external motivation is stopped (Cook and
\par
Berrenberg, 1981).
\par
The importance of knowledge (and the impact of a lack of knowledge) in the
\par
decision making process has been demonstrated in numerous studies. For exam-
\par
ple, a study of recyclers and non-recyclers (DeYoung, 1988) found no difference
\par
in attitudes or motives between the two groups, but a significant difference in
\par
knowledge. Confusion about the process of recycling was a strong predictor of
\par
non-recycling behavior. Others have shown that belief in a goal isn't enough to
\par
influence behavior; people must also have sufficient knowledge of appropriate
\par
behavior (Cook and Berrenberg, 1981; Weigel and Amsterdam, 1976). In a meta-
\par
analysis of 128 studies dealing with responsible environmental behavior Hines
\par
\emph{et al. }(1986-87) explored the link between knowledge and behavior. They found
\par
that in those studies which included the measures, there was a positive correla-
\par
tion (r = 0.37, \emph{SD }= 0.202) between knowledge (measured as knowledge of
\par
environmental issues and/or knowledge of how to take action on those issues) and
\par
behavior.
\par
Without adequate knowledge an individual may not be confident enough to
\par
act (Ehrlich, 1969) or may not know how to achieve a goal (Levanthal, 1970).
\par
Confusion about a situation has been shown to have serious detrimental effects,
\par
causing people to give up on a problem (Halford and Sheehan, 1991) or show
\par
defensive avoidance of the issue (Janis and Mann, 1977).
\par
\emph{2.1. Informational Characteristics of Global Change Issues}
\par
The state of an individual's knowledge about environmental issues and about
\par
appropriate behaviors for ameliorating environmental problems plays a large role
\par
in the decision making process. It follows that people must have an adequate
\par
understanding of global change issues if they are to be expected to respond with
\par
a change in behavior. Unfortunately, it appears that such an understanding is not
\par
widespread. Though researchers have only recently begun to explore nonexperts'
\par
understanding of global change, several recent studies have addressed the issue in
\par
relation to climate change. A nationwide survey (Research/Strategy/Management,
\par
Inc., 1989) found that 79\% of those polled had heard of the 'greenhouse effect'; it
\par
seems that information is indeed reaching the public. Understanding the process-
\par
es and consequences associated with the greenhouse effect, however, remains a
\par
challenge for many people. An in-depth study of lay people's conceptualization of
\par
global climate change (Kempton, 1991) found that lay people conceptualize both
\par
the processes of and behavioral solutions to climate change quite differently than
\par
do experts. The lay conceptualization of the processes of climate change was based
\par
on four categories: (1) stratospheric ozone depletion; (2) plant photosynthesis; (3)
\par
tropospheric pollution; and (4) personally experienced temperature variation. It
\par
Climatic Change August 1994\emph{Understanding Global Change }423
\par
is interesting to note that few of the subjects recognized the connection between
\par
energy consumption and global warming and that they typically regarded their
\par
personal fuel consumption as inelastic. In a prior study of Kempton \emph{et al. }(1985), it
\par
was again shown that many people have a faulty conception about which behaviors
\par
have the greatest effect on energy use.
\par
A recent study entitled 'The Joint Climate Project to Address Decision Makers'
\par
Uncertainties' (Bernabo and Eglington, 1993) suggests that this lack of under-
\par
standing is not limited to lay people. In his presentation of the results of the study
\par
to the House Committee on Science, Space and Technology, Bernabo (1993) indi-
\par
cated that the need for cohesive mental models was identified as a priority by
\par
decision makers. He noted that decision makers need to understand the issues in
\par
terms of potential impacts rather than predictions of changing global mean values
\par
of climate variables. Apparently, even decision makers feel they are not receiving
\par
enough useful information about climate change.
\par
One of the problems in communicating about global change lies in the selectivity
\par
of knowledge acquisition and the resultant difficulty in transferring information.
\par
Acquiring knowledge about global change is particularly difficult. With respect to
\par
the environment, much of people's understanding of the word and the way it works
\par
is based on experience; this model of the world tends to be maintained unless it is
\par
modified or contradicted by new experiences (Kaplan and Kaplan, 1982; Halford
\par
and Sheehan, 1991). Global change, then, is likely to be understood by most people
\par
only to the extent that it impacts their everyday life. The nature of global change,
\par
however, is such that it is virtually impossible to detect and experience. There are
\par
three major characteristics of global change that make it difficult to understand:
\par
\emph{1. There is no experiential base for global change. }Scientists say the Earth's
\par
temperature has increased by 0.5 degrees Fahrenheit in the last century and it
\par
is expected to continue rising (estimates range from 1.5 degrees to 4.5 degrees
\par
over the next 40 years) (Levy-Leboyer and Duron, 1991). This change is
\par
virtually impossible to perceive amidst daily, seasonal, and random temperature
\par
variations. Another reason that it is difficult to perceive human-caused global
\par
change directly is the gap between behavior and behavioral consequences. The
\par
time lapse between human activities and noticeable environmental effects is
\par
typically measured in years to decades and frequently extends beyond a single
\par
generation (e.g., as in the case of global warming) (Pawlik, 1991; Brewer,
\par
1992; Halford and Sheehan, 1991).
\par
\emph{2. Global change happens on a huge scale. }Global changes affect entire natural
\par
systems, span continents and impact many populations. The very scale of
\par
these problems makes them hard to grasp and difficult to relate to everyday
\par
life. In light of these huge problems, individual behavior change can seem
\par
insignificant, causing people to feel helpless (Kaplan and Kaplan, 1982) and,
\par
hence, to avoid even thinking about the problem.
\par
Climatic Change August 1994424 Anne R. Kearney
\par
\emph{3. Global change issues are inherently abstract. }Understanding of abstract issues
\par
is built through repeated experience with the elements, or concepts, that are
\par
related to that issue (Kaplan and Kaplan, 1982). For example, nuclear war is a
\par
fairly abstract issue, but the concepts that relate to the issue (e.g., mushroom
\par
clouds, radiation poisoning, damage from a nuclear blast) are familiar to many
\par
people. Because of this, 'nuclear war' has a meaning for people that extends
\par
far beyond the mere meaning of the words. Another example of an abstract
\par
issue that is at least somewhat understandable is the economy (Halford and
\par
Sheehan, 1991). The economy doesn't exist as a concrete thing, but everyone
\par
has personal experience with economic effects; people pay interest rates, cringe
\par
at inflation, and see unemployment rising when the economy is in a slump.
\par
Global change is different. It may be easy to understand that the congestion on
\par
the highway is causing smog at a local level, but how does one think about an
\par
entire climatic system? What is carbon dioxide? What is ozone? They can't be
\par
smelled or seen and people generally don't perceive their impact as being as
\par
direct or significant as that of a nuclear blast or rising interest rates.
\par
Because of these factors, it cannot be hoped that people will come to fully under-
\par
stand issues of global change in the course of their daily lives without help. The
\par
challenge is to design effective communication techniques that make salient an
\par
important and essentially abstract issue. An obvious choice is written information,
\par
or text. But as we have seen, not just any text will do. Many information-based
\par
programs fail because they never get the intended audience's attention, or because
\par
they get their audience's attention but don't present the information in a meaningful
\par
and understandable way (Dennis \emph{et al., }1990; Ester and Winett, 1982). The nature
\par
of global change issues (discussed above) provides some direction for design-
\par
ing effective communication techniques (i.e., the problems of lack of experiential
\par
base, abstraction, and scale must be dealt with). The next section will examine how
\par
people acquire knowledge and which informational characteristics facilitate this
\par
process. Understanding these cognitive processes will provide further constraints
\par
and direction for designing effective communication techniques.
\par
\textbf{3. Cognitive Structure - A Theoretical Overview}
\par
A basic argument in cognitive science is that individuals have mental models that
\par
serve as internal representations of the world. The position taken in this paper is
\par
that these models - cognitive maps - can be represented as networks of objects, or
\par
'mental entities', that come to be linked through experience (Kaplan and Kaplan,
\par
1982).* The structure of these mental entities, called prototypes, and the linkages
\par
between them are discussed in more detail in the next section.
\par
* Other models of knowledge organization (e.g., schemas, scenarios, and scripts) have been
\par
proposed yet lack physiological support. The cognitive map model has been accepted in fields as
\par
diverse as neuropsychology, psychology, and architecture (Downs and Stea, 1973; Alexander, 1965).
\par
Climatic Change August 1994\emph{Understanding Global Change }425
\par
\emph{What is a cognitive map ? }Take a moment to imagine the town in which you live.
\par
If you have been there for any length of time, you likely have a well-developed
\par
cognitive map of those areas in which you spend time. The prototypes, in this case,
\par
represent landmarks - your house, the building in which you work, a grove of
\par
evergreens, the park where you run, your neighbor's flower garden. The linkages
\par
between these prototypes represent the physical paths you take to get from place to
\par
place. If your structure is well-developed, you can think of many different routes
\par
to get from place to place, even if you've never actually taken one of those routes.
\par
In contrast, imagine a city that you've been to only once or twice. In this case,
\par
you have a very weak cognitive map of the area; there are few landmarks you
\par
recognize and getting from one place to the next is difficult. In these situations, the
\par
chances of getting lost are quite high and most people don't relish exploring without
\par
some guidance. Notice the important role of an individual's \emph{state }of knowledge in
\par
the decision making process. We tend to feel most comfortable in those areas for
\par
which we have a well-developed cognitive map. In contrast, we are often wary of
\par
those areas for which we have no, or at most a very weak, cognitive map. In the
\par
case of the latter, we may very well make a decision not to enter an area even if we
\par
were offered an external reward or were otherwise enticed.
\par
The cognitive map model can be used equally well to describe the structure of
\par
information that is not purely physical (Siegel and White, 1975; Kaplan, 1976);
\par
in this case, the map consists of a group of associated prototypes that represent
\par
objects and concepts relating to a particular domain. For example, if you recycle
\par
regularly, you will have a well-developed map of the recycling process. The process
\par
is familiar to you, and you have a good sense of how all the objects and concepts that
\par
are relevant to recycling (e.g., plastic resins, cleaning out cans, waste reduction,
\par
curbside pickup) are related to each other (i.e., are linked together). For people
\par
without a cognitive map of the recycling process, thinking about recycling can be
\par
daunting. Just as we don't relish the prospect of roaming around a city for which we
\par
have no cognitive map, we are also reluctant to delve into situations for which we
\par
have no structure to guide our actions. In this case, not being sure how to recycle
\par
or what is expected of you may be enough to deter you from recycling even if there
\par
is a city ordinance (or some other incentive) to recycle.
\par
\emph{3.1. A More Detailed Analysis of Cognitive Structure}
\par
\emph{Prototypes- the Basic Mental Entity. }Things in the world come to be represented in
\par
the brain by groups of tightly interconnected neurons; these structures are called cell
\par
assemblies (Posner, 1986; Rosch, 1973) or prototypes. A prototype is an abstraction,
\par
or distillation of multiple experiences with a particular object or concept in the
\par
world (e.g., tree, deforestation, global warming); thus prototypes take numerous
\par
and varied experiences to create. Once prototypes are formed, they can be activated
\par
by something in the world (i.e., if an object has enough features in common with a
\par
particular prototype - or alternatively, enough of the most typical or salient features
\par
Climatic Change August 1994426 Anne R. Kearney
\par
\textbf{- }then it will activate that prototype) resulting in 'recognition'. Prototypes can also
\par
be activated internally, allowing us to think about things that aren't present in the
\par
environment. The more experience we have with a particular object or concept, the
\par
more tightly connected the corresponding prototype becomes and the easier it is to
\par
activate.
\par
Prototypes appear to be hierarchically arranged, with lower-level prototypes
\par
representing concrete things (e.g., your house, a tree, water) and higher level pro-
\par
totypes representing more abstract concepts (e.g., waste reduction, global warming,
\par
sustainable development) (Kaplan and Kaplan, 1982). At all levels, prototypes can
\par
be connected to modifiers such as language,* color, spatial orientation, and affec-
\par
tive codes (Kaplan and Kaplan, 1982).
\par
\emph{Networking - Making Associations. }Once a prototype has been formed, it can
\par
become linked, or associated, with other prototypes. These linkages are a reflec-
\par
tion of experience; things that are related in the world (temporally, spatially, or
\par
conceptually) become associated in the mind. Activation spreads from prototype
\par
to prototype through these links, allowing the mind to go from one object or con-
\par
cept to the next - a 'train of thought'. In this way, we are able to make plans,
\par
run various scenarios in our head before taking action, and put individual objects
\par
and concepts into a larger context (Brunet, 1973). In short, cognitive maps are the
\par
means by which we model our world. This model, in turn, directs and constrains
\par
our thoughts, decisions, and actions.
\par
\emph{3.2. Cognitive Biases in Knowledge Acquisition}
\par
Because an individual's cognitive maps are built from experience in a particular
\par
environment, we would expect great variability in the components of people's
\par
maps. The process of map construction (building prototypes and making linkages
\par
between prototypes), however, is universal; cognitive maps have been found in very
\par
young children and non-human primates as well as in human adults (Olton, 1979;
\par
Ratuer and Myers, 1981). There are two important implications of these biases in
\par
knowledge acquisition and organization. The first is that unless new information is
\par
integrated into existing maps, it will be very difficult to access in the future. The
\par
nature of a cognitive map is such that activation can spread from one prototype to
\par
associated prototypes. In this way, the system has immediate access to concepts
\par
and thoughts that are related to the situation at hand. Information that is stored
\par
* Though words and thoughts are often strongly related, they are not the same things (Ong, 1973);
\par
we know this intuitively from our own experience with forgetting a word but remembering the
\par
associated image, or visa versa. Several investigators have formally conceptualized the separation
\par
between verbal and imagistic thought in terms of separate yet interconnected systems (Paivio, 1978;
\par
Tulving, 1983). Though it is not entirely clear how this separation is manifested in the brain, evidence
\par
overwhelmingly supports the notion that language and thought are not synonymous (Furth, 1971).
\par
For example, numerous studies show a lack of interference between verbal and spatial tasks (see
\par
Reed, 1982 for an overview). If the processes were the same, we would expect one task to inhibit, or
\par
interfere, with the other (as is the case with two verbal or two spatial tasks).
\par
Climatic Change August 1994\emph{Understanding Global Change }427
\par
with few or no associations to existing prototypes is referred to as inert knowledge
\par
(Bereiter and Scardamalia, 1985, Schank, 1991) and cannot be activated in this
\par
way. The problem of inert knowledge has received much discussion in the science
\par
education literature. One recent study (Brown, 1992) explored the effect of textual
\par
information on high school students' ability to understand a physics problem. It
\par
was found that even after reading a text that contained the correct answer to the
\par
problem, the students reverted to their preconceptions to solve the problem. In this
\par
case, it is likely that information from the text, that didn't relate to the students' prior
\par
conceptions and did not draw upon familiar experiences, was stored in isolation.
\par
When confronted with the physics problem, this information was not activated and
\par
students relied on their familiar (and incorrect) models.
\par
A second implication of the biases in knowledge acquisition and organization
\par
is that knowledge is \emph{constructed }rather than \emph{received }(Resnick, 1984); there is no
\par
simple correspondence between information presented and information stored. In
\par
education literature this is referred to as the constructivist theory of learning. It
\par
recognizes that the learner builds internal representations of new experiences in
\par
relation to past experiences. (For a more detailed discussion of this position and
\par
the support it receives from neurobiological theory, refer to Anderson, 1992.)
\par
Because knowledge acquisition is not a trivial process and because we are
\par
bombarded by an almost infinite amount of information every day, we cannot
\par
possibly process and store all the information we encounter. A system of selective
\par
learning is necessary to guide us towards that information which is most important.
\par
Selectivity operates at two different phases of information processing: the first
\par
is perception (the activation of prototypes by the environment), the second is
\par
knowledge integration (making linkages between new information and existing
\par
maps). For example, in order to maximize learning from a text, the reader must
\par
first attend to the text (perception) and then, while reading, integrate the information
\par
in the text with existing knowledge (knowledge integration).
\par
3.3. \emph{Information Selection through Attention}
\par
One of the most powerful mechanisms of selectivity in learning is attention. Atten-
\par
tion is the means by which we are able to select and persist in processing particular
\par
information while ignoring other information. James (1892), distinguishes two
\par
types of attention: involuntary and voluntary (or directed) attention. Involuntary
\par
attention is based on interest; it is spontaneous and effortless and easily inhibits
\par
competing thoughts. Involuntary attention can either involve things of innate inter-
\par
est (James' list includes "strange things, moving things, wild animals, bright things,
\par
pretty things, metallic things, words, blows, blood, etc... ") or be based on interests
\par
that have been gained through extended experience. Examples of learned interests
\par
include the interest an avid bird watcher takes in an unusual call or the interest
\par
of an expert chess player in a novel board configuration. The spontaneous nature
\par
Climatic Change August 1994428 Anne R. Kearney
\par
of involuntary attention is quite adaptive as it assures that potentially important
\par
events and objects in the environment will be noticed without effort.
\par
Directed (voluntary) attention is used to deal with numerous activities through-
\par
out the day that are necessary and important but that are intrinsically uninteresting
\par
(Cimprich, 1990). Invoking directed attention allows us to engage in purposeful,
\par
directed activity and thought. However, there is a cost associated with directed
\par
attention: it requires mental effort to sustain; it is neither spontaneous nor effortless
\par
and is thus subject to fatigue.*
\par
Involuntary attention often falls under the general heading of \emph{interest. }Informa-
\par
tion that we find interesting "elicits spontaneous rather than conscious, selective
\par
allocation of attention" (Hidi, 1990, p. 561). The role of interest in learning from
\par
text has received some attention (see Hidi and Baird, 1986; and Hidi, 1990, for
\par
excellent reviews). In general, interesting texts have been found not only to moti-
\par
vate people to read but also to increase comprehension of the text and to increase
\par
the likelihood that information in the text will be applied to novel situations. Draw-
\par
ing on involuntary attention is an effective strategy for increasing the probability
\par
that the reader will begin a text, learn the information contained in the text, and
\par
be able to use this information in the future. In this case, involuntary attention
\par
is acting on both stages of knowledge acquisition: \emph{activation of prototypes }and
\par
\emph{knowledge integration. }The section below provides a more detailed analysis of
\par
the characteristics of information that draw involuntary attention at each stage of
\par
knowledge acquisition.
\par
\emph{3.4. Indirection Attention and Knowledge Acquisition}
\par
3.4.1. \emph{Activating Prototypes}
\par
Cognitive maps are accessed through the activation of prototypes. Once a prototype
\par
is active, a host of associated prototypes can potentially become active. In this way,
\par
new information becomes integrated into existing cognitive maps, giving it context
\par
and making it memorable. There are four major characteristics of text that aid in
\par
the activation of prototypes: content of the text, the degree to which it relates to
\par
the reader's prior knowledge, the concreteness of the text, and the use of vivid detail.
\par
\emph{Content. }As discussed earlier, attention can be involuntary drawn to text because
\par
of the text's specific content. Some themes draw on innate interest (e.g., death)
\par
and will be interesting to almost everyone. Other themes draw on learned interest
\par
(e.g., a text about the discovery of a new plant species may be very interesting to
\par
a botanist) and will vary among different cultural, age, and socio-economic groups.
\par
* Directed attention appears to involve a different inhibitory mechanism than involuntary attention
\par
(Kaplan and Kaplan, 1982). The mechanism associated with directed attention is subject to fatigue
\par
through use while the inhibitory mechanism used in conjunction with involuntary attention is not.
\par
As the inhibitory mechanism fatigues, it becomes harder to keep out other thoughts, making directed
\par
attention difficult or impossible to sustain.
\par
Climatic Change August 1994\emph{Understanding Global Change }429
\par
\emph{The Role of Prior Knowledge. }Only a prototype that exists can become active,**
\par
and the stronger a prototype is (i.e., the more familiar it is to the reader) the easier it
\par
will be to activate. The importance of 'knowing your audience' - of personalizing
\par
information so that it relates to the prior knowledge of a particular group- cannot be
\par
overemphasized. Anderson \emph{et al. }(1987) suggested that 'character identification'
\par
and 'life themes' were important attributes of text that contribute to text-based
\par
interest. That is, they found that information was considered interesting if it had
\par
characters and themes with which the reader could identify. Other studies have
\par
shown that readers ignore or rework events and details that are unfamiliar. Bartlett
\par
(1932), presented a group of students with Native American folk tales. In gener-
\par
al, the students forgot those events that seemed bizarre, reorganized sequences of
\par
actions to better fit their own expectations, and invented information to fit their
\par
own interpretations.
\par
\emph{Concreteness. }A short route to prototype activation in the absence of a physi-
\par
cal environmental stimulus is through imagery. An image is, essentially, an active
\par
prototype (or group of prototypes) that represents something not present. The image
\par
can be formed through mental effort or it can be elicited by something in the envi-
\par
ronment (e.g., a word, a smell). The ease with which an image is formed is directly
\par
related to the ease with which a prototype is activated. In the case of written text,
\par
access to an individual's cognitive maps (i.e. prototype activation) can be achieved
\par
by choosing words that aid in imagery.
\par
Paivio and Yuille (1969) argue that the concrete-abstract dimension is the most
\par
important determinant of the ease with which one can form an image (i.e., can
\par
activate a prototype). Concrete refers to a word or group of words that can be
\par
represented by a single image. The word 'clown' is concrete; most people cannot
\par
help conjure up an image when presented with this word. In contrast, abstract words
\par
(e.g. 'justice') stand for concepts that cannot be encapsulated within a single image.
\par
Paivio and Yuille (1969) presented pairs of words to subjects and found that imagery
\par
was reported for 62\% of the concrete word-pairs and only 26\% of the abstract word-
\par
pairs. Images for concrete words are often created spontaneously and effortlessly
\par
(pointing to the role of involuntary attention) while abstract words generally take
\par
longer to image, if they can be imaged at all (Clark and Paivio, 1987). Hidi and
\par
Baird (1988) showed that students tend to recall concrete, personally involving
\par
material better than generalized, abstract material, even when the abstract material
\par
was more important in expressing the main ideas of the passage.
\par
A striking example of the importance of concrete information in understanding
\par
text comes from a study looking at how children solve word problems in mathe-
\par
matics (Hudson, 1983). The same problem was presented in two different forms:
