\par
\emph{Vividness. }While concrete refers to something that can be imaged, vivid can
\par
be defined in terms of the \emph{ease }with which imagery occurs (Tulving \emph{et al., }1965).
\par
Superior recall is found for text that is concrete and contains vivid detail over text
\par
that is concrete but not vivid (Tulving \emph{et al., }1965). Vividness can be increased
\par
through the addition of detail (e.g. 'a storm' vs. 'a storm with a vicious wind and
\par
torrential rains'). Bower (1979) found that the rated vividness of a particular word-
\par
pair was predictive of the later recall of the pair; this effect was found even under
\par
incidental learning conditions, suggesting that in these situations, imaging occurred
\par
with no directed effort. Using vivid detail can also add power or importance to a
\par
message. A study on fear-arousing persuasive communication (Sherer and Rogers,
\par
1984) showed that while concrete information significantly affected attitudes and
\par
increased recall, making the information more vivid by adding detail had a main
\par
effect not only on attitudes but on behavioral intent as well.
\par
3.5. \emph{Knowledge Integration}
\par
Learning from text requires more than the activation of prototypes; a series of
\par
isolated facts or details do not create meaning. In fact, understanding and the
\par
ability to make generalizations from a text can be undermined by the provision of
\par
interesting but irrelevant detail (Garner \emph{et al., }1991). This means that it is not enough
\par
to entice the reader to the end of a text through the use of interesting detail; the
\par
information in the text must also be integrated with the reader's existing cognitive
\par
maps. This is necessary so that the information can be accessed in the future,
\par
combined with other information in new ways, and applied to novel situations
\par
(Garner \emph{et al., }1991; Schiefele, 1991).
\par
Climatic Change August 1994\emph{Understanding Global Change }431
\par
Knowledge integration requires making linkages, or associations among pro-
\par
totypes; this requires a certain level of involvement, or \emph{cognitive engagement }on
\par
the part of the reader. Cognitive engagement refers to the process by which active
\par
prototypes are able to continually activate other, associated, prototypes that are per-
\par
tinent to the issue or environment at hand. In this way, predictions can be made and
\par
compared to what occurs next in the environment. Cognitive engagement implies
\par
that competing thoughts are inhibited, allowing for a sustained train of thought.
\par
Involuntary attention plays a large role here. If the structure of the text is interesting
\par
(draws involuntary attention), it can direct thoughts and cause the reader to make
\par
associations while expending little mental effort. If the structure of the text is not
\par
interesting, the reader must invoke voluntary attention, purposively focusing on
\par
the flow of information and relating it to knowledge they already have. When using
\par
directed attention, the mental effort required to block out competing thoughts can
\par
be great and as soon as this effort is relaxed or as soon as fatigue sets in, the train
\par
of thought is interrupted. There is also often little incentive to continue a text that
\par
requires a great deal of mental effort.
\par
Interesting stories are structured so that they are cognitively engaging, allowing
\par
for an effortless integration of new knowledge with existing cognitive structure.
\par
Sadoski \emph{et al. }(1990) found that students who read an interesting short story not
\par
only formed powerful visual images from the text, but they also elaborated on and
\par
synthesized information in the text to the point that these elaborations were recalled
\par
as part of the original text. In this case, information from the story was being inte-
\par
grated with the readers' existing knowledge to form a map, or model, of the story.
\par
Interestingly, even students who were told to read the story for typographical errors
\par
(not for enjoyment or for recall) made these kinds of elaborations, supporting the
\par
notion that imaging and integration occur spontaneously and effortlessly when a
\par
text is interesting (Hidi, 1990). Interesting stories (as compared to stories of low
\par
interest) are also related to greater comprehension of the story's main ideas, greater
\par
ability to answer complex questions about the story, and greater ability to apply the
\par
information in the story to new situations (Schiefele, 1991; Berustein, 1955). There
\par
are two major structural characteristics of text that aid in knowledge integration:
\par
the coherence of the text, and the presence of mystery in the text.*
\par
\emph{Coherence. }An analysis of stories by Stein and Glenn (1979) showed a strik-
\par
ing linear relationship between the percentage of causally related events in the
\par
story and recall, showing that memorability of a story depends on causal cohesion
\par
among its events. In terms of cognitive engagement, if there is no cohesion or
\par
connection between the various elements making up a story, it is difficult to make
\par
associations between them and a train of thought cannot be created. The informa-
\par
tion must hang together sufficiently well so that the reader is able to see how the
\par
