\par
various parts of the text relate (Kintsch, 1980). An incoherent text is unlikely to
\par
hold one's interest for long.
\par
Coherence can be effectively achieved through a sense of movement, or direc-
\par
tion through time (this need not be linear movement). In order to do this, the reader
\par
must be at least marginally aware of a goal state or valued endpoint (e.g., the
\par
destruction of something evil, victory over something, the discovery of something
\par
interesting or precious). Events in the text must then be arranged so that they relate
\par
in some gross way to the goal state (e.g., rendering it more or less probable) (Ger-
\par
gen and Gergen, 1986).
\par
\emph{An Element of Mystery. }Though a text needs to make sense, it shouldn't give
\par
everything away. Engagement is increased if the reader must predict and anticipate
\par
what will happen next (Kintsch, 1980; Schank, 1979). The presence of mystery,
\par
or uncertainty, causes the reader to generate a set of expectations (e.g., about what
\par
will happen next, about possible outcomes of the story, about motivations of the
\par
characters) against which future information can be matched. The reader is put in
\par
the position of wanting to know what will happen next, of searching for the very
\par
information that will be given in the proceeding text. When working optimally, this
\par
process will occur involuntarily and will wholly involve the brain in the text. As
\par
Dewey (1916, p. 126) writes, the "self and the world are engaged with each other
\par
in a developing situation."
\par
In more specific terms, prediction and anticipation cause an active prototype
\par
to activate (or partially activate) several associated prototypes - these are the
\par
nominations, or expectations, for what is to come. Notice that these associated
\par
prototypes are part of the reader's existing cognitive maps; they are, in turn,
\par
associated with numerous other prototypes in the map. In this way, the information
\par
in the text becomes integrated into the map. When the map is accessed in the future,
\par
information from the text will become available.*
\par
\textbf{4. Towards Effective Communication}
\par
The nature of global change and the selectivity of knowledge acquisition give us
\par
useful direction and constraints in designing effective communication strategies.
\par
Specifically, in order to effectively communicate about issues of global change,
\par
text should: (1) address the characteristics of global change that make it difficult
\par
to communicate; and (2) be compatible with the processes of human knowledge
\par
acquisition. More specifically, the text must fulfill the following:
\par
\emph{1. It must be interesting. }With the plethora of information bombarding people
\par
everyday, it is understandable that much of it is ignored. We cannot assume that
\par
people will read what they are given. Because of this fierce competition for an
\par
* The description here is quite abbreviated. The interested reader should consult Kaplan and
\par
Kaplan (1982) for a general overview of the concepts and Hidi (1990) for an overview of the process
\par
specifically as it relates to text processing.
\par
Climatic Change August 1994\emph{Understanding Global Change }433
\par
individual's attention, we must design information that draws on involuntary
\par
attention. Information must be able to 'hook' the reader, carry them through to
\par
the end of the text, and leave them with useful knowledge. As we have seen
\par
in the previous section, interesting content, coherence, a presence of mystery,
\par
and vivid detail are effective ways to increase interest.
\par
\emph{2. It must present abstract information in such a way that the reader can imagine}
\par
\emph{it. }Much of the information about global change is in the form of scientific data
\par
\textbf{- }highly abstract facts and figures that often have little impact on an individual's
\par
understanding of the issue. To promote understanding, information should be
\par
made concrete (e.g., through the use of specific examples) and vivid (through
\par
the use of detail).
\par
\emph{3. It must relate to the reader's existing knowledge. }New information that is
\par
wholly unrelated to the way people understand the world is likely to be ignored
\par
or, at best, stored as a chunk of inaccessible knowledge that is unlikely to be
\par
factored into everyday decisions and behavior. Information about global change
\par
must speak to the beliefs that people already hold about the environment; it
\par
must also relate to their daily experiences and address perceived obstacles to
\par
behavior change.
\par
\emph{4. It must present global issues at a human scale. }Information about large-scale
\par
events can be overwhelming. Telling examples come from the literature on
\par
natural disasters. In the face of large-scale disasters, people have a tendency
\par
to ignore information and warnings, leading them to deny the threat, refuse to
\par
leave an area, and return to the same site to rebuild (Burton \emph{etal., }1968; Watt,
\par
1974). This behavior can be understood when we consider that thinking about
\par
something that you can't do anything about leads to a state of helplessness;
\par
this is a state that people actively avoid (Kaplan and Kaplan, 1982).
\par
The current admonition to 'think globally, act locally' is a recognition that it
\par
is hard for people to feel they are making a contribution to a global problem
\par
(Bardwell, 1991). Bringing these global issues down to a human scale may be
\par
achieved through the use of characters or by highlighting local problems or
\par
behaviors that are related to global changes in the environment.
\par
\emph{5. It must be extendible, to provide a large experiential base. }One interesting and
\par
useful text is a good start, but in most cases it will not be enough. Recall that the
\par
prototypes that make up a cognitive map are built up slowly through repeated
\par
experience. Because building prototypes is a slow and conservative process,
\par
people generally need to be exposed to a number and variety of presentations
\par
of a particular concept or idea in order to generalize the information and
\par
apply it to new situations. Those prototypes which are the strongest (i.e., those
\par
corresponding to objects and ideas that are familiar - supported by numerous
\par
experiences - to an individual) will have a larger influence during decision
\par
making than will concepts that are less familiar (Tversky and Kahneman,
\par
1982).
\par
Climatic Change August 1994434 Anne R. Kearney
\par
\emph{4.1. Communicating through Story}
\par
A good story (note that story is broadly used here to include both fiction and
\par
non-fiction, case-studies, and some analogies) is one form of text that fulfills the
\par
above criteria. Stories have been proposed as effective tools for teaching and
\par
encouraging behavioral change (Monroe and Kaplan, 1988; Schank, 1991). They
\par
are inherently interesting, provide concrete examples of abstract issues or concepts,
\par
can be extended to provide multiple and varied 'experiences', can be crafted to
\par
relate to people's lives and what they already know, are inexpensive to create and
\par
distribute, and can be used across all age groups.
\par
Stories have traditionally been used in all cultures to explain natural phenomena,
\par
convey morals and values, and guide appropriate behavior (Stein and Glenn, 1979;
\par
Bettelheim, 1976). Oral stories were often the only records of a culture passed down
\par
through generations. These tales were told with great care as they were recognized
\par
to be influential in the evolution of a given society (Baker and Greene, 1977).
\par
Stories are still very much a part of the way we understand our world. Much
\par
of our daily communication is in the form of stories told and listened to (Schank,
\par
1990b). A study of 'excellent companies' (Peters and Waterman, 1982) found
\par
that myths, stories, and legends appear to be very important in passing on and
\par
conveying the organization's shared values and culture. Stories have also been used
\par
by photocopy repairmen to diagnose problems with photocopiers; these stories
\par
are told and retold and become part of the knowledge base of the repairmen
\par
(Gudmundsdottir, 1991).
\par
Stories have been particularly influential in decision making and education
\par
(Schank, 1990a; Halford and Sheehan, 1991). One study on the decision making
\par
process in government showed that people generally make decisions based on sto-
\par
ries of incidents that had previously occurred and were similar to the situation at
\par
hand (Neustadt and May, 1986). Traditional midwives have been found to share
\par
stories to aid in diagnosis of problems and identify remedies (Gudmundsdottir,
\par
1991). Monroe and Kaplan (1988) found that the use of case studies (stories) and
\par
talking about what others do to solve environmental problems may be more effec-
\par
tive than 'learning by doing' for teaching environmental problem-solving skills. A
\par
later study of Monroe's (1991) showed a significant correlation between interesting
\par
stories and attitudes towards taking environmental actions. The impact of stories on
\par
behavior was directly shown by the effectiveness of an energy conservation tactic
\par
that made use of case-studies (stories) of 'super-conservers' (Yates and Aronson,
\par
1983).
\par
\emph{4.1.1. Stories and Global Change}
\par
Stories, especially story-like analogies, are widely used in formal science education
\par
and are often considered indispensable for providing students with the beginnings
\par
of understanding in unfamiliar domains (Thagard, 1991). The \emph{Journal of Chemical}
\par
\emph{Education }even runs a regular feature on 'Application and Analogies'. In one
\par
Climatic Change August 1994\emph{Understanding Global Change }435
\par
recent study (Brown, 1992), a story-like extended analogy was used to bridge the
\par
gap between students' preconceived notions of force and a Newtonian conception
\par
of force. The analogy specifically addressed the students' misconceptions and made
\par
use of concrete situations from their experience. After reading the analogy, students
\par
were much more likely to be able to correctly solve a variety of force problems
\par
than students who had read an excerpt from a text on the same subject.
\par
Though most studies specifically on stories or analogies and global change
\par
are anecdotal rather than strictly empirical, there is sufficient evidence to suggest
\par
that they can increase people's understanding of global change processes and can
\par
provide imagery about the consequences of global environmental change (see
\par
Glantz, 1992 for an excellent review). Simple analogies (e.g., the greenhouse
\par
analogy) may be useful in conveying a single idea, while more complex, 'story-
\par
like' analogies may prove more useful in conveying an understanding of how
\par
global changes might impact the future.
\par
Schneider takes this tact in creating plausible scenarios of the consequences
\par
of global changes. Several of these scenarios are included in his book \emph{Global}
\par
\emph{Warming: Are We Entering the Greenhouse Century? }(1990). The book begins
\par
with a chapter entitled 'Shadows of the Climate Future', an obviously fictitious
\par
account of what a year in the 'greenhouse century' might be like if nothing is done
\par
to deal with the growing problem of global warming. He writes:
\par
"What happened that Sunday afternoon in Chicago actually originated over
\par
a thousand miles away in Saskatchewan, where a very intense three-week
\par
heat wave had come on the heels of a dry spring. Relative humidity had
\par
been abnormally low for the previous week. Then a cold front came through,
\par
triggering many relatively rainless thundershowers. From the lightning, fires
\par
broke out simultaneously over several thousand square miles. Within days,
\par
an area nearly the size of New Jersey had burned, and the drifting smoke was
\par
so thick over the Midwest that the late afternoon looked more like twilight
\par
for several days, including the Sunday afternoon when the second game of an
\par
important doubleheader was dimmed out of the record book.
\par
Other climatic effects were far more serious than a called baseball game,
\par
however..." (p. 2).
\par
Notice how the scenario has the effect of drawing the reader into the scene. The
\par
potential effects of climatic change are made real and imaginable, and the leading
\par
sentence at the end encourages the reader to continue. Contrast this with a rather
\par
dry description of a possible scenario printed in \emph{Nature:}
\par
"What would be the human (social, economic and political) impact of changes
\par
of the type presented here? We can only speculate on some of the possibilities
\par
\textbf{- }while noting that impact is at least as much related to the year-to-year
\par
variability of climate as it is to the mean or to changes in the mean. First,
\par
a substantial increase in the intensity of the Indian monsoon could have a
\par
significant impact. Although the impact of the monsoon in India is generally
\par
Climatic Change August 1994436 Anne R. Kearney
\par
linked to its failure, increased and more violent precipitation can also have
\par
most detrimental effects (as witnessed by the flooding in 1979). Second,
\par
in some parts of the world (in particular in Europe and European Russia)
\par
temperature increase may occur in association with reductions in precipitation.
\par
These two factors will reinforce each other in increasing soil moisture deficits
\par
and so tend to have greater agricultural impact than either factor would alone.
\par
The climatic differences between warm and cold years which we have shown
\par
for Europe and European Russia appear to be of special importance as they
\par
indicate that conditions in a high-CO2 world could be radically different from
\par
those given by Kellogg for the Hypsithermal" (Wigley \emph{et al., }1980, pp. 20-21).
\par
Notice the lack of specific examples of what the 'detrimental effects' and 'impacts'
\par
might be, the large-scale nature of the description, and the absence of interesting
\par
detail. For the non-expert reading this except, it would be very difficult to imagine
\par
and understand the scenario described. Unfortunately, much of the information
\par
presented by the scientific community and intended for the public, for decision
\par
makers, or for scientists in other disciplines is of this form. The contention of this
\par
paper is that this style of information presentation is not particularly effective at
\par
transferring information because it is not compatible with the way people process
\par
information.
\par
A word of caution is necessary here. Though stories and analogies can be quite
\par
effective, they should not be used indiscriminately. Thagard (1992) discusses the
\par
pitfalls of using inappropriate analogies in education. In addition to the risk of
\par
creating a false understanding, the use of analogies or specific case studies can
\par
be misleading guides to action (Glantz, 1992). For instance, the careless use of
\par
historical analogies or the use of grossly untrue stories can lead to poor policy
\par
formation. Emphasis should be placed on stories as possible guides for action and
\par
not necessarily probabilistic forecasts.
\par
This also underscores the need for numerous and varied analogies or stories
\par
relating to a particular topic or problem. A lone story will not likely impart the
\par
extent of knowledge and understanding necessary for sound decisions. Recall the
\par
process by which prototypes and cognitive maps are created. Multiple experiences
\par
not only strengthen individual prototypes but will create a more complex cognitive
\par
map. The resultant cognitive structure will be one that allows for exploration of
\par
ideas and for the discovery of several potential solutions to a problem.
\par
5. \textbf{Conclusions}
\par
Learning from text is greatly facilitated when the text is structured to take advantage
\par
of cognitive processes. Specifically, the effectiveness of text depends on activating
\par
pre-existing prototypes and integrating the information contained in the text into
\par
existing cognitive maps so that it becomes part of the readers 'real-world' knowl-
\par
edge. Textual characteristics that facilitate this process are: \emph{interesting content,}
\par
\emph{relatedness to a reader's prior knowledge, concreteness, vividness, coherence, }and
\par
Climatic Change August 1994\emph{Understanding Global Change }437
\par
\emph{mystery. }These characteristics are perfectly encapsulated within a 'good story'. A
\par
prototypical story is organized into cohesive, thematic episodes that are causally
\par
related; it often contains a character, a predicament with an uncertain outcome, and
\par
resolution of the predicament (Stein and Policastro, 1984). Stories are generally
\par
of concrete things (though their overall meaning may be much more abstract), and
\par
are told with vivid detail.
\par
Stories may be a particularly effective way to communicate about issues in
\par
global change; they can speak to people's lives and experiences the way no other
\par
text can. Stories are also able to put large-scale issues into a familiar context
\par
reducing "reality to a smaller scale that mimics it and yet enables us to deal with it
\par
in some manageable way" (Common, 1986).
\par
It is no accident that stories have had such powerful influences on thought and
\par
behavior throughout history and throughout different cultures (Stein and Policas-
\par
tro, 1984). Stories can make the abstract concrete and accessible, turning what
\par
was only dimly perceived into something vivid and powerful. Stories put issues
\par
on a people-appropriate scale, filling one's head without overwhelming it with
\par
information. A good story is incorporated into the pre-existing cognitive structures
\par
representing what the reader already knows. In this way, their knowledge about the
\par
world is extended and, where necessary, corrected, with an ultimate effect on their
\par
beliefs and or how they might act in the future. Stories allow people to vicariously
\par
experience many different places, events, and situations; in this way they can pre-
\par
pare themselves in imagination for what life might bring and learn how to solve
\par
problems they have not yet experienced.
\par
Though stories are particularly powerful tools for transferring information, they
\par
have not been widely used in education and behavioral change strategies. One
\par
possible reason is that stories and storytelling are associated with fantasy and
\par
pretending and thus may make the serious scientist or educator skeptical of their
\par
use, and, instead, favor the use of factsheets or textbooks. This reasoning has
\par
largely limited the use of stories in formal education to the primary level (Rosen,
\par
1985). The urgency of global change problems, however, requires that we put
\par
preconceptions aside and utilize strategies that work with human nature rather than
\par
against it. Stories are one such strategy. A good story is particularly effective at
\par
transmitting information in a memorable and useful way because it is compatible
\par
with human information processes; the characteristics of a story take advantage of
\par
the way people process, store, and utilize information.
