  publisher = {Edward Elgar Publishing Ltd Northampton},
  langid = {english},
  note = {View metadata, citation and similar papers at core.ac.uk
\par
\emph{brought to you by }\textbf{CORE}
\par
\emph{provided by }Research Papers in Economics
\par
\textbf{Integrated Assessment Models}
\par
\textbf{For Climate Change Control}{$\ast$}
\par
David L. Kelly{$\ast\ast$}
\par
Charles D. Kolstad{$\ast\ast\ast$}
\par
Current Version: November 1998
\par
Forthcoming: Henk Folmer and Tom Tietenberg (eds), \emph{International Yearbook of}
\par
\emph{Environmental and Resource Economics 1999/2000: A Survey of Current Issues}
\par
(Cheltenham, UK:Edward Elgar, 1999).
\par
{$\ast$} Research supported by US Department of Energy grant number DE-FG03-96ER62277.
\par
Portions of this paper are adapted from Kolstad (1998).
\par
{$\ast\ast$} Department of Economics, University of Miami, Box 248126, Coral Gables, FL 33124 and
\par
Department of Economics, University of California, Santa Barbara, Calif. 93106-9210.
\par
{$\ast\ast\ast$} Department of Economics, Bren School of Environmental Science and Management, and
\par
Environmental Studies Program, University of California, Santa Barbara, CA 93106-9210.\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
Climate change is one of the most fascinating and thoroughly studied environmental topics in
\par
the economics and scientific literature. The control of climate change is a formidable challenge:
\par
no other environmental problem has so much uncertainty, requires so much coordination among
\par
countries, and is so dynamically linked across time.
\par
Climate change is so interesting because the problem encompasses all aspects of
\par
environmental science and economics. On the scientific side, significant uncertainties remain
\par
about the transport of the greenhouse gas (GHG) pollutants through the atmosphere and the
\par
effect of GHGs on the atmospheric temperature, ocean temperature, rainfall, and sea level. The
\par
study of such climate processes requires the use of large computer models called general
\par
circulation models (GCMs).
\par
The economics of climate change is even less well-understood. Significant uncertainties
\par
remain about the impacts of climate change on industries such as agriculture and recreation (e.g.,
\par
skiing). The costs of controlling a long-run problem like climate change depend primarily on the
\par
degree of technical innovation, which is obviously difficult to predict. For example, innovations in
\par
the automobile industry such as more inexpensive electric cars could dramatically reduce the
\par
seriousness of the climate change problem.1 Assigning weight to benefits received far in the
\par
future is quite difficult. Finally implementation and credible enforcement of a climate change
\par
control policy across countries is a challenge for any regulation instrument.
\par
\textbf{1.1. Key Aspects of Integrated Assessment Models}
\par
On an individual level, many aspects of climate change are now under study in the literature.
\par
However, many researchers combine the scientific and economic aspects of climate change in
\par
order to assess policy options for climate change (as opposed to advancing knowledge for its own
\par
1 Because climate change is a long run problem, costs associated with slow adoption of new
\par
technologies such as fuel efficient cars are small.
\par
2sake). These models are known as integrated assessment models (IAMs). Examples include:
\par
Dowlatabadi and Morgan (1993, 1995), Kelly and Kolstad (1997a), Kolstad (1996), Lempert,
\par
Schlesinger, and Banks (1995), Manne, Mendelsohn, and Richels (1993, 1995), MIT (1994),
\par
Morita, et. al. (1994), Nordhaus (1994), and Peck and Teisberg (1992). IAMs are receiving
\par
increasing attention of late. The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), which in
\par
the past has focused primarily on the physics of climate change, has released a report on the
\par
socio-economic dimensions of climate change. The report includes a chapter which reviews
\par
IAMs (Weyant, et. al. 1996).
\par
We define an integrated assessment model broadly as any model which combines scientific
\par
and socio-economic aspects of climate change primarily for the purpose of assessing policy
\par
options for climate change control. Other authors have similar definitions; for example Weyant,
\par
et. al. (1996) defines integrated assessment even more broadly as any model which draws on
\par
knowledge from research in multiple disciplines.
\par
Weyant, et. al. (1996) give three purposes for integrated assessment: (1) assess climate
\par
change control policies, (2) constructively force multiple dimensions of the climate change
\par
problem into the same framework, and (3) quantify the relative importance of climate change in
\par
the context of other environmental and non-environmental problems facing mankind. An example
\par
of (1) is the assessment of an Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC, 1990 and
\par
IPCC, 1992) control policy on sea level rise and the resulting effects on coastal ecology or
\par
tourism. Another example of (1) is the computation of the optimal climate control policy. An
\par
example of (2) is ranking the importance of the temperature change from a doubling of CO2 (the
\par
climate sensitivity parameter) and the discount rate parameter, which can only be done within an
\par
interdisciplinary framework. Another example of (2) is identifying the driving forces behind
\par
climate change by identifying to which sectors climate change is most sensitive. An example of
\par
(3) is ranking the benefits of climate change control with improving sanitation or improving
\par
medicine in developing countries. In Section 4, we show that IAMs have addressed all of these
\par
questions and have achieved specific results, although the degree to which policy makers have
\par
used the results of IAMs is open to question. Note that the Weyant, et. al. (1996) definition is
\par
particularly broad, which reflects the wide variety of uses for IAMs.
\par
3IAMs vary widely in several key areas. IAMs are divided into two broad categories, which
\par
vary according to the policy options available to the regulator (both GHG control policies and
\par
economic policies). IAMs also differ in the complexity of the economic and climate sectors. An
\par
important difference between IAMs is the treatment of uncertainty, a fundamental concern in
\par
climate change policy. Finally, IAMs differ in the responsiveness of agents within the model to
\par
climate change policies.
\par
Policy evaluation IAMs (hereafter policy evaluation models) consider the effect of a single
\par
policy option (often one proposed in IPCC, 1990, 1992) on the biosphere, climate, and sometimes
\par
economic systems. Policy evaluation models are also known as simulation models. In contrast,
\par
policy optimization IAMs (hereafter policy optimization models) have a dual purpose: (a) to seek
\par
to find the optimal policy which trades off expected costs and benefits of climate change control
\par
(regulatory efficiency) or the policy which minimizes costs of achieving a particular goal
\par
(regulatory cost-effectiveness) and (b) to simulate the effect of an efficient level of carbon
\par
abatement on the world economy. The distinction between these two kinds of models is critical
\par
because the degree of complexity in the policy options dictates the degree of complexity in the
\par
IAM. Policy evaluation models consider a single exogenously specified policy and seek to
\par
estimate the effects of that policy on the environment. Policy optimization models search for the
\par
optimal policy, which is a complex process and computationally expensive. Hence, such models
\par
typically have economic and climate sectors which are relatively simple.
\par
Policy optimization and evaluation models also differ on a deeper level. Policy optimization
\par
models invoke well-known theorems on the equivalence between an efficient solution and the
\par
market equilibrium with an internalized public good provision.2 That is, policy optimization
\par
models find the same level of GHG control we expect to observe in a government elected by
\par
rational voters, and the same responses we expect to observe from rational agents. Policy
\par
2 Indeed, many policy optimization models start with a market economy in which the
\par
regulatory instrument is a tax and then convert the model to an equivalent problem which finds
\par
the optimal emissions (see, for example, Nordhaus and Yang, 1996). Such models maximize a
\par
weighted sum of utilities where the weights are adjusted until individual budgets balance, which is
\par
equivalent to a Pareto optimum by the second welfare theorem. Other policy optimization models
\par
start with optimal emissions and convert the results into a tax.
\par
4optimization models are thus normative and provide a description of the world, given the
\par
assumptions of the equivalence theorems. Policy evaluation models take actions by agents and
\par
governments as given, provided by policy proposals, assumption, observation, and/or expert
\par
opinion. Policy evaluation models thus have more freedom to model phenomena that are
\par
inconsistent with some of the assumptions of policy optimization models. For example, in a policy
\par
optimization model implicitly assumes optimal economic growth rates (and therefore future
\par
emissions), which are higher than has been observed in some developing countries.3 A policy
\par
evaluation model is free to assume that growth in the developing world matches the historically
\par
observed growth rates. A policy evaluation model is, however, only as good as the modeler' s
\par
ability to predict decisions. A reader thus cannot readily interpret why agents respond the way
\par
they do in policy evaluation models (i.e. why economic growth is so slow in some countries).
\par
Thus, policy evaluation models are more like `` black boxes'' and the meaning of the results is less
\par
clear.
\par
The advantage of policy evaluation models versus policy optimization models is that policy
\par
evaluation models allow for much greater detail on the physical, economic and social aspects of
\par
the very complex climate change problem. However, added complexity is usually a mixed
\par
blessing since what drives the results in complex models is rarely clear. Conversely, optimization
\par
models have complex policies which can depend on state variables such as the state of the climate
\par
and economic growth. Further, optimization models allow for economic responses to policies.
\par
An optimization model may let producers and consumers determine endogenously the optimal mix
\par
of GHG intensive and non-GHG intensive fuels given a climate change control policy, while in an
\par
evaluation model the modeler specifies exogenously the mix of fuels used. Hence policy
\par
evaluation models must rely essentially on the skill of the modeler in taking into account how
\par
consumers and producers behave. However, optimization models lack detail and abstract away
\par
from what some consider important details in climate change.
\par
3 Usual reasons for slow growth are differences in variables such as education and life
\par
expectancy (see for example, Summers and Heston, 1991) and differences in economic
\par
institutions and structure (see for example, Mauro, 1995 or North 1996). Such complications are
\par
not included in any policy optimization model.
\par
5Within the policy evaluation or optimization model types, significant variation exists in the
\par
level of complexity of the economic and climate sectors. Some IAMs (both optimization and
\par
evaluation) are geographically complex and divide the world into as many as 19 regions, each
\par
differing in emissions and impacts. Other models have a highly complex energy sector that
\par
forecasts conversions to alternative energy technologies as the cost of fuels which emit GHGs
\par
rise. On the physical side, some models based on GCMs model the climate with great care and
\par
account for variations in climate change across different types of topography, while others have a
\par
complex model of atmospheric chemistry, allowing for many different types of GHGs. As pointed
\par
out in Tol, et. al. (1995), there does not appear to be any consensus about what aspects of the
\par
climate change problem should be included in an IAM model. For example, Weyant et. al. (1996)
\par
states that `` IAMs should consider the problem of local air quality,'' because changes in weather
\par
affect the removal of local air pollutants. Modeling changes in local air quality would add
\par
enormous complexity. Why local air quality is more important than other un-modeled aspects of
\par
climate change that could be added (with perhaps less complexity) is not clear.
\par
Many IAMs are designed to answer smaller questions about climate change. For example, if
\par
a researcher is interested in the degree of technical change that can be expected to arise from an
\par
aggressive control strategy, a policy evaluation model with a highly complex energy sector may be
\par
appropriate. But in most models it is not clear why some aspects of climate change are included
\par
and others are not. Tol, et. al. (1995) proposes that IAM models should be `` balanced.'' That is,
\par
IAMs should pay relatively equal attention to the various components of the climate change
\par
problem.
\par
Another idea is to model those aspects of climate change for which the optimal or best
\par
climate change policy is most sensitive. For example, if climate change control rates are very
\par
sensitive to the inclusion of sulfur dioxide (which is discharged from burning coal and acts to cool
\par
the earth), then the case for including sulfur dioxide in IAMs is strong. If an IAM finds similar
\par
control rates when sulfur dioxide is integrated with other GHGs into a single pollutant, then
\par
modeling sulfur dioxide separately needlessly adds complexity. A problem with this approach is
\par
that few studies actually compute for what aspects of climate change the control policy is most
\par
sensitive. A study might vary some parameters of the model and compute how the results change,
\par
6but few studies compute how the results change if another sector or another GHG or another
\par
region is added. A few new results in this area, detailed below, indicate the importance of
\par
economic growth assumptions and the role of discounting. Few IAMs have a complex model of
\par
how economic growth occurs and fewer still have a complex model of discounting (through, for
\par
example, an overlapping generations framework).
\par
An important feature of some policy optimization models is allowing agents to respond to
\par
GHG control policies. For example, a policy evaluation model might take as exogenous a
\par
forecast of uncontrolled emissions growth over the next century, and then evaluate the impact of a
\par
proposed IPCC control policy and compare the impacts to those that occur with no control
\par
policy. However, emissions growth projections are based on economic growth. Economic
\par
growth will be quite different in a world with a restrictive control policies versus a world with no
\par
control policies (due to the response of economic agents to the GHG control policy). Hence
\par
uncontrolled emissions are quite different, which violates the implicit assumption that
\par
uncontrolled emissions do not vary. All policy evaluation models and even some policy
\par
optimization models let uncontrolled emissions be exogenous, although a few fully specified
\par
policy optimization models take into account such changes in behavior. Modeling uncontrolled
\par
emissions has important consequences. For example, Manne and Richels (1993) show that if
\par
consumers believe the chance that emissions will be reduced to 1990 levels in 2010 is 50\%,
\par
consumers will reduce emissions in the year 2000 as if a tax of \$17.50 per ton of carbon were
\par
already in place.
\par
\textbf{1.2. Uncertainty in Integrated Assessment}
\par
One of the dominant issues in the economics of climate change is the role of uncertainty.
\par
Manne and Richels (1992), in their important book on controlling precursors of climate change,
\par
focus almost entirely on hedging against uncertainty. Nordhaus (1994), in his equally important
\par
book on the economics of climate change, devotes considerable space to the implications of
\par
uncertainty for forming control policies.4 The policy debate world-wide is dominated by the
\par
4 Other papers that have considered these issues include Hammitt et al. (1992), Kolstad
\par
(1993), and Peck et al. (1989).
\par
7question: "Do we know enough to control the problem now or should we wait until more is
\par
known about climate change?" Although uncertainty is always present in problems of
\par
environmental policy, the uncertainty is much greater in the case of climate change if for no other
\par
reason than the problem and its solution span decades or even centuries. Implicit in the decision
\par
about controlling now or waiting is how fast uncertainty is resolved. If research resolved
\par
uncertainty quickly (as is currently predicted), then waiting until more is known costs little. If
\par
uncertainty is not resolved quickly, then costs associated with waiting can add up.
\par
The uncertainty problem has two dimensions: parametric uncertainty and stochasticity.
\par
Some aspects of climate change are not well understood, although presumably over time the
\par
problem will be better understood. We are uncertain about particular parameters of the problem,
\par
but we expect that uncertainty to diminish with time or effort (e.g., R\&D). We might also be
\par
concerned with the structure of the problem. These are examples of what we term parametric
\par
uncertainty.
\par
A close relative of parametric uncertainty is stochasticity. Stochasticity arises from
\par
phenomena that affect the economic or physical processes, but are not modeled. First, regardless
\par
of how complex an economic or physical model is, some elements are inevitably not modeled.
\par
Often the sum of the not modeled parts of a process behave like a stochastic shock. For example,
\par
volcanoes, sunspots, and hundreds of other phenomena affect the global mean temperature in a
\par
way that is approximated by an AR(1) process (see Kelly, Kolstad, Schlesinger, and Androva,
\par
1998). Secondly, one cannot know the future values of many economic and technology processes
\par
because if the future were known, consumers would act on that knowledge in ways which change
\par
the future. For example, future economic growth rates (and hence emissions) are stochastic
\par
because if an IAM could predict future values of GDP, consumers and investors could use the
\par
same model to predict GDP and use the results to change savings and consumption behavior.
\par
Here a random walk approximates the stochastic GDP process quite well. Hence climate change
\par
is subject to stochastic shocks which affect climate, technology and costs; but the future values of
\par
these shocks are always uncertain. These two elements--parametric uncertainty and stochasticity-
\par
8-generate significant uncertainty in trying to formulate policy for controlling greenhouse gases
\par
(GHGs).
\par
To make things more complex, parametric uncertainty is not constant over time; that is,
\par
learning takes place. Over time, learning can reduce parametric uncertainty. By investing in
\par
R\&D, or observing climate behavior, we can learn about uncertain parameters. Of course, \emph{ex}
\par
\emph{ante}, we do not know how parametric uncertainty will be resolved.
\par
Learning has many dimensions. Learning can take place at various levels of a policy
\par
problem, ranging from agents in the economy who are learning in order to adapt to changes in
\par
their environment to policy-makers who are trying to formulate the best policy in an uncertain and
\par
changing world. When agents within the economy react to changed circumstances, it is usually
\par
termed adaptation. If these agents perceive uncertainty but learn over time, then adaptation takes
\par
time. While learning is taking place agents adjust, making suboptimal decisions (relative to
\par
perfect information) with resulting welfare losses. To offer an overly simplistic example, suppose
\par
the climate has changed in the Midwestern US, resulting in a higher frequency of flooding and
\par
more rainfall. It may take decades before farmers realize the change is permanent and combat the
\par
increased rainfall by changing the crops (perhaps planting flood resistant strains). In the
\par
meantime, significant crop losses occur. Even if farmers could adapt perfectly to the changed
\par
climate, the delay in realizing a change has occurred results in significant losses.
\par
Policy-makers also base decisions on some body of knowledge. When that knowledge
\par
base evolves over time, regulatory decisions evolve over time. More subtly, current regulatory
\par
decisions must take into account the fact that more will be known tomorrow. This process takes
\par
time and the decisions made in the interim influence the rate at which information is being
\par
acquired.
\par
In addition to who learns, we can characterize how learning occurs.5 Passive learning
\par
involves the exogenous arrival of information. This may occur all at once, as in Manne and
\par
Richels (1992), or more gradually as a function of time, as in Kolstad (1993, 1996). Obviously
\par
5 See Cunha-e-sa (1994) and Kolstad (1996) for further discussion of different types of
\par
learning.
\par
9there has to be some process whereby information is generated and arrives; however, with passive
\par
learning, that process is exogenous to the system being examined.
\par
We let active learning denote learning where agents have some influence over the rate at
\par
which information arrives. For instance, investment in research and development (R\&D) yields
\par
information. R\&D is an obvious way in which information is acquired and a clear example of
\par
active learning. It is also a major factor in learning about climate change.
\par
Learning from experience is a form of active learning which is also very important in
\par
climate change. If a monopolist is uncertain about a demand curve, she can experiment by
\par
varying price and observing sales, learning over time about demand (Balvers and Cosimano,
\par
1990). We could interpret our climate change policy as a grand experiment: by increasing GHG
\par
emissions we obtain information about how emissions influence the climate.
\par
In the example of the farmer learning from realizations of the climate, learning occurs
\par
without R\&D, simply by observation. Furthermore, much effort has been expended by the
\par
research community in trying to detect a climate change footprint/fingerprint in the temperature
\par
record of the last century. For example, Schlesinger and Ramankutty (1995) and Bassett (1992)
\par
examine the statistical properties of the relationship between global mean temperature and
\par
greenhouse gas concentrations. Researchers also search for signs of climate change in ice core
\par
and tree ring data (for example, see Folland, Karl, and Vinnikov, 1990). Both types of learning
\par
are necessary to conclude the climate is changing. The laboratory offers a controlled environment
\par
which isolates the problem. Observation confirms that the problem remains in the uncontrolled
\par
environment. If a signal is clearly evident, a much stronger case can be made for controlling the
\par
problem.
\par
Uncertainty poses special challenges from the point of view of IAMs. IAMs are already
\par
typically complex, and \emph{explicitly} modeling uncertainty (i.e. the decision maker or planner makes
\par
climate change control decisions without perfect knowledge of some parameters or variables)
\par
adds significantly to the complexity since the decision maker must consider each possible
\par
resolution of uncertainty. Explicitly modeling learning also adds significant complexity.
\par
Because of the complexity, most IAMs treat uncertainty more simply via sensitivity analysis
\par
in various forms. The most common treatment is a type of Monte Carlo analysis known as
\par
10\emph{stochastic simulation} (Weyant, et. al. 1995). Suppose uncertainty exists over a vector of
\par
parameters. Stochastic simulation is a technique which simulates the model under various
\par
parameter values. First a random draw is made from a given distribution over the uncertain
\par
vector. Then the modeler finds the results of the IAM under certainty for the randomly drawn
\par
parameter vector. Then another vector is drawn and the modeler again computes the results of
\par
the IAM. After a large number of draws, the average result becomes a measure of how the IAM
\par
might perform under uncertainty. This method has a particular advantage in that several
\par
uncertainties considered together may have a compound effect on the IAM results. Models which
\par
do sensitivity analysis one parameter at a time under estimate the effect of uncertainty because
\par
such compound effects are in effect ignored.
\par
Under a special case known as certainty equivalence, stochastic simulation correctly gives
\par
the optimal action under uncertainty. Certainty equivalence occurs when the optimal action under
\par
uncertainty is equivalent to the expected value of the actions under each realization of the
\par
uncertain parameters with certainty. However, because of risk aversion certainty equivalence
\par
does not hold in climate change models. Furthermore, stochastic simulation offers no model of
\par
learning, or how uncertainty might be resolved over time. All uncertainty is (unrealistically)
\par
resolved immediately. Decisions cannot be made based on the results of learning and the
\par
relationship between learning and GHG control policies is unclear.
\par
The problems with stochastic simulation has led a few modelers to consider a model where
\par
the decisions are actually made under uncertainty, such as Peck, Chao, and Teisberg (1989). Due
\par
to the complexity of decision making under uncertainty, such IAMs consider only limited forms of
\par
uncertainty. Most of these models consider only discrete uncertainty with passive learning.
\par
Discrete uncertainty modeling usually consists of a single uncertain parameter, which can take on
\par
one of two possibilities with some probability. Uncertainty is resolved at a fixed time in the
\par
future, and hence learning is passive. Such models can compute the value of information, or the
\par
difference in discounted consumption between the model under certainty and under uncertainty.
\par
Most modelers find the value of information is small. This result is, however, sensitive to when
\par
uncertainty is resolved, which is itself uncertain (Peck, Chao, and Teisberg, 1989). Furthermore,
\par
11total climate change damages are themselves small relative to the gross world product,6 so it is not
\par
clear if the value of information is small relative to the total costs of climate change.
\par
Discrete uncertainty does not assume certainty equivalence and hence gives the optimal
\par
policy under uncertainty (although the assumption that a single parameter takes on only one two
\par
values understates the true uncertainty policy makers face). Further, discrete uncertainty allows
\par
information to change over time and hence gives an idea about how decisions relate to learning.
\par
However, because learning is passive, learning is unaffected by control policies. Kelly and
\par
Kolstad (forthcoming) model active learning by observation and determine that uncertainty is
\par
resolved after 90-160 years, far longer than assumed in most passive learning models. Further,
\par
when uncertainty is resolved is determined in part by GHG control policies. For example,
\par
restrictive GHG control policies such as those proposed by the IPCC slow learning considerably.
\par
Kelly and Kolstad (forthcoming) also find that optimal control policies are quite sensitive to
\par
learning, which gives an indication of the value of learning relative to total climate damages.
\par
\textbf{1.3. Review of Integrated Assessment Models}
\par
Table I gives a list of IAMs and how IAMs vary in the above dimensions. Of the 21 IAMs
\par
analyzed, ten are evaluation and 11 are optimization, suggesting no consensus exists on what type
\par
of IAM is best. Table I also shows that of the 21 IAMs analyzed, only one third allow any
\par
economic response to policy, and only five allow for more than one type of economic response.
\par
Those models that allow economic response to policy typically allow agents to vary the
\par
consumption/investment decision. This arises naturally out of the optimal growth framework,
\par
which all of these models use. Within the optimal growth model class, many IAMs have a
\par
production technology which specifies energy usage (either GHG intensive energy or non-GHG
\par
intensive energy) as an input. Hence producers may respond to GHG control policies by reducing
\par
GHG intensive energy usage, by either conservation by using more capital and labor or by using
\par
more non-GHG intensive energy. IAMs still have only very limited economic responses,
\par
6 For example, Nordhaus (1994) estimates a three degree Celcius tempertaure rise (which
\par
occurs long after uncertainty is resolved) reduces gross world product by 1.33\%. Mendelsohn,
\par
12however. For example, none of the models we have seen model adaptation or technological
\par
change endogenously.
\par
Table I also shows the differences in complexity across policy evaluation and policy
\par
optimization models. Our definition of complexity in each category is not very restrictive. The
\par
only requirement for a complex model of atmospheric chemistry is a separate model of at least
\par
two GHGs (for example CO2 and sulfur dioxide). A complex climate has an explicit model of
\par
more than two climate layers or regions (for example temperature change in northern and
\par
southern hemispheres or a seven layer climate model) or explicitly models more than one climate
\par
change effect (such as rainfall or sea level rise). A complex economic model has an explicit
\par
energy sector or a complex model of impacts across regions or industries. In spite of our
\par
relatively weak definition of complexity, no policy optimization model has a complex model of
\par
atmospheric chemistry, none have a detailed climate, and few have a detailed economy.
\par
Conversely, many policy evaluation models have both a complex atmospheric chemistry model
\par
and a complex climate model. Indeed, two of the ten policy evaluation models have a complex
\par
climate, chemistry, and economic model.
\par
Table I also shows the wide range of treatments of uncertainty. A majority (seven) use the
\par
stochastic simulation method described earlier, which allows multiple parameters to vary
\par
simultaneously from a joint distribution, but still assumes certainty equivalence. Five models
\par
allow for a limited form of actual decision making under uncertainty, where the uncertainty is
\par
discrete and resolved at a specific time in the future. Many models still have no formal treatment
\par
of uncertainty. Only one model allows for continuous decision making under uncertainty where
\par
resolution of uncertainty is endogenous and that model allows for only one uncertain parameter.
\par
\textbf{2. Critical Assumptions of IAM models.}
\par
et. al. (1996) estimates a 3 degree temperature rise to be approximately neutral on the United
\par
States economy.
\par
13Like all models, results from IAMs are driven by the critical assumptions of the models. A
\par
few of the assumptions of IAMs have received some attention in the literature, especially the
\par
debate over the appropriate rate of time discount. But for the most part, discussion of critical
\par
assumptions is noticeably lacking. However, a number of critical assumptions have been
\par
identified, which for the most part drive the results that are common to all IAM models.
\par
\textbf{2.1. Discounting}
\par
Perhaps the most often debated critical parameter is the rate of time discount (for example,
\par
see Arrow, et. al., 1996, Cline, 1992, and Nordhaus, 1994). Most of the benefits of climate
\par
change control (or the costs of climate change) are derived far in the future. Hence the more
\par
weight that is placed on the future, the more optimal climate change control rates rise. The
\par
discount rate is critical since small changes in the yearly discount rate compound over decades and
\par
centuries. Indeed in models which allow for economic response to policy, if a low rate of time
\par
discount is used, investment rates are higher and hence growth is higher and hence uncontrolled
\par
emissions are higher, which further increases the optimal control rates.
\par
In infinite horizon models such as IAMs, several parameters determine the rate of time
\par
discount. The first parameter is the pure rate of time preference. The pure rate of time
\par
preference is where IAMs differ. The second parameter is the weight assigned to future
\par
generations. Most IAM models weight future generations equal to the present generation (except
\par
through the pure rate of time preference). A final parameter which affects the rate of time
\par
discount is the economic growth rate, along with the concavity of the utility function. Future
\par
generations are inherently better off because per-capita economic growth is generally rising due to
\par
advances in technology. Because marginal utility is declining, benefits to more wealthy future
\par
generations are less socially beneficial than benefits given to the current generation. The social
\par
rate of time preference is thus equal to pure rate of time preference plus the the economic growth
\par
rate times the elasticity of marginal utility. IAMs range from Cline (1992), where the pure rate of
\par
time preference is set to zero (based on equity considerations), the economic growth rate is 1.6\%,
\par
and the elasticity of marginal utility is 1.5, which would give gives a social rate of time preference
\par
of about 2.4\%. Other models calibrate rates of time preference by observing real world interest
\par
14rates. Calibrated models find a pure rate of time preference as high as 5\%, which along with a
\par
1.5\% growth rate implies a social rate of time preference of 8\%. The same IAM model with these
\par
two discount rates yield quite different results, especially in the future. Cline (1992) finds an
\par
optimal control rate as high as 50\% by 2100, while Nordhaus finds an optimal control rate of only
\par
13\%. One problem with rates of time preference set via equity considerations is that the results of
\par
such models are difficult to sell to the developed world. In effect, such models say that the
\par
developed world must make sacrifices for the benefit of future generations in countries with high
\par
population growth rates such as China and India. Such models usually give investment rates far in
\par
excess of that which is observed, which leads to unrealistically high forecasts of uncontrolled
\par
emissions. However, calibrated models have been criticized based on the use of observed interest
\par
rates, which do not in general equal the pure rate of time preference because of distortions from
\par
non-optimal tax rates and because some environmental goods are not traded and not included in
\par
national accounts.
\par
An early related technical issue was the appropriate terminal period to use in numerical
\par
calculations. Cline (1992, pg. 309) argued that the inclusion of very long term time horizons (and
\par
thus long term warming) generated significantly higher control rates compared with other
\par
researchers such as Nordhaus. However, in fact researchers such as Nordhaus (1994) actually
\par
used very long time horizons of 600 years, but simply did not report control rates beyond the year
\par
2100 because the results were `` too conjectural.'' Indeed, Nordhaus (1991) shows clearly that the
\par
higher control rates obtained by Cline are due soley to the low rate of time preference. This is
\par
further supported by Nordhaus (1994), which shows that his model is not sensitive to the choice
\par
of terminal period (beyond 400 years), and by Kelly and Kolstad (forthcoming) who use an
\par
infinite horizon model and get similar results.
\par
\textbf{2.2. Economic Growth}
\par
The second most critical assumption is the assumed amount of economic growth in GHG
\par
intensive industries. Most IAMs tie GHG emissions to industrial production either implicitly
\par
through emissions projections, which are based on the growth in industrial production or
\par
explicitly through an actual model of industrial production. Economic growth results directly
\par
15from advances in technology and population growth, and from a high marginal product of capital
\par
in the developing world. As demonstrated in Kelly and Kolstad (1996), without growth in
\par
population and advances in technology, there will not be enough emissions to drive any climate
\par
change (much beyond that which has already occurred). Similarly, scenarios with high growth
\par
consistently find considerably more climate change and hence more spending on climate change
\par
control. This is readily apparent in the 1990s, where high growth around the world has produced
\par
higher than expected emissions of GHGs across the world. In fact, IAMs are usually quite
\par
pessimistic in their assumptions about the growth rate of production and hence emissions. A
\par
survey from Nordhaus and Yohe (1983) reveals that most IAMs assume a large slowdown in
\par
technology growth over the next century (primarily to make the problem computationally
\par
tractable). Similarly, most IAMs take population growth numbers from the United Nations
\par
(1994) or World Bank (1991) studies which predict zero population growth by the year 2200.
\par
Hence projected emissions growth also slows down and ends by the year 2200.
\par
\textbf{2.3. Economic Response to Control Policy}
\par
A third important assumption is when and where economic agents are able to respond to
\par
emission reduction policies. History is full of IAMs in other fields which made poor predictions,
\par
because the models did not allow for the response of economic agents to policy. For example,
\par
consider the limits to growth debate of the 1970s (see Meadows, 1972). These models made dire
\par
(and yet-to-be-realized) predictions about the effect of growth on the environment and natural
\par
resources, because they did not take into account the economic response of rising prices,
\par
substitution, and innovation to scarcity.
\par
By `` economic response'' we mean here an \emph{endogenous} response, that is an agent makes a
\par
decision optimally given a control policy. Many IAMs have \emph{exogenous} built-in responses by
\par
agents, such as an exogenous change in fuel usage at a specific energy price. However, an
\par
exogenous response cannot easily capture the complex relationships that go into an endogenous
\par
decision. For example, suppose an IAM modeler perfectly specifies (exogenously) usage of solar
\par
energy as the price of fossil fuels rise. Now suppose that sensitivity analysis is performed with a
\par
parameter change that results in higher economic growth. Then the relationship between the price
\par
16of fossil fuel and the usage of solar energy changes, because incomes and other factors which
\par
affect demand change. But since the response is exogenously fixed, the relationship is incorrect,
\par
even though the relationship between fuel usage and prices was designed perfectly for the base
\par
case.
\par
An IAM cannot possibly include all economic responses, because each possible type of
\par
economic response is really an extra decision variable, which adds significant complexity and
\par
computational cost. Therefore IAMs tend to differ in how and when (if at all) agents can respond
\par
to policy, which is an important factor in interpreting the results of IAMs. A variety of possible
\par
responses exist, from the adoption and/or development of cleaner energy technology to changes in
\par
consumption and investment.
\par
The most common type of economic response is the ability of agents to change investment
\par
decisions in the face of GHG control policies. For example, agents may compensate for an overly
\par
restrictive GHG control policy. Suppose the world economy is in equilibrium and a restrictive
\par
control policy is put in place (ie a control policy which is more restrictive than the optimal policy).
\par
A restrictive policy is paid out of current income, which invariably results in a reduction in both
\par
consumption and investment. Lower investment implies lower future capital stocks and hence
\par
lower uncontrolled emissions.
\par
Another common economic response in many IAMs is the ability of producers to reduce
\par
usage of GHG intensive fuels as the price rises. Producers may compensate for higher GHG
\par
intensive fuel prices by using non-GHG intensive fuels (ie natural gas, wind, or solar), or by using
\par
more capital and labor in the production process.
\par
Although a few IAMs allow for investment and energy responses, other economic responses
\par
are much more rare in IAMs. For example, economic agents clearly act to mitigate climate
\par
change damages and costs. Some IAMs allow for more convex damages when climate change is
\par
abrupt (implicitly assuming climate change is unexpected), but such a response is fixed and not
\par
endogenous. A more general model would allow for adaptation based on expected climate
\par
change.
\par
A final critical assumption is the degree of aggregation. Many models have but one or two
\par
regions of the world and find the optimal world GHG emissions level, but do not address how
\par
17countries would share the burden. Those models that do divide the world regionally are better
\par
able to assess the benefits and costs of climate change control across regions. However, such
\par
models must take into account the puzzle of designing enforceable international agreements. In
\par
an international agreement on climate change each country has a relatively small impact on the
\par
total world emissions, hence the incentive to cheat is strong.
\par
\textbf{2.4. Long Term Forecasting}
\par
How important is projecting/predicting the future accurately for integrated assessment?
\par
Some say that the purpose of an integrated assessment model is to pull together the diverse
\par
strands of understanding about a problem to generate the best prediction possible about what the
\par
future may bring or about the future effects of a specific policy. However, the quest for accuracy
\par
often breeds complexity. And complexity breeds opaqueness which for an IAM can mean
\par
uselessness.
\par
At the other end of the spectrum are the small models of climate change that seek to capture
\par
the essence of the climate change process without adding complexity. Nordhaus (1994) fits into
\par
this category. With such a simple structure, it is possible to communicate the model' s operation
\par
to others and for others to learn from its use. This is one reason why the model of Nordhaus
\par
(1994) has been used by many other analysts as a starting point. The problem with the more
\par
simplified approach is that simplified models may be too simple to be useful for policy-making.
\par
For instance, the original Nordhaus model aggregates the world into one large region and
\par
simulates the economy with three equations. This is the essence of economic modeling--- strip
\par
away unnecessary detail and focus on the core structure of the problem. We learn from this
\par
exercise but cannot place any `` faith'' in the numerical results that emerge from the model, at least
\par
as a basis for policy. Manne, Mendelsohn, and Richels (1993) are mid-way between simplicity
\par
and complexity in the Merge model and its progeny. Manne, Mendelsohn, and Richels (1993)
\par
have a structurally simple economic sector with significant detail on the energy sector. Despite
\par
this, some consider their model structure overly simple as a basis for policy.
\par
Thus tension between detail/realism and simplicity/transparency characterize IAMs. Finding
\par
the right balance between these two conflicting goals is difficult. Given the purpose of IAMs -- to
\par
18educate rather than predict -- our preference is to err on the side of transparency and simplicity, at
\par
the expense of precision and accuracy. That is the essence of abstraction -- to focus in on the core
\par
operation of a system and learn from the resulting simplified model.
\par
\textbf{3. Main Results of IAM Models.}
\par
IAMs share many common results and tend to agree more than disagree. The modeling
\par
community have also taken unprecedented steps to compare and contrast results in settings such
\par
as the Energy Modeling Forum (see for example EMF, 1993). This has even led to some ``model
\par
convergence,'' whereby initially diverse model predictions have converged over time. A number
\par
of significant results have come out of IAMs.
\par
Perhaps the most surprising result is the consensus that given calibrated interest rates and
\par
low future economic growth, modest controls are generally optimal. This was the key result of
\par
Nordhaus (1994), which has now been found by many modelers. Generally results are in the
\par
range of a 5-10\% reduction in emissions versus no control. This corresponds roughly to a tax of
\par
\$5-10 per ton of carbon. Hence most models find that only a modest slowing \emph{of the rate of}
\par
\emph{increase} in emissions is warranted. This contrasts visibly with proposed policy measures of the
\par
IPCC and the Protocol conference which are in the range of 5-10\% \emph{below 1990 levels}. Still, 5-
\par
10\% emissions control is significantly tighter than the current policy of no control.
\par
The result on modest controls comes primarily from the critical assumptions listed above.
\par
First, since economic growth is modest, especially in the next century, emissions growth is modest
\par
and therefore the need for GHG control is modest. The result is also sensitive to the assumptions
\par
in the discount rate. If a zero rate of time preference is used, control rates are substantially
\par
higher. Finally, substantial exogenous innovations in the energy sector are built into most IAMs.
\par
If the true economic response is such that improvements do not arrive, or arrive more quickly
\par
than expected, the results would differ substantially.
\par
The discordance between the results of IAMs and the currently proposed policy options
\par
raises important questions about the purpose of integrated assessment. IAMs seem to have
\par
19answered two major questions, the role of climate change in context of other problems and the
\par
assessment of optimal and other climate change control policies. However, the influence of these
\par
two major results on the policy debate is open to question. The Kyoto and IPCC policies are
\par
more restrictive than most IAMs. Perhaps policy makers are guided by political agendas or are
\par
unable to trust the `` black-box'' nature of IAM results (that is, in many IAMs it is not clear what
\par
assumptions drive the model). For example, the year 1990 is critical to politically-minded policy
\par
makers, but not to IAMs. In 1990 the former East Germany' s outdated factories were emitting
\par
very high levels of many pollutants. Thus to reduce emissions below 1990 levels, Germany needs
\par
only to replace the outdated factories, something that would have been done anyway. Modelers
\par
must take a more active role in the policy debate and bring the results to the forefront of the
\par
policy debate. Similarly, modelers must do more to explain what drives IAMs, which makes the
\par
results more understandable and trustworthy.
\par
A related area of study is the optimal timing of GHG control policies. One possible control
\par
strategy is to act now, and institute a control policy immediately. Acting now hedges against
\par
unknown, but possibly severe, future damages. A second option is to wait-and-see, deferring
\par
action until more is known about the problem. Several results have emerged in comparing these
\par
two policies. First, IAMs which calculate the optimal GHG control level generally find some
\par
small immediate reduction in emissions is warranted. However, the cost of delaying control for a
\par
decade or two is quite small and the heaviest reductions are in the later years (see for example
\par
Richels and Edmonds, 1995 and Manne and Richels, 1992 and 1993). These results tend to
\par
advocate acting now, but again in a modest way. Results by Kelly et al (1998) also show little
\par
resolution in uncertainty in the next decade, which make the informational gains from waiting
\par
small. Finally, such results are sensitive to assumptions about technological innovation and the
\par
speed at which new capital is adopted.
\par
As we have noted, from the early debates between Cline (1992) and Nordhaus (1994), most
\par
IAMs find that one critical parameter is the discount rate. This has generated some research in
\par
the environmental economics area which tries to better pin down the appropriate discount rate
\par
(see for example, Laibson, 1996, Lind, 1994, Manne, 1996, Schelling, 1995, Weitzman, 1994, and
\par
Weitzman, 1997). For example, Weitzman (1997) shows that if uncertainty over the rate of
\par
20return to capital in the distant future exists, then the lowest possible interest rate (and hence the
\par
discount rate which is closest to one) that occurs with positive probability should be used for
\par
discounting. This occurs because eventually the present value of all other possible outcomes
\par
becomes small relative to the lowest-interest rate outcome. Manne (1996) compares the
\par
overlapping generations framework with the more standard infinitely lived agent framework. The
\par
overlapping generations model allows for more transparent modeling of bequests and other
\par
important aspects of long term discounting. Manne (1996) finds that there is an overlapping
\par
generations model and an infinite horizon model yield similar results when the consumption (not
\par
utility) discount rates are equal. Laibson (1996) considers hyperbolic discount rates (the
\par
psychology literature gives some evidence of hyperbolic discounting, see Laibson, 1996), which
\par
leads to under saving, and hence in an IAM would lead to under-investment in GHG control.
\par
Another useful result is the evaluation of proposed policy scenarios, such as the IPCC
\par
scenarios. The IPCC (Watson, et. al. 1990) has proposed 5 scenarios for GHG emissions over
\par
the next century (including scenario ``A'', which corresponds to business-as-usual). These
\par
scenarios differ quite widely: The business-as-usual case results in a CO2 concentration nearly
\par
twice as high as the most aggressive control scenario by 2100. Other proposed policies focus on
\par
stabilizing emissions at 0-10\% below 1990 levels at some point in the future.
\par
IAMs show that such policies result in drastically different outcomes. In Nordhaus (1994)
\par
the present discounted difference between the optimal policy and immediate stabilization at 1990
\par
emissions levels is about 12 trillion 1989 dollars. Manne and Richels (1997) examine a variety of
\par
emissions scenarios and evaluate the economic costs. Costs can range from as little as \$.5 trillion
\par
for the least-cost trajectory to as high as \$8-9 trillion.
\par
\textbf{4. Directions for Research.}
\par
Most integrated assessment models are now quite complicated, with explicit models of many
\par
features of the climate and economic systems. Still, a number of under-studied areas deserve
\par
attention. Many of these areas are now currently under study.
\par
21\textbf{4.1. Endogenous Technical Change}
\par
An elusive, but essential, aspect of the climate change problem is technical change.
\par
Technical change may be both emissions increasing: improvements in productivity leads to
\par
economic growth, or emissions decreasing: improvements primarily in the energy sector
\par
(improved energy efficiency or improvements in non-GHG intensive fuels) lead to less emissions
\par
per unit of production or consumption. Productivity-enhancing technical change in the 1990s has
\par
led to economic growth, which has substantially increased emissions. Estimates for economic
\par
growth in most models are quite low relative to historical observations. Still, most models
\par
recognize the importance of this kind of technical change and at least consider high growth in
\par
sensitivity analysis.
\par
Technical change in the energy sector is more problematic for IAMs. Technical change has
\par
the possibility of being critically important. For example, innovations such as inexpensive electric
\par
automobiles or solar power has the potential to significantly change the threat of climate change
\par
without any regulation. Since climate change is a long run problem, innovations have plenty of
\par
time to penetrate the market. Of course such innovations are unpredictable, but at least some
\par
innovation seems quite likely.
\par
Previously, modeling of technical change has been exogenous. One way to get an
\par
exogenous estimate of the degree of technical change is to examine the historical record. The
\par
CO2 emissions intensity of output has fallen world-wide from 0.409 tons of carbon per thousand
\par
dollars of output in 1929 to 0.232 tons/\$1000 in 1989 (Nordhaus, 1994). This includes
\par
substantial increases in the intensity of output in the former USSR and China. Nordhaus
\par
calculates an improvement in emissions intensity of about 1-1.5\% per year. Some IAMs simply
\par
assume that further improvements occur at a similar, but declining rate of change as that observed
\par
in the past.
\par
The other way to get an exogenous estimate of technical change is to build a model of
\par
energy usage where shifts in energy technology occur at specific energy prices. As energy prices
\par
rise from regulation, usage of GHG-emitting energy declines. Such models also often have a
\par
`` back-stop'' technology whereby at a certain energy price a GHG-free energy source becomes
\par
cost-efficient. Manne, Mendelsohn, and Richels (1993) have a particularly detailed model of the
\par
22energy sector. The model has an exogenous rate of improvement in emissions intensity of about
\par
0.5\% per year. Further, capital, labor, and energy form a constant returns to scale, constant
\par
elasticity of substitution production function. Hence producers may substitute some capital and
\par
labor for energy if energy costs are high. Producers may choose between many different energy
\par
technologies which have different costs and GHG emissions properties. Some carbon-free
\par
technologies become feasible only at specified dates in the future. In spite of the complexity of
\par
the model, innovation is exogenous. The set of alternative technologies is fixed and exogenous
\par
and becomes available at fixed and exogenous times and prices.
\par
Such models do not take into account endogenous innovation. As prices rise and regulation
\par
becomes more stringent, firms may spend more on innovation to attempt to reduce emissions
\par
costs. In fact, one of the more attractive features of emissions taxes and tradable permits is that
\par
such regulation instruments spur innovation. Indeed Goulder and Schneider (1996) find
\par
theoretically that the costs of achieving a specific abatement target falls when endogenous
\par
technical change is included. However, researchers are still struggling with numerous aspects of
\par
endogenous technical change. First, considerable uncertainty exists over `` knowledge spillovers,''
\par
or the degree to which other energy producing firms can use innovations from another firm.
\par
Another important distinction is the degree of previous distortion in the R\&D market. Since
\par
R\&D is a positive externality research should be supported via subsidies or patent rights.
\par
However, it is unclear how close we are to the optimal level of R\&D spending. Further, other
\par
forms of innovation, such as learning-by-doing, have yet to be examined within the climate change
\par
framework.
\par
\textbf{4.2. Specifying Regulation Instruments}
\par
As described in Fisher et. al. (1996), policy makers have a wide variety of regulation
\par
instruments to use for climate change control. These include well-known regulatory instruments
\par
of taxes, command-and-control methods, and tradable permits. Many complimentary regulation
\par
instruments also control climate change: R\&D subsidies, population control, taxes on gasoline,
\par
subsidizing energy which is not GHG intensive, subsidizing adaptation (by encouraging
\par
replacement of outdated equipment), and subsidizing reforestation all can potentially reduce GHG
\par
23emissions. IAMs, on the other hand, generally consider only one of two policy instruments. Most
\par
IAMs calculate only the optimal or prescribed GHG emissions target, and then convert the
\par
optimal emissions target into an optimal tax on carbon, by calculating the tax needed to achieve
\par
the specified emissions. Other IAMs model the tax on carbon directly, then construct a social
\par
planning problem. Such models miss important features of many regulation instruments, however.
\par
Consider first revenue recycling provided by taxes and permits which are sold. In many
\par
proposed policies by the IPCC, carbon taxes will likely be quite high, leading to significant
\par
revenues which can be used to offset distortionary income taxes. Even in models which compute
\par
the optimal tax, (although probably not fully, according to Goulder, 1995). Revenue recycling
\par
has yet to be included in most IAMs.
\par
Another challenge is creating a regulation instrument which is usable internationally. Since
\par
each country is sovereign, therefore incentives for each country not to comply with optimal GHG
\par
reduction numbers are strong. Within a country, a carbon tax (unlike complementary regulation
\par
instruments such as gasoline taxes) is difficult to collect and monitor. Carbon dioxide emission
\par
occur both through gasoline consumption and through electricity production, as well as a host of
\par
other means. Each emissions source would likely require a different taxation and monitoring
\par
scheme. Hence any regulation instrument should include monitoring and penalties for non-
\par
compliance, which increase the costs of regulation.
\par
Finally, inefficient regulations can increase adaptation costs. For example, farm subsidies
\par
and subsidies to flood-prone areas add significantly to the adaptation costs of climate change. In
\par
general, by examining only the optimal or proposed emission targets, IAMs miss important costs
\par
and benefits of climate change regulation. Further, incorporation of actual policies improves the
\par
usefulness of IAMs for policy making, as such a model gives not only a target, but a way of
\par
achieving the target.
\par
\textbf{4.3. Adjustment Costs and Damages}
\par
Perhaps the most important, but not yet modeled, economic response is adaptation. Agents
\par
within the economy respond not only to policies, but to climate change itself, irrespective of the
\par
existence of regulation. For example, given changes in temperature and rainfall, farmers modify
\par
24input decisions such as irrigation and crop choice in order to lessen the impact of climate change.
\par
Such adaptation occurs regardless of the climate change policy.
\par
Of course for adaptation to occur, economic agents must know exactly how the climate has
\par
changed. If climate change is uncertain (as it now is) adaptation is more difficult. In a world of
\par
imperfect information about climate change, damages are greater due to imperfect adaptation.
\par
Many studies which estimate the damage from climate change assume complete information
\par
and perfect adaptation. Such models find relatively little damage from climate change
\par
(Mendelsohn, et. al., 1994). Most of the damage occurs on the adjustment path. This could be
\par
incorporated into an IAM by making damages greater under uncertainty. When uncertainty is
\par
resolved, damages are smaller. Further, most damage studies are done in the developed world,
\par
and the developing world is less able to adapt to climate change. Hence damage studies need to
\par
be expanded, and the results need to be incorporated into IAMs.
\par
While sensitivity analysis gives important insights into the behavior of IAMs under
\par
alternative parameter specifications, sensitivity analysis falls well short of modeling optimal
\par
behavior under uncertainty. Direct incorporation of uncertainty goes a long way toward making
\par
the results more useful for policy-makers.
\par
\textbf{5. Conclusions}
\par
In the climate change literature, integrated assessment models have a relatively short history
\par
of less than a decade, yet a surprising amount of knowledge has emerged. Before the first IAM
\par
introduced by Nordhaus (1991), there was no clear way to combine the immense amounts of
\par
information on climate change. Hence, researchers and policy makers had little information about
\par
the expected costs, damages, and effects of climate change were. IAMs have now sucessfully
\par
integrated scientific information on climate change, and ordered together the various components
\par
of climate change. IAMs then pointed out the expected costs and benefits of climate change
\par
control, and the results surprised many climate change researchers and policy makers.
\par
25Probably the most striking result is that our current understanding of climate change costs,
\par
damages, etc. does not justify more than modest emissions control given a discount rate calibrated
\par
from interest rates and slow economic growth. The optimal amount of emissions control is well
\par
below current proposed policies, yet more than the current policy of doing nothing. The result
\par
holds up surprisingly well to sensitivity analysis with respect to many uncertainties. However,
\par
IAMs do show the sensitivity of emissions policy to the treatment of future generations through
\par
the discount rate and to estimates of economic growth. Other results include that information
\par
acquisition makes little difference to current period levels of emissions control, and the cost of
\par
following other proposed emissions policies.
\par
Many challenges still remain, but the above conclusions are unlikely to be altered. A better,
\par
endogenous model of technical change is essential. Further, most models allow for an overly
\par
limited set of responses on the part of consumers and producers in the model, especially in
\par
modeling adjustment costs and damages. Most models still rely on certainty equivalence, which
\par
does not hold, in uncertainty analysis. However, preliminary work indicates the above results on
\par
modest controls and sensitivity to the discount rate are not altered when endogenous technical
\par
change or actual decision making under uncertainty are considered. Finally, recent work on
\par
calibrating the damage from climate change needs to be expanded and incorporated into IAMs,
\par
which now use expert surveys or best guesses.
\par
The integrated assessment community has done an excellent job of analyzing, comparing,
\par
and contrasting the multitude of IAMs. Because of the analysis, IAMs give a remarkably
\par
consistent message. However, despite the consistent message and the large amount of
\par
government research money which has been spent, the message is not known far outside the
\par
integrated assessment community. The integrated assessment community must still do more to
\par
bring the results to the forefront of the debate on what to do about climate change.
