@article{limJudgmentalForecastingInteractive1996,
  title = {Judgmental Forecasting with Interactive Forecasting Support Systems},
  author = {Lim, J.S. and O'Connor, M.},
  year = 1996,
  journal = {Decision Support Systems},
  volume = {16},
  number = {4},
  pages = {NaN-NaN},
  doi = {10.1016/0167-9236(95)00009-7},
  langid = {english},
  note = {\textbf{\emph{Doeaslo" n Support}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{8y .m}}
\par
E L S E V I E R Decision Support Systems 16 (1996) 339-357
\par
\textbf{Judgmental forecasting with interactive forecasting support}
\par
\textbf{systems}
\par
J o a S a n g L i m , M a r c u s O ' C o n n o r *
\par
\emph{School of lnformation Systems, The University of New South Wales, P.O. Box 1, Kensington, NSW 2033, Australia}
\par
\textbf{Abstract}
\par
With an increasing use of DSS/EIS, managers are often required to process information coming from a variety
\par
of sources in making a final decision. However, we have little understanding of the efficiency with which people
\par
select and use the multiple pieces of information. This issue was examined under various conditions usii'\textasciitilde g a DSS in a
\par
forecasting task where multiple items of information were displayed on request in an interactive manner. Results
\par
indicate that overall people underacquired information. Moreover, people often selected less-reliable information.
\par
This sub-optimal beh\textasciitilde viour did not diminish over time (it became worse). But an aggregation DSS was helpful at the
\par
task. This suggests that people seemed to have a problem in aggregating multiple pieces of information. It was also
\par
found that the independent preparation of an initial forecast improved forecast accuracy significantly. Perhaps,
\par
forecasters may prepare the initial forecast independently and use decision aids for the subsequent tasks of the
\par
forecasting process.
\par
\emph{Keywords: }Forecasting support systems; Judgmental forecasting; Information acquisition and utilisation
\par
\textbf{I. Introduction}
\par
The use of DSS and EIS has increased dramat-
\par
ically since their inception ([47]). The focus is on
\par
the provision of information for subsequent man-
\par
agement judgment. Typically, such systems are
\par
designed to permit access to as much information
\par
that is possible within the constraints of rele-
\par
vance, security and availability. The overall de-
\par
sign philosophy is that managerial judgment is
\par
enhanced by the provision of relevant and timely
\par
information and by the use of statistical support,
\par
where appropriate. Of course, such systems pre-
\par
sume that the problem of information overload
\par
will not occur, since users are able to self regulate
\par
the amount they select and use. Some studies
\par
have questioned the efficacy of this assumption
\par
([29]). Another assumption of such systems is that
\par
people are able to judiciously select the informa-
\par
tion they wish to use. In other words, people are
\par
presumed to adopt a rational cost/benefit model
\par
in their information selection. Recognising that
\par
information comes to them at a cost (in both its
\par
use and its provision), they will only select infor-
\par
mation where the marginal benefit is greater than
\par
its marginal cost. Whilst there have been a num-
\par
ber of studies in psychology that have investi-
\par
gated this issue (see [14] for a review), few studies
\par
* Corresponding author. Fax: (612) 662-4061.
\par
0167-9236/96/\$15.00 \copyright{} 1996 Elsevier Science B.V. All rights reserved
\par
\emph{SSDI }0167-9236(95)00009-7340 \emph{J.S. Lim, M. O'Connor/Decision Support Systems 16 (1996) 339-357}
\par
have examined the way people using D S S / EIS
\par
make choices in the information they wish to use.
\par
This study addresses this issue for a forecasting
\par
task. A third issue concerns the integration of
\par
information from a variety of sources. Since EIS
\par
are predicated on the need for human judgment,
\par
can people effectively integrate multiple pieces of
\par
information from a variety of sources? Again, this
\par
issue has received considerable investigation in
\par
psychology (e.g., [49]), with an overwhelming con-
\par
clusion that people have great problems in infor-
\par
mation integration. This study examines the issue
\par
of information selection and integration from a
\par
Forecasting Support Systems (a DSS designed to
\par
support the forecasting task).
\par
The forecasting task has been chosen for a
\par
number of reasons. First, it is one of the most
\par
pervasive tasks faced by middle management, of-
\par
ten the target of D S S / E I S design ([18]). Second,
\par
it is one task that is amenable to D S S / EIS
\par
support. The forecasting task ([54]) often begins
\par
with a consideration of past data (the time series),
\par
the consideration of statistical time series fore-
\par
casts, and concludes with the integration of im-
\par
portant non-time series information into the final
\par
estimate ([24]). All such components can be effec-
\par
tively supported by a D S S / EIS. Finally, as oth-
\par
ers have demonstrated ([13]), the forecasting task
\par
provides an ideal environment for studying the
\par
impact of information systems design on organi-
\par
sational behaviour.
\par
This paper reports a study of the way people
\par
select and use items of information and statistical
\par
support that are provided by a DSS in a time
\par
series forecasting task. It also examines the way
\par
people learn to change their behaviour over time
\par
as they use the DSS. Investigation of this issue
\par
may identify possible biases involved and provide
\par
some useful guidelines in the design of an inter-
\par
active D S S / E I S for time series judgmental fore-
\par
casting (e.g., [91,51]).
\par
\textbf{2. Prior literature on information purchase}
\par
An understanding of information acquisition
\par
behaviour is useful in the design of D S S / EIS
\par
(e.g., [71]) and in general requirement analysis
\par
specification. Two fundamental issues of most
\par
investigators in this field include (1) what infor-
\par
mation decision makers attend to (i.e., informa-
\par
tion acquisition) and (2) how they utilise the
\par
information accessed (i.e., information utilisa-
\par
tion). Empirical studies with respect to these two
\par
issues are subsequently reviewed.
\par
\emph{2.1. Information acquisition}
\par
Forecasters in the use of D S S / E I S very often
\par
require integrating their own opinion with multi-
\par
ple items of information coming from different
\par
sources: (1)judgmental reports from subordinates
\par
in other sales branches, (2) statistical forecasts
\par
and (3) causal information from a variety of often
\par
conflicting sources. An underlying cognitive con-
\par
trol mechanism in information acquisition is a
\par
cost and benefit consideration associated with
\par
information ([83]). The concept of information
\par
value has received considerable attention in a
\par
number of schools - accounting ([30,69]), infor-
\par
mation economics ([41,11,83]) and psychology
\par
([25]). Among others, a \emph{Bayesian }model was de-
\par
veloped by Edwards ([25]) in a binary \emph{optional}
\par
\emph{stopping }task. This model has some unrealistic
\par
assumptions to apply to time series forecasting,
\par
which include (1) no limit in resources, (2) the
\par
computational capability of a decision maker and
\par
(3) the binary nature of a decision. Later, a
\par
regression model was proposed based on the
\par
Brunswik's ([8]) \emph{Lens }model and subsequently
\par
used by Connolly and his associates (e.g., [15]).
\par
Unlike the \emph{Bayesian }model, the regression model
\par
does not necessarily require the decision maker
\par
to conduct sequential computations of the costs
\par
and benefits associated with buying additional
\par
piece of information. In this model, an optimal
\par
decision maker should minimise total costs, being
\par
the sum of monetary penalty for wrong decisions
\par
and information costs (see [15] for mathematical
\par
details). Thus, a rational decision maker should
\par
not purchase any information if its marginal cost
\par
outweighs the expected monetary penalty without
\par
it (i.e., maximisation of payoffs).
\par
A considerable degree of sub-optimal be-
\par
haviour in information acquisition compared to
\par
the optimal rule discussed above has been persis-\emph{J.S. Lim, M. O'Connor\textasciitilde Decision Support Systems 16 (1996) 339-357 }341
\par
tently shown in most empirical studies. Some
\par
([40,56]) found a tendency of people to \emph{overpur-}
\par
\emph{chase }more than suggested by the normative
\par
model. On the other hand, others ([46,78]) claimed
\par
the opposite \emph{underpurchase. }Indeed, there are
\par
numerous biases that account for sub-optimal
\par
behaviour in information acquisition ([14]). The
\par
analysis presented in Table 1 reveals that people
\par
are often insufficiently responsive to the \emph{norma-}
\par
\emph{tive }factors that must be considered in making
\par
purchase decisions ([25,76]), i.e., costs, payoffs,
\par
diagnosticity, prior probability and reliability
\par
([92]). A considerable body of evidence suggests
\par
that information acquisition appears to be in the
\par
theoretically appropriate direction, but people are
\par
only partially sensitive to the information ([101]).
\par
People also make wrong purchase decisions un-
\par
der the influence of the optimally irrelevant fac-
\par
tors, by which they should not be affected (e.g.,
\par
framing). Whilst there are some individual differ-
\par
ences due to cognitive styles ([22]), this suggests
\par
some serious flawed mechanism in cognitive
\par
cost-benefit calculations as shown in the psycho-
\par
logical literature (e.g., [4]). Surprisingly enough,
\par
however, this sub-optimality was not easily miti-
\par
gated by learning. The main reason suggested
\par
may be \emph{bounded rationality }([90]), i.e., people do
\par
not gather an optimal amount of information but
\par
purchase an amount that permits them to satis-
\par
rice. This issue has significant implications to the
\par
