\emph{3.2. Hypotheses about information reliability}
\par
\emph{Impact on information acquisition: }In an infor-
\par
mation purchase task, reliability can be manipu-
\par
lated as either a \emph{between- }(e.g., a set of equally
\par
reliable information) or a \emph{within }factor (e.g., a
\par
mix of high and low reliability information) ([55]).
\par
In the \emph{between-reliability }context, less amount of
\par
information is generally acquired in the high-
\par
than in the low-reliability condition ([55,16]). In
\par
the other \emph{within-reliability }context, more reliable
\par
information is prefe:rred but there appears to be
\par
considerable mispurchase of less reliable infor-
\par
mation ([46,16]). A null hypothesis is formulated
\par
to examine some biases involved in information
\par
seeking strategies such as mis-acquisition and
\par
sub-optimality as follows:
\par
\textbf{H2a: }There is no difference in information
\par
acquisition behaviour between the \emph{high-reliability}
\par
and the \emph{low-reliability }conditions.
\par
\emph{Impact on information utilisation: }It may be
\par
apparent that the acquisition of high-reliable in-
\par
formation would contribute to decision perfor-
\par
mance if decision makers are capable of appro-
\par
priately taking advantage of the acquired infor-
\par
mation. Whilst only a few studies examined this
\par
issue in an information purchase context, this
\par
expectation was upheld by Levine and Samet
\par
([55]). They found that accuracy was in an ex-
\par
pected order of relizbility: that is, the accuracy of
\par
343
\par
the high-reliability group was significantly greater
\par
than that of the low-reliability group (p {$<$} 0.01).
\par
This suggests that "subjects sought to identify a
\par
high-reliable source and then took advantage of
\par
information received from it" (p. 417). The deci-
\par
sion accuracy was, however, generally low, less
\par
than half correct (37\% to 46\% correct). In sup-
\par
port of Levine and Samet ([55]), Lim and O'Con-
\par
nor ([59]) found that whilst people were far from
\par
optimal, they appeared to be able to take advan-
\par
tage of reliable information. We will vary the
\par
reliability of the information cues and examine its
\par
impact on forecast performance. The null hypoth-
\par
esis is formulated:
\par
H2b: There is no difference in forecast perfor-
\par
mance between the \emph{low-reliability }group and the
\par
\emph{high-reliability }group.
