\emph{3.3. Hypotheses about learning}
\par
\emph{Impact on information acquisition: }A prime
\par
concern of learning in information acquisition is
\par
whether people could discern the information
\par
value and learn over trials to acquire information
\par
where its value outweighs its costs, given multiple
\par
pieces of information with varying accuracy. The
\par
empirical evidence generally suggests that people
\par
are not rapid learners about optimal purchasing
\par
([52]). The learning, if any, was generally slow
\par
([17]). Perhaps, it is due to the complexity of the
\par
task itself where people need to exercise more
\par
cognitive efforts. In stressing this point, Connolly
\par
and Gilani ([15]), p. 345 stated "given the com-
\par
plexity of the (information purchase) task .... it is
\par
perhaps surprising that any learning was demon-
\par
strated." On the other hand, some ([52]) found
\par
that people appeared to learn slowly only for the
\par
highly-reliable information. The learning condi-
\par
tional on information reliability has also been
\par
suggested in Connolly and Serre ([16]). This mixed
\par
evidence leads us to a null hypothesis that:
\par
H3\textasciitilde : People do not learn over trials to acquire
\par
the optimal amount of information.
\par
\emph{Impact on information utilisation: }Improvement
\par
in decision performance over trials in an informa-344 \emph{J.S. Lim, M. O'Connor\textasciitilde Decision Support Systems 16 (1996) 339-357}
\par
tion purchase task is closely related to people's
\par
ability at the prior stage (i.e., information acquisi-
\par
tion). They would most likely improve perfor-
\par
mance if they are able to select good information
\par
as much as they can process. However, most
\par
empirical studies suggest that people are not nor-
\par
mative in purchasing good information. Lim and
\par
O'Connor ([59]) also showed that people did not
\par
appropriately discern good information. Thus, the
\par
evidence on the improvement of decision perfor-
\par
mance over trials is equivocal. A pessimistic find-
\par
ing was reported by Wallsten ([100]) who had
\par
students make a decision about a dominating
\par
colour based upon their observations of a series
\par
of light flashes. They could buy as many observa-
\par
tions as they wished before making a final deci-
\par
sion. There was "virtually no change from block 1
\par
to block 5" (p. 245) in stopping probabilities,
\par
though the experiment was practised over three
\par
days. On the other hand, a contrasting finding
\par
was reported by Connolly and Gilani ([15]) who
\par
found significant improvement in accuracy over
\par
time in an MCPL (Multiple Cue Probability
\par
Learning) task. We wish to determine whether
\par
people learn over time to improve their acquisi-
\par
tion and use of information cues. The mixed
\par
evidence leads to a null hypothesis formulated as:
\par
H3b: There is no improvement in forecast per-
\par
formance over trials.
\par
\emph{3.4. Hypotheses about decision support}
\par
\emph{Impact on information acquisition: }Information
\par
overload ([68]) may be a problem which time
\par
series forecasters often face in real business set-
\par
tings. It is expected that as information load
\par
increases, they might try to reduce mental efforts
\par
and thus, more frequently use an aggregation
\par
DSS. Few studies have addressed this issue at an
\par
information purchase task. Recently, Todd and
\par
Benbasat ([97]) showed that those with a DSS did
\par
not attend to more information than those with-
\par
out it. This behaviour seems contrary to intuition.
\par
They attributed this finding to a cost and benefit
\par
mechanism ([4]), i.e., people did not necessarily
\par
use more information due to its increased mental
\par
efforts, despite the easier interface for informa-
\par
tion access offered by the DSS. We wish to exam-
\par
ine this issue in a time series context. A null
\par
hypothesis is that:
\par
H4a-" There is no difference in the frequency of
\par
using DSS, regardless of the amount of informa-
\par
tion acquired.
\par
\emph{Impact on information utilisation: }The claim
\par
made by researchers ([47,94]) concerning the pos-
\par
itive impact of DSS on decision quality has not
\par
been well borne out ([89]). Empirical evidence is
\par
limited and mixed in information seeking task
\par
settings: Connolly and Thorn ([17]) in favour of
\par
decision aids and Lewis et al. ([57]), p. 272 claim-
\par
ing them as "superfluous". Lewis et al. ([57])
\par
found that even if people tended to employ the
\par
\emph{representativeness }heuristic ([99]), there was no
\par
significant deviation in accuracy between sub-
\par
jects' decision and the model of a man based on
\par
the same cues as attended by the subjects. Me-
\par
chanical combination has been, however, strongly
\par
endorsed by most researchers in judgmental psy-
\par
chology ([27,19]) and forecasting ([53]). Connolly
\par
and Thorn ([17]) supported this claim at an infor-
\par
mation purchase task by demonstrating greater
\par
benefits due to the \emph{black-box }decision aids which
\par
enabled both weighting and aggregation of infor-
\par
mation selected by people. Overall the decision
\par
aid substantially improved decision performance
\par
by 49\% (p {$<$} 0.05).
\par
The aforementioned two studies did not di-
\par
rectly examine the ability of people to attach
\par
subjective weighting for the information they se-
\par
lected. It is, however, an important issue which
\par
should not be neglected in the provision of an
\par
aggregation DSS. Lim and O'Connor ([59]) ques-
\par
tioned if people can perform cue weighting well
\par
in a single cue task. In forecasting, there has also
\par
been considerable debate between equal and un-
\par
equal weighting in combining multiple forecasts.
\par
Some advocated equal weighting whereas others
\par
did not ([12]). Using the framework of the \emph{Lens}
\par
model ([8]), some research has been devoted to
\par
the comparison of subjective weights to statistical
\par
weights and the results have also been equivocal
\par
([81]). Unlike Connolly and Thorn ([17]), we de-
\par
signed a DSS which allows mechanical aggrega-\emph{J.S. Lim, M. O'Connor/Decision Support Systems 16 (1996) 339-357 }tion of information based on subjective weighting.
\par
Thus, any difference, in judgmental forecast per-
\par
formance could be attributed to a problem of
\par
aggregation. Howeve r, equivocal findings ([88,73])
\par
on the ability of people to accurately indicate cue
\par
weights lead to a null hypothesis on the utility of
\par
this DSS as:
\par
H4b I There is no difference in forecast perfor-
\par
mance between the DSS group and the no DSS
\par
group.
