\textbf{5. Results}
\par
\emph{5.1. Effect of initial forecasts}
\par
\textbf{Hla: Impact on information acquisition: }The
\par
first hypothesis examines whether a reluctance to
\par
update the initial forecast would lead to reduced
\par
information acquisition for the \emph{initial forecast,}
\par
compared to the \emph{no-initial forecast }group. As
\par
expected, the \emph{no-initial }group acquired more in-
\par
formation (0.92 pieces) than the \emph{initial forecast}
\par
group (0.85 pieces). Those in the \emph{initial forecast}
\par
condition appeared to acquire none or one piece
\par
of information more frequently. On the other
\par
hand, those in the \emph{no-initial forecast }condition
\par
appeared to prefer to attend causal information,
\par
particularly temperature and visitor information
\par
in combination. However, the results of
\par
MANOVA (reproduced in the Appendix) indi-
\par
cated that this difference did not reach signifi-
\par
cance. That is, neither the main (p = 0.507) nor
\par
any interaction effect (p = 0.571) was found to be
\par
significant for the factor \emph{initial forecast }regarding
\par
information acquisition. Thus, H l a is accepted
\par
and there was no significant difference in infor-
\par
mation seeking between the two groups.
\par
\textbf{Hlb: Impact on information utilisation: }The
\par
second hypothesis was to test the claim by some
\par
forecasting researchers that the independent
\par
preparation of an initial forecast would improve
\par
decision performance. MANOVA revealed that
\par
there was no significant difference in forecast
\par
performance between the \emph{initial forecast }and the
\par
\emph{no-initial forecast }group (p = 0.153). It should be,
\par
however, noted that the interactive nature of
\par
\emph{Greviser }allowed people to finalise their forecasts
\par
at any stage of the task even without seeing any
\par
information. In this case, there existed practically
\par
no experimental difference between the two
\par
groups. The forecast performance would be af-
\par
fected by having the initial forecast only if sub-
\par
jects acquired any information. Thus, separate
\par
analyses were made for the two groups, cate-
\par
gorised into (1) those who acquired none and (2)
\par
those who acquired at least one item of informa-
\par
tion. In the first case where no information was
\par
acquired, the \emph{no-initial }(8.48) and the \emph{initial fore-}
\par
\emph{cast }group (8.65) did not differ in forecast accu-348 \emph{J.S. Lim, M. O'Connor\textasciitilde Decision Support Systems 16 (1996) 339-357}
\par
racy (p = 0.732). However, in the other case where
\par
more than a piece of information was acquired
\par
the separate preparation of an initial forecast
\par
improved forecast accuracy (see Table 2). That is,
\par
those who made an initial forecast before seeing
\par
any information (7.19) were significantly more
\par
accurate than those without it (8.06) (t = 2.08,
\par
p = 0.038). Whilst this superior performance of
\par
the \emph{initial forecast }group over the \emph{no-initial fore-}
\par
\emph{cast }group was also observed in both IMPbest and
\par
charges, they did not reach significance (p {$>$} 0.1
\par
for both). This evidence leads us to reject only
\par
weakly H lb. However, the independent prepara-
\par
tion of an initial forecast appeared to be gener-
\par
ally helpful.
