\emph{5.3. Learning}
\par
\textbf{H3a: Learning in information acquisition: Re-}
\par
\textbf{member, }the subjects performed a total of 21
\par
trials which then, were divided into three blocks
\par
to see any learning effect. A prime interest here
\par
was if people learnt to acquire the optimal amount
\par
of information over trials. The results of
\par
MANOVA suggest a significant effect of \emph{block}
\par
on the number of information items acquired
\par
(F = 2.294, p = 0.074). Follow-up ANOVAs
\par
showed a significant linear pattern over the three
\par
blocks for the number of items they acquired
\par
(F = 5.641, p = 0.021). But, an examination of the
\par
means for each block revealed the pattern was350 \emph{J.S. Lira, M. O'Connor/Decision Support Systems 16 (1996) 339-357}
\par
downwards (see Table 5). The same learning pat-
\par
tern was revealed for both reliability conditions.
\par
A good learner would be capable of changing
\par
his/her acquisition strategy according to the costs
\par
and benefits of information. Over trials, (s)he
\par
would increase acquiring information, where its
\par
benefits outweigh its costs, but decrease acquiring
\par
information with no/little marginal benefits.
\par
Thus, the number of information items they ac-
\par
quired would approach over trials the optimal
\par
number, set at two and one respectively for the
\par
high- and the low-reliability condition. People
\par
were, however, deficient in weighing information
\par
value against its costs. They were far from opti-
\par
mal even at the last block. In fact, information
\par
acquisition strategy of both groups rather wors-
\par
ened significantly. Thus, n 3 a is accepted.
\par
H3b: \textbf{Learning in information utilisation: }The
\par
decreasing pattern in the number of information
\par
items acquired over time suggests that people
\par
relied on their intuition more and more in fore-
\par
casting. This sub-optimal behaviour in informa-
\par
tion acquisition would have necessarily led to
\par
little, if any, improvement in forecast perfor-
\par
mance over time. The results of MANOVA re-
\par
vealed a significant impact of \emph{block }on forecast
\par
performance (F = 14.432, p {$<$} 0.0005). However,
\par
it was a significant linear downward trend (F =
\par
41.605, p {$<$} 0.0005). Their accuracy (MAPE) dete-
\par
riorated from 6.55 at the first, 8.09 at the second
\par
to 9.49 at the last block (see Table 5). It is
\par
mysterious why they persistently relied on their
\par
intuition despite a considerable deterioration of
\par
their accuracy. This reliance would be justified
\par
only if their forecasts outperformed the best in-
\par
formation provided. More disappointedly, how-
\par
ever, the improvement from the best information
\par
also gradually deteriorated, as seen in Table 5
\par
(F = 45.620, p {$<$} 0.0005). Not surprisingly, total
\par
charges significantly also increased over trials in a
\par
linear fashion (see Table 5) (F = 56.384, p {$<$}
\par
0.0005). This evidence leads us to accept H3b.
