\textbf{6. Discussion}
\par
Judgmental approaches to forecasting are of-
\par
ten adopted in prac\textasciitilde :ice ([66,18]). Development of
\par
computer software has, however, changed the
\par
practice of judgmental forecasting. Forecasters
\par
get access interactively to DSS/ EIS which pro-
\par
vides not only graphical time series but also a
\par
variety of other information from different
\par
sources. The effectiveness of this practice is gen-
\par
erally conditional upon the ability of forecasters
\par
to select diagnostic information that would de-
\par
crease the uncertainty of forecasting and appro-
\par
priately incorporate it into final forecasts ([59]).
\par
While this ability has been of interest to some
\par
researchers ([25,15--17]), most empirical time se-
\par
ries forecasting studies have generally failed to
\par
deal with complexities involved in this practice
\par
and simply concentrated on accuracy and im-
\par
provement. Furthermore, few DSS/ EIS studies
\par
have been concerned with the effectiveness of
\par
information acquisition and selection strategies.
\par
The dysfunctional effect of the anchoring-ad-
\par
justment heuristic (1199]) has been overwhelmingly
\par
documented in lit,erature. The impact of the
\par
preparation of an initial forecast has received
\par
little attention, despite a strong recommendation
\par
of its separate preparation ([53,62]). We found
\par
that this was helpful, perhaps by lessening any
\par
possible anchoring ,an a useless value, i.e., people
\par
produced more accurate forecasts with an inde-
\par
pendent initial forecast than those without it.
\par
While this practice could not eliminate the an-
\par
choring-adjustment bias ([9]), it would prevent
\par
people from starti\textasciitilde ag with an irrelevant anchor
\par
([43]). This finding :\textasciitilde upports the advice from most
\par
forecasting researchers that the initial forecast be
\par
prepared separately. Moreover, people's reluc-
\par
tance to process additional information was com-
\par
monplace, no matter whether the initial anchor
\par
was present or not. On a broader scale, it also
\par
raises the question of whether people should
\par
make their own initial judgments before interro-
\par
gating and using information supplied by the EIS.
\par
The conservatism (i.e., under-acquisition of in-
\par
formation, [46,78,17]) was also observed regard-
\par
less of the information reliability condition. Peo-
\par
ple relied very heavily on their intuition in fore-
\par
casting and thus, did not acquire much informa-
\par
tion. This under-acquisition may be surprising in
\par
that their forecast accuracy was inferior to the
\par
good information provided. Such conservative be-
\par
haviour in information acquisition appeared to be
\par
generally consistent with Lim and O'Connor ([59])
\par
where persistent anchoring on their initial fore-
\par
cast was also observed. A more pessimistic find-
\par
ing is that this sub-optimality was not easily over-
\par
come. Indeed, they did not learn over trials and
\par
became significantly worse. This mis-acquisition
\par
also carried over to forecast performance that
\par
also deteriorated over time (cf. [100]). Thus, those
\par
in the \emph{high-reliability }condition outperformed only
\par
slightly those in the \emph{low-reliability }condition. The
\par
limited ability of people to adjust their behaviour
\par
over time is not surprising in information pur-
\par
chase tasks. People did not learn a very simple
\par
purchasing strategy in a much simpler task ([46]).
\par
Slow learning was also observed in Connolly and
\par
Thorn ([17]). In their study, people learnt over
\par
time to buy good information only slowly even
\par
when the good information was far more reliable
\par
(96\% accountable for the criterion value).
\par
Learning has long been an issue for MCPL
\par
researchers ([35,36,7,34]). After reviewing the lit-
\par
erature in relation to learning, Brehmer ([7]) made
\par
a pessimistic conclusion, as suggested by the title
\par
of his paper \emph{"not from experience". }Two prime
\par
reasons which have been suggested for impaired
\par
learning ([34]) include: (1) unavailable or flawed
\par
feedback ([39,42]) and (2) cognitive biases in-
\par
volved in decision making, due to inadequate
\par
learning from feedback ([7]). In the present study,
\par
the feedback was both available and unambigu-
\par
ous. However, the feedback was often ignored.
\par
We found some cognitive biases, acting as obsta-
\par
cles to task learning: (1) mis-acquisition of less352 \emph{J.S. Lim, M. O'Connor\textasciitilde Decision Support Systems 16 (1996) 339-357}
\par
reliable information ([16]) and (2) under-acquisi-
\par
tion and reluctance to pay for the best informa-
\par
tion ([46]), despite the inferiority of judgmental
\par
forecasts to it. Mis-acquisition may not be surpris-
\par
ing because even experts are often influenced by
\par
irrelevant information ([33]). These findings ques-
\par
tion the ability of time series forecasters to select
\par
good information, a claim made by most MCPL
\par
researchers ([20,27]). This also lends some sup-
\par
port for the extensive use of Expert Systems to
\par
select the best forecasting method, as in most
\par
commercial statistical forecasting packages. An
\par
alternative explanation is that the subjects gave
\par
up any hope of learning anything useful from the
\par
information they purchased. The cost of buying
\par
information is "up-front" and certain, but the
\par
benefits are delayed and uncertain. If little steady
\par
gain was achieved, subjects may have simply
\par
sought to minimise their (certain) expenses by
\par
buying less information.
\par
In addition to mis-acquisition, some part of
\par
sub-optimality in this task could also be at-
\par
tributed to the limited cognitive ability in combin-
\par
ing multiple pieces of information ([58,53]). The
\par
pessimistic finding discussed above suggests some
\par
need for a DSS in this task. Connolly and Thorn
\par
([17]) suggested beneficial effects of the decision
\par
aids that provided automatic aggregation and took
\par
away even weightings from people. Although this
\par
design principle could overcome considerable
\par
cognitive biases, there are some issues to be dis-
\par
cussed. Indeed, such automatic DSS may not be
\par
effective in incorporating \emph{broken-leg s }cues ([49])
\par
because the required weightings should be ob-
\par
tained from past events. A behavioural issue
\par
which also needs to be addressed is whether
\par
people are willing to accept and rely on such
\par
DSS. It is also at issue in the \emph{bootstrapping }litera-
\par
ture ([65,21]) that people are reluctant to rely on
\par
the model, due to their being afraid of "replace-
\par
ment" by the model. It may be more serious for
\par
risky and important decisions ([74]). A more plau-
\par
sible approach is to trust the job of weighting to
\par
5 A "broken-leg" cue refers to an unusual piece of informa-
\par
tion that cannot be incorporated into any model, but which is
\par
vital to successful prediction.
\par
people. We found that this framework (automatic
\par
combination based upon subjective weighting)
\par
significantly improved forecast performance and
\par
encouraged people to attend to more informa-
\par
tion. This suggests that conservatism could be
\par
overcome to an extent. In this framework of the
\par
interactive DSS, people may feel less threat of
\par
being replaced. Moreover, people may easily in-
\par
corporate new event information through weight-
\par
ings. Further research is, however, required on
\par
this notion of the DSS, since people also have a
\par
problem of specifying weights ([59]). This design
\par
framework may be useful, particularly in situa-
\par
tions (e.g., new products, lack of data, etc.) where
\par
contextual information needs to be incorporated
\par
into the process of judgmental adjustment.
\par
To summarise, this study has investigated the
\par
effectiveness of judgmental forecasting with an
\par
interactive DSS. Results indicate that people ap-
\par
peared to favour their intuition in forecasting.
\par
Thus, they were reluctant to process additional
\par
information and under-acquired important diag-
\par
nostic information. This sub-optimal behaviour
\par
was not easily overcome. Some reasons may be
\par
advanced. First, these sceptical findings may be
\par
due to unfamiliarity with the DSS. However, they
\par
were given a practice session to get used to the
\par
tool and the subjects seemed to enjoy the session.
\par
It is also interesting to note that the subjects were
\par
post-graduate students who were majoring in In-
\par
formation Systems. They were, thus, arguably
\par
committed to the use of aids like the D S S / EIS.
\par
Second, the subjects might have been bored with
\par
the task. The monetary incentive scheme, how-
\par
ever, appeared to be a sufficient motivator. In
\par
addition, the task took less than an hour. At the
\par
end, they indicated the task was challenging.
\par
Thirdly, the purchase task itself might have been
\par
too difficult ([15]). Moreover, the dynamic nature
\par
of the time series task may have added to more
\par
difficulty. Lastly and more importantly, they might
\par
have felt that they could get all the information
\par
they wanted from the time series graph and did
\par
not need any extra information.
\par
The findings of the present study should be
\par
generalised with caution. First, only three pieces
\par
of information were made available. In practice,
\par
forecasters need to process much more informa-\emph{J.S. Lim, M. O'Connor\textasciitilde Decision Support Systems 16 (1996) 339-357 }353
\par
tion. However, if, as this study suggests, people
\par
have difficulty with just three pieces of informa-
\par
tion, they should have a lot more difficulty with
\par
more information. Second, only one time series
\par
was provided in the present study. The character-
\par
istics of the time series were, however, found to
\par
affect judgmental adjustment ([86,87]). Thirdly,
\par
the intercorrelations of cues ([28]) were not con-
\par
sidered. The time series used in causal adjust-
\par
ment were temperature data over certain periods
\par
with visitor information generated from them.
\par
Therefore, it may not reflect the true relation-
\par
ships between the time series and the causal
\par
information. It should be, however, noted that in
\par
real business forecasting contexts, cues are more
\par
often correlated with each other. Further re-
\par
search may be conducted with time series data
\par
and real causal information associated with the
\par
series, although this may be difficult to discover.
\par
Lastly, \emph{Greviser }was designed to repeat the task
\par
over a certain number of trials with short inter-
\par
vals. This may not represent the learning in real-
\par
ity ([42]). Due to th,\textasciitilde{} problems (e.g., time, other
\par
intervening factors) in observing the adjustment
\par
process with longer intervals, however, longitudi-
\par
nal laboratory studies have been adopted as a
\par
viable approach to real decision making ([23,89]).
\par
Further research is required to address these
\par
additional issues. Considering that judgmental
\par
adjustment is a common practice in most firms
\par
([54]), research is also needed to understand when
\par
decision aids can be most helpful for judgmental
\par
adjustment. In particular, this study can be repli-
\par
cated with the eye movement technique to ex-
\par
plore the interactive nature of information acqui-
\par
sition in time series judgmental forecasting (e.g.,
\par
[841).
\par
\textbf{7. Conclusions and iimplications for DSS design}
\par
This study examined the efficacy of how peo-
\par
ple make forecasts in the light of information
\par
coming from different sources. This issue is of
\par
great importance to the design of the DSS, since
\par
they assume that people are able to effectively
\par
integrate such information into the final forecast.
\par
The findings of this paper questioned the effec-
\par
tiveness of this design framework. The prime
\par
reason is that the \emph{benefits }of having the element
\par
of judgment within the DSS may outweigh the
\par
\emph{detriments }they may bring into the task. Here, the
\par
benefits include forecasters' contextual expertise
\par
([87]) and ability to identify broken-leg cues
\par
([64,49]). On the other hand, the detriments are
\par
the cognitive biases as found in psychology (1441,
\par
1851. 151).
\par
The most conspicuous hazard for permitting
\par
judgmental intervention in the DSS may be \emph{con-}
\par
\emph{servatism }([26,63]). We observed this heuristic
\par
persistently operating at time series tasks, i.e.,
\par
people placed too much faith in their own judg-
\par
ment and were reluctant to process any addi-
\par
tional diagnostic information ([99]). They em-
\par
ployed this anchoring adjustment heuristic, ar-
\par
guably to save their cognitive efforts ([96]) re-
\par
quired in processing additional data. Indeed, the
\par
DSS may not entirely prevent people from adopt-
\par
ing short cuts to reduce their cognitive efforts (cf.
\par
[97]) and thus, is subject to a number of cognitive
\par
biases ([51]). The sceptical view over the utility of
\par
DSS has been documented in the DSS literature
\par
([50,89]).
\par
The results of this paper suggested two alter-
\par
natives for the design of DSS. The first is that the
\par
DSS should be designed to allow for only a mini-
\par
mal role of unaided forecasters ([70]) in any judg-
\par
mental adjustment, especially when the model is
\par
quite reliable and people do not have much ex-
\par
tra-model information to contribute ([37]). This
\par
guideline is in parallel with the pessimistic views
\par
about judgmental decision making ([44]). Given
\par
that the element of judgment is necessary for
\par
forecasting in most forms, the second and more
\par
reasonable approach would be to incorporate
\par
some internal \emph{debiasing }mechanisms into the DSS
\par
(see [31,82]). We suggest that decision support be
\par
provided for every stage of the judgmental adjust-
\par
ment processes and some debiasing mechanisms
\par
should be incorporated into the DSS to help
\par
forecasters at every stage of judgmental adjust-
\par
ment - anchor development, selection of refer-
\par
ence forecasts, combination and lastly feedback.
\par
A suggested approach would be to incorporate
\par
any contextual knowledge into an independent354 \emph{J.S. Lim, M. O'Connor/Decision Support Systems 16 (1996) 339-357}
\par
judgmental forecast. Then, the DSS may combine
\par
it into the final forecast in a mechanical way
\par
([53]). Alternatively, the DSS may allow people to
\par
take some role in the weighting of cues. The DSS
\par
should be, however, designed to monitor their
\par
performance and alert (more strongly) forecasters
\par
against any systematic bias (e.g., conservatism)
\par
(e.g., [1]).
