\textbf{ABSTRACT}
\par
This paper sketches what I deem to be the landmarks and major research trends
\par
since the second World War (WWII) in academic accounting research. The
\par
presentation is fairly chronological. It puts some stress on the "revolutionary science"
\par
of the 1960s : the introduction of analytical and empirical-statistical tools to
\par
{$\bullet$} 1 take this opportunity to thank the editor of the \emph{Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting }for
\par
inviting me to deal with that topic. Having myself been involved in accounting research
\par
during most of this half decade, the paper is bound to reflect my own views and experience.
\par
Although this presentation stresses accounting literature of the English language area, I
\par
have tried to be as international as possible by including authors from Algeria, Argentina,
\par
Austria, Brazil, Chile, China. Colombia, Denmark, Egypt, India, Israel, Italy, Japan, France.
\par
Finland, Germany, Greece. the Netherlands. Norway, Poland, Russia, the South African
\par
Union, Sweden, Switzerland, Spain, Turkey and Venezuela (though some of their
\par
publications may be in English). This paper concentrates on academic accounting research
\par
but also takes some professional publications into consideration. The major criterion for
\par
including a publication was the question whether it is representative as regards trend,
\par
time, or country.
\par
Finally, I have taken the liberty of drawing on some of my previous publications, and
\par
should like to acknowledge gratefully the leniency of the CGA-Canada Research
\par
Foundation for allowing me to adapt a few short passages from my own contributions to
\par
our anthologies on \emph{Modern Accounting Research: History, Survey, and Guide }(1984) and
\par
its supplementary volume, \emph{Accounting Research in the 1980s and Its Future Relevance}
\par
(1991). I also want to thank Prof. Dieter Schneider of the Ruhr-University, Bochum, for
\par
reading the first draft of the manuscript and his valuable advice, as well as to Prof. Jerry
\par
Feltham (UBC) for reading the section interpreting some of his papers. particularly that
\par
by Feltham and Ohlson (1995). Special thanks go to the two commentators, Profs. Haim
\par
Falk and Yuji ljiri -see separate papers and my response. Further thanks go to the
\par
Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council of Canada for financial support.
\par
Arthur Andersen \& Co. Alumni Professor emeritus of the University of British Columbia,
\par
Vancouver, Canada.4 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
accounting, the pioneering work in computer application and simulation of
\par
spreadsheets, the refinements in current value and inflation accounting, as well as
\par
the dispute over the correct valuation basis. But above all, it outlines the
\par
consolidation of these achievements during subsequent decades, thus emphasizing
\par
the increasing attention which the many empirical-statistical as well as informational-
\par
agency aspects and organizational issues received; furthermore, it draws attention
\par
to "positive accounting theory" and the reaction to it from the British "critical-
\par
interpretive school" as well as from other quarters. In the Conclusion the paper
\par
points at the present crisis, and considers a possible long-term solution.
\par
\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Progress in academic accounting research since the end ofWW II has, despite the
\par
numerous publications, been moderate compared with the enormous changes in
\par
other scientific and technological fields during the same time. 1 But if I compare the
\par
present state of affairs in this area with that of 50 years ago, I must admit that much,
\par
incredibly much, has changed in our discipline as well. But is it genuine progress?
\par
Is it merely theoretical or does it have practical implications? Perhaps the reader
\par
may be able to resolve these questions after having perused this paper. Yet there
\par
can be little doubt that novel ideas have been generated in modem accounting
\par
research, be it out of an internal impetus or through the application of other fields
\par
to this area. Above all, there are some four or five subareas where research and
\par
theory have had noticeable impact on accounting practice during the period under
\par
consideration: (I) the introduction of the "flow-of-funds statement" (Statement of
\par
Changes in Financial Position) as the third pillar in the presentation of financial
\par
statements; (2) the application of statistical methods (including hypotheses testing,
\par
etc.) to auditing; (3) simulation and computerization of accounting spread-sheets
\par
and similar devices; (4) the supplementation of historical cost by current entry-
\par
values in income statements (and occasionally other financial statements) or other
\par
inflation accounting procedures in several countries; (5) various attempts by
\par
accounting bodies and other institutions (also in several countries) to develop a
\par
conceptual framework of accounting. If item 4 above seems to have been merely a
\par
temporary success (during the late 1970s and early 1980s ), the reader must be
\par
reminded that this limitation pertains primarily to the legislative aspects in Canada,
\par
the U.K. and the U.S.A., but neither to similar legislations in other countries (like
\par
Mexico, Brazil, etc.) nor to "current value supplementation" on a voluntary basisAcademic Research in Accounting: The Last 50 Years 5
\par
by corporations. And if item 5 is still controversial, this may have been less due to
\par
the underlying research than to the practitioners' execution of research
\par
recommendations.
\par
On the other hand, it must be admitted that fundamental questions,
\par
predominantly those of valuation and accounting measurement, repeatedly raised
\par
during the last hundred years or so, have still not found generally accepted answers.
\par
This may be surprising in the face of the enormous research output generated
\par
during those five decades. 2Some people might argue that it proves the inferiority
\par
of accounting research and its adepts, particularly when compared to the ingenuity
\par
of researchers in other disciplines. Few academic accountants will accept this
\par
argument, despite the general realization that our discipline does not attract the
\par
geniuses occasionally encountered in the natural sciences. Although research
\par
success is greatly accelerated by extraordinary abilities, I cannot believe that the
\par
lack of them is the ultimate reason for the limited progress in our field. On the other
\par
extreme lies the argument that accounting is a discipline much more difficult to
\par
explore than any natural science (to which the discrepancy of research funding
\par
between those areas has to be added). Though there seems to be a grain of truth in
\par
this argument, again I doubt whether this is the main reason for the limited practical
\par
success of accounting research--even if one concedes that social reality is much
\par
more difficult to grasp scientifically than physical or even biological reality. I
\par
rather believe that the major reason for the long-standing dilemma of our research
\par
area is to be found elsewhere. Perhaps it lies in the fact that the very essence of
\par
accounting has been misconceived by academics; perhaps they have used the
\par
wrong methodology to tackle the problems peculiar to our discipline (see ultimate
\par
section), thus boxing themselves into a blind alley; perhaps the political forces of
\par
specific interest groups defy any effort by academics to materialize truly
\par
meaningful and socially just accounting standards and accountability systems.
\par
Indeed, serious problems arose in practice: fiscal deficits (exponentially
\par
growing, decade after decade) and gigantic frauds as well as bankruptcies in
\par
private industry cast an immense shadow, not only over leading public accounting
\par
firms but onto the entire discipline. Can academia be blamed for such a trend?
\par
Certainly not entirely, but perhaps we did allocate too much of our energy, time
\par
and other resources to sophisticated statistical and other mathematical techniques
\par
while neglecting to explore sufficiently the ethical and other normative aspects as
\par
a prerequisite to genuine accountability.
\par
Such introductory words may be reason enough to view accounting research
\par
from a bird's perspective. Instead of examining individual trees, let us here look at
\par
the forest of accounting research and its growth during half a century. But some6 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
words of caution are necessary. First, a sweeping view like this can offer only a
\par
rough road-map that disregards innumerable studies and subtle details; and for a
\par
full understanding ofhistory, precisely those insights are crucial. Just as one cannot
\par
judge belles-lettres merely from the plots of the most notable fictions, so it is
\par
impossible to convey all the richness and cultural-scientific significance of our
\par
discipline through such a sketch. On the other side, concentrating merely on details
\par
is no less dangerous. Without having an overall view, one loses easily one's way in
\par
the esoteric underbrush. Thus, a sketch like this may fill a latent need to see the
\par
main trends, no less than the goals and controversies of accounting research during
\par
half a century. It is the hope that this will inspire the reader to seriously study past
\par
accounting literature and its many fascinating aspects (i.e., the major works as well
\par
as different kinds of surveys).3 To write a truly objective historical account is
\par
hardly possible by an active participant, but I have honestly tried to avoid any
\par
conscious bias. And if there are any grave omissions or distortions, the reader may
\par
attribute them to my ignorance rather than to the intention of slighting any author.4
\par
\textbf{1945--1959: Emergence of a New Academic Spirit}
\par
The first 15 years or so after WW II formed the transition from pre-war concerns--
\par
dominated by the traditional approach to valuation issues (e.g., Illetschko 1952;
\par
Mellerowicz 1952; Bouffier 1956); the concern with charts of accounts; the early
\par
search for accounting standards; the discussion or controversy of \emph{equity theory}
\par
\emph{versus entity theory }or the corresponding controversy between several major
\par
"Bilanztheorien'' in the German language area--to the introduction of elements of
\par
modem mathematics and science to our discipline. Typical for the late 1940s is
\par
Vatter's \emph{The Fund Theory of Accounting }(1947). Apart from being one of the tirst
\par
significant publications of this period, it still looks back to the pre-war controversy
\par
but rejects the proprietorship theory as well as entity theory as inadequate in specific
\par
situations; it rather pleads for various "funds" to establish localized entities in
\par
controlling particular activities of administration. Although actual practice did not
\par
accept the resulting recommendations, the theory influenced the introduction of
\par
the flow-of-funds (financial position) statement as the new and third element (beside
\par
the balance sheet and the profit and loss statement) in financial reporting--see also
\par
Zlatkovich ( 1949) and, for a somewhat later thesis on funds statements, Anton
\par
(1953/62), Homgren (1956) and, even later, the historical survey of funds statement
\par
by Rosen and DeCoster ( 1969). Vatter's theory may also have had some impact on
\par
the ensuing controversy of cash-flow versus profit analysis and on some aspects ofAcademic Research in Accounting: The Last 50 Years 7
\par
modern budgeting; finally, it may have foreshadowed some aspects of conditional-
\par
normative methodology (see ultimate section) which, like Vatter's approach, opposes
\par
single-value theories.
\par
Accounting literature in Japan too harked back to the time before the war, for
\par
example Kurosawa (1947). This was also true for the intense concern with basic
\par
charts of accounts: in Germany, the \emph{Kontenrahmen----originally }introduced by
\par
Schmalenbach--continued to arouse some interest (e.g., Scherpf 1955), but more
\par
so in France where the \emph{Plan Comptable }(e.g., Lauzel and Cibert 1959) still
\par
constituted a major concern of academic accountants (for details about the French
\par
\emph{Plan Comptable, }see Fortin 1991). Some notable French works are: Garnet (1947),
\par
also emphasizing the chart of accounts but in disagreement with the particular
\par
versions by Schmalenbach or the traditional French plan; Brunet ( 1951 ), pursuing
\par
such objects as the control of prices as well as competition, and other social
\par
policies, improved efficiency, etc.-this publication seems to have ultimately
\par
aimed at something like Leontiefs input-output table (cf. Colasse and Durand
\par
1994)-----finally there is Lassegue ( 1995), presenting accounting through the lenses
\par
of traditional economics.
\par
More characteristic for the new trend towards modern scientific methods were
\par
the attempts by Vance (1950), Trueblood and Cyert (1954\textbraceright, Trueblood and Cooper
\par
(1955), Vance and Neter (1956), as well as Cyert's and Trueblood's (1957) book
\par
\emph{Sampling Techniques in Accounting, }to apply modern statistical methods to
\par
accounting and particularly to auditing (see also Cyert and Wheeler 1960). In
\par
addition to these, audit case studies as well as audit experiments were pursued (see
\par
Churchill 1966, and Churchill and Cyert 1966). To all those efforts may be added
\par
the econometric studies of cost behaviour by managerial economists like Dean
\par
(1951) and others, as well as operations research (OR) studies preparing the later
\par
application of OR to cost and managerial accounting (e.g., Charnes and Cooper
\par
1957, 1961 ). In the area of general accounting theory, the search for the pragmatic
\par
foundations of our discipline in form of accounting postulates or axioms (and
\par
related mathematical approaches) found an early manifestation during this time
\par
(Chambers 1955, 1957; Mattessich 1956, 1957), in contrast to the search for
\par
"mere" principles ofaccountiag, as still encountered in Littleton (1953). But those
\par
postulational efforts became more prominent during the subsequent period when
\par
they were further developed. My own attempts (particularly in Mattessich 1956
\par
and, later, 1964a) also tended towards a \emph{common basis of micro- and macro-}
\par
\emph{accounting. }Such a trend was then in the air (cf., Cooper 1949, Powelson 1955;
\par
perhaps also Bray 1953 as well as Edey and Peacock 1954) and continued in the
\par
next period: e.g., Nose (1961) and Aizaki (1966), in Japan, and Goldberg (1965,8 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
including his "commander theory"). in Australia. Later, most accountants seem to
\par
have lost interest in such an endeavour, except Perochon (1971) in France and,
\par
above all, in Japan where Harada's (1978) pertinent book won the Award of the
\par
Japanese Accounting Society.
\par
Rapid price increases in the first post-war years also led to enhanced interest in
\par
\emph{inflation accounting, }which prompted pertinent academic research ( cf., Baxter
\par
1949, May 1949, Alexander 1950, Jones 1955, 1956) to prepare the path for the
\par
valuation debate of the 1960s and beyond. In the area of cost or managerial
\par
accounting, the trend of the 1960s is well reflected in Benston ( 1970). The need to
\par
recognize appropriate cost accounting systems for serving managerial decision
\par
making (instead of merely inventory valuation for financial statement presentation)
\par
brought forth a number of publications (e.g., Sorter and Homgren 1962), and not
\par
only by accountants (e.g., Churchman and Ackoff 1955; Churchman 1961); it
\par
finally led to the direct-costing debate ( cf., Green 1960). The fifties also brought
\par
forth anthologies on costing issues (e.g. Solomons 1952) or \emph{historical studies,}
\par
either on cost accounting. as in Gamer ( 1954), or on accounting in general, as in
\par
Mel is (1950), De Roover ( 1955), Littleton and Varney (1956) and Peragallo
\par
( 1956), or about specific accountants as, for example, on "Theodore Limperg" by
\par
Mey ( 1966). Closely related to these problems were those of capital maintenance
\par
(e.g., Hax 1957) which were frequently debated until the 1980s (e.g., Busse von
\par
Colbe 1960; Sieben and Schildbach 1973; Coenenberg and Macharzina 1976;
\par
Sterling and Lemke 1982-for a summary of the latter see Lemke 1982). In
\par
management accounting, conceptual problems and studies on depreciation (and
\par
other allocation problems) as well as pricing policy were also prevalent (e.g.,
\par
Devine 1950; Anthony 1957). Even the first steps towards more rigorous
\par
empirical-behavioural accounting research belong to this early period as evidenced
\par
by Argyris' (1952) study on budgeting, later pursued in the award-winning
\par
dissertation by Stedry ( 1960).
\par
\textbf{The 1960s: A Golden Age of A Priory Accounting}
\par
The first prominent work of the 1960s was the study on current value accounting
\par
(CVA) by Edwards and Bell (1961), \emph{The Theory and Measurement of Business}
\par
\emph{Income. }Its basic ideas were developed since the early 1920s in a series of articles
\par
and books by Schmidt (e.g. 1921/29/33). But Edwards and Bell, unfamiliar with
\par
German, had little access to Schmidt's ideas and developed their theory pretty much
\par
independently. In many respects these two authors went well beyond Schmidt's
\par
work5
\par
:Academic Research in Accounting : The Last 50 Years 9
\par
(I) Edwards and Bell's profit determination is primarily based on the assumption
\par
of real financial capital maintenance (though its concept of"current operating
\par
profit" would also permit taking physical capital maintenance into
\par
consideration--for details see Mattessich 1995, Chapter 6) while Schmidt's
\par
model is based exclusively on the notion of \emph{physical }capital maintenance. This
\par
seems to be one major advantage of Edwards and Bell's approach; it accounts
\par
for most of the discrepancies between those two theories.
\par
(2) Edwards and Bell's ultimate "business profit" \emph{includes all holding gains,}
\par
whereas Schmidt's ultimate income concept \emph{excludes }most realized and
\par
unrealized holding gains and thus corresponds only to Edwards and Bell's
\par
"current operating profit".
\par
(3) Edwards and Bell distinguished clearly between \emph{various types of holding gains}
\par
(monetary vs. nonmonetary, real vs. fictitious, realized vs. unrealized) and
\par
presented a more subtle conceptual apparatus than did Schmidt. In contrast to
\par
the latter, Edwards and Bell ( 1961) incorporates the realized and unrealized
\par
holding gains into the income statement (Schmidt relegates those holding
\par
gains to a special capital reserve account).
\par
(4) Although Schmidt seems to have been willing to include net monetary holding
\par
\emph{losses }in his gain from operating activity, he did not do so for net monetary
\par
holding \emph{gains, }regarding them as speculative gains (financed by debt equity).
\par
(5) Edwards and Bell could then afford to present two versions, a \emph{nominal }as well
\par
as a real CV A-model (a \emph{nominal }model does not take general price-level
\par
changes \emph{specifical(v }into consideration, whereas a \emph{real }model does). Schmidt,
\par
in contrast, was limited to a nominal CV A model-if he occasionally took
\par
general price-level changes into consideration, it was in a confused and
\par
unsystematic way.
\par
For other well-known publications of this period, dealing with income analysis
\par
or accounting for price-level adjustments and related problems, see Bedford and
\par
Dopuch (1961), Ferrara \textbraceleft 1961), Hansen (1962), Zeff(l962), Schneider (1963),
\par
Mueller ( 1964 ), Bedford ( 1965), Solomons (1961, 1966), Lemke \textbraceleft 1966), Gynther
\par
(1966), Dyckman (1969) and Simmonds and Gray ( 1969\textbraceright -as well as Holzer and
\par
Schoenfeld ( 1963a, 1963b ), as far as Germany and France are concerned, and
\par
Johansson (1965), referring to the situation in Sweden. Edwards and Bell's
\par
influential book-which, in the late 1970s, ultimately led to the current price-level
\par
legislation of the Financial Accounting Standards Board (F ASB 1979, 1981,
\par
l984a, 1984b ), the Institute of Chartered Accountants of England and Wales
\par
(ICAEW 1980), the Canadian Institute of Chartered Accountants (CICA 1982)
\par
and other publications-already belongs to what Nelson ( 1973, p. 4) called "a
\par
golden age of \emph{a priori }research in accounting". But this expression also refers to10 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
the attempts of searching for a postulational or axiomatic basis. As pointed out,
\par
such attempts began already in the late 1950s; they stimulated the accounting
\par
community to devote more thought to foundational research (for a survey see Zeff
\par
1982a).
\par
Maurice Moonitz, the unofficial leader of the "Berkeley School", was invited
\par
around 1960 by the American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA)
\par
to become its Research Director and organize a series of "Accounting Research
\par
Studies" focusing on foundational issues. The first of those studies was authored
\par
by Moonitz ( 1961) himself, and was widely referred to. Under the title \emph{The Basic}
\par
\emph{Postulates of Accounting, }it formulated a total offourteen basic propositions (about
\par
quantification, exchange, identification of entities, time period, unit of measure,
\par
financial statements, market prices, structure of entities, tentativeness, continuity,
\par
objectivity, consistency, stable unit, and disclosure) and generated broad interest
\par
in this kind of research. A subsequent AICPA Accounting Research Study was
\par
coauthored by Sprouse and Moonitz ( 1962) and, as its title suggested, was
\par
expected to deduce a set of broad accounting principles from the Moonitz
\par
postulates. However, as the "Comments of Leonard Spacek" (contained in Sprouse
\par
and Moonitz 1962, p. 77-79) pointed out ''there is very little attempt to demonstrate
\par
how these principles flow from or are based on the postulates set forth in the
\par
previous study". This concise sentence proved to be prophetic for the failure of all
\par
subsequent postulational accounting studies (including "conceptual frameworks")
\par
that-in contrast to the axiomatic research-attempted to forego a rigorous
\par
mathematico-logical approach (as first applied to macro-accounting by Aukrust
\par
1955).
\par
Spacek's ( 1962) insight was in conformity with my own axiomatic
\par
experiments (Mattessich 1957) that presented theorems (supported by
\par
mathematical proofs) based on matrix algebra and set theory. Thus it was natural
\par
that I would further develop my original thoughts and present them in the form of
\par
an axiomatic conceptual framework (Mattessich 1964a), though ultimately aimed
\par
at pragmatic purposes. In this work, I tried to introduce rigorous mathematical and
\par
logical thinking to the whole gamut of accounting, as its title, \emph{Accounting and}
\par
\emph{Analytical Methods-Measurement and Projection of Income and Wealth in the}
\par
\emph{Macro- and Micro-Economy, }suggests. Apart from applying S. S. Stevens'
\par
measurement theory to accounting, this book introduced, together with its
\par
companion volume (Mattessich 1964b ), among other aspects, financial simulation
\par
by means of computerized spread-sheets, thus preparing the ground for the spread-
\par
sheet programmes, like Lotus 1-2-3, that became so popular in accounting practice
\par
two decades later. The mathematical trend in accounting was reinforced by a series
\par
of other books like those by Williams and Griffin (1964, 1967), and Ijiri ( 1965a,Academic Research in Accounting : The Last 50 Years 11
\par
1965b) in the area of \emph{management accounting, }as well as by a host of papers on
\par
\emph{matrix accounting and linear programming applications }(e.g., Corcoran 1964,
\par
Demski 1967). Further notable research in management accounting of this decade
\par
(though not of a mathematical nature) were: Solomons' (1965) book on transfer
\par
pricing (see also Goetz 1967); methodological problems of income measurement,
\par
as in Gordon ( 1960); overhead costs in income measurement, as in Ferrara ( 1961 );
\par
cost-volume-profit analysis, as in Jaedicke and Robichek (1964); standard costs
\par
and probabilistic control, as in Zannetos (1964); planning and control in industry,
\par
as in Lal3mann (1968); measuring operating efficiency, as in Arney ( 1969), etc.
\par
As to the further pursuit of accounting postulation and axiomatization, the
\par
following contributions were to be particularly significant: \emph{Accounting, Evaluation}
\par
\emph{and Economic Behavior }by Chambers ( 1966) and \emph{The Foundations of Accounting}
\par
\emph{Measurement }by ljiri ( 1967). Apart from the many subtle conceptual and
\par
foundational details-which, regrettably, can here not be discussed-, the
\par
following highlights may be mentioned: Chambers' work attempted to provide a
\par
comprehensive postulational framework in non-mathematical fashion, formulating
\par
numerous propositions \textbraceleft premises and, what are claimed to be, conclusions, though
\par
merely with vague hints instead of proofs) not only for accounting but also for
\par
relevant neighbouring disciplines such as economics, sociology, psychology, etc.
\par
This book may well have foreshadowed and stimulated the interest in behavioural
\par
accounting that developed in the 1970s and 1980s, even if the later developments
\par
were based on a more rigorous empirical basis. The book might also be regarded as
\par
the starting point for Chambers' later publications that pleaded for current exit-
\par
values (cf., Chambers 1967, 1980) such as potential resale or liquidation prices
\par
(net realizable value or "current cash equivalent").
\par
ljiri's ( 1967) work too presented a postulational or axiomatic approach (cf.,
\par
his set-theoretical formulations in the appendix), but in contrast to Chambers, he
\par
promoted, from the very outset, an historical cost approach. As Dyckman's ( 1968,
\par
p. 200) review indicates, the "importance of this book, does not stem directly from
\par
the newness of the material contained .... Rather this volume combines, elaborates
\par
and synthesizes these ideas toward a 'better understanding of the foundations of
\par
accounting as it is' .... ljiri, specifically is not concerned in this book with what
\par
accounting ought to be" (for ljiri's contribution to the latter, see the penultimate
\par
section). It was this book and further important works by ljiri, which secured him
\par
the leadership in analytical accounting for over a decade. Further contributions of
\par
the 1960s to the issues of accounting postulates and principles were made by
\par
Gordon ( 1964), Bray ( 1966), Kohler ( 1966) and Blough ( 1967), and to the problem
\par
of"realization" by Davidson (1966). Closely related to all those problems is the
\par
issue of "accounting objectives" (see also later sections); some of the earliest of12 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
these studies were those by Rappaport (1964) and Backer (1966), and a
\par
methodological one by Buckley, Kircher and Mathews ( 1968), and one on
\par
sensitivity analysis by Rappaport (1967). A first contribution to the philosophy of
\par
auditing was made at the beginning of this period by Mautz and Sharaff ( 1961) and
\par
by papers on related topics, such as changing audit objectives (e.g., Brown 1962)
\par
or auditing standards (e.g., Berryman 1960), or management audits (e.g.,
\par
Langenderfer and Robertson 1969). Among the contributions to methodology are
\par
Devine (1960), Zeff (1963) and the book by Goldberg (1965); as regards the
\par
history or theory of accounting (Littleton and Zimmerman 1962), or the history of
\par
direct costing (Weber 1966) and on the endeavours of forging accounting
\par
principles in various countries (Zeff 1972), may be noted. As to anthologies
\par
representative for the period, see Baxter and Davidson ( 1962), Moonitz and
\par
Littleton ( 1965), Buckley ( 1969); as to historical accounting thought, see Chatfield
\par
( 1968), and as to a historical perspective of management accounting, see Parker
\par
(1969).
\par
Considering that, during this time, there existed a considerable movement
\par
pleading for the use of the "economic" or "present value" approach (discounting
\par
expected dividends or net cash or income flows) in accounting, one is confronted
\par
with a variety of major viewpoints on valuation: Edwards and Bell ( 1961) and
\par
Sprouse and Moonitz ( 1962) were primarily in favour of \emph{current-entry values; }the
\par
monograph of the American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (1963)
\par
recommended \emph{general price-level adjustments;6 }Chambers ( 1967\textbraceright --later also
\par
Sterling ( 1970) and Thomas ( 1969, 1974\textbraceright --regarded current-exit values as the
\par
only proper valuation basis for accounting; ljiri (1967, 1975) favoured the
\par
traditional historical cost valuation, as did previously Saario (e.g., 1945), Kosiol
\par
(e.g., 1956, 1978) and others. A third major group of scholars, like Honko ( 1959),
\par
Alexander (1950), Corbin ( 1962), Kafer (1962), Albach (1965), Lemke (1966),
\par
Brief 1968, 1969\textbraceright --and later Seicht ( 1970) who was discussed by Schneider
\par
(1973)7
\par
, as well as Beaver ( 1981 ), Ohlson (1987a) and others-promoted the
\par
application of "economic" or present values in accounting. Thus, one should not
\par
be surprised that students, practitioners and laymen wondered whether the
\par
valuation controversies of previous times were being revived ( cf. Lechner 1967),
\par
though possibly on a more sophisticated level. The bewilderment over the question
\par
"which of these competing values really fits the bill?" was now greater than ever.
\par
As to postulational research, Dopuch ( 1989a, p. 42) asserts that "although each
\par
work seemed to take off from the same set ofbasic postulates, each was still able to
\par
arrive at a different destination." This is not only gravely misleading but incorrect.
\par
As here indicated, the individual approaches vary greatly in their assumptions,
\par
particularly as far as valuation is concerned.Academic Research in Accounting : The Last 50 Years 13
\par
Some publications--e.g., Vatter (1947) and the American Accounting
\par
Association's ( 1966, p. 9-10, 28-31) \emph{A Statement of Basic Accounting Theory}
\par
(ASOBAT\textbraceright --favoured a \emph{multi-value approach }but, to launch it methodically, a
\par
theoretical basis was required. The notion of \emph{value to the owner }(often called
\par
"deprival value", see also Lee 1975\textbraceright --accepting one or the other of the above-
\par
mentioned values, depending on the circumstances--has been promoted by what
\par
some call the "LSE Triumvirate" (cf., Whittington 1994 and Edwards 1994, p.
\par
252): Edey (1962), Solomons (1966), and Baxter (1967, 1975) laid the ground for
\par
a systematic choice of the appropriate but \emph{relative }deprival value. Other scholars
\par
who, at times, emphasized relative values or some kind of \emph{purpose-oriented}
\par
accounting are: Le Coutre (1949), Iwata (1954), Kimura ( 1954), Backer (1966),
\par
Heinen ( 1978), possibly Schneider ( 1981 ), Devine ( 1985). But there may be a
\par
more fundamental methodology for creating such a basis; to develop it Mattessich
\par
( 1964a) distinguished in its set of basic assumptions (the majority of which are,
\par
contrary to common belief, of empirical nature) a separate category (surrogate
\par
assumptions) which are empirically empty but hold the place for specific pragmatic
\par
or instrumental hypotheses (formalized means-end relations). Such an approach,
\par
general yet flexible (exposed and illustrated in several of my later publications),
\par
was little understood and, therefore, disregarded by the majority of accountants.
\par
Thus the search of academics for the Holy Grail-i.e., for the one and only \emph{true }or
\par
\emph{neutral }accounting valuation basis---continued unabatedly. One cannot ignore the
\par
fact that different managerial or political goals require different capital
\par
maintenance concepts (nominal, financial, real, etc.) as confirmed in Lemke
\par
(1982); nor can one deny that different objectives require different cost concepts
\par
(cf., Pfaff 1994) because reality is very different from the ideal world of perfect
\par
markets and complete information, so often assumed in our theoretical models.
\par
Other notable research of this period was concerned with such topics as the
\par
examination of the realization concept (Homgren 1965), or changing depreciation
\par
methods (Archibald 1967), planning horizons and future cash flows (Bailey and
\par
Gray 1967), or with budget control in Hofstede (1967).
\par
Towards the end of this decade, further innovations manifested themselves.
\par
Apart from Sorter's (1969) "events approach" to basic accounting theory-which
\par
recommends a disaggregation into specific building stones to be recombined
\par
according to different needs-two significant trends prepared themselves to burst
\par
forth in all their vigour in the next period. The first of these trends was a shift in
\par
analytical accounting from the "axiomatic approach" to the application of
\par
information economics (which ultimately led to a "fusion" of this accounting-
\par
informational work with agency theory). Three Berkeley dissertations, (all\textbf{14 }Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
submitted in the same year) by Butterworth (1967), Feltham (1967) and Mock (for
\par
some of his subsequent publications, see Mock 1969, 1971 ), constituted the
\par
starting point of this trend, as every one of them dealt with information issues of
\par
accounting-shortly later followed other publications (e.g., Crandall 1969)\_8
\par
Subsequent work by Feltham and Demski proved to be path-breaking and led to
\par
garnering one of the most prestigious accounting awards (see next section). In
\par
addition to these, another, yet related, alternative information theoretic approach to
\par
accounting was presented by Lev ( 1969).
\par
The other trend taking off in the 1960s was the influence of finance theory
\par
upon accounting (e.g., Staubus 1961) and the quest for rigorous statistical-
\par
empirical methods first felt in finance but soon spilling over into accounting. Such
\par
publications as Schrader (1962), Loitlsberger (1963), Dopuch and Drake (1966),
\par
Dyckman ( 1966), Benston ( 1967), Cook ( 1967), Mautz (1968) and, above all. Ball
\par
and Brown's (1968), "An Empirical Evaluation of Accounting Income Numbers",
\par
and Beaver's ( 1968), ''Information Content of Annual Earnings Announcements",
\par
proved to be revolutionary for empirical accounting-for a study on market based
\par
accounting tests, see McDonald (1968). Although the paper by Ball and Brown
\par
(1968) was rejected by \emph{The American Accounting Review, }it was accepted by the
\par
\emph{Journal of Accounting Research }(of the "Chicago School" that prepared this
\par
empirical trend since the early 1960s) and, in 1965, received the most prestigious
\par
AAA Award for Seminal Contribution to Accounting Theory-three years later
\par
the same award was given to Beaver ( 1968).
\par
\textbf{The 1970's and the New Direction of}
\par
\textbf{Accounting Research}
\par
Sterling's \emph{Theory and Measurement of the Enterprise Income }( 1970) may be, due
\par
to its delayed publication, considered a latecomer to \emph{a priori }research; indeed, it
\par
belongs to the preceding decade. It too examines foundational issues but, as pointed
\par
out, it is one of the major works pleading for current exit-values (i.e., exit prices as
\par
valuation basis). The two monographs by Thomas (1969, 1974) on accounting
\par
allocation issues also promote current exit-values and belong, in spirit, more to the
\par
1960's than 1970's.
\par
The search for accounting postulates or axioms continued (even into the 1980s
\par
and 1990s) but, as time went by, it became peripheral to the main-stream approach.
\par
The interest in postulational and axiomatic accounting issues first spread to
\par
Germany: apart from a German translation ofMattessich (1964a), it can be found
\par
by Kosiol (l970b, 1978) and Schweitzer ( 1970, 1972), both using the pagatoricAcademic Research in Accounting : The Last 50 Years 15
\par
(i.e., cash-flow) basis. This approach has been criticized by Schneider (1972, p.
\par
50-52) for paying insufficient attention to purpose-orientation (Zwecksetzung) and
\par
to the valuation problem. Indeed, in this neglect lies the decisive difference
\par
between the postulational or axiomatic attempts by Kosiol and Schweitzer versus
\par
my own. I have tried to provide surrogate assumptions to hold the place for more
\par
specific or instrumental hypotheses that refer to purpose, valuation, etc. (e.g.,
\par
Mattessich 1964a). However, a heavy price has to be paid for this advantage: those
\par
instrumental hypotheses can usually be determined only by trial and error. Yet this
\par
should rather challenge (instead of discourage) scholars to study those trial and
\par
error procedures, improve them, and to find more systematic ways of explaining
\par
them. Originally, I limited myself to illustrating this approach for the case of
\par
computerized financial and budgetary simulations which, considering the success
\par
of the many best-selling computer spreadsheet programmes, may have proved its
\par
value. Later, Harada (e.g., 1978) and Koguchi (1990) have found applications of
\par
this methodology in situations of macro- and meso-accounting (the later refers
\par
primarily to more local environmental accounting issues); and most recently, in
\par
Mattessich ( 1995, Chapter 6), I tried to illustrate this purpose-oriented approach
\par
through the existence and use of different capital maintenance approaches. This
\par
may conform with Schneider's ( 1973, p. 51) view, who pointed out that: \emph{"Kosiol,}
\par
in the formulation of his theory, had clearly recognized: every contextual
\par
formulation of the profit concept relates to a specific notion of capital maintenance
\par
of the firm' [footnote referring to Kosiol (1944)]. But in the further development of
\par
the pagatoric balance-sheet theory this contextual formulation disappears into the
\par
background" (translated\textbraceright -! have tried to bring such purpose-oriented formulation
\par
to the foreground again.
\par
The search for an axiomatic basis also continued in America, as in ljiri ( 1967,
\par
1971, 1975) and Mattessich (1972), as well as in Japan by Saito (1972, 1973)
\par
together with the reply by Mattessich (1973a-c \textbraceright -the latter paper contains some
\par
technical improvements of my axiomatic approach. Towards the end of this decade
\par
appeared other works along similar lines: a doctoral dissertation by Orbach ( 1978),
\par
a paper by Tippett ( 1978) and some books in Italian by Onida (1970) and,
\par
particularly, Galassi ( 1978), as well as a series of papers in Spain by such authors
\par
as Buenos-Campos ( 1972), Garcia-Garcia ( 1972), and Requena-Rodriguez ( 1972).
\par
The publications by Ijiri (e.g., 1975, p. 51-74 and 1989, p. 104--106) and, in a
\par
certain way, the one by Wells (1971 ), might also be counted to this postulational-
\par
axiomatic literature. Related to such foundational problems are measurement
\par
issues as, for example, dealt with by Mock (1976) and Abdel-Magid (1979), but
\par
particularly as regards income and wealth measurement, as in Anderson (1976) or
\par
Beaver and Demski (1979). To this has to be added the search for acceptable16 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
accounting standards, as in Moonitz (1974), or the attempt to apply
\par
Schmalenbach's (1925) accounting theory to the problem of income taxation by
\par
Jacobs (1971 ), though criticized by Schneider (1973).
\par
However, as the modern trend gained momentum, the so-called \emph{a priori}
\par
approach,9 in general, was subjected to growing criticism----e.g., several papers in
\par
Dopuch and Revsine (1973)-particularly the one by Nelson (1973), to which
\par
Wells ( 197 6) responded fervently. Empirical accounting research, on one side, and
\par
agency-information research, on the other, began to dominate academic accounting
\par
and assumed during the 1970s the character of normal science-in contrast to the
\par
\emph{revolutionary }accounting research of the l960s-provided, of course, that Kuhn's
\par
( 1962) approach and terminology can be applied to accounting. 10 Empirical
\par
accounting began to spread rapidly and, towards the end of this decade, brought
\par
forth "positive accounting theory" (PAT). The latter was disseminated in two
\par
papers by Watts and Zimmerman, "Towards a Positive Theory of the
\par
Determination of Accounting Standards ( 1978) and "The Demand and Supply of
\par
Accounting Theories: The Market for Excuses" ( 1979). In spite of the great
\par
popularity of the first of these papers and, to a lesser extent, even the second one,
\par
this approach became highly controversial and encountered much reaction. Apart
\par
from the general criticism of empirical research (mentioned in the previous
\par
section), it became obvious in the mid-1980's that a \emph{direct }influence ofPA T (which
\par
I regard to be a methodology rather than a theory) upon accounting policy is
\par
unlikely. Yet, in this area, major academic journals continued publishing more
\par
papers during the last half of this decade than during the first. In spite of much
\par
enthusiasm for PAT, the 1980s and early 1990s brought growing opposition to it:
\par
e.g., Laughlin, Lowe and Puxty (1982), Tinker, Merino and Neimark ( 1982 ),
\par
Christenson (1983), Kelly (1983), Lowe, Puxty and Laughlin ( 1983), Mattessich
\par
(1984), Schreuder (1984), Peasnell and Williams (1986), Whittington ( 1987),
\par
Demski ( 1988), Whitley ( 1988), Hines ( 1988), Mouck ( 1989), Sterling ( 1990),
\par
Boland and Gordon ( 1992), Schneider ( 1992), Ballwieser ( 1993), Chambers
\par
(1993). These papers range from mild misgivings to vehement rejection of PAT.
\par
Although Watts and Zimmerman (1990) reacted to a few ofthese attacks, their
\par
response is still a point of controversy. In that paper the two authors point out that
\par
their theory has revealed the following regularities and explanations: ( 1) the \emph{bonus}
\par
\emph{plan hypotheses }(i.e., in bonus plans, it is more likely that management selects an
\par
accounting method which \emph{reports }a relatively higher earning figure); (2) the \emph{debt/}
\par
\emph{equity hypothesis }(i.e., management is more likely to select such an "income
\par
increasing" method, the higher a firm's debt/equity ratio is); (3) the \emph{political cost}
\par
\emph{hypothesis }(i.e., management is more likely to select the other method, the "income
\par
decreasing" one, if the firm is relatively large). The variables chosen for thoseAcademic Research in Accounting : The Last SO Years 17
\par
"choice hypotheses" usually are: managerial compensation, other incentives, firm
\par
size, leverage, risk, interest coverage as well as limitations on dividends. As a
\par
major argument in favor of PAT, Watts and Zimmerman ( 1990, p. 140) mention
\par
"the lack of an alternative model with greater explanatory power" in comparison to
\par
the acclaimed low explanatory power of the traditional theory. This, however,
\par
hardly affects the limitations of such a restrictive methodology. Thus PAT can
\par
hardly be regarded to be the very core of our discipline, as occasionally believed.
\par
Empirical accounting research comprises many aspects, and brought forth an
\par
immense number of publications. Above all, it asserted that security prices provide
\par
a measure of information content for such events as changes in statement
\par
presentation, accounting regulations. earnings announcements. etc. The official
\par
AAA ( 1977) evaluation of the "state of the art". the \emph{Statement on Accounting}
\par
\emph{Theory and Theory Acceptance }(SATTA). attempted to summarize this and related
\par
research. but is pessimistic as to the possibility of a general accounting theory.
\par
SATTA also proved to be less influential than the corresponding AAA ( 1966 or
\par
ASOBAT) of the preceding decade. The major areas are. first, \emph{earnings research,}
\par
dealing with such topics as the relationship between share prices and .. abnormal
\par
earnings"' and. second, research in \emph{earnings foreca.Hs }as well as the i1\textasciitilde jormation
\par
\emph{content }of such forecasts. etc. (e.g., Crichfield, Dyckman and Lakonishok 1978;
\par
Givoly and Lakonishok 1979). Since most of these problems stem from financial
\par
accounting, managerial accounting aspects were primarily examined by
\par
experimental and behavioural-organizational research (sec penultimate paragraph
\par
of this section, and the next section). Finally, studies on assessing the risks of
\par
industries, as in Falk and Heintz ( 1975) and Falk and Gordon ( 1978), may also be
\par
counted to this line of empirical research.
\par
The major \emph{results }ofthis first phase ofnonexperimental empirical studies may
\par
be summarized as follows: (I) earnings announcements bear information content;
\par
(2) the market appears to rely on the firm's earnings forecasts; (3) other financial
\par
data of the financial statements are not highly associated with unexpected returns;
\par
(4) there seem to be no major correlations between changes in accounting methods
\par
on a voluntary basis and unexpected returns (apart from auditing changes related
\par
to changes in expected cash flows); (5) the market appears to see through "cosmetic
\par
changes" in financial statements, and F ASB regulations seem not to affect
\par
stockholders' wealth or earning power.
\par
Apart from the provisional nature of those results, they seem to be modest and
\par
disproportionate to the extensive effort of highly sophisticated research. Indeed,
\par
most empirically oriented scholars themselves admit this freely. But considering
\par
the vacuum of rigorous empirical research before the late 1960s, one might see the
\par
results in a different light. Nevertheless, empirical accounting research has been18 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
further criticized (for example, by Daley 1994, p. 44) on two points: (I) the
\par
repetitiveness of studying the same phenomenon, and (2) documenting phenomena
\par
far removed from actual observations. Beyond this, one may add, that few attempts
\par
were made to examine means-end relations from an empirical point of view
\par
(despite an undeniable concern of empirical accountants with policy issues, as
\par
manifested, for example, by Ball and Smith 1992). The results of empirical
\par
accounting and its effect on the "philosophy of financial statement presentation"
\par
and its relation to informational aspects ("security price research" up to the end of
\par
the 1970's) has found its most representative manifestation and summary in Beaver
\par
( 1981-see next section). As to research on adaptive behaviour relating to
\par
information analysis, managerial control and budgeting, there are such
\par
publications as ltami ( 1977), Shank and Churchill ( 1977) on variance analysis, and
\par
Otley (1978) on the use of budgets. Among experimental studies--particularly as
\par
regards earnings vs. cash flows and purpose-orientation, as well as the functional
\par
fixation involved--Abdel-khalik and Keller (1979) may be mentioned. For an
\par
annotated bibliography on cost benefit analysis, see Wood and Campbell ( 1970)
\par
and, for a study on empirical methodology, Abdel-khalik and Ajinkya (1979).
\par
Empirical and other research of the 1970s also tried to clarify accounting
\par
objectives and information goals: e.g., the so called "Trueblood Report" by the
\par
AICPA ( 1973) and its implementation problems (Beaver and Demski 1974; Cyert
\par
and Ijiri 1974; Anton 1976; Goetz and Bimberg 1976, and other contributions,
\par
particularly in JAR's special issue on \emph{Studies in Financial Accounting Objectives}
\par
1974). Of special importance was the "Wheat Report" (AICPA 1972),
\par
recommending the establishment of the Financial Accounting Standards Board
\par
which, in tum, brought forth a long series of statements, among which was F ASB
\par
( 1979), "legislating" compulsory, though supplementary, current cost and price-
\par
level adjustments to income statements of larger corporations. The latter resulted
\par
in lengthy discussions and reactions (e.g., Dopuch and Sunder 1980). A special
\par
focus of empirical research during this period was on behavioural aspects (e.g.,
\par
Acland 1976) including human resource accounting, as well as on the economic
\par
consequences of standard setting, earnings forecasts and stock price behavior-for
\par
the later see, for example, Patell (1976) and, on accounting system choice, see
\par
Uecker (1978); on decision styles, contingency frameworks, etc. Tiessen and
\par
Baker ( 1977); Waterhouse and Tiessen ( 1978). As the individual contributions are
\par
too numerous to be listed here, I refer to the following reviews and anthologies:
\par
Bruns and DeCoster (1969), Hakansson (1973), Gonedes and Dopuch ( 1974),
\par
Abdel-khalik and Keller (1978), Dyckman, Gibbins and Swieringa (1978) and,
\par
later, Dyckman and Zeff ( 1984 ).Academic Research in Accounting : The Last 50 Years 19
