Almost as prominent as empirical accounting, became the endeavour to apply
\par
information economics and agency theory to accounting. The early efforts by
\par
Feltham (e.g .{$\bullet$} 1967, 1972), and other students of the Berkeley School in the
\par
application of information economics to accounting were extended and greatly
\par
elaborated in a series ofpublications (e.g .{$\bullet$} by Demski and Feltham 1972; Feltham
\par
and Demski 1970), mainly by these two authors, either individually or in
\par
cooperation with each other. The most prominent of these joint publications are
\par
Demski and Feltham ( 1976), \emph{Cost Determination-A Conceptual Approach, }and
\par
Demski and Feltham ( 1978), "Economic Incentives in Budgetary Control
\par
Systems"--the latter received in 1994 the prestigious AAA Award for Seminal
\par
Contribution to Accounting Theory. At first this direction consisted in an
\par
application of information economics (concerned with the probability distribution
\par
and value of additional information. as in Feltham 1968: Mock 1969, 1971;
\par
Demski 1972: Baiman 1975) to accounting problems, or with information
\par
inductance (Prakash and Rappaport 1977), etc. But in the second half of the 1970s.
\par
when these issues seemed to be exhausted. it was noticed that descriptive agency
\par
theory (Jensen and Meckling 1976\textbraceright - dealing with the contractual relation
\par
between "principal" and "agent". various agency costs. risk-sharing and incentive
\par
problemr-lcnds itself excellently to a fusion with information economic aspects.
\par
thus resulting in what might be called "analytical agency contract theory" (for
\par
pertinent survey articles. see Baiman 1982. 1990; and Feltham 1984 ). It is
\par
remarkable that this approach reinstated \emph{stewardship }as the central paradigm of
\par
accounting (cf., Gjesdal 1982), despite previous attempts to make \emph{investment}
\par
\emph{decision-making }the pivot of our discipline. Agency theory has even been related
\par
to issues of ethics (see Noreen 1988). To these efforts may be added further
\par
endeavours in funds-flow accounting, such as Weilenmann ( 1981 ), Chesley and
\par
Scheiner (1982) and Moxter (1982, who later may have distanced himself from
\par
this view), and on cash-flow accounting, Lee ( 1972), Heath ( 1978) and ljiri ( 1978);
\par
and, above all, the examination of auditing in the light of modem accounting theory
\par
(e.g., Arens, May and Dominiak 1970; Ijiri and Kaplan 1971; Kaplan 1973; Scott
\par
1975; Joyce 1976; Gibbins 1977; Moonitz and Stamp 1979; Ng and Stoeckenius
\par
1979), as well as the application ofBrunswik's "lens model" to auditing by Ashton
\par
(1974).
\par
Another significant direction emerged during the 1970s in Great Britain (e.g.,
\par
Hopwood 1972, 1978; Gambling 1974), which gained growing attention in the
\par
subsequent decade in America as well as in Continental Europe. Much of its
\par
research is associated with the journal \emph{Accounting. Organizations and Society }and
\par
deals, as this title suggests, with the societal and organizational aspects of our20 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
exceptions, like Mcfarland ( 1970) or, on opportunity costs, by Gould ( 1974) in
\par
England, or publications in Continental Europe (e.g, Adam 1970, Lallmann 1973,
\par
Magnusson 1974, Schoenfeld 1974, Coenenberg 1976), as well as explorations of
\par
a "contingency theory of managerial accounting" (Hayes 1977), or the
\par
investigation of common costs (Wells 1978), or a multi-stage input-output model
\par
of cost accounting (Butterworth and Sigloch 1971 ). This trend is related to the
\par
emerging theory of human resource accounting, as proposed, for example, by
\par
Flamholtz (1972, 1974); for an experiment on human assets statements, see Elias
\par
( 1972). A special place assumes Briloff ( 1972), with his renowned \emph{Unaccountable}
\par
\emph{Accounting, }as one of the earlier manifestations of the "ethical trend" in American
