\par
\textbf{The 1980s and early 1990s: Growth, Further}
\par
\textbf{Consolidation, and Model Building}
\par
This period is unparalleled when it comes to the diversification and volume of
\par
accounting research. The number of academic accounting journals has more than
\par
\emph{trebled }in the 1980s alone, and accounting literature has expanded, I am tempted to
\par
say "exploded". into many directions. 11 The result is not merely a great richness of
\par
contemporary research. but a bewildering variety which is difficult to survey in a
\par
nutshell. The older generation, relatively unsophisticated in modern mathematics.
\par
statistics, probability and investment theory, was followed by a generation much
\par
better trained in these areas. Originally, some of the younger academics seem to
\par
have aimed for a complete break with the past; but during the 1990s those persons
\par
have entered their fiftieth and are acquiring wisdom beyond mere cleverness. Slowly
\par
some of them begin to realize the debt to their predecessors and, what is more
\par
important, to see that no science, particularly not an applied one, can be restricted
\par
to the Procrustes bed of mere formalization and statistical testing. If my cautious
\par
optimism does not deceive me, there are indications that the contempt for, and the
\par
utter rejection of, research beyond "model building" appears to be waning. Model
\par
building (in the analytical as well as statistical-empirical sense) reigned supreme in
\par
the 1980s as a kind of \emph{Glasperlenspiel-and }I do not use this expression in any
\par
derogatory sense, as anyone familiar with Hermann Hesse's (1943/1969) Nobel
\par
prize-winning book (of the same name, but translated as \emph{Magister Ludi, the Glass-}
\par
\emph{Bead Game), }will be well aware. Such a game is of extreme intellectual and cultural
\par
importance, but in the end even its greatest master, the \emph{Magister Ludi }himself, had
\par
to face reality-which seems to be the point and conclusion of Hesse's profound
\par
masterwork. Personally, I have always greatly admired the "model builders" and
\par
recognized their importance, but have often been puzzled by the narrowness and
\par
methodological intolerance of some of them.22 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
In the empirical area it was \emph{Financial Reporting: An Accounting Revolution}
\par
by Beaver ( 1981) which reviewed and summarized the research of the preceding
\par
decades (particularly with regard to the "philosophy of financial reporting" and the
\par
information function), manifesting its future aspirations. Above all, this work
\par
shows that for perfect and complete markets, \emph{economic income }(based on the
\par
discount of net future cash flows) is "virtually unassailable". But where these ideal
\par
conditions do not hold, there is as yet no way of determining the scientifically
\par
correct income concept.
\par
Similarly, the book \emph{Positive Accounting Theory }by Watts and Zimmerman
\par
(1986) recapitulated and consolidated those authors' previous endeavour.
\par
Empirical accounting research continued to flourish in innumerable studies from
\par
the economic consequences of accounting choices (Holthausen and Leftwich
\par
1983) to those of price changes and dividend policies, etc. (Holthausen and
\par
Verrecchia 1988; Richardson, Sefcik and Thomson 1988), including
\par
methodological research (e.g., Ball and Foster 1982); also research in the area of
\par
