\par
( 1) While earnings information is confirmed to be useful, the "usefulness" of
\par
nonearnings information remains uncertain; (2) as regards some voluntary changes
\par
in accounting methods, the \emph{rational }reaction of investors is no longer confirmed,
\par
and irrational tendencies are suspected; it is also claimed that all accounting
\par
changes can have some \emph{real }effect; (3) research that assesses stock market
\par
reactions to financial regulations seems to be consistent with the following three
\par
items: (i) replacement cost accounting, (ii) oil and gas accounting and (iii) lines-of-
\par
business information; (4) MBAR has contributed to finance in two different ways:
\par
(i) it has forced researchers to reconsider the capital asset pricing model as well as
\par
market efficiency by supplying evidence inconsistent with either or both, and (ii)
\par
MBAR has presented evidence linking basic variables of the firm to the systematic
\par
risk of common stocks ( cf., Beaver, Kettler, and Scholes 1970). It has also beenAcademic Research in Accounting : The Last 50 Years 23
