\par
dynamics of accounting. Its central ideas are: ( 1) the notion of income momentum
\par
(shortly "momentum" = growth-rate of wealth per time unit), corresponding in
\par
physics to the linear momentum (mass times velocity= mass-distance per time);
\par
\emph{(2)force }or better \emph{income force }(growth-rate of income momentum per time unit),
\par
corresponding to physical force (mass times acceleration =growth rate of linear
\par
momentum); (3) \emph{impulse }(or \emph{income impulse, }i.e., the increase in income
\par
momentum growth rate), corresponding somewhat to impulse in mechanics
\par
(increase in momentum= force x time). In contrast to most other studies during
\par
this period, Ijiri's is not a derivative from finance but grew out of accounting
\par
proper; in other words, ljiri \emph{internalizes }and incorporates managerial-behavioural
\par
features into the framework of accounting proper. Other recent theories more or
\par
less abandoned this framework, thus converting accounting either into a kind of
\par
economics or finance, or into a branch of the behavioural sciences, thus
\par
\emph{externalizing }our discipline. To the category of foundational work also belongs a
\par
series of papers concerned with \emph{reality issues }(e.g., Tomkins and Groves 1983;
\par
Abdel-khalik and Ajinkya 1983; Morgan 1983; Willmott 1983) and the closely
\par
related question whether income, capital, etc., are notions backed by reality or
\par
mere fictions-see Heath ( 1987) and Sterling ( 1988), countered by Thornton
\par
(1988b) and Mattessich (1995, Chapter 3). Finally, there is the long-standing
\par
question of whether accounting is a normative or positive discipline, or to which
\par
extent it is one and the other, and how a proper integration could come about (e.g.,
\par
Hakansson 1969; Hilton 1980; Ryan 1982; Tinker, Merino and Neimark 1982;
\par
Schreuder 1984; Waller and Jiambalvo 1984; Zebda 1987; Mattessich 1995, Chpts.
\par
10 and 11).26 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
