A particularly fundamental problem of accounting and information economics is
\par
the quest for the necessary and sufficient conditions for \emph{public information }to
\par
possess value. Hakansson, Kunkel, and Ohlson ( 1982) solved this problem for
\par
pure exchange economies: then Kunkel ( 1982) included also \emph{production activity,}
\par
but under most restrictive conditions; finally Ohlson ( 1988) appears to have stated
\par
these conditions on a more general basis. For the centralization with regard to
\par
communication see Melumad and Reichel stein ( 1987); for multiple agencies,
\par
Amershi and Butterworth ( 1988); and for signalling under asymmetric information,
\par
Hughes ( 1986): for decentralization of monitoring systems, Demski, Pate II and
\par
Wolfson (1984): for communication of private information, Feltham and Hughes
\par
( 1988); for voluntary disclosure, Wagenhofer (1990); for multi-task efforts in
\par
agency analysis, see Feltham and Xie (1994), as stimulated by Holmstrom and
\par
Milgrom (1991) and Bushman and Indjejikian (1993).
\par
A series of publications by Ohlson (1983, l987a, l987b, 1989, 1990, 1991,
\par
1995) deal with incentives in multi-task or multi-date settings and other aspects of
\par
capital market theory, leading him to formulate his "theory of clean surplus". This
\par
may be considered a return to a more traditional basis; yet this approach not merely
\par
reformulates basic thoughts on the accountants' "comprehensive income theory"-
\par
previously expressed by Preinreich ( 1938), Peasnell ( 1981, 1982b) and others--,it
\par
also draws new conclusions from it.
\par
Ohlson ( 1987b) demonstrated that income measurement demands a
\par
connection to welfare without any other requirement, such as shareholders'
\par
unanimity (apparently in contrast to Beaver and Demski 1979). Ohlson also shows
\par
that Beaver's and Demski's \emph{rejection }of the "measurement perspective of
\par
accounting" is mistaken; in other words, even where the conditions of perfect
\par
competition, complete markets, and stockholders' unanimity are \emph{not }fulfilled,
\par
income measurement may remain a meaningful and fundamental objective of
\par
accounting. He sees in accounting more than Beaver's and Demski's "cost-
\par
effective communication procedure" and pleads for combining the \emph{informational}Academic Research in Accounting: The Last \emph{50 }Years 27
\par
\emph{aspect }with the \emph{measurement aspect }of accounting. Furthermore, Ohlson (1991)
\par
showed that the paper by Ball and Brown ( 1968) falls short of "an equilibrium
\par
model linking value with earnings through expectations (or information)", and that
\par
Ball's and Brown's notion ofunexpected earnings "appears to have the status of a
\par
'folklore concept' with limited economic content". All this necessitates a rigorous
\par
theory, the starting point for which he sees in the "clean surplus equation" (change
\par
in book value of owners' equity + dividends = earnings) as a basis for security
\par
evaluation. Ohlson also shows why this equation need not be a tautology but can
\par
be explained in economic and empirical terms (i.e., each of the three variables of
\par
the clean surplus equation act as an independent information component, leading
\par
in the long-run to an equilibrium in the empirical sense). Although Ohlson, among
\par
other recent researchers, does not particularly favour cash-flow accounting, this
\par
topic is continued to be pursued in the literature: e.g., Street and Stanga ( 1989),
\par
Lawson ( 1992), Lee ( 1993 ), Dec how ( 1994 ), and Finger ( 1994 ).
\par
However, Ohlson ( 1990) comes up with the somewhat surprising result that
\par
without conditions other than "no arbitrage" (between securities), \emph{on(v anticipated}
\par
\emph{dividends, }but neither cash flows nor anticipated earnings, can serve for valuation
\par
on an expected present value basis. This can be best understood when considering
\par
that, properly speaking, (i) dividends, and not earnings or even cash flows (unless
\par
better defined than usually), are the primary variables to be discounted. and (ii) the
\par
proper connections between dividends, earnings and other variables have to be
\par
established before the discounting of earnings becomes "legitimate". This analysis
\par
reveals (in rigorous mathematical terms) how the earnings process affects the
\par
valuation of the firm and how such flow variables as earnings and cash flow
\par
reconcile with the change in such stock variables as owners' equity. Ohlson regards
\par
\emph{the balance sheet and its stock variables as basic, }while the flow variables are
\par
regarded as derivatives, serving to explain the changes of stock variables under
\par
uncertainty-some of this may remind of Rieger's ( 1936) \emph{opposition }to
\par
Schmal en bach's ( 1925) Dynamische Bilanztheorie. The major conclusions of
\par
Ohlson (1990) were: (1) there is no basic valuation theory; (2) only anticipated
\par
dividends can serve as a generically valid capitalization (present value) attribute of
\par
security; (3) the notion of risk in general, and models such as the CAPM occur
\par
only as special cases; (4) the notion that one can capitalize cash flows rather than
\par
dividends requires additional (and relatively stringent) assumptions; (5) the
\par
existing theory of pure earnings under uncertainty Jacks unity regarding its
\par
meaning and characteristics. In other words, he argues that only a simple notion of
\par
"pure" earnings is economically relevant and sufficient to determine a security's
\par
pay-off (i.e., its price plus dividend).28 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
The papers by Ohlson (1995) and Feltham and Ohlson (1995) further develop
\par
Ohlson's original ideas and, despite their relatively recent publication, have
\par
already become quite famous. 12 Lundholm (1995, p. 749), for example, states that
\par
these two papers "are landmark works in financial accounting. The papers provide
\par
a logical consistent framework for thinking about the valuation of accounting
\par
numbers. They show how to use book value and income together in the same
\par
valuation model properly, rather than in an \emph{ad hoc }fashion; they show how the
\par
valuation model can be used to capture different properties of different asset
\par
classes, such as operating and financial assets; and they use the model to illustrate
\par
the effect of conservative accounting on the relation between equity value,
\par
accounting book value, and future earnings." And Bernard (1995, 733-734), too,
\par
acknowledges that:
\par
The Ohlson (1995) and Feltham and Ohlson (1995) studies stand among
\par
the most important developments in capital markets research in the last
\par
several years. The studies provide a foundation for redefining the
\par
appropriate objective of research on the relation between financial statement
\par
data and firm value. At the same time, they provide some structure for
\par
modeling in a field where structure has been sorely lacking.
\par
The value of Ohlson ( 1995) and Feltham and Ohlson ( 1995) can best be
\par
appreciated when one recognizes where the studies fit on the evolutionary
\par
tree of research. These studies return to issues so basic as to render them
\par
direct descendants of work done no later than the 1960s (e.g., Edwards
\par
and Bell 1961; Modigliani and Miller 1958; Miller and Modigliani 1961;
\par
and Preinreich 1938). Ohlson ( 1995) and Feltham and Ohlson (1995)
\par
represent the base of a branch that capital markets research might have
\par
followed, but did not. Instead, framed within the so-called informational
\par
perspective, research since the late 1960s developed without much emphasis
\par
on the precise structure of the relation between accounting data and firm
\par
value. In a sense, Ohlson ( 1995) and Feltham and Ohlson ( 1995) return to
\par
"step one" and attempt to build a more solid foundation for further work.
\par
A fair evaluation of this work must consider that it is only a first step, not
\par
yet intended to represent a fully developed framework. Nevertheless, even
\par
in its embryonic state, the path laid down in Ohlson ( 1995) and Feltham
\par
and Ohlson ( 1995) offers some important and immediate contributions ....
\par
Very little prior research has been framed as a contribution to fundamental
\par
analysis; indeed, the very words \emph{fundamental analysis }were out of vogue
\par
for years (Penman 1992).
\par
The crucial contribution of Feltham and Ohlson ( 1995) lies in relating the
\par
market value of a firm to its \emph{financial }as well as \emph{operating }data (the latter are
\par
considered to be an important residual, after borrowing and lending data are
\par
separated). If the market value is taken to be the net present value of discounted
\par
anticipated dividends (which might be disputed under less ideal conditions), theAcademic Research in Accounting: The Last 50 Years 29
\par
authors show that Ohlson's "clean surplus" equals the book value enhanced by the
\par
