Feltham and Ohlson ( 1995) then introduce a set of secondary assumptions,
\par
conditions. restrictions, etc., and derive a series of interesting results (propositions
\par
or theorems), the most important ones of which deal with three major issues: (i) the
\par
separation of operating activities and financial activities and the different
\par
behaviour of these two activities. (ii) the comparison between unbiased and
\par
conservative accounting, and (iii) the analysis of predicting future earnings and
\par
different asset growth. This research may, for the following reasons, become of
\par
special significance:
\par
I. It constitutes a break with the overemphasis on stock price behaviour, so
\par
typical for "modem" accounting research.
\par
2. Instead, the analysis concentrates more on the relation between book value,
\par
market value, earnings, dividends and cash flow, as well as the prediction of
\par
earnings and growth patterns.
\par
3. It thus connects the "more traditional" direction with rigorous stochastic model
\par
building.
\par
4. It establishes a framework for future research broad enough to incorporate
\par
''traditional" as well as "modem" accounting research, offering a wide range
\par
of possibilities (perhaps even incorporating price-level adjustments, current
\par
value accounting, etc.).
\par
5. It might establish a basis for comparing the pragmatic values for pursuing a
\par
variety of information objectives (see Mattessich 1995, Chapter II) with the
\par
ideal or \emph{scientific }value (though this might require a procedure for determining
\par
such a scientific value beyond mere perfect markets and/or completeAcademic Research in Accounting : The Last 50 Years 31
\par
infonnation). If those hopes can be fulfilled, the new approach might constitute
\par
a genuine turning point in our discipline; one at least as important as the advent
\par
of empirical accounting at the end of the 1960s.
