aspects of management accounting research had continued, consideration of its
\par
more practical aspects received a boost after publication of Johnson's and Kaplan's
\par
( 1987) \emph{Relevance Lost-The Rise and Fall of Management Accounting }(possibly
\par
foreshadowed by Anthony 1975), preceded, accompanied, or followed by many
\par
publications in America (e.g., Kaplan 1978; Hayes 1983; Ferris and Livingstone
\par
1987; Antle and Demski 1988; Foster and Homgren 1988; Magee 1988; Kanodia
\par
1993), as well as ''overseas" (for the latter see such papers as, Czamiawska-Joerges
\par
1988; Lukka 1988; Schneider 1988; Albers 1989; Hiromoto 1989, 1991; Ewert
\par
1992; Schoenfeld 1992; Vodrazka 1992; Wiese 1994; Pfaff 1995; Wagenhofer
\par
1995). Indeed, the time was ready to reassess this area. This was done by a series of
\par
relevant. occasionally even award-winning, publications (e.g., Hayes 1983;
\par
Tiessen and Waterhouse 1983; Armitage 1985; Bromwich and Hopwood 1986;
\par
Atkinson 1987a, 1987b; Bruns and Kaplan 1987; Ferris and Livingstone 1987;
\par
Staubus 1987; Bromwich 1990; Wagenhofer and Ewert 1993; Weber 1993). This
\par
"renaissance" of managerial accounting not only brought forth new concepts or
\par
revived old slogans, such as "strategic cost management", "activity based costing"
\par
(e.g., Aiyathurai, Cooper and Sinha 1991), etc., but, in some instances, tried to
\par
combine financial management with management accounting (e.g., BanerjeeAcademic Research in Accounting : The Last 50 Years 35
\par
1984), or generated a series of progressive (sometimes more or less revised) texts
\par
or reference works in managerial accounting (e.g., Mepham 1980; Schneider 1984;
\par
Magee 1986; Balachandran, Li and Magee 1987; Bruns and Kaplan 1987; Kaplan
\par
and Atkinson 1989; Busse von Colbe and La13mann 1991; Cooper and Kaplan
\par
1991; Schweitzer and Kupper 1991; Anthony, Dearden and Govindarajan 1992;
\par
Miinnel 1992; Coenenberg 1993; Kilger and Vikas 1993; Kloock and Schildbach
\par
1993; Shank and Govindarajan 1993; Riebel 1993; Stepan and Zechner 1993; Pfaff
\par
1993; Swoboda 1993; Weber 1994; Homgren, Foster and Datar 1994; Ewert and
\par
Wagenhofer 1995). Occasionally, this brought about a surprising shift in
\par
methodology, namely a \emph{revival of the case method }(cf., Smith, Whipp and Willmott
\par
1988), originally pioneered by the Harvard Business School. Thus, clinical and
\par
field-based research were considered to be the major remedy for overcoming the
\par
loss of relevance. To improve industrial productivity in the short-run, this kind of
\par
research may, indeed, be more suited, whereas the hopes for analytical and
\par
behavioural-statistical research lie in the long-run. Though the critical aspect of
\par
this new trend aims at replacing traditional overhead allocation procedures (e.g.
\par
the direct labour basis) by new ones (e.g. set-up times), it is uncertain whether
\par
these reforms are sufficient. Why, for instance, do industrial giants. such as the
\par
Siemens Co .{$\bullet$} abandon separate cost accounting systems in favour of integrating
\par
them into financial accounting (see Ziegler 1994; Pfaff 1994a)? Furthermore, the
\par
refom1 tendencies have hardly produced a general, yet purpose-oriented, theory
\par
that is flexible enough to embrace all possible allocation bases. However, as time
\par
goes on, the call for such purpose-orientation becomes more pressing as the
\par
following quotation from Pfaff confirms:
\par
The problem of relative costs can be properly characterized with the words
\par
"different costs for different purposes" [footnote referring to Clark ( 1923 )].
\par
On the one side it would be necessary-and this has become clear in
\par
Sections 3 and 4--to determine for every problem those costs which are
\par
relevant in each case. On the other hand, such an approach is frequently
\par
not realizable in practice .... A possible solution could be to differentiate
\par
cost accounting according to the two main purposes mentioned. For tasks
\par
of influencing behaviour, a simple system, transparent for decision makers,
\par
might possibly suffice--essentially it would have to supply a stimulus for
\par
economic behaviour. For tasks of decision making, however, special cost
\par
calculations would, from a specific point onwards, be required to determine
\par
the costs relevant for the pertinent situation. (Pfaff 1994a, translated, p.
\par
1081-1082).
\par
A similar, though more general, point of view has repeatedly been emphasized
\par
in several ofmy own publications (most recently in Mattessich 1995). As pointed
\par
out, management accounting was not quite as much neglected during the 1970s36 Asia-Pacific Journal of Accounting June 1996
\par
and early 1980s as some scholars may have claimed. Apart from dealing with more
\par
traditional topics such as \emph{transfer pricing }(e.g., Manes and Verrecchia 1981-82;
\par
Swieringa and Waterhouse 1982; Eccles 1985; Amershi and Cheng 1990), as well
\par
as the relation to organization theory, several researchers, endeavouring to apply
\par
information economics and agency theory to accounting (for an overview see
\par
Namazi 1985), have shown considerable interest in management accounting (cf.,
\par
the book in honour and memory of John Butterworth, edited by Feltham, Amershi
\par
and Ziemba 1988). But most of this research was based on highly simplified and
\par
unrealistic assumptions; though rigorous and theoretically valuable, this kind of
\par
research is of predominantly academic interest. 17 The complexity of reality seems
\par
to offer only two choices: first, the abstract-atomistic or "simple-minded"
\par
approach, and second, the holistic-global or "muddle headed" one. But actually
\par
both of them are \emph{indispensable }for any branch of science but particularly for an
\par
applied discipline. Yet the general trend in management accounting during the late
\par
1980s may not have merely been a reaction to analytical accounting but, possibly,
\par
an overreaction. Each of these two methods has its particular strength as well as its
\par
weakness, and the only reasonable course seems to be a relativistic one, i.e., an
\par
application of one or the other method, depending on the specific situation.
