\par
\textbf{Conclusion: The Reawakening of Normative Issues,}
\par
\textbf{and Pouring Old Wine into New Skins}
\par
A realistic assessment leaves little doubt that, in spite of the incredible expansion
\par
during the last decade or two, accounting is in a crisis (or is approaching one); this
\par
is confirmed by several publications or other presentations (e.g., Belkaoui 1989;
\par
Demski et al. 1991; Ernst \& Young Foundation 1994). In Mattessich ( 1995, Chapter
\par
I) I have argued that one might distinguish between: (I) a \emph{legalistic and social}
\par
\emph{crisis }of accounting (insufficient auditor independence, lobbying for standards by
\par
large corporations, etc.); (2) a \emph{moral and spiritual crisis }(declining ethical attitudes
\par
in government, business and the profession); and (3) an \emph{academic crisis }(overrating
\par
the decision function at the cost of the stewardship function of accounting, and
\par
preferring PAT over a methodology that takes value judgments and means-end
\par
relations into consideration). I firmly believe that accounting professors have
\par
overextended their purely academic ambitions and neglected the fact that accounting
\par
is ultimately an \emph{applied }science-see also Schipper (1994) who refers to "policy
\par
relevant research" in accounting, which she clearly distinguishes from "conventional
\par
academic research", and Mozes ( 1992) who tries to provide a framework for
\par
nonnative accounting research. To make this point even more emphatic, I attempted
\par
in Mattessich ( 1995) a critical examination of the foundations and the normative
\par
structure of our discipline, presenting a conditional-normative methodology. By
\par
this I hoped to promote a \emph{synthesis }between the "positive accounting theory" of
\par
America and the "critical-interpretive camp" of Europe (see items 2 and 3 of the
\par
preceding Section). 1 R The present situation, where the adherents of PAT try, more
\par
or less, to ignore the "critical-interpretive camp", is hardly tenable in the long-run.
\par
Some resolution has to be found, the more so as the number of academic accountants
\par
clamouring for greater attention to ethical and other value judgments in accounting
\par
has become a world-wide movement. Once it is realized that the behavioural science
\par
of accounting requires a different methodology than does accounting proper, a
\par
synthesis might be possible. An applied science, like accounting, cannot exclusively
\par
rely on cause and effect hypotheses, it has to supplement them by instrumental orAcademic Research in Accounting: The Last 50 Years 43
\par
means-end relations. Contrary to common belief, the latter are accessible, but sofar
\par
merely through trial and error. The big challenge for accounting, no less than for
\par
other applied disciplines, is to find scientific means that could explain and improve
\par
this trial and error approach. But beyond the growing interest in normative (and
\par
conditional-normative) issues, there comes encouragement from another direction.
\par
This is the return to traditional issues and their modern extension, as encountered,
\par
partly in agency theory (e.g., the stewardship function), partly in more recent research
\par
introduced by Feltham and Ohlson (e.g., the present value relation, the clean surplus
\par
principle, etc. as axioms with their theorems). Indeed, the latter approach has been
\par
hailed as a "breeze of fresh air". Thus there is hope that accountants of the next
\par
century may bring a broader vision to our discipline, helping to master hitherto
\par
unresolved issues no less than new problems looming on the horizon.
