R esearchers in artificial intelligence and decision analysis share a concern with the construction
\par
of formal models of human knowledge and expertise. Historically, however, their ap-
\par
proaches to these problems have diverged. Members of these two communities have recently
\par
discovered common ground: a family of graphical models of decision theory known as influence
\par
diagrams or as belief networks. These models are equally attractive to theoreticians, decision
\par
modelers, and designers of knowledge-based systems. From a theoretical perspective, they
\par
combine graph theory, probability theory and decision theory. From an implementation per-
\par
spective, they lead to powerful automated systems. Although many practicing decision analysts
\par
have already adopted influence diagrams as modeling and structuring tools, they may remain
\par
unaware of the theoretical work that has emerged from the artificial intelligence community.
\par
This paper surveys the first decade or so of this work.
\par
(Decision Analysis; Artificial Intelligence; Influence Diagrams; Belief Networks)
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Researchers in Artificial Intelligence (Al) and Decision
\par
Analysis (DA) both study formal models of human
\par
knowledge and expertise. This common concern not-
\par
withstanding, the first few efforts to define an intersec-
\par
tion of Al and DA (see, e.g. Edwards (1962), Feldman
\par
and Sproull (1977)) were less than successful. In the
\par
years between 1976 and 1981, however, Howard,
\par
Matheson, and their colleagues developed influence
\par
diagrams (IDs), which can be viewed as a graphical
\par
middle ground between the psychologically valid elic-
\par
itation techniques used by decision analysts and the
\par
computational power of the computer (Miller et al. 1976,
\par
Owen 1978, Howard and Matheson 1984). This in-
\par
novation-and the subsequent introduction of belief
\par
networks (BNs) in Al-led to a convergence of segments
\par
of the DA and Al communities. The distinction between
\par
an ID-based Al system and a system for automated DA
\par
is now more a matter of the designers' backgrounds and
\par
affiliations than of the systems themselves (Levitt
\par
1988b).
\par
This paper surveys the rapidly growing body of work
\par
devoted to IDs and BNs. Many references about their
\par
theoretical underpinnings already exist (Shachter 1986,
\par
Pearl 1988, Lauritzen and Spiegelhalter 1988, Shachter
\par
1988). Discussions of their applicability to elicitation
\par
and modeling are fewer, but readily available (Howard
\par
1989, Abramson 1991c). Writeups of specific systems
\par
provide lucid accounts of actual implementation efforts
\par
(Heckerman et al. 1992, Heckerman and Nathwani
\par
1992a, 1992b, Abramson and Finizza 1991b, Andreas-
\par
sen et al. 1987). The community of researchers inter-
\par
ested in these topics is growing quite rapidly, however,
\par
and many of its members have been exposed to only
\par
one aspect of the models. We have targeted this survey
\par
at one such group: DA practitioners who are more
