2. The Basics
\par
2.1. Motivation
\par
Al and DA have traditionally played to different au-
\par
diences. The primary consumers of decision analyses
\par
have been decision makers (DMs) faced with particu-
\par
larly important decisions. These DMs hire analysts to
\par
encode the information relevant to their decisions, to
\par
help them organize their own thoughts, and to rec-
\par
ommend "rational" decisions. Since computation has
\par
always been one of the greatest barriers to successful
\par
analyses, members of the DA community have long
\par
recognized the importance of automation (Edwards
\par
1962). For the most part, however, automated DA re-
\par
mained restricted to calculations on decision trees until
\par
the early 1980s when Howard and Matheson introduced
\par
the ID (Howard and Matheson 1984, Howard 1988).
\par
In addition to automation, IDs addressed a long-stand-
\par
ing problem of the DA community, namely the com-
\par
plexity of representing hierarchical Bayesian inference.
\par
The difficulty inherent in this task can probably best be
\par
appreciated by comparing Schum's excellent analysis
\par
of inference without the use of IDs (Schum 1989) to
\par
any of the analyses that use them (Howard 1989).
\par
Throughout most of Al's formative years, on the other
\par
hand, the field was essentially restricted to a few re-
\par
search laboratories. This isolation changed in the early
\par
1980s, with the development of expert (or knowledge-
\par
based) systems. These systems are based on a simple
\par
idea: elicit large amounts of information about a general
\par
problem domain from human experts, encode it into a
\par
system, and then apply it to solve individual instances
\par
of the problem as they arise. One of the most con-
\par
founding research problems to plague the designers of
\par
these systems was uncertainty. How should knowledge-
\par
based systems use information that is incomplete, im-
\par
perfect, or otherwise less than certain? Several schools
\par
of thought emerged, ranging from the qualitative and
\par
symbolic to the highly mathematical (Ng and Abramson
\par
1990b). Despite the obvious appeal of probability the-
\par
ory, many Al researchers preferred ad hoc symbolic ap-
\par
proaches and / or quasi-probabilistic calculi because they
