The more-or-less simultaneous need for graphical rep-
\par
resentations of decision theory by two communities has
\par
led to a great deal of cross-disciplinary communication.
\par
Unfortunately, it has also led to a plethora of terms that
\par
describe fundamentally similar constructs. Since many
\par
of these terms introduce distinctions without a differ-
\par
ence, however, most readers would probably be served
\par
best by making only a single distinction, namely the
\par
one between a BN and an ID.
\par
IDs and BNs are both directed acyclic graphs (DAGs)
\par
in which (i) nodes represent individual variables, (ii)
\par
arcs demonstrate influence among the nodes, and (iii)
\par
functions associated with the arcs indicate the nature
\par
of that influence. Most researchers speak about BNs
\par
when their models capture only chance occurrences and
\par
deterministic events, and IDs when they capture value
\par
judgements and decisions, as well.
\par
In very general terms, BN/ID models capture four
\par
types of domain information: objects, states, influences,
\par
and functionalities. Objects include all items that might
\par
have an impact on the domain. Each one is represented
\par
by an abstract random variable and is modeled as a
\par
node. States define the sets of values that a variable may
\par
assume. State-sets may be binary, multivalued, contin-
\par
uous, or even symbolic. Influences are shown as arcs,
\par
which are drawn to indicate the possibility of a direct
\par
relationship between two variables. In a valid model,
\par
no set of arcs forms a cycle. In a well-designed network,
\par
only strong degrees of influence lead to arcs; weaker
\par
interrelations are captured by sequences of arcs. Func-
\par
tionalities assign specific relationships (such as condi-
\par
tional probability distributions or algebraic functions)
\par
to sets of arcs.
\par
Several authors have related from experience that
\par
objects, states, and influences can often be elicited with
\par
relative ease, while the elicitation of functionalities
\par
generally creates a bottleneck (Abramson and Ng 1993,
