2.3. Levels of Abstraction
\par
Two levels of abstraction are used as part of the ID
\par
representation scheme (Henrion et al. 1988, Henrion et
\par
al. 1991, Agogino and Rege 1987): the relational (to-
\par
pological), and the numeric (functional). In very general
\par
terms, the relational level captures objects and influ-
\par
ences, the numeric level states and functionalities.
\par
The Relational Level. The relational level captures
\par
the qualitative structure of a problem or a domain (Ago-
\par
gino and Rege 1987, Henrion et al. 1988, Henrion et
\par
al. 1991). A relational description of a domain reveals
\par
the variables that the model's author considers impor-
\par
tant enough to include in the model, and the relation-
\par
ships and the flow of information among those vari-
\par
ables.
\par
Relational-level nodes capture four broad classes of
\par
objects:
\par
(i) Chance Nodes, usually drawn as circles or ovals,
\par
represent abstract random variables.
\par
(ii) Deterministic Nodes, usually drawn as double cir-
\par
cles or ovals, capture constant values and/ or variables
\par
whose values are assigned through algebraically deter-
\par
mined relationships.
\par
(iii) Decision (Control) Nodes, usually drawn as
\par
squares, represent (off-line) actions selected by the DM.
\par
(iv) Value Nodes, drawn as diamonds, model a DM's
\par
objectives.
\par
One common (albeit not necessary) assumption is
\par
that an ID contains exactly one value node.
\par
Arcs in IDs are either influential or informational
\par
(Agogino and Rege 1987, Schachter 1986). Arcs point-
\par
ing to decision nodes are informational because the di-
\par
rect predecessors of a decision node correspond to un-
\par
certain variables whose values will be known at the
\par
time that the decision is made. Arcs connecting two
\par
chance nodes are influential; they signify that an influ-
\par
ence may exist between the two nodes. Consider the
\par
BN of Figure 1 (a). One interpretation of this diagram
\par
is A may influence B, where "influence" means that
\par
knowing A is relevant to assessing the chances asso-
\par
ciated with B's outcome. Now consider the equivalent
\par
BN of Figure 1 (b). The equivalence of these networks
\par
arises because they both imply that knowledge of A
\par
imparts information about B, and vice versa.
\par
The Numeric Level. The numeric level captures
\par
functional relationships among nodes. Although many
\par
types of relationships are allowable, the basic purpose
\par
of the numeric/functional specification of a model is
\par
to allow a global joint probability distribution to be fac-
\par
tored into marginals and conditionals. This factorization
\par
allows the model's author to specify only the best-un-
\par
derstood direct relationships among nodes. The deeply
\par
nested aggregate relationships that define a global
\par
model, and that actually reassess desired output vari-
\par
ables in terms of readily available inputs, are calculated
\par
automatically and algorithmically.
\par
In somewhat less technical terms, the states assumed
\par
by a node's predecessors define its inputs. The func-
\par
tional form associated with each node must specify an
\par
output for every possible combination of states that its
\par
predecessors may assume. The most obvious types of
