3. Theoretical Foundations
\par
Certain relationships, such as qualitative characteriza-
\par
tions of relevance and dependence among variables,
\par
seem to be particularly well suited to graphs. By com-
\par
bining the qualitative representational power of graphs
\par
with the numeric precision of probabilities, IDs and BNs
\par
promise to become important and powerful modeling
\par
tools. The combination of two disparate theories-one
\par
essentially qualitative and visual, the other inherently
\par
quantitative and conceptual-also raises a variety of
\par
interesting questions. Some of them, of course, address
\par
the representational capabilities and limitations of these
\par
new models: What types of dependencies and inde-
\par
pendencies can be deduced from the topological prop-
\par
erties of a graph? Other questions, however, are some-
\par
what subtler; they deal with the relationship between
\par
the visual and the conceptual, between the graphs and
\par
the probabilities. Much of the underlying theory thus
\par
addresses the following broad classes of questions:
