\par
* Which types of relationships can be inferred from
\par
probabilistic calculations, and how can they be captured
\par
in a graph?
\par
* Which types of relationships can be inferred from
\par
graphical manipulations, and how can they be repre-
\par
sented probabilistically?
\par
* How are graphs updated when probabilities are
\par
manipulated, how are probabilities updated when
\par
graphs are manipulated, and what happens to both
\par
graphs and probabilities when new information is dis-
\par
covered?
\par
3.1. Basic Terms and Concepts
\par
Most people recognize that some domain items (vari-
\par
ables) are more closely interrelated than others. Ob-
\par
servations of this sort lead to descriptions of depen-
\par
dence, indirect dependence, independence, and con-
\par
ditional independence. As a result, it is often useful to
\par
think of information provided by an expert as a collec-
\par
tion of dependence and independence statements about
\par
certain domain items. These collections of statements,
\par
under the name dependency models, are central to the
\par
theory of BNs.
\par
In its most basic and intuitive form, independence
\par
means irrelevance to inference; if items x and y are mu-
\par
tually independent, then no amount of information
\par
about x will reveal anything about y, and vice versa.
\par
The easiest way to conceptualize independence in a
\par
graph is by picturing two nodes with no paths between
\par
them; the nodes are pictorially unconnected to represent
\par
their conceptual independence. Probability theory also
\par
provides a simple method of capturing independence;
\par
if the probability of x remains unchanged by information
\par
about y, (P(x) = P(x I y)), then x and y are independent.
\par
The difference between the ways that graphs and prob-
\par
abilities represent independence highlights a key point.
\par
Although many mathematical formalisms provide
\par
mechanisms for capturing independence, most of them
\par
only approximate abstract independence models. Pearl
\par
proved that some forms of probabilistic independence
\par
can not be represented in a graph (Pearl 1988). As a
\par
result, anything that is said about independencies cap-
\par
tured in (and propagated through) a BN is only an ap-
\par
proximation to the sorts of independencies that could
\par
be captured by an abstract independence model.
\par
These difficulties notwithstanding, independence is
\par
a very powerful concept, which when recognized can
\par
increase the efficiency of almost any procedure by in-
\par
dicating what pieces of information are immaterial to the
\par
question on hand, and where not to bother looking for an
\par
answer. Unfortunately, complete independence among
\par
variables in the same domain is rare enough to be almost
\par
meaningless. A somewhat weaker, but much more
\par
widely applicable, concept is conditional independence:
\par
sometimes two variables are interdependent only in the
\par
absence of information about a third variable. Once that
\par
third variable becomes known, the first two are mutually
\par
independent.
\par
Conditional independence increases the compactness
\par
of representation and the efficiency of data collection
\par
and information processing. It is thus a crucial concept,
\par
and one that deserves a great deal of scrutiny, partic-
\par
ularly as it relates to the relationship between the way
\par
in which it is captured in a graph and the way in which
\par
it is captured in a probability distribution. The use of
\par
probability distributions as dependence models is
\par
straightforward. In order to use graphs to represent and
\par
to derive independence relations, on the other hand, a
\par
suitable set of topological characteristics had to be des-
\par
ignated. Independence among a DAG's variables, for
\par
example, is said to be captured by the lack of a directed
\par
path between them, or if every path between them con-
\par
tains at least one pair of converging (i.e., head-to-head)
\par
arrows.
\par
Pearl, his colleagues, his students, and several inde-
\par
pendent investigators introduced many definitions (and
\par
a great deal of mathematical machinery) to discuss the
\par
relationships among colloquial (or abstract) indepen-
\par
dence, graphical independence, and probabilistic in-
\par
dependence (Pearl and Paz 1986, Pearl 1988, Geiger et
\par
al. 1990, Russel et al. 1990, Studeny 1990). In particular,
\par
Pearl proved that there are probability distributions that
\par
can not be translated directly into a graph without losing
\par
some important information (Pearl 1988). In these in-
\par
stances, graphical translations may capture either all of
\par
the dependencies, or all of the independencies, but not
\par
both. If a graph contains arcs between all pairs of le-
\par
gitimately dependent nodes, (as well as between some
\par
pairs of nodes that are actually independent), then
\par
graphical independence implies probabilistic indepen-
\par
dence; a graph with this property is called an I-Map of
