\par
the distribution. Similarly, if a graph accounts for all of
\par
the distribution's independencies (as well as possibly
\par
some spurious ones), then graphical dependence im-
\par
plies probabilistic dependence; a graph with this prop-
\par
erty is called a D-Map of the distribution. Graphs that
\par
are both I-Maps and D-Maps are called perfect maps.
\par
Note that for any probability distribution, an empty
\par
graph is a trivial D-Map and a complete graph is a trivial
\par
I-Map.
\par
Pearl's theory of BN's progressed from the introduc-
\par
tion of these new terms for graphical independence,
\par
through the axiomatization of a class of dependency
\par
models called graphoids and semigraphoids, to the formal
\par
definition of a BN as a DAG that is a minimal I-Map of
\par
a probability distribution (Pearl and Paz 1986, Pearl
\par
1988). (A minimal I-Map is one from which no arc may
\par
be removed without indicating a spurious indepen-
\par
dence. Appropriate minimization increases the efficiency
\par
of both elicitation and computation.) Two particularly
\par
important points to emerge from the development of
\par
this theory were (i) the establishment of formal rules
\par
within which the ternary relation I (X, Z, Y ) -read "X"
\par
is independent of Y, given Z "-may be discussed, and
\par
(ii) the derivation of a formal mathematical definition
\par
of a BN.
\par
The mathematical formalization of BNs, in addition
\par
to being of theoretical interest, opened the door to heu-
\par
ristic algorithms for the automated BN construction.
\par
Many automated construction techniques are based on
\par
Pearl's own boundary strata method (Pearl 1988). These
\par
methods emerged from the observation that a "good"
\par
I-Map is not only minimal, but also sparse. The property
\par
of being minimal simply means that no arc may be re-
\par
moved; it does not mean that a completely different
\par
configuration could not capture the same information
\par
more efficiently. This distinction is crucial because only
\par
a sparse BN is likely to be comprehensible to the user,
\par
to lead to reasonable elicitations, and to be computa-
\par
tionally tractable (Russel et al. 1990). The boundary-
\par
strata-based techniques address this call for sparseness
\par
by iteratively reordering the relations in a dependency
\par
model before (and while) building the BN. Different
\par
orders lead to computationally equivalent models with
\par
vastly different numbers of arcs.
\par
The boundary-strata-based methods are, of course,
\par
not the only known BN-construction techniques. Others
\par
are discussed in ?5. It is particularly important to note
\par
that automated BN construction techniques (e.g., the
\par
boundary strata method) lead to BNs that meet the for-
\par
mal, topological definition of a BN as a minimal I-map.
\par
BNs built by eliciting structure from experts are unlikely
\par
to meet this requirement. Nevertheless, most (if not all)
\par
of the algorithms derived within a theoretical setting
\par
for minimal I-map BNs should be equally applicable to
\par
those built through direct elicitation.
\par
3.2. Key Lessons
\par
Although every modeler likes to know that the models
\par
that he or she uses are firmly rooted in mathematical
\par
theory, most modelers are content to know that the the-
\par
ory exists without spending too much time worrying
\par
about its details. At the same time, many theoreticians
\par
like to believe that their work could (at least in theory)
\par
be put to practical use. The theory of BNs is mathe-
\par
matically elegant and quite detailed. Few of the specific
\par
definitions, theorems, proofs, and algorithms, however,
\par
are likely to be interesting enough to transcend the
\par
community of theoreticians. In this section, we have
\par
attempted to extract some of the theory's most salient
\par
points. By way of summary and review, these points-
\par
all of which should interest theoreticians and practitio-
\par
ners alike-include:
\par
* The common concept captured by both DAGs and
\par
probability distributions is independence.
\par
* The semigraphoid and graphoid axioms provide a
\par
language in which abstract independence relations can
\par
be described. Probability distributions and DAGs can
\par
both be shown to be special cases of these abstract in-
\par
dependence relations.
\par
* There are sets of independencies induced by prob-
\par
ability distributions that can not be captured by DAGs.
\par
In other words, it may be impossible to generate a DAG
\par
that captures all of the dependencies and all of the in-
\par
dependencies that are captured by an input probability
\par
distribution.
\par
* A BN representation of a given probability distri-
\par
bution captures all of the distribution's dependencies,
\par
but not necessarily all of its independencies. No arc
\par
may be removed from the BN, however, without losing
\par
a necessary dependency.
