\par
4. Inference Methods and
\par
Algorithms
\par
The significance of IDs and BNs to modelers and system
\par
designers lies more in their representational power than
\par
in their theoretical elegance. The design of a useful BN-
\par
or ID-based system, in turn, can only be accomplished
\par
if techniques exist to (i) elicit the information necessary
\par
to build the model, and (ii) draw inferences and update
\par
information within a model once it has been built and
\par
put on line. Unfortunately, the elicitation of the infor-
\par
mation necessary to build a BN-like the elicitation
\par
necessary to build most models-remains essentially
\par
an art. Although several articles have addressed the is-
\par
sue (Howard 1989, Abramson and Ng 1993), model
\par
construction has received far less than its fair share of
\par
attention. Algorithms for drawing inferences and an-
\par
swering queries, on the other hand, have been widely
\par
discussed in the literature.
\par
Inference algorithms are used to respond to queries
\par
that the user asks the system. The type of query depends
\par
(among other things) on the modeled domain and on
\par
the relevant functionalities-and in particular on the
\par
formalism chosen to capture uncertain relationships.
\par
One common setting, for example, is a diagnostic system
\par
using subjective probabilities. In this setting, a typical
\par
query might be to determine a posterior probability,
\par
P(X I Y ), where X and Y are sets of nodes (i.e., vari-
\par
ables). Another common setting is a forecasting system,
\par
in which a typical query would be to ask for the value
\par
(or the distribution of values across the states) of some
\par
variable, X, at some time in the future. In most ID set-
\par
tings, inference is used to evaluate alternatives and to
\par
derive optimal policies; typical queries concern evalu-
