4.1. Exact Methods
\par
An early and obvious approach to probabilistic inference
\par
across the set of objects captured by an ID was the ex-
\par
plicit computation of the global joint distribution as the
\par
product of prior and conditional distributions. Howard
\par
and Matheson suggested transforming an ID into a de-
\par
cision tree and then using a standard roll-back method
\par
(Howard and Matheson 1984). Unfortunately, this
\par
simple, straightforward method falls prey to the com-
\par
binatorial explosion common among brute-force tech-
\par
niques; in this case, the size of the joint distribution is
\par
exponential in the number of variables, and the com-
\par
putational and memory requirements are quite heavy
\par
even for relatively small sets of variables.
\par
Recall, however, that part of the motivation behind
\par
the development of BNs and IDs was the circumvention
\par
of this combinatorial explosion through the exploitation
\par
of graphical independence properties. These properties
\par
allow inference algorithms to factor the joint distribu-
