\par
computation. In Henrion's words, ". . . the key to
\par
computational efficiency for inference in BNs is to take
\par
advantage of conditional independence specified by the
\par
network topology, and so to find ways of propagating
\par
the impact of new evidence locally without having to
\par
calculate the entire joint distribution" (Henrion 1990).
\par
Several algorithms have attempted to avail themselves
\par
of these topological properties. Although some of these
\par
algorithms work quite well, they all fall prey to the
\par
combinatorial explosion at some point; Cooper proved
\par
the general problem to be WN-hard (Cooper 1990).
\par
4.1.1. Exact Inference in BNs. Since the general
\par
problem of exact (probabilistic) inference in BNs with
\par
no topological constraints is intractable, all general-
\par
purpose methods have one or more steps that risk be-
\par
coming victims of the combinatorial explosion. Not all
\par
algorithms, however, are general purpose. Some of them
\par
are restricted to a single class of topologically con-
\par
strained networks, such as the polytrees, or singly-
\par
connected networks. (A polytree is simply a tree with
\par
more than one rooted node.) Pearl and Kim derived an
\par
efficient (i.e., linear in the number of variables) con-
\par
straint satisfaction-based algorithm for inferences on
\par
polytrees (Kim and Pearl 1983, Pearl 1988). Pearl then
\par
extended this algorithm to multiply connected networks
\par
by showing how to use loop cutset conditioning to re-
\par
formulate complex networks as polytrees (Pearl 1987,
\par
Pearl 1988). Peot and Shachter presented a sequential
\par
polytree algorithm that insures that no node is ever vis-
\par
ited more than twice, thereby completing Pearl's algo-
\par
rithm and improving upon its general-case running time
\par
(Peot and Shachter 1991). Network reformulation and
\par
the polytree algorithms led to a particularly powerful
\par
approach to probabilistic inference in a BN, first intro-
\par
duced by Lauritzen and Spiegelhalter (Speigelhalter
\par
1986, Lauritzen and Spiegelhalter 1988), and later im-
\par
proved upon by Jensen and his colleagues (Jensen et
\par
al. 1990, Jensen et al. 1991). This method uses a series
\par
of graphical manipulations to reformulate an input BN
\par
as a computationally equivalent polytree of cliques
\par
(maximal sets of interconnected nodes), which may
\par
then be solved by any of the polytree algorithms.
\par
Not all inference algorithms, however, require poly-
\par
trees. Shachter presented a method that applies se-
\par
quences of value preserving operations to reduce a
\par
multiply connected BN to the node or the set of nodes
\par
whose value is desired. This reduction is, of course, de-
\par
structive; most of the information contained in the orig-
\par
inal network is lost when it is collapsed. Nevertheless,
\par
operations like arc reversal, chance node removal and
\par
barren node removal are called value-preserving be-
\par
cause they preserve the joint distribution of the node (s)
\par
being considered. (A "barren" node is one from which
\par
no arcs emanate.) An example of this property was il-
\par
lustrated in Figure 1, where the reversal of an arc was
\par
shown to leave the relational-level implications of a
\par
network unchanged. The application of Bayes' rule al-
\par
lows functional and numeric information to be pre-
\par
served under arc reversal. The value preservation in-
\par
herent in chance node removal can similarly be illus-
\par
trated by considering the fate of a chance node (A),
\par
whose only successor is another chance node (B). A can
\par
be removed from the BN by "absorbing" it into B and
\par
redirecting all of A's direct predecessors to point to B.
\par
Repeated arc reversals and node removals reduce the
\par
network to the targeted subset of nodes, and return the
\par
desired distribution. The order in which these operations
\par
are applied, in turn, must be guided by the topology of
\par
the network (Shachter 1986b, Shachter 1988). Shachter
\par
later extended this method to handle experimental ev-
\par
idence by including evidence reversal operations
\par
(Shachter 1990a).
\par
Yet another different class of approaches to proba-
\par
bilistic inference was introduced by D'Ambrosio and
\par
his colleagues as the Symbolic Probabilistic Inference
\par
(SPI) method (D'Ambrosio 1989, 1991; Shachter et al.
\par
1990a). SPI performs only the calculations that are re-
\par
quired to respond to a given query, as opposed to, say,
\par
Pearl's belief propagation algorithm (Pearl 1988), in
\par
which all local distributions are computed, regardless
\par
of their relevance to the query at hand. Several exten-
\par
sions of SPI have also been introduced, including SEPI
\par
(Symbolic Probabilistic Inference with Evidence Poten-
\par
tial) (Chang and Fung 1991b), and SPIC (SPI with
\par
continuous variables) (Chang and Fung 1991a).
\par
Finally, Shachter, Andersen, and Szolovitz posed the
\par
Clustering Algorithm as a unifying framework for the
\par
exact methods (Shachter et al. 1993). They showed how
