4.1.2. Exact Evaluation of IDs. The primary dif-
\par
ference between inference in a BN and inference in an
\par
ID lies in the nature of the query. Whereas users of BN-
\par
based systems desire information about probability dis-
\par
tributions across sets of variables, queries posed to IDs
\par
normally involve determinations of optimal policies (i.e.,
\par
sets of decisions). Since BNs are a proper subset of IDs,
\par
it should come as no surprise that: (i) not all algorithms
\par
that work on BNs work on IDs, and (ii) some algorithms
\par
that work on BNs can work on IDs with only a few
\par
minor modifications.
\par
Shachter's BN evaluation method based on value-
\par
preserving transformations, for example, works quite
\par
well on a class of IDs with specific topological properties.
\par
In addition to the chance and barren node removal and
\par
arc reversal operations used in the evaluation of BNs,
\par
operations for decision node removal and an ID variant
\par
of chance node removal are also necessary (Shachter
\par
1986). When Shachter's algorithm is applied to an ID
\par
of the appropriate topology, it reduces the input network
\par
to a single value node with no predecessors whose value
\par
is the expected value of the decision problem (assuming
\par
that an optimal policy is followed). In addition, the set
\par
of optimal decision policies is generated during the al-
\par
gorithm's removal of decision nodes. Breese (1987) of-
\par
fered two variations on Shachter's algorithm, one of
\par
which evaluated the diagram as described above, while
\par
the other evaluated a chance node rather than a value
\par
node (i.e., it returned a probability distribution across
\par
the state of a specified chance node).
\par
Node elimination methods such as Shachter's algo-
\par
rithm and its variants have several major drawbacks,
\par
including the use of excessive memory and a need for
\par
global knowledge about the network to order the trans-
\par
formations. In addition, their destructiveness makes it
\par
very difficult to provide ex post explanations of the rea-
\par
soning used in manipulating the network. These prob-
\par
lems motivated Pearl's suggestion that an ID should be
\par
evaluated by instantiating decision and observation
\par
nodes sequentially, and that the remaining nodes should
\par
be viewed as a BN that supplies probabilistic parameters
\par
(Pearl 1988). Shachter's node-elimination method (or
\par
any other BN propagation method) can then be used
\par
to compute these parameters.
\par
Not all methods for calculating IDs are based on de-
\par
structive operations like arc reversal and node elimi-
\par
nation. Ndilikilikesha, for example, introduced an ID-
\par
variant called the potential influence diagram, in which
\par
chance nodes are related by arbitrary nonnegative
\par
functions, rather than by conditional probability distri-
\par
butions. He showed that in these models, inference can
\par
proceeds without reversing any arcs (Ndilikilikesha
\par
1991). Perhaps more generally, Shachter and his stu-
\par
dents used a modified clustering algorithm to solve de-
\par
cision problems under uncertainty without reversing
\par
arcs or eliminating nodes (Tatman 1985, Tatman and
\par
Shachter 1990, Shachter and Peot 1992). Their algo-
\par
rithm converts all decision and value nodes into
\par
probabilistic components, thereby generating a one-
\par
directional, rooted cluster tree. They show how the
\par
entire decision problem can be solved in a single pass
\par
through this tree. The key to this method, of course,
\par
is that it translates an ID into a BN, and then applies
\par
BN-evaluation techniques to solve the ID. Additional
\par
ID-to-BN translation algorithms were introduced by
\par
Pearl (1988), and others. Finally, Shachter provided a
\par
method for probabilistic inference with continuous ran-
\par
dom variables (an algorithm which required the stan-
\par
dard ID machinery to be extended to include relation-
\par
ships specified as continuous distributions) (Shachter
\par
and Kenley 1989, Shachter 1990b).
\par
4.2. Approximation Methods
\par
Cooper's derivation of the NP-hardness of exact in-
\par
ference has led to the consideration of alternative in-
\par
ference methods, most of which rely on some sort of
\par
approximation (Henrion 1990, Henrion et al. 1988,
\par
1991). Unfortunately, Dagum and Luby recently
\par
showed that even approximation falls prey to intract-
\par
ability; the general problem of approximating proba-
