\par
bilistic inference in a BN remains NA/)-hard (Dagum
\par
and Luby 1993). Nevertheless, not all approximation
\par
algorithms tackle inference problems-some of them
\par
simply want to approximate the value of a single vari-
\par
able under a wide range of possible inputs-and there
\par
remain a wide variety of settings in which approxi-
\par
mations can yield useful results. Thus, Dagum and Lu-
\par
by's results notwithstanding, the development of ap-
\par
proximation techniques for BNs and IDs remains an
\par
important area of research.
\par
The fundamental idea underlying most approxima-
\par
tion techniques is stochastic simulation, or random
\par
sampling of network instantiations. Each scenario (or
\par
completely instantiated network) defines a point in a
\par
probability space, through which standard Monte Carlo
\par
simulations (or other sampling techniques) are applied.
\par
Henrion's probabilistic logic sampling (Henrion 1988)
\par
is the simplest and most straightforward approach to
\par
stochastic simulation. Since BNs are acyclic by defini-
\par
tion, they must contain a set of rooted nodes (or nodes
\par
with no incident arcs). The numeric-level specification
\par
of these nodes can be expressed in only two ways: a
\par
constant value or a (prior) probability distribution. In
\par
either case, a rooted node may easily be instantiated,
\par
and a single value (or state) may be propagated to its
\par
successors. Since the instantiation of a rooted node and
\par
the propagation of its instantiated value effectively re-
\par
moves the node from the underlying BN, it also creates
\par
new rooted nodes (i.e., those whose successors have
\par
already been instantiated), which may now be instan-
\par
tiated. This procedure can continue until the target node
\par
(i.e., the one whose value is being approximated) is
\par
instantiated. The instantiation of the target node gen-
\par
erates a single data point. Multiple iterations of the pro-
\par
cedure yield multiple data points, which may be aggre-
\par
gated to yield an approximate probability distribution
\par
across the states of the target variable.
\par
Probabilistic logic sampling is thus a forward sam-
\par
pling approach in which the instantiations follow the
\par
direction of the arcs, and the states with which each
\par
variable is instantiated is conditioned on those assumed
\par
by its parents. One of the key assumptions underlying
\par
forward sampling simulations, however, is that mid-
\par
network (nonrooted) nodes are never observed before
\par
their parents. In a BN built for prediction or forecasting,
\par
this assumption is reasonable because nodes are gen-
\par
erally arranged in chronological order. In a causal or
\par
diagnostic network, on the other hand, effects are often
\par
observed before causes. Since these observations man-
\par
date recalculations of various probability distributions
\par
and independence relations, they also limit the appli-
\par
cability of forward sampling procedures.
\par
This potential conflict between stochastic instantia-
\par
tions and actual observations helped motivate Pearl's
\par
Markov Sampling (Pearl 1987, 1988). This procedure
\par
attempts to shield nodes from the influences of distant
\par
nodes by focusing on its Markov blanket, or the node
\par
set that contains its immediate predecessors, its im-
\par
mediate successors, and the immediate predecessors of
\par
its immediate successors. The simulation proceeds by
\par
selecting a node at random, instantiating its Markov
\par
blanket, recalculating its probability, and then instan-
\par
tiating it accordingly (i.e., as per the resulting probability
\par
distribution across its states). Although Markov sam-
\par
pling does reduce the impact of observations on the
\par
simulation, the method also has some negative side-
\par
effects. Unlike pure logic sampling, successive instan-
\par
tiations of nodes in Markov sampling are not indepen-
\par
dent. The method's convergence rate may therefore de-
\par
teriorate when the network contains links that are near
\par
deterministic because the network may get trapped in
\par
scenarios from which it takes many instantiations to
\par
escape (Shachter and Peot 1990, Chin and Cooper 1989,
\par
Chavez and Cooper 1990). In addition, the computation
\par
per trial is often greater than that needed for straight
\par
logic sampling because the preprocessing step must take
\par
place for every node and for every trial.
\par
Several other approaches have been proposed as ways
\par
of overcoming logic sampling's difficulties with actual
\par
observations. Evidence integration reverses arcs (by it-
\par
erative applications of Bayes' rule) to convert nodes
\par
whose values have been observed into sources (i.e., all
\par
arcs emanate outward) (Chin and Cooper 1989). This
\par
technique works where it is applicable, but remains
\par
general-case intractable. In likelihood weighting or ev-
\par
idence weighting, posed independently by Fung and
\par
Chang (1990) and by Shachter and Peot (1990), re-
\par
spectively, nodes representing variables that have al-
\par
ready been observed are not instantiated as part of the
\par
simulation. This approach thus modifies logic sampling
\par
by simulating only a subset of the nodes, namely those
\par
that have yet to occur. Although the convergence prop-
\par
erties of evidence weighting are hard to predict, em-
\par
pirical results are encouraging (Fung and Chang 1990).
