7. Extensions and Current Research
\par
Topics
\par
The more-or-less simultaneous development of a theory
\par
of BNs and IDs, a set of elicitation and modeling tools,
\par
and a collection of implemented systems speaks vol-
\par
umes for the power and the promise of decision-analytic
\par
networks. Nevertheless, no model can capture every-
\par
thing, and many questions remain unanswered. A sec-
\par
ond wave of research has already begun to address some
\par
of these questions. Researchers are currently investi-
\par
gating ways of incorporating value of information cal-
\par
culations, sensitivity analyses, quasi-probabilistic calculi,
\par
and multiple contributors.
\par
7.1. Value of Information and Sensitivity
\par
Analysis
\par
The value of information is an important concept in
\par
DA. When a decision problem is particularly important,
\par
DMs often want to obtain additional, relevant infor-
\par
mation in order to improve the quality of their decisions.
\par
Informally stated, the value of an information source is
\par
the difference between the utilities of two optimal strat-
\par
egies, one of which provides a freedom of choice among
\par
different actions for different source outcomes, and the
\par
other of which does not (Howard 1966, Matheson
\par
1968). This criterion can be used to rate the usefulness
\par
of various information sources and to decide whether
\par
a piece of evidence is worth acquiring. Value-of-
\par
information calculations were first introduced in a BN
\par
setting by Levitt and Binford (Levitt 1988a, Levitt et al.
\par
1990), and in an ID setting by Matheson (1989). Hor-
\par
vitz's doctoral dissertation provided a detailed discus-
\par
sion of value-of-information and related issues in a va-
\par
riety of Al settings (1990).
\par
Value of information calculations become increasingly
\par
complex in the presence of multiple information sources
\par
because the value of each source can only be calculated
\par
relative to the value of other sources and to the order
\par
in which they are investigated. In fact, the general
\par
problem of determining an optimal order of information
\par
sources is intractable (Pearl 1988). A heuristic policy
\par
that is often used in the case of multiple information
\par
sources in myopia, or greediness. This policy is based
\par
on the following two assumptions (or constraints): (i)
\par
no competition, and (ii) one step horizon. In other
\par
words, given a set of information sources (e.g., sets of
\par
experts or tests), the best source to poll is the one that
\par
would be the most valuable if action were taken im-
\par
mediately after its information were provided (Heck-
\par
erman et al. 1991). In order to avoid the types of con-
\par
straints imposed by a myopic policy, several researchers
\par
have proposed alternative, nonmyopic, approximation
\par
methods (Heckerman et al. 1991).
\par
The procedure for determining the value of missing
\par
information is an example of a sensitivity analysis. In
\par
this instance, what is being analyzed is the sensitivity
\par
of the decision to the addition of information; standard
\par
decision analytic sensitivity analyses have long ad-
\par
dressed this issue. Other types of sensitivity analyses
\par
are also possible in the context of ID- and BN-based
\par
systems. Several, in fact, have been conducted on one
