7.2. Two-level BNs
\par
A good deal of recent work has focused on a special
\par
class of BNs, the two-level, or BN2, networks. This class
\par
of bipartite BNs is considered to be particularly impor-
\par
tant in diagnostic settings; it is defined as the networks
\par
that are composed of independent hypotheses and con-
\par
ditionally independent findings. Approximations de-
\par
rived by searching the space of instantiated hypotheses
\par
are widely used on the BN2 networks. Examples of these
\par
search strategies include NESTOR's branch-and-bound
\par
algorithm (Cooper 1984), Peng and Reggia's application
\par
of minimal covering sets of diseases to explain observed
\par
findings (Peng and Reggia 1987), and Henrion's TopN
\par
algorithm, which imposes a heuristic search on top of
\par
a branch-and-bound algorithm to prune inadmissible
\par
paths from the search tree, and to identify the n most
\par
probable diagnoses (Henrion 1989b).
\par
BN20s are BN2s with noisy-OR gates and leaks.
\par
These additions trade elegance for realism by recogniz-
\par
ing that some observations may arise spontaneously
\par
without an explicitly modeled cause (Henrion 1991).
\par
Methods for solving BN20's include a variant of TopN
\par
(Henrion 1991), a general approximation method called
\par
random sampling with likelihood-weighting (Chin and
\par
Cooper 1989, Shachter and Peot 1990), and an exact
\par
method called the QuickScore algorithm for BN20s
\par
(Heckerman 1 990b). Although exact methods are pref-
\par
erable when applicable, Quickscore suffers from nu-
\par
merical instability and from some unrealistic assump-
\par
tions. Henrion's empirical studies imply that search-
\par
based algorithms such as TopN appear to be both
\par
promising and practical.
\par
7.3. Quasi-probabilistic Networks
\par
IDs and BNs have been developed by members of the
\par
Bayesian research community. Not everyone interested
\par
in the representation and manipulation of uncertain in-
\par
formation, however, belongs to that community. Var-
\par
ious researchers have long felt that the language of
\par
probability theory is insufficiently expressive; they have
\par
developed a variety of quasi-probabilistic and logic-
\par
based calculi for manipulating uncertainty in Al systems.
\par
Some of them have also attempted to develop BN-like
\par
structures for their calculi; their general strategy seems
\par
to be to accept the relational level advantages of the
\par
model, but to rethink the functional (and by extension,
\par
the numeric) levels. Shafer and Shenoy, for example,
\par
applied Dempster-Shafer theory to BNs by emphasizing
\par
factorization over conditional probabilities, and by re-
\par
jecting completeness as a necessary property of a belief
\par
(Shafer and Shenoy 1990). Shenoy then used this idea
\par
to develop graphical valuation networks (VNs) (Shenoy
\par
1989). Like IDs, VNs depict decision variables, chance
\par
variables, utility functions, and informational con-
\par
straints. Unlike IDs, however, VNs depict probability
\par
and utility through explicit functions called valuations.
\par
Inference in a VN proceeds through two operations,
\par
combination and marginalization. VNs have been
\par
claimed to be general and powerful enough to capture
\par
several quasi-probabilistic calculi, including Dempster-
\par
Shafer theory (belief functions) (Shafer 1976), and
\par
Spohn's theory of epistemic beliefs (Spohn 1988).
\par
Shenoy's discussion of VNs highlights several areas in
\par
which they can outperform decision trees and IDs
\par
(Shenoy 1991).
\par
Fertig and Breese introduced interval IDs, and de-
\par
scribed a method for performing probabilistic reasoning
\par
in IDs using interval, rather than point-valued, proba-
\par
bilities (Fertig and Breese 1990). Graphical represen-
\par
tations of fuzzy sets were proposed by de Campos and
\par
Moral, who used a class of fuzzy measures to represent
\par
uncertain information (de Campos and Moral 1991),
\par
and by Dubois and Prade, who developed a model
\par
called the possibilistic hypergraph (Dubois and Prade
\par
1991). Finally, early work on Pathfinder considered
\par
quasi- and nonprobabilistic calculi, and found them to
\par
be sorely lacking. The system's designers noted that the
\par
