7.4. Consensus Network Models
\par
The literature on group decision making recognizes two
\par
broad classes of approaches, mathematical and behav-
\par
ioral. In a mathematical approach, each contributor ar-
\par
rives at an independent answer, and submits it (with
\par
no information about how it was generated) to a math-
\par
ematical aggregation function. In a behavioral approach,
\par
contributors work out their differences and negotiate a
\par
compromise. Various combinations of mathematical and
\par
behavioral approaches have also been suggested.
\par
Both classes of approaches have been considered by
\par
designers of BN-based systems. In the context of an
\par
automated system, behavioral approaches lead to
\par
design-time consensus; contributors all suggest inputs
\par
to a single network, and the system is not brought on-
\par
line until they agree (see e.g., Gaines and Boose (1988)).
\par
A mathematical approach, on the other hand, leads
\par
to run-time consensus; independently designed BNs
\par
representing distinct knowledge sources each produce
\par
black-box responses, which are then fed into a math-
\par
ematical aggregation function. The probabilistic mul-
\par
tiknowledge-base architecture (or PMKBS) was pro-
\par
posed as a way of implementing this approach (Ng
\par
1990), Ng and Abramson 1994, Abramson and Ng
\par
1993). A PMKBS consists of several distinct BNs, all
\par
designed for the same diagnostic task of differentiating
\par
among a common set of hypotheses, and some addi-
\par
tional machinery for broadcasting data and combining
\par
responses. Several experimental prototype systems were
\par
developed for portions of Pathfinder's domain (Ng
\par
1990), and a series of simulation experiments were
\par
conducted to compare different aggregation functions
\par
in the diagnostic context (Ng and Abramson 1992).
\par
Another approach that may overcome some of the
\par
limitations of design- and run-time consensus is
\par
compile-time consensus, in which information provided
\par
by several contributors is combined into a single BN
\par
prior to the introduction of any case-specific informa-
\par
tion. Heckerman touched upon this idea in his devel-
\par
opment of the similarity network (Heckerman 1991).
\par
In these models, a large diagnostic problem is partitioned
\par
into a set of small problems. A distinct, "local" BN is
\par
elicited for each problem, and then combined into a
\par
"global" network for the original problem via graph
\par
union. Bonduelle approached this coordination problem
\par
in an ID-setting by using graph intersection rather than
\par
graph union (Bonduelle 1987). Shachter considered
\par
seeking agreement about the direction of information
\par
flow through a BN by imposing a preset ordering across
\par
the BNs nodes. Arcs that violated that ordering were
\par
reversed (accompanied by all other modifications nec-
\par
essary to perform a legal arc reversal) (Shachter 1990).
\par
Finally, the authors of this survey introduced several
\par
algorithms that merge the DAGs underlying BNs pro-
\par
vided by different contributors into a single "consensus"
\par
BN (Matzkevich and Abramson 1992, 1993a, 1993b).
\par
8. Summary
\par
This paper reviewed a recent wave of exciting work
\par
surrounding a family of graphical models of Bayesian
\par
decision theory. These models fill a long-standing rep-
\par
resentational gap in our ability to view practical prob-
\par
lems within a probabilistic framework. Prior to the ad-
\par
vent of IDs and BNs, large-scale probabilistic models
\par
were viewed as either unrealistic (under the assumption
\par
of universal independence) or intractable (under the
\par
assumption of universal interdependence). These dif-
\par
ficulties caused many researchers to reject probability
\par
theory altogether, and to favor quasi-probabilistic calculi
\par
and/or ad hoc symbolic reasoning schemes. IDs and
\par
BNs allow dependence to captured only where appro-
\par
priate and direct. They thus constitute a framework
\par
within which probabilistic representations are realistic
\par
and probabilistic inference algorithms are practical (if
\par
still general-case intractable).
\par
This article surveyed the first decade or so of serious
\par
research on IDs and BNs. It drew material from the
\par
work of theoreticians, experimentalists, and implemen-
\par
tors, and attempted to integrate them into a coherent
\par
whole. One of the characteristics of the research com-
\par
munity that has led to the rapid rise of IDs and BNs
\par
from proposed models to actual systems has been the
\par
dialogue between these three oft-distinct groups. This
\par
article was our attempt to keep this dialogue going, while
\par
at the same time providing an overview of the issues
\par
to readers who may have heard of BNs and / or IDs,
\par
but who did not realize how widespread and powerful
\par
they had become.
