advantage for firms. This paper looks at individual-level resources that may have important com-
\par
petitive implications for a special class of firms: entrepreneurial organizations.
\par
Jay B. Barney
\par
Abstract
\par
This paper examines the link between a firm' s resources and its
\par
efficient organization form with a focus on entrepreneurial re-
\par
sources. Entrepreneurial resources are defined as the propensity
\par
of an individual to behave creatively, act with foresight, use
\par
intuition, and be alert to new opportunities. This paper assumes
\par
that these entrepreneurial resources can be distributed in two
\par
ways throughout the firm: they can be held by one or a few
\par
individuals-"individual entrepreneurial resources"-or they
\par
can be dispersed among a team of individuals-"team entre-
\par
preneurial resources." Agency theory is used to consider how
\par
various organizational characteristics-such as the assignment
\par
of decision responsibilities, incentives and risk bearing, the
\par
number of hierarchical levels, horizontal linking structures, mu-
\par
tual monitoring and bonding devices, and information sys-
\par
tems-will differ in the individual and team entrepreneurial
\par
forms. Generally, the individual entrepreneurial form resembles
\par
the "classic entrepreneurial firm" (Fama and Jensen 1983) in
\par
which various decision making roles and risk bearing are per-
\par
formed by a single entrepreneur. The team entrepreneurial form
\par
is similar to what Bartlett and Ghoshal (1993) call "beyond the
\par
m-form." Responsibility for steps in the decision process is dis-
\par
persed in the team form, and monitoring the decision process
\par
will occur at the organization's apex. The CEO will not make
\par
operational or strategic decisions; instead, he manages the in-
\par
ternal market for managers and develops an organization's cul-
\par
tural context and information systems. Below the CEO level,
\par
team entrepreneurs are responsible for ratifying innovative de-
\par
cisions, and below this team entrepreneurs are responsible for
\par
initiating innovative decisions. After presenting these two en-
\par
trepreneurial forms, this paper relates them to extant research
\par
on corporate entrepreneurship and organizational forms.
\par
(Entrepreneurship; Agency Theory; Innovation; Organi-
\par
zational Forms)
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Research on the resource-based view of strategy (Penrose
\par
1959; Rubin 1973; Lippman and Rumelt 1982;
\par
Wernerfelt 1984; Barney 1986a, 1991; Rumelt 1991;
\par
Dierickx and Cool 1989; Conner 1991; Mahoney and
\par
Pandian 1992; Peteraf 1993; Mosakowski 1993) argues
\par
that firms' internal resources determine their competitive
\par
outcomes. While research in this tradition continues to
\par
grow and differentiate itself, as in the movement stressing
\par
competence-based competition (Hamel and Heene 1994,
\par
Sanchez et al. 1996, Heene and Sanchez 1997), it retains
\par
a common emphasis on knowledge-based resources. For
\par
example, Winter (1987) equates knowledge and compe-
\par
tence with strategic assets. Amit and Schoemaker (1993,
\par
p. 35) define capabilities to be information-based pro-
\par
cesses. In an article focusing explicitly on intangible re-
\par
sources and capabilities, Hall (1993) highlights various
\par
forms of knowledge-such as patents, trade secrets, da-
\par
tabases, information in public domains, and know how of
\par
employees, suppliers, etc.-and restricts attention to a
\par
fairly narrow set of other intangible resources: reputation,
\par
organization culture, and contracts and licenses. Langlois
\par
(1992), Kogut and Zander (1992, 1996), Fransman
\par
(1994), Conner and Prahalad (1996), and Madhok (1996)
\par
argue for the centrality of knowledge in the theory of the
\par
firm. Efficient knowledge management is the rationale
\par
behind Hedlund's (1994) model of the n-form corpora-
\par
tion.
\par
Although knowledge may at least partially explain
\par
firms' competitive successes and failures, there is grow-
\par
ing interest in other types of intangible resources. For
\par
example, Hamel and Prahalad's (1993, 1994) work on
