\par
strategic stretch and leverage points to a broad spectrum
\par
of resources, such as firm's motivational resources and
\par
its attention to the future. Barney and Hansen (1994) fo-
\par
cus on the competitive implications of a firm's trustwor-
\par
thiness. Several scholars (Dierickx and Cool 1989,
\par
Barney 1986b, Bartlett and Ghoshal 1993) emphasize an
\par
organization's culture or context as a resource whose
\par
properties allow it to generate sustainable competitive ad-
\par
vantage. Mosakowski (1998) draws upon literature on
\par
motivation to understand how the best practices of mo-
\par
tivational techniques may affect a firm's ability to gen-
\par
erate and sustain economic rents.
\par
This paper considers the importance of intangible en-
\par
trepreneurial resources (see Penrose's (1959) discussion
\par
of entrepreneurial services). I define these resources to be
\par
the creativity, foresight, intuition, and alertness of indi-
\par
viduals. Conner (1991, pp. 133-134) suggests:
\par
In a resource-based view, discerning appropriate inputs is ulti-
\par
mately a matter of entrepreneurial vision and intuition; the cre-
\par
ative act underlying such vision is a subject that so far has not
\par
been a central focus of resource-based theory development.
\par
Similarly, Winter (1994) argues that speculative abil-
\par
ity-possesing a superior eye for resource value-and
\par
creative ability-the skill of combining resources in
\par
novel ways-underlie a dynamic resource-based perspec-
\par
tive. Whereas knowledge pertains to something that has
\par
held true in the past, entrepreneurial resources points to-
\par
ward the future by revising expectations about the future
\par
or even allowing for its creation as reflected in the
\par
Schumpeterian process of innovation and creative de-
\par
struction (Schumpeter 1942). This distinction is reflected
\par
in Hirshleifer's (1971) discovery versus foreknowledge.
\par
In discussing entrepreneurial resources, I begin by as-
\par
suming that firm heterogeneity, and ultimately, differ-
\par
ences in firm performance, may result from differences
\par
in entrepreneurial resources held by individuals working
\par
within the firm. I then develop arguments concerning the
\par
efficient organizational forms for firms in which entre-
\par
preneurial resources are embedded in different ways. In
\par
particular, positive agency theory is used to describe the
\par
efficient organizational form for firms in which these re-
\par
sources are concentrated in one or a few key individuals
\par
versus firms where these resources are diffused. Thus, I
\par
focus on a specific example of how a firm's resources and
\par
organization may be interrelated.
\par
This attention to organizational choices is driven by the
\par
idea that, while critical resources may be held by indi-
\par
viduals, organizational choices determine whether and
\par
how individual resources are translated into organiza-
\par
tional competencies, which eventually lead to a firm's
\par
superior competitive position (Nelson and Winter 1982,
\par
ch. 5). The resource-based view of strategy informs this
\par
issue because it allows for differences among individuals
\par
based on psychological characteristics, biographical ex-
\par
periences, cognitive abilities, intrinsic motivation, and
\par
knowledge, all of which may be important sources of last-
\par
ing firm heterogeneity (Woodman et al. 1993). Agency
\par
theory provides a useful theoretical lens for examining
\par
this issue because, by focusing on the interaction of in-
\par
dividuals, it proposes that different organizational param-
\par
eters, such as who bears the residual risk of an operation,
\par
who controls the decision process, etc., will affect firm
\par
efficiency.
\par
The remainder of the paper is divided into three major
\par
sections. The next section defines entrepreneurial re-
\par
sources and discusses how their differences might affect
\par
the firm's competitive outcomes. In the subsequent sec-
\par
tion, agency theory is used to suggest how the entrepre-
\par
neur might organize to reduce agency costs associated
\par
with employing these entrepreneurial resources. The final
\par
section summarizes the discussion by briefly relating my
\par
results to several streams of research, including entrepre-
\par
neurship literature and research on organizational forms.
\par
2. Entrepreneurial Resources
\par
2.1. Definition of Entrepreneurial Resources and
\par
Outcomes
\par
In economics literature, the definition of entrepreneurship
\par
typically employed is a functional one: entrepreneurship
\par
is the process that generates entrepreneurial outcomes.
\par
For example, see Schumpeter's definition (1942, p. 132):
\par
We have seen the function of entrepreneurs is to reform or rev-
\par
olutionize the pattern of production by exploiting an invention
\par
or, more generally, an untried technological possibility for pro-
\par
ducing a new commodity or producing an old one in a new way,
\par
by opening up a new source of supply of material or a new
\par
outlet for products, by revolutionizing an industry and so on.
\par
Similarly, Knight (1921, p. 278) employs a functional
\par
definition that ascribes to the entrepreneur "his peculiar
\par
twofold function of (a) exercising responsible control and
\par
(b) securing the owners of productive services against
\par
uncertainty and fluctuation in their incomes." Central to
\par
Kirzner's (1973, 1979) view is the idea that the entrepre-
\par
neur resolves disequilibrium situations; in this way, the
\par
entrepreneurial function is similar to that of the arbitra-
\par
geur.
\par
Given this tradition of defining an entrepreneur, the
\par
emphasis is now upon the entrepreneurial function. I de-
\par
fine entrepreneurial outcomes to be novel competitive
\par
outcomes; e.g., producing goods or services at lower costs
\par
or higher quality than competitors can. This definition
