\par
allows for Schumpeter's (1942) broad inclusion of dif-
\par
ferent ways these novel competitive outcomes might
\par
come about: by including inventions, new product tech-
\par
nologies, new production technologies, new suppliers,
\par
and new channels of distribution as unique resources or
\par
combination of resources. In addition, my definition re-
\par
quires that entrepreneurship sets a new standard for com-
\par
petition in any of the industry's relevant competitive di-
\par
mensions. Thus, I incorporate to some extent the
\par
relationship between entrepreneurship and the Schum-
\par
peterian process of creative destruction.
\par
Which resources might generate novel competitive out-
\par
comes? I propose that the creativity, foresight, intuition,
\par
and alertness possessed by an individual constitute en-
\par
trepreneurial resources. The next subsection describes
\par
why these particular individual resources are emphasized.
\par
First I define these resources.
\par
As will be explained shortly for each, an entrepreneur-
\par
ial resource reflects the propensity of an entrepreneur to
\par
engage the environment in a given way, but does not
\par
completely determine the outcome. In other words, gen-
\par
eral and specific personality characteristics, abilities, and
\par
aptitudes influence an entrepreneur's propensity to act in
\par
a particular fashion, and these actions influence organi-
\par
zational outcomes through complex relationships (Naylor
\par
et al. 1980). By definition, these characteristics influence
\par
how an individual is likely to behave but do not neces-
\par
sarily predict how an individual will behave in a specific
\par
situation or what outcomes will be generated by an in-
\par
dividual's behavior. This contrasts with prior work on
\par
entrepreneurship and personality (e.g., McClelland 1961;
\par
Brockhaus 1980, 1982; Begley and Boyd 1987; Miner et
\par
al. 1989), which presumed a direct relationship of per-
\par
sonality characteristic to organizational outcomes. In gen-
\par
eral such research demonstrated no consistent results
\par
(Low and MacMillan 1988, Brockhaus and Horwitz
\par
1986, Busenitz and Barney 1996).
\par
I emphasize behavioral propensities in this paper be-
\par
cause the generation of entrepreneurial outcomes must be,
\par
to a significant extent, unpredictable. Given this random-
\par
ness, an individual's propensity to engage in many trials
\par
of a particular behavior, e.g., many experiments at start-
\par
ing a business, may have a greater effect on entrepre-
\par
neurial outcomes than will an individual's aptitude or
\par
skill. The common wisdom is that a key success factor
\par
for entrepreneurs is their willingness to continue trying
\par
regardless of failures. With a model that optimizes an
\par
entrepreneur's choices, Gifford (1992) suggests that the
\par
attention an entrepreneur allocates toward innovation is
\par
critical. In addition, behavioral explanations have been
\par
offered for why entrepreneurs may engage in trials when
\par
their chances of success are low. Entrepreneurs may not
\par
believe that their past experiences accurately reflect their
\par
future success (March and Shapira 1987), and unrealistic
\par
estimates of their abilities may be necessary for eventual
\par
success (Cooper et al. 1988).
\par
Therefore, creativity refers to an individual's propen-
\par
sity to engage in generating novel variations on existing
\par
themes (Stein 1953, 1974; Amabile 1979, 1988, 1996;
\par
Amabile et al. 1990; Amabile et al. 1996; Sternberg 1988;
\par
Glover et al. 1989; Woodman et al. 1993; Boden 1995).
\par
This definition focuses on an individual's tendency to
\par
participate in the creative process, not on the "product"
\par
or outcome of the creative process. Findlay and
\par
Lumsden's unusual evolutionary perspective on creativ-
\par
ity (1988) shares this focus by suggesting that individuals
\par
vary in their likelihood of displaying creative tendencies
\par
such that, when given free rein, more creative individuals
\par
will spend their time engaged in the creative process. Al-
\par
though few empirical studies of creativity emphasize such
\par
behavioral propensities (Eysenck 1995), Ghiselin et al.
\par
(1964) offer a step in this direction. Using a creative pro-
\par
cess checklist, Ghiselin et al. (1964) observe the temporal
\par
sequence of individuals' states of attention and affective
\par
states during problem-solving tasks.
\par
Similarly, foresight is defined as a behavioral propen-
\par
sity. Foresight refers to an individual's tendency to spend
\par
significant amounts of time engaged in thought or care
\par
for the future. Psychological research on future time per-
\par
spective (Wallace and Rabin 1960, Gorman and
\par
Wessman 1977, De Volder 1979, Gjesme 1983a) is
\par
closely related to this concept. For example, Gjesme
\par
(1979, p. 174) suggests that "the future time perspective
\par
in man is related to the cognitive elaboration of needs in
\par
plans, intentions, and tasks which have a more or less
\par
elaborated temporal structure." This research generally
\par
proposes at least two important components of a future
\par
orientation: how far into the future one conceptualizes
\par
one' s plans, and the degree of organization of events in
\par
the future time span (Wallace 1956). In other words, fu-
\par
ture orientation reflects an individual' s degree of involve-
\par
ment in the future, either in thought or action (De Volder
\par
1979), and is similar to the concept of foresight. While
\par
consensus on temporal orientation is relatively low (Ruiz
\par
et al. 1967, Lamm et al. 1976, Gjesme 1983b, De Volder
\par
1979), several empirical studies agree with the definition
\par
of foresight as a behavioral propensity. One approach to
\par
measuring temporal orientation (Teahan 1958, Ruiz et al.
\par
1967, Klineberg 1967, 1968, Jason et al. 1989) reports
\par
data from subjects' self-ratings of the frequencies of past,
\par
present, and future thoughts.
\par
Intuition refers to an individual's tendency to solve
\par
problems without explicit reasoning or analysis (Agor
\par
1989, Simon 1990). A widely accepted definition is
\par
