\par
Vaughan's (1979, p. 46): "knowing without being able to
\par
explain how we know." Individuals who rely heavily on
\par
intuition in decision-making tend to process information
\par
abstractly using complex schema for recognizing and
\par
combining information received (MacCrimmon and
\par
Taylor 1976, Simon 1986). A person relying on intuition
\par
is more able to deal with information overload and am-
\par
biguity in a turbulent and complex environment
\par
(Schroeder et al. 1965). While Westcott's Test of Intuitive
\par
Ability (Westcott 1968) measures an individual's intui-
\par
tiveness and is common in this literature (Shirley and
\par
Langan-Fox 1996), this measure would be inappropriate
\par
for my definition. A key aspect of Westcott's test is how
\par
frequently an individual is correct in drawing inferences
\par
based on little information-not just the tendency to do
\par
so. An alternative measure proposed is closer to my def-
\par
inition of intuition as a behavioral propensity in that it
\par
focuses on how easily an individual perceives coherence
\par
in patterns (Bowers et al. 1990), whether this perception
\par
is correct or not.
\par
Alertness refers to the tendency to spend significant
\par
amounts of time engaging the environment with a search
\par
for profit opportunities. Previous research has adopted
\par
quite different views of alertness. Kirzner (1973, 1979)
\par
distinguishes alertness from the rational decision to invest
\par
in information. Unlike my definition, Kirzner considers
\par
transient or momentary alertness that waxes and wanes
\par
as individuals engage in market processes. Thus, Demsetz
\par
(1983) equates Kirzner's concept of alertness with luck.
\par
By contrast, an approach consistent with the view of alert-
\par
ness as a rational decision is Gifford's (1992) research on
\par
allocating entrepreneurial attention between two problem
\par
areas: improving current operations and innovating new
\par
products. A third perspective consistent with my behav-
\par
ioral propensity definition of alertness is Kaish and
\par
Gilad's (1991). They equate alertness with information-
\par
seeking behavior. In their empirical research, Kaish and
\par
Gilad (1991) offer a scale for measuring alertness as the
\par
time an individual spends collecting information and
\par
thinking about business opportunities (i.e., processing
\par
this information).
\par
Several points regarding these definitions require elab-
\par
oration. I define entrepreneurial resources to be behav-
\par
ioral propensities and not aptitudes, abilities, and skills.
\par
However, propensities, aptitudes, abilities, and skills may
\par
be closely related. For example, an individual's propen-
\par
sity to act is based on past performances as well as self-
\par
perceived abilities and skills (Bandura 1997). In addition,
\par
individuals who possess entrepreneurial resources will be
\par
more likely to identify and exploit profit opportunities
\par
and may be successful in the future as a result. Thus,
\par
aptitudes, abilities, or skills are not necessarily over-
\par
looked.
\par
Focusing on behavioral propensities answers Busenitz
\par
and Barney's (1996) call to study entrepreneurship as a
\par
function of actions, since propensities represent the gen-
\par
eral likelihood that an individual will engage in a behav-
\par
ior across a wide range of situations. Yet contrary to the
\par
call to shift attention from individual differences to
\par
context-specific differences (Shapero 1984, Aldrich and
\par
Zimmer 1986, Gartner 1988, Low and MacMillan 1988,
\par
Busenitz and Barney 1996), the current paper highlights
\par
individual characteristics. Rather than exploring deeply
\par
embedded personality traits such as extroversion or neu-
\par
roticism (McCrae and Costa 1987), need for achievement
\par
(McClelland 1961), or unobservable skill sets, I empha-
\par
size an individual's propensity to display behavior sets
\par
derived, in part, from individual differences characteris-
\par
tics (Dunnette 1976). As characteristics of an individual,
\par
propensities are important because they satisfy Caves'
\par
(1980) definition of a resource, which includes all tangi-
\par
ble and intangible assets that may be tied in a relatively
\par
permanent fashion to an individual or a firm. The defi-
\par
nitions for the entrepreneurial resources clearly stipulate
\par
that each resource will be permanently tied to the indi-
\par
vidual. This is the key test given agency theory's focus
\par
on individual actors. Without this, only transient or
\par
context-specific behaviors could be considered and these
\par
might not be firm resources in the future.
\par
2.2. Entrepreneurial Resources Versus Other
\par
Types of Resources
\par
This attention to an individual's creativity, foresight, in-
\par
tuition, and alertness differs from research emphasizing
\par
other resources that generate innovative outcomes. In par-
\par
ticular, extant research on innovation highlights a firm's
\par
knowledge stock and to a some extent its risk-bearing
\par
propensity, both central to agency theory. This subsection
\par
discusses how creativity, foresight, intuition, and alert-
\par
ness relate to knowledge stocks and, to a lesser extent,
\par
risk-bearing propensities.
\par
In commenting upon the entrepreneur in economic
\par
theory, Demsetz (1983) directs attention to knowledge
\par
and risk bearing. He suggests that the entrepreneur re-
\par
mains neglected in economic theory because there is no
\par
unique entrepreneurial function (Demsetz 1983, p. 278).
\par
While the term entrepreneur is useful grammar for discussing
\par
such ventures, it adds no new economic agent or variable to the
\par
study of competition under conditions of imperfect knowledge.
\par
This is true even though such risk-bearing planned ventures
\par
may be the most important source of economic progress. [em-
\par
phasis added]
\par
