\par
Demsetz's emphasis on knowledge and risk bearing
\par
versus my emphasis on the above four entrepreneurial
\par
resources derives from the view of uncertainty adopted.
\par
Knight (1921) distinguishes between uncertainty, in
\par
which types of categories of possible outcomes cannot be
\par
imaged or articulated by decision makers, and risk, which
\par
refers to measurable, quantifiable, and imaginable uncer-
\par
tainty. Problems in which "knowledge is costly"
\par
(Demsetz 1983, p. 278) fundamentally differ from prob-
\par
lems in which knowledge of the future is impossible. The
\par
former problems do not incorporate the type of uncer-
\par
tainty to which Knight (1921) refers, and the latter re-
\par
mains the entrepreneur's domain.
\par
Other economists' characterization of the entrepreneur
\par
agrees with this assertion. Penrose (1959) tends toward a
\par
Knightian view of uncertainty by focusing on resources
\par
affect the investigation of whether there are opportunities
\par
of which the firm's managers are unaware. Kirzner's
\par
(1973, 1979) theory of entrepreneurship goes slightly fur-
\par
ther by discussing the identification of profitable oppor-
\par
tunities that no one else has identified. Similarly, Shackle
\par
(1972) suggests that entrepreneurs do not solve problems
\par
rooted in the past, but instead look to and actually create
\par
the future. Baumol (1968, p. 67) differentiates between
\par
managers and entrepreneurs by suggesting that the former
\par
use available information and calculative techniques to
\par
solve well-defined problems, whereas the latter respond
\par
to ill-defined problems using enterprise and initiative.
\par
Thus, I exclude superior knowledge and risk propen-
\par
sities from the current definition of entrepreneurial re-
\par
sources. However, I do not assume that knowledge or risk
\par
propensities are unimportant to innovation. Certainly,
\par
knowledge can be valuable to the entrepreneur, just as
\par
other resources like capital and labor can be valuable. In
\par
addition, individuals' risk propensities will affect whether
\par
they are able to bear the risks of an enterprise. Nonethe-
\par
less, reframing the entrepreneurial problem as decision
\par
making under Knightian uncertainty may be a more pro-
\par
ductive and less-traveled path. For this problem, creativ-
\par
ity, intuition, foresight, and alertness will be especially
\par
useful.
\par
However, entrepreneurial resources may or may not
\par
become cospecialized to other resources, such as different
\par
types of knowledge. For example, an individual may pos-
\par
sess considerable creativity in one area, such as verbal
\par
witticisms, but not in another, such as geometric problem
\par
solving. In this example, creativity in verbal witticisms
\par
may be cospecialized to literary knowledge, whereas cre-
\par
ativity in geometric problem solving may be cospeciali-
\par
zed to knowledge of engine design. In addition, individ-
\par
uals may possess a general level of an entrepreneurial
\par
resource that transcends problem arenas or bodies of
\par
knowledge. Yet competency traps (Levitt and March
\par
1988; Levinthal and March 1993) may work against this
\par
cospecialization when the most competent individuals or
\par
organizations might be the least able to identify new op-
\par
portunities.
\par
Empirical research offers support for relationships be-
\par
tween entrepreneurial resources and knowledge/exper-
\par
tise. Working from a psychological perspective, Amabile
\par
(1988) finds that expertise and cognitive ability in specific
\par
areas are positively associated with creativity in problem
\par
solving. Barron and Harrington (1981) and Amabile
\par
(1988) also show that broad general knowledge, experi-
\par
ence in many domains, and overall intelligence levels are
\par
positively associated with creativity. However, Stein
\par
(1989) finds both positive and negative relationships be-
\par
tween creativity and previous experience and learning.
\par
With regard to intuition, Larkin et al. (1980) suggest a
\par
positive relationship between intuition and expertise/
\par
knowledge, such that master chess players and expert
\par
physicists rely more heavily upon intuition than do nov-
\par
ices. Thus, the following discussion allows for linkages
\par
between entrepreneurial resources and knowledge.
\par
3. An Agency Model of the Locus of
\par
Entrepreneurial Resources
\par
This section offers a simple agency model of entrepre-
\par
neurship based on the locus of entrepreneurial resources.
\par
The first subsection defines terms and makes explicit sev-
\par
eral key assumptions. The second subsection presents ar-
\par
guments that lead to two very distinct forms of entrepre-
\par
neurial organization.
\par
3.1. Definitions and Assumptions
\par
Agency research focuses attention on the relationships
\par
among individuals and shies away from organizations as
\par
actors-which Jensen and Meckling (1976, p. 310) de-
\par
scribe as "legal fictions which serve as a nexus for a set
\par
of contracting relationships among individuals" [empha-
\par
sis in the original]. Nonetheless, my interest is how or-
\par
ganizational policies and practices affect whether entre-
\par
preneurial resources are put to good use. For example,
\par
research on creativity (Amabile 1988, Woodman et al.
\par
1993) suggests that, holding constant an individual's pro-
\par
pensity to act creatively, organizational factors promote
\par
or inhibit the generation of novel and useful ideas by an
\par
individual or small group of individuals. This interest in
\par
organizational factors is not inconsistent with a focus on
\par
the individual, however, because organizational policies
\par
and practices reflect the nature of contracting relation-
\par
ships among individuals.
\par
The key distinction is whether entrepreneurial re-
\par
sources are possessed by a single individual/few key in-
\par
dividuals or are widely distributed through the organi-
\par
zation, with joint input (team) production and several
\par
