\par
input owners (Alchian and Demsetz 1972). Although it
\par
is common to think of the individual entrepreneur, the
\par
idea of team entrepreneurship relates to Schumpeter' s in-
\par
stitutionalization of entrepreneurship and Alford's (1976)
\par
"diffused entrepreneurship." Schumpeter (1942, p. 132)
\par
suggests:
\par
... it is much easier now than it has been in the past to do things
\par
that lie outside familiar routine-innovation itself is being re-
\par
duced to routine. Technological progress is increasingly becom-
\par
ing the business of teams of trained specialists who turn out
\par
what is required and make it work in predictable ways. [em-
\par
phasis added]
\par
If what is routine innovation differs across firms, there
\par
may be different forms of entrepreneurship coexisting at
\par
any time.
\par
The following terminology is used. For the case of one
\par
or a few key individuals possessing entrepreneurial re-
\par
sources, these resources are referred to as "individual en-
\par
trepreneurial resources"; the individuals possessing them
\par
are "individual entrepreneurs"; and the aggregation of
\par
these individuals is the "individual entrepreneurial form."
\par
For the case of multiple individuals operating as a team
\par
with entrepreneurial resources distributed across team
\par
members, these resources are referred to as "team entre-
\par
preneurial resources"; the individuals possessing them are
\par
"team entrepreneurs"; and the aggregation of these indi-
\par
viduals is the "team entrepreneurial form."
\par
3.2. How Will These Distinct Forms Be Organized?
\par
3.2.1. Entrepreneurial Resources and Entrepreneurial
\par
Outcomes. What is the relation between entrepreneurial
\par
resources and entrepreneurial outcomes? Foresight and
\par
alertness will affect which future scenarios an individual
\par
envisions and which of these contains profitable oppor-
\par
tunities. Intuition and creativity may also play useful roles
\par
in constructing and analyzing the options associated with
\par
these scenarios. However, the effect of entrepreneurial
\par
resources on performance is not necessarily positive. In-
\par
tuition may mislead a decision maker; creativity may pro-
\par
duce a bad idea; foresight may lead a decision maker
\par
down a blind alley; alertness may distract a decision
\par
maker from attention to current operations. A simple
\par
working proposition is that entrepreneurial resources lead
\par
to choices likely to be superior on average to those gen-
\par
erated without the benefit of these resources. The reason-
\par
ing here is that entrepreneurial resources may be a source
\par
of idiosyncracy. In other words, these resources will often
\par
produce unique variations, some of which will affect the
\par
firm's possible competitive outcomes. However, it is dif-
\par
ficult to map out completely the complex relationship be-
\par
tween entrepreneurial resources and entrepreneurial out-
\par
comes. To do so would require considerable further
\par
research.
\par
At this stage, I offer the following simple proposition.
\par
PROPOSITION 1. Over a series of decisions, individ-
\par
uals possessing entrepreneurial resources are more likely
\par
to generate entrepreneurial outcomes than are individu-
\par
als without these resources, ceteris paribus.
\par
This proposition acknowledges the stochastic element by
\par
focusing on the probability of generating a novel com-
\par
petitive outcome, not the certainty of doing so or the av-
\par
erage effect on firm profitability. Thus, some possessors
\par
of entrepreneurial resources may never generate an entre-
\par
preneurial outcome while others may produce many. De-
\par
rived from this same logic is a need to observe the effect
\par
of entrepreneurial resources over many decisions.
\par
Will individual entrepreneurial resources be more or
\par
less effective at generating novel competitive outcomes
\par
than team entrepreneurial resources? Certainly, social
\par
psychological research on teams suggests that teams may
\par
alternatively reinforce or break out of "status quo" think-
\par
ing (Hackman 1976, McGrath 1984, Lamm and Myers
\par
1978). However, economic models of decision making
\par
suggest that it is primarily the organization of the re-
\par
sources, e.g., whether decision makers are organized as a
\par
hierarchy or a committee, that determines the associated
\par
outcomes, such as the incidence of Type 1 and Type 2
\par
errors (Sah and Stiglitz 1988).
\par
3.2.2. The Decision Process and Entrepreneurial Re-
\par
sources. Agency theory is used here to elaborate on the
\par
efficient organization of team and individual entrepre-
\par
neurial forms. As Eisenhardt (1989) advises, I draw upon
\par
the positive stream of agency theory to generate propo-
\par
sitions that yield empirically testable hypotheses related
\par
to incentives, risk, organizational structure, task assign-
\par
ment, culture, information systems, and the limits to firm
\par
size.
\par
A basic premise of agency literature is that any con-
\par
flicting interests between the principal and agent or
\par
among agents lead to agency costs: the combined costs
\par
of the monitoring costs incurred by the principal, the
\par
bonding costs incurred by the agent, and the residual loss
\par
incurred because the cost of fully enforcing contracts ex-
\par
ceeds the benefits (Jensen and Meckling 1976). An inter-
\par
est in entrepreneurial resources is not inconsistent with
\par
agency literature as several authors in this stream of re-
\par
search have included creativity in defining managerial ef-
\par
fort (Jensen and Meckling 1976, p. 313; Marcus 1979).
\par
In addition, agency costs are likely to be great when en-
\par
trepreneurial resources are an important production factor
\par
because monitoring the acts of creativity, foresight, in-
\par
tuition, and alertness is costly. Thus, agency costs will
\par
likely dictate the most efficient ways that individual and
\par
team entrepreneurial forms might be organized.
