\par
Similarly, one can conduct this comparative exercise
\par
at the firm level. Given a pattern of realized outcomes,
\par
one can ascertain the residual risk of the firm. Fama and
\par
Jensen (1983, p. 302) define residual risk as "the risk of
\par
the difference between stochastic inflows of resources
\par
and promised payments to agents." Although residual risk
\par
is a well-defined concept in agency literature, residual
\par
risk may also appropriately measure the ex post valuation
\par
of a firm' s overall entrepreneurial capability. The residual
\par
risk generated is equivalent to the "entrepreneurial rents"
\par
to which Kirzner (1973) and Rumelt (1987) refer. Thus,
\par
although this paper does not define the entrepreneur as
\par
the bearer of uncertainty in the Knightian tradition, one
\par
can relate the resulting residual risk to entrepreneurship
\par
as suggested here (see also Barzel 1987a).
\par
Who bears this risk? In a paper that argues for sepa-
\par
rating the managerial and risk-bearing roles, Fama (1980)
\par
suggests that the manager does not generally need to bear
\par
the residual risk of a firm's operations. Instead, by pro-
\par
posing a multiperiod model with stochastic outcomes,
\par
Fama (1980) shows that agency costs can often be re-
\par
duced in the managerial labor market. Frequent revisions
\par
of the manager's wages should reflect the manager's ex-
\par
pected marginal product, which reflects his past actual
\par
marginal products. As long as future periods abound, this
\par
produces full ex post settling up and there is no necessity
\par
to shift risk onto the managers. However, Fama (1980, p.
\par
298) acknowledges an exception to this:
\par
Of course, changes in a manager's wealth as a consequence of
\par
anticipated future wage revisions are not always equivalent to
\par
full ex post settling up. When a manager does not expect to be
\par
in the labor market for many future periods, the weight of future
\par
wage revisions due to current assessments of performance may
\par
amount to substantially less than full ex post settling up.
\par
In other words, managers playing an end-game strategy
\par
may shirk more than those facing many years in the labor
\par
market because wage reductions in future periods that
\par
result from shirking will be less.
\par
Individuals possessing entrepreneurial resources might
\par
be more likely to exit the labor market than other indi-
\par
viduals, suggesting that mechanisms other than wage ad-
\par
justments may be necessary to reduce shirking. The rea-
\par
son for this is not so obvious as it may first appear. That
\par
an individual acting on the same set of expectations can
\par
be equally compensated by the labor market (assuming
\par
some risk adjustment) or the product market must be con-
\par
sidered. Why an individual possessing entrepreneurial re-
\par
sources might leave the labor market may rest upon what
\par
Knight (1921) calls "confidence" in judgment. Individu-
\par
als who consider implementing a creative idea in their
\par
own firm (outside the labor market) will choose whether
\par
to go ahead with the commitment of resources based on
\par
confidence in their own judgment. When this same ques-
\par
tion arises within the labor market, others' confidence in
\par
an entrepreneur's judgment will determine whether re-
\par
sources are allocated to this idea. Clearly, significant dif-
\par
ferences between self-confidence and the confidence of
\par
others may exist and affect whether an individual en-
\par
dowed with entrepreneurial resources will exit the labor
\par
market. If self-confidence is generally higher than others'
\par
confidence in an individual's judgment, those possessing
\par
entrepreneurial resources may exit the labor market more
\par
than individuals not possessing entrepreneurial resources.
\par
Even in those cases where managers are not more likely
\par
to exit the labor market, frequent revisions of their wages
\par
may not be efficient at reducing shirking. The impact of
\par
frequent wage revisions depends upon the actual mana-
\par
gerial product in past periods. Barzel (1987b, p. 105) sug-
\par
gests that, when an individual engages in nonroutine
\par
work, "the renumeration of the entrepreneur takes the
\par
form of a residual because his contribution to output and
\par
to its value are difficult to measure." Thus, individuals
\par
who possess entrepreneurial resources will generally bear
\par
the firm's residual risk.
\par
3.2.4. The Individual Entrepreneurial Form. This
\par
section suggests that, for the individual entrepreneurial
\par
form, an efficient organization is one in which the man-
\par
agerial, entrepreneurial, and risk-bearing roles are all
\par
played by the individual or few key individuals possess-
\par
ing the individual entrepreneurial resources. In other
\par
words, individuals possessing these resources will con-
\par
duct the managerial and risk-bearing roles in the long run.
\par
Why? With individual entrepreneurial resources, it may
\par
be more efficient for decision initiation and ratification to
\par
be conducted by the individual entrepreneur(s). Note that
\par
this argument says little about who should implement and
\par
monitor the decision. As noted above, pure implementa-
\par
tion could be easily delegated to an individual who pro-
\par
vides labor in return for a set payment. In addition, be-
\par
cause the residual risk of the decision is likely to be borne
\par
by the same individuals who initiate and ratify decisions,
\par
monitoring becomes relatively unimportant to the indi-
\par
vidual entrepreneurial form. Under these circumstances,
\par
there is little need to measure inputs and assign rewards
\par
to decision agents, because these rewards will be realized
\par
as residual payments tied to outcomes.
\par
In the efficient individual entrepreneurial form, key in-
\par
dividuals possessing entrepreneurial resources will be re-
\par
sponsible for the decision initiation and ratification, while
\par
other employees will be involved in implementation and
\par
will be engaged by the firm via fixed contracts for labor
\par
inputs. One might describe the individual entrepreneurial
