3.3. Comparison of Results
\par
This subsection compares the results of this agency model
\par
involving entrepreneurial resources with the results of the
\par
traditional model involving knowledge resources (Fama
\par
and Jensen 1983). The form in Fama and Jensen's (1983)
\par
work closest to my individual form is the "classic entre-
\par
preneurial firm" in which decision initiation, ratification,
\par
implementation, monitoring, and risk bearing are all per-
\par
formed by a single entrepreneur possessing considerable
\par
knowledge. In the classic entrepreneurial firm, the single
\par
entrepreneur is not only the visionary, but also the doer
\par
and overseer of the process. I would expect that the clas-
\par
sic entrepreneurial firm and the individual entrepreneurial
\par
form would be structured similarly.
\par
Comparing the above propositions with the organiza-
\par
tional forms described in Fama and Jensen (1983) is more
\par
interesting for the form in which the key resource is dif-
\par
fused across actors. Fama and Jensen's organizational
\par
form is closest to my team entrepreneurial form is the
\par
"complex" organization. "Complex organization" refers
\par
to a firm in which knowledge relevant to critical decisions
\par
is diffused throughout the organization and knowledge is
\par
costly to transfer among individuals. In the complex or-
\par
ganization, Fama and Jensen (1983) suggest that top-level
\par
controllers will perform the decision ratification and
\par
monitoring steps, whereas next in the hierarchy come the
\par
managers who initiate and implement decisions. In this
\par
discussion, those at the top of the hierarchy would have
\par
considerable control over which proposals are pursued.
\par
In my description of the team entrepreneurial form, the
\par
monitor at the top level of the hierarchy serves a different
\par
function. The monitor in the team entrepreneurial form
\par
focuses primarily on hiring, firing, and promoting indi-
\par
viduals based on his judgment of their propensities to
\par
shirk and act with guile. In this way, the monitor acts like
\par
a personnel manager. In addition, to reduce shirking by
\par
entrepreneurs engaged in decision initiation and ratifica-
\par
tion, the monitor may invest in other mechanisms, such
\par
as mutual monitoring and bonding devices or information
\par
systems. Overall, one might characterize the monitor as
\par
playing the role of designer or architect, rather than the
\par
builder exerting hands-on intervention. He is the organi-
\par
zational visionary who creates the organizational context
\par
out of which innovations emerge. Innovative steps in the
\par
decision process will occur lower in the hierarchy and be
\par
dispersed among the entrepreneurial team. For both the
\par
team entrepreneurial form and Fama and Jensen's (1983)
\par
complex organization, individuals in the top level of man-
\par
agement will bear the residual risk of the enterprise if they
\par
are few in number and able to bear the risk.
\par
Given these different results generated by an emphasis
\par
on diffused knowledge versus team entrepreneurial re-
\par
sources, an obvious, but difficult, question arises. What
\par
will the efficient organizational form be when both
\par
knowledge and entrepreneurial resources are important
\par
and dispersed? Whether entrepreneurial resources are in-
\par
puts to a team production process may be independent of
\par
whether knowledge resources are inputs to a team pro-
\par
duction process. Thus, one may observe individual entre-
\par
preneurial resources alongside knowledge diffused
\par
among a large number of agents in the organization.
\par
Which of these will have the greater influence on the ef-
\par
ficient type of organization may depend on whether en-
\par
trepreneurial or knowledge resources contribute more to
\par
the firm's agency costs. The efficient organizational form
\par
will be that which balances the agency costs associated
\par
with entrepreneurial resources against the agency costs
\par
associated with knowledge resources against the costs of
\par
reducing discretionary behavior.
\par
Another difference between the above arguments and
\par
