4. Conclusion
\par
This paper's model of entrepreneurship emphasizes the
\par
locus of entrepreneurial resources, defined as the behav-
\par
ioral propensities of individuals to be creative, intuitive,
\par
alert, and farsighted. Two extreme cases were compared:
\par
one in which entrepreneurial resources are held primarily
\par
by one or a few key individuals (the individual entrepre-
\par
neurial form), and one in which entrepreneurial resources
\par
are dispersed throughout the organization (the team en-
\par
trepreneurial form). Drawing upon agency theory, I de-
\par
scribed different organizational characteristics that will
\par
be associated with these two forms. The individual entre-
\par
preneurial form is likely to have decision initiation and
\par
ratification and bearing of residual risk performed by the
\par
possessor of the entrepreneurial resources. By contrast,
\par
decision initiation and ratification will be separated and
\par
dispersed throughout the team entrepreneurial form, yet
\par
these functions will also be performed by the many pGs-
\par
sessors of entrepreneurial resources. As such, the overall
\par
decision-making process will be more diffused and de-
\par
centralized. In addition, monitoring will become central
\par
to the team entrepreneurial form and will be executed by
\par
individuals possessing entrepreneurial resources that help
\par
in judging individuals. This role will result in an addi-
\par
tional hierarchical layer for overseeing decision initiation
\par
and ratification, with the top monitor level bearing a large
\par
portion of the enterprise's residual risk. However, the
\par
monitor level will not be involved in operational or stra-
\par
tegic decisions, but focus instead on managing an internal
\par
market for managers and developing an organization's
\par
cultural context and information systems.
\par
The question of whether individual or team entrepre-
\par
neurial organizations are likely to be more risk-seeking
\par
defies a simple answer. The accepted wisdom derived
\par
from agency theory is that the classic entrepreneurial
\par
firm-in this case paralleled by the individual entrepre-
\par
neurial form-will be more risk averse. I proposed an
\par
alternative prediction based on the idea that more shirking
\par
will occur in the team entrepreneurial form. This shirking
\par
will take the form of withholding entrepreneurial ser-
\par
vices-or more likely, employing entrepreneurial re-
\par
sources for ends that do not enhance the interests of re-
\par
sidual claimants-which may reduce the variability of
\par
outcomes.
\par
In developing these propositions, I have to some extent
\par
joined agency theory's retracing of steps taken by orga-
\par
nizational theorists that suggests the need for decentral-
\par
izing the decision process. Weber's (1930, 1947, 1968)
\par
work on bureaucracy suggests that by adding layers of
\par
hierarchy, dispersing decision-making responsibilities,
\par
reducing the financial risk to individuals, and instituting
\par
other bureaucratic practices, one can create a social sys-
\par
tem in which individual whim and discretion can be re-
\par
placed with technical efficiency embodied by hierarchical
\par
position. More recent organizational research on the mul-
\par
tidivisional form (m-form)-such as Williamson (1975),
\par
Williamson and Bhargava (1972), and Chandler (1977)-
\par
suggests that in large complex organizations not only
\par
must decisions be decentralized, but also the autonomy
\par
of the divisions must be maintained. Corporate head-
\par
quarters' role is limited to general strategic thinking and
\par
the overseeing of the division managers. Bartlett and
\par
Ghoshal (1993) take this decentralization even further by
\par
suggesting that a corporate headquarter's role in strategy
\par
making and entrepreneurship should be more limited than
\par
in the m-form: to create a context and set of organiza-
\par
tional processes that define an organization's purpose.
\par
However, my work serves to enhance rather than sim-
\par
ply replicate the conclusions drawn from this related re-
\par
search. The entrepreneur is notably missing from the ef-
\par
ficient Weberian bureaucracy; in fact, this bureaucracy is
\par
expected to stifle individual creativity and discretion to
\par
the point that Weber (1930, p. 181) described the pursuit
\par
of economic ends under capitalism as an "iron cage." In
\par
my description of the team entrepreneurial form, formal
\par
bureaucratic structures and systems may coexist with
\par
high levels of autonomy and discretion that allow entre-
\par
preneurs to be creative, foresightful, intuitive, and alert.
\par
In addition, the information processing requirements as-
\par
sociated with decisions carried out in a large, complex
\par
organization are the primary motivation for separating
\par
operational decisions (assigned to divisions) from stra-
\par
tegic decisions (assigned to corporate headquarters) in the
\par
m-form. This separation differs slightly in the team en-
\par
trepreneurial form: operational and strategic decisions oc-
\par
cur within the divisions and the apex of the organization
\par
is responsible for facilitating an internal market for man-
\par
agers. In addition, this separation occurs for reasons not
\par
directly related to information processing requirements,
