\par
In developing this model of entrepreneurial forms,
\par
however, I have assumed that the type of entrepreneurial
\par
resources determines which form emerges. Implicitly, the
\par
supply of both team and individual entrepreneurial re-
\par
sources is assumed to be exogenous, consistent with def-
\par
initions based on behavioral propensities. If entrepre-
\par
neurial resources are defined to be learned or acquired
\par
skills, one would need to propose a full-equilibrium
\par
model in which investments in these skills-by the in-
\par
dividual and the firm-would be determined by market
\par
forces. When the supply of entrepreneurial resources is
\par
allowed to vary, however, many potentially interesting
\par
questions arise. If the individual and team entrepreneurial
\par
forms represent two extremes along a continuum, what
\par
does a firm's choice along this continuum depend upon?
\par
For example, the internal corporate venture movement to
\par
construct autonomous innovative subunits in the 1970s
\par
(Fast and Pratt 1981) might be seen as an attempt to move
\par
unsuccessful examples of team entrepreneurial forms to-
\par
ward the individual entrepreneurial form. What caused
\par
these firms to attempt such a transformation rather than
\par
to improve the existing team form? The internal corporate
\par
venture example is particularly compelling in presuppos-
\par
ing the harmonious coexistence of individual and team
\par
entrepreneurial forms. This raises the question of what
\par
firms operating somewhere between the extremes might
\par
look like and why (Hennart 1993).
\par
In addition, my concept of entrepreneurship may be
\par
relevant to many questions that underlie research on cor-
\par
porate entrepreneurship. My first example has to do with
\par
the roles tlhat corporate entrepreneurs play. According to
\par
agency literature, the breakdown of the decision process
\par
roughly corresponds to common terms in corporate en-
\par
trepreneurship literature (e.g. Schon 1963, Rothwell et al.
\par
1974, Maidique 1980, Garud and Van de Ven 1992). The
\par
innovation visionary corresponds roughly to the decision
\par
initiator, the innovation or product champion to the de-
\par
cision ratifier, the inventor to the decision implementor,
\par
the skeptic (or what Rothwell et al. (1974) refer to as the
\par
"chief executive") to the decision monitor, and the firm
\par
owner to the bearer of residual risk. The theoretical ar-
\par
guments in this paper might shed light on why discussion
\par
of these separate roles-i.e., visionary, champion, inven-
\par
tor, skeptic, etc.-has occurred in research on corporate
\par
entrepreneurship. These roles are much more likely to be
\par
carried out by different individuals in the team entrepre-
\par
neurial forms, and are therefore more apparent.
\par
My second example is that research on corporate en-
\par
trepreneurship often emphasizes the following factors
\par
that affect an extant organization's innovation: Rewards,
\par
management support, resources, organizational structure,
\par
and risk-taking (see the review of this literature in
\par
Kuratko et al. 1990). A well-developed theoretical lens
\par
like agency theory focuses on why these and not other
\par
factor influence entrepreneurial outcomes and how these
\par
factors may be interrelated.
\par
Finally, my concept of entrepreneurial resources
\par
speaks to the issue of where innovation and entrepre-
\par
neurship occurs in a corporate hierarchy: top-, middle-,
\par
or low-level managers or from workers at the bottom.
\par
This question has fascinated scholars studying entrepre-
\par
neurship (Bower 1970, Burgelman 1983, Day 1994,
\par
Kanter 1983, Chandler 1962, Galbraith 1985, Galunic
\par
and Eisenhardt 1996) yet no clearcut reconciliation of dif-
\par
ferences has emerged in the literature. The propositions
\par
in this paper suggest one solution that separates out the
\par
condition under which innovation emerges from the top
\par
of the corporate hierarchy-namely, when entrepreneur-
\par
ial resources are possessed by one or a few key individ-
\par
uals. I also show the condition under which innovation
\par
emerges from lower down in the hierarchy-when entre-
\par
preneurial resources are diffused among a large number
\par
of individuals. In addition, the differentiated roles of
\par
top-, middle-, and lower-level managers suggest that top
\par
management will play the role of monitor, while next in
\par
the hierarchy is decision ratification, followed by decision
\par
initiation lower down. Thus, in the team entrepreneurial
\par
form, innovations may be initiated by the lower manage-
\par
ment tier.
\par
Having noted these links with other literatures, I ac-
\par
knowledge the limitations of the agency model proposed
\par
here. Perhaps the most fundamental limitation is an em-
\par
phasis on the internal consistency of the argument at the
\par
expense of managerial grounding. Leading this critique,
\par
Bartlett and Ghoshal (1993) suggest the need to develop
\par
"managerial theories" derived from analyzing actual firm
\par
operations from the perspective and using the language
\par
of its managers. Clearly the high level of abstraction in
\par
the current paper might seem divorced from reality.
\par
Nonetheless, the model presented here converges with the
\par
