\par
managerial theory that Bartlett and Ghoshal (1993) pro-
\par
posed after analyzing Asea Brown Boveri' s (ABB) opera-
\par
tions. Their conclusions are similar to the team entrepre-
\par
neurial form. In the organizational form observed at ABB,
\par
the entrepreneurial role is taken away from top manage-
\par
ment and assigned to front-line managers. Middle man-
\par
agers play critical roles in supporting and developing this
\par
lower level initiative, including establishing horizontal
\par
linkages. Top managers establish the appropriate culture/
\par
context and organizational processes-including "trans-
\par
lating those values into action through their selection and
\par
promotion of individuals reflecting such behaviors ..
\par
(Bartlett and Ghoshal 1993, p. 34). The organizational
\par
form that Bartlett and Ghoshal describe is no perfect
\par
match with the team entrepreneurial form. For example,
\par
Bartlett and Ghoshal (1993) suggest that the role of man-
\par
agers has moved away from one of control to collabora-
\par
tion; I continue to emphasize the need for managerial
\par
oversight. However, Bartlett and Ghoshal's (1993) gen-
\par
eral focus on the roles and relationships defining the or-
\par
ganization is shared with agency theory. This conver-
\par
gence of methods for theory development suggests a need
\par
for parallel paths to both challenge and reinforce one an-
\par
other.
\par
In conclusion, this research suggests the need for future
\par
research into many forms that acknowledge the impor-
\par
tance of entrepreneurship to organization and strategy.
\par
The premise that entrepreneurial resources may generate
\par
rents is fundamental to both the resource-based view of
\par
strategy as well as most work on entrepreneurship from
\par
a strategic perspective. Understanding where entrepre-
\par
neurial resources come from, how they become embed-
\par
ded organizationally, and how they generate rents via
\par
competitive processes can only advance research. In ad-
\par
dition, further attention to other types of resources will
\par
only broaden the reach of the resource-based view of
\par
strategy. While the current paper has highlighted individ-
\par
ual propensities toward creativity, alertness, foresight,
\par
and intuition, future resource-based research might study
\par
very different types of resources. For example, present
\par
knowledge of the importance of value systems-either at
\par
the individual, organizational, industry, or nation-state
\par
level-as a source of competitive advantage is limited.
\par
Although studying a wide range of intangible assets may
\par
prove even more difficult than studying knowledge, we
\par
cannot talk about concepts like strategic vision and or-
\par
ganizational culture without noting their ties to an orga-
\par
nizational member's motivations, values, and even emo-
\par
tions.
