\par
models.46 \emph{C.A. Oriade, C.R. Dillon/ Agricultural Economics 17 ( 1997) 45-58}
\par
and/or biological issues in agriculture. Such infor-
\par
mation will help identify the potential strengths of
\par
simulation that the modelers can build upon, as well
\par
as the possible pitfalls to avoid. Now that simulation
\par
is increasingly being used for decision support, infor-
\par
mation from such a periodic evaluation could pre-
\par
vent costly mistakes from being made or repeated.
\par
In the sections that follow, some conceptual is-
\par
sues in simulation are first discussed. Next, factors
\par
that could enhance or limit the appeal of simulation
\par
are highlighted. Third, the distinguishing features of
\par
the class of simulators commonly used in agricul-
\par
tural production research are presented. Fourth, a
\par
number of common simulators and their applications
\par
are surveyed. Finally, an appraisal of these simula-
\par
tors and their applications is undertaken while some
\par
of the factors that could enhance the continued rele-
\par
vance of this methodology are indicated.
\par
\textbf{2. Certain concepts in simulation}
\par
For the sake of exposition, a brief overview of
\par
certain issues in simulation is presented as a thor-
\par
ough discourse of these concepts has been under-
\par
taken elsewhere (Dent and Anderson, 1971; Ander-
\par
son, 1974; Johnson and Rausser, 1977). Simulation
\par
is a numerical manipulation of a symbolic model of
\par
a system with a view to generating experimental
\par
information about the systems or the model of sys-
\par
tems (Anderson, 1974; Johnson and Rausser, 1977;
\par
Czaki, 1984). Five phases in simulation are generally
\par
recognized. These are: the model specification, pa-
\par
rameter estimation, verification (which may also in-
\par
clude calibration), validation and revision stages
\par
(Anderson, 1974; Johnson and Rausser, 1977; Musser
\par
and Tew, 1984). The tasks to be accomplished in
\par
these phases are somehow self-explanatory except
\par
for the verification and validation functions which
\par
are often misconstrued. As will be seen in the next
\par
section, these two steps are arguably the mostly
\par
criticized components of simulation which deserve
\par
further elucidation.
\par
Verification is the process of establishing the truth
\par
or correctness of model's reality (Dent and Ander-
\par
son, 1971). It is the process by which the investiga-
\par
tor determines whether or not the model performs in
\par
accordance with the intended purpose (Johnson and
\par
Rausser, 1977). Verification may be undertaken at
\par
the level of programming the model (Dillon et al.,
\par
1991) and/or debugging the program (Anderson,
\par
1974; Law and Kelton, 1991). On the other hand,
\par
validation is the process of ascertaining the closeness
\par
of the model to reality which is reflected in the
\par
model's ability to duplicate the required character-
\par
istics of the systems and fulfilling the modelling
\par
objectives (Dent and Anderson, 1971; Wright, 1971;
\par
Johnson and Rausser, 1977). Simply put, the objec-
\par
tive of validation is to ascertain the usefulness, rather
\par
than the truthfulness, of the model (Dillon et al.,
\par
1991).
\par
Implicit in the above concepts of verification and
\par
validation is the fact that both duties need not be
\par
performed by the same set of investigators. Often,
\par
simulators are usually not developed oy the end-users.
\par
Therefore, while the function of model verification
\par
may rest with the developers, the users are certainly
\par
responsible for validating the models to establish the
\par
relevance of such simulators to the specific problems
\par
under investigation.
\par
Naylor and Finger (1971) proffer three method-
\par
ological concepts of validation in economics which
\par
can be extended to validation in computer simula-
\par
tion. Law and Kelton ( 1991) expatiate on these
\par
concepts by providing specific recommendations on
\par
how these concepts can be implemented. The first
\par
concept, rationalism, holds that economic models are
\par
simply a system of logical deductions from synthetic
\par
premises of unquestionable truth that are not them-
\par
selves open to empirical validation (Naylor and Fin-
\par
ger, 1971). Thus the role of validation is to identify a
\par
set of basic assumptions governing the behavior of
\par
the system being modeled. For this concept, the
\par
operational framework proposed by Law and Kelton
\par
(1991) is that the model should possess high face
\par
validity that seems reasonable to people who are
\par
knowledgeable about the system being modeled.
\par
These underlying assumptions may be identified by
\par
using any combination of these methods: conversa-
\par
tions with system experts, observation of the system,
\par
studying the results from earlier attempts, checking
\par
the existing theories, the use of experience and intu-
\par
ition.
\par
The second concept, empiricism is in complete
\par
opposition to rationalism as it refuses any assump-
\par
tions that cannot be independently verified. To im-\emph{C.A. Oriade, C.R. Dillon/ Agricultural Economics 17 (1997) 45-58 }47
\par
plement this concept, Law and Kelton (1991) recom-
\par
mend the testing of model's assumptions empirically
\par
by using either the goodness of fit statistics, sensitiv-
\par
ity analyses or other testing parameters. Finally, the
\par
last concept, positive economics holds that a satisfac-
\par
tory validation is the one that demonstrates an effi-
\par
cient ability of the model to predict the dependent
\par
variables that are treated by the model regardless of
\par
whether the underlying assumptions are valid or not.
\par
Naylor and Finger (1971) further argue that a
\par
satisfactory validation is one that incorporates basic
\par
elements of the three concepts in a multistage valida-
\par
tion (MV) fashion. In MV, each of the three concep-
\par
tual positions is regarded as a necessary but not
\par
sufficient procedure for validating simulation experi-
\par
ments (Naylor and Finger, 1971; Ray and Richard-
\par
son, 1978). The first stage in MV calls for the
\par
formulation of a set of hypotheses or postulates
\par
underlying the model. The second stage attempts to
\par
validate the postulates subject to the limitations im-
\par
posed by existing statistical tools. The third and final
\par
stage in MV tests the model's ability to predict the
\par
behavior of the system under investigation. For this
\par
definitive test, when real systems exist, validation
\par
may be a relatively easy task of comparing the
\par
simulated data to real, out of sample data (King et
\par
al., 1993). If the population distribution of the real
\par
systems is known, the generated data can be tested
\par
statistically to see whether they could come from
\par
such a distribution (Anderson, 1974). However, if
\par
information on the real systems is not available,
\par
which is the common reason for most ex ante simu-
\par
lation analyses, validation becomes subjective and
\par
the expert opinion plays a key role in determining
\par
the credibility of the model (Law and Kelton, 1991).
\par
In all cases, an acid test of satisfactory validation is
\par
the ability to show that the simulation model leads to
\par
a better decision which cannot be readily obtained
\par
with alternative techniques (Wright, 1971).
\par
\textbf{3. Strengths and }weaknesses of \textbf{simulation}
\par
Numerous factors have contributed to the
\par
widespread use of simulation in agricultural eco-
\par
nomics research. First, by training, economists are
\par
generally not equipped to undertake physical experi-
\par
mentation that would produce needed information
\par
for economic studies. Therefore, when satisfactory
\par
experimental data can not be sourced from physical
\par
scientists, simulation becomes inevitable because of
\par
the relative ease of generating the input-output data
\par
that are needed. Also, simulation provides a means
\par
for incorporating certain stochastic variables (e.g.,
\par
states of nature) that are not easily observable but
\par
can facilitate a better understanding of the system
\par
being modeled. Simulation becomes a more powerful
\par
tool when the generated data lead to sound manage-
\par
ment decisions that cannot be outclassed by using
\par
alternative techniques. Simulation can substitute for
\par
large scale physical experimentation while ex ante
\par
simulation can aid the understanding of a system that
\par
does not exist or that is currently impracticable to
\par
experiment (Anderson, 1974; Johnson and Rausser,
\par
1977).
\par
Another factor that is responsible for the explo-
\par
sive use of simulation is the increasing computer
\par
power and affordability (Dillon et al., 1991; King et
\par
al., 1993). One of the early concerns of simulation is
\par
its high cost. For this reason, Dillon (1971) notes
\par
that simulation, once started, will continue until
\par
available funds are exhausted. The observation of
\par
Anderson (1974) that a study through simulation
\par
always absorbs more resources than anticipated a
\par
priori duly complements this position. Therefore, It
\par
is not surprising to see an increased use of simula-
\par
tion as technological advances lower computation
\par
costs and make it very competitive when compared
\par
to physical experimentation or other methods.
\par
The bulk of systems simulation has been used to
\par
address complex stochastic and/ or dynamic prob-
\par
lems in agriculture. Application to irrigation projects
\par
represents a substantial component of simulation
\par
(Anderson, 1974; Johnson and Rausser, 1977; King
\par
et al., 1993). In general, simulation has been used
\par
primarily as a research tool with little consideration
\par
for its credibility where credible models are simula-
\par
tors that have been widely accepted to the extent of
\par
being used as an aid in making decisions (Law and
\par
Kelton, 1991). However, the increasing demand for
\par
such credible models is now stimulating interest in
\par
the development of specialized simulators that meet
\par
these needs (King et al., 1993).
\par
The criticisms of simulation are also worthy of
\par
consideration. Virtually all empirical applications
\par
have not placed much premium on the importance of48 \emph{C.A. Oriade, C.R. Dillon/ Agricultural Economics 17 ( 1997) 45-58}
\par
model validation (Johnson and Rausser, 1977; King
\par
et al., 1993). Since a majority of these simulators are
\par
usually developed by parties that are external to their
\par
subsequent usage, validation is a prerequisite for
\par
discerning whether the predetermined model assump-
\par
tions and the resultant model prescriptions are trans-
\par
portable and applicable to the new environment or
\par
problem under investigation. Also, some users may
\par
need to strengthen their justification for employing
\par
simulation tools as it is possible that feasible, cost-
\par
effective alternative techniques that can yield supe-
\par
rior results are available.
\par
While the modelling objectives of economists of-
\par
ten involve some optimization, potential developers
\par
and users sometimes fail to recognize that simulation
\par
is not necessarily an optimal procedure and its appli-
\par
cation to a problem does not automatically lead to an
\par
optimal solution. While the search for an efficient
\par
way of using simulation as a means of optimization
\par
is an area deserving attention, few approaches have
\par
been suggested to deal with optimization of systems
\par
through simulation modelling (Anderson, 1974;
\par
Czaki, 1984; Azadivar and Lee, 1988). According to
\par
Azadivar and Lee (1988), these are: the Response
\par
Surface Methodology (RSM), direct search, and
\par
Stochastic Approximation Method (SAM). RSM en-
\par
tails the estimation of the regression parameters of
\par
linear or quadratic form of simulation outcomes (Box
\par
and Wilson, 1951; Smith, 1976). If the number of
\par
feasible simulation outcomes is limited, direct search
\par
for optimal results may be possible either with re-
\par
peated iterations (Anderson, 1974; Czaki, 1984) or
\par
by careful enumeration and evaluation of all possible
\par
outcomes (King et al., 1993; Swinton and King,
\par
1994). However, since a sizeable number of simula-
\par
tion models are often stochastic and more compli-
\par
cated in nature, the third category, SAM, which is a
\par
group of statistically convergent search methods,
\par
may be more appropriate (Kushner and Clark, 1978).
\par
A heuristic procedure developed by Azadivar and
\par
Lee (1988) for optimizing complex stochastic sys-
\par
tems modeled by simulation is a good example of
\par
the SAM method. However, users need to balance
\par
the gains of optimization with the extra costs of
\par
pursuing any of these optimization procedures.
\par
Other criticisms of simulation include: the need to
\par
validate the simulator in every application which
\par
makes its use time-consuming and expensive
\par
(Ramos, 1987), the divergence between model re-
\par
sults and real world situations (Anderson, 1974), the
\par
fact that many simulation decisions and inputs are
\par
predetermined and uncontrollable thus necessitating
\par
an interdisciplinary cooperation for the simulators to
\par
be adaptable for other research (Musser and Tew,
\par
1984).
\par
\textbf{4. Biophysical and bioeconomic simulation}
\par
Surveys of simulators and their applications have
\par
usually been conducted by subject area (Dent and
\par
Anderson, 1971; Johnson and Rausser, 1977; Czaki,
\par
1984; Musser and Tew, 1984). Although there is no
\par
consensus as to the appropriate jargon for this classi-
\par
fication, the categories provided by Johnson and
\par
Rausser (1977) will suffice. Their five broad classes
\par
are the firm and process, the market, the aggregate,
\par
the development and the natural resource models.
\par
In agricultural production, a sub-set of firm and
\par
process models, otherwise known as biophysical
\par
simulators, are very common (Boggess, 1984; Musser
\par
and Tew, 1984). Biophysical simulators (BPS) are
\par
process models that explicitly account for the biolog-
\par
ical andjor physical components of agricultural pro-
\par
duction (Musser and Tew, 1984). These simulators
\par
provide the means of generating production response
\par
surfaces required for empirical production research
\par
(Boggess, 1984).
\par
Often, BPS and bioeconomic simulators or mod-
\par
els have been used interchangeably. A bioeconomic
\par
model is a mathematical representation of a biologi-
\par
cal system which describes biological processes and
\par
predicts the effects of management decisions on
\par
those processes (King et al., 1993). In practical
\par
applications, there seems to be no clear distinction
\par
between BPS and bioeconomic models (BEMS). For
\par
instance, the literature cited in BPS and BEMS
\par
surveys often overlap (see, e.g., Musser and Tew,
\par
1984; Dillon et al., 1991; King et al., 1993). How-
\par
ever, a few attempts have been made to distinguish
\par
between both classes of sii):lulators. In general, BPS,
\par
by themselves, do not irytlude an economic compo-
\par
nent. Musser and Tew \textbraceleft 1984) contend that it is the
\par
magnitude of physical processes that differentiates
\par
BPS from BEMS. However, this distinction fades as
\par
BPS is intensively used to solve economic problems.\emph{C.A. Oriade, C.R. Dillon/ Agricultural Economics 17 (1997) 45-58 }49
\par
A more definitive difference between BPS and BEMS
\par
can be gleaned from King et al. (1993). They notice
\par
that BEMS generally fall within the class of predic-
\par
tive models where biological processes are repre-
\par
sented by a relatively small number of equations
\par
relating current state and control variable levels to
\par
future states of the system. Conversely, BPS belong
\par
to a class of process models where relatively detailed
\par
subsystems of physical and biologically processes
\par
are linked in an overall representation of plant (or
\par
animal) growth in such a way that the structure and
\par
parameters of component subsystems are based on
\par
general theoretical models of specific physical or
\par
biological processes. Nevertheless, it is rather diffi-
\par
cult to distinguish between both simulators in prac-
\par
tice and the remainder of this survey will focus on
\par
both sets of simulators jointly.
\par
\textbf{5. Survey of BPS and BEMS simulators}
\par
The simulators reviewed in this section are neither
\par
representative nor exhaustive of all relevant models.
\par
Rather, the choice was influenced either by their
\par
acceptance, as reflected in the number of applica-
\par
tions, or by the extensive involvement of agricultural
\par
economists in their development. Even with these
\par
criteria, it is conceivable that some important simula-
\par
tors would have been inadvertently left out.
\par
SOYGRO (Wilkerson et al., 1983) and CERES
\par
(Ritchie and Otter, 1985) models are among the most
\par
famous process models that have been used in simu-
\par
lating whole crop-soil systems. Although SOYGRO
\par
was developed as a decision support tool for irriga-
\par
tion and pest management, it has been largely used
\par
for research purposes (Hoogenboom et al., 1992).
\par
SOYGRO simulates the yield, growth and develop-
\par
ment of soybeans \emph{(Glycine max }L. Merr) as a func-
\par
tion of soil, weather and management variables
\par
(Wilkerson et al., 1983). From its early version in
\par
the early 80's, SOYGRO has undergone substantial
\par
modifications such that it now ranks among the most
\par
flexible models whose validity of results is no longer
\par
location-specific or environment-dependent. Hoogen-
\par
boom et al. (1992) describe the measures that were
\par
taken to ensure that the predictions of the model are
\par
reliable. During verification, the code was checked
\par
so as to ensure that the programming logic was
\par
consistent and a true reflection of the system being
\par
modeled. This was accomplished with the aid of
\par
debugging tools of FORTRAN compilers and some
\par
static program analyzers. For calibration, data from
\par
experimental sites in Gainesville, Florida were com-
\par
pared to model predictions. SOYGRO has been ex-
\par
tensively validated in various locations in USA and
\par
Europe (Hoogenboom et al., 1992; Moulin and
\par
Beckie, 1993; Nagarajan et al., 1993; Savin et al.,
\par
1994; Gabrielle et al., 1995). Unlike many simula-
\par
tors, transportability of SOYGRO is enhanced by the
\par
generic format that was adopted for data entry so
\par
that site-specific information can be used for model
\par
predictions.
\par
To a large extent, the above features of SOYGRO
\par
are shared by other -GRO models for grain legumes.
\par
They are PNUTGRO for peanuts \emph{(Arachis hypogea}
\par
L.) and BEANGRO for common beans ( \emph{Phaseolus}
\par
\emph{vulgaris }L.). Also, these attributes characterize
\par
CERES (an acronym for crop estimation through
\par
resource and environment synthesis) class of models
\par
for cereals. The purpose of CERES models is to
\par
generate yield estimates by modelling factors that
\par
were considered to be most relevant in yield determi-
\par
nation. These factors include plant growth and devel-
\par
opment, soil water and nutrient status (Ritchie and
\par
Otter, 1985). Random factors that could be con-
\par
trolled by management such as pest and disease
\par
infestations were not considered. CERES Wheat and
\par
CERES Maize are the common types of CERES
\par
models. CERES and -GRO models are among the
\par
models within the framework of IBSNAT-DSSAT
\par
(the International Benchmark Sites Network for
\par
Agrotechnology Transfer Project Decision Support
\par
Systems for Agrotechnology Transfer) whose goal
\par
was to standardize the structure of these models in
\par
order to enhance their suitability for any region
\par
(Hoogenboom et al., 1992). Validation results for all
\par
or any components of SOYGRO and CERES models
\par
have generally shown a satisfactory level of corre-
\par
spondence between observable data and simulated
\par
variates, although certain sub-routines often require
\par
some recalibration before realistic model estimates
\par
are obtained (Moulin and Beckie, 1993; Nagarajan et
\par
al., 1993; Savin et al., 1994; Gabrielle et al., 1995).
\par
FLIPSIM (Richardson and Nixon, 1986) is a
\par
FORTRAN computer simulation model that was de-
\par
veloped for analyzing the probable consequences of50 \emph{C.A. Oriade, C.R. Dillon/ Agricultural Economics 17 (1997) 45-58}
\par
alternative policies and income tax developments on
\par
typical farms. It is a firm level, recursive, simulation
\par
model which generates the annual production, farm
\par
policy, marketing, financial management, growth,
\par
and income tax components of a farm over a plan-
\par
ning horizon of up to ten years (Richardson and
\par
Nixon, 1986). FLIPSIM identifies the optimal path
\par
of crop enterprises by systematically enumerating
\par
and evaluating the outcomes of all feasible alterna-
\par
tives. Verification and validation of FLIPSIM was
\par
fashioned after the multistage approach suggested by
\par
Naylor and Finger (1971).
\par
HERB (Wilkerson et al., 1991), WEEDSIM
\par
(Swinton and King, 1994) and WEEDCAM
\par
(Lybecker et al., 1994) are all recent BEMS which
\par
are handy decision support systems for the manage-
\par
ment of agricultural pests (mainly weeds). HERB
\par
was first in the series of these pest management
\par
models to be developed. HERB is a computer-based,
\par
interactive, menu-driven program that can generate
\par
post-emergence herbicide recommendations for prof-
\par
itable soybean production (Wilkerson et al., 1991).
\par
Using the field-specific information supplied by the
\par
user, the model provides a number of feasible weed
\par
control alternatives and their associated impacts on
\par
weeds and returns (King et al., 1993). Data obtained
\par
from North Carolina experiments were used to cali-
\par
brate HERB model. Information on the specific pro-
\par
cedures employed for model verification and valida-
\par
tion is rather scanty but satisfactory results with
\par
HERB applications have been reported (Wilkerson et
\par
al., 1991; Wiles et al., 1992). WEEDCAM is similar
\par
to HERB except it is a bioeconomic model for
\par
managing both pre- and post-emergence weeds in
\par
irrigated com \emph{(Zea mays }L.).lt is a spreadsheet-based
\par
computer software which was developed and vali-
\par
dated in Colorado. Its format assumes the knowledge
\par
of spreadsheet data management. This novel ap-
\par
proach enhances the flexibility and robustness of the
\par
model with its liberal data entry and manipulation
\par
procedures.
\par
WEEDSIM extends the frontiers of the above
\par
weed management models by recognizing a more
\par
plausible scenario of multiple weeds and multiple
\par
control strategies within a dynamic setting. It is a
\par
computer-based, dynamic, bioeconomic weed man-
\par
agement model for com and soybeans that identifies
\par
a two-year path of weed control treatments that
\par
maximize net returns per acre. The two-year horizon
\par
enables the model to track the carryover effects of
\par
current weeds and control measures in subsequent
\par
season. WEEDSIM, like the other two pest manage-
\par
ment models, identifies the weed control strategies
\par
that maximize returns by systematically enumerating
\par
and evaluating profits under all possible sequences
\par
of feasible weed control actions, rather than using
\par
dynamic programming or other optimization proce-
\par
dures. For verification, the steps described in Law
\par
and Kelton (1991) were followed while statistical
\par
tools and expert opinions were used in model valida-
\par
tion. Evidence from on-field validation trials sug-
\par
gests that WEEDSIM is a promising tool for enhanc-
\par
ing returns and/or reducing herbicide use when
\par
compared to farmers' conventional practices (Buhler
\par
et al., 1996; Porcella et al., 1996). A related model,
\par
GWM (Wiles et al., 1994), which has a better users'
\par
interface and which is considerably more flexible
\par
than WEEDSIM in terms of its data entry capabili-
\par
ties is presently being field-tested in several loca-
\par
tions.
\par
GLEAMS (Groundwater Loading Effects of Agri-
\par
cultural Management Systems) (Leonard et al., 1987)
\par
is a simulation model for evaluating the effects of
\par
different management systems on the movement of
\par
agricultural chemicals. It is a suitable tool for ad-
\par
dressing the groundwater quality concerns of chemi-
\par
cal usage. Another related model is AGNPS (Agri-
\par
cultural NonPoint Source) (Young et al., 1989) which
\par
is an event-based water quality model for analyzing
\par
nonpoint-source pollution in agricultural watersheds.
\par
Since a major constraint to the widespread use of
\par
these water quality models is their time-consuming
\par
input data demands, geographic information systems
\par
(GIS) have been successfully integrated with AG-
\par
NPS (Tim and Jolly, 1994). This development has
\par
proved to be an effective way to collect, manipulate,
\par
visualize and analyze the input and output data of
\par
AGNPS (Srinivasan and Arnold, 1994). GLEAMS
\par
and AGNPS have ben found to be useful tools for
\par
environmental planning in various locations (Bingner
\par
et al., 1992; Sugiharto et al., 1994; Foltz et al., 1995;
\par
Rode and Frede, 1997).
\par
EPIC (Erosion-Productivity Impact Calculator)
\par
(Jones et al., 1991) was developed by a multidisci-
\par
plinary team of USDA researchers at the Blacklands
\par
Research Center in Temple, Texas. EPIC is a dy-\emph{C.A. Oriade, C.R. Dillon/ Agricultural Economics 17 (1997) 45-58 }51
\par
namic model that can be used to estimate soil ero-
\par
sion and its impacts on crop production. The nine
\par
major components of EPIC are: weather, hydrology,
\par
erosion, nutrient cycling, soil temperature, tillage,
\par
crop growth, crop and soil management, and eco-
\par
nomics. EPIC has been extensively used and the
\par
model appears to give acceptable results with mini-
\par
mal calibration (Bryant et al., 1992; Rosenberg et al.,
\par
1992; Foltz et al., 1995; Hughes et al., 1995).
\par
CEEPES (the Comprehensive Environmental Eco-
\par
nomic Policy Evaluation System) (Bouzaher et al.,
\par
1995) is an integrated system of simulation models
\par
and policy appraisal tools that facilitates a systematic
\par
evaluation of trade-offs from alternative agroenvi-
