6. A survey of selected applications
\par
As with simulators, this survey does not consider
\par
all empirical work involving some simulation. In-
\par
stead, the few that were selected only serve to illus-
\par
trate the breadth of issues for which simulation
\par
methods have been deemed appropriate. The cate-
\par
gories include irrigation, crop management, pest
\par
management, livestock management and agriculture
\par
and environment.
\par
\emph{6.1. Irrigation}
\par
BPS and BEMS have been used extensively to
\par
determine the optimal allocation of irrigation water
\par
without over-exploiting the aquifers. Mapp and Bid-
\par
man (1976) employed GAFS to simulate a represent-
\par
ative farm and estimate returns under alternative
\par
duration and severity of soil moisture and atmo-
\par
spheric stresses during the critical stages of crop
\par
development. Simulated farm incomes under alterna-
\par
tive strategies facilitated the recommendation of
\par
strategies and policy initiatives that are optimal un-
\par
der various water resource situations. Bernardo et al.
\par
(1987) used a biophysical simulation model,
\par
SPAW-IRRIG, to generate yield response to irriga-
\par
tion schedules. The irrigation activities so generated
\par
were later employed in a mathematical programming
\par
model that identified irrigation strategies for con-
\par
strained profit maximization under limited water
\par
supply. Bernardo (1988) employed SORGF to evalu-
\par
ate the effects of spatial variability of irrigation
\par
applications on the efficiency of irrigation. The
\par
growth and yields of sorghum were simulated under
\par
both the assumption of uniformity and non-uniform-
\par
ity in water application. This formed the basis for
\par
comparing benefits under spatial variability to re-
\par
turns from conventional practice of uniform sprinkler
\par
irrigation.
\par
On irrigation and risk, Boggess and Amerling
\par
(1983) employed SOYGRO to explore the implica-
\par
tions of irrigation investments on risk and returns to
\par
irrigation strategy. This crop model was used to
\par
estimate yield response of crops to irrigation with
\par
seventeen years of historical weather data. Using
\par
cost estimates for alternative irrigation practices gen-
\par
erated by a computer-based irrigation cost generator
\par
program, irrigation strategies that could maximize
\par
present value of returns under various weather condi-
\par
tions were identified. Harris and Mapp (1986) em-
\par
ployed SORGF to simulate sorghum returns to
\par
stochastic soil water and other production and envi-
\par
ronmental conditions. The returns were later sub-
\par
jected to stochastic dominance comparisons in order
\par
to identify the water-conserving irrigation strategies
\par
for grain sorghum farmers that depend on the Ogal-
\par
lala aquifer in Oklahoma. Bosch and Eidman (1987)
\par
utilized a bioeconomic model to explore the value of
\par
soil and weather information for choosing optimal
\par
irrigation strategies when risk preferences of deci-
\par
sion makers are not neutral. The study simulated the
\par
value of information for an expected utility maximiz-
\par
ing decision maker whose representative farm com-
\par
prises both irrigated and non-irrigated acreage under
\par
corn, soybean and rye production in Minnesota.
\par
Although the irrigation scheduling strategy was ex-
\par
amined, the model proposed can be employed to
\par
analyze the returns to any production input under
\par
risk.
\par
\emph{6.2. Crop management}
\par
With FLIPSIM, Duffy et al. (1986) evaluated the
\par
effects of alternative farm programs and levels of
\par
price variability on the survival and growth of Texas
\par
cotton farms. Five levels of price variability and
\par
three farm policy options under 1981 Farm Bill were
\par
simulated for two representative cotton farms. Perry
\par
et al. (1986) employed the model to evaluate alterna-
\par
tive cropping patterns and share leasing arrange-\emph{C.A. Oriade, C.R. Dillon/ Agricultural Economics 17 ( 1997) 45-58 }53
\par
ments that can enhance the economic viability of
\par
tenant rice and soybean farmers in Texas. FLIPSIM
\par
was used to simulate probability distributions of
\par
returns to a typical producer. Stochastic dominance
\par
techniques were later applied to these returns in
\par
order to identify efficient strategies and establish the
\par
relationship between crop rotation practices and the
\par
nature of share-leasing arrangements. Against the
\par
backdrop of declining acreage under rice production
\par
in the Mississippi River Delta region of the United
\par
States, Salassi et al. (1987) also used FLIPSIM to
\par
determine the economic survivability of rice farms if
\par
the prevailing economic conditions persist. The study
\par
led to the identification of enterprise mix and pro-
\par
duction practices that would sustain a higher level of
\par
production.
\par
With CERES models, Johnson et al. (1991) pre-
\par
dicted crop yields under different input levels to
\par
facilitate their evaluation of on-farm effects of crop-
\par
ping strategies that could reduce nitrate groundwater
\par
pollution. These crop yields were in tum employed
\par
in optimization and linear programming models from
\par
which inferences concerning leaching-profit trade-
\par
offs of representative farms were drawn. Parsch et al.
\par
(1991) developed an amalgam of CERES-wheat and
\par
SOYGRO models which they used to assess the
\par
risk-return tradeoffs of double-cropped wheat-
\par
soybean producers such that the costs of early har-
\par
vest of winter wheat are compensated by improved
\par
returns of early soybean plantings. With other mod-
\par
els, Misra and Spurlock (1991) utilized GOSSYM to
\par
generate yield information required for the identifica-
\par
tion of efficient cotton cropping systems that balance
\par
the dual objective of maximizing whole-farm returns
\par
while reducing the risk of scarce fieldwork time. In a
\par
study that investigated the optimal crop mixes as risk
\par
preferences of farmers change in the Blackland
\par
prairies of Texas, Dillon et al. (1989) employed four
\par
crop simulation models (i.e., CORNF, SORGF,
\par
T AMW and COTTAM) to generate the needed in-
\par
put-output data. The economic decision problem
\par
was solved by applying mean-variance programming
\par
to the simulated returns.
\par
\emph{6.3. Pest management}
\par
Reichelderfer and Bender (1979) developed a
\par
simulation model that described the physical produc-
\par
tion relationships between Mexican bean beatles,
\par
control inputs and soybean yields under different
\par
conditions. The simulation model was later used to
\par
investigate the relative social and economic advan-
\par
tages of alternative methods of controlling these
\par
insect pests in soybeans. Boggess et al. (1985) devel-
\par
oped and employed a multi-species bioeconomic
\par
simulation model, the Florida Soybean Integrated
\par
Crop Management Model (SICM), that could pre-
\par
scribe optimal pest management strategies for soy-
\par
beans within a deterministic, multiple species setting.
\par
SICM, which has been incorporated into SOYGRO,
\par
was used to simulate the population dynamics
\par
(fecundity, development and mortality) for common
\par
insect pests of soybeans and under varying weather,
\par
cultural and soil conditions.
\par
Szmedra et al. (1991) used the Cotton Insect
\par
Management ( CIM) simulation model to study the
\par
impact of extending the boll weevil eradication
\par
(BWE) program to Mississippi River Delta region of
\par
the United States on producers' returns and the
\par
efficacy of such pest control actions. The model
\par
aided the determination of the effectiveness of BWE
\par
participation and non-participation under low and
\par
high levels of integrated pest management (IPM).
\par
Nyangito et al. (1996) employed a whole farm simu-
\par
lation model, the Technology Impact Evaluation Sys-
\par
tem, to assess the impact of alternative disease con-
\par
trol methods on the economic viability of dairy cattle
\par
production among small holder farmers in East
\par
Africa. Returns under various alternative immuniza-
\par
tion programs were simulated and stochastic domi-
\par
nance tools facilitated the identification of efficient
\par
strategies for resource poor, risk averse farmers in
\par
the study area.
\par
For site-specific management of weeds, Wiles et
\par
al. (1992) used HERB to investigate the value of
\par
information about weed patchiness for improving the
\par
recommendations of HERB that were based on non-
\par
patchy weed distributions. In a related study, Oriade
\par
et al. ( 1996) employed a modified version of WEED-
\par
S 1M to examine the potential economic and environ-
\par
mental benefits of site-specific weed management
\par
relative to the conventional uniform control of weeds.
\par
Simulations with this model were carried out within
\par
a dynamic but deterministic framework.
\par
For assessing the implications of pesticide policy
\par
on returns to pest control, Swinton et al. (1995) used54 \emph{C.A. Oriade, C.R. Dillon/ Agricultural Economics 17 (1997) 45-58}
\par
WEEDSIM to simulate the effect of local bans and
\par
taxes on triazine herbicide use in com \emph{(Zea mays}
\par
L.).
\par
\emph{6.4. Livestock management}
\par
Brorsen et al. (1983) developed and utilized a
\par
stocker cattle growth simulation model to estimate
\par
physical and economic results of alternative stocker
\par
production systems. The model was used to calculate
\par
energy requirements, dry matter intake, and stock
\par
gains and weights of alternative production systems
\par
in Oklahoma. Parsch and Loewer (1987) utilized a
\par
biophysical model, GRAZE, to determine whether
\par
rotational or continuous grazing results in optimal
\par
performance for steers pastured on Bermuda grass
\par
( \emph{Cynodon dactylon }L. Pers.) in western Arkansas
\par
and when faced with weather uncertainty. The pas-
\par
ture growth and animal weight gain under nine rota-
\par
tional grazing strategies were simulated with the
\par
model. Strategies were compared on the basis of net
\par
returns while variance and other measures of disper-
\par
sion were used to gauge the riskiness of each grazing
\par
strategy.
\par
\emph{6.5. Agriculture and environment}
\par
Sabbagh et al. (1992) developed and used EPIC-
\par
PST model to simulate the effects of different agri-
\par
cultural management practices on crop yields and
\par
pesticide losses by surface runoff, sediment move-
\par
ment, and leaching under irrigation in the High
\par
Plains region of Oklahoma. Kozloff et al. (1992)
\par
used AGNPS in a Minnesota watershed to simulate
\par
the relative effectiveness of alternative cropland re-
\par
tirement schemes with respect to budget outlays for
\par
annual payments to landowners, reduction in down-
\par
stream sediment yield and nutrient loss, and reduc-
\par
tion in on-site erosion. Foltz et al. (1993) combined
\par
output from EPIC and GLEAMS with a farm level
\par
linear programming model to assess the economic
\par
and environmental implications of selected eastern
\par
Com Belt farming systems. Sugiharto et al. (1994)
\par
used EPIC and AGNPS to simulate the effects of
\par
selected management practices on associated sedi-
\par
ment and phosphorus yields originating from agricul-
\par
tural land used for dairy operations. They simulated
\par
1990-1991 land management alternative practices
\par
on three soils in the Upper Bower Creek area of
\par
Wisconsin and compared the quality of effluents
\par
from fields and watersheds to current standard prac-
\par
tices. Bouzaher et al. (1995) used CEEPES to assess .
\par
the economic and environmental implications of
\par
atrazine ban in the Com Belt region of United States.
\par
Hughes et al. (1995) employed EPIC and budgeting
\par
tools to evaluate both the short-run profitability and
\par
long-run sustainability of a number of agricultural
\par
practices in the Barley cropping area of Jordan.
\par
\emph{6.6. Miscellaneous}
\par
Other applications of BPS and BEMS include the
\par
determination of optimal timing of harvests, evalua-
\par
tion of research productivity and marketing analysis.
\par
Kellogg et al. (1988) developed a bioeconomic model
\par
which they used, in conjunction with optimal control
\par
techniques, to determine the optimal time periods for
\par
opening the harvest season for Bay Scallop fishery in
\par
North Carolina. Bosch and Shabman (1990) devel-
\par
oped a bioeconomic model which they later used to
\par
study the effects of alternative types of research
\par
information on the returns to oyster production. The
\par
model results provide an insight into the nature of
\par
research priorities that is appropriate for enhancing
\par
productivity in the oyster industry. Finally, in
\par
marketing, Bailey and Richardson (1985) have used
\par
FLIPSIM to evaluate alternative marketing strategies
\par
for cotton. Daily features and cash cotton prices were
\par
simulated by the model and the impact of alternative
\par
marketing strategies to the farm's survival and eco-
\par
nomic success was determined within a whole-farm
\par
framework.
