Abstract
\par
This paper reviews the features and uses of some recent biophysical and bioeconomic simulation models in agricultural economics
\par
research. Problems associated with earlier models are discussed and the extent to which recent simulators have addressed these concerns is
\par
appraised. Some salient factors that will enhance the continued relevance of simulators as research and decision support tools are indicated.
\par
\copyright{} 1997 Elsevier Science B.V.
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Perhaps more than any other research methodol-
\par
ogy employed in agricultural economics, the growing
\par
popularity of systems approach and simulation tech-
\par
niques is rather exceptional judging from the re-
\par
ported number of applications in recent decades. The
\par
use of this technique in agricultural research, which
\par
was described as relatively novel in 1971 (Dent and
\par
Anderson, 1971), had become so widespread a few
\par
years later to the extent that two surveys of over 300
\par
literature each did not exhaust available publications
\par
in the field (Anderson, 1974; Johnson and Rausser,
\par
1977). Ever since, no serious attempt has been made
\par
to undertake such a comprehensive review as it is
\par
doubtful if the value of such an exercise would
\par
justify the amount of resources required. Instead, the
\par
recent trend is to narrow the focus of such studies to
\par
some specific simulation systems andjor agricultural
\par
practices (Musser and Tew, 1984; Braat and Van
\par
Lierop, 1987; Dillon et al., 1991).
\par
In almost all the surveys of simulation use in
\par
agriculture, certain conclusions are strikingly similar
\par
regardless of the scope of such studies. The surveys
\par
have attributed the attractiveness of simulation to the
\par
dearth of feasible alternative methodologies for tack-
\par
ling the inherent dynamic and/ or stochastic nature
\par
of agricultural problems. Also, the appeal of simula-
\par
tion has been enhanced by increasing computer capa-
\par
bility at a relatively cheaper cost. However, certain
\par
concerns have been raised pertaining to the wisdom
\par
of employing simulation tools in every study. The
\par
objective of this exercise is to review some of the
\par
recent simulators 2 and their applications to physical
