themselves into "general acceptance" groups in order to earn in-
\par
come as testifying expert witnesses-likely had a biasing effect on
\par
the content of scientific evidence supplied to litigation.
\par
The error of the Frye rule was in ignoring its own effect on the
\par
supply of scientific information to litigation. I characterize this er-
\par
ror as a "free lunch" fallacy because it embodies the assumption
\par
that the supply of scientific information to litigation is "free" in the
\par
sense that its supply (and its content) are entirely independent of the
\par
demand created by litigation and the methods used to determine its
\par
admissibility, which in effect set its "price" to the litigants.8 The
\par
assumption of a "free lunch" is unlikely to be valid because the sup-
\par
ply of scientific evidence to litigation, like the supply of all other
\par
commodities, is likely to be responsive to the opportunity cost of its
\par
production and to the nature of the demand for the commodity. If sci-
\par
entific evidence is not "free" in this sense, then Frye produced both
\par
a suppression of some supply (of "unaccepted" opinions) and a bias
\par
in supplied opinions toward what is "accepted."
\par
Unless there is some reason to believe in a high correlation be-
\par
tween "general acceptance" and some objectively verifiable stan-
\par
dard of accuracy or "truth," then the Frye rule did not promote the
\par
efficiency or neutrality of fact-finding in litigation. The correlation
\par
must be high, not just positive, because admissibility determina-
\par
tions under Frye influenced future cases as well as the cases in
\par
