\par
lunch" expression date back to the nineteenth century, but "[a]ll efforts to identify
\par
the true originator proved unavailing." Robert Hessen, "Free Lunch," in John Ea-
\par
twell, Murray Milgate, and Peter Newman, eds, 2 The New Palgrave: A Dictionary
\par
of Economics 420 (Macmillan, 1987) ("Eatwell, Milgate \& Newman, The New Pal-
\par
grave"). As used here, the expression is intended to draw attention to the fact that
\par
scientific information, like all other products of human action, is costly. See Part IV
\par
below.4 Daubert's Debut
\par
which they were made. The correlation between "general accep-
\par
tance" and "truth," however, does not appear to be very close. In
\par
addition, the incentives that Frye created for the production of
\par
"general acceptance" also appear to be unrelated to "truth" or even
\par
to neutrality. Instead, those incentives encouraged adherence to a
\par
consistent opinion within relatively small "general acceptance" in-
\par
terest groups of testifying experts, whose income from testifying
\par
would have increased if they followed such a strategy.
\par
Seen in this light, Daubert is a good decision because it at least
\par
partially recognizes that general acceptance cannot provide a "free
\par
lunch." Rather than allowing relatively small "general acceptance"
\par
groups to dictate admissibility, Daubert requires only that proffered
\par
expert testimony be "science" in some generic and vaguely defined
\par
sense. This is an improvement over Frye in two ways.9 First, if
\par
"truth" in fact is more positively correlated with diversity than
\par
with commonality of opinion, then Daubert may improve results
\par
simply by expanding the range of potentially admissible opinion
\par
(whether it raises or lowers the average cost of adducing expert
\par
opinion). Second, to the extent that Daubert dilutes the effect of
\par
"general acceptance" on admissibility, it also reduces the incentives
\par
for rent-seeking behavior by external interest groups, and thereby
\par
reduces the amount of systematic bias on production incentives
\par
that is introduced by the evidentiary rules themselves."'
\par
With the more internalized threshold standard of admissibility
\par
supplied by Daubert, incentives to invest in scientific evidence are
\par
more closely aligned with the stakes that the parties to litigation
\par
have in the particular lawsuit at issue. These stakes, in turn, are
\par
driven primarily by the rules of substantive law or, if by the pro-
\par
cedural system, by features other than the law of evidence. One
\par
might still decry the use of "junk science" in litigation. But that is
\par
not a problem that rules of evidentiary admissibility are likely to
\par
address effectively.
\par
9 Arguably, however, Daubert does not go far enough because it still adheres to
\par
an external standard of "scientific method," and for this reason may be assuming a
\par
"free snack."
\par
10 Part of the analysis here is similar to, and draws upon, recent work by Bruce
\par
Kobayashi and Luke Froeb. See Luke M. Froeb and Bruce H. Kobayashi, Competition
\par
in the Production of Costly Information: An Economic Analysis of Adversarial ver-
\par
sus Court-Appointed Presentation of Expert Testimony (GMU School of Law Work-
\par
ing Paper, 1994) ("Froeb and Kobayashi, Competition"). Their paper shows that ad-
\par
versarial presentation of expert testimony to a "naive" decisionmaker is not
\par
dominated by the alternative of "perfect" court-appointed experts, and that Frye,
\par
even if modeled favorably as simply narrowing the range of permissible testimony,
\par
is at best neutral. This article suggests that, once public-choice considerations are
\par
brought in, both Frye and court-appointed experts appear to be worse than unres-
\par
tricted adversarial presentation. See Part IV.B below.Jeffrey S. Parker 5
\par
Daubert is a step in the right direction because it is more con-
\par
sistent with the structure of adversarial litigation than any attempt
\par
to "regulate" the content of scientific evidence through centralized
\par
control, which is fraught with the potential for manipulation by
\par
interest groups. The alternatives to the Daubert standard all suffer
\par
from one or another form of the "free lunch" fallacy. The Frye stan-
\par
dard of "general acceptance" and its variants embody the fallacy in
\par
their assumption of an immutable external standard, whereas in
\par
fact any such standard is vulnerable to interest group manipulation.
\par
Similarly, replacing the litigants' incentives with governmental or-
\par
gans-as through court-appointed experts or publicly chosen "sci-
\par
entific panels"-creates the same problems in a more familiar form
\par
and to a more severe degree. None of these variations of admissi-
\par
bility doctrine seems likely to produce better results, in terms ei-
\par
ther of disciplining the content of scientific evidence or of mini-
\par
mizing the costs of litigation, than an internalized standard such as
\par
Daubert's, applied by presumably uninformed trial court judges in
\par
a systematically unbiased manner. The problems that remain are
\par
not soluble by evidence law doctrine because they are problems of
\par
deviations from the standard case of adversarial litigation with sym-
\par
metrical stakes that are internalized by the immediate parties.
\par
Daubert will produce a pattern of disparate outcomes on similar
\par
facts, which may not make a pretty picture for those who equate
\par
adjudication with a search for factual consistency. But the adver-
\par
sarial system of adjudication has never produced a neat and tidy
\par
picture, and no system of adjudication can pretend to find immu-
\par
table "truth." As the Daubert decision itself notes, even the sci-
\par
entists do not make that claim for their own disciplines," and ad-
\par
judication operates under a far more severe set of constraints. Let
\par
the philosophers seek for "truth." And let the law courts get about
\par
the practical business of resolving litigated disputes between the
\par
immediate parties to those disputes, on the narrowest possible
\par
grounds and with a minimum of external effects.
\par
This argument is developed through the remaining five parts of
\par
this article. Part II sets the context by locating the admissibility of
\par
scientific evidence within evidence law doctrine and reviewing the
\par
competing tests of admissibility involved in the pre-Daubert de-
\par
bate. Part III describes the Supreme Court's decision in Daubert.
\par
Part IV develops the economic analysis of scientific evidence as a
\par
form of costly information, showing why the Daubert standard is
\par
preferable to alternative tests of admissibility.
\par
Part V briefly examines commentators' responses to Daubert,
\par
and the post-Daubert experience in the federal and state courts,
\par
11 See Daubert, 113 S Ct at 2795.6 Daubert's Debut
\par
including the federal court of appeals' decision in the Daubert case
\par
itself on remand from the Supreme Court. Although preliminary
\par
indications are ambiguous, there already appears to be some disa-
\par
greement on the meaning and application of Daubert, which may
\par
produce inconsistencies in future admissibility outcomes. If that
\par
pattern develops, it will be a positive sign for the efficacy of the
\par
Daubert standard, as it will suggest that the standard is having pre-
\par
cisely the unbiased effect that is desirable. Part VI concludes with
\par
a discussion of this effect, and of its broader implications for the
\par
adversarial system of adjudication.
\par
The Supreme Court's future challenge will be to resist the temp-
\par
tation to tamper with a sensible decision for an imperfect world.
\par
As Daubert makes its way through the lower federal courts, there
\par
is likely to be a dispersion of outcomes on the admissibility of given
\par
types of scientific testimony in different cases. So long as the dis-
\par
parities in outcome appear to be relatively random and unbiased,
\par
there is no need to intervene. This conclusion will not be intui-
\par
tively obvious to all observers, many of whom will undoubtedly see
\par
the dispersion of outcomes as a sure sign of some underlying pa-
\par
thology. Forgetting, or not realizing, that society has little interest
\par
in the factual findings in particular cases, critics may denounce the
\par
system's "inconsistency" and call for further "reform." But for the
\par
Supreme Court-and for the judiciary's rulemaking committees and
\par
the Congress-the better part of wisdom will be to leave well
\par
enough alone.
