\par
B. The Pre-Daubert Debate
\par
The special problems of expert testimony arise from a seeming de-
\par
ficiency in the competence of the trial judge to determine whether
\par
proposed expert opinion testimony offers the fact-finder any advan-
\par
tage in drawing conclusions. The trial judge might reasonably be
\par
expected to determine whether a lay opinion would be "helpful" to
\par
the fact-finder, because the judge, as another "lay" person, presum-
\par
ably is in a position to appreciate the inferential process involved
\par
in forming that opinion. But how can the judge make the similar
\par
determination that expert opinion will "assist" the fact-finder,
\par
when the expert opinion rests on an inferential process that, by
\par
definition, is beyond the understanding of lay persons, including the
\par
judge? This is the question that has haunted the debates over the
\par
admissibility of scientific evidence, from Frye through Daubert, and
\par
the one that has tempted some courts and commentators to the
\par
vain hope that there is a free lunch.
\par
The logic of the situation suggests two different points of view,
\par
which in fact did emerge as opposing schools of thought in the pre-
\par
Daubert debate. One point of view is "internal" to the litigation
\par
process, while the other is "external." The "internal" point of view
\par
manifests a confidence in the ability of the trial judge, with the
\par
assistance of adversarial presentation, to distinguish between useful
\par
and misleading (or pointless) expert testimony. In contrast, the "ex-
\par
ternal" point of view reflects the belief that the adversarial process
\par
is easily fooled, confused, or misled by proffered scientific evidence,
\par
making it necessary to judge the admissibility of such evidence by
\par
a standard that is external to the litigation process.
\par
The "internal" perspective is associated with what came to be
\par
called the "relevancy" or, after its principal exponent, the "McCor-
\par
