\par
mick" test of admissibility.38 Under this view, there is no special
\par
prerequisite to the admissibility of scientific evidence, which there-
\par
fore is subject only to ordinary admissibility requirements, primar-
\par
ily relevance. According to McCormick, this was the "traditional"
\par
approach to scientific evidence, followed prior to the twentieth cen-
\par
tury, and the "notion of a special rule for scientific evidence origi-
\par
