\par
The "external" perspective is represented by the Frye (or, more
\par
descriptively, the "general acceptance") test for the admissibility of
\par
scientific evidence. Under this test, scientific evidence is inadmis-
\par
sible unless it is shown to be based on reasoning that is "sufficiently
\par
established to have gained general acceptance in the particular field
\par
in which it belongs."40 In essence, the rationale for this test is that
\par
neither courts nor juries-nor any litigation processes-are capable
\par
of distinguishing between "good" science and "bad" science by
\par
themselves, so that the scientific community must be given the
\par
