\par
The emergence of the Frye test opened up a division of authority
\par
in the case law, and a spirited debate in the secondary literature,
\par
all of which was intensified by the 1975 enactment of the Federal
\par
Rules of Evidence.42 Because the Federal Rules did not mention
\par
Frye, it was uncertain whether the Rules displaced or incorporated
\par
the Frye test or its variants. That question created a conflict among
\par
the federal courts of appeals, which finally prompted the Supreme
\par
Court to grant certiorari in Daubert.43
