Why was there ever any need for an "external" standard of any
\par
kind? The argument for an external standard was apparently based
\par
on the view that fact-finders are easily misled by expert opinion.93
\par
But what has never been clear in this critique is why the adversary
\par
system is inadequate to cope with that danger.
\par
Property Rights in the Adversarial System. The fear that fact-find-
\par
ers are easily misled by expert opinion seems to overlook the role
\par
of the adverse party in the adjudicative system. The adversary may
\par
counter expert testimony through a variety of means-cross-ex-
\par
amination and impeachment of the expert, argument, rebuttal ev-
\par
idence that undermines the factual basis for the expert's assump-
\par
tions, and, most obviously, by hiring its own expert to give rebuttal
\par
expert testimony. Aside from Frye-like constraints on admissible
\par
opinion, the law of evidence generally places very few limitations
\par
on the rebuttal mechanisms available to the adversary. Here as else-
\par
where, the law of evidence is more enabling than prohibitive in
\par
nature: so long as the minimal standard of relevance is satisfied,
\par
and the evidence is cast in acceptable form, the adversary's options
\par
are very broad. Moreover, the basic norm observed in trials is to
\par
afford the adversary at least as much of the fact-finder's time and
\par
attention as the proponent of expert testimony is afforded.
\par
In its traditional and still predominant form, the adversarial sys-
\par
tem also provides a symmetry of incentives to invest in presenta-
\par
tion, through a symmetry in the stakes of the litigation, secured by
\par
a system of private "property rights" in litigation.94 The paradigm
\par
of litigation is a private civil action for damages, in which the stake
\par
is simply a transfer payment from the defendant to the plaintiff,
\par
contingent upon the outcome of the litigation. Because the stakes
\par
are the same from each side's point of view, both sides-at least
\par
under the American rule that litigants bear their own costs-have
\par
equivalent private incentives to invest in their presentations of ev-
\par
idence and argument. If the plaintiff wins, then the plaintiff has a
\par
private property right to collect the judgment from the defendant,
\par
and only the defendant. If the defendant prevails, then the defen-
\par
dant has a private property right to protection from further litiga-
\par
tion of the same claim by the same plaintiff, and only by the same
\par
93 See, for example, United States v Baller, 519 F 2d 463, 466 (4th Cir 1975)
\par
