B. Public Choice and Public Agency Cost
\par
By imposing an external standard of acceptability as the threshold
\par
to the admissibility of scientific evidence, Frye and similar concepts
\par
create a scenario familiar in public-choice economics, which is con-
\par
cerned with the influence of private interests on governmental ac-
\par
tion, classically legislation and more recently administrative ac-
\par
tions such as licensing.98 In essence, Frye created an opportunity
\par
for rent-seeking behavior by non-party interest groups in litigation
\par
that is analogous to the opportunities presented by legislation or li-
\par
censing.99 Moreover, several of the suggested alternatives to Frye and
\par
Daubert-such as court-appointed experts or a government-spon-
\par
sored expert "science court"-would make that same problem
\par
worse, by adding the self-interest of government officials to the pri-
\par
vate interests involved. All of these devices share the common
\par
theme of granting external or public agents more control over the lit-
\par
igants' factual presentation, thereby diluting the system of private
\par
property rights in litigative fact-finding. Given the minimal social
\par
interest in the particular factual outcome of the litigants' case, this
\par
is likely to have a detrimental effect from a social perspective.
\par
Traditionally, adjudication has been the most insulated of gov-
\par
ernmental actions from the influences of private interest groups.
\par
The mechanisms for insulating adjudication have included une-
\par
lected judges, limits on adjudicative jurisdiction, and the system of
\par
private property rights in litigation discussed above.'00 Ideally, the
