C. Stakes, Symmetry, and External Effects
\par
So far, the analysis has focused on the basic model of private liti-
\par
gation involving symmetrical stakes and no external effects of the
\par
fact-finding outcome. What about variations from that model?
\par
The important variations are two: (1) litigation to which the gov-
\par
ernment is a party; and (2) external preclusive effects through non-
\par
mutual collateral estoppel. Both cases involve asymmetrical stakes.
\par
In the government case, this is caused by the detachment of gov-
\par
ernmental agents' incentives from the stakes of a particular case.
\par
In the preclusion case, it is caused by the "capitalization" effect of
\par
the abandonment in many jurisdictions of the traditional rule of
\par
"mutuality" of collateral estoppel, which was one of the legal
\par
mechanisms assuring the internalization of litigation fact-finding
\par
results."13
