\par
D. The Domain of Admissibility Doctrine
\par
Asymmetries of stakes created by external effects are not the only
\par
cause of "junk science" in the courtroom, nor the most important
\par
one. Far more important are the magnitudes and variance in the
\par
stakes of litigation, which are functions of the substantive law of
\par
liability and damages. The ever expanding scope of legal liability,
\par
together with the ever increasing likelihood of large damage awards,
\par
punitive and otherwise, are the principal reasons for the growth in
\par
the use and importance of expert testimony in litigation. In simple
\par
economic terms, there is a strong demand for expert testimony
\par
(along with all other forms of litigation expenditure) derived from
\par
the increasing returns to litigation as a means of wealth transfer.
\par
As the explicit pricing of "fact" evidence is suppressed by the for-
\par
mal system of unremunerative compulsion, the explicitly priced
\par
supply of expert and scientific evidence makes it an easy target of
\par
criticism.
\par
The returns to litigation activity are raised still higher by certain
\par
features of the modern procedural system, such as liberal pleading
\par
rules and broad discovery and, most recently, "disclosure" obliga-
\par
tions. The emergence of "mass tort" and other forms of multiple
\par
litigation, together with non-mutual collateral estoppel, raises the
\par
stakes in litigation further, and provides incentives for interest
\par
groups to organize for coordination across multiple cases. With or
\par
without facilitative legal rules, interest groups have formed to lit-
\par
igate "public interest" cases that seek to establish broad principles
\par
or policies rather than overtly to secure a transfer payment from a
\par
particular defendant to a particular plaintiff. And, of course, with
\par
the growth of statute and regulatory law, government at all levels
\par
