\par
But no standard of evidentiary admissibility could stem that tide.
\par
More fundamentally, no test of evidentiary admissibility that
\par
looks to an "external" guidepost seems likely to improve the per-
\par
formance of litigation fact-finding because all such standards will
\par
partake to some extent in the "free lunch" fallacy. It is a tempting
\par
fantasy to believe that somewhere out there is the polestar that can
\par
guide the imperfect course of adjudication at law. Tempting as it is,
\par
it is a fantasy nonetheless. Questing for the Polaris of "true" sci-
\par
entific evidence-assuming that it exists at all-cannot be done
\par
with the naked eye. It requires a telescope. Someone, who wants
\par
to get paid, has to make the telescope. Not all telescopes are equal,
\par
and the "consensus" telescope may very well be distorted. In terms
\par
of the fact-finding needs of a particular case, it is likely to be dis-
\par
torted by the broader interests of the consensus-makers.
\par
Daubert does not entirely eliminate this problem. On one level,
\par
it seems to replace the "consensus" telescope with a scenario of
\par
