As was discussed in Part II above, the entire problem of admissi-
\par
bility of scientific evidence is simply a matter of finding an accept-
\par
able analog in expert testimony to the foundational requirement of
\par
"first-hand knowledge" in ordinary testimony. Daubert makes that
\par
analogy explicit, and therefore provides a basis for extending the
\par
underlying logic of foundational requirements to scientific evi-
\par
dence. For ordinary testimony, the requirement of first-hand knowl-
\par
edge is a minimal standard designed to ensure that the witness has
\par
an observational advantage over the fact-finders. It does not require
\par
or imply that the witness is the definitive source, or that the wit-
\par
ness is correct in his or her perceptions.
\par
There does not appear to be any reason why a similar approach
\par
should not guide the application of the Daubert standard. As with
\par
opinion testimony by ordinary witnesses, if the expert's testimony
\par
