\par
in the specialized "scientific" knowledge of the expert's field) and,
\par
as the Court stated in Daubert, "pertinent" to the factual issues of
\par
the case, then the testimony presumptively is admissible, subject
\par
to the ordinary counterweights.
\par
Asking more of the standard of admissibility is asking too much
\par
of evidence law. If problems remain with "junk science" in the
\par
courtroom, they are far more powerfully related to the incentives
\par
of substantive law and other features of the procedural system, and
\par
