VI. CONCLUSION
\par
Assessing the ultimate effect of Daubert on the admission of sci-
\par
entific evidence is likely to require several more years of judicial
\par
experience followed by rigorous empirical study. Certainly, the
\par
amount of attention lavished on Daubert to date by the commen-
\par
tators, and the emerging disagreements among the federal and state
\par
courts on its meaning, suggest that its doctrine will matter. But at
\par
this point, there is little agreement on how it will matter. Respect-
\par
able opinion predicts both less and more stringent attitudes towards
\par
the admissibility of scientific evidence, and some predict little or
\par
no effect on outcomes at all.
\par
This article has argued that the disagreements over Daubert's
\par
meaning may themselves be indicators of yet another effect of
\par
Daubert that ultimately may be more profound. Whether Daubert
\par
increases or decreases the cost of presenting scientific evidence gen-
\par
erally, it is likely to reduce the influence of external interest groups
\par
on the content and costs of scientific evidence proffered under its
\par
standard. If that prediction is correct, the results certainly will do
\par
no harm to science, and will be favorable for the adjudicatory sys-
\par
tem, as it will remove or at least reduce one source of external bias
\par
on the supply of scientific evidence to litigation.
\par
In a broader view, Daubert focuses our attention on the virtues
\par
and vices of the adversarial system as it operates on fact-finding at
\par
trial. Whatever its vices, adversarial presentation of evidence has
\par
the signal virtue of internalizing the costs and benefits of fact-find-
\par
ing to the immediate parties, much as substantive property rights
\par
do in the contracting process. While litigation, given its coercive
\par
aspect, is not entirely analogous to contracting, it comes closest in
\par
the context of fact-finding in a particular case, where the social
\par
interest in the particular outcome approaches zero. Much of the
\par
criticism of the adversarial system in recent years seems to ignore
\par
this point. The Supreme Court's decision in Daubert reminds us
\par
133 If the absence of measurable effects at present is mainly the result of the fact
