ABSTRACT
\par
We review recent work in the integrated assessment modeling of global climate
\par
change. This field has grown rapidly since 1990. Integrated assessment mod-
\par
els seek to combine knowledge from multiple disciplines in formal integrated
\par
representations; inform policy-making, structure knowledge, and prioritize key
\par
uncertainties; and advance knowledge of broad system linkages and feedbacks,
\par
particularly between socioeconomic and biophysical processes. They may com-
\par
bine simplified representations of the socioeconomic determinants of greenhouse
\par
gas emissions, the atmosphere and oceans, impacts on human activities and
\par
ecosystems, and potential policies and responses. We summarize current projects,
\par
grouping them according to whether they emphasize the dynamics of emissions
\par
control and optimal policy-making, uncertainty, or spatial detail. We review the
\par
few significant insights that have been claimed from work to date and identify
\par
important challenges for integrated assessment modeling in its relationships to
\par
disciplinary knowledge and to broader assessment seeking to inform policy- and
\par
decision-making.
