1. INTEGRATED ASSESSMENT MODELS OF GLOBAL
\par
CLIMATE CHANGE: CONTEXT, SCOPE, AND
\par
BACKGROUND
\par
We review recent work in integrated assessment (IA) modeling of global climate
\par
change. To bound the discussion and place it in context, we first briefly discuss
\par
assessment, then integrated assessment, then alternative methods of conduct-
\par
ing integrated assessment including formal modeling, the focus of this review.
\par
Assessment consists of social processes that bridge the domains of knowledge
\par
and decision-making, assembling and synthesizing expert scientific or technical
\par
knowledge to advise policy or decision-making (1, 2). Assessment processes
\par
might seek to mine current knowledge to answer specific policy-relevant ques-
\par
tions, to identify policy-relevant implications, or to help policy-makers under-
\par
stand what questions they should be asking. Assessment has been practiced
\par
for three decades in particular policy domains under such titles as environmen-
\par
tal impact assessment, risk assessment, and technology assessment (3, 4), and
\par
for longer under such less systematic processes as National Research Council
\par
committees or other advisory bodies.
\par
Assessment is integrated according to the breadth of the expert knowledge
\par
it synthesizes in advising the issue at hand (1, 2, 5, 6). It is plausible that most
\par
useful assessment is integrated to some degree, since few real policy issues
\par
or decisions can be usefully advised by drawing only on the knowledge of a
\par
single research community. Practitioners and critics of integrated assessment
\par
differ over the precise conditions that make an assessment integrated, but we
\par
contend that this debate mistakenly treats integration as a binary characteristic
\par
and mistakes the means for the end. Assessments can be more or less inte-
\par
grated. And assessment activities should be judged by their contribution to
\par
advising policy issues or decisions and perhaps to the advancement of knowl-
\par
edge. Being more or less integrated is a means toward these ends. Below weINTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 591
\par
discuss some specific issues of integration that arise in integrated assessment
\par
(IA) models.
\par
Two methods of assessment have dominated practice and research for more
\par
than 20 years: deliberation by interdisciplinary expert panels and formal mod-
\par
eling. Deliberation of interdisciplinary panels has been the main method of de-
\par
livering official assessment to policy-making bodies through this period, and it
\par
remains predominant in such bodies as the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate
\par
Change (IPCC) and the Assessment Panels of the Montreal Protocol. Formal
\par
integrated modeling as an assessment method has been a primary interest of
\par
independent researchers and analysts over a similar period and has experienced
\par
a surge of activity since approximately 1990 for global climate change. Other
\par
approaches that have recently been advocated combine and extend the distinct
\par
strengths of panels and models (7, 8).
\par
A landmark early integrated assessment of a global environmental issue was
\par
the Climatic Impacts Assessment Program (CIAP), which assessed impacts
\par
of stratospheric supersonic flight (9). CIAP comprised six parallel interdisci-
\par
plinary expert teams, which each examined one link of a causal chain stretching
\par
from scenarios of supersonic flight and jet engine design, through atmospheric
\par
chemistry and radiation, to biological, economic, and social impacts. While its
\par
immediate contribution to policy was limited, CIAP made a major contribution
\par
to developing an interdisciplinary atmospheric research community (10). Al-
\par
though several other climate assessments conducted through the late 1970s and
\par
early 1980s used interdisciplinary expert panels (11--13), no assessment since
\par
CIAP has attempted such ambitious integration of any global environmental
\par
issue without using a formal integrated model.
\par
While panels are a \emph{deliberative }approach to assessment, integrated modeling
\par
is a \emph{representational }approach: It seeks to advance understanding by con-
\par
structing a formal representation of the issue to be studied. Current integrated
\par
assessment models of global climate descend from two streams of work. The
\par
representational components that they integrate draw, often in highly simplified
\par
form, on models of energy systems and economies, of atmospheric chemistry
\par
and climate, and of ecosystems developed in various disciplines, whereas their
\par
bold pursuit of integrated representation descends from the global models of
\par
the 1970s (15--18).
\par
The most prominent heritage of many current IA climate models lies in the
\par
energy-economic models of the 1970s (19). From these, an early step toward
\par
climate integration was the addition of fuel-specific CO2 emissions coefficients,
\par
which allowed the emissions paths associated with particular energy scenarios
\par
(20--22) or the cost of meeting emission constraints (23) to be calculated. But
\par
the first assessment model of a global environmental issue that pursued inte-
\par
gration from sources to impacts did not examine climate but rather the more592 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
analytically tractable issue of acid rain. The RAINS model of acidification in
\par
Europe integrates national emissions, a previously developed model of atmo-
\par
spheric transport and chemistry, and maximum permissible deposition levels,
\par
called critical loads (24). RAINS can calculate deposition on a 50-km grid
\par
resulting from specified growth scenarios and control measures or calculate
\par
cost-minimizing controls to meet specified deposition limits. The modelers
\par
sought close relations with European policy-makers, and RAINS was used ex-
\par
tensively in negotiations for a 1994 agreement to limit sulfur emissions. Similar
\par
integrated modeling projects elsewhere appear to have been less used in policy-
\par
making (25--28).
\par
Beginning in the late 1980s, several modeling projects began to seek broader
\par
integration in climate assessments, first by adding non-CO2 greenhouse gases
\par
and simple atmospheric representations. Mintzer projected emissions and con-
\par
centrations of multiple greenhouse gases, combining a widely used energy
\par
model with exogenous scenarios, and added simple radiative-forcing equations
\par
to project equilibrium, or ``committed'' global temperature change (20, 29). The
\par
Atmospheric Stabilization Framework (ASF) refined the projection of emis-
\par
sions, particularly from land-use and agriculture, and added a simple calculation
\par
of realized global temperature change (30). The IMAGE 1 model (Integrated
\par
Model for the Assessment of the Greenhouse Effect) added a simple calcula-
\par
tion of sea-level rise, its impact on the Netherlands, and optimal coastal defense
\par
(31, 32). ASF and IMAGE 1 were used to generate the IPCC's 1990 scenarios
\par
of greenhouse gas emissions and concentrations (33).
\par
In this article, we review the rapid surge of IA modeling activity for cli-
\par
mate change that has taken place since 1990, increasing the number of projects
\par
from 3 to approximately 40 (2, 34). This surge of activity has reflected the
\par
combined influence of several factors, including the sudden political promi-
\par
nence of climate change; widespread belief that understanding and managing
\par
it requires integrating knowledge from a broad set of biophysical and socio-
\par
economic domains; advances in computing power and in sectoral modeling
\par
of energy systems, the atmosphere, and ecosystems; the increasing availabil-
\par
ity of consistent global data sets; and the activity of research entrepreneurs
\par
building interdisciplinary projects, responding to general interest in the field
\par
and the availability of funding. The past two years show increasing signs of
\par
maturation of the field---the first review articles and conferences, and pub-
\par
lished contributions summarizing cumulative results, critiquing current prac-
\par
tice, and calling for development of a more connected professional community
\par
(2, 35--38).
\par
Section 2 summarizes the purposes that have been claimed for IA models
\par
and the meaning of the two primary dimensions of integration in IA models,
\par
``vertical'' and ``horizontal.'' Section 3 follows the structure of IA models defined
\par
by vertical integration, following a causal chain from emissions to impacts, andINTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 593
\par
summarizes the range of approaches that IA models have taken to represent
\par
each component. Section 4 summarizes major IA modeling projects, sorting
\par
them according to differences in emphasis and apparent purpose into three
\par
categories---models that stress dynamics and optimization, uncertainty, and
\par
spatial detail, respectively. Section 5 summarizes and evaluates the significant
\par
results and insights claimed for IA models so far. Section 6 offers concluding
\par
observations on the current state, and contributions to date, of IA modeling of
\par
global climate change.
\par
2. PURPOSES OF INTEGRATED ASSESSMENT
\par
MODELING AND MODES OF INTEGRATION
\par
Four broad contributions have been claimed for integrated assessment (IA) mod-
\par
eling: evaluating potential responses to climate change; structuring knowledge
\par
and characterizing uncertainty; contributing to broad comparative risk assess-
\par
ment; and contributing to scientific research. In principle, IA models can make
\par
any of these contributions; in practice, work to date has served these purposes
\par
to different degrees, and basic elements of structure and design of any particular
\par
IA model strongly influence which of these purposes it can serve and how well.
\par
We elaborate on each of these four contributions below.
\par
First, IA models can help evaluate potential responses to climate change
\par
in several ways: by characterizing potential climate impacts; by describing
\par
the cost, effect, and other consequences of specific responses or of alternative
\par
means of attaining a specific environmental goal; or by balancing costs and
\par
benefits of particular responses. Any of these contributions require that IA
\par
models be able to compare future projections of relevant aspects of the climate
\par
issue, under alternative assumptions and alternative specified responses. While
\par
responsible IA modeling will make clear that these projections are appropriately
\par
qualified, contingent on a host of assumptions, and responsibly reflect currently
\par
understood uncertainties, this use of IA modeling is a predictive endeavor and
\par
subject to all the usual risks of predicting the future.
\par
Second, by their very nature as tools to integrate knowledge coherently from
\par
multiple relevant domains, IA models can provide a framework for structuring
\par
present knowledge about climate change. Having such a framework can serve
\par
several purposes. It can assist in keeping the whole problem in view, hence sup-
\par
porting systematic search for responses and resistance to premature closure on a
\par
few options. It can help organize and interpret advances in relevant knowledge
\par
as they occur. Perhaps most importantly, such a framework can help identify
\par
and characterize the magnitude of key uncertainties and their importance for
\par
key impacts and decisions.
\par
Third, by constructing broad characterizations of future development of
\par
the climate issue, IA models can help put climate in the context of other594 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
environmental stresses and other social and economic changes that might occur
\par
over the same time, to give insight into the broadest of policy questions---How
\par
important is climate change?
\par
Finally, IA models can be research tools independent of any direct assess-
\par
ment function, serving to advance basic knowledge and understanding. In part
\par
this purpose is closely related to the use of IA models in identifying key un-
\par
certainties, which can help research communities identify priority directions
\par
for investigation. But in addition, IA models may seek to represent linked sys-
\par
tem behavior across domains, including biosphere-atmosphere feedbacks and
\par
linkages between biophysical and socioeconomic aspects of the system. To the
\par
extent that such broad integration captures quantitatively important linkages
\par
and feedbacks, IA modeling can contribute to basic understanding of system
\par
behavior.
\par
Assessment can be integrated on many dimensions, including such political
\par
and managerial dimensions as participation, process, and oversight, which may
\par
be important in explaining how effectively assessment contributes to policy or
\par
decision. Questions of integration in IA modeling are more narrow and substan-
\par
tive, concerned with what knowledge domains are included and what questions
\par
are posed. These substantive aspects of integration have conventionally been
\par
grouped into two dimensions, vertical and horizontal integration.
\par
Vertical integration is integration along the primary causal chain that links
\par
the causes and consequences of climate change: emissions and their sources;
\par
atmospheric and biogeochemical processes; physical and ecological impacts;
\par
social and economic impacts; and response options at various points in this
\par
chain. An IA model that seeks to provide benefit-cost evaluation of responses,
\par
weighing the impacts of climate change against measures to reduce it or adapt
\par
to it, must include at least simple representations of all major components of
\par
this chain.
\par
Two kinds of integration have been described as horizontal. The term has
\par
been used to refer to expanding each link of the causal chain laid out by ver-
\par
tical integration, i.e. integrating more source activities and emissions, more
\par
atmospheric and biotic processes, or more forms of impact or response. But
\par
increasingly, horizontal integration describes an IA model's representation of
\par
linkages between climate change and other issues, including both other at-
\par
mospheric issues such as ozone depletion, acidification, or photochemical air
\par
pollution, and linked social issues such as health and development. While these
\par
issues have causal links of various strengths, they are conventionally identified
\par
and managed separately. The IA models that are making preliminary attempts
\par
at such horizontal integration are consequently not strictly IA models of climate
\par
change. Rather, they are moving some distance ahead of current structures of
\par
decision-making to sketch an integrated conception of sustainable development
\par
(39--41).INTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 595
