3. BUILDING BLOCKS: COMPONENTS
\par
OF VERTICALLY INTEGRATED
\par
CLIMATE-ASSESSMENT MODELS
\par
This section summarizes the component domains of IA models, while the next
\par
section summarizes complete IA modeling projects. Here, we follow the struc-
\par
ture of vertical integration to summarize the approaches IA models have taken to
\par
representing particular components of the causal chain. A vertically integrated
\par
IA model combines representations of some or all of the following: emissions
\par
and their socioeconomic determinants, the atmosphere-ocean-climate system,
\par
ecosystems, socioeconomic impacts, and policy and responses. IA models have
\par
differed in the prominence and detail they give to particular components, reflect-
\par
ing the project's disciplinary heritage, the interests and skills of team members,
\par
and the intended purpose. Many attempts at vertical integration in IA model-
\par
ing, including all early attempts, treated these components as links in a single
\par
straight causal chain, as shown in Figure 1. More recent work in IA modeling
\par
has begun to enrich this structure with more complex linkages and feedbacks,
\par
such as direct human modification of ecosystems and CO2 fertilization, yielding
\par
more complex structures as illustrated in Figure 2.
\par
3.1 \emph{Representation of Emissions and Their Socioeconomic}
\par
\emph{Determinants}
\par
Climate change will depend on the future path of world emissions, which any in-
\par
tegrated assessment study must project. IA models have employed three related
\par
methods, alone or in combination, to project emissions: externally specified
\par
emission scenarios, detailed bottom-up representation of technologies, and ag-
\par
gregate economic modeling. All these methods involve speculative projections
\par
about future events but differ in the detail and explicitness of different compo-
\par
nents of the projections. The biggest challenges for any of these methods are
\par
integrating multiple source gases and activities and credibly projecting the two
\par
most basic drivers of emissions trends---population growth and technological
\par
change.
\par
Scenarios are exogenously specified time paths of key variables, which are
\par
used as inputs to drive other parts of an assessment. Scenarios may provide time
\par
paths of emissions directly or of population, economic, and technological trends
\par
from which emissions paths are calculated. Scenarios may be estimated through
\par
prior analysis or modeling or through disciplined, consistent guesswork. Sce-
\par
narios provide input values for parts of a model that are not represented directly,
\par
e.g. for emissions in a model that focuses primarily on atmospheric dynamics
\par
and impacts. Scenarios drawn from an authoritative source such as the IPCC can
\par
facilitate standardization and controlled comparison between models, relieving
\par
each of the need to select and defend its own inputs.596 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
