\par
The second approach is detailed bottom-up specification of the technologies
\par
that generate emissions. The mix of technologies in each region at each time is
\par
described by its costs, inputs, and outputs, including emissions. This approach
\par
allows precise specification of known or projected innovations, e.g. that an
\par
advanced gas turbine with specified cost and efficiency will be available in
\par
2005. But the approach can require specifying time paths of huge numbers of
\par
technical coefficients, risking arbitrariness and spurious precision.
\par
The third approach is aggregate economic modeling with embedded emission
\par
coefficients, projecting future emissions as the outcome of specified produc-
\par
tion relationships, preferences, and economic growth. Such models can project
\par
unconstrained emissions growth paths, estimate the cost of specified emis-
\par
sions constraints, or assess the stringency of taxes or other policies needed to
\par
meet a specified emissions limit. The models can range from those of simple
\par
aggregate economies with embedded abatement-cost functions (45), to macro-
\par
models coupled to a detailed energy sector (20, 46), to full dynamic general
\par
equilibrium models (47, 48). Basic design choices include the kind of op-
\par
timizing behavior represented (e.g. meeting specified final energy demands
\par
at minimum cost, or maximizing the present value of consumption or of some
\par
utility function); whether agents have foresight and what choices they can make598 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
(labor-leisure and consumption-saving decisions, or only consumption deci-
\par
sions); how capital is structured and vintaged, and whether it is malleable once
\par
invested; and the degree of disaggregation among world regions and how they
\par
interact through flows of capital, people, and goods. A problem common to all
\par
such models, which is particularly acute when they are applied to developing
\par
or transitional economies, is the use of common equilibrium assumptions and
\par
determinants of behavior worldwide. Stanford University's Energy Modeling
\par
Forum has compared 14 such models under various scenarios of population,
\par
resource availability, technology, and emissions abatement (49).
\par
Combinations of the three approaches are most common in projecting emis-
\par
sions of multiple gases. Detailed atmospheric modeling can require specify-
\par
ing emissions of multiple gases and aerosols at fine spatial resolution. While
\par
fossil-CO2 emissions can be well modeled through either energy-economic or
\par
technology representations, other emissions are more difficult to measure and
\par
model. Consequently, many IA modeling projects have combined modeled
\par
emissions of CO2 and sometimes other gases from fossil combustion with fixed
\par
scenarios for other emissions. Weak representation of multiple emissions in
\par
present IA models limits their ability to evaluate abatement measures that affect
\par
multiple gases.
\par
Land use is both a significant emissions source---as a result of clearing,
\par
burning, cultivation, and other activities---and an important route for climate
\par
impacts, both direct and those mediated by the effect of demand for food, fiber,
\par
and possibly biofuels on land resources. The approach of early IA models to
\par
land use has been primitive. Either global land-use emissions are assumed to be
\par
balanced by terrestrial carbon uptake (the ``neutral biosphere'' assumption) or
\par
fixed scenarios of land-use emissions with no feedbacks are used. Further ad-
\par
vances require detailed spatial disaggregation. Current work seeks to improve
\par
understanding of socioeconomic processes driving land use and its change,
\par
including competing uses for land, and to investigate land-related feedbacks
\par
between biophysical and socioeconomic processes. Attempts thus far to in-
\par
tegrate land use into IA models have stressed either the biophysical or the
\par
economic side, with limited treatment of linkages (50--52).
\par
Over multi-decade periods, population and technological change are crucial
\par
drivers of emissions but pose sharp challenges to IA models because understand-
\par
ing of their determinants is weak. Most IA models use exogenous population
\par
scenarios, while a few include demographic accounting modules that allow re-
\par
gional age-specific fertility, mortality, and migration to be specified. Models of
\par
social and economic determinants of fertility have been constructed, but these
\par
are purely illustrative, as knowledge of these relations remains very weak (53).
\par
Future technological change will also strongly influence emissions and the costs
\par
of abatement (54, 55). While aggregate technological trends are evident in theINTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 599
\par
long-term record, such as substitution of major primary energy sources and
\par
economy-wide decarbonization (56, 57), knowledge of their determinants---
\par
and hence of the magnitude and even direction of technology's effect on future
\par
emissions---is very limited. Most IA models make exogenous assumptions of
\par
technological change, represented as specific technologies including backstops
\par
or as economy-wide parameters such as input-output coefficients or autonomous
\par
rates of efficiency increase. Attempts to endogenize technological change,
\par
through statistically estimated learning-curve effects (58--60) or as functions
\par
of investment or fossil energy prices (61, 62), demonstrate that the magnitude
\par
of such effects is large, but the foundation for any particular representation
\par
remains weak.
\par
3.2 \emph{Representation of the Atmosphere and Oceans}
\par
Any attempt to assess environmental consequences of climate change requires
\par
an IA model to represent atmospheric and oceanic processes. Three basic
\par
processes must be represented: the carbon cycle, atmospheric chemistry, and
\par
climate. The level of detail in a representation of the atmosphere and ocean
\par
is a basic design choice for an IA model. Scientifically credible models of
\par
atmospheric chemistry and climate impose very large computational burdens,
\par
which can limit an IA model's accessibility and use in policy settings as well as
\par
the extent of uncertainty analysis that can be conducted. In addition, research
\par
and understanding in these areas is rapidly progressing, so IA models that seek
\par
to include scientifically current models must decide how to treat disparities
\par
among available models.
\par
Moving from carbon emissions to atmospheric concentrations requires a
\par
carbon-cycle model to represent how carbon moves among the reservoirs of
\par
the atmosphere, the terrestrial biota, and the surface and deep oceans. Ap-
\par
proaches of widely varying complexity have been used. The simplest are
\par
airborne-fraction models, in which a specified fraction of each year's emissions
\par
is retained in the atmosphere indefinitely (29). Most widely used are impulse-
\par
response functions that partition emissions into shares, each of which decays
\par
exponentially with a distinct lifetime. Such functions can be fitted statistically
\par
to results of more detailed carbon-cycle models or to observed historical con-
\par
centrations and emissions (63--65). Some IA modelers have developed their
\par
own simple carbon-cycle models, statistically refitting data series on atmo-
\par
spheric concentrations and historical emissions and land-clearing (45, 66, 67).
\par
Various alternative specifications are consistent with the historical record. One
\par
IA model has used a more complex carbon cycle (68, 69). The enrichments
\par
captured by detailed carbon-cycle models may be important for understand-
\par
ing regional-scale biosphere-atmosphere feedbacks, but their importance for
\par
global-scale IA modeling has not yet been determined (70, 71).600 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
Non-CO2 greenhouse gases react chemically in the atmosphere, so modeling
\par
the relationship between emissions and concentrations requires representing
\par
atmospheric photochemistry. Some IA models exclude other emissions or rep-
\par
resent them in carbon-equivalent terms and do not model their atmospheric fate
\par
explicitly. Those that do project non-CO2 emissions most commonly represent
\par
their fate as a fixed global atmospheric lifetime for each species, ignoring non-
\par
linearities, photochemical feedbacks, and dependence on fine-scale processes.
\par
IA models that stress spatial detail must represent atmospheric chemistry ex-
\par
plicitly. Present IA models have resolved chemically coherent regions such
\par
as hemispheres and land vs ocean (72--74). One IA modeling project is spon-
\par
soring development of a two-dimensional, land-ocean resolving atmospheric
\par
chemistry model.
\par
Changed trace-gas concentrations change climate by altering the atmo-
\par
sphere's radiative properties and hence its temperature distribution. The most
\par
detailed representations of these physical processes in the atmosphere are
\par
General Circulation Models (GCMs) with coupled oceans, which divide the
\par
atmosphere into cells of a few degrees and a dozen-odd vertical layers and cal-
\par
culate numerical approximations to the properties of these cells and the flows
\par
of mass and energy between them (75). No IA model uses a full GCM; rather,
\par
less computationally demanding reduced-form models are used, which can be
\par
calibrated to results of full GCMs or to specified regional climate sensitivities.
\par
The simplest approach, used by many IA models, treats the entire lower at-
\par
mosphere together. A single equation represents the incremental contribution of
\par
each trace gas to radiative forcing and hence to equilibrium global-average tem-
\par
perature change (76, 77). These temperature equations can be scaled to match a
\par
specified value of climate sensitivity (e.g. 1.5--4.5\textopenbullet C)---the equilibrium global
\par
temperature change from doubled CO2 concentration---which summarizes as-
\par
sumed effects of feedbacks (e.g. clouds) not captured in such an aggregate rep-
\par
resentation. Realized temperature change at any time also depends on lags in the
\par
system, which many IA models estimate using simple zero or one-dimensional
\par
models of ocean-atmosphere heat transfer (78--80). The most spatially de-
\par
tailed IA models include two-dimensional atmosphere-ocean climate models
\par
(81, 82).
\par
Calculated global-average temperature can be used directly to estimate global
\par
sea-level rise (74, 83). To obtain projections of regional climate change without
\par
running a GCM, the most common approach is statistical downscaling, which
\par
fits higher-dimension or finer-scale data calculated by a GCM to a latitudinal
\par
or global average provided by a simple model (84, 85). Such techniques al-
\par
low reduced-form models to replicate the behavior of full GCMs quite well.
\par
Meaningful studies of ecosystem, agricultural, or other impacts require climate
\par
variables specified at finer resolution than GCM grid-cells. Consequently, IAINTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 601
\par
models that pursue spatially detailed impacts must also downscale climate pro-
\par
jections from the few-degree grid-cell size typical of GCMs to the roughly
\par
half-degree resolution of ecosystem models. Various statistical smoothing tech-
\par
niques are used, which match historically observed local weather patterns to
\par
GCM grid-cell averages (86, 87). The validity of GCM results and of these
\par
statistical techniques for impact studies is controversial (88--90). Basic prob-
\par
lems include uncertainty over whether observed correlations between large-
\par
and small-scale climate will persist under changed global climate and the im-
\par
portance in impacts of changes in precipitation, for which GCM projections in
\par
many regions differ even in sign, and of extreme events as well as means. In
\par
view of these difficulties, many detailed sectoral climate-impact studies have
\par
used analog climates derived from other locations, observed extremes, or his-
\par
torical or paleo records, instead of, or in addition to, GCM results (91--93).
\par
3.3 \emph{Representation of the Biosphere}
\par
Changed climate can affect ecosystems, as can changes in atmospheric trace
\par
gases directly. While many climate impacts will occur through effects on mar-
\par
kets, highly managed ecosystems, or human structures and settlements, some
\par
will occur through changes in less-managed ecosystems. Climate change might
\par
shift the location or functioning of ecosystem types, affecting human welfare
\par
through changes in such environmental services as water retention, productivity,
\par
or nutrient cycling or via direct preferences for particular landscapes. Imper-
\par
fectly understood feedbacks between the atmosphere and either the terrestrial
\par
or oceanic biosphere may be important for understanding the entire climate
\par
system (94, 95).
\par
While work in modeling terrestrial ecosystems is proceeding rapidly, there is
\par
yet no dynamic global model of terrestrial ecosystem response to changes in cli-
\par
mate and atmospheric trace gases. Consequently, while biosphere-atmosphere
\par
interactions may be key components for understanding climate change, their
\par
representation is presently limited by underlying knowledge of ecosystem dy-
\par
namics. IA models have taken two approaches to this situation. Many ig-
\par
nore ecosystems, treating socioeconomic impacts as direct functions of climate
\par
change, as discussed in the next section. A few are incorporating spatially
\par
detailed ecosystem models that draw on active, rapidly developing work in ter-
\par
restrial ecology (68, 69, 96). This work is attempting to represent climate and
\par
CO2 effects on production and nutrient cycling processes of specific plant types;
\par
to represent dynamics of ecosystem response; and to introduce stochasticity to
\par
the climate dependence of biome types (97--104). While this is a key field in
\par
IA modeling, advances here depend on advances in underlying knowledge, and
\par
IA models that link climate and ecosystem change to socioeconomic feedbacks
\par
and impacts are far from realization.602 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
3.4 \emph{Representation and Valuation of Impacts}
\par
Most IA projects seek to characterize and evaluate the social and economic im-
\par
pacts of climate change. Impacts are likely to be of diverse character, to depend
\par
on different aspects of climate, and to require different bodies of knowledge
\par
to inform them. Salient impacts may occur through agriculture or forestry,
\par
storm damage, sea-level rise or other aspects of coastal management, water
\par
management, transport, construction, health, or other sectors. Some may be
\par
mediated by changes in unmanaged terrestrial ecosystems, but many likely will
\par
not be. All are likely to depend in complex ways on other social and economic
\par
trends (e.g. on population and economic growth, consumption patterns, and
\par
settlement patterns) and on management changes or adaptive measures taken
\par
by individuals, communities, and nations. Impacts may interact or propagate
\par
across sectors or regions through economic or other linkages.
\par
To date, the most common approach to representing impacts in IA models
\par
has been to avoid all details, linking monetized measures of projected impacts
\par
directly to aggregate climate variables. This approach originated with a review
\par
of climate-sensitive sectors of the US economy that estimated losses from a
\par
3\textopenbullet{} equilibrium global temperature change as a 0.25\% GNP loss, then judg-
\par
mentally adjusted upward to 1--2\% to reflect cross-sectoral linkages or other
\par
overlooked damages (105, 106). Subsequent similar analyses reviewed by the
\par
IPCC have yielded estimates of 1--1.5\% for industrialized countries and 2--9\%
\par
for developing countries (107). Many IA models have used such point es-
\par
timates to calibrate simple (linear or quadratic) climate-damage functions of
\par
global temperature change. Such damage functions can be used to calculate
\par
the marginal social damage of a unit emission over time (45, 108, 109) or to
\par
construct impact-based measures of the relative damage caused by emissions
\par
of different gases (110--112). These functions can be enriched by introducing
\par
variation between regions or by dependence on time, wealth, or other climate
\par
variables such as precipitation or the rate of temperature change. Increasingly,
\par
IA models also define separate damage functions for market and nonmarket
\par
impacts, which may be driven by different processes and mitigated by different
\par
adaptive possibilities.
\par
While it is widely acknowledged that these damage functions are unsatisfac-
\par
tory, they remain widely used because specifying impacts in more detail requires
\par
disaggregation that may be limited by underlying process understanding, com-
\par
putational power, and particularly in developing countries, availability of data.
\par
Disaggregating specific impacts, particularly in nonmarket sectors, while re-
\par
taining a common metric for evaluation, has posed severe problems. Even if
\par
contingent valuation methods are accepted as measures of present damages,
\par
valuing environmental changes over long periods is much more problematic, as
\par
memories fade and tastes, behavior, and technology change in unknown ways.INTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 603
\par
In contrast with the aggregated approach to impacts, many studies have in-
\par
vestigated direct impacts of climate change in such sectors as agriculture, sea
\par
level, water, biological diversity, and health, and some IA models include a
\par
few such first-order sectoral impacts (113--119). The principal challenges to
\par
more effectively integrating such studies into IA models are representing adap-
\par
tive responses and characterizing linkages across sectors and regions to permit
\par
aggregation. Adaptation has been studied for agriculture and sea-level rise,
\par
more effectively at the level of individual decision makers than of communi-
\par
ties or markets (114, 120--122). Impacts have been aggregated across sectors
\par
by simple summing (i.e. assuming no cross-sectoral linkages), input-output
\par
modeling, and models of general equilibrium and international trade (92, 124;
\par
R Mendelsohn \& J Neumann, manuscript submitted). Neither of these enrich-
\par
ments has yet adequately been incorporated into IA models. An alternative to
\par
aggregating impacts, proposed but not yet implemented, is to present vectors of
\par
multiple valued environmental and social consequences. These could be used to
\par
define multi-attribute utility functions, to investigate the effect of constraining
\par
one consequence on others, or to provide multidimensional output projections
\par
to provoke policy debate.
\par
Recent reviews of impact studies are increasingly identifying low-cost adap-
\par
tation measures, and in some regions off-setting benefits, that reduce or in
\par
some cases eliminate estimated market impacts of doubled-CO2 climate change,
\par
particularly in OECD countries (107, 109, 125; R Mendelsohn \& J Neumann,
\par
manuscript submitted). These results are increasingly directing attention to the
\par
less well--studied areas of nonmarket impacts, extreme events, cross-sectoral
\par
linkages, and multiple environmental stresses. There is as yet no consensus on
\par
the likely magnitude of these impacts, even for the OECD countries. A finding
\par
that they are also small would highlight the interregional distributional aspects
\par
of climate change.
\par
3.5 \emph{Representation of Responses}
\par
A primary purpose claimed for IA models is to assess potential response op-
\par
tions. Potential responses include abatement (reducing emissions), adaptation
\par
(reducing the harm caused by a changed climate), and geoengineering (alter-
\par
ing the climate system directly to compensate for changes caused by increased
\par
emissions). In both policy and assessment, abatement has received the most
\par
attention, adaptation less, and geoengineering the least. In principle, IA models
\par
may represent any of these forms of response, but to date representation of all
\par
forms has been weak. How well each can be represented in a particular IA model
\par
depends on the model's representational detail in the corresponding domain:
\par
emissions and their socioeconomic determinants for abatement responses, im-
\par
pacts for adaptation responses, and specific elements of the atmosphere or bio-
\par
sphere for geoengineering. Assessing any of these responses is difficult under604 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
the long time horizon of climate change, for any response would be undertaken
\par
against a background of other changes likely to be large, but whose character
\par
and effects cannot be anticipated.
\par
Abatement measures can be represented either as directly specified changes
\par
in emissions, population, production, or technology from a baseline case---
\par
though this begs the question of what feasible policies might bring about such
\par
changes---or as policies such as taxes or other economic measures, regulation,
\par
or direct investment intended to effect such changes. Representing either form
\par
requires that emissions be modeled with substantial technological or economic
\par
detail. IA models have been used to study the effects of broad generic abate-
\par
ment policies, such as national emission caps, economy-wide carbon taxes, or
\par
uniform international systems of tradable emission permits, though such stud-
\par
ies do not require vertical integration beyond modeling of the determinants
\par
of emissions (49, 126). In addition, IA models with regional detail have ex-
\par
amined simple bargaining outcomes for international allocation of abatement
\par
effort, e.g. comparing globally optimal distributions of abatement with cases
\par
in which certain regions abate either less or more than this optimal level. These
\par
studies have found large savings from cooperative international abatement,
\par
half or more of the cost of abatement through separate regional efforts (108,
\par
127--129).
\par
But the utility of IA models' representation of any specific abatement poli-
\par
cies is presently limited by their coarse regional and sectoral resolution; by
\par
their inability to represent details of realistic policies and their implementation;
\par
by the difficulty of disentangling proposed policy interventions from baseline
\par
projections; and by limitations in knowledge about the effects of near-term
\par
policy choices on such long-term drivers as rates of investment, research, and
\par
technological innovation and diffusion.
\par
Adaptation measures are even more difficult to represent within IA models
\par
than abatement, in part because of the diversity of adaptive responses and the
\par
agents who undertake them. Most IA models have excluded explicit adaptation,
\par
in effect embedding assumed adaptation within impact functions, so explicit
\par
adaptive responses cannot be considered. Detailed sectoral impact studies
\par
have increasingly included adaptive responses, and IA models have examined
\par
adaptation to sea-level rise through large infrastructure investments (32, 92), but
\par
in general the representation of adaptive response remains a particularly weak
\par
area of IA modeling. Geoengineering measures, which seek to alter aggregate
\par
parameters of the climate system such as the earth's albedo or the rate of ocean
\par
carbon uptake, may be easier to represent in IA models than either abatement
\par
or adaptation. But the assessment of geoengineering measures depends first on
\par
detailed process and technical studies of the potential effect, cost, and risks of
\par
specific proposed interventions (130--133). IA models may contribute to thisINTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 605
\par
assessment by identifying potential feedbacks or impacts and by comparing
\par
and studying interactions among multiple forms of policy response.
\par
4. SUMMARY OF INTEGRATED ASSESSMENT
\par
PROJECTS
\par
This section summarizes recent and continuing projects in IA modeling, sorting
\par
them according to differences in their emphasis and purpose. Section 4.1 sum-
\par
marizes projects that emphasize the characterization of optimal responses to
\par
climate change, considering dynamics inherent in economic growth and abate-
\par
ment and in atmospheric accumulation. Section 4.2 summarizes projects that
\par
emphasize uncertainty, including some that highlight both dynamic optimiza-
\par
tion and uncertainty through sequential and adaptive decision formulations.
\par
Section 4.3 summarizes projects that emphasize fine spatial detail in the char-
\par
acterization of atmospheric change, impacts, and feedbacks.
\par
Previous attempts to categorize IA models have found it difficult to draw
\par
clean boundaries (2, 5, 134), and this scheme is likely to be no exception. Some
\par
IA projects incorporate more than one of these aspects to a limited extent or use
\par
them in diverse ways; others aspire to provide a sophisticated representation
\par
of all these aspects. But given the constraints of resources, underlying knowl-
\par
edge, and computational power, IA models that are simultaneously strong on
\par
all these aspects will not be realized soon. We contend that this categoriza-
\par
tion highlights basic differences in disciplinary heritage and emphasis of IA
\par
models---differences that have long-lived effects on the directions of enquiry
\par
and the kinds of questions for which each is best suited.
\par
4.1 \emph{Projects that Emphasize Emission Dynamics}
\par
\emph{and Optimization}
\par
One major theme of IA modeling has been investigating alternative paths of
\par
global emissions over time, considering their linked effects on economic growth
\par
and atmospheric accumulation of greenhouse gases. Several projects have
\par
sought to identify dynamically optimal emission paths that balance, over time,
\par
the costs of emissions abatement and damages from climate change. Others
\par
have assessed differences in abatement cost of broadly different emission paths
\par
that meet specified atmospheric-concentration constraints, or they have identi-
\par
fied emission corridors that simultaneously meet several constraints on allowed
\par
atmospheric change and on maximum rates of emission reduction.
\par
The projects that calculate dynamically optimal emission time paths show
\par
several common features that reflect the conceptual and computational require-
\par
ments of dynamic optimization. They all must postulate a single long-lived
\par
representative producer-consumer, either for the world or for each region, on606 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
whose behalf the optimization is performed. They all require that abatement
\par
costs and climate damages be specified in a common metric and therefore
\par
must represent regional or global climate damages by simple aggregate func-
\par
tions of global temperature change. These functions are usually calibrated to
\par
Nordhaus's point estimates for equilibrium doubled-CO2 and are sometimes
\par
presented in a few alternative forms. They all require that these costs be com-
\par
parable over long time horizons, so they require---and typically are highly
\par
sensitive to---specification of a discount rate.
\par
Nordhaus presents a dynamically integrated climate-economy model (DICE)
\par
that extends his earlier comparative-static analysis of optimal greenhouse abate-
\par
ment (45, 105). A single global producer-consumer chooses between current
\par
consumption, investment, and reducing greenhouse emissions, so as to max-
\par
imize the present value of utility. The basic framework is of a neoclassical
\par
optimal-growth model (135), with production losses introduced by emission
\par
abatement and climate damages. No energy-sector detail is represented. Rather,
\par
an abatement cost function is fitted statistically to the collection of point es-
\par
timates from prior studies, such that reducing emissions by half from their
\par
unconstrained level at any time costs about 1\% of output. Simple statistically
\par
fitted carbon-cycle and climate models, and a damage function, complete DICE.
\par
A fixed fraction of each period's carbon emissions adds to atmospheric con-
\par
centrations, while realized temperature change is represented by a three-box
\par
model including the atmosphere, mixed-layer upper ocean, and deep ocean.
\par
Climate damage is a quadratic function of realized temperature, in which a 3\textopenbullet{}
\par
rise reduces output by 1.3\%.
\par
DICE has been used to calculate the marginal damage of a ton of carbon emis-
\par
sions over time (equivalent to the optimal emissions tax to impose), to evaluate
\par
abatement policies, and to identify optimal emissions time paths. Marginal
\par
damages are calculated at a few dollars per ton, rising to \$20 per ton over the
\par
next century. Optimal emission paths involve only very modest reduction from
\par
unconstrained emissions growth, while stabilizing emissions or concentrations
\par
is estimated to cost as much as 3--9\% of output. Adding uncertainty to the
\par
formulation doubles to triples the optimal emission taxes. A regionally dis-
\par
aggregated form of DICE has been developed (129), and a stochastic form is
\par
under development.
\par
Two projects sponsored by the Electric Power Research Institute (EPRI) have
\par
constructed dynamic-optimization IA models around prior energy-economic
\par
models developed by EPRI. Peck \& Teisberg have built CETA (Carbon
\par
Emissions Trajectory Assessment model), extending the Global 2100 macroeco-
\par
nomic-energy model by adding simple models of the carbon cycle and global-
\par
average temperature change, and a damage function (46, 136). Energy CO2
\par
emissions are modeled by embedding an energy sector with substantial technicalINTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 607
\par
detail within the production function, while other emissions follow fixed sce-
\par
narios similar to the IPCC's. Doubled-CO2 sensitivity is tuned to 3\textopenbullet, while
\par
damage estimates are calibrated to 2\% GNP loss from 3\textopenbullet equilibrium temper-
\par
ature change. The model is driven by a single global consumer-producer who
\par
maximizes the present value of utility of consumption, net of climate-change
\par
damages, over the period from the present to the year 2200.
\par
CETA has been used to study the effects of alternative forms of climate-
\par
damage function, including dependence on the rate of global temperature
\par
change, as well as uncertainty about climate damages and global cooperation
\par
in a two-region world. As in Nordhaus's analysis, estimated emission damages
\par
and optimal abatements are small, \$10--\$20 per tonne of carbon, increasing
\par
modestly with nonlinear or rate-dependent damages (137). The value of in-
\par
formation about damage severity is high relative to current research budgets,
\par
but the cost of a 20-year delay in gaining this information is modest (138). A
\par
study introducing large stochastic climate losses (139), using subjective expert
\par
probabilities, found that optimal abatement was highly sensitive to probability
\par
of loss, which natural scientists put an order of magnitude higher than social
\par
scientists (140, 141).
\par
MERGE (Model for Evaluating Regional and Global Effects), like CETA,
\par
combines a detailed energy-economy model with simple carbon and climate
\par
models and a damage function. MERGE uses Global 2200, a five-region dy-
\par
namic general equilibrium model with a detailed energy sector. New technolo-
\par
gies, fossil resource depletion, technological change, and emissions coefficients
\par
are specified exogenously. A single agent in each region makes consumption
\par
and savings/investment decisions to maximize the present-value of utility of
\par
consumption. Energy-related emissions of CO2, CH4, and N2O are modeled,
\par
while exogenous scenarios are used for other emissions (48). A simple impulse-
\par
response function represents the carbon cycle, while CH4 and N2O have fixed
\par
atmospheric lifetimes. Radiative forcing functions and a lagged adjustment
\par
model for realized global temperature are drawn from the IPCC. Regional tem-
\par
perature change over land is assumed to be equal to the global mean in temper-
\par
ate latitudes and half as large in the tropics. Simple regional damage functions
\par
separate market and nonmarket damages. Both are quadratic in temperature,
\par
and nonmarket damages also depend on regional income. Policy studies with
\par
MERGE, both comparing specified abatement policies and incorporating cli-
\par
mate damages into the dynamic optimization, found that delaying abatement
\par
was preferable (48, 142). A similar dynamic optimization study by Maddison,
\par
assessing specified levels of abatement relative to a baseline emissions scenario
\par
drawn from the IPCC and EMF 12, yielded similar results: Optimal abatement
\par
levels are small, roughly 10--20\% from unconstrained baseline growth paths,
\par
with minimal costs to a 20-year delay in implementing controls (66).608 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
These studies stress the dynamics of economic growth and investment and of
\par
atmospheric accumulation. Grubb et al report a simple model that instead stud-
\par
ies dynamics of technological change, in which abatement cost depends both
\par
on the level of abatement and on its rate of change. When the second, adaptive
\par
component of abatement cost dominates, they obtain results that contradict the
\par
preceding studies: High abatement is preferred even if climate damages are
\par
small, and delays of a few decades in starting abatement effort are very costly
\par
(55). Hourcade \& Chapuis find similar benefits to early abatement activity
\par
in a dynamic formulation that combines positive feedbacks in abatement with
\par
nonlinear climate damage functions (143).
\par
The arbitrary climate-damage functions used in these dynamic optimization
\par
studies have been problematic and contentious (38). An alternative approach
\par
replaces these damage functions by environmental constraints, in effect replac-
\par
ing a cost-benefit approach by a cost-effectiveness approach. In this approach,
\par
a specified environmental constraint is imposed (e.g. a limit on atmospheric
\par
concentrations or temperature in an endpoint year), and the costs of alternative
\par
specified emissions trajectories that meet the constraint are calculated or cost-
\par
minimizing trajectories are determined. This replacement of a climate-damage
\par
function with an atmospheric constraint has different effects on preferred abate-
\par
ment levels in the near and the long term: In the near term, because damages
\par
below the constraint are treated as zero, the approach favors smaller abatement
\par
effort; but in the long term, because damages above the constraint are treated
\par
as infinite, the approach favors larger abatement (144, 145).
\par
Recent applications of this approach have compared the costs of alternative
\par
emissions paths to stabilize atmospheric concentrations at specified levels be-
\par
tween 350 and 750 ppm, finding that trajectories in which global emissions
\par
climb through 2050 and then decline sharply reduce present-value abatement
\par
costs by as much as half relative to trajectories with near-term emissions sta-
\par
bilization or reduction. The lower costs reflect four factors---technological
\par
change, avoiding premature capital retirement, exploiting natural carbon ab-
\par
sorption, and discounting---and optimal trajectories depend strongly on the
\par
discount rate (146--148).
\par
An approach that goes further in replacing hypothesized cost functions by
\par
constraints is the ``safe emissions corridor'' approach, which calculates emission
\par
trajectories that simultaneously meet several externally specified environmental
\par
and economic constraints such as maximum limits on total global temperature
\par
increase and its rate and limits on the maximum rate of emission reduction
\par
attainable per year (149, 150). The safe emission corridors calculated by these
\par
approaches involve larger near-term reductions than either those that calculate
\par
dynamically optimal emissions paths or those that impose only environmental
\par
constraints in a future year.INTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 609
\par
4.2 \emph{Projects that Emphasize Uncertainty}
\par
Uncertainty is central to climate change, and many IA modeling projects include
\par
it to at least some degree. Indeed, characterizing key policy-relevant uncertain-
\par
ties to help guide inquiry is a central purpose for IA. But rich treatment of
\par
uncertainty imposes heavy computational demands, so stronger treatment of
\par
uncertainty is associated with weaker dynamics and less spatial detail.
\par
Nearly all IA modeling projects have addressed uncertainty at least to the
\par
extent of conducting sensitivity analyses, either on a few key parameters or
\par
between discrete clusters of related parameter choices representing broad al-
\par
ternative scenarios. For those projects that make uncertainty central to their
\par
analysis, the primary approach has been stochastic simulation with sampling
\par
over distributions of many uncertain input parameters. Such sampling generates
\par
probability distributions of outputs that reflect both the sensitivity of outputs to
\par
particular inputs and the uncertainty in the inputs. Other approaches to uncer-
\par
tainty have included evaluating adaptive decision strategies that can respond to
\par
progressive resolution of uncertainty over time, investigating the implications
\par
of low-probability climatic catastrophes, and representing uncertainty as sub-
\par
jective, consisting of correlated sets of preferences plus beliefs about uncertain
\par
parameters and model structure.
\par
The first probabilistic IA model was PAGE (Policy Analysis of the Green-
\par
house Effect), in which roughly 80 parameters describing emissions, atmo-
\par
sphere, and impacts were represented by user-definable triangular probability
\par
distributions, with uncertainty propagated through the model. Costs of abate-
\par
ment were calculated relative to assumed regional baseline emissions scenarios
\par
for four world regions, centered on Europe (151).
\par
The Integrated Climate Assessment Model (ICAM) has been developed
\par
within the integrated assessment project of Carnegie Mellon University, using
\par
a specialized system for probabilistic modeling (152). ICAM derives regional
\par
emissions of CO2, N2O, CH4, and sulfate aerosols from exogenous assump-
\par
tions by a demographic and economic accounting framework. Simple linear
\par
atmospheric models yield concentrations and changes in radiative forcing, while
\par
regional impact functions combine direct dependence on regional climatic vari-
\par
ables with explicit representation of a few specific impacts, including sea-level
\par
rise and changes in terrestrial ecosystems. Certain specific forms of abatement
\par
and geoengineering measures can be represented explicitly.
\par
Representation of uncertainty is the central goal of ICAM: Successive ver-
\par
sions of the model have included hundreds to thousands of uncertain parameters
\par
and some experimentation with sampling over uncertainties in model structure
\par
(37, 153, 154). The project has also included various detailed sectoral analyses,
\par
which all stress uncertainty but of which only reduced forms are incorporated in610 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
ICAM. These have included analyses of expert judgment of climate uncertain-
\par
ties, stochastic modeling of ecosystem change under climate change, coastal
\par
impacts, trace-gas indexes, geoengineering policy, and value-of-information
\par
calculations (37, 111, 120, 131, 141). A consistent result of many analyses with
\par
ICAM has been that socioeconomic uncertainties and variation in preferences
\par
are more important than biophysical uncertainties in determining uncertainty
\par
in impacts or preferred policy responses (37, 155).
\par
Tol has presented a nine-region IA model that combines exogenous emission
\par
scenarios with moderately detailed endogenous abatement costs and climate
\par
damages, including quantified, highly illustrative impacts in particular sec-
\par
tors, defined as functions of temperature change and income---agricultural and
\par
coastal effects, flooding, storm damage, deaths from heat stress and malaria, mi-
\par
gration, and hurricanes. Optimal regional abatement from baseline emissions
\par
is calculated to be modest, with or without global interregional cooperation
\par
and financial transfers, while a stochastic simulation with 13 uncertain param-
\par
eters increases optimal abatement to roughly 10--20\% of baseline emissions
\par
(156, 157). Other IA projects have conducted stochastic simulations with un-
\par
certain inputs, using submodels when the complete IA model is too complex to
\par
permit the required replication (158, 159).
\par
As an alternative to stochastic simulation, a decision-analytic approach can be
\par
taken to uncertainty, assuming that uncertainties will be resolved at a specified
\par
future date. Studies taking this approach combine optimal decision-making and
\par
uncertainty. In contrast with the dynamically optimizing analyses discussed
\par
in Section 4.1, these studies normally only compare a few near-term policy
\par
strategies. In contrast with the stochastic simulation studies discussed in this
\par
section, they normally consider only a few key uncertainties. But by combining
\par
these two elements, they allow study of adaptive decision strategies that can
\par
hedge against uncertainty and respond to new information; how uncertainty
\par
alters preferred or optimal policy choices in the near term; and the value of
\par
information and the costs of waiting to obtain more information.
\par
Yohe \& Wallace sample nine uncertain parameters to develop a set of prob-
\par
abilistic scenarios. With these they examine optimal near-term response to
\par
the chance that a binding atmospheric-concentration limit will be revealed in
\par
the year 2020. Under a wide variety of scenarios, they find that optimal near-
\par
term abatement is small, even if the probability of subsequent imposition of a
\par
concentration constraint is high (160).
\par
Hammitt et al present an IA model of two-period sequential choice between
\par
two levels of abatement effort, each based on a moderately detailed energy-
\par
sector model. Two parameters, climate sensitivity and a global temperature
\par
target, are uncertain in the first period but revealed in the second. Damages
\par
can be represented either as a function of temperature or by imposing a limitINTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 611
\par
on global temperature change. They find that moderate near-term abatement
\par
is preferred under most parameter values, and adding risks of abrupt climate
\par
change does not alter the qualitative conclusion (161, 162). A game-theoretic
\par
application of the model to a two-region world finds that uncertainty reduces
\par
the benefits of cooperation and that delaying action for 20 years can eliminate
\par
them (128).
\par
In other uncertainty-based analyses, Kolstad introduced irreversibilities and
\par
learning into Nordhaus's DICE model and found that irreversibility of abate-
\par
ment investment, which reduced preferred abatement levels, had a larger effect
\par
than environmental irreversibility (163). Chao represented climate damages
\par
not as a smooth function but as a temperature-dependent Poisson process gen-
\par
erating climatic catastrophes, and found a higher optimal carbon tax but a lower
\par
discount rate, yielding an ambiguous aggregate effect (164).
\par
A novel and controversial approach to uncertainty has been presented in the
\par
TARGETS project, which is not an IA model of climate change but a broad
\par
horizontally integrated IA model of sustainable development that links multiple
\par
environmental, social, and economic issues (39). The project has used heuristic
\par
representations of uncertainty inspired by the three social types of Cultural
\par
Theory (165) to define scenarios that cluster linked subjective beliefs about
\par
uncertainties with policy preferences (166). Defining such scenarios can reveal
\par
previously unobserved subjectivity in models and provides one solution to the
\par
operational problem of handling correlations among many uncertain inputs.
\par
But the particular structuring of subjective beliefs employed is arbitrary, absent
\par
a better empirical and conceptual foundation for the particular social types
\par
employed.
\par
In summary, IA models take diverse approaches to uncertainty. Some com-
\par
bine probability distributions of uncertain inputs or model structures to project
\par
distributions of consequences, while others integrate limited uncertainty into
\par
optimal choice frameworks. No IA modeling project performs full stochastic
\par
dynamic optimization.
\par
4.3 \emph{Projects that Emphasize Spatial Detail}
\par
Several IA models stress the characterization of global climate change at fine
\par
spatial scale. This approach tends to emphasize the biophysical over the socio-
\par
economic aspects of climate change. A fine spatial scale allows more explicit
\par
treatment of the atmospheric chemistry of multiple emissions and the radiative
\par
effect of aerosols, though parameterizations of these are also available at larger
\par
scales. This approach also allows explicit treatment of biosphere-atmosphere
\par
feedbacks and of multiple linked global-change issues. It allows examination
\par
of the spatial variability of impacts, one often-sought component of policy rele-
\par
vance. Finally, it allows linking IA models with global, spatially referenced data612 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
sets, principally from remote-sensing but also from field campaigns, increasing
\par
the tools available for validating IA models.
\par
The practical obstacles to this approach to IA modeling, however, are severe.
\par
Neither the climate nor the ecosystem models used in IA models yet offer
\par
either transient or well-validated representations at fine spatial scales. Limits to
\par
underlying process knowledge, as well as massive computational demands, have
\par
thus far limited spatially disaggregated IA models to illustrative projections,
\par
which lack realistic behavior or socioeconomic feedbacks and which admit
\par
very limited uncertainty analysis.
\par
The first spatially detailed IA model, ESCAPE, used technically detailed
\par
exogenous scenarios of multiple emissions for four world regions centered
\par
on Europe. Simple global-scale atmospheric chemistry and climate models
\par
calculated global mean temperature and sea-level rise, while regional changes
\par
in temperature and precipitation were estimated at fine spatial resolution over
\par
Europe through GCM downscaling. Simple illustrative impacts were calculated
\par
in six sectors (74, 167).
\par
The first IA model to include some fine spatial detail globally was IMAGE 2,
\par
whose three linked systems represented energy and emissions, the terrestrial
\par
environment and land cover, and the atmosphere and oceans, each operating at
\par
a different scale (168). Emissions are projected for 13 regions by a bottom-up,
\par
technologically detailed model with exogenous energy demand and primary fuel
\par
prices, which includes fuelwood demand (169). Land cover, soil type, element
\par
fluxes, and direct ecosystem impacts are represented on a global half-degree
\par
grid. Land cover is modeled at this fine scale through the interaction of four fac-
\par
tors: regional agricultural demands derived from simple income-elasticity rela-
\par
tionships; the FAO crop-suitability model, which calculates climate-dependent
\par
yields, modified by a local soil factor (170); land conversion as required to
\par
meet regional food and fuelwood demands, following a few simple heuristic
\par
rules to determine which land is converted; and the BIOME model of poten-
\par
tial land cover as a function of climate and soil, which determines the cover
\par
of abandoned agricultural land (171). Emissions are calculated from land use
\par
and conversion, including climate and CO2 fertilization feedbacks. Land cover
\par
interacts with the atmosphere via emissions and changes in albedo (172). The
\par
globally parameterized atmospheric chemistry model and climate-ocean model
\par
calculate annual-average temperature and precipitation change for 10\textopenbullet latitude
\par
bands, plus ocean circulation and transport of heat and CO2. Calculated climate
\par
over latitude bands is translated to grid-scale by GCM downscaling (72, 81).
\par
The model has been calibrated to reasonably good agreement on 1970--1990
\par
data for regional energy and emissions, deforestation, and atmospheric con-
\par
centrations. Prospective runs have contrasted a conventional wisdom scenarioINTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 613
\par
(similar to the IPCC's IS92a) with three discrete alternatives intended to yield
\par
instructively divergent land-use patterns: two that assume very high and very
\par
low use, respectively, of modern biofuels; and one that postulates a large shift in
\par
ocean circulation (173). The project's detailed treatment of land cover has al-
\par
lowed detailed investigation of direct climate-change impacts and of the effects
\par
of large-scale biomass-energy and afforestation responses (133, 174). Its lack
\par
of realistic representation of behavior, however, makes it unsuitable for valuing
\par
the effects of abatement or impacts or for estimating the emissions effect of
\par
other policy instruments.
\par
Three other current IA modeling projects incorporate moderate to high levels
\par
of spatial detail. A project at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology has
\par
been developing a large IA model with fine spatial detail since 1991. Mul-
\par
tiple emissions, including aerosols, are modeled by a dynamic computable
\par
general equilibrium (CGE) model that includes 13 sectors and 12 world re-
\par
gions. Technical change is represented through exogenous changes in input-
\par
output coefficients and specified backstop technologies (175--177). Newly de-
\par
veloped two-dimensional, land-ocean resolving linked chemistry and climate
\par
models represent the atmosphere and calculate average temperature, precipi-
\par
tation, cloudiness, and humidity for 23 latitude bands (82, 158). Ecosystems
\par
are represented by the Terrestrial Ecosystems Model (TEM), a process-based
\par
model that calculates net primary production and nitrogen cycling at half-degree
\par
resolution, as functions of changed climate, CO2 concentration, and nutrient
\par
deposition (96, 101). Global models of methane and nitrous oxide flux from
\par
natural ecosystems have also been developed, while representations of managed
\par
ecosystems, human land use, and impacts are under development.
\par
PGCAM is a spatially detailed IA model under development by Battelle
\par
Pacific Northwest National Laboratories, the University Corporation for At-
\par
mospheric Research, and Texas A\&M University (178). Emissions are repre-
\par
sented by a set of computable general-equilibrium models for 13 world regions,
\par
developed in collaboration with regional researchers. These models provide
\par
substantial technical detail in the energy sectors and vintaged capital stocks,
\par
so they permit fairly detailed modeling of policies that influence technological
\par
development or investment (179). Land-use emissions are estimated by models
\par
that allocate regional land among an unmanaged state and several competing
\par
uses, based on prices of biomass energy and agricultural products (51). The
\par
MAGICC and SCENGEN models represent atmospheric chemistry and climate,
\par
projecting global changes in temperature and sea level and using GCM down-
\par
scaling to project regional temperature and precipitation at half-degree detail
\par
over selected high-resolution windows (presently North America and Europe),
\par
and 5\textopenbullet elsewhere (73, 74). Impacts will be projected, first for North America,614 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
using the BIOME model for unmanaged ecosystems, and detailed agricultural
\par
crop and hydrology models for managed ecosystems (171).
\par
The Asian-Pacific Integrated Model (AIM) is being developed collabora-
\par
tively by nine institutions in five countries, coordinated by Japan's National
\par
Institute of Environmental Studies (180, 181). AIM is designed to investigate
\par
regional mitigation and adaptation strategies for the Asia-Pacific region. Emis-
\par
sions of major greenhouse gases and aerosols from energy and deforestation
\par
are represented through technologically detailed bottom-up national models
\par
for five Asian nations, linked by a global model that maintains equilibrium
\par
and represents emissions from the rest of the world. Simple global carbon-
\par
cycle and climate models are used, with statistical downscaling to a half-degree
\par
spatial scale for the Asia-Pacific region. Sectoral impacts are projected for hy-
\par
drology, agricultural production, natural ecosystems, and malaria, at fine spa-
\par
tial scale, and integrated with other data sets using a Geographic Information
\par
System.
\par
5. RESULTS AND INSIGHTS FROM INTEGRATED
\par
ASSESSMENT MODELS
\par
Despite its rapid growth, IA modeling is at an early stage of development, so
\par
the significant insights to emerge from it so far are few and tentative. Different
\par
kinds of projects permit different kinds of insights. For example, identifying key
\par
uncertainties requires rich specification and propagation of uncertainty, while
\par
examining alternative international bargaining solutions requires adequate re-
\par
gional resolution and policy detail.
\par
All IA modeling projects have stressed qualitative insights over quantitative
\par
predictions, suggesting that these are more robust to uncertainty and hence
\par
more persuasive (2, 5). This difference is one of degree, though, not of kind.
\par
Qualitative insights depend on underlying quantitative predictions and can be
\par
rendered invalid by erroneous underlying assumptions (36, 38). Confirmation
\par
by multiple IA models does not eliminate this risk if the models share the
\par
relevant assumptions. Claimed insights of IA models should consequently be
\par
regarded as consequences of particular assumptions, and both the reasonable-
\par
ness of the assumptions and the possibility of corroboration through dissimilar
\par
means should be explicitly considered. It has been argued that contributions of
\par
IA models should be evaluated by several criteria: not just the persuasiveness
\par
and novelty of the insights, but their robustness, their specificity, and the extent
\par
to which the IA model was needed to obtain them (182).
\par
The specific insights that have been claimed from IA models include, first,
\par
a cluster of related results that all appear to demonstrate that large near-term
\par
abatement is not justified (182). These include results showing that economicINTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 615
\par
climate-change impacts in rich (though not in poor) countries will be small
\par
and that ecosystem effects from climate change, agricultural management, and
\par
urbanization are all of similar magnitude (2, 125; R Mendelsohn \& J Neumann,
\par
manuscript submitted); cost-benefit results showing that optimal abatement
\par
involves reductions of only a few percent from unconstrained paths, which
\par
can be accomplished by carbon taxes of a few dollars per tonne and achieve
\par
gains on the order of 1\% of world product (45, 48, 183, 184); and the result,
\par
obtained from adaptive decision models and from cost-effectiveness analyses
\par
of future concentration constraints, that delays of a few decades in controlling
\par
emissions are preferred even if we later learn that stringent reductions are needed
\par
(146, 147, 160, 161).
\par
These results depend on several levels of assumptions. The analyses showing
\par
small climate impacts in rich countries increasingly do incorporate uncertainty,
\par
though not low-probability events. More importantly, they are all based on
\par
reference doubled-CO2 scenarios and hence do not admit the highest levels
\par
of trace-gas concentrations that aggressive fossil-fuel growth scenarios would
\par
generate by late next century. IA models have so far provided no insights on the
\par
implications of such scenarios, because they drive atmospheric, ecosystem, and
\par
impacts models far out of their validated ranges. These impact results are also
\par
based on weak representation of nonmarket impacts, cross-sectoral linkages of
\par
impacts, and interactions of multiple environmental stresses such as climate and
\par
air pollution. Better consideration of such interactions would likely increase
\par
projected impacts, but present IA models offer no insight into whether this
\par
increase would be large enough to change the qualitative conclusion and justify
\par
more stringent abatement. That climate impacts will be higher in poor countries
\par
depends on assumptions of less wealth and infrastructure, which obstruct the
\par
adaptation responses largely responsible for estimated rich-country impacts
\par
being small. Together, these two results imply that climate is fundamentally a
\par
problem of interregional distribution of wealth.
\par
The cost-benefit result that optimal OECD abatement is small depends on
\par
the impact results summarized above and their associated assumptions, and
\par
also on assumptions that shape the unconstrained path of future emissions (e.g.
\par
population and economic growth and technological change, in particular the
\par
continuing extent of autonomous decarbonization trends), the cost of abatement,
\par
fossil resources, and the basis for balancing costs and benefits, in particular
\par
the social discount rate. Plausible alternative formulations of technological
\par
change strongly reduce abatement costs and hence shape optimal emission
\par
paths (54, 55, 143), though technical change can also increase future emissions
\par
(61, 160), while optimal abatement is greatly increased in all formulations by
\par
assuming low or zero social discount rates (though such rates would imply high
\par
rates of saving that we do not presently observe) (185--187).616 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
The result that it is preferable to delay abatement even if it is ultimately to
\par
be done again depends partly on the same assumptions, plus two linked ad-
\par
ditional ones: that the value of information to be obtained by waiting is high
\par
and that the expected cost of waiting to obtain it is low. These are fairly re-
\par
strictive assumptions about the dynamics of technological change and policy.
\par
They presume that the most important uncertainties are scientific rather than
\par
economic or social, a presumption at odds with a major claimed insight from
\par
uncertainty-based IA modeling (37, 160). They presume that significant reso-
\par
lution of these uncertainties in a few decades is likely and hence that ``research''
\par
means something other than undertaking immediate abatement activity to climb
\par
up technological learning curves (contrast 188); and that positive returns, lock-
\par
ins, and learning effects are not dominant in the economy or in policy-making,
\par
so large abatement after several decades is consistent with little or none in the
\par
near term. If different assumptions hold, then optimal emission paths might
\par
rise less and fall sooner, or paths that rise then fall abruptly might be consistent
\par
with, or even require, substantial near-term commitment to abatement in the
\par
form of research, investment, and policy.
\par
Several other diverse insights have been claimed from IA models to date.
\par
One that may be obvious, but which all IA models that have examined the
\par
question confirm, is that OECD abatement alone cannot sustain reasonable
\par
limits on atmospheric concentrations because global cooperation is required.
\par
While back-of-envelope calculations of regional emission growth rates generate
\par
the same result, IA models have also permitted calculation of the magnitude of
\par
benefits available from various levels and forms of international cooperation.
\par
IA models have elaborated the importance of sulfate aerosols in climate
\par
change. While the magnitude of aerosol contributions to radiative forcing can
\par
be calculated from atmospheric models alone, identifying their implications for
\par
abatement strategies requires detail about spatial patterns of aerosol sources and
\par
how economic growth, fuel switching, technological change, and policy will
\par
affect them. Similarly, IA models have provided preliminary characterization
\par
of the effects of linked demographic, economic, and climatic pressures on
\par
land cover, with attendant atmospheric feedbacks; further elaboration of these
\par
patterns will require further integrated analyses.
\par
Contributions from IA models can take the form of identification of key uncer-
\par
tainties or research priorities, rather than specific results. These may be particu-
\par
larly significant when IA projects combine models with other forms of analyses.
\par
While large uncertainties are present in every component of climate change, one
\par
striking result has been that socioeconomic uncertainties---including economic
\par
structure, technological change, and social values---contribute more strongly
\par
to uncertainties in impacts and in preferred responses than do biophysical un-
\par
certainties (21, 37, 155, 160, 189).INTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 617
\par
6. EVALUATION AND CONCLUSIONS
\par
In conclusion, we review the contributions of IA modeling to date relative to the
\par
four purposes stated in Section 2. We then identify a few prominent challenges
\par
facing the field, with particular reference to the dual relationships of IA mod-
\par
eling to research and to broader processes of assessment and policy-making.
\par
Four potential contributions have been claimed for IA models: evaluating
\par
policies and responses, structuring knowledge and prioritizing uncertainties,
\par
contributing to basic knowledge of the whole climate system, and broad com-
\par
parative risk assessment. We consider these in turn. First, as Section 5 outlines,
\par
several important insights relevant to evaluating responses have been drawn
\par
from IA models. These have not addressed specific policies but have more
\par
broadly characterized generic responses. IA models appear unsuitable for eval-
\par
uating highly specific policies, as their representations lack necessary sectoral,
\par
institutional, and political detail.
\par
A few of these claimed insights, while contentious, have made real contri-
\par
butions to policy debate. That they depend on specific, restrictive assumptions
\par
does not negate this contribution, for clarifying these relationships can and has
\par
advanced both the agenda for IA investigation (e.g. highlighting the importance
\par
of further study of nonmarket effects and linkages) and the relative contribu-
\par
tions of different assumptions and political values in associated policy debates
\par
(e.g. in controversies provoked by the large savings apparently available from
\par
flexibility in the location and timing of abatement effort). The IA models that
\par
have claimed these policy-relevant insights, though, have been those that are
\par
rich and detailed, if anywhere, in their representation of economies and market
\par
impacts, and not in ecosystems, nonmarket impacts, or linkages. Their claims
\par
of small impacts and the preferability of modest and delayed abatement should
\par
consequently not be taken as definitive, but should focus investigation on these
\par
less-understood domains.
\par
Second, IA models have made large contributions to structuring knowledge
\par
and identifying uncertainty, principally because the attempt to construct IA
\par
models has revealed how extreme and pervasive uncertainties are in every do-
\par
main of the climate issue. This may be the complement of the more modest
\par
contributions IA models have made thus far to evaluating responses: When the
\par
gains available from identifying and characterizing uncertainties are large, it
\par
may be premature to seek specific policy advice.
\par
The most important component uncertainties for controlling whole-system
\par
behavior or for guiding policy and decision might be neither the most important
\par
for intrinsic understanding nor the largest in magnitude. Particular IA models
\par
have reported rank orderings of specific uncertainties, but these typically de-
\par
pend on the formulation of the model. Indeed, the primary work identifying618 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
and investigating uncertainties within particular domains remains the job of
\par
disciplinary research, not IA modeling.
\par
As with evaluating responses, IA models' contributions to understanding
\par
uncertainties have been, and are likely to remain, at more synoptic scale. IA
\par
models are particularly valuable for comparing broad uncertainties across sub-
\par
domains of the climate issue---comparisons that are necessary for understanding
\par
whole-system behavior, valued outcomes, and decisions, but that disciplinary
\par
investigations are not motivated or equipped to make. For example, the emerg-
\par
ing result that socioeconomic uncertainties dominate biophysical uncertainties
\par
in contributing to uncertainty in impacts and preferred responses, if further
\par
corroborated, may be an insight of key importance. When decisions must be
\par
made under substantial uncertainty, the structuring of available knowledge and
\par
ignorance can also be an important, more direct contribution of IA models to
\par
policy.
\par
Third, IA models have offered some preliminary contributions to basic knowl-
\par
edge, particularly in characterizing linked socioeconomic and environmental
\par
pressures on land cover, and associated feedbacks. But these insights are highly
\par
preliminary and depend strongly on advances in extremely incomplete, rapidly
\par
developing disciplinary knowledge. While better characterization of these links
\par
and feedbacks remains a central goal of both IA modeling and associated dis-
\par
ciplinary work, the great majority of this work remains to be acomplished.
\par
Finally, IA models have thus far contributed very little to broad compara-
\par
tive risk assessment. This purpose addresses the most basic policy question:
\par
How important is climate change, and for whom, relative to other competing
\par
concerns? Significant contributions here would depend on IA models provid-
\par
ing consistent comparative representations of multiple trends and issues, and
\par
linkages between them, over the relevant periods. This requires very broad
\par
horizontal integration rather than precision. IA models with broad horizontal
\par
integration could contribute to this task, and it is worth pursuing, but work to
\par
date is highly preliminary. Of the four purposes of IA models, this one may
\par
most compellingly require that IA models be embedded in broader, more delib-
\par
erative assessment processes, for while IA models may plausibly contribute to
\par
the identification of substantive linkages across multiple environmental issues,
\par
it is unlikely that they can contribute to a useful debate about broad priorities
\par
in social goals and multiple risks without broader inputs.
\par
For each of these four areas, the contributions of IA models have largely
\par
come from models whose particular emphasis and design highlight relevant
\par
dimensions---optimal or adaptive decision-making, uncertainty, spatial detail,
\par
and horizontal integration, respectively.
\par
At the broadest level, IA modeling faces two challenges: managing its rela-
\par
tionship to research and disciplinary knowledge and managing its relationshipINTEGRATED ASSESSMENT 619
\par
to other assessment processes and to policy-making. In its relationship with
\par
disciplinary knowledge, IA modeling's two challenges are to define appro-
\par
priate intellectual standards for integrating activity and to balance attempts at
\par
integration with advances in component knowledge. IA models supplement
\par
and depend on disciplinary inquiry but do not supersede or replace it (36, 38).
\par
Consequently, IA results are limited by weaknesses in underlying knowledge.
\par
But the attempt to integrate can both challenge and serve the constituent disci-
\par
plines, both in identifying priority investigations for broader understanding and
\par
in clarifying the uncertainties, weaknesses, and limits of pieces of constituent
\par
knowledge (35). Serving these purposes inverts the conventional argument that
\par
integrated modeling should await adequate understanding in all constituent
\par
domains, by requiring that integration attempts proceed ahead of underlying
\par
knowledge. Indeed, IA models may make contributions of different kinds de-
\par
pending on their relationship to current knowledge, and it has been persuasively
\par
argued that waiting for all component science to be done minimizes the con-
\par
tribution of integrating models (27). Proceeding iteratively between aggregate
\par
integrating studies and component disciplinary studies may advance knowledge
\par
faster, while also advancing policy relevance. This is likely true of integrated
\par
modeling and of other processes of integrated assessment; both qualitative and
\par
quantitative approaches to integration can serve in this iterative process.
\par
The direct contributions of IA modeling projects to policy so far have been
\par
limited. The difficulties are not surprising and reveal no particular fault of IA
\par
modelers. Long-known difficulties continue to obstruct attempts to provide
\par
useful scientifically grounded advice to policy-making: failure to converge on
\par
the set of things that analysts can assert and policymakers care about; rea-
\par
sonable skepticism about specific claims when limited means are available to
\par
engage underlying analyses; and results that are too complex to be accessible
\par
or are ineffectively communicated. These difficulties have prompted interest
\par
in developing very simple, reduced-form IA models that can be operated in
\par
real time by nonexperts, some of them free-standing and some developed in
\par
parallel with, or embedding key insights from, more extensive IA modeling
\par
projects (41, 190). These may serve a useful didactic function for specific and
\par
well-founded insights but are not likely fully to solve the problems of nontrans-
\par
parency or eliminate risks of misunderstanding, hidden assumptions, or bias.
\par
For real policy advice, even simple models are likely to require mediation and
\par
interpretation (1).
\par
IA modeling is not, and cannot be, all of assessment. There are fundamental
\par
limits to any assessment method based entirely (or nearly so) on representation.
\par
IA models can contribute best to assessment when embedded in broader assess-
\par
ment processes that introduce more deliberation, criticism, diversity of views,
\par
and common sense. When relevant constituent knowledge is highly complex,620 PARSON \& FISHER-VANDEN
\par
linked across multiple domains, and uncertain, IA models may be essential in
\par
such processes, or nearly so, but they cannot be sufficient. IA models cannot
\par
identify what questions to ask; what kinds of responses, risks, or linkages to
\par
consider; or make broad evaluative judgements, though they may be able to
\par
make valuable contributions to any of these discussions. The use of IA models
\par
in the second round of the IPCC was a promising, though limited, start. Their
\par
structuring role could be expanded in subsequent IPCC assessments. There
\par
may also be substantial promise to developing novel process-based methods
\par
such as simulations or policy exercises that embed IA tools or models, and
\par
other forms of expert knowledge, into broader processes for investigating op-
\par
tions, clarifying values, identifying contingencies, or teaching (7, 8, 191). As
\par
with the connection of IA models to underlying knowledge, the longer-term use
\par
of IA models in advising policy will likely involve repeated iterations in which
\par
IA models serve as one mediating device in the exchange between policy and
\par
scientific domains over time.
\par
Beyond these broad challenges, several acute weaknesses particularly limit
\par
the policy contributions of IA models at present. While these largely reflect
\par
underlying weaknesses in knowledge or data, they make IA models unable now,
\par
and perhaps for some time, to advise certain specific high-priority concerns of
\par
policy-makers. These include limited understanding of impacts and adaptation;
\par
weak or nonexistent representation of social and behavioral processes in de-
\par
veloping countries; and extremely limited treatment of major low-probability
\par
climatic change.
\par
Despite these current weaknesses and long-term challenges, integrated as-
\par
sessment is necessary for responsible, informed policy-making on issues of
\par
global environmental change, and integrated assessment models are likely to
\par
play an increasing, perhaps essential, role in broader processes of integrated as-
\par
sessment. IA models can also make important contributions to the development
\par
of underlying knowledge. The field is progressing and can make substantial
\par
contributions if it avoids misunderstanding or overpromising what it can de-
\par
liver. Continued diverse activities, in multiple projects with diverse substantive
\par
and methodological emphases, with continued attempts to develop appropri-
\par
ately ecumenical but intellectually demanding professional and methodological
\par
standards, promise the most useful contribution. Early attempts to bring closure
\par
on a single focus, method, or approach should be resisted.
