\par
We present an uncertainty analysis conducted using CETA-R, a model in which the costs of climate
\par
change are specified as \textbf{\emph{Risks }}of large losses. In this analysis, we assume that three key parameters
\par
may each take on ``high'' or ``low'' values, leading to eight possible states of the world. We then
\par
explore optimal policies when the state of the world is known, and under uncertainty. Also, we
\par
estimate the benefits of resolving uncertainty earlier. We find that the optimal policy under uncer-
\par
tainty is similar to the policy that is optimal when each of the key parameters is at its low value.
\par
We also find that the value of immediate uncertainty resolution rises sharply as the alternative to
\par
immediate resolution \textbf{is }increasingly delayed resolution.
\par
