\par
value of information.
\par
\textbf{1. INTRODUCTION}
\par
Scientific predictions of global climate change
\par
brought on by emissions of C02 and other greenhouse
\par
gases have stimulated a number of efforts to develop
\par
``integrated assessment'' models of the climate change
\par
problem. These models typically combine very simple
\par
representations of the economic and climate systems,
\par
thereby providing a consistent framework for analyzing
\par
alternative policies that might be adopted to limit or slow
\par
climate change. Nordhaus's DICE rnodel`l) and our
\par
CETA model(2) are early examples of such integrated
\par
assessment models.
\par
In this paper, we present an uncertainty analysis
\par
conducted with a version of the CETA model, which we
\par
refer to \textbf{as }CETA-R.'3) CETA-R, like CETA, represents
\par
worldwide economic growth, energy consumption, en-
\par
ergy technology choice, global warming, \textbf{and }global
\par
warming costs. In CETA-R, however, warming costs are
