points in time. Based on these results, we identified the
\par
following three parameters as key: (1) the probability of
\par
loss, (2) the climate sensitivity, and (3) the utility dis-
\par
count rate.
\par
The first key parameter, the probability of loss, re-
\par
fers to the chance that a large welfare loss will occur in
\par
each time period as a result of climate change. In our
\par
sensitivity experiments, we consider two loss probability
\par
functions of temperature rise, the ``Median'' and ``Sci-
\par
entists'.'' These probability functions are derived from
\par
some expert survey results reported in NordhausJ6)
\par
Nordhaus surveyed about 20 experts in economics
\par
and the natural sciences. Among other things, the experts
\par
were asked to assess the probability of a `` `high-con-
\par
sequence outcome'---one defined as a lowering of global
\par
incomes by \emph{25\% }or more (the economic equivalent of
\par
the Great Depression),'' both for a 3{$^\circ$}C warming and a
\par
6{$^\circ$}C warming by 2090 (see Ref. 6, p. 47). We then used
\par
the probability assessments for these two warming sce-
\par
narios to fit a probability of loss hnction of temperature
\par
rise (see Ref. 3 for details). Since the experts who are
\par
natural scientists estimated probabilities which are
\par
roughly an order of magnitude greater than the median
\par
estimate for the group as a whole, we estimated two
\par
probability functions, the ``Median'' and ``Scientists' ''
\par
probabilities of loss.
\par
The second and third key parameters require less
\par
explanation. The climate sensitivity is simply the equi-
\par
librium change in average global temperature associated
\par
with a doubled atmospheric CO, concentration. The util-
\par
ity discount rate reflects a preference for increased wel-
\par
fare now relative to increased welfare in the future. This
\par
rate, together with the rate of growth of income per cap-
\par
ita, determine the money rate of discount, or interest
\par
rate. In CETA-R, the money rate of discount tends to be
\par
1.5-2\% above the utility discount rate. The discount rate
\par
is important in the context of global climate change be-
\par
cause the costs of reducing greenhouse gas emissions are
\par
incurred early, while the resulting benefits of reduced
\par
emissions are delayed by decades or centuries.
\par
Since the ``curse of dimensionality'' imposes prac-
\par
tical limits on the number of states of the world that we
\par
can consider in our analysis, we limit possible values for
\par
each of the three key parameters to twe-a low and a
\par
high case. We think of the low case as a most likely
\par
case, and assign it a subjective probability of \textbf{.8. }For the
\par
loss probability function, we identify the low and high
\par
cases with the probability functions calibrated to the Me-
\par
dian and Scientists' loss probability estimates, respec-
\par
tively. For the climate sensitivity, we set the low case
\par
value at 3{$^\circ$}C per doubled CO,, and we set the high case
\par
value at 4{$^\circ$}C. Last, for the utility discount rate, our low
\par
value is the Central case CETA-R assumption of a 3\%
\par
rate for 100 years, then a 2\% rate for 100 years, and,
\par
finally, a 1\% rate for the remainder of the time horizon.
\par
For the ``high'' value of this parameter, we reduce the
\par
utility discount rate by a factor of .67 for time periods
\par
after 2050 (note that this is different from our definition
\par
of high and low in Table I). The resulting eight states
\par
of the world and their associated probabilities are shown
\par
in Table IV.
