\par
and information tends to have higher value, in the later
\par
time frame. Thus the value of information now rises as
\par
the alternative is resolution after 2030 or 2050.
\par
The second reason that uncertainty resolution now
\par
has increasing value as the alternative is increasingly
\par
delayed resolution has to do with the dependence of tem-
\par
perature rise, and hence loss probability, on the stock of
\par
atmospheric CO,. Since loss probability depends on the
\par
stock of emissions, early emissions can be offset to some
\par
degree by extra emission reductions later on, provided
\par
one learns early enough that such \textbf{an }offset is desirable.
\par
But as the amval of new information is increasingly de-
\par
layed, offsetting earlier excessive emissions becomes
\par
more and more difficult. Thus, for this reason also, the
\par
benefit of immediate uncertainty resolution rises as the
\par
alternative is increasingly delayed resolution.
