\par
believe that it is this large effect on the optimal invest-
\par
ment policy that tends to increase the benefit of resolv-
\par
ing discount rate uncertainty, relative to the benefits of
\par
resolving the other uncertainties.
\par
\textbf{4. SUMMARY}
\par
In this paper, we have explored the implications of
\par
uncertainty about underlying parameter values in the
\par
context of the CETA-R model. CETA-R incorporates a
\par
novel approach to specifying the costs of global climate
\par
change in terms of large warming-related losses and the
\par
probabilities of those losses. Because of some strong as-
\par
sumptions we have made, it is possible in this model to
\par
obtain optimal policies under uncertainty about future
\par
warming-related losses, without having to explicitly rep-
\par
resent all states of the world that could arise under
\par
uncertainty about these losses.
\par
As a preliminary to specifying additional uncer-
\par
tainty about underlying parameter values in CETA-R,
\par
we first analyzed the sensitivity of optimal policies to
\par
variations in underlying parameter assumptions. Based
