\par
importance of including uncertainty in the analysis. Compared to an optimization
\par
with a single set of central parameter values, does inclusion of uncertainty
\par
generate different policy prescriptions? Do alternative policy instruments behave
\par
differently under uncertainty? If so, which instruments are better?
\par
If including uncertainty does lead to significantly different conclusions, it
\par
suggests that analyses which ignore uncertainty may be misleading. This is in fact
\par
the case: uncertainty encourages more stringent policy and leads to a preference
\par
for taxes over rate controls. Both of these consequences involve significant welfare
\par
gains.
\par
Many authors have made important contributions to the analysis of climate
\par
change policy under uncertainty but none have directly addressed how ignoring a
\par
realistic model of uncertainty might lead to significant policy errors. Instead, much
\par
of the best known work in this area has focused on the question of learning: what
\par
\v Z happens if uncertainty is resolved now rather than later Manne and Richels, 1992;
\par
Nordhaus, 1994b; Manne and Richels, 1995; Manne, 1996; Kelly and Kolstad,
\par
. 1996b; Nordhaus and Popp, 1997 . Other work has focused on sensitivity analysis:
\par
how do policy consequences andror optimal policy choice vary when underlying
\par
\v Z parameters change Dowlatabadi and Morgan, 1993; Nordhaus, 1994b;
\par
. \v Z . \v Z . Dowlatabadi, 1997; Nordhaus and Popp, 1997 . Nordhaus 1994b , Manne 1996
\par
\v Z . and Nordhaus and Popp 1997 touch on the consequences of uncertainty for
\par
policy stringency, but do so with stylized models of uncertainty comprised of five
\par
or fewer states of nature. Use of such stylized models of uncertainty can generate
\par
misleading conclusions.
\par
In this paper, an integrated climate--economy model based on optimal intertem-
\par
poral behavior is used to examine alternative policies under uncertainty. Several
\par
features distinguish this model from other climate--economy models. First, a fast
\par
simulation technique is used to permit thousands of different states of nature to be
\par
simulated simultaneously, each capturing optimal consumer behavior under differ-
\par
ent parameter realizations. Second, the distribution of the uncertain parameters is
\par
determined, where possible, from econometric estimates. Third, these states of
\par
nature---each of which exhibits different consumer preferences---are consistently
\par
aggregated using an explicit social welfare function.
\par
Each of these features is important given the focus on uncertainty. A large
\par
number of states is necessary to explore the realistic policy consequences of
\par
uncertainty in a non-linear model. Otherwise, important features of the uncertain
\par
parameter space may be concealed, biasing the results. 1 At the same time,
\par
intertemporal prices are likely to be wrong without a plausible model of intertem-
