\par
poral consumer behavior. Such prices are essential for aggregating consequences
\par
over long time horizons. Meanwhile, econometric estimates of economic parame-
\par
ter distributions are important to capture well-known parameter relations and their
\par
associated uncertainty. In order to be consistent with historic interest rates, for
\par
example, time preference and intertemporal substitutability must be positively
\par
correlated. By how much and with what degree of certainty is a question best
\par
addressed by data. Finally, without a consistent method of aggregating over
\par
uncertain preferences, policy evaluation may be inadvertently skewed toward one
\par
set of preferences. Simple averaging of utility functions with different parameteri-
\par
zations, for example, can create an unintended and undesirable weighting scheme
\par
as discussed later in the paper.
\par
The results of this exercise indicate that uncertainty does in fact matter.
\par
Ignoring it leads to policy recommendations which are too slack. Initially, the
\par
policies themselves are fairly close: the control rates in 1995 are 8.4\% and 7.9\%
\par
with and without uncertainty, respectively. Over time, however, these rates diverge
\par
to a difference of 20\% in 30 years and over 50\% after 80 years. In welfare terms,
\par
the policy ignoring uncertainty generates a US\$58 per capita consumption gain vs.
\par
US\$73 when uncertainty is considered.
\par
A related observation is that half of this observed increase in stringency can be
\par
\v Z reproduced by modeling preferences as the sole source of uncertainty vs. technol-
\par
. ogy, growth, climate or cost parameters . This follows from the estimated distribu-
\par
tion of risk aversion and time preference parameters which indicates that discount
\par
rates in the future may be close to zero in the presence of a slowdown in
\par
productivity growth. For an issue like climate change where the benefits from
\par
policy decisions in one period are spread out over a long horizon, yet costs are
\par
incurred immediately, small changes in a discount rate near zero lead to large,
\par
asymmetrical swings in the desired level of stringency. This is analogous to small
\par
changes in the interest rate producing much larger changes in bond prices.
\par
Finally, we observe that taxes are more efficient under uncertainty than rate
\par
controls. While the optimal rate control generates welfare gains equivalent to a
\par
US\$73 increase in current per capita consumption, the optimal tax policy generates
\par
an US\$86 increase. The intuition for this result is that the damage function is
\par
relatively flat and that damages are likely to be negatively correlated with costs.
\par
Both of these features lead to a preference for price controls, as discussed by
\par
\v Z . Weitzman 1974 .
\par
In order to understand the assumptions underlying these results, details of the
\par
model are first laid out in Section 2. This includes a discussion of the intertempo-
\par
ral model of the economy and climate, the aggregation of states into one welfare
\par
measure, and the quantification of uncertainty. Section 3 examines the question of
\par
optimal stringency under uncertainty. In particular, do models with and without
\par
uncertainty recommend the same rate of emission reductions? Section 4 explores
\par
the difference in efficiency between price and rate instruments as mechanisms for
\par
regulating carbon dioxide emissions. Section 5 concludes.258
