\par
\textbf{2. Model}
\par
\emph{2.1. O}\~O\emph{er}\~O\emph{iew}
\par
The purpose of the model is to translate a climate change policy---either a tax
\par
or control rate---into a meaningful welfare measure of its consequences. This
\par
\v Z . welfare measure, in turn, has two purposes: 1 to facilitate the calculation of
\par
optimal policies by maximization over the parameters of a particular policy, and
\par
\v Z . 2 to provide a meaningful metric for comparing alternative kinds of policies.
\par
Such alternatives might involve taxes vs. quantity controls, or policies which
\par
consider uncertainty vs. those which ignore it.
\par
Computing the welfare associated with a given policy can be viewed as a
\par
three-step process. First, in any given state of nature, we need to determine how
\par
the economy and climate will evolve in the presence of the policy. This requires a
\par
model of consumer and producer behavior, a climate model, and links between the
\par
two. Second, a mechanism for comparing outcomes across states must be devel-
\par
oped. Since preferences vary from state to state, this requires assumptions about
\par
the interpersonal comparability of utility among representative agents existing in
\par
different states of nature. Finally, uncertainty must be quantified in a reasonable
\par
way.
\par
This paper approaches the first step by using a modified stochastic growth
\par
model to capture optimal consumer behavior in each state. Standard stochastic
\par
growth models develop optimal decision rules to automatically choose the correct
\par
consumption level each period in the face of random shocks to productivity. 2
\par
These models typically assume that the distribution of random shocks is exoge-
\par
nous and remains the same over time. Here, the trend in productivity declines over
\par
time and endogenously depends on climate change. Although these distinctions are
\par
ignored by the representative consumer, the approach continues to predict optimal
\par
consumption levels. 3 This technique eliminates an important obstacle to optimal
\par
policy analysis under uncertainty. Properly valuing policies over long horizons
\par
requires an optimal allocation of consumption across time to correctly determine
\par
intertemporal prices. Yet, determining the optimal consumption path is usually
\par
computationally intensive, making it difficult if not impossible to consider a large
\par
number of uncertain outcomes. This approach solves the problem by using an
\par
analytically derived optimal consumption rule to rapidly determine the correct path
\par
of consumption and intertemporal prices.
\par
2 For a recent summary of the solution techniques for stochastic growth models, see Taylor and
\par
\v Z . Uhlig 1990 .
\par
3 The productivity slowdown and economy--climate links are based on the DICE model of Nordhaus
\par
\v Z . 1994b . The fact that the consumer ignores these features has a negligible impact on the resulting path
\par
\v Z . of consumption because the productivity slowdown and climate consequences e.g., loss of output
\par
occur quite gradually in the DICE model.\emph{( )}
\par
