\par
The second step develops a mechanism for aggregating the results over many
\par
states into a single welfare measure. Based on the concept of cardinal unit
\par
comparability among suitably scaled utility measures in each state, social welfare
\par
is computed as an arithmetic average. This approach requires a subjective choice
\par
of utility scaling and eliminates the possibility of certain distributional considera-
\par
tions. While such choices can be discomforting, the process highlights a point
\par
which has been ignored in much of the discussion of climate change: uncertainty
\par
about consumer andror social preferences cannot be addressed without simultane-
\par
ously addressing the question of how to properly aggregate over those preferences.
\par
In the final step, uncertainty is quantified two ways. Economic uncertainty
\par
describing consumer preferences and technology is based on an econometric
\par
analysis of historical data. Climate, cost, damage and long-term trend uncertainty
\par
is based on discrete distributions developed in previous work.
