\emph{2.3. Social welfare}
\par
A distinguishing feature of this analysis is the use of an econometrically
\par
estimated parameter distribution describing uncertainty in the economic model.
\par
\v Z . However, the consumer's objective function given by Eq. 1 makes no allowance
\par
for uncertainty about the preference parameters r and t which are fixed from his
\par
or her perspective. In order to encompass uncertainty about preferences, it is
\par
necessary to step back and imagine a social planner who would like to maximize
\par
\v Z . the objective given in Eq. 1 but is unsure of the parameters. Since a policy
\par
change which raises the expected utility for one set of parameters may lower the
\par
expected utility for another set, the social planner will need to specify a social
\par
welfare function to compare such gains and losses. This social welfare function
\par
provides a single objective specifying how changes in utility measured with
\par
different preferences are aggregated. It is important to recognize that although
\par
parameter values in the representative agent model can be inferred from observed
\par
consumer behavior, there is no information available to estimate parameters in a
\par
social welfare function. Such information would be revealed only by observing the
\par
behavior of an actual social planner. Instead, we must rely on social choice theory
\par
and our own sense of fairness to specify the relation.
\par
It is useful to note that the common approach in the climate change literature
\par
avoids the issue of preference aggregation by reporting a range of policy prescrip-
\par
tions based on a range of possible preferences and states of nature. For example,
\par
\v Z . \v Z . Cline 1992 Tables 7.3 and 7.4 presents benefit--cost analyses for 92 different
\par
\v Z . cases. Dowlatabadi and Morgan 1993 integrate out much of the uncertainty in
\par
their analysis, but still present results for 48 scenarios. Chap. 8 of Nordhaus
\par
\v Z . 1994b gives one of the few examples where even preference uncertainty is
\par
integrated out, yielding a single welfare metric and a single policy recommenda-
\par
\v Z . tion. In a similar analysis, however, Nordhaus and Popp 1997 choose to fix
\par
preferences because of the difficulties with preference aggregation. Regardless,
\par
these authors ubiquitously observe that uncertainty about time preference has large266
\par
