\textbf{3. Does uncertainty matter?}
\par
To understand whether uncertainty significantly affects decision making and
\par
welfare consequences, two optimizations are performed. The first looks for the
\par
optimal CO control rates based on the model developed in Section 2 and the 2
\par
parameter distributions given in Tables 1 and 2. 14 The second assumes there is no
\par
uncertainty and again looks for optimal control rates. All uncertain parameters are
\par
fixed at their median values and the variance of the random exogenous growth
\par
\v Z . shocks is set to zero as indicated by the narrow bars in Tables 1 and 2 . There is
\par
only one state of nature.
\par
\emph{3.1. Policy stringency}
\par
The resulting policies are shown in Fig. 2. The initial difference between
\par
policies is small, 7.9\% ignoring uncertainty vs. 8.6\% with uncertainty included. In
\par
30 years, these differences are higher, 10.4\% vs. 12.8\%, and by the end of the next
\par
century, the rate is almost doubled when uncertainty is considered. Uncertainty
\par
clearly has a dramatic effect on the future path of the optimal control rate, but with
\par
a relatively small consequence for immediate decisions.
\par
What explains this pattern of small policy consequences now and large ones
\par
later? Surprisingly, roughly half of the observed consequences can be traced to
\par
uncertainty about future discounting, rather than climate or other economic
\par
uncertainty. This can be seen by reoptimizing the policy for a model with only
\par
pure time preference r and risk aversion t uncertain, as illustrated in Fig. 3. In
\par
contrast, a reoptimized policy based on modeling only the climate, cost and
\par
\v Z .
\par
damage parameters g, \emph{D }, \emph{b }, b, and 4.1rl as uncertain yields essentially the
\par
s 0 1
\par
same results as completely ignoring uncertainty.
\par
It should be noted that these conclusions do not contradict those studies which
\par
\v Z
\par
ascribe a high value to \emph{learning }about climate parameters Peck and Teisberg,
\par
. 1993; Lempert et al., 1996; Nordhaus and Popp, 1997 . Learning that damages are
\par
zero, for example, would lead to the abandonment of any abatement policy and
\par
result in a large savings even in this model. The results in Fig. 3 instead suggest
\par
that in the absence of learning---which realistically may not occur for many years
\par
---the prudent course does not depend on the \emph{range }of these parameters, but rather
\par
the central tendency.
\par
Intuitively, Fig. 3 simply highlights non-linearities in the first-order conditions
\par
generating the optimal policy. When these first-order conditions are linear in the
\par
uncertain parameters, replacing uncertain parameters with central estimates has
\par
little effect, as occurs with the climate, damage and cost parameters. As it turns
