\textbf{5. Conclusion}
\par
This paper has sought to inject a dose of skepticism into current discussions of
\par
climate change policy, especially regarding the limitations of models which ignore
\par
uncertainty. Excluding uncertainty tends to reduce expected marginal benefits due
\par
to non-linear relations, in turn leading to policy recommendations which are too
\par
lax. Much of this effect can be related to agent preferences and their implications
\par
for future discount rates. Indeed, over half the noted consequences is replicated
\par
with preferences as the sole source of uncertainty. This raises the issue of how to
\par
appropriately aggregate over preferences, a question which is at once both
\par
controversial but necessary. Finally, uncertainty about costs introduces a di-
\par
chotomy between alternative policy instruments. In the case of carbon dioxide
\par
emissions, taxes perform better than rate controls. This preference arises because
\par
\v Z . marginal damages are relatively flat as well as potentially correlated negatively
\par
with marginal costs. The welfare gain associated with the optimal tax instrument is
\par
US\$86 per person compared to only US\$73 for the optimal rate instrument. An
\par
optimal rate policy computed in the absence of uncertainty leads to a welfare gain
\par
of only US\$58.
\par
The results of this analysis should be taken with the same caveats accorded
\par
other integrated assessment models. There is still considerable dependence on
\par
functional forms, specification and parameterization. However, the nature of the
\par
conclusions---that uncertainty matters---would only be amplified by correcting
\par
these omissions. Further, there are several important features that distinguish this
\par
effort from other integrated assessments. This is the first dynamic general equilib-
\par
rium model to incorporate a large number of uncertain states of nature along with
\par
the capacity to search for optimal policies. The procedure also takes advantage of
\par
econometrically quantified uncertainty where possible. Lastly, the model explicitly
\par
addresses the issue of aggregation over uncertain preferences.
\par
The most important point of this paper may be to encourage a reassessment of
\par
the current agenda for climate change research. Considerable effort has been
\par
directed towards the importance of learning. Perhaps more effort should be
\par
directed at the consequences of uncertainty itself---without learning. A great deal
\par
of attention has focused on climate uncertainty. This paper suggests that important
\par
uncertainty surrounds the economy. In particular, the course of productivity
\par
growth has an important bearing on both future emissions and the valuation of
\par
future benefits via the interest rate. Finally, much of the current policy discussion
\par
is focused on quantity controls. This paper suggests that taxes have more desirable
\par
efficiency properties. To the extent that we are interested in policies which
\par
improve welfare, these questions deserve further scrutiny.\emph{( )}
