@article{reckhowWaterQualitySimulation1994,
  title = {Water Quality Simulation Modeling and Uncertainty Analysis for Risk Assessment and Decision Making},
  author = {Reckhow, Kenneth H.},
  year = 1994,
  journal = {Ecological Modelling},
  volume = {72},
  number = {1-2},
  pages = {1--20},
  publisher = {Elsevier},
  doi = {10.1016/0304-3800(94)90143-0},
  langid = {english},
  note = {\emph{Ecological Modelling,} 72(1994) 1-20
\par
Elsevier Science B.V., Amsterdam
\par
Water quality simulation modeling and uncertainty
\par
analysis for risk assessment and decision making
\par
Kenneth H. Reckhow
\par
\emph{Center for the Analysis and Prediction of River Basin Environmental Systems (CARES)}
\par
\emph{Nicholas School of the Environment and Earth Sciences}
\par
\emph{Duke University, Durham, NC 27708-0328, USA}
\par
(Received 20 August 1992; accepted 16 March 1993)
\par
\textbf{ABSTRACT}
\par
The usefulness of water quality simulation models for environmental management is
\par
explored with a focus on prediction uncertainty. Ecological risk and environmental analysis
\par
often involve scientific assessments that are highly uncertain. Still, environmental manage-
\par
ment decisions are being made, often with the support of a mathematical simulation model.
\par
In the area of pollutant transport and fate in surface waters, few of the extant simulation
\par
models have been rigorously evaluated. Limited observational data and limited scientific
\par
knowledge are often incompatible with the highly-detailed model structures of the large
\par
pollutant transport and fate models. Two examples are presented to illustrate data and
\par
knowledge weaknesses that are likely to undermine these large models for decision support.
\par
An alternative to comprehensive structured simulation models is proposed as a flexible
\par
approach to introduce science into the environmental risk assessment and decision making
\par
process.
\par
\textbf{1. INTRODUCTION}
\par
The recent interest in ecological risk assessment in the United States,
\par
and in particular in the US Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), has
\par
led to the realization that scientific uncertainties must be given more
\par
attention than in current practice. Indeed, the USEPA has recently recom-
\par
mended that uncertainty analyses be a routine part of ecological risk
\par
assessments (Risk Assessment Forum, 1992). This includes risk assessments
\par
that are proposed to make use of detailed pollutant transport and fate
\par
models.
\par
Certainly the new emphasis on uncertainty analysis in environmental
\par
assessments can be viewed as a positive development. In environmental
\par
decision making, there are compelling reasons to always conduct uncer-
\par
tainty analyses, to avoid the mistaken impression that assessments are
\par
precise and well-understood:
\par
0304-3800/94/\$07.00 \copyright{} 1994 - Elsevier Science B.V. All rights reserved
\par
\emph{SSDI} 0304-3800(93)E0030-7K.H. RECKHOW 2
\par
1. Environmental managers need to know the expected uncertainty in the
\par
assessed response. This should be an a priori estimate so that managers
\par
or decision makers may opt for another (more precisely-assessed) end-
\par
point, may consider the value of additional experimentation or monitor-
\par
ing, and/or may hedge decisions away from large losses.
\par
2. EPA, Congress, the public, etc. need to know the extent of critical scientific
\par
uncertainties in environmental assessments, so that future research funding is obtained
\par
and properly focused.
\par
Scientific uncertainty is present in all ecological simulation modeling and
\par
risk assessments. Uncertainty does not prevent management and decision
\par
making; rather, it provides a basis for selecting among alternative actions
\par
and for deciding if (and what) additional information (experimentation
\par
and/or observation) is needed (Morgan and Henrion, 1990; Reckhow,
\par
1994). Uncertainty can be used in this way because the magnitude of the
\par
uncertainty provides a measure of value of information: the smaller the
\par
uncertainty, the more confident (and valuable) is the assessment. Not only
\par
does this hold for formal environmental assessments, but it also reflects our
\par
day-to-day reasoning and decision making. Perhaps the best example of this
\par
is the response to probabilistic forecasts of rain. Many of us adjust our
\par
outdoor plans on the basis of these probabilities. A forecast of "it will rain"
\par
or "it will not rain" is unsatisfactory; we like to know the odds and act
\par
according to our attitude toward risk. This same reasoning should apply to
\par
uncertainty in environmental assessment and decision making.
\par
However, for these and other advantages of uncertainty analysis to be
\par
realized, the analysis of uncertainty must be complete. That is, \emph{all} scientific
\par
uncertainties in an ecological risk assessment or in pollutant transport and
\par
fate modeling must be estimated and included in the analysis. Failure to be
\par
complete can result in decisions that are not only far from optimal but are
\par
far from satisfactory in outcome. This issue is of immediate concern
\par
because some of the models proposed for ecological risk assessments are
\par
large mechanistic models which have yet to be subjected to thorough
\par
uncertainty analyses. Further, the complexity and size of these models,
\par
coupled with the limited observational data bases that are usually available
\par
to support modeling efforts, may make it unlikely that a thorough uncer-
\par
tainty analysis will be conducted.
\par
The objective of this paper is to examine the particular topic of surface
\par
water transport and fate modeling for environmental risk assessment and
\par
decision support under uncertainty. The examination continues from this
\par
introductory discussion of uncertainty analysis to a review of extant trans-
\par
port and fate models. Difficulties encountered in uncertainty analyses on
\par
existing models are discussed using examples from the literature. Two case
\par
studies are then presented illustrating knowledge and data weaknesses thatK.H. RECKHOW 3
\par
undermine mechanistic transport and fate models. The paper concludes
\par
with a proposal for model selection in ecological risk assessments, in
\par
consideration of the need for uncertainty analysis.
\par
\textbf{2. WATER QUALITY TRANSPORT AND FATE MODELS}
\par
In the previous section, the meaning and importance of uncertainty in
\par
risk assessment and decision making are discussed. With that foundation,
\par
much of the remainder of this paper is devoted to an examination of
\par
surface water transport and fate models as methods for scientific analysis
\par
and prediction. Throughout the following sections, uncertainty in knowl-
\par
edge, data, and ultimately, predictions is considered as a key criterion for
\par
the usefulness of models as methods for assessment and decision making.
\par
Water quality simulation models of pollutant transport and fate are
\par
usually categorized as either mechanistic or empirical. Mechanistic models
\par
(J0rgensen, 1980; Chapra and Reckhow, 1983; Thomann and Mueller,
\par
1987; Ambrose et al., 1988) are based on the modeler's expression of
\par
theory as one or more mathematical equations describing transport and
\par
fate. The mechanistic model is intended to be a correct, although simpli-
\par
fied, description of the system of interest. In contrast, an empirical water
\par
quality model is based on a statistical summary of observational data. The
\par
empirical modeler often uses the observational data to guide in the
\par
selection of the model specification and to provide estimates of parame-
\par
ters. In empirical models, correct theoretical description is of secondary
\par
concern to parameter estimation.
\par
The conventional approach for the determination of parameters for
\par
mechanistic water quality simulation models is for the modeler to use his
\par
judgment in selecting parameters that are consistent with any available
\par
data as well as with tabulations of accepted coefficient values (e.g., Schnoor
\par
et al., 1987). Parameters are often selected in a sequential manner (perhaps
\par
based on a sensitivity analysis) and the model is finally judged adequate
\par
based on a visual comparison of predictions and observations. Formal
\par
mathematical optimization is not usually involved.
\par
Parameter estimation in empirical water quality models has traditionally
\par
been undertaken using classical optimization methods such as maximum
\par
likelihood or least squares. Judgment is typically involved in the specifica-
\par
tion of the model but not in the actual estimation algorithm.
\par
For the mechanistic models, the governing philosophy is that correct
\par
description of processes in the models enhances the prospect for successful
\par
application of the models to previously unmodeled systems and for extrapo-
\par
lation of the models beyond the bounds of previous application. In addi-
\par
tion, greater mechanistic detail should increase the chance that the modelK.H. RECKHOW 4
\par
describes features of the problem that are of direct management concern.
\par
In theory, this philosophy yields versatile models that may be applied to a
\par
range of problems without major adjustments.
\par
Until recently, none of the mechanistic pollutant transport and fate
\par
models supported by the USEPA included uncertainty analysis. This
\par
changed with QUAL2E-UNCAS (Brown and Barnwell, 1987), which is a
\par
significant contribution by EPA both as simulation model with uncertainty
\par
analysis and as a technical guidance document on the importance and
\par
interpretation of uncertainties. QUAL2E-UNCAS allows the user to select
\par
either first-order error analysis or Monte Carlo simulation for error propa-
\par
gation. The current version of the procedure does not include covariance
\par
terms or model equation error; unfortunately, this can result in prediction
\par
error estimates that severely misrepresent overall uncertainty as noted by
\par
Di Toro and van Straten (1979).
\par
One difficulty encountered by Brown and Barnwell in QUAL2E-UN-
\par
CAS is the limited amount of evidence on parameter error terms reported
\par
in the literature. In addition, it is not uncommon that, for the waterbody
\par
being modeled, there is only a small amount of observational data available
\par
to aid parameter selection, and even fewer data are typically left for model
\par
testing and evaluation. As a consequence, opportunities to rigorously assess
\par
success of these models in prediction have been rare and not necessarily
\par
meaningful.
\par
To date, limited survey and anecdotal evidence suggest the following
\par
conclusions concerning the value of large mechanistic transport and fate
\par
modeling for surface water quality management:
\par
1. Scientists and engineers at EPA, and others in the model development
\par
community (primarily at universities and at research laboratories), be-
\par
lieve that these models are useful for water quality management. Among
\par
the reasons to support this perspective are the numbers and affiliations
\par
of attendees at EPA shortcourses, and the continued federal financial
\par
support for these models.
\par
2. Scientists and engineers at state and regional agencies (who are the
\par
most common users of water quality simulation models) use the large
\par
mechanistic models infrequently for water quality management plan-
\par
ning, with the exception of QUAL2E. Most of the routine modeling
\par
work (e.g., wasteload allocation studies) undertaken by these individuals
\par
is done using relatively simple models. Among the reasons for lack of
\par
use are that dollars and staff time are insufficient to support this level of
\par
modeling effort and skepticism among this group of model users that the
\par
large mechanistic models are reliable.
\par
3. Many managers and decision makers without technical training in water
\par
quality modeling tend at first to favor use of a large mechanistic modelK.H. RECKHOW 5
\par
for management decisions in the belief that it will yield more accurate,
\par
detailed predictions. Cost of application and difficulty in understanding
\par
the model and results appear to have ultimately led to a mixed view
\par
among members of this group.
\par
4. Successful case studies, in which a mechanistic surface water quality
\par
model other than QUAL2E has aided decision making (and yielded
\par
predictions that have been shown to be fairly accurate) appear to be
\par
rare.
\par
\textbf{3. HOW "GOOD" ARE EXTANT WATER QUALITY MODELS?}
\par
There appear to be few essentially complete error analyses for predictive
\par
applications of surface water quality transport and fate models. The re-
\par
quirements for completeness adopted here are:
\par
1. an estimate of error is chosen for all uncertain model terms, including
\par
inputs, initial conditions, and parameters, and covariances are included
\par
where appropriate;
\par
2. an estimate of model equation error is determined.
\par
These error components are then combined using an error propagation
\par
method such as Monte Carlo simulation or first-order error analysis. An
\par
alternative approach involves:
\par
3. model predictions compared to observations in a predictive scenario.
\par
For this approach to be acceptable, the model must be calibrated on a data
\par
set that is different from the data used for the predictive goodness-of-fit
\par
assessment. Being "different" does not mean simply different years of
\par
observation, as it is possible that essentially identical conditions will be
\par
observed each year. Rather, it means that the forcing functions and/or
\par
response are/ is different, and the degree of difference is assessed statisti-
\par
cally and reported along with the goodness-of-fit results.
\par
To make the error analysis feasible, minor error terms or errors that are
\par
found to have a negligible effect on predictions (perhaps due to insensitiv-
\par
ity) can be ignored. In addition, error distribution shape may be difficult to
\par
characterize with confidence.
\par
As noted above, few models come close to meeting these requirements.
\par
Among empirical models, only the steady-state nutrient input-output mod-
\par
els have undergone reasonably thorough error analyses. For example,
\par
Reckhow and Chapra (1979) and Reckhow et al. (1992) report prediction
\par
error (based on leave-one-out cross-validation) of approximately 30\%-40\%
\par
for cross-sectional models that predict average growing season total phos-
\par
phorus or total nitrogen concentration based on measured annual loading,
\par
Prediction errors are apt to be higher for applications based on estimated
\par
or predicted loading. Prediction error will be higher still when these simpleK.H. RECKHOW 6
\par
models are linked to statistical models to predict chlorophyll \emph{a} or Secchi
\par
disk transparency.
\par
Most error analyses conducted on mechanistic water quality models have
\par
also focused on eutrophication. For example, Scavia et al. (1981) applied
\par
error propagation methods to model several trophic state variables in
\par
Saginaw Bay, Lake Huron. In the uncertainty analysis, they included error
\par
terms for: (1) seven of the 22 parameters, (2) all of the eight initial
\par
conditions, (3) all of the five nutrient loads, and (4) the mixing parameter at
\par
the boundary between inner and outer Saginaw Bay segments. The remain-
\par
ing 15 (of 22) parameters and the model equations were assumed known
\par
without error. Results of this analysis indicate that, for the eight state
\par
variables (phytoplankton, herbivores, organic-N, ammonia, nitrate-nitrite,
\par
organic-P, PO4, and carnivores), the summer spatially-averaged daily coef-
\par
ficients of variation (CV) range from 33\% (for organic-N) to 407\% (for
\par
nitrate-nitrite), with maximum CVs of 148\% to 772\%.
\par
Scavia et al. did not include covariances in their error propagation study.
\par
In perhaps the most thorough study of parameter error involving covari-
\par
ances, Di Toro and van Straten (1979), and van Straten (1983), used
\par
maximum likelihood to determine point estimates and covariances for
\par
parameters in a seasonal phytoplankton model for Lake Ontario. Among
\par
the areas of investigation in these studies were the effect of unobserved
\par
state variables (no data on selected variables) on parameter errors, the
\par
importance of parameter covariance terms, and the consequences of fixing
\par
selected parameters at constant values.
\par
Of particular note, Di Toro and van Straten found that prediction error
\par
decreased substantially when parameter covariances were included in error
\par
propagation, underscoring the importance of including covariance terms in
\par
error analyses. This result occurred because, while individual parameters
\par
might be highly uncertain, specific \emph{pairs} of parameters (e.g., the half-
\par
saturation constant and the maximum growth rate in the Michaelis-Menten
\par
model) may vary in a predictable way (expressed through covariance), and
\par
thus may be \emph{collectively} less uncertain. For the error propagation study for
\par
the six nutrient and phytoplankton state variables, Di Toro and van Straten
\par
found the prediction coefficient of variation to range from 8\% (for nitrate-
\par
N) to 390\% (for ammonia-N), with half of the values falling between 44\%
\par
and 91\%. Zooplankton prediction errors tended to be much higher.
\par
In a study of uncertainty in modeling transport and fate for synthetic
\par
organics, Halfon (1984) used a mechanistic model to predict mirex behavior
\par
in Lake Ontario. Halfon noted the inadequacy of existing data to specify
\par
certain model functions and estimate particular model terms, and he
\par
attempted to assess the sensitivity of the model to uncertain parameters
\par
and look at prediction error. This analysis was done: (1) with the assump-K.H. RECKHOW 7
\par
tion that most parameters were known to within \textpm 20\%, (2) without a term
\par
for model error (Halfon did identify problems with the model), and (3) with
\par
no measurements of mirex loading (loadings were "back-calculated").
\par
Halfon set the \textpm 20\% parameter error level based on his sense of the
\par
literature, and used it as the full range (max-min) on the uncertainty in the
\par
parameters. This seems low (given natural variability, measurement error,
\par
and bias due to model mis-specification) for many of the model terms,
\par
particularly when used as an estimate of range. Unfortunately, Halfon's
\par
analysis and thoughtful discussion are constrained by data and model
\par
limitations, many of which he identifies.
\par
Additional results presented by Beck (1987) in his thoughtful and thor-
\par
ough review paper indicate that the error levels cited in these studies are
\par
typical of those reported elsewhere. He concludes "Most of the evidence
\par
suggests that the current models of water quality, in particular, the larger
\par
models, are easily capable of generating predictions to which little confi-
\par
dence would be attached." Yet despite this view, there appears to be the
\par
belief among many modelers that since the world is complicated, so too
\par
must be our simulation models if they are to be accurate.
\par
In a previous section, models were characterized as either mechanistic or
\par
empirical. In truth, simulation models exist on a continuum on a mechanis-
\par
tic (or empirical) axis, and the degree of mechanism attributed to a given
\par
model is apt to vary among individuals and among disciplines. For example,
\par
among water quality simulation modelers, WASP4 (Ambrose et al., 1988) is
\par
universally considered to be a mechanistic model. However, environmental
\par
chemists and aquatic biologists are apt to view the mathematics for the
\par
individual mechanisms as simplifications of the actual processes, and often
\par
as empirical simplifications, particularly when first-order reactions are
\par
employed. This does not mean that the simplifications are inappropriate.
\par
Rather, it means that "correct process description" should not be cited as
\par
the basis for selection of a "mechanistic" model.
\par
Beck's conclusions concerning the problems with prediction error magni-
\par
tude for the larger models raises interesting questions. Given current
\par
knowledge and available data, at what point does the level of detail in the
\par
model exceed the modeler's ability to produce an acceptable model? Can
\par
we model the transport and fate of every organism in a waterbody? Can we
\par
model the transport and fate of each species of phytoplankton in a
\par
waterbody? Can we model the transport and fate of major phytoplankton
\par
groups (e.g., greens, blue-greens, diatoms) in a waterbody? Can we model
\par
the transport and fate of overall phytoplankton biomass in a waterbody? A
\par
similar series of questions could be posed about space/time detail in a
\par
simulation model.
\par
An interesting analogy to this is found in physics in the area of statisticalK.H. RECKHOW 8
\par
mechanics. Physicists have recognized that, although they have an under-
\par
standing of the theory and general agreement on the equations of motion.
\par
the movement of molecules or particles in gases or fluids is most effectively
\par
modeled by describing aggregate behavior, not by modeling individual
\par
molecules. Probability density functions are therefore used to define aver-
\par
age response and variability in that response. Reif (1965) makes the key
\par
point quite effectively in the introductory chapter of his text on statistical
\par
and thermal physics:
\par
'This book will be devoted to a discussion of systems
\par
consisting of very many particles... For, although it might be possible to
\par
write down the equations of motion for any one of these systems, the
\par
complexity of a system containing many particles is so great that it may
\par
make the task of deducing any useful consequences or predictions almost
\par
hopeless. The difficulties involved are not just questions of quantitative
\par
detail which can be solved by the brute force application of bigger and
\par
better computers. Indeed, even if the interactions between individual
\par
particles are rather simple, the shear complexity arising from the interac-
\par
tion of a large number of them can often give rise to quite unexpected
\par
\emph{qualitative} features in the behavior of a system. It may require very deep
\par
analysis to predict the occurrence of these features from a knowledge of
\par
the individual particles... The task of understanding systems of many
\par
particles is thus far from trivial, even when the interactions between
\par
individual atoms are well known. Nor is the problem just one of carrying
\par
out complicated computations... When the systems under consideration
\par
are not too complex and when the desired level of description is not too
\par
detailed, considerable progress can indeed be achieved by relatively simple
\par
methods of analysis.'
\par
This analogy is not intended as a recommendation that only highly
\par
aggregated, statistical behavior is the appropriate level of detail for water
\par
quality models. Rather, it is intended to support a call for thinking about
\par
the level of detail in a model as a compromise between what is desirable
\par
(what would we like to know?) and what is feasible (what is the best we can
\par
do, given current knowledge and available data?). This thought is ad-
\par
dressed again below.
\par
\textbf{4. PARAMETER DETERMINATION IN MECHANISTIC MODELS}
\par
Mechanistic surface water quality models have been developed by scien-
\par
tists and engineers as mathematical descriptions of hydrologic and ecologic
\par
processes. Mechanistic modelers have tended to concentrate on the mathe-
\par
matical expression of theory, probably as a consequence of: (1) scientific
\par
interest and challenge, (2) a belief that the theory was reasonably well-un-
\par
derstood and that this understanding could be expressed mathematically,K.H. RECKHOW 9
\par
(3) limited available data to fit and evaluate models, and (4) limited
\par
resources to collect additional data. For these reasons, model coefficients
\par
and reaction rates in the models generally characterize actual processes
\par
and are not (at least initially) intended to be empirically-fitted constants.
\par
Since the parameters of mechanistic models are intended to describe
\par
real processes, it may be assumed that an experimental study of a particu-
\par
lar process can yield a parameter estimate that can be directly inserted into
\par
the model. In some cases, it is acknowledged that the rate or coefficient in
\par
the model is affected by other conditions in the waterbody (e.g., turbu-
\par
lence), and thus adjustments must be made to the experimentally-based
\par
value. However, if the model truly is a complete mechanistic description of
\par
the system of interest, then adjustment should be unnecessary. This view is
\par
reflected in Halfon's (1984) statement concerning EXAMS (Burns and
\par
Cline, 1985): "Since the EXAMS model is based on documented formulas,
\par
it does not require calibration."
\par
However, given the relative simplicity of all simulation models in com-
\par
parison to the complexity of nature, it seems reasonable to question the
\par
legitimacy of any "mechanistic" mathematical description of surface water
\par
quality. Further, given data limitations and scientific knowledge limitations,
\par
it seems reasonable to question even the goal to strive for a model that
\par
need not be calibrated. The correctness of model structure, the knowledge
\par
of the model user, and the availability of experimental and observational
\par
evidence all influence parameter choice for mechanistic models. Unfortu-
\par
nately, too often knowledge and data are extremely limited, making choice
\par
of parameters and choice of important model processes guesswork to a
\par
distressingly large degree. The examples presented in the next two sections
\par
are not re-assuring with respect to these two issues: (1) scientific support
\par
for the selection of model parameters, and (2) scientific support for the
\par
specification of appropriate model functional relationships.
\par
