That information is typically the extent of technical guidance employed
\par
by modelers when selecting this parameter using a reference like Table 1
\par
from Bowie et al. (1985). The range of options in Table 1 is substantial,
\par
even within a single category (e.g., diatoms) for algal type. The algal cell
\par
size, shape, and other features mentioned in the previous paragraph can
\par
vary from species to species within a single type category, so this may be
\par
responsible for some of the variability in Table 1. However, even if the
\par
modeler who must choose a point estimate has data that identify dominant
\par
species in a water body at a particular time and location, dominance is apt
\par
to change with time and location. Further, models contain at most only a
\par
few distinct phytoplankton compartments, so a choice must still be made
\par
concerning species to be modeled and their characteristics.
\par
Examination of the original references from which Table 1 was created
\par
does little to enlighten the parameter selection process. Most of the
\par
references summarized in Table 1 do not present observational studies onK.H. RECKHOW 12
\par
phytoplankton; rather, they are simulation model studies, and the value for
\par
phytoplankton settling velocity listed in Table 1 is the value chosen for the
\par
model. In some of the references checked, little or no basis was provided
\par
for the choice. When a rationale for choice was given, it was usually to
\par
adopt or adjust the few values presented in the literature from experimen-
\par
tal studies, or to adopt a value from another modeling study. In one way or
\par
another, it appears that virtually all of the values presented in Table 1 have
\par
some dependency on the early experimental work of Smayda and Boleyn
\par
(1965) and other work by Smayda.
\par
Unfortunately, evaluation studies of simulation models have provided
\par
little insight on good point estimates for this parameter. Observational data
\par
on surface water quality are almost always inadequate for testing functional
\par
relationships and assessing parameter choices. Typical observational data
\par
sets are noisy, with few measurements of each of only a few variables. In
\par
the case of phytoplankton settling velocity, observational data are apt to
\par
consist of phytoplankton cell densities at various dates, times, and areal
\par
locations, but probably not depths. Since phytoplankton are also removed
\par
from the water column through consumption by higher food chain organ-
\par
isms, the observational data do not permit separate identification of the
\par
removal mechanisms.
\par
Given this situation, modelers have relied almost exclusively on the few
\par
experimental studies in the laboratory and their judgment concerning
\par
adjustments to these values. For one-dimensional models without explicit
\par
modeling of hydrodynamics, the chosen value may be as much as an order
\par
of magnitude higher than the laboratory values. Two- or three-dimensional
\par
models with hydrodynamics may incorporate the unadjusted laboratory
\par
value. After early modeling studies presented chosen values, these values
\par
were sometimes adopted in subsequent studies without comment (in effect,
\par
"default" values were identified). Thus, there is probably much less infor-
\par
mation in the columns of Table 1 than implied by the number of values
\par
reported.
\par
In summary, the choices for phytoplankton settling velocity appear to be
\par
based on ad hoc adjustments to a few values measured under controlled
\par
conditions. There is virtually no field confirmation of choices made for
\par
parameters individually (as opposed to collectively). This situation is fairly
\par
typical of the state-of-the-art in surface water quality simulation modeling.
\par
\textbf{6. FROM SCIENTIFIC KNOWLEDGE, EXPERIMENTATION, AND}
\par
\textbf{OBSERVATION TO MODEL SPECIFICATION: BIODEGRADATION OF PCBS}
\par
Several transformations may affect the concentration of synthetic or-
\par
ganic chemicals (SOCs) in natural waterbodies; among them are: volatiliza-K.H. RECKHOW 13
\par
tion, adsorption and sedimentation, hydrolysis, photolysis, and biodegrada-
\par
tion (Chapra and Reckhow, 1983). Biodegradation covers a number of
\par
distinct processes including mineralization, detoxication, and metabolism.
\par
Biodegradation by-products may be inorganic, or they may be organic
\par
compounds that are simpler or related to the original SOC.
\par
In most natural water bodies, SOCs are at relatively low concentrations.
\par
Bacteria tend to exist at fairly high stable population levels, and they
\par
generally prefer other more abundant carbon sources over SOCs. As a
\par
consequence, the Michaelis-Menten relationship, which is often used to
\par
model uptake and growth, is frequently simplified to a first-order expres-
\par
sion when applied to the biodegradation of SOCs:
\par
\emph{biodegradation loss (mass/time) = kbVc,} (3)
\par
where:
\par
\emph{kb =} first-order degradation rate constant (t-1)
\par
\emph{V =} system volume (m3)
\par
\emph{c} = total contaminant concentration (g/m3)
\par
While it has been shown that most SOCs (like PCBs; polychlorinated
\par
biphenyls) are biodegradable under controlled conditions (e.g., in labora-
\par
tory experiments), there is often a great deal of uncertainty concerning the
\par
magnitude, and even the existence, of biodegradation occurring naturally in
\par
waterbodies. As Madsen (1991) notes, "In situ biodegradation of organic
\par
compounds is very difficult to prove." For example, as Chapra and Reck-
\par
how (1983) observe, "many laboratory degradation studies are performed
\par
using microbial populations that have been acclimated to a particular
\par
organic compound. Whether such species would tend to behave similarly in
\par
the lake environment is questionable and makes it difficult to apply some
\par
of the laboratory work to natural situations." Unfortunately, Madsen
\par
(1991) concludes, "The problems of extrapolating from laboratory data to
\par
the field have never been solved despite decades of debate." Even with
\par
these difficulties, biodegradation is frequently included as a transformation
\par
process when models such as WASP4 are used for SOC transport and fate.
\par
In a study of the scientific basis for parameter determination in simula-
\par
tion models, Wolpert et al. (1993) looked at biodegradation and other
\par
processes affecting PCBs in surface waterbodies. Their review of the
\par
mechanistic modeling literature indicated that for the most part, even in
\par
modeling studies with the most thorough databases, the source for the
\par
biodegradation rate constant either was not specified or was based on a
\par
synthesis of literature values. In other words, there did not appear to be in
\par
situ evidence for a particular biodegradation rate.
\par
In their own modeling work on PCB transport and fate in the upper
\par
Hudson River (NY), Wolpert et al. (1993) stated "It is believed that thereK.H. RECKHOW 14
\par
is some biodegradation of PCBs in the sediment [of the Hudson River], but
\par
even that is uncertain." The biodegradation rate depends on many factors
\par
including temperature, dissolved oxygen, nutrients such as nitrogen and
\par
phosphorus, and the mix and concentration of micro-organisms. Biodegra-
\par
dation may be either aerobic or anaerobic; if biodegradation occurs natu-
\par
rally, it seems plausible that aerobic processes could dominate in the water
\par
column and anaerobic processes could dominate in the sediments.
\par
One problem that confronted Wolpert et al. (1993) in their modeling
\par
study is that PCBs exist not as a single compound, but as 209 congeners.
\par
These congeners are a function of the number and placement of chlorine
\par
atoms bonded to the ten carbon atoms in all PCBs. The congeners not only
\par
vary with chlorine, but they also vary by molecular weight (from 189 to 499
\par
g/mol) and by chemical/physical behavior. Thus, in effect, PCB transport
\par
and fate prediction involves the modeling of 209 compounds. Unfortu-
\par
nately, as Wolpert et al. note, it is difficult to distinguish among congeners
\par
in natural water bodies using conventional monitoring techniques.
\par
The literature on biodegradation rates illustrates one of the problems
\par
with the congeners. Bedard et al. (1986) measured aerobic biodegradation
\par
rate for PCBs in the laboratory. Over a 72-h period, they measured
\par
degradation rates varying from less than 20\% to greater than 90\%, depend-
\par
ing on congener. As a consequence, the effective first-order aerobic degra-
\par
dation rate for these experiments ranged from less than 0.86 {$\bullet$} 10-6 s-1 to
\par
greater than 17.7 {$\bullet$} 10-6 s-1. The situation is similar for anaerobic degrada-
\par
tion. Under experimentally-controlled conditions, Rhee et al. (1979) found
\par
anaerobic degradation of PCB congeners from Hudson River sediment to
\par
range from 8\% to 63\% over a 7-month period. This translates to first-order
\par
anaerobic biodegradation rates between 4.5 {$\bullet$} 10-9 s-1 and 54.0 {$\bullet$} 10-9 s-1
\par
,
\par
depending upon congener.
\par
Even for a single congener, the measured biodegradation rates can vary.
\par
For two different congener mixtures, Bedard et al. (1986) measured PCB
\par
congener concentrations after exposure to bacteria. For a single congener
\par
(labeled 4, 4') common to both mixtures, the apparent degradation rate
\par
differed. As Wolpert et al. (1993) inquire, "Should this difference be
\par
attributed to:
\par
{$\bullet$} Measurement error?
\par
{$\bullet$} Different biodegradation rates, depending on the mixture of congeners
\par
present and their relative abundance?
\par
{$\bullet$} Biotransformation of one congener into another (with fewer chlorine atoms) through
\par
dechlorination, so that the reported differences are only the \emph{net} changes in each
\par
congener?"
\par
Focusing on the implications for modeling PCB transport and fate in the
\par
Hudson River, Wolpert et al. (1993) continue, "If the rates do depend onK.H. RECKHOW 15
\par
the mixture of PCBs present, it is hard to see how to use the reported rates
\par
to predict biodegradation rates in the Hudson River bed with its unknown
\par
mixture of PCB congeners unless each congener is modeled separately \emph{and}
\par
it is known how different ratios of congener concentrations affect the
\par
biodegradation rates. Clearly, congener-specific degradation rates for all
\par
possible mixtures are unavailable. Our ability to model individual con-
\par
geners is also limited by uncertainty about the initial congener composition
\par
of the river loadings and of the observed water column and sediment
\par
samples. Other reasons for suspecting the laboratory data may not reflect
\par
field conditions accurately include:
\par
{$\bullet$} It is likely that laboratory growing conditions are more conducive to
\par
bacterial populations than are river conditions. Populations of PCB-de-
\par
grading bacteria could well be orders of magnitude smaller in the river
\par
than in the laboratory;
\par
{$\bullet$} The experiments do not include enough measurements over time to
\par
verify a stationary biodegradation rate constant, as assumed in [the]
\par
model;
\par
{$\bullet$} Environmental conditions in the sediment must be expected to differ
\par
from those in the laboratory - for example, the ratio of carbon dioxide
\par
to hydrogen is a particularly sensitive environmental parameter for
\par
bacteria and can vary from place to place in the sediment."
\par
Initial reaction to the uncertainty regarding in situ biodegradation of
\par
PCBs is apt to be one of concern. This may be followed quickly by the
\par
observation that, in fact, PCBs are 209 compounds; thus, why expect that a
\par
single mechanistic model will be adequate for transport and fate? Perhaps
\par
so, yet:
\par
{$\bullet$} PCBs are among the most studied SOCs in the environment; thus,
\par
scientific knowledge, experimental evidence, and observational data
\par
should provide a "best case" for the state-of-the-art in SOC transport
\par
and fate modeling,
\par
{$\bullet$} Decisions (including the decision to do nothing) are being made concern-
\par
ing PCBs in the aquatic environment, and models should be considered for decision
\par
support.
\par
Certainly, we must have an understanding of cause-effect relationships
\par
to make reliable predictions of pollutant transport and fate. Predictions
\par
based on statistical relationships alone may work for interpolation in
\par
well-studied situations (e.g., aggregate response for lake eutrophication),
\par
but they may fail to account for important mechanisms in new situations.
\par
Yet, uncertainties in the mechanistic transport and fate models should
\par
cause us to search for other ways to provide predictive scientific analyses
\par
for decision support. A suggested alternative is presented below.K.H. RECKHOW 16
\par
\textbf{7. A PROPOSAL FOR MODEL SELECTION IN DECISION SUPPORT FOR}
\par
\textbf{RISK ASSESSMENT}
\par
As noted, prediction uncertainty is apt to be quite large for mechanistic
\par
transport and fate models like WASP4 and EXAMS. Among the reasons
\par
why there continues to be support for large water quality simulation
\par
models, even in a situation of apparently great uncertainty, are: (1) the
\par
belief that correct mathematical descriptions allow extrapolation to other
\par
unmodeled systems, and (2) the desire to fully capture within the model a
\par
complete description of a system from management controls to meaningful
\par
system responses. This second justification for large models presumably
\par
reflects a view that a decision support system should be based on an
\par
automated analysis that minimizes human intervention.
\par
An alternative perspective may be found in the medical diagnosis area.
\par
Decision support may take the form of an expert system that receives
\par
information on symptoms and identifies likely causes. There is no attempt
\par
to replace the physician; rather, the expert system provides information to
\par
the physician to improve decision making. The physician interprets the
\par
evidence, including the output from the expert system, and makes a
\par
judgment and recommendation.
\par
In surface water quality management, the experienced subject matter
\par
scientist may fail to identify patterns in multivariate data that a simulation
\par
model or data analysis may easily interpret. However, the scientist has
\par
experiences to provide insight and to interpret information in a more
\par
flexible way than does the logical but structured simulation model. When
\par
the subject matter scientist is aided by a simple expert system to help reveal
\par
patterns, the result could be both an ability to discover patterns and an
\par
effectiveness in interpreting their consequences. This improved interpreta-
\par
tion may refer both to prediction of system response and to assessment of
\par
the nature and value of sample information to aid decision making.
\par
Thus, one option for the design of a decision support system in environ-
\par
mental management is to specify the nature and scale of information to be
\par
presented to a subject area scientist, who then interprets the information
\par
for decision makers. Given the current state-of-the-art in transport and fate
\par
modeling, is decision making better served by large, detailed simulation
\par
models that describe a system from inputs to meaningful effects, even if the
\par
prediction uncertainty is extremely large? Or, will better decisions result if
\par
simple models are used to provide predictions of aggregate behavior for a
\par
subject matter scientist to interpret detailed response for decision makers?
\par
The roles of: (1) the expert system for decision support, (2) the subject
\par
matter scientist, and (3) the decision maker may be guided by general
\par
criteria, and by certain specific criteria that may differ among subject areas.K.H. RECKHOW 17
\par
Ideally, as described above, there is interaction between the decision maker
\par
and the subject matter scientist in defining the scope of the analysis. Any
\par
simulation model that provides decision support must have uncertainties of
\par
acceptable magnitude; in some subject areas this may require simple
\par
aggregate-behavior models, while in other areas complex models may be
\par
useful.
\par
The actual choice of simulation models used within this proposed
\par
decision support system should be based on a compromise between feasibil-
\par
ity and desirability. To see how this might occur, consider the following
\par
hypothetical example as a way to interactively arrive at model choice:
\par
Initially, the "environmental managers" should identify desirable objec-
\par
tives and attributes ("endpoints" or "measures of effectiveness"). The
\par
attributes represent quantities that are measurable (predictable) and mean-
\par
ingful to the issue of concern; for eutrophication, floating mats of algae
\par
(probably not chlorophyll \emph{a,} unless a chl\emph{a} standard exists) is one possible
\par
example.
\par
A possible next step is for the "environmental modelers" to identify the
\par
feasibility of a predictive scientific assessment of the proposed attributes,
\par
based on spatial, temporal, and ecological scales. This should be expressed
\par
in terms of scientific uncertainty (e.g.,\textbf{ }"We can predict abundance of the
\par
species with a standard error of \textpm{} 100\%").
\par
Then, the environmental managers determine if this is acceptable, given
\par
the value function (e.g., losses or net benefits associated with management
\par
options); it is conceivable that the desired attribute has an undesirable level
\par
of predictive uncertainty (e.g.,\textbf{ }"We prefer abundance as an endpoint, but
\par
\textpm{} 100\% is unacceptable. What alternatives can be predicted with greater
\par
precision?").
\par
If the problem is then referred back to the environmental modelers, they
\par
may consider aggregation to reduce uncertainty (e.g., "We can predict
\par
presence/absence to \textpm 30\%; is that useful?"). This type of interaction is
\par
intended to lead to good compromise choices for the level of the analysis
\par
and the nature of the predicted response.
\par
This interplay is critical, but for this interaction to be effective, we need
\par
both scientific uncertainty analyses and some notion of the value function.
\par
The environmental modeler should not solely determine the nature (cost of
\par
effort and level of detail) of the ecological assessment, yet the environmen-
\par
tal manager should not dictate endpoints to the environmental modeler.
\par
There should be frequent interaction, with consideration of uncertainty
\par
and valuation, in order to balance what is desirable with what is feasible.
\par
This decision theoretic approach helps us assess the value of new
\par
(sample) information; this, too, requires interaction between modelers and
\par
managers. For example, the need for additional experimentation or moni-K.H. RECKHOW 18
\par
toring is evaluated in terms of the value function (e.g., "Including the cost
\par
of experimentation or monitoring, is the expected utility higher or lower
\par
with additional information?"). In addition, a decision theoretic approach
\par
to environmental risk assessment illustrates the critical role of uncertainty
\par
analysis in the interaction between modelers and managers discussed
\par
above.
\par
\textbf{8. A CONCLUDING THOUGHT}
\par
No one can claim that scientific uncertainty is desirable; yet, no one
\par
should claim that scientific uncertainty is best hidden or ignored. Estimates
\par
of uncertainty in predictions are not unlike the point estimates of predicted
\par
response. Like the point predictions, the uncertainty estimates contain
\par
information that can improve risk assessment and decision making. The
\par
decision support system proposed above will not eliminate this uncertainty
\par
nor will it change the fact that, due to uncertainty, some decisions will yield
\par
consequences other than those anticipated. It will, however, allow risk
\par
assessors and decision makers to use the uncertainty to structure the
\par
analysis and present the scientific inferences in an appropriate way. In the
\par
long run, that should improve environmental management and decision
\par
making.
\par
