\par
approach, Range/Confidence Estimation, for hand-
\par
ling information that uses margins of safety is pre-
\par
sented along with an example to illustrate how the
\par
uncertainties may be addressed directly, using the
\par
structures described in the paper.
\par
2. HIERARCHY OF DATA SOURCES BASED
\par
UPON EMPIRICAL VALIDATION
\par
The following hierarchy is one way of categoriz-
\par
ing data sources based on the degree to which the
\par
data can be validated empirically at the top to unvali-
\par
dated data at the bottom :
\par
1. Standard Distribution. Empirical data vali-
\par
dated by many different investigations and generally
\par
agreed upon as a standard. For example, the distribu-
\par
tion of adult weight in the United States.
\par
2. Empirical Distribution. Empirical data vali-
\par
dated for specific instances. For example, the distri-
\par
bution of arsenic concentration in 20 samples for
\par
ten wells.
\par
3. Validated Model. A model that has been vali-
\par
dated empirically in repeated experiments. For exam-
\par
ple, determining altitude from local pressure instru-
\par
ments from surface pressure and humidity
\par
measurements.
\par
4. Unvalidated Model. A model which has not
\par
been empirically validated. For example, inference
\par
about the extrapolation of effects on human cancer
\par
at low doses from high dose data in animals. Valida-
\par
tion may or may not be possible.
\par
5. Alternate Models. Alternate unvalidated
\par
models to those selected above that seem to be less
\par
reasonable, but possible, at the time that the model
\par
is used in a decision process. For example, a threshold
\par
model for cancer. Alternate models may actually be
\par
superior to the primary model from differing perspec-
\par
tives.
\par
6. Expert Value Judgment. In the absence of em-
\par
pirical data the judgment of experts may be used.
\par
Since the information is unvalidated, the selection,
\par
framing and bias of experts becomes dominant.
\par
Sometimes consensus may be important; otherwise
\par
diversity may be important and retained. For exam-
\par
ple, should benign tumors that do not progress to
\par
malignancy be counted in mouse exposure data?
\par
7. Best Guess Estimate. A form of presumably
\par
expert judgment based upon reasonable expectation
\par
and the framework in which the decisions are asked,
\par
but acknowledging the lack of integrity in the esti-
\par
mates at the outset.
\par
8. Test Case. Values used to test models for their
\par
sensitivity, critical variables and the value of addi-
\par
tional information.
\par
There are degrees between each step in the hier-
\par
archy, and one may add or delete levels; but the list
\par
provides a means to differentiate between sources
\par
of uncertainty.
\par
Different uses require different levels of the hi-
\par
erarchy. Presumably, one always wants to start with
\par
the best scientific data (the top of the hierarchy).
\par
However, if the policy decisions involved do not re-
\par
quire this precision, then the associated costs in time
\par
and resources may be exorbitant. There are a variety
\par
of uses such as Scientific Information---empirical
\par
data stressed, Regulatory Decisions---mix of empiri-
\par
cal and value data, Economic Decisions---cost and
\par
value data stressed, and Public Policy---values are
\par
the drivers.
\par
3. TYPES OF UNCERTAINTY3
\par
Different types of uncertainty are inherent at
\par
each level. How do they impact the process at each
\par
level.
\par
Temporal. Uncertainty in future states and un-
\par
certainty in past states.
\par
Structural. Uncertainty due to complexity
\par
(modal uncertainty).
\par
Metrical. Uncertainty in measurement.
\par
Translational. Uncertainty in explaining uncer-
\par
tain results.
\par
All four types occur in any situation, but de-
\par
pending on the situation one or more dominates.
