\par
Future uncertainty uses the concept of probabil-
\par
ity as a discrimination parameter, and is based on a
\par
model (structural uncertainty) which presupposes
\par
that the future will behave similarly to past experi-
\par
ence. The model may be expressed as an a priori, a
\par
frequentist, or a subjectivist approach. The outcome
\par
is valued by ``luck.'' Before an event, a probability
\par
model of the likely outcomes can be constructed.
\par
After the event occurs, one considers a desirable
\par
outcome as ``lucky,'' and an undesirable one as ``un-
\par
lucky.'' Selecting a case where an undesirable out-
\par
come has low probability makes good sense, but the
\par
undesirable outcome can still occur.4
\par
4 This case might be considered as ``competent error,'' the unlucky
\par
outcome of a rare event, as opposed to ``negligent error,'' know-
\par
ingly or unknowingly choosing a high probability for an un-
\par
wanted outcome.
\par
Past information is subject to different interpre-
\par
tations as requirements change or ``political correct-
\par
ness'' imposed. The past is not always a useful pro-
\par
logue to the future. The degree to which historical
\par
data were recorded, stored, and made available, in-
\par
cluding the biases and resources of the collectors,
\par
differentiates between adequate and inadequate in-
\par
formation. The correctness of the interpretation, tak-
\par
ing into account quality and quantity of the historical
\par
data and the biases of both the collector and inter-
\par
preter, determines the value of the result. Measure-
\par
ment error occurs when the data needed are not the
\par
historical data that were collected or were inade-
\par
quately collected.
\par
Structural uncertainty, as a result of complexity,
\par
depends on both the number of model parameters
\par
and their interaction as well as the degree to which
\par
models of the complex situation are useful. If the
\par
models used also correspond to reality to a suitable
\par
degree, they tend to have higher value. This is partic-
\par
ularly the case when the models can be validated
\par
empirically. The idea of likelihood of membership is
\par
one method for differentiating among model parame-
\par
ters; for example, the plausibility that model A is
\par
more likely to be valid than model B. Note that both
\par
probability and likelihood use similar mathematical
\par
approaches, but each address a different set of con-
\par
cerns, the former the prediction of future event out-
\par
comes, and the latter degree of membership to a set
\par
of model conditions. The confidence in the estimates
\par
in terms of validation is the valuation parameter.
\par
Measurement uncertainty lies in our inability to
\par
measure; that is, the limitations in measuring instru-
\par
ments. The ability of a measuring device to discrimi-
\par
nate among values of the parameter being measured
\par
is its precision. Accuracy addresses errors made in
\par
using the device, that is, how good was the measure-
\par
ment. (For example, if a ruler is only precise to centi-
\par
meters, but a five centimeter line is measured as seven
\par
centimeters, then the measurement is inaccurate by
\par
two centimeters). Statistical sampling methods are
\par
used to detect and control the measurement errors
\par
of this type.72 Richards and Rowe
