4. VALUE OF INFORMATION (TO THE
\par
DECISION)
\par
Several techniques provide a process for analyz-
\par
ing the value of new information.5
\par
5 Mathematics of structure vs mathematics of content must be
\par
addressed. If one is looking for an ultimate methodology with
\par
structural completeness, its application may be so general as to
\par
limit the effectiveness of valuing information. If one is willing to
\par
constrain the methodology to specific cases with definitive con-
\par
tent, the information content itself lends itself to valuation.
\par
1. Identify the most critical parameters and de-
\par
termine the range of uncertainty in them. In multipli-
\par
cative and raised power models, those parameter esti-
\par
mates with greatest measures of dispersion will be
\par
dominant. In additive models, the dispersion in the
\par
parameters with largest values will dominate. Gener-
\par
ally speaking, great attention should be paid to pa-
\par
rameters having estimates with large standard devia-
\par
tions.
\par
2. In terms of the decision alternatives, deter-
\par
mine what data will be sufficient and non-confound-
\par
ing to resolve the decision. A sensitivity analysis using
\par
test data may provide this information. One approach
\par
to performing sensitivity analyses is by way of the
\par
``expected value of perfect information'' approach
\par
(Morgan and Henrion, 1990, pp. 197, 263), which is
\par
a measure of the sensitivity of a decision analysis
\par
to uncertainty.
\par
3. Determine the costs and likelihood of ob-
\par
taining sufficient information. Can the costs be
\par
avoided or offset by simultaneously accepting more
\par
uncertain data in noncritical parameters. That is, can
\par
one move down the hierarchy for noncritical vari-
\par
ables without affecting the outcome of the decision?
\par
4. If the costs in time and/or resources are exor-
\par
bitant, or if the uncertainty is unresolvable, are there
\par
other parameters and methods that may be used to
\par
retreat from a totally unresolvable decision?
\par
5. Where differences exist in expert judgement
\par
over choice of models (model uncertainty) or in val-
\par
ues assigned to parameters (measurement uncer-
\par
tainty), consider preserving the diversity to the end
\par
of the decision-making process where the implica-
\par
tions of these differences can be ascertained by those
\par
making the decisions as well as the stakeholders. As
\par
important in postponing a final decision on the choice
\par
of models and/or parameters can be the providential
\par
arrival of further information or data which can help
\par
to clarify the choice of model. An example of this is
\par
the well-known story of the drug thalidomide in the
\par
U.S. : Because of disagreements among pharmaceuti-
\par
cal and medical experts, approval to sell this drug in
\par
the U.S. was delayed; after the terrible birth defects
\par
caused by the drug started to appear in Europe, at-
\par
tempts to issue the drug were halted.
\par
4.1. Identifying Critical Parameters
\par
The identification of critical parameters may be-
\par
gin with an examination of the range of uncertainty
\par
in each of a model's parameters. As will be described
\par
subsequently, an uncertainty index based upon rangeDecision-Making with Heterogeneous Sources of Information is used with Range/Confidence estimates, and these
\par
indices may be used directly for this purpose. The
\par
wider the range or the larger the value of an uncer-
\par
tainty index, the higher the range of the underlying
\par
distribution (known or unknown). Focusing on pa-
\par
rameters with higher uncertainty provides a first cut
\par
at identifying critical parameters.
\par
Next, the parameters may be separated into the
\par
major categories that dominates each parameter :
\par
Metrical Uncertainty. These parameters are the
\par
most amenable to uncertainty reduction by acquiring
\par
better information through improved measurements
\par
and/or additional data points.
\par
Temporal Uncertainty. Better historic data does
\par
not always provide better forecasts. Mitigation of
\par
possible events (at additional cost for increased capa-
\par
bility) may be a better way to constrain the range of
\par
consequences.
\par
Structural Uncertainty. Better validation of mod-
\par
els are necessary for uncertainty reduction in this
\par
category with costs associated with the validation.
\par
Development of alternate or more complex models
\par
won't help if they can't be validated. Constraining
\par
parameter ranges can be useful in limiting the deci-
\par
sion in some cases.
\par
Translational Uncertainty. Finding better means
\par
to communicate and present uncertainties to decision
\par
makers and stakeholders may be very cost-effective
\par
for reducing this type of uncertainty.
\par
The costs of uncertainty reduction may also be
\par
addressed for each parameter, as well as the likeli-
\par
hood that further information will, indeed, bring clo-
