5. MEANS OF EXPRESSING UNCERTAINTY
\par
In analyzing the data, it is important to give a
\par
careful review of the important uncertainties existing
\par
within the framework of the conclusions. Therefore
\par
we will review briefly some methods for expressing
\par
the uncertainties contained within an expert
\par
judgment.
\par
5.1. Spread
\par
The spread inherent to any random dataset will
\par
preclude the expert from stating conclusions of com-
\par
plete certainty. Several classical point estimates of
\par
the spread or dispersion of a dataset are well-known
\par
to be powerful statistical tools for expressing, in a
\par
concise numerical or graphical manner, the uncer-
\par
tainties caused by the variability of the observed data.
\par
to which the sampling procedure is complete must
\par
be ascertained, and the methods of analysis must be
\par
suited to the manner in which the data were collected.
\par
For instance, it is a not-uncommon error to collect
\par
data by means of a stratified random sampling scheme
\par
but then to attempt to analyze the data using the
\par
statistical procedures proper only for simple random
\par
sampling schemes. Similarly, heteroskedastic pro-
\par
cesses cannot be analyzed correctly by means of
\par
methods developed for homoskedastic processes. To
\par
avoid this type of error, the expert must develop a
\par
deeper understanding of the underlying process.
\par
An analysis of the shape of the distribution or
\par
histogram is important to the process of determining
\par
whether or not the data appear to be drawn from,
\par
say, a symmetric population. One- and two-sided con-
\par
fidence intervals and regions also are well-known
\par
procedures for estimating parameters of the process.
\par
Confidence intervals also capture succinctly the de-
\par
gree of confidence, hence also the uncertainty, which
\par
the expert may have in parameter estimates. Hypoth-
\par
esis tests and P-values, some of which may be derived
\par
from confidence intervals or regions, also form a well-
\par
studied set of procedures for capturing the degree of
\par
uncertainty in hypothetical statements about a
\par
process.
\par
5.2. Range
\par
The ``range'' of a dataset, which is the difference
\par
between the largest and smallest observations, is easy
\par
to compute, but suffers from a lack of robustness to
\par
potential outliers. The ``sample standard deviation''
\par
is the quintessential measure of dispersion; although
\par
this measure is also not robust in the presence of
\par
outliers, it is well-known to possess superb inferential
\par
properties. The ``coefficient of variation'' measures
\par
the dispersion of a dataset relative to the mean of
\par
the data; in the presence of heteroskedastic data,
\par
the coefficient of variation often is superior to the
\par
standard deviation.
\par
5.3. Measures of Confidence
\par
Although point estimates of dispersion are use-
\par
ful for assessing uncertainty, more fundamental tools
\par
for the measurement of uncertainty must be utilized.
\par
To review some of these procedures, we consider
\par
three general setups :
\par
5.3.1. Empirical Data
\par
In this situation, the expert will review the data
\par
from an empirical standpoint. For instance, the extent
\par
5.3.2. Models
\par
At the center of the analysis is the expert's goal
\par
to identify a class of models which correctly describe
\par
the process. A proposed model must be capable of
\par
validation by the procedures applicable to empirical
\par
data, as described above. For instance, a probabilistic
\par
model for a data set must be well-suited to describe
\par
the shape and standard deviation of the observed
\par
histogram; and the parameters in the model should
\par
be capable of accurate estimation by probabilistic
\par
methods. Models should withstand simple tests of
\par
reasonableness and common-sense, and should be
\par
complete in the sense that they are able to describe
\par
future data sets which may be moderately different
\par
from the data already collected.
\par
The degree of complexity of a proposed model
\par
should be minimized; here we are reminded of Oc-
\par
cam's razor and of information-theoretic procedures
\par
for minimizing the number of parameters in a pro-
\par
posed model. Finally, statistical tests (e.g., chi-square
\par
goodness-of-fit tests) for the correctness of proposed
\par
models need to be performed.Decision-Making with Heterogeneous Sources of Information 5.3.3. Expert Judgment
\par
An expert judgment is simply an expression of
\par
confidence by individuals who have demonstrated
\par
expertise in their chosen field. It is important to re-
\par
member that although a consensus of experts is usu-
\par
ally a happy occasion, consensus does not imply or
\par
guarantee validity. In short, a consensus of experts
\par
is an expression of their joint agreement as to the
\par
correctness of their opinion. Thus it is as important
\par
to carefully review a consensus opinion as it is impor-
\par
tant to review a diversity of opinions.
\par
Computer-based expert systems (e.g., neural
\par
networks or other fully-automated methods), for ana-
\par
lyzing data are now an important part of the industrial
\par
and technological scene. In this situation, several
\par
computing machines are connected in a ``voter sys-
\par
tem''; with individual machine computations subject
\par
to approval by all machines. If sufficiently many com-
\par
puters ``vote'' in favor of a given computation then
\par
the results are passed on to the next level of the
\par
system. These systems are especially useful when the
\par
data are generated by very-high-speed processes and
\par
are of a technically routine nature; an example is a
\par
busy trading day on the NYSE.
\par
