\par
feasible to perform extensive simulation of com-
\par
plex processes.
\par
In constructing simulation procedures, a basic
\par
problem is to simulate the probability distributions
\par
of random variables. There are several methods of
\par
solving this problem : the standard Monte Carlo tech-
\par
nique, Markov chain methods, Gibbs sampling proce-
\par
dures. These procedures have found wide-ranging
\par
applications in the engineering sciences.
\par
6.1.2. Resampling Techniques
\par
Resampling methods, such as the bootstrap and
\par
jackknife procedures, have revolutionized some as-
\par
pects of statistics since the 1980's (Shao and Tu, 1995).
\par
These techniques allow the scientist to repeatedly
\par
sample the observed data, and to estimate accurate
\par
solutions to complex inference problems. Resam-
\par
pling methods can provide accurate answers to some
\par
complex, multidimensional probabilistic problems.
\par
These techniques require large amounts of computer
\par
time and memory, and they have become increasingly
\par
popular as computers have become more efficient.
\par
Classical statistical techniques have demon-
\par
strated how to estimate the population mean and
\par
standard deviation, and to measure the accuracy of
\par
those estimates. An important advantage of the jack-
\par
knife and bootstrap resampling methods (over classi-
\par
cal techniques) is that they allow us to estimate any
\par
parameter and to evaluate the accuracy of the esti-
\par
mating procedure.
\par
