\par
have of explaining a Gibbs sampling simulation of76 Richards and Rowe
\par
the Gaussian distribution. Thus, it is important for
\par
engineers to continue to require a proper grounding
\par
in classical statistical techniques as part of the entry
\par
fee for the profession (Nelson, 1990).
\par
These methods generally are used to address
\par
measurement uncertainty through measures of cen-
\par
tral tendency and higher moments. Factor analysis,
\par
ANOVA and correlation approaches address uncer-
\par
tainty due to complexity. Probability theory is used
\par
to address future uncertainty.
\par
particularly address uncertainty of past states; and to
\par
the extent that past behavior is likely to continue in
\par
the future, can be a predictor of future behavior.
\par
6.2.2. Multivariate Models
\par
Multivariate models also play a central role in
\par
this area. These models are crucial to studying
\par
multifactor processes in which correlations between
\par
different factors are of concern. Multivariate reliabil-
\par
ity models are fundamental to analyzing the pattern
\par
of observed failures of a system and to gaining insight
\par
into the reasons underlying the failures. Multivariate
\par
logistic models have proved important in analyzing
\par
the velocity of automobile occupants during acci-
\par
dents, and to identifying those factors (e.g., occupant
\par
weight, velocity of automobile at the time of crash,
\par
weight and size of the automobile, usage/non-usage
\par
of airbags and/or seatbelts) which are of primary
\par
importance in explaining the level of severity of injur-
\par
ies. Multivariate survival models have been applied
\par
to study : the failure of electrical cable insulation;
\par
dosage levels of radiation in energy-generating com-
\par
plexes; failure times of small electrical appliances
\par
(Johnson and Wichern, 1992). In these problems, it
\par
is the multivariate nature of the models which give
\par
insight into the interdependence among the factors
\par
underlying the observed data. These methods partic-
\par
ularly address structural uncertainty and conflicting
\par
goals.
\par
7. RANGE/CONFIDENCE ESTIMATES
\par
Range/Confidence Estimation is a methodology
\par
where three values and their respective confidence
\par
levels are used to study the distributions of input
\par
variables, providing ranges rather than point esti-
\par
mates. The combinations of joint values and joint
\par
confidence levels are then ordered and displayed.
\par
This approach is useful for certain classes of
\par
problems : (1) when data are insufficient for a more
\par
comprehensive probabilistic treatment, (2) when an
\par
initial analysis is needed rapidly for developing a
\par
strategy for further data acquisition, (3) when the
\par
data are from widely different sources with differing
\par
degrees of precision, (4) when safety factors are used
\par
to provide margins of safety, and (5) as a means to
\par
provide screening for uncertainty analysis.
\par
The approach provides for direct use and evalua-
\par
tion of heterogeneous data sources. An uncertainty
\par
index, based upon the range of the estimates, pro-
\par
vides a direct measure of the uncertainty inherent in
\par
the estimates in conjunction with a confidence ex-
\par
pression about those estimates. The initial results
\par
may then be subjected to an Information Value Anal-
\par
ysis Process (IVAP) which provides a strategy for
\par
cost-effective acquisition of further information to
\par
reduce critical uncertainties. In addition, criteria for
\par
deciding when to stop the process can be provided.
\par
6.2.3. Time Series Methods
\par
Approaches based on time series methods are
\par
of primary importance when the data are continuous
\par
in nature. Autoregressive or moving average models
\par
may be useful in the study of data sets in which
\par
observations at neighboring time points are corre-
\par
lated. In a study of signal-to-noise ratios for noise-
\par
detection or mine-detection systems, time series
\par
models may greatly contribute to an understanding of
\par
the important explanatory variables. These methods
\par
7.1. Basis for Range/Confidence Estimates
\par
7.1.1. Range/Confidence Estimating Protocol
\par
There are many ways to make range estimates.
\par
However, the method of making range estimates used
\par
here is a purposely one-sided approach for eliciting
\par
information on each input parameter from heteroge-
\par
neous data sources.6 For risk and costs an overesti-
\par
mate is made of how confident is the analyst or data
\par
6 One-sided distributions, either high or low, as well as two-sided
\par
distributions can be addressed. For simplicity here only the one-
\par
sided approach for eliciting information for each input parameter
\par
is addressed here, and the one-sided distribution direction (over-
\par
estimate) is selected to provide consistency throughout the deci-
\par
sion model example used.Decision-Making with Heterogeneous Sources of Information source is that the actual risk or cost lies below the
\par
estimate. For benefits and performance it is the re-
\par
verse, that is an underestimate is made. Three esti-
\par
mates of a parameter (e.g., probability of an event
\par
with a given consequence magnitude) are made using
\par
alternative models or alternative levels of parameters
\par
within a model.
\par
Bare Estimate. A ``bare estimate'' is made with
\par
no margins of safety. This is, by definition, a best
\par
estimate of what is likely to occur, but the actual risk
\par
that is being estimated may be either higher or lower.
\par
It represents an estimate where all margins of safety
\par
have been removed. This type of estimate is often
\par
very difficult to make for engineers who are trained
\par
to build in margins of safety. However, it is necessary
\par
to insist on this type of analysis if the margins of
\par
safety are to be made subsequently visible and used
\par
as a tool for addressing uncertainty. A central value
\par
of the estimate (mean, median, mode) may be used
\par
as appropriate here; for example, in the case of a
\par
normal distribution, the sample mean is the appro-
\par
priate choice.
\par
Conservative Estimate. A ``conservative esti-
\par
mate'' is made using margins of safety high enough to
\par
provide confidence (a reasoned subjective judgment)
\par
that actual risk lies below the estimate. This type of
\par
estimate is consistent with conservative engineering
\par
design. For example, if the underlying process was a
\par
normal distribution then an illustrative example is
\par
the 95th percentile.
\par
Worst Case Estimate. A ``worst case estimate''
\par
attempts to make sure that the actual risk is always
\par
below the model estimate. The estimate takes into
\par
account all reasonable, imaginable excursions. In a
\par
normal distribution it might exceed the 99th per-
\par
centile.
\par
These three (or more) estimates make up a range
\par
estimate as shown in the illustrative example in Fig.
\par
2, represented by the solid line connecting the three.
\par
Any one of the three estimates cannot be taken out
\par
of context with the other two. To do so invalidates
\par
the framework.
\par
The estimate for each parameter is independent
\par
of the other parameters, and represents the state of
\par
knowledge and confidence about knowledge that the
\par
estimator has at the time of the estimate. If the total
\par
distribution of all the parameters were known to the
\par
same degree, other methods such as Monte Carlo
\par
would be appropriate. Conversely, only gross value
\par
judgments can be made about the critical parameters,
\par
the total distribution of noncritical parameters has
\par
little value to the decision.
\par
77
\par
Fig. 2. Example of a range/confidence estimate.
\par
7.1.2. Uncertainty Indices
\par
The distance between the bare and the worst-
\par
case estimate of a parameter is a measure of its uncer-
\par
tainty, and an uncertainty index may be derived by
\par
dividing the result of the worst-case estimate by that
\par
of the bare case :
\par
Uncertainty Index (UIX)
\par
Worst Case Result/Bare Case Result
\par
The magnitude of the index is used as a means
\par
to make comparisons about the relative uncertainty
\par
of range estimates. A higher index implies higher
\par
uncertainty in the range estimate.
\par
An important aspect of these estimates is the
\par
recognition that many of the explicitly expressed con-
\par
fidence levels are subjective. They represent the sub-
\par
jective evaluation of the estimator, and are subject
\par
to challenge, requiring documentation of the assump-
\par
tions used to make the estimates. The subjectivity
\par
employed has always been there with point estimates,
\par
but was normally invisible to the decision maker.
\par
This process makes it visible and manageable.
\par
Challenges to particular subjective estimates are
\par
best addressed by sensitivity analysis when both the
\par
risk estimates and confidence level estimates are com-
\par
bined to make a range estimate for an achievement
\par
standard. Experience has shown that increasing range
\par
values at high levels of confidence are often relatively
\par
insensitive to the decision when a number of range
\par
estimated parameters are combined in risk models.78 Richards and Rowe
\par
7.1.3. Combining Range/Confidence Levels
\par
in Models
\par
Different types of risk analysis use different
\par
models and parameters suitable for the problem be-
\par
ing addressed. In a Range/Confidence Estimate all
\par
parameters in models are estimated using ranges,
\par
and are combined according to model requirements.
\par
Since there are three estimates for each parameter
\par
and all combinations of estimates must be made there
\par
are many more computations to be made. For exam-
\par
ple, if there are two parameters, nine estimates of
\par
risk are required. In general, the number of risk calcu-
\par
lations to be made (N) is determined by N 3n where
\par
n is the number of variables.
\par
Since all the calculations are arithmetic, they are
\par
easily automated. Spreadsheets work very well and
\par
a number of templates have been designed for this
\par
purpose.
\par
The risk level and combined confidence level (as
\par
well as costs when appropriate) are calculated for
\par
each of the n combinations. These Range/Confidence
\par
Estimates are presented as a plot of increasing risk
\par
on the abscissa vs. the expressed combined confi-
\par
dence level on the ordinate for all combinations of
\par
risk and confidence levels. The resulting curve usually
\par
has a very sharp knee, indicating very high levels of
\par
over conservatism, and provides a totally new per-
\par
spective on deciding on achievement levels and ac-
\par
ceptable confidence levels. Moreover the very wide
\par
ranges of uncertainty in the estimates are shown di-
\par
rectly to the decision makers. Based upon combined
\par
confidence levels, a new combined range estimate
\par
may be made with a much smaller uncertainty index,
\par
eliminating unnecessary margins of safety.
\par
In a Monte Carlo analysis, random combinations
\par
of values of each parameter are used to exercise the
\par
model, leading to an array of joint probability values.
\par
The range/confidence approach uses selected sam-
\par
ples of the distributions rather than the whole distri-
\par
bution, and computes both the joint probability and
\par
the joint confidence for the joint probabilities.
\par
of each event probability along with the confidence
\par
level for each estimate. Note that a Range/Confi-
\par
dence estimate is a compound estimate, and no indi-
\par
vidual estimate may be taken out of context with the
\par
other two. To do so is invalid, until a management
\par
decision is made, using and specifying a given level of
\par
confidence that the actual risk lies below the estimate.
\par
The confidence in a parameter value is assigned
\par
by the estimator, and represents his or her subjective
\par
evaluation. For empirical data, the estimate is much
\par
less subjective than for a value estimate (e.g., what
\par
points on the empirical distribution to use). Con-
\par
versely, the confidence of a ``guess estimate'' may be
\par
totally subjective.
\par
7.1.4. Reporting Results
\par
Range estimates, as opposed to point estimates,
\par
will be made for all parameters in risk/benefit or
\par
cost/benefit analyses. For risk analyses both the prob-
\par
ability of occurrence of events and the magnitude of
\par
consequences are addressed. As a minimum, a bare,
\par
conservative and worst case estimate will be made
\par
7.2. Templates for Risk Analysis
\par
Each generic type of risk analysis, using Range/
\par
Confidence Estimates requires a template which pro-
\par
vides the risk analysis structure and models peculiar
\par
to the type of problem on hand. An example shown
\par
below for arsenic in drinking water is an example of
\par
a template, in this case it addresses health risks from
\par
a RCRA perspective, using U.S. EPA Regulatory
\par
Assessment Guides (RAGs) as a basis. A number of
\par
such templates have already been used successfully.
\par
