7.3. Some Implications
\par
Range/Confidence Estimation results provide
\par
both the joint values of the model and the joint con-
\par
fidence for each value. When ordered as shown in
\par
Fig. 3, for example, the margins of safety can be
\par
addressed directly. The choice is how much margin
\par
of safety, as expressed in the confidence that the joint
\par
risk lies below the estimate, does the decision maker
\par
want. Combined with cost estimates of the alterna-
\par
tives, the cost of increasingly larger margins of safety
\par
can be ascertained, that is, the cost of increased credi-
\par
bility as expressed by larger margins of safety is
\par
made visible.
\par
Admittedly, Range/Confidence Estimates rep-
\par
resent only a small sample of the data that may be
\par
available for a parameter (the whole distribution may
\par
be known empirically). However, if the critical vari-
\par
ables are unvalidated models or judgment of experts,
\par
the loss of this noncritical information is diminished.
\par
Any judgments made on these critical parameters
\par
may be no better than the Range/Confidence Esti-
\par
mates made using the protocol outlined here. The
\par
situation determines what methods are appropriate.
\par
When confidence levels for risk values are avail-
\par
able, one can address the uncertainty in risk estimates
\par
directly by specifying the level of confidence accept-
\par
able rather than just a range of uncertainty in the
\par
risk estimates or at worst, a single value for risk.
\par
In many cases, more information and more detailed
\par
analyses may be required, but the Information Value
\par
Analysis Process provides a means to address these
\par
8. CONCLUSIONS
\par
Analyze your models before performing Monte
\par
Carlo analyses. Identify the critical variables and
\par
concentrate on those. Once a variable is determined
\par
to be noncritical, don't worry too much about it.
\par
Address the data corresponding to critical variables
\par
so you can focus your resources on these data,
\par
particularly if the data available about these critical
\par
variables are based upon unvalidated models or
\par
expert judgment. Do not expend resources on vari-
\par
ables that can be easily measured and validated,
\par
but are not critical. Providing data because you
\par
can easily obtain it for noncritical values only tends
\par
to obfuscate the analysis and the uncertainties in-
\par
volved.
\par
Keep in mind the uncertainty in your results
\par
and do not take the results to mean more than
\par
they do. One should not only calculate the joint
\par
values for a Monte Carlo analysis, but the confi-
\par
dence limits for these joint values as well. Knowing
\par
the confidence allows better judgments to be made
\par
by decision-makers since these confidence levels
\par
provide insight into the reliability of the results
\par
and the margins of safety available along with the
\par
cost of these margins.
\par
Critical parameters which use unvalidated mod-
\par
els, have competing alternative models, or depend
\par
upon the judgment of experts, dictate the limitations
\par
of your results. Evaluate what you do not know and
\par
make it explicitly visible for the evaluator of your re-
\par
sults.
\par
Methods such as Range/Confidence Estimation
\par
provide means to rapidly assess data from highly
\par
uncertain sources as well as for known distributions,
\par
determine if enough information is available for a
\par
decision. Regardless of the methodology used for
\par
estimation, an Information Value Analysis Process
\par
can provide cost-effective data acquisition when
\par
better information is necessary.
