Imaginable surprise in global change science
\par
STE PH E N H . SCH NE ID E R
\par
D epartm ent of Biological Sciences and Institu te for International Studies, Stanford University, Stanford,
\par
CA 94305--5020, USA
\par
B . L. TU R N E R II, H O LLY MO R E H O U SE G A R R IG A
\par
G eorge Perkins M arsh Institute and Gradu ate School of G eography, Clark University, Worcester,
\par
M A 01610, USA
\par
A bstract
\par
D ecisionmakers at all scales (individuals, rms, and local, national, and international
\par
governmental organizations) are concerned about reducing their vulnerability to (or the
\par
likelihood of) unexpected events, `surprises.' A fter brie y and selectively reviewing the
\par
literature on uncertainty and surprise, we adopt a de nition of `surprise' that does not
\par
include the strict requirement that it apply to a wholly unexpected outcome, but rather
\par
recognizes that many events are often anticipated by some, even if not most observers.
\par
Thus, we de ne `imaginable surprise' as events or processes that depart from the expec-
\par
tations of some de nable community. Therefore, what gets labelled as `surprise' depends
\par
on the extent to which what happens departs from community expectations and on the
\par
salience of the problem. We offer a typology of surprise that distinguishes imaginable
\par
surprises from risk and uncertainty, and develops several kinds of impediments to over -
\par
coming ignorances. These range from the need for more `normal science' to
\par
phenomenological impediments (e.g., inherent unpredictability in some chaotic systems)
\par
to epistemological ignorance (e.g., ideological blocks to reducing ignorance). Based on
\par
the input of some two dozen scholars at an A spen Global Change Institute Summer
\par
Workshop in 1994*
\par
, we constr uct two tables in which participants offer many possible
\par
`imaginable surprises' in the global change context, as well as their potential salience
\par
for creating unexpectedly high or low carbon dioxide emissions. Improving the antici-
\par
pation of surprises is an interdisciplinary enterprise that should offer a sceptical
\par
welcoming of outlier ideas and methods.
\par
1. Surprise! Ozone hole discovered over A ntarctica in 1985
\par
G iven all the modern satellite technology in the possession of NA SA and other U S
\par
agencies, it is ironic that the opening of a hole in the ozone over the South Pole in the
\par
late 1970s went undetected for years. The satellite instrumentation did not fail us; rather,
\par
the compu ter programs written to diagnose the vast volu mes of satellite data were
\par
speci cally instructed to reject measurements that diverged sharply from expected
\par
*The authors wish to thank the members of 1994 Aspen Global Change Institute Workshop for two stimulating
\par
weeks of discussions on which much of this article is based. In particular, we appreciate the extensive comments,
\par
reference su ggestions and other assistance we received from J.X. Kasperson, and the help in modifying Fig. 1 from
\par
David Victor.
\par
1366-9877 \copyright{} 1998 E \& FN Spon166 Schneider et al.
\par
normal conditions. For example, every time a very high or low value came in, the
\par
computer programs omitted it. The rejected values were called to no one's attention.
\par
Incredibly, the phenomenon overhead went undetected by our high-technology for
\par
nearly a decade. Rather, incredulous British scientists, plotting by hand their own
\par
ground-based records of how much ultraviolet (U V) radiation was reaching the earth's
\par
surface at their station on the coast of A ntarctica (Farman et al., 1985), detected a
\par
steady decrease in the springtime ozone in the southern hemisphere from the mid-1970s
\par
to the mid-1980s. This unexpected phenomenon immediately triggered a reprogram-
\par
ming of the US data to allow all values, and there in beautiful living (false) colours for
\par
all to see (ideal for television) were maps showing a deep hole in the ozone over the
\par
Antarctic continent growing in intensity over time and drifting over nearby oceans and
\par
continents. This example shows that, sometimes, the knowable remains undetected
\par
because of the assumptions or views that frame the question or methods of analysis.
\par
2. Not so surprising: climatic surprises are imaginable
\par
In the wake of the heat waves, res, and droughts of 1988, media coverage of global
\par
warming exploded. Not surprisingly, so did congressional hearings on climate. Robert
\par
Watson, then NA SA scientist and later head of the U S delegation to the plenary session
\par
of the 1995 Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) testi ed to the Senate
\par
Energy Committee1 that `scienti c uncertainty is a thing that scares me more than
\par
absolute knowledge. The fact that we have said that climate could possibly change
\par
dramatically and quickly has me concerned. The A ntarctic ozone thing came as a
\par
surprise.' To that remark, New Jersey Senator Bill Bradley pressed the panel of scien-
\par
tist witnesses to speculate out-lou d on what kinds of surprises they had in mind. One
\par
of us (SH S), after quipping that surprises are things you don't know about, nonethe-
\par
less followed with a list of highly uncertain, but plausible outcomes, like a ip- op in
\par
North Atlantic ocean currents, a surge in polar ice masses, or unexpected regional
\par
patterns of climate response to build-ups of greenhouse gases. O ther witnesses also had
\par
their hunches as to a number of such possible `su rprises'
\par
. The willingness of the witnesses
\par
to specu late on speci c `surprises' illustrates that scientists, as well as members of the
\par
public, deal often with the anticipation of the `unexpected'!
\par
Strictly speaking, a su rprise cannot be anticipated; by de nition it is an unexpected
\par
event. Potential climate change, and more broadly global environmental change, is
\par
replete with this kind of surprise -- the truly unexpected -- because of the enormous
\par
complexities of processes and interrelationships involved and our insuf cient under-
\par
standing of them (such as coupling ocean, atmosphere, and terrestrial systems) (e.g.,
\par
Darmstadter and Toman, 1993a; Broecker, 1994; Casti, 1994; Chapter 1 and Summary
\par
for Policymakers, IPCC, 1996) as well as our improved u nderstanding of the existence
\par
of chaos in such complexity (e.g., Cohen and Stewart, 1994; R obinson, 1982). The IPCC
\par
(1996) Summary for Policymakers concludes:
\par
`Future unexpected, large and rapid climate system changes (as have occurred in the past)
\par
are, by their nature dif cult to predict. This implies that future climate changes may also
\par
involve ``surprises.'' In particular these arise from the non-linear nature of the climate
\par
system. When rapidly forced, non-linear systems are especially subject to unexpected
\par
behavior.'
\par
1 See chapter 2 of Schneider, 1990, for details on this hearing.G lobal change science 167
\par
Yet, by focusing on the perceptions of and responses to environmental events by the
\par
public and expert communities alike, as indicated in the Senate Energy Committee
\par
exchange noted, the existence of a different kind of `surprise' emerges, one that can
\par
be anticipated (Kates and Clark, 1996). R isk-hazard and related research demonstrates
\par
repeatedly that the event, process, or outcome registered as a su rprise by the commu-
\par
nity in question was frequently known or forecast by others or the same event was
\par
knowable within the competing frameworks of understanding (Darmstadter and Toman,
\par
1993b). This message also emerged from the 1994 Aspen Global Change Institute
\par
(A G CI) session on `A nticipating G lobal Change Surprise,' (A ppendix 1) the resu lts of
\par
which we, the co-chairs (Schneider and Turner, 1995) of that session, report here. We
\par
sketch the rationale used to reach the conclusion that the search for anticipated surprises
\par
constitutes a fruitful avenue of research and then present the results of exercises to
\par
identify what some of these surprises might be, how they could affect the impacts of
\par
climate and other global changes, and how we might enlarge or focus our vision of this
\par
kind of surprise.
\par
3. From uncertainty to surprise: a brief review
\par
Surprise is an attribu te of events or interpretations of them that is closely akin to the
\par
related attributes of risk and u ncertainty. Various meanings of these attributes may be
\par
fused at times, owing to the different communities and cultures employing them. The
\par
interdisciplinary risk-hazard research commu nity, however, has devoted considerable
\par
attention to this and related concepts, a brief review of which informs this work.
\par
Much of the cu rrent work on surprise has grown out of an extensive body of research
\par
on uncertainty.
\par
2 Yet, although widely acknowledged and studied, uncertainty remains
\par
a dif cult concept to de ne or codify. Different conceptualizations and approaches to
\par
uncertainty abound in the literature, crossing numerous elds of study and touching a
\par
wide range of problem types. Two basic options are invariably followed in the face of
\par
uncertainty, however. The rst is to reduce the uncertainties through data collection,
\par
research, modelling, simulation techniques, and so forth. Following this option, the
\par
objective is to overcome uncertainty, to make the unknown known. But the dau nting
\par
nature of uncertainties surrou nding global environmental change, as well as the need
\par
to make decisions before the `normal' science option can provide resolution, force a
\par
second option -- that of managing or integrating uncertainty directly into the decision-
\par
making or policy-making process. Before uncertainty can be so integrated, however,
\par
the nature and extent of the uncertainty must be clari ed. This understanding is
\par
approached in several ways, which we brie y review here.
\par
The elds of mathematics, statistics, and more recently physics, provide the `science
\par
of uncertainty' with many powerful means and techniques to conceptualize, quantify,
\par
and manage uncertainty, ranging from the frequency distributions of probability theory,
\par
to the possibility and belief statements of Bayesian statistics, and even to a method for
\par
quantifying ignorance (between belief and disbelief) found in D empster-Shafer theory
\par
(for examples see Tonn, 1991; A yyub et al., 1992; Yager, 1992). A ddressing other aspects
\par
of uncertainty, fuzzy set logic offers an alternative to classical set theory for situa-
\par
tions where the de nitions of set membership are vagu e, ambiguous, or non-exclusive
\par
2 For early discu ssions on this subject, the reader is directed to the literature cited, as well as the following: Clark
\par
and Mu nn, 1986; Kasperson et al., 1988; Kates, 1985; Svedin and Aniansson, 1987; Timmermann, 1986; and Toth
\par
et al., 1989.168 Schneider et al.
\par
(e.g., Zadeh, 1965, 1990). More recently, researchers have proposed chaos theory and
\par
complexi cation theory to focus on expecting the unexpected in models and theory
\par
(e.g., Casti, 1994).
\par
The practical application of many of these techniques was originally pioneered by
\par
researchers in decision analysis (see Raiffa, 1968). In the elds of economics and
\par
decision theory, researchers continue to study rational decision making u nder uncer-
\par
tainty and how to assess the value of collecting additional information (e.g., Clemen,
\par
1991). Methods for modelling risk attitudes, leading to the terms risk-prone and risk-
\par
adverse, attempt to capture how different people faced with making a decision react
\par
to the u ncertainty su rrounding the expected ou tcomes. Looking towards future
\par
outcomes in projecting climate change impacts and estimating marginal costs and bene-
\par
ts of mitigation efforts, economists such as Nordhaus (1991, 1993) and Yohe (1991,
\par
1993) explicitly address uncertainties, and discuss methods for better measuring nonlin-
\par
earities leading to surprises. In the context of energy, tables of possible, but uncertain,
\par
risks of alternative energy systems have been prepared (e.g., Schneider, 1979).
\par
Uncertainty and, in its related context, surprise, are treated largely as the realization
\par
that events, currently unknown, will occur affecting the nal outcome of a decision.
\par
This acknowledgment of uncertainty has found a prominent place in many other elds
\par
of study, each one speaking its own language of uncertainty. For example, researchers
\par
making risk assessments and setting safety standards nd it most useful to distinguish
\par
between risk (the probability of a certain negative effect resulting from a hazard occur-
\par
rence, given the speci ed level of exposure), variability (inter-individual differences in
\par
vulnerability and susceptibility), and uncertainty (model parameter variability and any
\par
unexplained residual) (e.g., Bogen and Spear, 1987). This three-pronged distinction
\par
provides information on uncertainty tailored to the needs and research questions of the
\par
risk analyst. Researchers in other elds of study, for instance computer science, may
\par
be most interested in addressing other aspects of uncertainty, such as how it affects the
\par
decision ow or logic of the computer system. Thus, in writing for other computer scien-
\par
tists, Bonissone and Tong (1985) suggest a conceptu alization that identi es four sources
\par
of uncertainty in an expert system: (i) inaccu racy in the set of facts making up the
\par
knowledge base; (ii) imprecision in the decision rule representation language; (iii)
\par
incomplete information; and (iv) aggregation of rules from different knowledge sources.
\par
The above examples are not meant to imply that separate uncertainty analyses follow
\par
strict disciplinary bounds. While the last two examples offer approaches to uncertainty
\par
targeted speci cally to their respective disciplines, some of the concepts are clearly
\par
transferable to other disciplines and to other types of decision making processes,
\par
including those concerning global environmental change. Consequently, research on
\par
uncertainty cross-cu ts a number of different disciplines. In work related to hazards and
\par
risk, sociologists, anthropologists, psychologists, and geographers have made important
\par
contributions to the discussions on risk perception, risk communication, and the social
\par
ampli cation of risk (Douglas and Wildavsky, 1982; Fischhoff et al., 1982; Kahneman
\par
et al., 1982; Kasperson et al., 1988). Similarly, work on visualizing or graphically
\par
conveying uncertainty also crosses a diverse set of disciplines including psychology,
\par
computer science, and geographic information systems (e.g., Butten eld and Beard,
\par
1991; MacEachren, 1992).
\par
Due to its pervasive nature, a desire to understand u ncertainty encourages researchers
\par
to join forces in interdisciplinary projects, conferences, and workshops drawing on the
\par
wider breadth of knowledge, techniques, and experiences. Recent interdisciplinaryG lobal change science 169
\par
efforts in the area of global environmental change, including the Intergovernmental
\par
Panel on Climate Change A ssessment, IPCC, (1996) and the Resources for the Future
\par
workshop (Darmstadter and Toman, 1993a,b), have made uncertainty an explicit and
\par
central research concern, as has a recent A spen G lobal Change Institute Workshop
\par
(Moss and Schneider, 1997). Given the interdisciplinary nature of the study of global
\par
environmental change, it is not dif cu lt to see how research in this eld has drawn on
\par
a wide range of techniques.
\par
These activities and accomplishments notwithstanding, the answer to the question of
\par
how to de ne or codify u ncertainty remains unresolved. Communities of researchers
\par
tend to develop de nitions and taxonomies of uncertainty speci cally related to the
\par
types and range of policy questions that they address in their research. Although no
\par
all-encompassing typology of uncertainty exists, the struggle to de ne the u nknown
\par
aspects of a eld of science serves a very real purpose. The typologies make explicit
\par
what is not known, and break it down into distinct components. D ecomposing uncer-
\par
tainty into different dimensions provides insight into the nature of the uncertainty. The
\par
dimensions also offer some structure on how to approach or reduce the uncertainties,
\par
make the most of available information, and utilize both what is known and what is
\par
unknown in decision making.
\par
One of the most extensive and comprehensive conceptualizations of uncertainty is
\par
that developed by Smithson (1988). Smithson's taxonomy is based on the sociology of
\par
knowledge, in which he moves the applicability of the social constructivists' dictum that
\par
knowledge is socially constructed and negotiated to ignorance; it too is a social construc-
\par
tion. Thus, uncertainty is de ned by Smithson as an incompleteness of knowledge or
\par
information that is caused by vagueness, probability, and ambiguity. To provide some
\par
perspective on how this de nition relates to others in his taxonomy, consider a few
\par
more of his categorizations: ignorance is encountered when a person fails to agree or
\par
show awareness of ideas that another person de nes as actually or potentially valid;
\par
error refers to being in an erroneous cognitive state; taboo is socially enforced irrele-
\par
vance; and irrelevance is the act of ignoring something.
\par
Other typologies of uncertainty exist, each making their own contributions to the
\par
wider understanding of uncertainty (Brooks, 1986; Funtowicz and R avetz, 1990; Faber
\par
et al., 1992; Wynne, 1992; Casti, 1994; Rowe, 1994; Moser, 1997). H ere, we will limit
\par
the discussion to three of these -- Functowicz and Ravetz (1990), Wynne (1992), and
\par
R owe (1994). Selected because they are widely cited and recognized in the global envi-
\par
ronmental change literature: they provide interesting insights into the role of uncertainty
\par
in science for policy making, and they provide the most appropriate context for the
\par
concept of uncertainty in our usage below.
\par
Functowicz and R avetz rank situations on an incremental scale from those possessing
\par
low to high levels of u ncertainty. They show how different management approaches
\par
correspond with the level of system uncertainty matched with varying degrees of deci-
\par
sion stakes. Where decision stakes and the level of systems uncertainty are low
\par
constitutes, the realm of applied science and the type of uncertainty encountered is
\par
technical. A s the decision stakes and the level of uncertainty increase, the realm of
\par
professional consultancy and methodological uncertainty exists. Functowicz and Ravetz
\par
use the term `professional consultancy' to describe a `learned art' involving personal
\par
judgment that depends on higher-level, interpretive skills. Science in this realm requires
\par
the scientist to make qualitative assessments or judgments, setting con dence limits
\par
(formal or informal), around methods and estimates. When both the decision stakes170 Schneider et al.
\par
and the level of systems uncertainty are high, epistemological u ncertainty is encoun-
\par
tered in the realm of `second order' science. Functowicz and Ravetz use the term `second
\par
order' science to de ne an area outside the traditional quantitative methods of applied
\par
science. This term takes into account the postmodernist argument that all knowledge
\par
is socially constructed and that science is only one way of producing knowledge. When
\par
they extend uncertainty into this realm, Fu nctowicz and Ravetz are acknowledging that
\par
the uncertainties identi ed by science are in part de ned by society as a whole. There
\par
may be ignorance abou t ignorance. Functowicz and Ravetz call for `post-normal'
\par
methods of evaluation to address these issues, although other terms have also been
\par
suggested (Weinberg (1972) uses the term `trans-scienti c' and O 'R iordan and Cameron
\par
(1994) propose `civic-scienti c').
\par
Wynne (1992) emphasizes that the modelling of environmental risk systems requires
\par
examination of not only the scienti c evidence and competing interpretations, but also
\par
investigation of the nature, assu mptions, and inherent limitations of the scienti c knowl-
\par
edge behind the data and the model. He identi es four types of uncertainty -- risk,
\par
uncertainty, ignorance, and indeterminacy -- each overlaying dimensions of uncertainty.
\par
Risk refers to a situation when the system behaviour is well known and the chances of
\par
different outcomes can be quanti ed by probability distributions. If, however, the impor-
\par
tant system parameters are known but not the associated probabilities, uncertainty
\par
exists. Ignorance is that which is not known (or even awareness that we do not know
\par
it) and, for Wynne, is endemic because scienti c knowledge must set the bounds of
\par
uncertainty in order to function. Indeterminacy captu res the unbounded complexity of
\par
causal chains and open networks. Uncertainty, in part, stems not only from an incom-
\par
plete understanding of determinate relationships, but from the interaction of these
\par
relationships with contingent and unpredictable actors and processes.
\par
Rowe (1994) de nes uncertainty as `the absence of information, information that
\par
may or may not be obtainable' (p. 743), and identi es four dimensions of uncertainty
\par
predicated on their sou rce inherent in any decision making process: temporal (in both
\par
past and fu ture states); structural (due to complexity); metrical (in measurement); and
\par
translational (derived in explaining uncertain results). The fourth dimension, transla-
\par
tional uncertainty, comes into play only after the rst three have been considered. These
\par
dimensions can exist simultaneou sly in any situation, although one or more may tend
\par
to dominate. R owe pays special attention to the issue of variability and identi es it as
\par
a contributor to uncertainty in all dimensions. R owe (1994) does not offer a distinct
\par
de nition of variability. However, he does identify three primary sources of variability:
\par
(1) U nderlying Variants -- variants inherent in natural systems contributing to the spread
\par
of parameter values, including randomness, inconsistent hu man behaviour, and chaotic
\par
or nonlinear dynamic systems behaviour; (2) Collective/Individual Membership
\par
Assignment -- the distinction between collective behaviour and a single instance of
\par
behaviour for a parameter; and, (3) Value D iversity -- varying perspectives and value
\par
systems among people.
\par
A lthou gh different in their characterizations of uncertainty, all three typologies
\par
attempt to expand the conceptualization to recognize that uncertainty is not purely of
\par
a technical or physical or biological character, but also social, cultural, and institutional
\par
in nature. By drawing out dimensions of uncertainty, such as epistemological uncer-
\par
tainty, ignorance, indeterminacy and translational uncertainty, these conceptualizations
\par
offer insights into the role of uncertainty and science in global environmental change
\par
(Wynne 1980, 1987, 1992; Morgan et al., 1990; O 'R iordan and R ayner, 1991; D oversG lobal change science 171
\par
and Handmer, 1995). In the areas of environmental policy and resource management,
\par
policy makers struggle with the need to make decisions utilizing vagu e and ambiguous
\par
concepts (such as sustainability), with sparse and imprecise information, in decisions
\par
that have far-reaching, and often irreversible, impacts on both environment and society.
\par
Not surprisingly, efforts to incorporate u ncertainty into the decision-making process
\par
quickly move to the forefront with the advent of decision-making paradigms, such as
\par
the precautionary principle, adaptive environmental management, the preventative
\par
paradigm or stewardship (e.g., Brown, 1997). A s noted by Wynne, the shift towards
\par
prevention in environmental policy `implies an acceptance of the inherent limitations
\par
of the anticipatory knowledge on which decisions about environmental discharges [and
\par
other environmental problems] are based' (1992, p. 111).
\par
Ravetz (1986) takes the concept of `usable knowledge in the context of incomplete
\par
science' one step further by introdu cing the idea of usable ignorance. To Ravetz,
\par
acknowledging the `ignorance factor' means becoming aware of the limits of ou r knowl-
\par
edge -- an idea we pursue below. Ravetz argues that ignorance cannot be overcome
\par
with any amount of sophisticated calcu lations. Rather, coping with ignorance demands
\par
a better articulation of the policy process and a greater awareness of how that process
\par
operates. H e recognizes that one can only replace ignorance by gaining more knowl-
\par
edge, but stresses that by `being aware of our ignorance we do not encounter disastrous
\par
pitfalls in our supposedly secure knowledge or supposedly effective technique' (p. 429).
\par
Following this lead, D overs and H andmer (1995) offer a step-by-step framework for
\par
what they term `ignorance auditing.' In this framework, the policy maker is asked to
\par
explicitly de ne the types, causes, and sources of uncertainty -- and more speci cally
\par
ignorance -- affecting the decision at hand. The policy maker then identi es methods
\par
to address these uncertainties where possible and consciously implements them within
\par
the policy process or management task.
\par
The emphasis on managing uncertainty rather than mastering it can be traced to
\par
work on resilience in ecology (most notably by H olling, 1973, 1986). Whereas resis-
\par
tance implies an ability to withstand change or impact within some measure of
\par
performance, resilience captures the ability to give with the forcing function, without
\par
disrupting the overall health of the system. In this framework, adaptation is an ecolog-
\par
ical mechanism whose aim is not to overcome or control environmental uncertainty,
\par
but to live with, and in some case, thrive upon, it. It is interesting to note that H olling's
\par
classical work did not focus on uncertainty per se bu t on surprise.
\par
4. De ning surprise
\par
The A G CI session on `Anticipating G lobal Change Surprise' drew upon many experts
\par
(A ppendix 1) in global environmental change and from the natural hazards community,
\par
most of whom were largely u nfamiliar with the extensive literature noted above.
\par
Nevertheless, drawing upon a portion of it, the resulting de nitions adopted in the session
\par
strongly correspond to principal ideas embedded within that literature. The following is
\par
a description of the workshop's distinctions among the terms as they relate to surprise:
\par
(i) risk-- the condition in which the event, process or outcome, and the proba-
\par
bility that each will occur, is known.
\par
In reality, of course (coins and unloaded dice notwithstanding) complete or perfect
\par
knowledge of complex systems rarely exists, which would permit the credible calculation of172 Schneider et al.
\par
objective probabilities. Likewise, the full range of potential outcomes is usually not known.
\par
Thus, risk almost always is accompanied by varying degrees of uncertainty.
\par
(ii) uncertainty-- The condition in which the event, process, or outcome is known
\par
(factually or hypothetically), but the probabilities that it will occur are not
\par
known, or are highly subjective estimates.
\par
Typically, when probabilities are assigned they are subjective (or depend upon subjec-
\par
tive assumptions -- Schneider, 1994), and the ways to establish the reliability of different
\par
subjective probability estimates are debatable (e.g., Morgan et al., 1990; Morgan and
\par
Keith, 1995; another A G CI workshop, this time on methods to deal explicitly and
\par
formally with uncertainty in international assessments, contains further discussion and
\par
references -- H assol and Katzenberger, 1997).
\par
(iii) surprise-- The condition in which the event, process or outcome is not known
\par
or expected.
\par
In this `strict' meaning, the attribution of surprise shifts toward the event, process,
\par
or outcome itself -- is it a new or wholly unexpected experience or not? We may expect
\par
surprises to occur, but we are surprised by the speci c event, process, or outcome
\par
involved. This meaning, as noted, begs the issue of anticipation because the very act
\par
of anticipation implies some level of knowledge or foresight. It is, therefore, not partic-
\par
ularly interesting or useful for policy purposes. O ne exception is those cases where the
\par
conditions that might induce surprises -- for instance, rapid forcing of non-linear systems
\par
as quoted earlier from the IPCC 1996 Summary for Policymakers -- are known, even
\par
though the actual surprise events are not. This exception, which we cou ld term `imag-
\par
inable conditions for surprise'
\par
, could have policy meaning, since actions could be
\par
proposed to mitigate the conditions in which surprise might be induced (e.g., slowing
\par
down the rate of global change forcing, as in Chapter 6 of Schneider, 1997).
\par
Because of the impracticality of the strict de nition of surprise for policy making,
\par
various studies advocate the use of another meaning for surprise, one in which the attri-
\par
bution of `surprise' shifts more towards the expectations of the observer. Holling (1986:
\par
294) recognized this meaning of surprise as a condition in which perceived reality
\par
departs qualitatively from expectations. It is this more interpretive or relational meaning
\par
of surprise -- one we label `imaginable surprise'
\par
-- that portends to be most useful for
\par
global change studies.
\par
(iv) imaginable surprise-- The event, process, or outcome departs from the expec-
\par
tations of the observing community or those affected by the event or process.
\par
`Seen from this point of view, surprise about one or another aspect of climate
\par
change is an after-the-fact reaction to an observation or new scienti c nding
\par
that, in some sense, lies outside our range of expectations' (Darmstadter and
\par
Toman, 1993b: 3).
\par
A lmost every event may constitute an imaginable surprise to someone. But since
\par
global change phenomena and their environmental and societal impacts are a commu-
\par
nity-scale set of issues, little can be gained for our purposes by focusing on whether
\par
someone, somewhere, may or may not have once predicted or hinted at some surprise
\par
event. More fruitful is the recognition that groups, communities, and cultures may share
\par
expectations such that a particular event is likely to qualify as a surprise for most within
\par
them. In these cases, what gets labelled as a surprise depends upon the extent to whichG lobal change science 173
\par
reality departs from commu nity expectations, and on the salience of the problems
\par
imposed.
\par
Imaginable surprise applies to communities of experts, policy makers, managers, and
\par
educators who share common ranges of expectation that are generated by group
\par
dynamics, leaders and signal processors, including the dominant educational and
\par
research paradigms (Kasperson et al., 1988). For these communities, shared expecta-
\par
tions follow from dominant interpretations among the expert community (e.g., global
\par
warming is likely, NR C, 1992 or IPCC, 1996), from their t with broader policy agendas
\par
(e.g., environmentally benign economic development is possible), and from vested
\par
interest, conscious or unconscious, of an agency or group to maintain a particular view
\par
(e.g., global population growth is environmentally damaging, or, alternatively, good for
\par
the economy -- e.g., Myers and Simon, 1995). Since policy making often re ects a blend
\par
of public and interest group perceptions of reality, the imaginable surprise formulation
\par
is mu ch more relevant to global change policy issues than a strict de nition of surprise
\par
as an unimaginable outcome.
\par
5. A typology of surprise
\par
The distinction between `strict' surprise and `imaginable' surprise is important but not
\par
su f ciently developed to be very useful for dealing with surprise phenomena. More
\par
exhaustive treatments of the sub-categories of surprise or the sources of surprise reveal
\par
that there are signi cant nuances with important implications for global change stu dies
\par
or policies (Kates and Clark, 1996). Just as with uncertainty, different de nitions and
\par
typologies of surprise have been proposed. Brooks (1986), for example, offers a simple
\par
tripartite typology: (i) unexpected discrete events (such as the oil shocks of 1973 and
\par
1979 or the Three Mile Island reactor incident); (ii) discontinuities in long-term trends
\par
(such as the acceleration of USA oil imports between 1966 and 1973); and (iii) the
\par
su dden emergence into political consciousness of new information (such as the relation
\par
between uorocarbon production and stratospheric ozone). This typology focuses on
\par
the qualitative nature of the disjuncture between the event as it occurs and the event
\par
as it was anticipated. Timmerman (1986), in contrast, focuses on the increasing inten-
\par
sity of the effects of surprises, distinguishing four types of surprises: anomalies, shocks,
\par
epiphanies, and catastrophes. In this typology, surprises are rated on a scale from anom-
\par
alies whose effects are barely noticed to catastrophes that cause unrecoverable damage.
\par
A typology by Faber and colleagues (1992) is particularly useful for the `imaginable'
\par
meaning because it highlights the relationships between the source of surprise and the
\par
observer, in particular identifying different sources of ignorance that in uence what is
\par
registered as a surprise by the observer. Since ignorance, or its opposite, understanding,
\par
in large part shapes expectations, the AG CI session, post-session review, and our own
\par
su bsequent work has led us to develop a variant of the Faber-Manstetten-Proops typo-
\par
logical map (Figure 1) that emphasizes the source of the expectations themselves and
\par
the impediments to changing them.
\par
The Faber-Manstetten-Proops version recognizes the distinctions made above
\par
between risk and uncertainty, and imaginable surprise. Because the two former involve
\par
events, processes, and outcomes that are known (expected) by most in the community,
\par
even if the probabilities are not fully understood, we may label imaginable surprise
\par
associated with risk and uncertainty as a `quasi-surprise.' We do not address these cate-
\par
gories, although we suggest that the impediments to understanding imaginable surprises174 Schneider et al.
