\par
Imaginable su rprises occur among events, processes, and outcomes that are not all
\par
known: the other path from sources of expectation on our typological map. This path
\par
divides further on the ability of the observer to change expectations, either `closed' or
\par
`open' in kind. Closed expectations are simplest, as they involve denial of the possi-
\par
bility of unexpected ou tcomes and thus an unwillingness (entrenched ignorance) even
\par
to consider the possibility of a larger range of events or understanding. This source of
\par
surprise cannot be changed unless cognitive alternatives are entertained by the observer
\par
and, by de nition, to entertain such alternatives engages avenues of open expectations,
\par
the more complex and interesting path in our typological map.
\par
O pen expectations -- or the recognition or acknowledgment that all events, processes,
\par
and outcomes are not known -- is further subdivided into `easy-
\par
' and `hard-to-enlarge'
\par
expectations. The `easy' sub-categories involve two impediments: personal (those of the
\par
individual), which can be enlarged through learning; and communal (those of groups,
\par
communities and cultures), which are enlarged through the application of more `normal'
\par
science, i.e., research. The research process is that of open experimentation and critiqu e,
\par
often leading to improved understanding, and hence expectations. Personal learning orG lobal change science 175
\par
`education' can lead an observer (or research can educate a community) back to the
\par
`quasi-surprise' category of risk and uncertainty, shown by the dashed line in the gu re.
\par
A s we note below, however, numerou s barriers impede this process of enlarging expec-
\par
tations by learning and research, and these barriers need to be u nderstood explicitly
\par
and taken into account in any attempt to reduce surprise.
\par
The `hard' sub-categories include two major classes of impediments to improved
\par
expectations. Phenomenological impediments involve the suite of factors that act as
\par
barriers to knowledge-building, at least in the short run, such as inadequate technology
\par
or modes of analysis. This sub-category should not be confused with closed expecta-
\par
tions or open expectations of the communal kind. Closed expectations rest within the
\par
rigid, unchanging belief system of the observing community, whereas communal imped-
\par
iments can be overcome in principle by providing more attention to the problem set
\par
in qu estion by applications of existing ways of knowing. Impediments of the phenom-
\par
enological kind, in contrast, rest in the sometimes momentary inadequacies of the tools
\par
and skills of the community. Non-momentary, fundamental limits may also exist,
\par
su ch as the inability to forecast accurately weather details beyond a few weeks.
\par
U npredictability, owing to the chaotic nature of large-scale atmospheric dynamics, is
\par
an example (e.g., Lorenz, 1969). A lthou gh so-called normal science may well lead to
\par
breakthroughs that will one day permit credible forecasts past currently believed
\par
predictability limits, it remains possible (even likely) that no amount of communal
\par
research effort can lead to a breakthrough that would breach this apparent phenome-
\par
nological impediment.
\par
Finally, epistemological impediments follow from the ways in which the various
\par
observing communities view the world and build their understanding within it (e.g., the
\par
compu ter programming that missed the ozone hole). If alternative epistemologies will
\par
not be explored in principle or because of social conventions, closed expectations will
\par
exist, even if unconsciously. For this set of `open' sub-categories, however, the episte-
\par
mological impediment follows largely from an uncritical belief in a favoured view,
\par
perspective, or paradigm: the kind of view inherent in the programming of data analysis
\par
in our ozone story. Science now well knows that it operates by way of prevailing
\par
paradigms (our epistemologies) that gu ide understanding, and hence expectations, for
\par
particular communities. A paradigm may dominate for a time, providing conditions
\par
temporarily not dissimilar to closed expectations. Science communities are usually su f -
\par
ciently diverse, however, to maintain competing paradigms that constantly challenge
\par
the prevailing perspective. When these challenges are successfu l (i.e., provide better
\par
understanding), the community eventually adopts the alternatives. It is important to
\par
recognize that contemporary behaviou ral and social sciences -- an important compo-
\par
nent of the study of imaginable surprise in climate change -- has been in a prolonged
\par
state of competing paradigms that constantly challenge one another (Guba, 1990).
\par
Our rudimentary typology (Figure 1) is useful for at least two reasons. First, it places
\par
su rprise in relation to observing commu nities, providing a logic for the `imaginable' kind.
\par
A nd second, the categories in the typology provide clues about how to deal with surprises
\par
associated with the source of expectation, a subject to which we will return below.
