7. Improving the anticipation of surprise in global change
\par
A lthough we cannot explore the sources of every surprise that appears in our tables,
\par
as this would require an extensive research endeavour, most of them would t well
\par
within the categories of the `open expectations' path in our typological map. Those
\par
su rprises involving the environmental forcing functions and consequences of climate
\par
change often tend to follow from inadequate attention given to the problem (communal
\par
impediments) or our inability at this time to measure or model adequately the processes
\par
involved (phenomenological impediments). Examples of communal impediments
\par
include:
\par
l Dimethyl sul de emissions decline with reduced sea ice.
\par
l Climate variability changes with global temperature rise.
\par
l Hurricane intensity changes with global warming.
\par
Expanded conventional research efforts could, in principle, reduce ignorance and thus
\par
alter expectations in these categories. Examples where fundamental levels of knowl-
\par
edge of baseline data or functional relationships create signi cant phenomenological
\par
impediments include:
\par
l Cascading ecological effects (e.g., where loss of one species causes a dramatic
\par
pest explosion).
\par
l Land-cover changes in South A merica.180 Schneider et al.
\par
Table 2. Candidate global climate change surprises arranged so that they (A) create a condition
\par
mu ch exceeding a doubling of CO2 by 2050, and (B) create a condition of mu ch less than doubling
\par
of CO2 by 2050.
\par
A . Greenhouse gas concentrations are far more than equivalent to a doubling of CO 2 by 2050
\par
-- More than 50\% of incremental CO2 remains in the atmosphere, sinks become saturated,
\par
and world emission rates grow sharply --
\par
(i) No signi cant policies are adopted because
\par
