\par
B. Greenhouse gas concentrations are far less than equivalent to a doubling of CO2 by 2050
\par
-- World emission rates peak and decline in the near future --
\par
(i) Strong international agreements to constrain emissions are implemented.
\par
l Dramatic weather extremes motivate political action.
\par
l Biodiversity preservation becomes of major societal value.
\par
(ii) Rapid decarbonization of energy systems takes place because
\par
l low-cost biomass alternatives are developed;
\par
l arti cial photosynthesis is mastered;
\par
l inherently safe, inexpensive nu clear power is developed;
\par
l large natural gas discoveries are made in India and China;
\par
l China and Brazil develop a large biomass-energy industry;
\par
l fuel-cell-powered cars trigger an ef cient, hydrogen-based economy.
\par
(iii) Energy/GNP ratio declines sharply because
\par
l low-energy using technology is improved and adopted globally;
\par
l development increases per capita GNP sharply in developing world.
\par
(iv) World economic growth rates decline sharply because
\par
l of the demise of the nation state leading to con ict and collapse;
\par
l of the emergence of a new, qu ick-acting and highly contagious virus redu cing
\par
populations globally.
\par
(v) Minimal deforestation takes place because
\par
l land-use cover stabilizes in South America and elsewhere in the tropics.G lobal change science 181
\par
Some of the surprises involving anthropogenic causes of and responses to climate change
\par
involve these kinds of impediments, but many more also follow from various com-
\par
munities preferred (conscious or not) perspectives and interpretations and their inability
\par
to reconcile the differences across community perspectives (epistemological impedi-
\par
ments). For example:
\par
l A n underclass of nations is maintained owing to the diminished process of glob-
\par
alization.
\par
l Ideological preconceptions and nationalism lock out political consciousness of
\par
`global commons' problems at scales larger than nation states.
\par
In either of these cases, competing epistemologies have fundamentally different (and
\par
often irreconcilable) interpretations of the intent, function, and outcomes of the preva-
\par
lent capitalist system as well as about the priority to be given to economic versus other
\par
social structures in these interpretations. Recognizing that communal, phenomeno-
\par
logical, and epistemological in uences on the range of expectations put restrictions on
\par
the range of imaginable surprise, the global change community can tap various means
\par
of dealing with them. For example, global change studies are forced to deal with the
\par
(i) connectivity and (ii) complexity of natural and human systems which, in turn, require
\par
that problem domains, disciplines, and perspectives be crossed (also Darmstadter and
\par
Toman, 1993b, p. 3). Connectivity and complexity may run counter to the predominant
\par
approach to problem solving in modern science -- to increase understanding by ever
\par
narrowing and re ning problems and analyses -- often within well-de ned disciplines.
\par
Such approaches, of course, carry with them implications for our three impediments.
\par
We suggest that some of the problems inherent in complexity and connectivity can be
\par
ameliorated: (a) by supporting work at and across the edges of the research cores that
\par
dominate the problem domains, disciplines, and perspectives (e.g., Schneider, 1988;
\par
Turner, 1991); (b) by complementing small- and large-scale approaches; and (c) by
\par
encouraging the role of synthesis and synthesizers in both (a) and (b). None of these
\par
su ggestions denies the fundamental importance of research within cores and by the core
\par
specialist -- the dominant current disciplinary science approach -- or the recognition that
\par
the root of global change studies involves systemic processes (e.g., embedded sub-cycles
\par
within the global nitrogen cycle), each of which must be understood as completely as
\par
possible at smaller scales. It does, however, argue for more integration and better
\par
balance among activities that could be labelled synthesis-edges versus specialization-
\par
cores. It is also important to recognize that systemic processes at large scales may not
\par
be captured adequ ately by a focu s on the patterns or trends of subsystems at small
\par
scales and that improved u nderstanding can be gained only by cycling back and forth
\par
between small- and large-scale levels of analysis, what Root and Schneider (1995) call
\par
strategic cyclical scaling.
\par
Global climate change research, for example, is, as it should be, dominated by the
\par
leading scientists and science centres (natural and social). These individuals and insti-
\par
tutions typically develop and share common views and opinions about various facets
\par
of the problem such as the likelihood that climate warming is taking place and the
\par
central role of models in the analysis. The dominance of these communities in the acad-
\par
emies and, hence, their role in agenda-setting for research and research funding (e.g.,
\par
see the debate on this issue between Brunner, 1996, and Edwards, 1996), may obscure
\par
alternative views and theses, making it dif cult for the alternatives to be evaluated
\par
properly (i.e., leading to epistemological or communal impediments). Since global182 Schneider et al.
\par
change science, both natural and social, remains in a range of developmental stages,
\par
the unknowns are suf ciently large to warrant attention to divergent themes about
\par
similar processes and outcomes. To facilitate this range of research, measures should
\par
be taken to ensure a more open discourse and evaluation of alternatives, such as (a)
\par
by a more open airing and professional evaluation (as opposed to uncritical, `equal
\par
time' and equal credibility often afforded to polarized viewpoints in the popular media
\par
-- e.g. Schneider, 1990, Chapter 7) of less dominant or unconventional views, including
\par
those by advocacy science and scientists; and (b) by reducing the redundancy of research
\par
focused on the dominant views and theses while still preserving a diversity of approaches
\par
within dominant paradigms, that is, create research `overlap-without-cloning.'
\par
Finally, global change portends alterations to the basic processes that govern the state
\par
of the biosphere. G lobal-change research, therefore, might do well to anticipate these
\par
alterations, an effort that will require us to do more than study extant processes and
\par
conditions alone. Various modes of analysis and approaches appropriate for such explo-
\par
rations, but typically underutilized in the research community, should be encouraged.
\par
Among these are: (a) backcasting scenarios from posited future states and/or recon-
\par
structing past scenarios in alternative ways to identify events or processes that might
\par
happen (recognizing, of cou rse, that diffusion processes usually are not reversible and
\par
diffusion-dominated systems cannot be meaningfully backcast); (b) increasing attention
\par
to and support for the study of `outlier' outcomes, searching for the reasons they appear
\par
deviant and the lessons that might be drawn from them (e.g., H assol and Katzenberger,
\par
1997); and, (c) exploring the `resilience' paradigm (e.g., precautionary principle) along-
\par
side the `optimization' paradigm (e.g., cost-bene t analyses) to inform policy making
\par
and diagnose alternative outcomes and risk management strategies. O ther means of
\par
improving the anticipation of surprise in global change science would emerge from
\par
convening additional expert groups and asking them for more exhaustive assessments
\par
of the issues than we have attempted here. We suspect, however, that balanced assess-
\par
ments will consistently lead to recommendations that `research as usual' be tempered
\par
with more `less-than-u sual' or even unusual research alternatives. Improvements in
\par
dealing with scienti c surprise in climate change in particular and global change in
\par
general, therefore, require the research and funding communities to seek a better
\par
balance among traditional and experimental research alternatives (also Kates and Clark,
\par
1996: 31). This aim, in turn, requires strategies that will facilitate this balance, including
\par
the dif cult problem of assessing `quality' in an interdisciplinary context (e.g., Schneider,
\par
1988).
\par
O f course, strategies that shift resources to more problem-oriented, high risk, or inte-
\par
grated studies are hard enough to fashion with expanding research su pport budgets, let
\par
alone the current situation where traditionally federally funded global change research
\par
(and its standard measures of excellence) are increasingly under scrutiny by ideolog-
\par
ical `anti-big-government' politicians or budget-cutting-for-its-own-sake legislators.
\par
Nevertheless, we contend that the biggest societal and environmental returns on
\par
research investments may, at least at the margins, lie in the su pport of unconventional
\par
studies focused on identifying and understanding outlier outcomes of the various inter-
\par
connected global change `experiments' now under way on Planet Earth.
\par
James L. Sweeney
\par
Dept. of Engineering Economic Systems and O perations R esearch, StanfordG lobal change science 183
