\textbf{Abstract}
\par
Decision analysis is a powerful methodology that can help clinicians make good decisions. Because it is not
\par
practical to place a decision analyst at the bedside in critical care units, the application of this methodology will
\par
require leveraging the analyst through computer-based systems. A decision class analysis is a collective analy-
\par
sis of a group of decisions that provides the high-level specification for such a computer system. This paper
\par
presents a decision class analysis of critical care life-support decisions. Key elements of this analysis are: the
\par
simplification of an otherwise extremely complex multistage sequential decision problem by using a sequence
\par
of two-stage models, and the use of six generic knowledge maps that capture the extremely complex relevant
\par
medical knowledge.
\par
Decision analysis is a mathematical discipline that
\par
clarifies difficult decisions [1]. Most applications of
\par
decision analysis have been in corporate settings,
\par
but medical applications are becoming more com-
\par
mon [2-5]. In an earlier paper we suggested that de-
\par
cision analysis has great potential for improving
\par
critical care decision-making [6]. Current decision
\par
analysis practice requires the participation of a pro-
\par
fessional decision analyst, much as medical practice
\par
requires a physician or surgeon and legal practice
\par
requires an attorney. Professional decision analysis,
\par
however, is quite costly. Furthermore, clinicians do
\par
not have the time to perform a detailed analysis of
\par
each life-support decision. But decision analysis can
\par
provide important insights not available from con-
\par
ventional critical care practice. Thus, we have
\par
sought to make the application of decision analysis
\par
faster and less dependent on rare and expensive
\par
analytical resources.
\par
Holtzmann has introduced the concept of the in-
\par
telligent decision system - a computer-based sys-
\par
tem that delivers expert-level decision analysis as-
\par
sistance [7]. An intelligent decision system that em-
\par
bodies both the domain expertise of critical care
\par
specialists and the modeling expertise of decision
\par
analysts could substitute for those professionals.
\par
Such a system could make decision analysis rou-
\par
tinely available to bedside physicians and nurses in
\par
the ICU. Clinicians would interact with the intelli-
\par
gent decision system to accomplish quickly and eco-
\par
nomically the activities required to obtain decision
\par
assistance based upon a rigorous analytical founda-
\par
tion.
\par
The first step in building such an intelligent deci-
\par
sion system is explicating expertise so that it can be
\par
represented by a computer program. This is a chal-
\par
lenging task. Life-support critical care decisions are
\par
marked by uncertainty, by difficult dynamics, and
\par
by a complex domain, Indeed, the applied physiolo-
\par
gy that underlies life-support therapies requires
\par
years of training and experience to be mastered by
\par
medical practitioners.
\par
Nevertheless, study of these decisions reveals re-
\par
current features. There is a common core of med-32
\par
ical knowledge that can be used to build the deci-
\par
sion model. There are typical modifications of the
\par
decision models that relate to specific clinical
\par
events, such as the acquisition of data. There are
\par
characteristic procedures that derive decision in-
\par
sights from these models. And there is a pattern to
\par
the interactions between the physician, the nurse,
\par
and decision analyst to produce a series of models
\par
and interpretations that can guide patient care.
\par
\textbf{Overview}
\par
This paper exploits these recurrent features to pre-
\par
sent a decision class analysis of critical care life-sup-
\par
port decisions. A decision class analysis may be
\par
viewed as a set of instructions for performing the
\par
analysis of decisions that belong to a defined group
\par
[7]. The work presented here is an initial step to-
\par
wards a set of instructions for performing decision
\par
analyses of life-support decisions. It represents part
\par
of the decision engineering that will be required to
\par
create intelligent decision systems for life-support.
\par
In this paper we use influence diagrams for the
\par
modeling of life-support decisions. Influence dia-
\par
grams are a mathematically rigorous graphical
\par
method for representing decision models [7-9]. At
\par
the core of each life-support decision is a trade-off
\par
between a physiological variable and a side effect.
\par
We will first present a simple influence diagram that
\par
models the choice of a therapy that optimally bal-
\par
ances these factors. Next, we will discuss how to
\par
model the extremely challenging dynamics inher-
\par
ent in these decisions, and will propose a template
\par
for building influence diagram decision models.
\par
The template substitutes a sequence of two-stage
\par
models with state inference for the intractable se-
\par
quential decision model.
\par
We will proceed to examine the extraordinarily
\par
complex medical domain and to suggest that the
\par
complexity be managed by resolving the domain in-
\par
to sub domains by physiological need. We will pro-
\par
pose that the knowledge in each sub domain be rep-
\par
resented by knowledge maps that have a generic
\par
structure. (In the appendix we present examples of
\par
these maps for the specific area of oxygen transport
\par
decisions.)
\par
We will incorporate this methodology for hand-
\par
ling dynamics and complexity into a generic proce-
\par
dure for formulating, evaluating, and appraising a
\par
life-support decision model. Finally, we will indi-
\par
cate how life-support decision analysis might be
\par
embedded into the clinical routine. The corre-
\par
spondence between elements in the decision analy-
\par
sis procedure and elements in the typical pattern of
\par
physician and nurse interactions will be highlighted.
\par
\textbf{Balancing therapeutic effects and side effects}
\par
The management of critical care patients in the in-
\par
terval between definitive interventions such as op-
\par
erative procedures is frequently described as being
\par
supportive. The goal is to meet basic physiological
\par
needs while the body heals itself. The assumption is
\par
that eventually the organ failure of critical illness
\par
will resolve and the body will be able to meet its
\par
physiological needs unsupported.
\par
In using critical care technology to meet these
\par
needs, however, the clinician faces a fundamental
\par
tradeoff. Life-support therapies not only have de-
\par
sirable direct physiological effects but also have un-
\par
desirable injurious side effects.
\par
For example:
