\textbf{Managing complexity}
\par
The large variety of life-support decisions can be
\par
categorized using the basic physiological needs that
\par
must be supported in critically ill patients. There
\par
are three need categories: nutrition, milieu int6-
\par
rieur, and microbiology.
\par
The need for nutrition encompasses the cellular
\par
need to consume substances to survive. The most
\par
critical of these substances is oxygen. Without ox-
\par
ygen many cells - particularly nerve cells and heart
\par
muscle cells - die within five minutes. This leads to
\par
significant injury or death for the patient. Other nu-
\par
trients are less critical, but still important. These in-
\par
clude glucose, protein, essential fatty acids, vita-
\par
mins, and trace elements. Ultimately, lack of these
\par
nutrients jeopardizes the patient's survival.The milieu intdrieur is a concept introduced by
\par
the early physiologists to describe the fluid bathing
\par
the cells of multicellular organisms [10]. Critical ill-
\par
ness impairs the body's ability to maintain this fluid
\par
at appropriate volume and concentration of dis-
\par
solved substances. Hence, a major concern of crit-
\par
ical care is the management of the fluid, electrolyte,
\par
and acid-base determinants of the body's internal
\par
environment.
\par
Microbiology refers to the need of the body to be
\par
free from invading microorganisms. These include:
\par
viruses, bacteria, fungi, and parasites. Critical care
\par
has been much more successful at meeting the
\par
needs for nutrition and maintenance of the milieu
\par
intdrieur than it has been at defending against mi-
\par
crobiological invasion. Antibiotics have been a su-
\par
perb therapy for some infections, but the develop-
\par
ment of resistant organisms has blunted their effec-
\par
tiveness. Additionally, it appears that early in infec-
\par
tion a positive-feedback cycle of immune activation
\par
and further infection is triggered that leads to wide-
\par
spread injury. Despite antibiotics, microbiological
\par
invasion is the leading cause of death in ICUs.
\par
Hence, treating or preventing microbiological inva-
\par
sion and its consequences is now the focus of a large
\par
category of life-support decisions.
\par
A hierarchy of life-support decisions based upon
\par
these categories is presented in Fig. 4. At the high-
\par
est level is the class of all life-support critical care
\par
decisions. At the next level are the categories of nu-
\par
trition, milieu int6rieur, and microbiology. Under
\par
nitrition are the decision categories of oxygen
\par
transport, glucose, protein, essential fatty acids, vi-
\par
tamins, and trace elements. Under milieu int6rieur
\par
are the categories of carbon dioxide, pH, sodium,
\par
potassium, calcium, phosphate. Finally, under mi-
\par
crobiology are decisions categories for antibiotics
\par
and immunomodulators.
\par
This taxonomy is proposed as a first step towards
\par
organizing the large domain of life-support critical
\par
care decisions. It is open to extension and revision.
\par
The categories can be subdivided into smaller and
\par
smaller units. For example, under the category of
\par
antibiotics, subcategories could be created for deci-
\par
sions about abscesses, pneumonias, wound infec-
\par
tions, urinary infections, and meningitis.
\par
37
\par
Each of the decision categories has associated
\par
medical domain knowledge that is specific to that
\par
category. For example, the physiologicaI, pharma-
\par
cological, and therapeutic knowledge that pertains
\par
to protein is very different in many of its details
\par
from the same types of knowledge that pertains to
\par
glucose. But both are subcategories within nutri-
\par
tion, and there are basic nutritional concepts com-
\par
mon to both domains.
\par
Capturing the medical knowledge in even a sub-
\par
subcategory of critical care decisions is extremely
\par
challenging. The complexity of the domain is man-
\par
aged by using six generic knowledge maps: the
\par
physiological mechanism map, therapy maps, fu-
\par
ture maps, survival maps, measurement maps, and
\par
updating maps. These capture the knowledge in a
\par
way the facilitates building a decision model with
\par
the form shown in Fig. 3. The next section presents
\par
the generic form of these maps. The Appendix de-
\par
velops specific examples of these maps for oxygen
\par
transport decisions.
