\textbf{Formulating, evaluating, and appraising}
\par
\textbf{life-support decision models}
\par
The procedure for formulating, evaluating, and ap-
\par
praising life-support critical care decision models
\par
uses the knowledge maps developed above. The
\par
first step is to link together knowledge maps to for-
\par
mulate a decision model with the structure of the
\par
decision template in Fig. 3. A therapy map corre-
\par
sponding to the therapy under consideration is
\par
joined to the physiological mechanism map corre-
\par
sponding to physiological need that is to be met.
\par
This assesses the nodes Therapeutic Effects n and
\par
Side Effects n. The frontier nodes of the resulting
\par
map become the state variables.
\par
Next Futures maps corresponding to these state
\par
variables are used to assess the nodes Future Ther-
\par
apeutic Effects and Future Side Effects. A survival
\par
map corresponding to the Aggregate Therapeutic
\par
and Side Effects is used to assess the node Survival.
\par
If this is the first in the sequence of life-support
\par
decisions then the node State n-1 is assessed judg-
\par
mentally by the physician. If this is not the first, then
\par
the distribution for State n \emph{from the previous deci-}
\par
\emph{sion model }is used to assess State n-1. The node
\par
State n for the current decision is assessed based on
\par
State n-1 using a State Updating map appropriate to
\par
the chosen state variables.
\par
Finally, the node Measurement n-1 is assessed us-
\par
ing measurement maps corresponding to the cho-
\par
sen state variables. This decision model is then eval-
\par
uated and a recommendation is obtained. The mod-
\par
el is continually updated as time passes and as re-
\par
peated measurements are made. Evaluation of the
\par
updated model provides revised recommendations
\par
for the life-support therapy. With each updating the
\par
model may be appraised. Value of information and
\par
value of control calculations can complement range
\par
checking on measurements to provide proactive
\par
alarms.
\par
To summarize, the template in Fig. 3 for modeling
\par
life-support decisions is part of a sequence of two-
\par
stage models with state inference at each stage. The
\par
decision process involves the following basic cycle:
