\par
\emph{Embedding decision analysis of hlfe-support critical}
\par
\emph{care problems into the clinical routine}
\par
The steps of the life-support critical care decision
\par
procedure are tied to recurrent clinical events in the
\par
care of the critically ill patient. Typically the patient
\par
undergoes a period of resuscitation early in the crit-
\par
ical care stay. The physician is usually present at the
\par
bedside in a direct supervisory role, and institutes a
\par
large number of therapies rapidly to bring the pa-
\par
tient's condition to an acceptable equilibrium.
\par
Once the patient's condition has equilibrated the
\par
physician delegates to the nurses management of
\par
the life-support technology. The management plan
\par
should dynamically recommend optimal therapeut-
\par
ic adjustments in response to continually or period-
\par
ically obtained measurements. Additionally, it
\par
should indicate when other measurements should
\par
be obtained, and when the physician should be
\par
called to consider altering the therapeutic or diag-
\par
nostic regimen.
\par
The first step of the proposed decision modeling
\par
will typically be done in the period after the resusci-
\par
tation and prior to the physician leaving the patient
\par
in the delegated care of the nurse. For the analysis to
\par
proceed rapidly, a full set of knowledge maps (de-
\par
47
\par
rived from a review of the literature, from discus-
\par
sion with physiologists and critical care specialists,
\par
and from extraction of databases of patient care re-
\par
cords) must be available. Only a few assessments
\par
should be elicited directly from the bedside physi-
\par
cian. Rather, the physician uses a computer work-
\par
station to construct the model by endorsing or per-
\par
haps modifying the assessments provided by ex-
\par
perts.
\par
The physician then leaves the bedside. In subse-
\par
quent steps the decision model is evaluated on the
\par
workstation to recommend to the nurse adjust-
\par
ments of therapy. At frequent intervals the value of
\par
perfect information and value of perfect control are
\par
calculated. If either the value of perfect information
\par
or value of perfect control exceeds a threshold de-
\par
fined by the physician, the nurse is advised to call
\par
the physician. The discussion between physician
\par
and nurse focuses upon new measurements or ther-
\par
apies that might be of benefit to add to the patient
\par
care regimen. If this discussion suggests that the
\par
model needs to be reformulated, the physician may
\par
return to the bedside, and the process returns to the
\par
first step.
\par
\emph{Accommodating delegated authority and}
\par
\emph{distributed expertise}
\par
The procedure described for formulating, evaluat-
\par
ing, and appraising life-support decision problems
\par
is compatible with existing patterns of distributed
\par
expertise and delegated responsibility in critical
\par
care. Distributed expertise means that the different
\par
members of the critical care profession have differ-
\par
ent knowledge and information. The academic crit-
\par
ical care physician and scientists, who influence the
\par
bedside team through publication and teaching, are
\par
the experts on life-support technology and the
\par
pathophysiology of critical illness. The attending
\par
physicians or surgeon is most likely to be familiar
\par
with aspects of the patient's illness that predate his
\par
or her admission to the ICU, such as details of out-
\par
patient or intra-operative management. Finally, the
\par
nurse, who is physically present at the bedside
\par
round-the-clock, is most familiar with minute-by-
\par
minute changes in the patient's history, physical ex-48
\par
am, monitored observations, and laboratory mea-
\par
surements. Delegated responsibility refers to the
\par
empowering of the nurse by the physician to make
\par
decisions about life-support critical care therapy in
\par
the physician's absence. The physician currently ac-
\par
complishes this delegation of decision-making
\par
through implicit and explicit orders, which are fre-
\par
quently ineffective [20].
\par
The procedure outlined for analyzing life-sup-
\par
port critical care decisions accommodates distrib-
\par
uted expertise and delegated responsibility by
\par
treating the decision process as a decision class
\par
analysis. This decision class analysis is progressively
\par
refined by critical care specialists, physicians, and
\par
nurses until it becomes the analysis of a decision
\par
class that consists of one decision.
\par
The academic critical care physicians and scien-
\par
tists provide the expertise that is encoded in the
\par
knowledge maps used to build the decision model.
\par
These maps form a decision class analysis of a cate-
\par
gory of life-support critical care decisions. The phy-
\par
sician refines this decision class analysis to one that
\par
is smaller. In linking the maps to formulate a deci-
\par
sion model, the physician analyzes the class of life-
\par
support decisions in that category that will be faced
\par
by the nurse caring for a particular patient over a
\par
particular interval. This interval class analysis is re-
\par
fined by the nurse into the analysis of single deci-
\par
sions by updating the state variables in the decision
\par
model to account for the passage of time or the
\par
availability of measurements.
\par
The hierarchy of decision class analyses provides
\par
a powerful mechanism for communicating exper-
\par
tise and delegating authority in life-support critical
\par
care decision-making. The knowledge of academic
\par
physicians and scientists is not simply recorded in
\par
textbooks and journals. Rather it is directly applied
\par
to patient care decisions. The interval class analysis
\par
performed by the physician represents a form of
\par
prescribing that goes far beyond existing physician
\par
orders, which are fundamentally condition-recom-
\par
mendation rules. The decision models generated
\par
from this interval class analysis provide an explicit
\par
and logical basis for helping the nurse understand
\par
what to do and why. The decision models also pro-
\par
vide a basis for proactive alarms that allow the
\par
nurse to help the physician determine when the pre-
\par
scription has become obsolete.
\par
\textbf{Summary}
\par
Developing intelligent decision systems for critical
\par
care will require encoding medical and decision
\par
analytical expertise into a computer program. The
\par
decision class analysis presented here describes
\par
how life-support decision can be modeled as a se-
\par
quence of two-stage influence diagrams that are
\par
formulated by linking together knowledge maps of
\par
generic structure. The recurrent formulation, eval-
\par
uation, and appraisal of these models corresponds
\par
to the pattern of collaboration between the physi-
\par
cian and nurse managing life-support in the critical-
\par
ly ill patient.
