\par
The notion of oxygen debt rests upon a simplify-
\par
ing conceptualization of the body as a single block
\par
of tissue. We assume that this block of tissue re-
\par
quires a given amount of oxygen per minute to pro-
\par
vide the energy for healing, fighting infection, and
\par
fundamental cellular life processes. This we term
\par
oxygen demand.
\par
We also assume that this tissue block is actually
\par
consuming an amount of oxygen - the oxygen con-
\par
sumption (VO2) - which may be less than what is
\par
required. The difference between what is required
\par
per minute and what is actually consumed per min-
\par
ute is the amount of oxygen debt per minute that
\par
the patient is developing - the debt rate. The debt
\par
rate multiplied by the length of time that the debt
\par
rate is maintained gives the amount of oxygen debt.
\par
(Oxygen debt, oxygen consumption, and oxygen
\par
demand are all typically normalized by patient
\par
weight or body surface area to allow comparison
\par
between patients.)
\par
Thus, oxygen debt is the difference between the
\par
amount of oxygen required by the tissues and the
\par
amount of oxygen actually consumed. As shown in
