\par
shown in Fig. A-9.
\par
The model makes the following analogies be-
\par
tween the electrical and the physiological domains:
\par
voltage is analogous to pressure; current is analo-
\par
gous to blood flow or cardiac output (CO); capac-
\par
itance is analogous to compliance; and charge is
\par
analogous to blood volume.
\par
Blood collects in the right atrium and great veins,
\par
which are treated as a capacitance, C w This capac-
\par
itance produces a central venous pressure, PR, that
\par
is related to the systemic venous blood volume. The
\par
systemic blood volume is analogous to charge and is
\par
represented by OR" There are two elements to this
\par
charge: a portion that produces no increase in volt-
\par
age across the capacitance, the unstressed volume,
\par
ORU, and a stressed volume that is equal to PR " CR-
\par
Blood then flows across the tricuspid valve, which
\par
ensures one-way flow into the right ventricle.
\par
Valves are represented in the electrical analogy by
\par
diodes.
\par
The right ventricle is a muscular vessel that ex-
\par
hibits a compliance that varies over the interval of
\par
each heart beat. First there is a period where the
\par
muscle relaxes. This period of increased compli-
\par
ance is known as diastole. It represents the period
\par
during which the right heart fills with blood from
\par
the right atrium. Then the muscle contracts. This
\par
marks the period of decreased compliance known
\par
as systole, during which the ventricle empties blood
\par
across the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary cir-
\par
culation.
\par
In the circuit model the right ventricle is a capac-
\par
itor with capacitance varying between CRVd, the ca-
\par
pacitance at diastole, and CRV S, the capacitance at
\par
systole. The frequency of this variation is the heart
\par
rate, or H.
\par
Once ejected from the right heart the blood flows
\par
