\emph{A.2. Oxygen transport therapy maps}
\par
A therapy map, thus, is a relevance map that cap-
\par
tures a medical intervention and its immediate
\par
physiological consequences. Additionally, it is use-
\par
ful to attach to a therapy map the side effects of an
\par
intervention. The general form of a therapy map for
\par
oxygen transport decisions is shown in Fig. A-11.
\par
The oxygen transport intervention is represented
\par
by a decision node, The therapy map explicates the
\par
relevance of the decision node for two key sets of
\par
nodes: oxygen transport mechanism nodes and side
\par
effect nodes.
\par
It is important to note that an intervention may
\par
be relevant to more than one node of the diagram.
\par
For example, positive end-expiratory pressure pro-
\par
vides imperfect control of the border node Palmo-
\par
nary Shunt. But positive end-expiratory pressure
\par
also has complex effects on the determinants of car-
\par
diac output, For example, positive end-expiratory
\par
pressure may alter the ventricular compliances,
\par
CLV s, CRy \textasciitilde, CLV d and CRvd, and the pulmonary resist-
\par
ance, ep.
\par
One approach might be to assess each of these
\par
border nodes with respect to the intervention. This
\par
requires many assessments. As is frequently the
\par
case, it is easier to assess a non-border node directly62
