\par
ological consequences of volume therapy. First, the
\par
infusion of fluid expands the amount of blood avail-
\par
able for pumping by the heart. This entity was in-
\par
troduced earlier in the development of the output
\par
component of the oxygen transport mechanism.
\par
The therapy map shows that a volume infusion
\par
results in a blood volume q, which is increased from
\par
a baseline, or pre-infusion, value by an amount
\par
equal to some fraction of the amount infused. The
\par
rest tends to leak out of blood vessels into the tis-
\par
sues and is effectively lost to the circulation. The
\par
amount that leaks out is a variable fraction - Intra-
\par
vascular Fraction- that tends to change with the pa-
\par
tient's evolving critical illness.
\par
The second important physiological consequenc-
\par
es of volume infusion is that the fluid that leaks out
\par
into the tissues frequently accumulates in lung tis-
\par
sues where it can impair the lung-gas-exchange per-
\par
formance. The lung performance is captured in the
\par
variable Pulmonary Shunt - another oxygen trans-
\par
port mechanism variable introduced in the devel-
\par
opment of the lung component.
\par
The therapy map asserts that the amount of fluid
\par
in the lung, the extravascular lung water, is equal to
\par
a baseline amount that is augmented by a fraction of
\par
the amount of volume infused. Again, this fraction
\par
is dynamic and uncertain. Additionally, there is an
\par
uncertain parameter, the water to shunt transform,
\par
that specifies the relationship between amount of
\par
lung water and pulmonary shunt. While the volume
\par
infusion therapy map greatly simplifies the physiol-
\par
ogy involved, it does capture the essential physio-
\par
logical relationships relevant to decisions about
\par
how much fluid to use.
\par
Figure A-16 shows how the Volume Infusion
\par
therapy map can be modified for infusions of fluid
\par
that are not simply salt-water. This therapy map for
\par
blood transfusion differs in two areas. First the par-
\par
ameters for extravascular lung water fraction and
\par
for intravascular fraction differ between blood and
\par
salt-water. Blood has a large component of red
\par
cells. Cells- unlike water and electrolytes- tend not
\par
to leak through the molecular-sized defects in the
\par
vascular system.
\par
Second, blood transfusion by placing red cells in-
\par
to the circulation increases the concentration of ox-
\par
ygen-carrying hemoglobin in the blood volume.
\par
Thus the hemoglobin concentration increases from
\par
a baseline amount by an amount proportional to the
\par
transfusion. The constant of proportionality tends
\par
to be about lmg/100 rise in the hemoglobin for each
\par
unit of blood transfused, but there can be some vari-
\par
ability. Thus this constant is treated as the uncertain
\par
parameter Hgb Transfusion Response. Figure A-16
\par
shows hepatitis as a side effect of transfusion. Other
\par
side effects could be modeled also.
\par
A therapy map for afterload reduction is shown
\par
in Fig. A-17. Afterload reduction is a therapy that
\par
increases cardiac output by decreasing the imped-
\par
ance against which the heart must pump. Resistance
\par
in the pulmonary and arterial circuits are frequently
\par
used as approximations to this impedance. Thus, af-
\par
terload reduction may be viewed as drug therapy
\par
that alters the variables Rp and R s in the cardiac
\par
output component of the oxygen transport mecha-
\par
nism.
\par
The effect of the drug is proportional to the dose
\par
given, but the individual patient response is dynam-
\par
ic and uncertain. This is captured in the therapy
\par
map with the stochastic variables Dose Response
\par
Parameter Rp and Dose Response Parameter R s.
\par
Each afterload agent has its own pharmacologic
\par
side effects which must also be incorporated into
\par
the therapy map.
\par
In Fig. A-18 the therapy map for a related ther-apy, vasodilator therapy, is presented. A vasodilator
\par
is a drug that dilates blood vessels. This dilation not
\par
only reduces the resistances, Rp and Rs, but also has
\par
an effect upon the cardiovascular capacitances, Cs,
\par
C R, Cp, and C L. The patient response for each of
\par
these cardiovascular variables is characterized by
\par
drug-specific dynamic and uncertain dose response
\par
parameters. Comparison of Figs A-17 and A-18
\par
shows how afterload reduction can be considered a
\par
special case of vasodilator therapy.
\par
Figure A-19 shows a therapy map for still another
\par
pharmacological manipulation of the cardiovascu-
\par
lar system. Inotropes are drugs that increase the
\par
ability of the heart to pump blood. Referring to the
\par
oxygen transport mechanism, inotropes may be
\par
viewed as increasing the stiffness of the ventricles
\par
during systole. This is captured in the therapy map
\par
by the deterministic assessment of the capacitance
\par
of the left and right ventricles (CRy s and CLvs) with
\par
respect to the Inotrope Dose decision and with re-
\par
spect to uncertain and dynamic dose response par-
\par
ameters. The side effect of inotropes is myocardial
\par
ischemia from increased cardiac work.
\par
The therapy maps discussed so far act on either
\par
the pulmonary or cardiac components of the ox-
\par
ygen transport mechanism. It is increasingly recog-
\par
nized in critical care practice that oxygen debt can
\par
be reduced not only by interventions that increase
\par
oxygen consumption but also by interventions that
\par
reduce oxygen demand.
\par
Anesthetic agents can reduce patient metabolic
\par
needs substantially. Figure A-20 shows a therapy
\par
map for an anesthetic agent. This map shows that
\par
the anesthetic dose is converted into an agent blood
\par
level using a relationship that depends upon a phar-
\par
macokinetic parameter. This level, in turn, together
\par
with a dose response parameter causes a change in
\par
the metabolic rate. The amount of this change is the
\par
anesthesia factor, a node in the oxygen transport
\par
mechanism. The therapy map also captures the an-
\par
esthetic agent-specific side effects.
\par
Thus, a therapy map links the therapeutic inter-
\par
vention to the oxygen transport mechanism. The
\par
therapeutic intervention typically has a desirable
\par
effect upon one or more of the nodes in this mecha-
\par
nism. This effect is typically balanced by an off-set-
\par
ting effect on either one or more nodes of the mech-
\par
65
\par
anism, or by a side effect that is mediated outside
\par
the oxygen transport chain. In the first case the
\par
countervailing effects are balanced by the net effect
\par
on oxygen debt; in the latter, by the net effect on
\par
survival.
