\emph{A.3. Other oxygen transport knowledge maps}
\par
Other oxygen transport knowledge maps have the
\par
structure described in the main body of the paper.
\par
For example, the measurement map for PaO 2 con-
\par
sists of a map that assesses PaO 2 Measure given the
\par
underlying PaO2 (analogous to Fig. lta) and a map
\par
that assesses the underlying PaO 2 given CaO2, a
\par
variable in the joint therapy/physiological mecha-
\par
nism map (analogous to Fig. llb).
\par
The underlying PaO 2 is assessed given CaO: in
\par
the physiological mechanism map. A function for
\par
the assessment of PaO 2 given CaO 2 is obtained by
\par
solving the following equations for PaO 2 in terms of
\par
Ca02:
\par
CaO2= PaO 2 . 0.0031+ Hgb 9 SaO 2 9 1.36,
\par
SaO2: 1/(1+ 23,400/(PAO23+ 150. PaO2))).
\par
Despite the statement by Severinghaus, an analytic
\par
solution for CaO 2 in terms of PaO 2 can be obtained
\par
using a computer program, Mathematica TM [25, 29].
\par
Numerical search techniques also work.
\par
Updating maps, and future effects maps are mod-
\par
eled quite simply in the main body of the paper. Un-
\par
like physiological mechanism maps, therapy maps,
\par
and measurement maps, specific assessments for
\par
other knowledge maps are currently derived from
\par
observations of large groups of patients similar to
\par
the patient under consideration, not from basic
\par
physiology. Better assessments for these knowledge
\par
maps will be greatly facilitated by the ongoing
\par
adoption of the electronic chart and availability of
\par
large critical care databases spanning the experi-
\par
ence of multiple institutions.
