\par
\textbf{THE IMPORTANCE OF TASK CHARACTERISTICS}
\par
Our analysis is heavily influenced by Brunswik's
\par
(1952, 1956) Probabilistic Functionalism and its ex-
\par
tensions-Hammond's Social Judgment Theory
\par
(Hammond \textbf{\emph{et }}\emph{al., }1975; Doherty \& Kurz, 1996) and
\par
Cognitive Continuum Theory (Hammond, 1980,
\par
1981)-which argue that the focus of the study of judg-
\par
ment is the relation between the judge and the environ-
\par
ment (or task), and that judgment cannot be understood
\par
without understanding the properties of the task. Task
\par
properties are important both because they can facili-
\par
tate or limit judgmental accuracy and because they
\par
describe the environment in which the judgment proc-
\par
ess was learned.
\par
Cognitive Continuum Theory (CCT) states that task
\par
characteristics tend to induce intuitive and analytical
\par
modes of cognition and that most tasks induce a combi-
\par
nation of modes called ``quasirational'' thought. CCT
\par
specifies three categories of important task characteris-
\par
tics: (a) complexity of task structure, (b) ambiguity of
\par
task content, and (c) form of task presentation. In this
\par
study, we will focus on complexity of task structure,
\par
which is determined by the formal, statistical proper-
\par
ties of the tasks.
\par
Because judgment tasks contain inherent and irre-
\par
ducible uncertainty (Brunswik, 1952; Hammond, 1996),
\par
they are not perfectly predictable. Task predictability
\par
influences judgmental accuracy in at least two ways.
\par
Not only does task predictability place an upper bound
\par
on potential accuracy (Tennekes, 1988; Stewart, 1990;
\par
Stewart \& Lusk, 1994), but a number of studies have
\par
found evidence that the reliability of judgment is lower
\par
for less predictable tasks (Brehmer, 1976, 1978;
\par
Camerer, 1981; Harvey, 1995). Judges respond to un-
\par
predictable tasks by behaving less predictably them-
\par
selves.
\par
Another important task property is the amount of
\par
information (usually operationalized as the number of
\par
cues) available when a judgment is made. It has been
\par
suggested that reliability decreases as the amount of
\par
information available increases (Einhorn, 197 1;
\par
Hogarth, 1987; Stewart \textbf{\emph{et }}\emph{al., }1992; Lee \& Yates, 1992).
\par
Faust (1986) reviewed several studies that suggest that
\par
judges are not able to make proper use of large numbers
\par
of cues. Lee and Yates (1992) found that G (see descrip-
\par
tion of the lens model equation below) decreases with
\par
increasing numbers of cues.
\par
Other authors have recognized the importance of task
\par
characteristics (Bolger \& Wright, 1994; Shanteau,
\par
19921, but, despite their importance, many studies of
\par
expert judgment do not address task characteristics. In
\par
some cases, the data necessary t o analyze important
\par
task properties are not obtained. In other cases, the
\par
data are obtained, but not analyzed.
\par
\textbf{LENS MODEL ANALYSIS OF ACCUFUCY}
\par
The conceptual and analytical framework for this
\par
study is based on Brunswik's Lens Model and
\par
Hammond`s Social Judgment Theory (Cooksey, 1996;
\par
Hammond, 1996). Our measures of both forecast accu-
\par
racy and agreement among forecasters are based on
\par
the correlation coefficient. One objection to the correla-
\par
tion is that it is not sensitive to the lack of calibration
\par
(bias) that is important to the users of forecasts. Stew-
\par
art (1990) showed how bias in judgment could be incor-
\par
porated into lens model studies using Murphy's (1988)
\par
decomposition of the skill score. In the present study,
\par
however, bias was very low for all tasks and all forecast-
\par
ers (ranging from 0.000 to 0.0141, and contributed noth-
\par
ing to understanding the differential accuracy of fore-
\par
casts. This is consistent with previous research showing
\par
that weather forecasters are well calibrated when mak-
\par
ing familiar forecasts (Murphy \& Winkler, 1974; Mur-
\par
phy \& Daan, 1984). In order to simplify this paper, we
\par
will not discuss bias.
\par
The lens model equation (LME) for decomposing the
\par
correlation coefficient (Hursch, Hammond, \& Hursch,
\par
1964; Tucker, 1964; Castellan, 1973; Stewart, 1976;
\par
Cooksey \& Freebody, 1985) shows how the correlation
\par
is influenced by properties of the task, the cognitive
\par
system of the judge, and the relations between them.
\par
The lens model equation assumes that variables repre-
\par
senting the observed event \emph{(0) }and the judgment (Y)
\par
have been partitioned into two linearly independent
\par
components-one that is a function of the cues and
\par
another that is unrelated to them.
