\textbf{METHOD}
\par
The data used in this study were gathered as part of
\par
an ongoing weather forecasting exercise conducted at
\par
the University at Albany. Forecasts of daily tempera-
\par
ture and precipitation have been made routinely by
\par
members of the Department of Atmospheric Science
\par
during the Fall and Spring academic semesters since
\par
September 1969 (Bosart, 1975,1983). In this paper, we
\par
analyze results from the game for the period September
\par
1988 through December 1992.
\par
Forecasts of maximum (daytime) and minimum
\par
(nighttime) temperature, measurable precipitation
\par
probability (precipitation greater than 0.01 inches,
\par
hereafter referred to as POP), and precipitation amount
\par
are made for the four sequential 12-h periods beginning
\par
at 0000 UTC of the following day. (In Albany, local stan-
\par
dard time is 5 h behind UTC.) Thus, a forecast submit-
\par
ted by 2045 UTC of Day 0 (the approximate forecast
\par
submission time) would include a minimum tempera-
\par
ture forecast for the period 0000-1200 UTC of Day 1,
\par
a maximum temperature forecast for 1200-0000 UTC of
\par
Day 1, a minimum temperature forecast for 0000-1200
\par
UTC of Day 2, and a maximum temperature forecast
\par
for 1200-0000 UTC of Day 2. In addition, POP and
\par
precipitation amount forecasts would also be submitted
\par
for each of these four periods. Figure 1 summarizes
\par
(in both UTC and local time) the forecast times and
\par
verification periods for the forecasts used in this study.
\par
Daily forecasts (Monday through Friday) are made
\par
by a collective group of 10-20 students and faculty of
\par
varying experience and background, with approxi-
\par
mately 66 forecast days per semester. Scores, which are
\par
public and posted weekly, are based on absolute error
\par
in temperature and precipitation amount forecasts and
\par
the Brier score (Brier, 1950) for POP forecasts.
\par
The information available to the Albany forecasters
\par
included virtually everything that was available to op-
