In order to conduct a lens model analysis of forecasts,
\par
it is necessary to obtain measures of the information
\par
or ``cues'' used by the forecasters. In typical laboratory
\par
studies, cues are determined by the researcher. In a
\par
naturalistic study, the forecaster can select any cueTHE TASK OF EXPERT JUDGMENT information that is available and the researcher must
\par
discover what information was used. We relied on physi-
\par
cal principles and forecasting experience to suggest the
\par
cues that should be useful to forecasters and that should
\par
correlate well with observed temperatures and precipi-
\par
tation. Table 2 lists the cues identified for the tempera-
\par
ture and precipitation forecasts, along with the physical
\par
basis for the proposed predictive validity of the cue.
\par
The list of cues is grounded in sound physical principles
\par
and reflects the extensive forecasting experience of two
\par
of the authors (LFB and PJR). All of the important
\par
information available to the forecasters is included in
\par
the cue set.
\par
Since this was a retrospective study, it was necessary
\par
to reconstruct the cues that were available to the fore-
\par
casters but had not been routinely recorded in the Al-
\par
bany forecasting game. In some cases, cues that would
\par
have been available to the forecasters were obtained
\par
from weather records. In other cases (variables marked
\par
by a superscript \emph{a }in Table \textbf{2), }the cues had to be recon-
\par
structed and their values were estimated from data and
\par
modeling results that were \textbf{not }available until after the
