\emph{Representativeness of Cues and Forecast Context}
\par
It is important to ask to what extent this, data set
\par
is representative of what forecasters do in operational
\par
settings with regard to information available, deadline
\par
pressure and related issues. One of us (PJR) has had
\par
considerable experience in both operational forecast
\par
settings and university games, while another of us
\par
(LFB) is quite familiar with \textbf{NWS }restrictions. Informa-
\par
tion available to forecasters is virtually identical in both
\par
settings. In our view, the most important distinction is
\par
that the deadline is softer in the university setting, so
\par
that if the forecaster chooses, he or she can examine
\par
the data to a greater extent than can the operational
\par
forecaster, who has many duties and a much stricter
\par
timetable. However, we also suspect that someone in
\par
continuous contact with the evolving weather situation
\par
(such as an operational forecaster) is in a better position
\par
by the end of a work session to have a ``feel'' for what
\par
pattern is emerging than a person with intermittent
\par
contact throughout the day (such as a university re-
\par
searcher or student). Existing data, such as that pre-
\par
sented by Bosart (1983) and Sanders (1986) suggests
\par
that consensus forecasts from university games com-
\par
pete quite favorably with WSFO products, and that
\par
the best individual forecasters have skill comparable
\par
to \emph{N W S }forecasters. Since WSFO forecasts were in-
\par
cluded in the data, it was possible to examine differ-
\par
ences between the Albany game forecasts and opera-
\par
tional forecasts.
