\textbf{RESULTS}
\par
We first examine the formal properties ofthe forecast-
\par
ing tasks and make some predictions about the fore-
\par
casts based on these properties. We then turn to an
\par
analysis of the forecasts.
\par
\emph{Analysis of the Forecasting Tasks}
\par
Although potentially important task characteristics
\par
have been described by Murphy and Brown (19841,
\par
Shanteau (19921, Klein (1993), Bolger and Wright
\par
(1994), and others, we have chosen to focus on a differ-
\par
ent set of characteristics: the formal (statistical) task
\par
properties that determine the ``complexity of task struc-
\par
ture'' in Cognitive Continuum Theory.2
\par
\emph{Relations among the cues.}
\par
There were substantial
\par
correlations among pairs of forecast cues for both tem-
\par
perature and POP forecasts. For temperature cues, in-
\par
tercorrelations varied from -0.414 to 0.911 with a me-
\par
dian of 0.630. Principal components analysis showed
\par
that three factors would account for 80\% of the variance,
\par
and seven factors would account for 95\% of the variance
\par
in all 13 cues. For POP cues, intercorrelations varied
\par
from -0.726 to 0.874 with a median of 0.013. Principal
\par
components analysis of the 24 cues for POP forecasts
\par
showed that eight factors would account for 81\% of the
\par
variance, and it would require 15 factors to account for
\par
95\% of the variance.
\par
Not only were there fewer cues for temperature fore-
\par
casts than for POP forecasts, the cues could be reduced
\par
to a smaller number of linearly independent variables,
\par
indicating that POP forecasting involved more indepen-
\par
dent items of information and was therefore more cogni-
\par
tively complex.
\par
Because of the linear dependencies in both sets of
\par
cues, estimates of the relative weighting of various cues
\par
by different forecasters were highly unstable. Another
\par
consequence of the linear dependencies is that accuracy
\par
was not sensitive to departures from optimal weights
\par
(von Winterfeldt and Edwards, 1986; see below).
\par
\emph{Fit of a linear model.}
\par
The results of linear multiple
\par
regression analysis of the four events, using the appro-
\par
priate cues as predictors, are summarized in Table \textbf{3.}
\par
For temperature events, the linear model was an excel-
\par
lent fit. Precipitation events were predicted moderately
\par
well by a linear combination of the 24 cues. MOS was
\par
the best single predictor for all events. Stepwise regres-
\par
sion indicated that, in all cases, the \textbf{\emph{R2 }}obtained with
\par
the full cue set could be closely approximated using
\par
only MOS and 1, 2, or 3 other cues.
\par
In the interest of space, certain analyses have been omitted. A
