\emph{Sensitivity to weights. }Following Dawes and Corri-
\par
gan (1974), we examined the effects of deviations from
\par
optimal weights by calculating G for equal-weight and
\par
random-weight models. The weights were given the cor-
\par
rect signs in all models. G was the only term calculated
\par
because it is the only component of accuracy that is
\par
affected by changes in weights.
\par
The Gs for equal-weight temperature forecasts were
\par
0.971 and 0.949 (12 and 24 h). The Gs for equal-weight
\par
POP forecasts were 0.678 and 0.687, suggesting that
\par
the precipitation forecasting task is more sensitive to
\par
deviations from optimal weights. Gs were also calcu-
\par
lated for 10,000 random weight models using Monte
\par
Carlo simulation. Median Gs were 0.961 and 0.931 for
\par
temperature and 0.667 and 0.675 for precipitation, pro-
\par
viding further indication of the greater weight sensitiv-
\par
ity of the precipitation task.
\par
Since the G for an optimal linear model is 1.0 by
\par
definition, the Gs for alternative weighting systems in-
\par
dicate the losses in predictability due to weights that
\par
are not statistically optimal. For example, the ryo of a
\par
forecaster using equal weights to forecast minimum
\par
temperature would be 97.1\% of that for a forecaster
\par
using optimal linear weights, assuming that all other
\par
components of accuracy are equal. For temperature
\par
forecasts, non-optimal weighting of information would
\par
have only a slight impact on accuracy (as long as the
\par
signs of the weights were correct).
\par
Getting the weights correct would be more critical
\par
for precipitation forecasting. One reason for this is the
\par
amount of irrelevant information in the cue set that
\par
should be ignored (Gaeth \& Shanteau, 1984). If that
\par
information is given weight, it dilutes the valid variance
\par
in the forecast.
\par
