\emph{Reliability of Forecasts}
\par
Direct measurement of forecast reliability requires
\par
the ability to compare pairs of forecasts made under
\par
identical conditions. Since weather conditions do not
\par
repeat themselves, reliability cannot be directly mea-
\par
sured, and indirect indicators of reliability must be
\par
used.
\par
Ryx is such an indirect, and imperfect, indicator of
\par
reliability (Stewart \& Lusk, 1994). Table \textbf{4 }shows that
\par
this indicator was higher for temperature forecasts
\par
than for precipitation forecasts, as expected. If Ryx is
\par
213
\par
adjusted for the greater number of predictor variables
\par
in the regression equation for POP forecasts, the differ-
\par
ence is even greater. Median adjusted \textbf{\emph{R2 }}for tempera-
\par
ture forecasts is 0.967 and for POP forecasts, it is 0.876.
\par
Another indicator of reliability of a forecast is its
\par
correlation with other forecasts. These correlations are
\par
estimates of a lower bound on reliability (Guilford,
\par
1954). \textbf{As }reported above, agreement was higher for
\par
temperature forecasts than for POP forecasts. Thus,
\par
both indicators of reliability suggest lower reliability
\par
for POP forecasts, as predicted.
\par
\emph{Accuracy }\textbf{\emph{of }}\emph{Forecasts}
\par
Overall forecast accuracy (rye in Table 4) was very
\par
high for temperature forecasts. \textbf{As }predicted, accuracy
\par
is somewhat lower for POP forecasts. There was little
\par
difference in accuracy between 12- and \textbf{24-h }forecasts.
\par
The results of the LME decomposition for all forecasters
\par
are also presented in Table 4. The G values for the
\par
human forecasters were uniformly high, ranging from
\par
0.881 to 0.997. Gs for POP forecasts were lower than
\par
for temperature forecasts.
\par
TabIe \textbf{4 }shows that the linear component of ry0 (the
\par
first term in the LME) accounted for nearly all of the
\par
forecast accuracy. The nonlinear component of accuracy
\par
amounted to less than 2\% of ry0 for temperature fore-
\par
casts and \textbf{from }3.08 to 8.83\% for POP. The small contri-
\par
bution to accuracy of the nonlinear component is consis-
\par
tent with findings in a number of studies (Camerer,
\par
1981). The greater nonlinear contribution for POP is
\par
consistent with our prediction that nonlinear variance
\par
would be more important for POP than for temperature.
\par
Although nearly all of the valid variance in the fore-
\par
casts is linear, the nonlinear variance did make a small
\par
positive contribution. All of the values of C for tempera-
\par
ture forecasts are statistically significant, and 7 out of
\par
10 values for POP forecasts were statistically signifi-
\par
cant (p {$<$} \textbf{.05), }indicating that some of the variance not
\par
captured by the linear regression was systematic, valid
\par
variance. Although the actual contribution to accuracy
\par
of the nonlinear component was small, it was positive
\par
for all forecasts. If the nonlinear component were com-
\par
prised entirely of nonsystematic error variance, its rela-
\par
tion to the event would be equally likely to be negative
\par
or positive. Since the importance of any increment in
\par
forecasting accuracy, no matter how small, depends on
\par
how that forecast is used (Roebber \& Bosart, 1996b1,
\par
the value of a small positive component of accuracy
\par
could be substantial for certain users of forecasts.
\par
The source of the valid nonlinear component could
\par
not be determined. One possibility is nonlinear or con-
\par
figural use of cues. In principle, this possibility could be
\par
investigated, but due to the small amount of nonlinear2 \textbf{14 }STEWART, ROEBBER, AND BOSART
\par
variance, the intercorrelations among the cues, and the
\par
limited size of the sample of forecasts, we chose not to
\par
pursue it. Since the cues were measured retrospectively,
\par
two other possibilities cannot be ruled out. One is that
\par
forecasters used information not included in the cues.
\par
Another is that a cue used by the forecasters was not
\par
included in the set, but was nonlinearly related to one
\par
or more of the cues that were included.
\par
\emph{Human us Model Forecasts}
\par
The comparison between computer models and hu-
\par
man forecasters has been a topic of considerable inter-
\par
est both in the weather forecasting literature (Snell-
\par
man, 1977; Murphy \& Brown, 1984; Clemen \& Murphy,
\par
1986; Murphy \emph{et al., }1988) and the expert judgment
\par
literature (Dawes \emph{et al., }1989; Blattberg \& Hoch, 1990;
\par
Bunn \& Wright, 1991). We compared two models with
\par
human forecasts: (a) the multiple linear regression
\par
model, fit to the events based on the sample data, and
\par
(b) MOS, which is used operationally for forecast guid-
\par
ance.
\par
\emph{Multiple linear }regression \emph{(MLR) us human fore-}
\par
\emph{casts. }\textbf{A }direct comparison of ry0 for humans and MLR
\par
would be unfair because the MLR model forecast is based
\par
on a model that was fit to the sample data. Since we did
\par
not have enough cases for cross-validation, an ``adjusted''
\par
ryo was computed by taking the square root of the ad-
\par
justedR2 from the regression analysis. The results (Table
\par
\textbf{5) }show that for temperature forecasts, \textbf{MLR }accuracy
\par
was approximately equal to the median for human fore-
\par
casters, while for POP forecasts, it was slightly above
\par
the human median. This provides weak support for the
\par
predicted greater advantage of the linear regression
\par
model for POP than for temperature forecasts.
\par
\emph{MOS us human }forecasts. Humans compared more
\par
favorably to MOS than to MLR. For temperature fore-
\par
casts, the accuracy of MOS was lower than for any
\par
human forecaster. For \textbf{12-h }POP forecasts, MOS per-
\par
formed better than only one human forecaster. For 24-
\par
h POP forecasts, MOS performed better than two of the
\par
human forecasters.
\par
The accuracy of MLR was greater than MOS for all
\par
forecasts, but this comparison is unfair because MOS
\par
was one of the predictors in the MLR model. \textbf{A }fairer
\par
comparison was made by removing MOS from the MLR
\par
model (MLR-MOS). The performance of the resulting
\par
regression model was similar to that of MOS for all
\par
forecasts. This is not surprising, since MOS is a linear
\par
regression model based on variables that are similar to
\par
our cues.
\par
The advantage that humans had over MOS may have
\par
been because: (a) forecasters were aware of MOS fore-
\par
casts and could therefore interpret them in the context
\par
of other information; (b) over time, forecasters developed
\par
an understanding ofwhen MOS predicted well and when
\par
it did not; and (c) forecasters may have had access to
\par
information that was not included in MOS. For example,
\par
Roebber and Bosart (1996a) have shown that experi-
\par
enced forecasters know that radiative effects on temper-
\par
ature forecasts are handled relatively poorly by MOS;
\par
such effects are apparent from particular synoptic pat-
\par
terns, and the forecasters, who are aware of the MOS
\par
forecasts, cad adjust temperatures accordingly. In addi-
\par
tion, since the numerical models are run only twice daily,
\par
the MOS output is based on data which may already be
\par
several hours old at forecast time. Under certain rapidly
\par
evolving conditions, such as a frontal passage, additional
\par
observations beyond the initial time of the model fore-
\par
cast may help forecasters better interpret the timing of
\par
such meteorologically significant events.
\par
Table 5 shows that MOS performed better than the
\par
human forecasters in one regard: Both C and the nonlin-
\par
ear contribution to accuracy are higher for MOS than
\par
for the human forecasts in every case. This indicates
\par
that, despite the fact that MOS was based on linear
\par
regression, there was a component that was not linearly
\par
related to the cues used in this study, and that compo-
\par
nent had some validity. That component must have
\par
been due to either: (a{$>$} variables included in MOS but
\par
not included in the cues, or (b) nonlinear relations be-
\par
tween one or more of the variables included in MOS
\par
and one or more of the cues used in this study. Evidence
\par
in favor of (a) is that MOS was based on model output
\par
that is updated every three hours, while the present
\par
study was based on cues updated every 12 h. While it
\par
might, in principle, be possible to examine MOS equa-
\par
tions to determine the source of the nonlinear compo-
\par
nent, that was beyond the scope of this study.
\par
Although human forecasters outperformed the opera-
\par
tional model, the model seemed to contribute to the
\par
accuracy of forecasts. It had, by far, the greatest weight
\par
of any cue in all the regression models of the forecasts,
\par
and it contributed to the predictability for all tasks
\par
(Table \textbf{3). }It is likely that the performance of the human
\par
forecasters would be poorer if they were denied access
\par
to MOS, but this has not been empirically tested.
\par
The results of our comparison between MOS and hu-
\par
mans would not have been predicted from much of the
\par
judgment literature on comparisons between humans
\par
and models. The superiority of models over people gen-
\par
erally results from the superior reliability of models
\par
(Dawes \& Corrigan, 1974; Camerer, 1981). Although
\par
MOS was perfectly reliable, its primary advantage over
\par
the human forecasters was in its nonlinear component.
