\emph{Summary of Key Findings}
\par
\emph{Quality of judgment. }In contrast with the negative
\par
view of human judgment reported in much of the litera-
\par
ture, the quality ofjudgmental forecasts studied in this
\par
paper was high. In particular:
\par
1. Forecaster bias was negligible. Forecasts were
\par
well calibrated.
\par
2. Forecasters were extremely accurate, particularly
\par
when forecasting temperature.
\par
3. There was a high level of agreement among fore-
\par
casters, suggesting that the forecasts were also reliable.\emph{2 }16 STEWART. ROEBBER, \textbf{AND }BOSART
\par
Linearity of judgment. Linear models accounted for
\par
nearly all of the valid variance in the judgments. How-
\par
ever, there was shared nonlinearity among the forecast-
\par
ers (with respect to the cues), and that nonlinearity
\par
represented a small positive contribution to the accu-
\par
racy of the forecasts.
\par
\emph{Models us }human forecasts.
\par
Although humans did
\par
not outperform a linear regression model, they were
\par
able to improve upon an operational forecasting model.
\par
Combining forecasts. The group average forecast
\par
performed about as well as the best humans.
\par
Task analysis. All but one of the predictions from
\par
the analysis of the formal properties of the tasks
\par
were confirmed:
\par
1. Temperature forecasts were more accurate than
\par
POP forecasts.
\par
2. The nonlinear variance was greater for POP fore-
\par
casts than for temperature.
\par
\textbf{3. }Agreement among forecasters was lower for POP
\par
forecasts than for temperature, and the indirect evi-
\par
dence suggested that reliability was also lower.
\par
\textbf{4. }Relative to the median of the human forecasters,
\par
MLR performed better for POP than for temperature,
\par
as predicted. Contrary to prediction, the same \textbf{was }not
\par
true for the group average forecast.
\par
agreement was high. No dramatic gains in performance
\par
were to be obtained by simply averaging forecasts or
\par
using a linear model to produce forecasts. The human
\par
forecasters were able to improve upon a model that is
\par
used in operational forecasting.
\par
The good performance of these weather forecasters
\par
contrasted sharply with that of one of the most studied,
\par
and most maligned, group of expert judges-clinical
\par
psychologists (Faust, 1986). The accuracy of their diag-
\par
noses of psychosis and neurosis from psychological test
\par
profiles was poor, they disagreed, and their perfor-
\par
mance was worse than a simple statistical model
\par
(Dawes et al., 1989). How is it possible that weather
\par
forecasters perform so much better than clinical psy-
\par
chologists (and many other experts)? One possible ex-
\par
planation is that they have developed cognitive pro-
\par
cesses that are more powerful than those of
\par
psychologists. This is unlikely since, when confronted
\par
with the task of predicting severe weather, meteorolo-
\par
gists also disagree and their forecasts are inaccurate
\par
(Stewart \textbf{\emph{et }}al., 1989,1992; Lusk et al., 1990; Heideman
\par
et al., 1993). A more likely explanation is to be found
\par
in the nature of the tasks confronting the clinical psy-
\par
chologist and the weather forecaster.
\par
The most important single factor related to forecast
\par
accuracy in this study was task predictability, as mea-
\par
sured by the multiple correlation for the events \textbf{(R0.x).}
\par
In Fig. 2, accuracy (rye) is plotted against task predict-
\par
ability \textbf{(Ro.x) }for the four tasks. The figure clearly shows
\par
that differences in accuracy among temperature and
\par
POP forecasters were small relative to differences be-
\par
tween forecasting tasks, and that differences in accu-
\par
racy were closely related to task predictability.
\par
Also shown in Fig. 2 are data from seven meteorolo-
\par
gists' forecasts of probability of hail (Stewart, 1990)
\par
and 29 clinical psychologists' diagnoses of patients with
\par
psychosis or neurosis from MMPI profiles (Goldberg
