\textbf{DISCUSSION}
\par
This study is unique in judgment research. Unlike
\par
previous laboratory studies, forecasters worked in a
\par
natural setting and their access to information was
\par
not restricted. Unlike previous field studies, several
\par
forecasters, working as individuals but with access to
\par
the same information, made forecasts of the same
\par
events. Unlike nearly all studies, four different forecast-
\par
ing tasks were analyzed and compared.
\par
Since we did not control the information available to
\par
the forecaster, and the information for forecasting is
\par
not routinely recorded, it was necessary to reconstruct
\par
retrospectively the cues that were available. \textbf{As }a result,
\par
perfect validity of cue measurement was not assured.
\par
We believed that this was an acceptable price to pay
\par
for the opportunity to analyze accuracy in a naturalistic
\par
study. As in studies where the cues are known, the
\par
linear regression accounted for most of the variance in
\par
both the forecasts and the events that were forecast,
\par
suggesting that the reconstructed cues were an ade-
\par
quate approximation to the actual cues.
\par
In contrast to the results of many previous studies,
\par
our results paint a positive picture of expert judgment.
\par
