Task predictability
\par
\textbf{FIG. \emph{2. }}Environmental predictability vs. forecast accuracy for
\par
weather forecasters and clinical psychologists.THE TASK OF EXPERT JUDGMENT 1965). Neither was a naturalistic study, but in both
\par
cases accuracy was consistent with task predictability
\par
and was much lower than found in the present study.
\par
Figure 2 shows that when meteorologists confront a
\par
task with low predictability, (predicting hail) their per-
\par
formance is similar to that of the clinical psychologists
\par
confronted with a task with a similar level of predict-
\par
ability. Both the meteorologists and the clinical psychol-
\par
ogists performed below the level of a linear multiple
\par
regression model (although one meteorologist did out-
\par
perform the model). Therefore, the high skill in temper-
\par
ature and POP forecasts relative to predictions of psy-
\par
chosis and neurosis from the MMPI can be attributed
\par
to differences in the tasks. It is not that meteorologists
\par
are better judges than clinical psychologists, but rather
\par
that temperature and precipitation forecasting have
\par
higher limits of predictability. This explanation for dif-
\par
ferences between weather forecasters and clinical psy-
\par
chologists was overlooked in a recent paper comparing
\par
these two types of experts (Monahan \& Steadman,
\par
1996).
\par
High task predictability for the weather forecasting
\par
tasks is partially a result of the high quality of informa-
\par
tion available to forecasters. That information is expen-
\par
sive and it has taken decades to develop the technology
\par
to make it routinely available to forecasters. If we had
\par
studied forecasts of temperature and precipitation \textbf{75}
\par
years ago, they might have looked much more like clini-
\par
cal psychologists.
\par
Another major reason for the high accuracy and
\par
agreement among forecasters is the availability of a
\par
good model-based forecast. This provides a starting
\par
point for the forecasting process, and the forecasters
\par
are able to improve upon it. We cannot estimate the
\par
importance of that improvement because that depends
\par
on the forecast user (see Roebber \& Bosart, 1996b). Our
\par
results simply indicate that the human contribution
\par
is positive.
\par
The strong relation between task predictability and
\par
accuracy shown in Fig. 2 is maintained when the results
\par
of many additional studies are considered (Stewart,
\par
1997). Typically, the results illustrated in Fig. 2 are
\par
described in terms such as ``the repeated failure of
\par
judges to outperform linear regression'' and are inter-
\par
preted with reference to the limitations of the human
\par
judgment process (Slovic \& Lichtenstein, 1971; Faust,
\par
1986; Hogarth, 1987; Johnson, 1988; Dawes, 1988). If,
\par
as noted above, the multiple correlation is \textbf{a }reasonable
\par
measure of task predictability, then a more positive
\par
view emerges: In many fields of expert judgment, per-
\par
formance is near the limit imposed by environmental
\par
uncertainty.
