\par
\textbf{Subjective/Political Models}
\par
Fig. 1. Meyer's (1984) decision models within Hirschheim and Smithson's (1988) IS assessment liter-
\par
ature framework.
\par
Schrems, 1978), more recent formulations consider both system efficiency and effective-
\par
ness (e.g., Kauffman \& Kriebel, 1988). FiscaI assessments use quantitative language. De-
\par
cision models (e.g., discounted cash flow analysis, data envelope analysis) use quantifiable
\par
data that is analyzed using computationally rational models of choice (Meyer, 1984).INFORMATION SYSTEM ASSESSMENT 95
\par
As information systems have evolved into tools for competitive advantage, \emph{strategic }as-
\par
sessments of them have grown in importance (Ives \& Learmonth, 1984). Strategic assess-
\par
ments determine if and how information systems fit within the organizational mission; they
\par
view information systems as long-term resource commitments that must fit within an over-
\par
all portfolio of applications that serve strategic ends. The most popular method of strate-
\par
gic assessment is critical success factors (CSF) analysis (Rockart, 1979). CSF analysis seeks
\par
to determine the critical management tasks and decisions that require information support.
\par
The language of CSF analysis is qualitative, with a methodology based upon structured in-
\par
terviews with executives (Bullen \& Rockart, 1981, p. 3).
\par
Implemented information systems are more than technical instruments intended to fa-
\par
cilitate shared organizational goals. They are also poiitical symbols and signals that com-
\par
peting interest groups fight for (Franz \& Robey, 1984; Kling \& Iacono, 1984). The \emph{political}
\par
model of assessment is invoked to understand the roles and relationships among coalitions
\par
competing to control computing resources. Political analysis may be undertaken merely to
\par
understand the relationship between resources and political behavior, or to exploit such un-
\par
derstanding for the political advantage of a coalition. Political model analysis generally uses
\par
natural language (e.g., English), although coalitions may communicate using specialized
\par
jargon (Meyer, 1984). Analysis of political relations is generally conducted via unstructured
\par
interviews using either covert or overt surveillance and investigation (Douglas, 1976).
\par
\emph{Decision models and language}
\par
Figure 1 assumes that the fiscal and clinical models rely principally upon quantitative
\par
language, while the strategic and political models rely upon qualitative language. One
\par
means of exploring the validity of these assumptions is to evaluate the perceived usefulness
\par
of the four decision models when conducted using structured versus unstructured assess-
\par
ment procedures.
\par
Theories of the role of language in decision making suggest that quantitative languages
\par
offer analytical precision but sacrifice descriptive accuracy, while qualitative languages offer
\par
descriptive accuracy but sacrifice analytical precision (Daft 8\textasciitilde{} Wiginton, 1979; Patton, 1987;
\par
Pondy, 1977). When applied to business problem solving, quantitative methods (e.g., op-
\par
erations research, statistics, financial analysis, etc.) manipulate precisely defined symbols
\par
using formal axioms (Daft \& Wiginton, 1979). Such methods yield precise solutions. How-
\par
ever, quantitative models sacrifice the accuracy with which problems are described, since
\par
the limited set of precisely defined symbols misrepresents ambiguous problem character-
\par
istics. For example, a common criticism of cost-benefit analysis is that intangible benefits
\par
cannot be expressed in quantitative form (Keen, 1975, 1981). In contrast, qualitative prob-
\par
lem analysis using natural language will more accurately capture ambiguous characteris-
\par
tics of the problem environment, but will not yield a precise solution.
