\par
professionals will perceive \emph{fixa/ }model methods as more useful in evaluating information
\par
systems.
\par
Senior executives are more likely to face ambiguous, unstructured problems than are stu-
\par
dents or professionals. Mintzberg (1973) argues that senior executives rarely engage in
\par
thoughtful, structured planning and decision making. They instead move towards imple-
\par
mentation and action in unstructured domains, and away from formal reports and anal-
\par
ysis of well-structured problems. Senior executives therefore have greater experience andINFORMATION SYSTEM ASSESSMENT 97
\par
training in strategically linking projects with organizational goals and in recognizing, react-
\par
ing to, and enacting the political environment of organizations. As a result, they are likely
\par
to perceive qualitative strategies as more useful in assessing information systems:
\par
H5: Relative to information system students and professionals, senior executives will perceive
\par
\emph{strategic }model methods as more useful in evaluating information systems.
\par
H6: Relative to information system students and professionals, senior executives will perceive
\par
\emph{political }model methods as more useful in evaluating information systems.
\par
The principle of incompatibility (Daft \& Wiginton, 1979; Zadeh, 1973) states that as a
\par
system becomes more complex, it is increasingly difficult to generate statements about the
\par
system that are both precise and significant. Ultimately, a threshold is reached beyond
\par
which precision and significance are mutually exclusive. Research suggests that managers
\par
face increasingly complex, ill-defined problems as they advance in organizations (McCaskey,
\par
1982). The principle of incompatibility suggests that qualitative, unstructured practices will
\par
be most useful in understanding such problems, since they are best able to capture the va-
\par
riety present in complex systems. Senior executives are therefore likely to value unstruc-
\par
tured, qualitative assessment practices more as they gain experience and advance to the
\par
highest ranks of management:
\par
H7: As the work experience of senior executives increases, they will perceive lesser value in
\par
\emph{structured }assessment procedures and greater value in \emph{unstructured }assessment procedures.
\par
H8: As the work experience of senior executives increases, they will perceive lesser value in
\par
\emph{quantitative }assessment procedures and greater value in \emph{qualitative }assessment procedures.
\par
An experiment was devised to test these hypotheses. The following sections describe the
\par
method, results, and implications of the experiment.
\par
METHOD
\par
\textbf{\emph{Task}}
\par
A case was developed based upon an actual implementation and assessment situation.'
\par
The case describes the implementation of an expert system used in developing financial
\par
plans for a financial services partnership. After a six-month trial period, the partners are
\par
unsure whether to continue using the expert system. The experimental task was to respond
\par
to Likert-scale questions asking the usefulness to the partnership of alternative assessment
\par
strategies. Prior to the experiment, the experimental materials were edited and revised based
\par
upon the results of a pilot experiment and the comments of two practicing knowledge
\par
engineers.
\par
The expert systems domain provides a setting in which all four decision models may be
\par
relevant. Expert systems are sometimes implemented to increase efficiency (e.g., the DEC
\par
system for configuring new computers), sometimes implemented to increase effectiveness
\par
(e.g., the PROSPECTOR expert system used in geological exploration), and may signifi-
\par
cantly impact political relationships in organizations (Sviokla, 1989; Turban, 1988). Fur-
\par
`Appendix A illustrates the case and experimental task.98 D.N. STONE
\par
ther, methods for assessing expert systems are not well established (Hayes-Roth, Waterman,
\par
\& Lenat, 1983; O'Keefe, Balci, \& Smith, 1987). Assessing expert systems therefore repre-
\par
sents a domain where objective/rational and subjective/political issues are potentially rel-
\par
evant, and where there are few established assessment guidelines.
\par
Participants rated the usefulness of eight assessment strategies: One structured and one
\par
unstructured methodology from each of the four decision models in Figure 1.3 The assess-
\par
ment strategies used were derived by examining extant literature on assessment practice
\par
(e.g., Hamilton \& Chervany, 1981a, 1981b; Kumar, 1990; Newman, 1989). The order of
\par
presentation of the strategies was counterbalanced between participants. Participants re-
\par
quired between 35 and 55 minutes to complete the task.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Participants}}
\par
Three groups participated in the research: information system students, information sys-
\par
tem professionals, and senior executives. Fifty-three students enrolled in an undergradu-
\par
ate Organizational Issues in Information Systems course participated for course credit. All
\par
had completed at least two previous courses in information systems. None had significant
\par
managerial experience. Because the case involved assessing an implemented expert system,
\par
15 professionals specializing in knowledge engineering were recruited from a large, inter-
\par
national consulting firm. The professionals had between 2 and 12 years experience in
\par
knowledge engineering, with a mean of 3.9 years. They completed the task during a regu-
\par
larly scheduled staff meeting.
\par
Senior executives were recruited from the Executive MBA program of a large, midwest-
\par
ern university. To gain enrollment to the Executive MBA program, prospective students
\par
must hold senior managerial positions and have extensive work experience. Based upon a
\par
description of the research, 18 executives agreed to participate. The experience level of par-
\par
ticipating senior executives ranged from 7 to 35 years, with a mean of 16.4 years.
\par
