\textbf{LANGUAGE, TRAINING, AND EXPERIENCE IN}
\par
\textbf{INFORMATION SYSTEM ASSESSMENT}
\par
DAN N. STONE
\par
\emph{University of Illinois}
\par
Abstracl-This paper proposes a framework of models for making information system assessments and
\par
provides empirical evidence relevant to the framework. Perceptions of the decision language and de-
\par
gree of structure appropriate to each model are tested, as are the impact of training and experience on
\par
the perceived usefulness of various assessment models. Results indicate that assessment models primarily
\par
based on quantitative language were perceived as more useful when executed as structured procedures,
\par
and models primarily based on qualitative language were perceived as more useful when executed as
\par
unstructured procedures. In addition, perceptions of a decision model's usefulness were affected by par-
\par
ticipants' training and experience. The findings suggest that no single model is perceived as rich enough
\par
to encompass a full range of decision languages and procedures, and that the perceived usefulness of
\par
any given model depends on an individual's training and experience. Triangulation and dialectic inquiry
\par
are suggested as possible multimode1 strategies useful in enriching information system assessment
\par
practice.
\par
\emph{Keywords: }Information system assessment, Language, Knowledge, Evaluation methods.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Assessments of information systems are performed for many different reasons, using a
\par
variety of methods. Surveys attempt to determine user satisfaction with a system (Newman,
\par
1989). Benchmark tests of hardware and software evaluate system component performance
\par
characteristics (Ferrari, 1978). System audits measure the extent of compliance with inter-
\par
nal control standards (Gallegos, Richardson, \& Borthick, 1987). Studies of changes in the
\par
nature and meaning of work caused by information technologies explore their social con-
\par
sequences (e.g., Braverman, 1974; Zuboff, 1982, 1988).
\par
The resulting assessments differ in significant ways. Some evaluate system efficiency,
\par
others system morality. Some are structured, others unstructured. Assessments may be un-
\par
dertaken to acquire knowledge, to legitimate political activity (Legge, 1984), to signal and
\par
symbolize rationality and competence (Feldman \& March, 1981), or to administer rewards
\par
and punishments to organizational actors (Hirschheim \& Smithson, 1988; Pfeffer, 1978).
\par
The breadth of practices used in assessing information systems confirm that information
\par
systems are complex, equivocal phenomena that impact organizations at many levels, in a
\par
variety of ways (Davis \& Taylor, 1976; Weick, 1990). Each assessment model is trying to
\par
capture the essence of that variety, from its own perspective.
\par
A problem in understanding information systems assessment practice and research is that
\par
alternative models of assessment exist in relative isolation. One body of literature proposes
\par
91\textbf{92 D.N. STONE}
\par
using quantitative, formal assessment methods based upon computationally rational models
\par
of choice (e.g., Kauffman \& Kriebel, 1988; King \& Schrems, 1978). Another argues for per-
\par
ceptual, quantitative measures of user satisfaction administered through retrospective sur-
\par
vey instruments (e.g., Newman, 1989). Still another for measurement of the social, political,
\par
and ethical dimensions of information systems (e.g., Zuboff, 1982, 1988). The existence
\par
of alternative paradigmatic approaches to assessment can be useful in generating multiple
\par
frames of reference for understanding the inherent complexity of information systems.
\par
However, existing literature is relatively unhelpful in making sense of the relationships
\par
among these alternative frames of reference.
\par
One approach to understanding relationships among assessment practices is based upon
\par
understanding evaluator assumptions (i.e., untested beliefs) and values (Hirschheim \&
\par
Smithson, 1988; Stone, in press). The goal of such research is not to limit the breadth of
\par
practices, or to unify practice under a single set of assumptions and values. Rather it is to
\par
understand how divergent practices can coexist and, by maintaining their separateness, be
\par
used to generate more complex thinking about complex phenomena (Burrell \& Morgan,
\par
1979).
\par
Differences in training and experience provide an important clue to making sense of as-
\par
sessment practice. Considerable evidence indicates that experience and training impact de-
\par
cision making (Johnson, 1988; Shanteau, 1988; Zmud, 1979). Experience differences have
\par
been found in a variety of domains, including accounting (David \& Solomon, 1989), mar-
\par
keting (Alba \& Hutchinson, 1987), medicine (Johnson, Hassebrock, Duran, \& Moller,
\par
1982) and physics (Chi, Glaser, \& Farr, 1988). Research in information systems has inves-
\par
tigated the role of experience in their usage (Benbasat \& Dexter, 1981) and perceptions of
\par
system usefulness (Lucas, 1975). However, little is known about how experience and train-
\par
ing impact assessment processes and decisions. Improved understanding of how training
\par
and experience give rise to assumptions and values that lead to choices of assessment meth-
\par
ods would provide valuable insight into building theories of assessment practice and research.
\par
This paper proposes a framework for information system assessment that joins assess-
\par
ment models derived from rational theories of choice with those derived from descriptions
\par
of practice. Implicit in the framework is the assumption that decision languages logically
\par
relate to decision models. This assumption is empirically tested. Hypotheses investigating
\par
the role of training and experience in shaping expectations of the usefulness of alternative
\par
models in the framework are also proposed and tested. The results show that the limited
\par
scope of decision language and structure that are associated with different assessment meth-
\par
ods are at least implicitly recognized by stakeholders, suggesting that the hope for one of
\par
the methods to encompass the full range of assessment needs is unfounded. Further, the
\par
experience and training of an individual affects their preference for an assessment method.
\par
The paper concludes by discussing the possibilities for using multiple decision models in
\par
assessment practice and with suggested future directions for assessment research.
\par
DECISION MODELS AND EXPERIENCE IN ASSESSMENT CHOICE
\par
Principles of scientific inquiry and knowledge are useful as metaphors for assessment
\par
research and practice (Legge, 1984). Assessment methods are chosen to test hypotheses.
\par
However, hypotheses result from untested assumptions and expectations about what con-
\par
stitutes valid knowledge, how knowledge is acquired, and what will be ``discovered.'' OneINFORMATION SYSTEM ASSESSMENT 93
\par
implication of this reasoning is that the choice of a methodology for assessment is tightly
\par
bound with issues of theoretical substance (Weick, 1984). Choices of assessment methods
\par
are heavily influenced by what investigators expect to see, since methodological choices flow
\par
from beliefs about what is ``out there.'' Such a perspective assumes that ``believing is see-
\par
ing'' (Weick, 1979, p. 135); that is, that the choice of an assessment methodology shapes
\par
what is subsequently ``seen.''
\par
Hirschheim and Smithson (1988) have recently highlighted the assumptions of alterna-
\par
tive information system assessment methods. Methods were placed on a continuum ranging
\par
from purely objective/rational to purely subjective/political. They argue that the informa-
\par
tion system literature exploring assessment has largely ignored their subjective/political di-
\par
mension, focusing instead upon quantitative, formal methodologies close to the objective/
\par
rational dimension of the continuum. Since existing research has largely failed to consider
\par
subjective and political factors, they argue that, ``. . . much of what has been done under
\par
the umbrella of information system evaluation has been ill-conceived'' (p. 17).
\par
\emph{Decision models}
\par
The extent to which assessment methods embody objective/rational versus subjective/
\par
political assumptions provides an important cornerstone for making sense of assessment.
\par
Using similar principles, Meyer (1984) identifies four decision models used in justifying and
\par
assessing capital investments. Based upon observation of capital budgeting and project as-
\par
sessment practices in hospitals, Meyer argues that these models operate as alternative frames
\par
or lens through which issues are viewed. ' Boland and Pondy (1986) demonstrate that the
\par
four models proposed by Meyer may have general relevance to investment and assessment
\par
decisions. They explored the role of accounting information in budget cutting decisions
\par
made by a school board and found that Meyer's models provided significant insight into
\par
understanding resource allocation decisions.
\par
Figure 1 adapts Meyer's decision models to information system assessment and places
\par
them within the framework proposed by Hirschheim and Smithson. For each model, the
\par
assessment goal and level of analysis (i.e., efficiency, effectiveness) are articulated, and ex-
\par
amples of assessment methods are described. Since decision models are logically related to
\par
