\emph{Training and experience in assessment choice}
\par
Decision makers are likely to specialize in particular decision models as the result of pro-
\par
fessional training and experience (Dearborn \& Simon, 1958). As the result of training, in-
\par
formation system students and professionals are likely to hold assumptions and values that
\par
lead them to ``see'' assessment as a quantitative decision problem. For example, Sprague
\par
and Carlson (1982) argue that decision support system assessment should be conducted as
\par
a formal, structured, quasi-experiment intended to determine system quality. Dickson and
\par
Wetherbe (1985) propose standard financial measures (e.g., present value analysis, inter-
\par
nal rate-of-return) to assess a system's financial contribution. information system students
\par
and professionals are therefore trained to see quantitative language as more appropriate
\par
to assessment than are senior executives.
\par
The professional training of information system students is heavily focused on detailed,
\par
technical issues of systems analysis and design. Information system students are therefore
\par
more likely to see assessment as a system quality issue. Ahhough information system pro-
\par
fessionals receive the same training, professional experience is likely to increase their aware-
\par
ness of issues beyond system quality. As a result, students are likely to perceive clinical
\par
strategies of assessment as more valuable since they directly pertain to system quality:
\par
