\par
students will perceive \emph{chical }model methods as more useful in evaluating information systems.
\par
In contrast to students, professionals are more practiced at using financial models and
\par
analysis, both as information gathering tools (e.g., King \& Schrems, 1978) and as devices
\par
for pushing systems assessment activities in desired directions. For example, Kling and Ia-
\par
cone (1984) found that system professionals were adept at using financial models as po-
\par
litical tools for increasing their own power and control. Information system professionals,
\par
relative to students and senior executives, will therefore ``see'' fiscal strategies of assessment
\par
as more vaIuable since they serve a dual purpose. Such methods are useful both as infor-
\par
mation gathering tools and as rhetorical devices for pushing systems towards particular
\par
goals:
\par
H4: Relative to information system students and to senior executives, information system
\par
professionals will perceive \emph{fixa/ }model methods as more useful in evaluating information
\par
systems.
\par
Senior executives are more likely to face ambiguous, unstructured problems than are stu-
\par
dents or professionals. Mintzberg (1973) argues that senior executives rarely engage in
\par
thoughtful, structured planning and decision making. They instead move towards imple-
\par
mentation and action in unstructured domains, and away from formal reports and anal-
\par
ysis of well-structured problems. Senior executives therefore have greater experience andINFORMATION SYSTEM ASSESSMENT 97
