DISCUSSION
\par
\emph{Assessment languages and decision models}
\par
The data provide direct empirical evidence linking assessment languages with decision
\par
models. Participants perceived decision models assumed to be based upon quantitative lan-
\par
guages to be more valuable when implemented using structured methods, while models
\par
assumed to be based upon qualitative languages were perceived as more valuable when im-
\par
plemented using unstructured methods. To date, investigations of the role of language in
\par
decision making have largely been based on case and field study evidence. This paper pro-
\par
vides evidence from a controlled experiment that directly supports the hypothesized link-
\par
ages between languages and decision models argued by Daft and Wiginton (1979), Meyer
\par
(1984), and Hirschheim and Smithson (1988).
\par
``Since rankings were used for the two regression analyses, a regression using the four unstructured procedures
\par
(regression no. 1) or the four qualitative procedures (regression no. 2) as dependent variables produces a mirror
\par
regression to the analyses actually performed (i.e., identical coefficients and RZ values, but with a positive coef-
\par
ficient for years of experience).\textbf{102 D.N. STONE}
\par
The results suggest that different assessment decision models are not interchangeable,
\par
but rather reflect fundamentally different ways of understanding. One implication of this
\par
observation is that assessment models provide alternative frames of reference for making
\par
sense of information systems. Triangulation is the use of multiple, independent measures
\par
to generate thicker, richer descriptions of phenomena (Jick, 1979). One way to increase the
\par
sense making capacity of alternative assessment models is to explore the possibilities for
\par
using them to generate me\textasciitilde urement tri\textasciitilde gulation. Each model invokes different ideas and
\par
associations that are used to expand understanding of the object under observation. The
\par
limited set of languages and procedures embedded within particular models prevent any sin-
\par
gle model from providing the sense making capacity needed to understand high equivocality
\par
phenomena such as information systems. Using multiple, independent models can be used
\par
to generate triangulated measurements that result in richer, more complicated understand-
\par
ing of information systems.
\par
Several techniques may be helpful in effectively using multiple models in assessment.
\par
Though expensive, one approach is to conduct multiple, simultaneous, independent eval-
\par
uations (Legge, 1984). For example, four evaluation teams could be used, one to conduct
\par
quantitative surveys, another to perform financial analysis, a third to assess strategic im-
\par
plications, and a fourth to evaluate political and social dimensions. Each team writes a sep-
\par
arate evaluative report.
\par
Another approach is to use or train cognitively complex evaluators (Bartunek, Gordon,
\par
\& Weathersby, 1983; Streufert \& Swezey, 1986) who use multiple frames of reference and
\par
write a single report that reflects multiple views. In conducting ``complicated'' evaluation,
\par
evaluators successively cycle among decision models. Such an approach is illustrated by
\par
Franz and Robey's (1984) analysis of user involvement in an insurance company system de-
\par
sign and implementation. Events are first described, and then given two different interpre-
\par
tations: one rational, one political. Though costly and demanding, such methods have the
\par
potential to generate more accurate descriptions of information systems.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Training, experience, and decision \textasciitilde\textasciitilde dg\textasciitilde\textasciitilde}}
\par
The results also support the hypothesized relationship between professional training and
\par
experience and choices of assessment methods. Information system students perceived clin-
\par
ical methods as relatively more useful, information system professionals perceived fiscal
\par
methods as relatively more useful, and senior executives perceived strategic and political
\par
methods as relatively more useful. The data therefore provide controlled experimental ev-
\par
idence supporting hypothesized linkages between professional training and experience and
\par
decision models suggested by Meyer (1984) and Boland and Pondy (1986). in addition,
\par
senior executives found increasing relevance in informal, qualitative methods with experi-
\par
ence, supporting an experience/decision model linkage suggested by Daft and Wiginton
\par
(1979).
\par
These results are particularly interesting when compared with previous survey research.
\par
Brancheau and Wetherbe (1987) used a Delphi method and survey sampling technique to
\par
investigate the importance of information system management issues among information
\par
system and other managers. The only consistent difference in rankings between the two
\par
groups was that other managers rated ``measuring effectiveness'' as a more important is-
\par
sue than information system managers. The present study suggests that these differences\textbf{INFORMATION SYSTEM ASSESSMENT 103}
\par
may arise because senior and nonspecialist executives conceptualize the problem of mea-
\par
suring effectiveness to include qualitative, unstructured dimensions. Such a conceptualiza-
\par
tion creates a more equivocal, ambiguous problem formulation and results in nonspecialist
\par
managers ``seeing'' the measurement of effectiveness as more difficult.
\par
One benefit of specialization resulting from differing professional training and experi-
\par
ence may be the natural capacity for organizations to implement dialectic inquiry methods.
\par
Using dialectic inquiry, differing models are invoked and maintained in tension to com-
\par
pletely and logically examine situations from differing frames of reference (Mason, 1969;
\par
Mitroff \& Emshoff, 1979). The ability to use such methods may naturally exist in organi-
\par
zations with differing functional specializations.
\par
Effectively using dialectic inquiry methods, however, requires maintaining (not integrating)
\par
alternative frames of reference (Mason, 1969). As a result, organizations may benefit from
\par
organizational structures that separate functional specializations yet provide opportunities
\par
for them to confront each other productively. An alternative approach to generating di-
\par
alectic tension however, may be to train people in multiple functional specializations. Such
\par
training may produce cognitively complex individu\textasciitilde s who learn to simult\textasciitilde eously trust and
\par
doubt the assumptions they learn from differing functional specializations (Wicker, 1985).
\par
\emph{Directions for research}
\par
Two important limitations of this research are that it investigates only the perceptions
\par
of information system stakeholders, and does not study the relationship among assessment
\par
decision models over time. Participants in the experiment were asked their \emph{perceptions }of
\par
the usefulness of alternative assessment strategies, but not their assessment \emph{actions. }How-
\par
ever, perceptions do not always give rise to intentions, and intentions do not always give
\par
rise to actions (March \& Olson, 1976). Melone (1990) has recently highlighted the impor-
\par
tance of building better theories of the linkage among perceptions, intentions, and actions.
\par
Such theories are particularly important to information system assessment, since informa-
\par
tion system professionals seemingly perceive that formal, structured assessments are impor-
\par
tant, but only infrequently act on such perceptions (Newman, 1989).
\par
In addition, the research investigated the assessment perceptions of decision makers at
\par
a single point in time, rather than examining the unfolding of the assessment process over
\par
time. Case study evidence suggests that alternative decision models are not invoked simul-
\par
taneously, or in consistent sequences, but rather in ways that resemble ``garbage can'' mod-
\par
els of decision making (Boland \& Pondy, 1986; Meyer, 1984). Evidence describing the order
\par
and manner in which assessment models are invoked is important to understanding how
\par
models can be made to effectively triangulate one another.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
This article provides evidence that languages are differentially embedded in decision
\par
models and that decision models are differentially embedded in the heads of information
\par
system stakeholders. When managers exercise their preferences and choose an info\textasciitilde ation
\par
system assessment method, they are choosing a language and a level of structure to use for
\par
looking at information systems. They are thus choosing how to shape and limit what they104 D.N. STONE
\par
will see, and are quite likely to see what they believe they will see. While such practices can-
\par
not be avoided, the illusion that one is free from assessment-shaping beliefs is preventable.
\par
By stretching one's vision of what constitutes an assessment model, the breadth of accept-
\par
able preconceptions and appropriate methodologies for assessment is expanded. Such
\par
broader vision is a prerequisite to making sense of the diverse and amorphous practices used
\par
in information system assessment, and to recognizing the fundamental differences in de-
\par
cision language and degree of structure inherent in each.
