\textbf{APPENDIX A -EXPERIMENTAL TASK}
\par
\textbf{West Coast Financial Planners}
\par
West Coast Financial Planners (WCFP), located in San Francisco, California, provides financial planning ser-
\par
vices to a wealthy clientele. George Moreland, president of West Coast Financial Planners, describes the strengths
\par
of the firm as, ``a commitment to quality, professionalism, and managed growth.'' The financial planning services
\par
provided by WCFP include tax planning, investment and insurance advIce, education planning, and retirement
\par
and estate planning. The goal of the firm in preparing financial plans is to increase the long-term net worth of
\par
clients by increasing earnings on assets, lowering taxes, and protecting assets. A typical client of the firm has over
\par
\$2 million in net worth and annual income of \$250,000 or more.
\par
The firm currently has five principals (i.e. owners), seven professional staff members, and three secretaries.
\par
Each principal owns a 20\% share of the firm and specializes in a different area of financial planning. John El-
\par
liot is an attorney, with eight years experience in estate and trust tax law. Frank Ryan has 17 years experience as
\par
a portfolio manager and investment adviser. Steven Kyle is a Certified Financial Planner (CFP) and CPA with
\par
12 years of experience in personal financial planning. Harold Wilson is a real estate broker with 14 years of ex-
\par
perience. \emph{George }Moreland, president of the firm, is an insurance agent with over 20 years of experience in es-
\par
tate planning and risk management. In 1985, each of the principals in WCFP earned approximately \$89,000 from
\par
the firm.
\par
The primary product of the firm is comprehensive annual financial plans for clients. Prior to 1986, the pro-
\par
cess for creating these plans was to: (a) gather the relevant data for the client, (b) prepare a two-page memo stating
\par
client goals, financial position, and personal characteristics, and (c) discuss options and prepare a final plan in
\par
three to five weekly meetings with all principals m attendance. The professional staff gathered initial client data,
\par
took notes during weekly meetings, and executed analysis suggested by the weekly meetings. After the plan was
\par
completed, it was presented to the client in a meeting with all principals in attendance.
\par
In the fourth quarter of 1986, however, it became apparent that success was causing problems of WCFP. In
\par
November and December of 1986 there were so many clients needing plans that it was impossible to schedule meet-
\par
ings at times when all principals could attend. Because each of the principals possessed unique expertise, George
\par
Moreland worried that the quality of plans might suffer as a result of generating plans without discussion among
\par
all of the principals. As a result of reducing the number of clients in which all principals were involved in the de-
\par
velopment and presentation of financial plans, however, 1986 was a record year for the firm, with each princi-
\par
pal earning around \$108,000.
\par
In January 1987, Steve Kyle suggested that the firm begin using an expert system called ``PlanAid'' to assist
\par
in preparing financial plans. Kyle argued that using PlanAid would enable the firm to continue expanding its cli-
\par
ent base, while simultaneously producing a lngh-quality product. He argued that PlanAid would act as a surro-
\par
gate for principals who might not be able to attend meetings during which plans were developed. While there was
\par
dissension among the principals on the value of using PlanAid, the decision was made to try PlanAid for the six
\par
month period between February and July of 1987 and to evaluate its effectiveness after the trial period. PlanAid
\par
and IBM-PS2s were leased for the trial period. The IBM-PS2s were needed since WCFP did not have existing hard-
\par
ware that could effectively run the Platid system. The principals and professional staff attended PlanAid training
\par
sessions \emph{on }January 25-28.INFORMATION SYSTEM ASSESSMENT 107
\par
The process used to generate plans using PlanAid differed substantially from that used previously. Data gath-
\par
ering using PlanAid took considerably longer and required more professional staff time, since PlanAid required
\par
specific data in a specific format. After the data was entered, PlanAid generated a summary memo, personal fi-
\par
nancial statements, and the client's comprehensive financial plan. The weekly meetings held to generate finan-
\par
cial plans changed from matching client snuations to planning tools, to a review and critique of the plan generated
\par
by PlanAid. Much of the time during meetings was spent figuring out how PlanAid arrived at its recommendations.
\par
Durmg the trial period (February-July), the perceived success and usefulness of PlanAid seemed to depend
\par
upon with whom you spoke, and on what day you spoke with them. Some comments that Moreland heard and
\par
jotted down during July 1987 included:
\par
Steve Kyle: ``PlanAid is a great aid in maintaining the quality of service we provide to our clientele. While there
\par
have been some start-up problems with using a new, highly sophisticated system, in the long run 1 think PlanAid
\par
will pay off for us. As I see it, PlanAid allows us to continue our focus on quality, and simultaneously grow to
\par
service more clients within our exclusive target clientele.''
\par
John Elliot: ``It seems to me that we cheapen our product by using PlanAid. Much of the satisfaction I get
\par
from our business is from the challenge of thinking long and hard about how to create financial plans for clients.
\par
Our meetings on client plans have gone from challenging to mechanical. We have a premium clientele that expects
\par
premium service. With PlanAid, I think we are generating plans of lower quality and I don't enjoy the process
\par
of generating them as much as I used to.''
\par
Harold Wilson: ``PlanAid provides us a distinct advantage over our competitors. We are using the latest tech-
\par
nology to improve our service, and to increase the number of target clients we can serve. I think using PlanAid
\par
will enhance our reputation as a top financial services company, and improve our long-run competitive position.''
\par
Frank Ryan: ``We miss the point by using PlanAid. PlanAid just changes the bottlenecks in generating finan-
\par
cial plans, it doesn't eliminate them. The bottleneck \textbf{used to }be our weekly meetings of principals to generate plans.
\par
Now it is in data collection and running revisions to plans. Getting all of the data into the right format takes Ion-,
\par
ger than it used to. Also, PlanAid takes two to four hours to generate a plan. If there is one error in the data used
\par
to generate the plan, then we have to rerun the entire plan. I believe we should focus on expanding our client base
\par
beyond our existing wealthy clientele, rather than tinkering with our methods of servicing existing clients. That's
\par
where the real potential for growth lies in this business. Because of the problems I see in PlanAid, it won't help
\par
us tap into that market.''
\par
