\textbf{Abstract.} This paper describes a tabu search heuristic for the vehicle
\par
routing problem with soft time windows. This problem allows lateness
\par
at customer locations although a penalty is then incurred and added to
\par
the objective value. By adding large penalty values, the vehicle routing
\par
problem with hard time windows can be addressed as well. In the tabu
\par
search, a neighborhood of the current solution is created through an
\par
exchange procedure that swaps sequences of consecutive customers (or
\par
segments) between two routes. The tabu search also exploits an
\par
adaptive memory that contains the routes of the best previously visited
\par
solutions. New starting points for the tabu search are produced through
\par
a combination of routes taken from different solutions found in this
\par
memory. Many best know solutions are reported on classical test
\par
problems.
\par
\textbf{K e y w o r d s . }Vehicle routing, time windows, tabu search, adaptive
\par
memory, neighborhood.
\par
\textbf{1. }Introduction
\par
An important component of many distribution systems is the
\par
routing of vehicles to service customers. For various applications, like
\par
bank deliveries, postal deliveries or school bus routing, a time interval
\par
or time window is also associated with each customer to constrain the2
\par
time of service. In practice, these time windows are often relaxed to
\par
allow for early or late arrivals at customer locations. In the literature,
\par
such problems are referred to as vehicle routing problems with soft
\par
time windows or VRPSTWs.
\par
From a graph theoretical perspective, the VRPSTW can be stated as
\par
follows. Let \emph{G }=(\emph{V }, \emph{E }) be a complete undirected graph with vertex set
\par
\emph{V }=\textbraceleft\emph{v0}, \emph{v1}, \emph{v2},..., \emph{vn}\textbraceright{} and edge set \emph{E }=\textbraceleft (\emph{vi},\emph{vj}): \emph{vi}, \emph{vj }{$\in$} \emph{V }, \emph{i}{$<$}\emph{j}\textbraceright. Each vertex
\par
\emph{vi }{$\in$} \emph{V }is associated with:
\par
(i) a fixed quantity \emph{q i} of goods to be delivered (with \emph{q 0 }=0 at
\par
vertex \emph{v0})
\par
(ii) a time window [\emph{ei}, \emph{li}], where \emph{ei} and \emph{li} are the lower and upper
\par
bound of the time window, respectively (with \emph{e0 }the earliest
\par
start time and \emph{l0 }the latest end time of each vehicle route,
\par
respectively).
\par
(iii) a service time \emph{si} for unloading the goods (with \emph{s0}=0 at vertex
\par
\emph{v0}).
\par
Finally, a symmetric distance matrix \emph{D }=(\emph{dij}) that satisfies the triangle
\par
inequality is defined on \emph{E }(with travel times \emph{tij} proportional to the
\par
distances).
\par
Given a fixed size fleet of \emph{m }identical vehicles, each with capacity
\par
\emph{Q }, the goal is to find a set of minimum cost vehicle routes, originating
\par
from and terminating at the depot, such that:
\par
- each vehicle services one route;
\par
- each vertex \emph{vi}, \emph{i}=1,...,\emph{n} is visited exactly once;
\par
- the quantity of goods to be delivered on a route never exceeds
\par
the vehicle capacity \emph{Q};
\par
- the start time of each vehicle route is greater than or equal to
\par
\emph{e0};
\par
- the end time of each vehicle route is less than or equal to \emph{l0};
\par
- the time of beginning of service \emph{bi} at each vertex \emph{vi}, \emph{i}=1,...,\emph{n} is
\par
greater than or equal to the time window's lower bound \emph{ei}; if
\par
the arrival time \emph{ti} is less than \emph{ei}, a waiting time \emph{w i} = (\emph{ei} - \emph{ti}) is
\par
incurred.
\par
The objective function \emph{f} to be minimized over the set of feasible
\par
solutions \emph{S} is:3
\par
