\par
where \emph{dk} is the total distance traveled on route \emph{k}, \emph{k}=1,...,\emph{m }, and {$\alpha$}\emph{i} is a
\par
lateness penalty coefficient associated with vertex \emph{vi}, \emph{i}=1,...,\emph{n}.
\par
This definition implies that each route must satisfy the following
\par
hard constraints:
\par
(a) the total load on a route cannot exceed the capacity of the vehicle
\par
servicing the route.
\par
(b) Each vehicle must start and terminate its route within the time
\par
window associated with the depot.
\par
Furthermore, a soft time window constraint is found at each customer
\par
location. The time window is "soft" because the vehicle can arrive before
\par
the lower bound or after the upper bound. If the vehicle arrives too
\par
early, it must wait up to the lower bound to begin its service. If the
\par
vehicle is too late, a penalty for lateness is incurred. That is, the upper
\par
bound of the time window is relaxed into the objective function in a
\par
Lagrangean relaxation fashion. According to (1.1), the penalty
\par
coefficients can be adjusted to each customer. For example, high
\par
coefficients can be associated with customers with rather strict time
\par
requirements and low coefficients with customers with some flexibility.
\par
Another closely related problem, known as the vehicle routing
\par
problem with hard time windows or VRPHTW does not allow late
\par
services. That is, a vehicle must arrive before the time window's upper
\par
bound at each customer location. Furthermore, the fleet size is typically
\par
a decision variable. Quite often, a hierarchical objective function is
\par
associated with such problems: first, minimize the number of vehicles
\par
and, for the same number of vehicles, minimize the total distance
\par
traveled. Most algorithms reported in the literature address the
\par
VRPHTW (with the exception of [Koskosidis et al. 92]). However, there
\par
are many good reasons for solving the VRPSTW:
\par
(a) The VRPSTW model is more general and includes the VRPHTW as
\par
well. The latter problem can be solved by appropriately raising
\par
the lateness penalty coefficients.
\par
(b) The VRPSTW more closely models situations found in practice
\par
and can be used to find a good trade-off between fleet size and
\par
service quality to customers.4
\par
(c) Since there are fewer hard constraints, feasible solutions are
\par
easier to find. In cases where the set of feasible solutions for the
\par
"hard" version of the problem is empty (assuming a fixed size
\par
fleet), a solution with a few time window violations can still be
\par
produced.
\par
However, the generality of the VRPSTW does not come for free. For
\par
example, hard time windows can be exploited within the VRPHTW
\par
model to quickly filter out infeasible solutions. Different techniques are
\par
reported in the literature to check solution feasibility in constant time
\par
after the insertion of a new customer or after a local modification to the
\par
solution [Savelsbergh 86, Savelsbergh 90, Savelsbergh 92, Duhamel et al.
\par
95]. Furthermore, the total distance of the new solution can also be
\par
easily evaluated in constant time. Obviously, the filtering mechanism
\par
cannot be applied to soft time windows, while the penalty component in
\par
(1.1) does not lend itself to a constant time evaluation. Rather,
\par
approximations must be used to achieve this result (see Section 3).
\par
The paper is organized as follows. Section 2 first presents a brief
\par
literature review on vehicle routing problems with time windows. Then,
\par
Section 3 introduces our neighborhood structure. Section 4 describes the
\par
problem-solving approach and provides a detailed explanation of its
\par
different components. Finally, Section 5 reports computational results on
\par
standard test problems.
\par
\textbf{2}. Literature review
\par
Due to their wide applicability in practical settings, vehicle routing
\par
problems with time windows have been intensively studied during the
\par
last ten years. These problems being NP-hard, a large spectrum of
\par
heuristics are reported in the literature (mostly for the VRPHTW). The
\par
interested reader will find excellent surveys in [Desrochers et al. 88,
\par
Solomon and Desrosiers 88, Desrosiers et al. 95].
\par
Generally speaking, these problem-solving approaches can be
\par
classified as follows:
\par
(a) \emph{exact algorithms based on branch-and-bound techniques} [Kolen
\par
et al. 87, Desrochers et al. 92]. Recently, Desrochers et al. found
\par
the optimum on a few problems with 100 customers. In this case,
\par
a column generation scheme was used to solve the linear
\par
programming relaxation of a set partitioning problem. The
\par
columns were generated by solving a shortest path problem with
\par
time windows.5
\par
(b) \emph{route construction heuristics}. Different sequential insertion
\par
heuristics are reported in [Solomon 87], while parallel route
\par
construction procedures are found in [Potvin and Rousseau 93,
\par
Russell 95]. A worst case analysis of some of these route
\par
construction heuristics is provided in [Solomon 86].
\par
(c) \emph{route improvement heuristics}. Edge exchange heuristics for
\par
problems with time windows may be found in [Or 76, Baker and
\par
Schaffer 88, Solomon et al. 88, Thompson and Psaraftis 93, Potvin
\par
and Rousseau 95]. Efficient implementations for speeding up the
\par
screening of infeasible solutions and the evaluation of the
\par
objective function are reported in [Savelsbergh 86, Solomon et al.
\par
88, Savelsbergh 90, Savelsbergh 92, Duhamel et al. 95].
\par
(d) \emph{composite heuristics}. These heuristics mix both route construction
\par
and route improvement procedures, see [Derigs and Grabenbauer
\par
93, Kontoravdis and Bard 95, Russell 95].
\par
(e) \emph{optimization-based heuristics}. In [Koskosidis et al. 92], the
\par
authors exploit a mixed integer programming model. Through
\par
heuristic means, the original problem is decomposed into an
\par
assignment/clustering subproblem and a series of routing and
\par
scheduling subproblems.
\par
(f) \emph{metaheuristics.} This new generation of heuristics is at the core of
\par
many recent developments. Near optimal solutions to the
\par
VRPHTW can now be produced with tabu search [Barnes and
\par
Carlton 95, Carlton 95, Rochat and Taillard 95, Potvin et al. 96],
\par
simulated annealing [Chiang and Russell 93] and genetic
\par
algorithms [Thangiah et al. 91, Blanton and Wainwright 93,
\par
Thangiah 93, Thangiah et al. 94, Potvin and Bengio 96]. An
\par
application of tabu search on a real-world vehicle routing
\par
problem with many side constraints, including time windows,
\par
may also be found in [Semet and Taillard 93].
\par
Finally, asymptotically optimal heuristics are reported in [Bramel and
\par
Simchi-Levi 93, Bramel et al. 93].
\par
Tabu search heuristics are of particular interest to us. [Potvin et al.
\par
96] describes a standard tabu search heuristic based on 2-opt* [Potvin
\par
and Rousseau 95] and Or-opt [Or 76] exchanges. The search alternates
\par
between the two neighborhoods and includes intensification procedures
\par
aimed at eliminating routes with only a few customers. The tabu search
\par
in [Rochat and Taillard 95] exploits a neighborhood based on the
\par
exchange of customers between routes. An adaptive memory is defined
\par
to record the best routes produced during the search. This memory is6
\par
then used to construct new starting solutions for the tabu search (see
\par
Section 3). Finally, [Carlton 95] describes a reactive tabu search that
\par
dynamically adjusts its parameter values based on the current search
\par
status. This approach is applied to many different types of problems
\par
with time windows, including the VRPHTW. Its robustness comes from a
\par
simple neighborhood structure that can be easily adapted to different
\par
problems. Namely, each customer is removed and reinserted at some
\par
other location in the current solution.
\par
The main contribution of our work is the development of a new
\par
neighborhood structure, coupled with approximation methods for
\par
evaluating each neighboring solution in constant time (in the presence of
\par
soft time windows). This is the topic of the next section.
\par
\textbf{3.} The neighborhood structure
\par
In this section, edge exchange heuristics are introduced in general
\par
terms. Then, our exchange method is described. Finally, simplifications
\par
and approximations aimed at speeding up the examination of the
\par
neighborhood are discussed.
\par
\textbf{3 . 1 }Edge exchange heuristics
\par
Edge exchange heuristics, like 2-opt or 3-opt [Lin 65] are widely
\par
used to improve vehicle routing solutions. Typically, these methods are
\par
embedded within a local search framework of the following type:
\par
1. Start with an initial solution and define this solution to be the
\par
current solution.
\par
2. Generate all solutions in the neighborhood of the current solution
\par
by applying all modifications associated with the method under
\par
consideration.
\par
3. Select the best solution in this neighborhood and define this
\par
solution to be the new current solution.
\par
4. Go back to step 2.
\par
Although the number of iterations needed to reach a local optimum
\par
can grow exponentially with the problem size (on carefully designed
\par
"pathological" problems), the neighborhood generated in step 2 is
\par
typically polynomial. This kind of iterative framework is exploited, in
\par
particular, within the tabu search heuristic [Glover 89, Glover 90].
\par
\textbf{3.2} A new exchange heuristic
\par
In this section, a new exchange heuristic is introduced which
\par
generalizes two edge exchange heuristics previously proposed for7
\par
problems with time windows. This method, called the CROSS exchange, is
\par
illustrated in Figure 1. In this figure, the black square stands for the
\par
depot and the white circles are customers along the routes (with the
\par
