\par
edges \emph{(X2, X2')} and \emph{(Y2, Y2') }are removed from the second route. Then,
\par
the segments \emph{X1'-Y1} and \emph{X2'-Y2}, which may contain an arbitrary number
\par
of customers, are swapped by introducing the new edges \emph{(X1, X2')}, \emph{(Y2,}
\par
\emph{Y1')}, \emph{(X2, X1')} and \emph{(Y1, Y2')}.
\par
Note that the time window constraints define an implicit orientation
\par
on each route. In this example, Y1 (respectively, Y2) is visited after X1'
\par
(respectively, X2'). So, the segment X1'-Y1 (respectively, X2'-Y2) has the
\par
