\textbf{3.3} Generating the neighborhood
\par
In this section, we explain how each move can be evaluated in
\par
constant time through approximations. Then, we discuss techniques
\par
aimed at discarding moves that are unlikely to yield any improvement.
\par
\textbf{3.3.1} Evaluating a move
\par
When we evaluate a move, we are interested in the difference {$\Delta$}\emph{f}
\par
between the value of the neighboring solution and the value of the
\par
current solution. An improvement is found when {$\Delta$}\emph{f} is negative. It is
\par
easy to evaluate the modification {$\Delta$}\emph{d} to the total distance of the solution
\par
after a CROSS exchange. Namely, (1) we sum up the lengths of the edges
\par
that have been introduced into the solution, (2) we sum up the lengths
\par
of the edges that have been removed from the solution and (3) we
\par
subtract (2) from (1). For the lateness penalty component of the
\par
objective, however, the impact of the CROSS exchange must be
\par
propagated to the end of the route, thus preventing a constant time
\par
evaluation. Hence, approximations must be used.
