\par
\textbf{3.3.2} Discarding moves
\par
The size of the neighborhood can be reduced by discarding moves
\par
that are unlikely to yield any improvement. In (3.4), four nested loops
\par
generate the entire neighborhood. At the level of \emph{Y 2 }, the loop is
\par
interrupted when the current segment leads to a violation of the
\par
capacity constraint when it is moved to the first route. Obviously, the
\par
situation can only be worse if the length of this segment increases. This
\par
loop is also interrupted when a monotonous degradation of the1 4
\par
(approximate) objective value is observed over three consecutive
\par
iterations. The same approach is applied at the level of \emph{X 2 }when a
\par
monotonous degradation of the objective value is observed over three
\par
consecutive iterations. In this case, the objective value associated with
\par
\emph{X 2} is the best solution found after iterating over \emph{Y1} and \emph{Y2}. Since the \emph{X 2}
\par
loop is at the second level within the nested structure, its interruption is
\par
much more beneficial in terms of computation time than the
\par
interruption at the level of \emph{Y 2 }. The interruption at \emph{X 2 }mostly occurs
\par
when the segments to be swapped cover different time periods. In this
\par
case, huge lateness is typically created on one route after the exchange.
\par
Hence, it makes sense to discard such "incompatible" moves.
\par
\textbf{3.3.3} Approximation matrix
\par
The overall computation time can be reduced by exploiting previous
\par
calculations stored in a data structure known as the approximation
\par
matrix. Each entry \emph{(i,j)}, \emph{i},\emph{j }=1,...,\emph{m }, \emph{i}{$<$}\emph{j}, in this upper triangular matrix is
\par
associated with a pair of routes \emph{i} and \emph{j}. It contains information about the
\par
best CROSS exchange (according to the approximation) for this pair of
\par
routes, namely, the value of the new solution as well as the edges
\par
introduced or removed from both routes to produce this solution. When
\par
a particular exchange involving routes \emph{i'} and \emph{j'} is accepted, only the
\par
information stored in row \emph{i'} and column \emph{j'} of the approximation matrix is
\par
updated (since only these two routes are modified by the exchange).
\par
That is, the information associated with any pair or routes that does not
\par
include neither route \emph{i'} nor route \emph{j'} remains the same and is not
\par
recalculated.
\par
\textbf{3.4 }Intra-route exchanges
\par
The CROSS exchanges of Section 3.2 swap customers between two
\par
routes. However, intra-route optimization is equally important to find
\par
good solutions. In order to optimize individual routes, the CROSS
\par
neighborhood is enlarged by including exchanges that apply to a single
\par
route. These exchanges are similar to those defined on a pair of routes.
\par
Namely, two edges are removed from a given route, and the segment
\par
between the two edges is moved at another location \emph{within the same}
\par
\emph{r o u t e }. This approach generalizes the Or-opt exchanges [Or 76], by
\par
allowing the relocation of segments of any arbitrary length.
\par
\textbf{4.} The problem-solving methodology
\par
The main ideas in this section, like the adaptive memory or the
\par
decomposition/reconstruction procedure have already been proposed in
\par
[Taillard 93, Rochat and Taillard 95]. The following presentation will1 5
\par
thus omit many details which can be found in the above papers (but
\par
without sacrificing completeness).
\par
The tabu search heuristic presented in this section follows the
\par
guidelines provided in [Glover 89, Glover 90]. It is embedded within the
\par
following problem-solving methodology:
\par
\emph{Construct p different solutions using a stochastic insertion heuristic.}
\par
\emph{Then, apply the tabu search heuristic to each solution and store the}
\par
\emph{resulting routes in the adaptive memory.}
\par
\emph{While the stopping criterion is not met do:}
\par
\emph{Construct an initial solution from the routes found in the adaptive}
\par
\emph{memory, and define this solution to be the current solution.}
\par
\emph{For I iterations do:}
\par
\emph{Decompose the current solution into C disjoint subsets of}
\par
\emph{routes.}
\par
\emph{Apply a tabu search on each subset of routes.}
\par
\emph{Reconstruct a complete solution by merging the new routes}
\par
\emph{found by the tabu search, and define this solution to be the}
\par
\emph{new current solution.}
\par
\emph{Store the routes of the current solution in the adaptive memory.}
\par
\emph{Apply a postoptimization procedure to each individual route of the}
\par
\emph{best solution.}
\par
This methodology includes many different components like an
\par
initialization procedure, a decomposition/reconstruction cycle (which
\par
will be referred to as D\&R in the following), an adaptive memory, and
\par
the tabu search as such. These components will now be described.
\par
\textbf{4.1} Initialization
\par
In order to fill the adaptive memory with different types of routes,
\par
a randomized insertion heuristic is used to construct \emph{P }different initial
\par
solutions. First, the routes are initialized by randomly selecting \emph{m }seed
\par
customers. Hence, each initial route only services a single customer. The
\par
remaining unrouted customers are then inserted one by one (in a
\par
random order) at the location that minimizes Solomon's insertion cost
\par
over the current set of routes (see Section 4.4 and the description of
\par
heuristic I1 in [Solomon 87]). This simple construction heuristic does not
\par
produce high quality solutions. Accordingly, the tabu search heuristic
\par
presented in Section 4.4 is applied to each solution before the resulting
\par
routes are stored in the adaptive memory. This phase introduces a high1 6
\par
level of diversity in the adaptive memory by storing routes extracted
\par
from different types of solutions. This diversity is then exploited by the
\par
tabu search.
\par
\textbf{4.2} Decomposition/reconstruction
\par
In order to reduce the computation time and intensify the search in
\par
specific regions of the search space, each initial solution is partitioned
\par
into \emph{C }disjoint subsets of routes, each subset of routes or subproblem
\par
being processed by a different tabu search [Taillard 93]. The best routes
\par
found for every subproblem are simply merged together to form the
\par
new solution for the next D\&R. After \emph{I} D\&Rs, the final routes are stored
\par
in the adaptive memory if it is not filled yet (see Section 4.3). Otherwise,
\par
the routes of the worst solution found in the adaptive memory are
\par
discarded and replaced by the new routes (if the new solution is better
\par
than the worst solution in memory).
\par
The decomposition is based on the polar angle associated with the
\par
center of gravity of each route. Using these polar angles, the domain is
\par
partitioned into sectors that approximately contain the same number of
\par
routes (see Figure 4). Note that the decomposition changes from one
\par
D\&R to the next by choosing a different starting angle for creating
\par
sectors, thus allowing the CROSS exchange heuristic to exploit new pairs
\par
of routes.
\par
\textbf{Figure 4. }Decomposition of a solution into subsets of routes1 7
\par
\textbf{4.3} The adaptive memory [Rochat and Taillard 95]
\par
The adaptive memory is a pool of routes taken from the best
\par
solutions visited during the search. Its purpose is to provide new
\par
starting solutions for the tabu search through selection and combination
\par
of routes extracted from this memory (in a manner reminiscent of
\par
crossover operators, which are found in genetic algorithms [Holland 75];
\par
however, the number of parent solutions in our case is generally greater
\par
than two).
\par
First, the memory is partially filled with routes produced during the
\par
initialization procedure (see Section 4.1). All routes of a given solution
\par
are contiguously stored in memory and the solutions are sorted
\par
according to their objective value. Hence, the routes associated with the
\par
best solutions are found in the first positions of the memory. This pool is
\par
then used to construct initial solutions for the tabu search. The selection
\par
process for creating a new solution is stochastic and biased in favor of
\par
the best routes. Namely, a selection probability is associated with each
\par
route, and this probability is higher for routes associated with better
\par
solutions [Rochat and Taillard 95].
\par
Once the first route is selected, the routes in memory with one or
\par
more customers in common with the selected route are discarded from
\par
the selection procedure. Then, a second route is selected among the
\par
remaining routes. This procedure is repeated until the set of selected
\par
routes covers all customers or until there is no admissible route in
\par
memory. In the latter case, Solomon's I1 insertion heuristic is invoked to
\par
insert the remaining customers (see [Solomon 87] and Section 4.4). If
\par
this insertion procedure cannot accommodate all customers, due to the
\par
capacity constraints or the time window at the depot, the unserviced
\par
customers are left aside. However, the insertion procedure is reinvoked
\par
at the end of each D\&R to try to insert these customers into the current
\par
solution.
\par
\textbf{4.4} The tabu search
\par
The tabu search presented in this section exploits the neighborhood
\par
structure of Section 3. It is applied to a subset of routes or subproblem
\par
through the decomposition/reconstruction procedure of Section 4.2. The
\par
tabu search is quite standard, and can be summarized as follows:1 8
\par
\emph{Set the current solution to some initial subset of routes.}
\par
\emph{While the stopping criterion is not met do:}
\par
\emph{Generate the neighborhood of the current solution by applying}
\par
\emph{CROSS exchanges.}
\par
\emph{Select the best (non tabu) solution in this neighborhood and}
\par
\emph{define this solution to be the new current solution.}
\par
\emph{If the current solution is better than the best overall solution}
\par
\emph{t h e n}
\par
\emph{reorder the customers within each route using Solomon's}
\par
\emph{I1 insertion heuristic and define this new solution to be}
\par
\emph{the new current solution and the best overall solution.}
\par
\emph{Update the tabu list.}
\par
\emph{Return the best overall solution}
\par
The main components of this algorithm are now briefly introduced.
\par
(a) \emph{Initialization}. As mentioned previously, the initial solution is
\par
produced by combining routes contained in the adaptive memory
\par
(see Section 4.3). Then, this set of routes is partitioned into
\par
subsets of routes, each subset being provided as input to a tabu
\par
search heuristic.
\par
(b) \emph{Stopping criterion}. The tabu search stops after a certain number
\par
of iterations. This number is calculated with the following
\par
formula:
\par
