\par
(e) \emph{Reordering of each route.} The customers within each individual
\par
route are reordered when an overall best solution is found. This
\par
reordering is based on Solomon's I1 insertion heuristic [Solomon
\par
87]. It works as follows for a given route. First, all customers on
\par
this route are unrouted. Then, the farthest unrouted customer
\par
from the depot is selected as a seed customer (i.e., the vehicle
\par
starts from the depot, services the seed customer and comes back
\par
to the depot). The remaining unrouted customers are then
\par
inserted one by one into the new route. The next customer to be
\par
inserted is the one that maximizes a generalized savings measure.
\par
The classical savings [Clarke and Wright 64] can be obtained by2 0
\par
setting the parameters of Solomon's heuristic to appropriate
\par
values. The selected customer is then inserted at the location that
\par
minimizes a weighted sum of detour in space and time. Solomon's
\par
heuristic is applied \emph{R }times with different parameter settings,
\par
and the best route is selected at the end. This strategy can be
\par
seen as a form of intensification in the neighborhood of an elite
\par
solution. Note that the original route is restored if the new route
\par
does not service all customers (due to the time window at the
\par
depot) or if the new route is worse than the original one.
\par
\textbf{4.5} Postprocessing of each individual route
\par
We applied a specialized heuristic developed for the TSP with time
\par
windows [Gendreau et al. 95] to each individual route in the final
\par
solution produced by our algorithm. This method is an adaptation of the
\par
GENIUS heuristic, originally devised for the TSP [Gendreau et al. 92].
\par
This heuristic only slightly improved the total distance of 10 solutions in
\par
Solomon's test set (the improvement was less than 1\% in all cases, but
\par
one), but it runs for only a few seconds and three additional best known
\par
solutions were obtained (see Section 5.2).
\par
\textbf{5. }Computational results
\par
Although our method was designed for the VRPSTW, it has been
\par
tested on Solomon's set of VRPHTWs [Solomon 87]. This choice comes
\par
from a need to compare our method with competing approaches on
\par
standardized test problems. In the following, these test problems are
\par
first introduced. Then, the best solutions produced by our algorithm are
\par
reported. Some figures about the improvement to solution quality with
\par
increasing computation times are also provided. Finally, the impact of
\par
the approximation techniques of Section 3 on solution quality and
\par
computation time is examined.
\par
\textbf{5.1} Test problems
\par
Our algorithm was tested on standard problems found in [Solomon
\par
87]. In these 100-customer Euclidean problems, the travel times are
\par
equivalent to the corresponding Euclidean distances. The customer
\par
locations are distributed within a [0,100]2 square. Six different sets of
\par
problems are defined, namely C1, C2, R1, R2, RC1 and RC2. The
\par
customers are uniformly distributed in the problems of type R and
\par
clustered in groups in the problems of type C. These characteristics are
\par
mixed in the problems of type RC. Furthermore, the time window is
\par
narrow at the central depot for the problems of type 1, so that only a2 1
\par
few customers can be serviced on each route. Conversely, this time
\par
window is large for the problems of type 2, so that many customers can
\par
be serviced on the same route. Finally, a fixed service time is found at
\par
each customer location (c.f., time for unloading the goods). This service
\par
time is set at 10 time units per customer for the problems of type R and
\par
RC, and 90 time units per customer for the problems of type C.
\par
\textbf{5.2} Numerical results on Solomon's test set
\par
The experiments reported in this section were performed on a Sun
\par
Sparc 10 workstation (50 Mhz). The real Euclidean distances between
\par
customers were rounded up or down at the third decimal place, and
\par
then transformed into integers by multiplying them by 103. In this way,
\par
precision problems were avoided during the calculations. However, the
\par
feasibility of the best solutions produced by our algorithm was later
\par
checked using real, double-precision distances. Our results were
\par
obtained with the parameter settings presented in Table 1.
\par
To reduce computation time, in particular on the problems of type
\par
2, the length of the route segments to be swapped could not exceed a
\par
threshold value \emph{L }. Different values were tried during our experiments.
\par
The best solutions reported in this section were obtained with \emph{L }=5 or
\par
\emph{L}=7. Note also that no D\&R took place on the problems of type 2, because
\par
only a few routes are needed to service all customers. In this case, each
\par
tabu search was applied to the entire set of routes.
\par
The best solutions produced by our algorithm are reported in Tables
\par
2 and 3 for all problems in Solomon's test set (based on real, double-
\par
precision distances), using the format: number of routes/total distance.
\par
The fleet size \emph{m }was set to the number of routes of the best solution
\par
reported in the literature for each problem. A feasible solution to the
\par
VRPHTW was produced in each case, with the exception of problem
\par
R101 (where one additional route is needed). On problems RC102 and
\par
RC106, the algorithm was able to save an additional route. In the tables,
\par
