Different transformations of the distance matrix and/or final
\par
solutions were applied by the authors referred to in Tables 2 and 3. In
\par
[Desrochers et al. 92], the distances were truncated at the first decimal
\par
place; in [Thangiah et al. 94], the final solution value was rounded up or
\par
down to the nearest integer; in [Rochat and Taillard 95, Potvin and
\par
Bengio 96], real double-precision distances were used. One consequence
\par
is that slightly different objective values can be associated with the
\par
same solution (in particular, for the problems in class C1). Another
\par
consequence is that solutions found on truncated or rounded distances
\par
are not necessarily feasible when real distances are used (unless the
\par
final solutions are checked for feasibility with real, double-precision
\par
distances, as we did).
