\par
A hierarchical objective function is typically associated with the
\par
VRPHTW. That is, the number of routes is first minimized and, for the
\par
same number of routes, the total distance is minimized. An exception is
\par
found in [Barnes and Carlton 95, Carlton 95] where the objective is
\par
simply to minimize the total distance. Accordingly, the distances of the
\par
best solutions reported in the work of Barnes and Carlton are typically
\par
lower than the numbers presented in Table 4. On the other hand, the
\par
number of routes is higher.
\par
In order to compare our method with the reactive tabu search of
\par
Barnes and Carlton, the fleet size \emph{m }was set to the numbers reported in
\par
[Carlton 95, Appendix B] for each problem. The results are summarized
\par
in Table 5. The first column is the average number of routes. The second
\par
column \emph{Reactive Tabu (best)} contains two numbers. The first one is the
\par
average of the best distances reported in [Carlton 95, Appendix B] when
\par
the parameter settings of the reactive tabu search are adjusted to each
\par
problem. The second number is the average computation time of the
\par
best runs on a IBM 6000 RISC workstation (in seconds). The third
\par
column \emph{Adaptive Tabu (avg.)} is the average distance produced by our
\par
algorithm over five different runs, when each run is given the same
\par
amount of computation time as the best run of the reactive tabu search.
\par
Here, the processing power of their machine was assumed to be similar
\par
to ours, which is true only for lower end IBM RISC 6000 workstations.
\par
Finally, the last column \emph{Adaptive Tabu (best)} is the average of the best
\par
solutions produced by our algorithm in the course of multiple
\par
experiments.
\par
First, it should be pointed out that the comparison between
\par
\emph{Adaptive Tabu (avg.)} and \emph{Reactive Tabu (best)} is quite unfair. Namely,
\par
the computation time associated with the reactive tabu search should be
\par
the sum of all runs leading to the best solution, not only the computation
\par
time of the best run. In spite of this enormous handicap, the \emph{a v e r a g e}
\par
solutions produced by our algorithm are competitive with the \emph{b e s t}
\par
solutions produced by the reactive tabu search, for the "same" amount
\par
of computation time. Furthermore, our best solutions are significantly
\par
better than those reported in [Carlton 95, Appendix B]. The reactive tabu
\par
search of Barnes and Carlton is thus able to produce good solutions in
\par
relatively low computation times. However, due to its simple
\par
neighborhood structure, it reaches its "peak" much earlier than our
\par
method.
\par
Finally, our tabu search heuristic was applied on two larger
\par
problem instances with 417 customers. These problems are known as
\par
D417 and E417 and are reported in [Russell 95]. A feasible solution with2 7
\par
55 vehicles was found in both cases, with a total distance of 3439.8 for
\par
D417 and 3707.1 for E417. Note that [Rochat and Taillard 95] report
\par
total distances of 3467.8 and 3693.2, respectively, for the same number
\par
of vehicles. Hence, a better solution was found on problem D417. It is
\par
worth noting that [Rochat and Taillard 95] also report solutions with 54
\par
vehicles, but the total distance then increases to 6264.8 for D417 and to
\par
7211.8 for E417. Our algorithm did not find feasible solutions with
\par
\emph{m}=54.
