\textbf{5.3} Solution quality versus computation time
\par
The results reported in Tables 2 and 3 are the best solutions
\par
produced by our algorithm during the course of multiple experiments.
\par
Accordingly, no meaningful computation times can be provided.
\par
However, Table 6 illustrates the improvement to solution quality with
\par
increasing computation times, using \emph{L}= 7. These numbers were obtained
\par
over five independent runs. For each problem set, we show the average
\par
computation time in seconds after 20, 50 and 100 calls to the adaptive
\par
memory, including all preprocessing that takes place before the search
\par
actually starts, the average solution value of the best run (\emph{M i n i m u m }) ,
\par
the average solution value of the worst run (\emph{M a x i m u m }) and the overall2 8
\par
average (\emph{A v e r a g e }). The first number is the average number of routes
\par
and the second number is the average distance.
\par
In these experiments, the number of routes was set to \emph{m }+1, where
\par
\emph{m }is the minimum value reported in the literature for each problem. By
\par
introducing an additional route, feasible solutions to the VRPHTW were
\par
quickly produced by our algorithm, so that all numbers in Table 6 refer
\par
to solutions that satisfy the time window constraint at each customer
\par
location. Note that the number of routes can decrease during the search
\par
if an empty route is created by a CROSS exchange. In the case of C1 and
\par
C2, the growth of the route segments was not stopped when a
\par
monotonous degradation of the objective value was observed over three
\par
consecutive iterations (c.f., Section 3.3.2). On these well-structured
\par
problems, the method quickly converges to good local optima and comes
\par
back to these same optima in the remaining of the run, thus causing an
\par
early stop (c.f., when the same best solution is found three times). By
\par
allowing a more thorough exploration of the neighborhood of the current
\par
solution, better solutions are found before the stopping criterion is met.
