\par
A comparison between \emph{Approx}, \emph{L}={$\infty$} and \emph{Exact}, \emph{L}={$\infty$} shows that the
\par
approximation generates solutions that are competitive with those
\par
obtained through the exact evaluation in a fraction of the computation
\par
time. The performance of the approximation degrades a little bit on the3 2
\par
large routes found in the problems of type 2 (in particular RC2). By
\par
comparing \emph{E x a c t }and \emph{A p p r o x }with \emph{L }= {$\infty$} and \emph{L }=7, we also note that
\par
solution quality does not degrade as much when the exact evaluation is
\par
used. However, \emph{E x a c t }with \emph{L = 7 }is still much more computationally
\par
expensive than \emph{Approx} with \emph{L=7}.
\par
\textbf{6.} Conclusion
\par
This paper has introduced a new neighborhood structure, based on
\par
CROSS exchanges, which is exploited within a tabu search heuristic. As
\par
the search progresses, good routes are stored in an adaptive memory
\par
and new starting solutions are produced by selecting and combining
\par
routes found in this memory. That is, new solutions are created from
\par
routes associated with different solutions visited during the search (in a
\par
manner reminiscent of the recombination or crossover operator of
\par
genetic algorithms). This problem-solving methodology has produced
\par
many best known solutions on Solomon's test set [Solomon 87] and
\par
seems to be fairly robust along the different classes of problems found
\par
in this test set.
\par
The CROSS exchanges are a useful generalization of the 2-opt*
\par
[Potvin and Rousseau 1995] and Or-opt [Or 76] exchanges and may be
\par
applied to other types of vehicle routing problems as well. Note also that
\par
our problem-solving methodology is readily amenable to parallelization.
\par
For example, many different starting solutions can be constructed from
\par
the adaptive memory and assigned to different tabu search processes
\par
that run in parallel. Furthermore, each tabu search process can be
\par
parallelized through the decomposition/reconstruction procedure that
\par
partitions a problem into smaller subproblems. With this parallelization,
\par
it is possible to envision the application of our methodology in a
\par
dynamic setting where each service request is dispatched soon after it is
\par
received.
