  note = {\textbf{Strategic Issues in}
\par
\textbf{Product Recovery}
\par
\textbf{Management}
\par
\textbf{Martijn Thierry}
\par
\textbf{Marc Salomon}
\par
\textbf{Jo Van Nunen}
\par
\textbf{Luk Van Wassenhove}
\par
of all used and discarded products, components, and materials that fail
\par
Product recovery management (PRM) encompasses the management
\par
under the responsibility of a manufacturing company. The objective of
\par
product recovery management is to recover as much of the economic
\par
(and ecological) value as reasonably possible, thereby reducing the ultimate
\par
quantities of waste.
\par
The traditional approach of many manufacturers towards used products
\par
has been to ignore them. Manufacturers typically did not feel responsible for
\par
what happened with their products after customer use. Most products were
\par
designed in such a way that while materials, assembly, and distribution costs
\par
were minimized, the repair, reuse, and disposal requirements were not talcen
\par
into account. Manufacturers generally believed that the costs of incorporating
\par
these requirements would outweigh the benefits. Most of their customers were
\par
not prepared to pay an additional fee for a "green" product. Most purchasing
\par
decisions were made with the intention of minimizing the purchasing costs,
\par
instead of optimizing life-cycle performance---which includes maintenance,
\par
reuse, and disposal issues. Consequently, the majority of used products in
\par
"developed countries" were landfilled or incinerated, with considerable
\par
damage to the environment.
\par
Today, both customers and authorities demand that manufacturers reduce
\par
the quantities of waste generated by their products. Customer pressure is trig-
\par
gered by environmental concern in general and by rising product disposal costs
\par
The first author was supported by N W O (Dutch Foundation for Scientific Research) in NFI-80 DSS
\par
program.The authors also acknowledge the financial support of INSEAD (Research pnsject 2042 C:
\par
Remanufacturing and Recycling fnsm a Business Perspective).
\par
114 CAUFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37, NO. 2 WINTER 1995Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
in specific. Product disposal costs have increased significantly in recent years as
\par
landfill and incineration capacity is being depleted. Governmental action encom-
\par
passes both legislation and purchasing programs. Legislative actions include dis-
\par
posal bans for specific products \textbraceleft e.g., appliances), recycled content mandates,
\par
recycling goals, and product take-back requirements. Purchasing programs favor
\par
products that are reusable or that have reused content.' Unfortunately, the con-
\par
tents of legislation and purchasing programs differ by country, or even by state
\par
within a specific country. This poses considerable problems to manufacturers
\par
that compete on a worldwide scale. A number of well-known international
\par
manufacturers \textbraceleft Including Shape, 3M, and Xerox) have decided to manufacture
\par
their products according to \textbraceleft or beyond) the highest existing environmental
\par
standards.'
\par
Many companies consider the issue of discarded products a threat to their
\par
business. Clearly, complying with rapidly changing regulations and fulfilling fast
\par
moving customer demands may require a fundamental change in doing busi-
\par
ness. However, there could be large opportunities for companies that succeed in
\par
ways of embodying current and future environmental demands in their business
\par
policy. Eor instance, by offering "green products," companies could attract and
\par
retain environmentally conscious customers and employees. Producing greener
\par
products could lower future liabilities, insurance rates, and customers' disposal
\par
costs. Pro-active companies could infiuence the formation of new legislation and
\par
avoid taking costly last-minute actions. Einally, companies could use discarded
\par
products as a valuable source of components and materials. These considerations
\par
indicate the necessity for manufacturing companies to develop an effective PRM
\par
policy.
\par
\textbf{PRM Options and the Acquisition of Information}
\par
Analyzing these PRM opportunities and threats should be the first step for
\par
any company that is (forced to get) involved in PRM. However, many companies
\par
find that the acquisition of the data required to make accurate analyses on PRM
\par
issues is problematic. Frequently, relevant data are scattered throughout the
\par
company, dispersed across companies in the business chain, or not available at
\par
all. In the case of PRM, the relevant business chain includes repair centers,
\par
reprocessors, and waste management companies.
\par
Required information can be classified into four categories:
\par
{$\bullet$} information on the composition of products;
\par
{$\bullet$} information on the magnitude and uncertainty of return fiows;
\par
{$\bullet$} information on markets for reprocessed products, components, and
\par
materials; and
\par
{$\bullet$} information on aaual product recovery and waste management
\par
operations.
\par
CALIFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37, NO. 2 WINTER 1995 115Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
\emph{Products}
\par
The first category of information entails the composition of manufactured
\par
products. Compiling information involves an analysis of the different material
\par
types, their relative quantities, their value, their potentially hazardous nature,
\par
and the way in which different components and different materials are fastened
\par
and combined. It also includes analysis of the technical feasibility of different
\par
product recovery options. Additionally, specific legislative requirements for indi-
\par
vidual products, components, and materials must be determined. A number of
\par
automobile manufacturers, such as BMW and VW, have set up pilot plants to
\par
examine reusability of current models and to develop design requirements for
\par
new models.'
\par
\textbf{\emph{Magnitude and Uncertainty of Return Flows}}
\par
The second category of information involves the "supply-side" of used
\par
products, components, and materials. Manufacturers need to examine the mag-
\par
nitude and uncertainties attached to each type of return flow. Each type of
\par
return flow may have different characteristics. One return flow type is that man-
\par
ufacturers could be required by law or by contract to take hack used products.
\par
For instance, VW and Opel have been selling some of their 1992 models in Ger-
\par
many with the guarantee that they will take back their products at the end of
\par
their lives at no charge to the customer." We expect that it will be hard for com-
\par
panies like VW and Opel to accurately predict the "supply" of used cars as it
\par
includes the need to estimate the number of cars that end their "lives" outside
\par
Germany (e.g., in Africa).
\par
Off-lease and\textbf{ off-rent products} constitute another type of return flows. A
\par
significant number of lease and rental contracts specify that manufacturers take
\par
back products upon contract expiry. In most cases, manufacturers are able to
\par
predict the quantities and timing of these return flows quite accurately. Making
\par
reliable estimations about the quality of these products can nevertheless be quite
\par
difficult.
\par
Products with technical failure form yet another type of return flow.
\par
Quantities of product returns depend on service contracts, product warranties,
\par
and the ability to repair products at the customer's location. The ability to fore-
\par
cast return flows of defective products depends on the nature of the products.
\par
For instance, electronic components tend to follow a random failure pattern
\par
over time, whereas mechanical components often tend to wear out with age.
\par
A limited number of manufacturers "buy-back" used products and
\par
components from their customers or used products' brokers. Buying back used
\par
products can have three advantages. First, manufacturers may acquire an inex-
\par
pensive source of components and materials for service or manufacturing pur-
\par
poses. Second, customers' disposal costs for landfilling and incineration can be
\par
reduced. Third, buying-back used products could provide manufacturers with
\par
the opponunity to sell or lease new products. The ability to predict return flows
\par
16 CALIFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37. NO. 2 WINTER 1995Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
in this case depends on the ability of the manufaaurer to set accurate buy-back
\par
prices.
\par
For each type of return flow, manufacturers must make an assessment of
\par
the expected quantities, quality, timing, and locations of return. This assessment
\par
gives manufacturers insight in the "supply-side" of used products.
\par
\emph{Markets for Reprocessed Products and Materials}
\par
Finding markets for used products can be very hard. The acceptance of
\par
used products in the marketplace depends basically on the perceived differences
\par
in quality and costs between used and "virgin" products or materials. Manufac-
\par
turers must make a "demand-side" assessment by examining potential markets
\par
for each of the product recovery options. Products and materials can be reused
\par
{$\bullet$} by the company itself,
\par
{$\bullet$} by other companies \textbraceleft e.g., suppliers) in the business chain, or
\par
{$\bullet$} by companies outside the business chain.
\par
\emph{Actual Product Recovery and Waste Management Operations}
\par
Analysis of current product recovery and waste management operations
\par
includes examination of:
\par
{$\bullet$} which organizations are involved,
\par
{$\bullet$} which obstacles occur (if any),
\par
{$\bullet$} which quantities of products are processed (for each product recovery
\par
and waste management option),
\par
{$\bullet$} costs and revenues, and
\par
{$\bullet$} the environmental impact of the entire process.
\par
Some manufacturers have found that cooperation with chain members
\par
can provide them with this information. IBM UK, for example, cooperates with
\par
Mann Organization, a UK-based equipment reprocessor.
\par
Since 1991, IBM UK has offered to take back used IBM equipment from
\par
any source. The company has outsourced the product recovery operations to
\par
Mann Organization. This organization reports back to IBM UK on the difficulties
\par
of product recovery and thus has an influence on IBM equipment design.'
\par
\emph{Product Recovery Options}
\par
Figure 1 presents an integrated supply chain where service, product
\par
recovery, and waste management activities are included. Returned products and
\par
components can be resold directly, recovered, or disposed \textbraceleft incinerated or land-
\par
filled). The five product recovery options are: repair, refurbishing, remanufactur-
\par
ing, cannibalization, and recycling. The options are listed in order of the required
\par
degree of disassembly \textbraceleft teardown). A H options are suitable for both products and
\par
components.
\par
