\par
Each of the produa recovery options involves collection of used products
\par
and components, reprocessing, and redistribution. The main difference between
\par
the options is in reprocessing. Hence, we concentrate on the reprocessing
\par
process in the description of the different product recovery options.
\par
Repair, refurbishing, and remanufacturing "upgrade" used products in
\par
quality and/or technology. These options differ with respect to the degree of
\par
upgrading. Repair involves the least, and remanufacturing the largest increase.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Repair}}
\par
The purpose of repair is to return used products to "working order." The
\par
quality of repaired products is generally less than the quality of new products.
\par
Product repair involves the fixing and/or replacement of broken parts. Other
\par
parts are basically not affected. Repair usually requires only limited product dis-
\par
assembly and reassembly. Repair operations can be performed at the customer's
\par
location or at manufacturer-controlled repair centers. A large number of durable
\par
products manufacturers \textbraceleft e.g., IBM, DEC, and Philips) are engaged in product
\par
repair.
\par
1 8 CAUFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL. 37, NO. 2 WINTER 1995Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
\emph{Refurbishing}
\par
The purpose of refurbishing is to bring used products up to specified qual-
\par
ity. Quality standards are less rigorous than those for new products. Following
\par
disassembly of used products into modules, all critical modules are inspected and
\par
fixed or replaced. Approved modules are reassembled into refurbished products.
\par
Occasionally, refurbishing is combined with technology upgrading by replacing
\par
outdated modules and parts with technologically superior ones. Military and
\par
commercial aircraft are examples of produrts that are refurbished. Refurbishing
\par
significantly improves their quality and extends their service-life. However,
\par
remaining service-life is generally less than the average service-life of new
\par
aircraft.
\par
\emph{Reman ufacturing}
\par
The purpose of remanufacturing is to bring used products up to quality
\par
standards that are as rigorous as those for new products. Used products are
\par
completely disassembled and all modules and parts are extensively inspected.
\par
Worn-out or outdated parts and modules are replaced with new ones. Repair-
\par
able parts and modules are fixed and extensively tested. Approved parts are
\par
sub-assembled into modules and subsequently assembled Into remanufactured
\par
products. Remanufacturing can be combined with technological upgrading. For
\par
example, a used machine tool can often be upgraded to "as new" quality and
\par
technology for 50-60\% of the cost of a new machine.'" BMW has been remanu-
\par
facturing high-value components such as engines, starter motors, and alter-
\par
nators for a number of years. The remanufactured components are tested
\par
according to strict quality standards to become a BMW Exchange Part. Exchange
\par
Parts are resold, with the same quality and warranty, at a price 30-50\% cheaper
\par
than new parts.'
\par
\textbf{\emph{Cannibalization}}
\par
\textbf{In} the first three product recovery options, a large proportion of used
\par
products is being reused. In cannibalization, only a small proportion is being
\par
reused. The purpose of cannibalization is to recover a limited set of reusable
\par
parts from used products or components. These parts are reused in repair, refur-
\par
bishing, or remanufacturing of other products and components. Quality stan-
\par
dards for cannibalized parts depend on the process in which they will be reused.
\par
Parts for remanufacturing have to fulfill stricter quality standards than parts for
\par
refurbishing or repair. Cannibalization involves selective disassembly of used
\par
products and inspection of potentially reusable parts. The remaining parts and
\par
modules are not used in cannibalization. For example, Aurora, a U.S. company,
\par
is mainly engaged in cannibalizing integrated circuits. The company pulls off the
\par
parts they want from a computer and tests, straightens, redips, polishes, and sells
\par
the chips. Sales have grown from zero to more than \$40 million between 1988
\par
and 1993.*
\par
CALIFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37. NO. 2 WINTER 1995 119Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
\emph{Recycling}
\par
The goal of the previous product recovery options is to retain the identity
\par
and functionality of used products and their components as much as possible. In
\par
recycling, the identity and funrtionality of products and components is lost. The
\par
purpose of recycling is to reuse\emph{ materials} from used products and components.
\par
These materials can be reused in production of original parts if the qtiality of
\par
materials is high, or else in production of other parts. Recycling begins when
\par
used products and components are disassembled into parts. These parts are
\par
separated into distinct material categories. These separated materials are subse-
\par
quently reused in the production of new parts. Recycling is currently being
\par
applied to a number of used products. For example, virtually all metals in dis-
\par
carded cars (on average 75\% of the weight of a car) are being recycled in West-
\par
em countries like Germany, the UK, and the United States."
\par
