\par
Products and materials may go through a sequence of different phases
\par
in their life. GE Plastics is experimenting with extending the life of a bumper
\par
through four successive phases: grinding it to become raw material for an inter-
\par
nal automotive component; regrinding it to become an ingredient for a plastic
\par
bench; recycling it into a building material; and designating it as fuel for
\par
incinerators."'
\par
Following is a case study based on the situation of a multinational copier
\par
manufacturer (nicknamed "CopyMagic") that has been involved in product
\par
recovery management for a number of years. This case study illustrates a
\par
selected set of strategic production and operations management issues in
\par
product recovery management.
\par
\textbf{Case Study: CopyMagic}
\par
CopyMagic is a multinational copier manufacturer that sells a number
\par
of different products in all segments of the copier market. Leasing of copiers is
\par
the most popular. Competition in the copier market is predominantly on price-
\par
per-copy, product reliability, product features, and customer service.
\par
Figure 3 shows the structure of CopyMagic's network. All products are
\par
assembled by CopyMagic itself out of a small number of modules. Assembly
\par
operations are carried out in factories located in Europe. Each product family is
\par
assembled on a separate assembly line. Products of a specific product family are
\par
assembled in one factory only. The factories engage in the sub-assembly of a
\par
limited number of modules. Each module consists of a large number of parts.
\par
The factories are responsible for the procurement of all parts and of all modules
\par
that are not produced by CopyMagic.
\par
Operating Companies (OPCOs), located in all major European countries,
\par
are responsible for the marketing, sale, and lease of CopyMagic's products.
\par
OPCOs are also responsible for providing (technical) service to CopyMagic's cus-
\par
tomers. OPCOs are managed as independent profit centers. Inventories of spare
\par
122 CALIFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37. NO. 2 WINTER 1995Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
parts, required to repair failed products, are held locally by the OPCOs and their
\par
service engineers and centrally by the Central Logistics Center (CLC).
\par
Located in one of the European countries, the CLC is responsible for all
\par
inbound and outbound logistics activities. This includes the management of all
\par
transportation of parts and modules from suppliers to factories and the distribu-
\par
tion of all finished products and spare parts to the OPCOs and their customers.
\par
Additionally, the CLC is the main storage center for inbound parts and modules
\par
and outbound products and spare parts.
\par
CopyMagic not only brings new products to markets, but also takes back
\par
used products from its customers. The majority of the return flows are off-lease
\par
products. These are products that CopyMagic has to take hack from its customers
\par
after expiry of their lease contracts. These products are returned to the OPCOs.
\par
A small part of the return fiows consists of demonstration models, copiers that
\par
have been used by CopyMagic itself, and copiers with technical failure.
\par
In this case study, we focus on the product recovery management system
\par
that CopyMagic has set up for its used products. Currently, CopyMagic operates
\par
an extensive product recovery management system that encompasses remanu-
\par
facturing, recycling, cannibalization, and
\par
repair operations. As we want to assess
\par
the influence of each of these reprocessing
\par
activities on the company, we have simpli-
