\par
The repair process did require additional spare parts to replace failed
\par
components. These spare parts were periodically shipped from the CLC to the
\par
OPCOs. Lead-times of spare parts could be very long (more than 4 weeks) when
\par
the CLC had gone "out of stock" due to unforeseen spare part demand. Conse-
\par
quently, OPCOs had to maintain large (expensive) inventories of spare parts in
\par
order to guarantee service. Figure 4 shows the structure of CopyMagic's network
\par
in this phase.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Cann ibaliza tion}}
\par
In the previous phase, the OPCOs were occasionally having problems
\par
with the supply of spare parts. These problems stimulated the OPCOs to engage
\par
in cannibalization of used \textbraceleft non-repairable or excess) products and components.
\par
Cannibalization reduced the need for "new" spare parts and decreased the
\par
amount of non-reusable waste. Cannibalization operations were quite simple.
\par
They consisted of partial disassembly of the used products; retrieval of poten-
\par
tially useful parts; and testing of the retrieved parts. The potential reduction in
\par
waste by means of cannibalization was limited. Typically, less than 10\% of the
\par
contents of used products was reused through cannibalization. The remaining
\par
fraction still had to be disposed of. All cannibalization activities were carried out
\par
at the OPCOs. Figure 4 represents this network structure.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Recycling}}
\par
The quantities of disposed products and components in the previous
\par
phases were considerable. As most of the used products' contents were not
\par
reused in any way, CopyMagic decided to engage in recycling of previously
\par
disposed products and components.
\par
The implementation of recycling involved a number of changes for Copy-
\par
Magic. First, CopyMagic had to change the design requirements for its products.
\par
Design requirements for recycling included the following aspects: design for
\par
disassembly, standardization of materials, and increasing the recycled content
\par
of products and components. Originally, CopyMagic did not design products
\par
according to these principles. This resulted in products that could hardly be recy-
\par
cled. CopyMagic's design for disassembly focused on improved fastening meth-
\par
ods and avoidance of non-separable parts and materials. In addition, CopyMagic
\par
concentrated on reducing and standardizing the different types of materials in
\par
its products. Fewer material types simplified recycling operations and made the
\par
recycling process more cost-effective. Non-recyclable materials were replaced
\par
with recyclable materials as much as reasonably possible. Materials were selected
\par
on the basis on\emph{ life-cycle costs and performance} instead of\emph{ purchasing and manufactur-}
\par
\emph{ing costs} only.
\par
CALIFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37, NO. 2 WINTER 1995 125Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
Separation and sorting of materials was greatly enhanced by the coding of
\par
materials in CopyMagic's products. This was especially true for plastics. Without
\par
coding, it was very difficult and expensive to separate and sort the different
\par
types of plastics. Additionally, CopyMagic increased the recycled content of its
\par
products. Finding markets for recycled materials is notoriously hard. Reusing
\par
these materials in manufacturing new products provides a (partial) solution for
\par
this problem, providing the quality of the materials is "as good as new" and the
\par
customers accept the use of recycled materials. An important effect of design for
\par
recycling was increased repairability. Due to easier disassembly, repair operations
\par
became simpler and more cost-effective.
\par
Second, CopyMagic had to determine which recycling activities it would
\par
perform in-house. As CopyMagic had limited experience in parts fabrication, it
\par
decided to perform all disassembly, separation, sorting, and cleaning activities in-
\par
house but to outsource all parts fabrication activities. CopyMagic's experience in
\par
produa assembly and knowledge on the composition of products facilitated exe-
\par
cution of the in-house activities.
\par
CopyMagic used recycling in the "green marketing" of its products. At the
\par
same time, CopyMagic had to convince its customers that products containing
\par
recycled materials still fulfilled all requirements with respect to product quality.
\par
Relations between CopyMagic and its suppliers changed in this phase.
\par
First, the number of suppliers decreased as CopyMagic standardized the different
\par
types of materials. Second, all suppliers were required to code materials accord-
\par
ing to CopyMagic's standards. Industry standards are being developed. Third,
\par
suppliers became more involved in CopyMagic's product design process by pro-
\par
viding useful advice on product recyclability.
\par
Finally, the relation between CopyMagic and some of its suppliers (the
\par
recycling companies) changed to a "two-way relation." In addition to being a
\par
buyer of new parts, CopyMagic became a seller of used materials. In addition
\par
to being sellers of new parts, these suppliers became buyers of used materials.
\par
Figure 5 gives an overview of the different forward and reverse flows
\par
in this phase. Repair and cannibalization operations were still performed by the
\par
OPCOs. All non-repairable and excess equipment were transported from OPCOs
\par
to a Central Recovery Center (CRC) located close to the CLC. Transportation of
\par
used equipment between the OPCOs and the CRC used the same trucks that
\par
brought new equipment from the CLC to the OPCOs. All disassembly, separa-
\par
tion, sorting and cleaning activities were performed at the CRC. Sorted and
\par
cleaned materials were transported to existing suppliers ("3" in Figure 5\textbraceright{} or
\par
to other companies ("4" in Figure 5). Reasons for centralizing these operations
\par
were threefold: faster learning by experience; higher utilization of capacity; and
\par
better coordinated and more cost-effective transportation both of recyclable
\par
materials to recycling companies and of disposable materials to disposal sites.
\par
126 CAUFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37, NO. 2 WINTER 1995Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
\emph{Remanufactu ring}
\par
Although recycling considerably reduced the amount of waste generated,
\par
CopyMagic decided to go one step further. In addition to reusing materials\emph{ \textbraceleft recy-}
\par
\emph{cling),} it decided to reuse components in making products that fulfill all quality
\par
standards for new products\emph{ \textbraceleft remanufacturing).} Potential benefits of remanufac-
\par
turing are: a reduction in material costs for parts and modules; a reduction in
\par
energy costs; a reduction in product disposal costs; and a reduction in the overall
\par
environmental impact.
\par
Today, CopyMagic is engaged in two forms of remanufacturing: remanu-
\par
facturing with the addition of new technology, and remanufacturing without
\par
the addition of new technology. Addition of new technology is usually done by
\par
replacing outdated modules and parts with superior new ones, and/or by adding
\par
new software or control systems. Technological upgrading is especially applied
\par
when new products with more advanced technology have been introduced.
\par
Design requirements for remanufacturing include: product disassembly,
\par
standardization of components, coding of products and components, and modu-
\par
lar design. These requirements are very similar to recycling requirements, the
\par
major difference being that remanufacturing focuses on components whereas
\par
recycling focuses on materials. Standardization of components facilitates reman-
\par
ufacturing since used components can be reused in several types of products.
\par
Coding of products and components is useful to monitor the condition of the
\par
products and components over time, and to identify the number of times com-
\par
ponents have been reused. Modular design enables separate development and
\par
testing, and stimulates easy disassembly.
\par
CopyMagic decided to perform all remanufacturing operations in-house.
\par
Being the "original equipment manufaaurer," it has a natural advantage over
\par
third parties. CopyMagic decided to perform the remanufacturing operations
\par
at the factories where the new products of the same type are produced. In this
\par
way, remanufactured and "new build" produas can use the same assembly lines,
\par
equipment, and operators. Start-up costs for remanufacturing were low, but
\par
production planning and control became more complex.
\par
Remanufacturing did require new skills and equipment that were not
\par
necessarily available---e.g., to perform testing, repair, and quality control opera-
\par
tions. In remanufacturing, quality control is essential as all used products are
\par
likely to have some defects.
\par
Since the quality of the remanufaaured products is "as good as new,"
\par
these products are treated in the same way as new products: similar warranties,
\par
similar service contracts. Lease prices for both product categories are identical.
\par
Sale prices of remanufactured products are somewhat lower than new products'
\par
sale prices. Remanufactured products are distributed through the same channels
\par
as new products, using the same transportation equipment and distribution
\par
facilities.
\par
CALIFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37. NO. 2 WINTER 1995 127Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
Remanufacturing has enhanced CopyMagic's "green image" in the mar-
\par
ket, but the company has had to put much effort in its marketing operations to
\par
convince its customers that the quality of remanufactured products is indeed
\par
"as good as new."
\par
Supplier relations are also affected by remanufacturing. Successful reman-
\par
ufaauring significantly reduces the need for outside procurement of parts and
\par
modules. There seems to be an interesting paradox. Suppliers that make the best
\par
components may lose most business (in comparison with other suppliers) since
\par
their components can be reused more often. Companies have to solve this "para-
\par
dox" if they want to engage in remanufacturing. Analogous to the previous
\par
phase, the number of component suppliers has been reduced as CopyMagic has
\par
standardized its components. All suppliers are required to code components in
\par
the same way as CopyMagic does.
\par
Benefits of remanufacturing are largest when companies are able to fore-
\par
cast (and control) the distribution (magnitude and timing) and quality of the
\par
return fiows of used products. The high share of lease contracts (usually with a
\par
fixed contract length) has enabled CopyMagic to predict these return fiows quite
\par
accurately. Occasionally, OPCOs buy-back used products from former customers
\par
when supply of suitable used products is below schedule. Nevertheless, Copy-
\par
Magic has had to set up new information systems to forecast, monitor, and
\par
analyze the return fiows of used products and the behavior of remanufactured
\par
products, modules, and parts "in the field.'
\par
Figure 6 shows the structure of the forward and reverse fiows. Manage-
\par
ment of all reverse flows has now been centralized at the CRC. Centralization
\par
has improved integrated control of all product recovery management processes.
\par
All return flows are sent from the OPCOs to the CRC (in the same trucks that
\par
are used for the distribution of new products). Here, all used products are
\par
inspected and categorized into four groups: repair, remanufacturing, cannibali-
\par
zation, and recycling. The first group encompasses products that have been used
\par
for a (very) short time. These products are repaired centrally and sent back to
\par
the OPCOs ("1" in Figure 6). They are primarily used as (temporary) replace-
\par
ment for failed copiers. The second group encompasses products that will
\par
undergo remanufacturing operations. These products are shipped from the cen-
\par
tral reprocessing location to the factories where they are remanufactured into
\par
original or technologically upgraded products ("2" in Figure 6). The third group
\par
encompasses those products that are "beyond repair or remanufacturing." Can-
\par
nibalization of these products is performed at the central reprocessing location.
\par
Reusable components are removed from the products, put into storage at the
\par
CLC, and reused in repair, remanufacturing or service operations. The last group
\par
encompasses the products that are recycled by "internal" ("3" in Figure 6) and
\par
"external" companies ("4" in Figure 6). Ultimately, a small fraaion of the used
\par
products (less than 10\%) still has to be disposed of.
\par
