\par
CopyMagic is convinced that PRM would also have been attractive with-
\par
out the threat of legislative and regulatory changes. The estimated benefits of
\par
remanufacturing have been the largest of all PRM options. According to the
\par
company, essential factors in the economic success of PRM have been:
\par
{$\bullet$} the high-value of the products,
\par
{$\bullet$} the modular design of the products,
\par
{$\bullet$} the ability to upgrade products by removing outdated components and
\par
adding superior new ones,
\par
{$\bullet$} the fact that most products are leased,
\par
{$\bullet$} the high quality image of CopyMagic which convinced customers that
\par
remanufactured produas are indeed "as good as new," and
\par
CALIFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37, NO. 2 WINTER 1995 129Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
{$\bullet$} the ability to integrate "new build" and product recovery operations in
\par
the fields of production and logistics management.
\par
\textbf{BMW and IBM}
\par
We now take a look at some of the efforts that have been undertaken by
\par
BMW and IBM, two companies that have been pro-aaive in product recovery
\par
management.
\par
\emph{BMW}
\par
BMW has been conducting recycling research in a pilot vehicle disassem-
\par
bly plant in Landshut, Germany, since June 1990. Research in Landshut has
\par
concentrated on three issues: recycling of materials from existing cars, reuse
\par
of high-value parts from existing cars, and design modifications for future cars.
\par
Materials recycling research has concentrated on improving the recycla-
\par
bility of plastics. Plastics constitute on average 10\% of the weight of each car.
\par
Until recently, virtually all plastics were landfilled. Recycling research specifically
\par
concentrated on ways to identify, sort, regranulate, remold, and reuse these
\par
plastics. BMW aims to reuse more than 80\% of all plastics. In some cases, BMW
\par
has been able to incorporate recycled plastics in the design of new parts, e.g.,
\par
the luggage compartment linings on the BMW 3-series are made of recycled
\par
bumpers."
\par
Remanufacturing of high-value components such as engines, starter
\par
motors, and alternators has been performed by BMW for a number of years.
\par
Each component is disassembled, tested, repaired, and reassembled according to
\par
strict quality standards. Remanufactured components have the same quality and
\par
are sold with the same warranty as new parts. They are sold as "Exchange Parts"
\par
for 50\% to 70\% of new product prices.
\par
Design modifications for improved produa recovery include reducing the
\par
number of materials used; avoiding composite components; marking parts and
\par
components; and using two-way fasteners instead of screws and glue. The BMW
\par
Zl, a two-seater, is an example of a car that was specifically designed for disas-
\par
sembly and recycling. It had an all-plastic skin that was designed to be disassem-
\par
bled from its metal chassis in 20 minutes. Its doors, bumpers, front, rear, and
\par
side panels were made from recyclable thermoplastics supplied by General Elec-
\par
tric Plastics. While evaluating design for disassembly, BMW has discovered that
\par
this approach to car design has resulted in cars with improved repairability."
\par
Other car manufacturers---including Volkswagen, Opel, Mercedes-Benz,
\par
Peugeot-Talbot and Ford---are also involved in setting up recycling research and
\par
operations."
\par
