\par
\emph{IBM}
\par
IBM Europe has set up a produa recovery management program in a
\par
number of European countries since 1990. The program was first set up in
\par
Switzerland, then in Germany, Great Britain, and the Netherlands.'"
\par
In Germany, IBM performs recovery operations in-house. In 1992, the
\par
Recycling Department of IBM Germany processed 4,000 tons of used main-
\par
frames and personal computers. This amount encompassed all equipment that
\par
IBM discarded itself (3,200 tons), and 800 tons of equipment from its customers.
\par
The majority are used mainframes. Transportation and other collection costs are
\par
the largest costs in reprocessing used mainframes. Surprisingly, current repro-
\par
cessing costs are significantly higher than they were 10 years ago. This is because
\par
previous designs of mainframes contained significantly more precious metals
\par
such as gold and silver than do current ones."
\par
In the United Kingdom, IBM UK has offered to take back old equip-
\par
ment from its customers since 1991. IBM charges a fee to cover the difference
\par
between processing costs and the value of reclaimed material. Since customers
\par
are not (yet) willing to pay for reprocessing of used products, only a small pro-
\par
portion of used computers are collected and reprocessed. As mentioned earlier,
\par
IBM UK cooperates with the Mann Organization, a used equipment reprocessor,
\par
in its product recovery operations. In 1990, IBM UK received 1,370 tons of
\par
material and was able to recover 1,000 tons.'* IBM UK aims to reach its recycl-
\par
ing target of 95\% by 1995."
\par
In the Netherlands, IBM and DEC have set up a joint program in coopera-
\par
tion with MIREC, a Dutch equipment reprocessor, to recycle used personal com-
\par
puter equipment. The majority of this equipment Is "off-lease" computers. IBM
\par
and DEC take care of the collection of all computers and the retrieval of reusable
\par
components. The partly disassembled computers are subsequently sent to
\par
MIREC. This company completely dismantles all computers, separates and
\par
sorts valuable and recyclable materials, and takes care of the disposal of non-
\par
recyclable materials. For this operation, MIREC charges IBM and DEC approxi-
\par
mately \$1 per kilo.'"
\par
\textbf{Managerial Implications}
\par
Product recovery management will become an important business activity
\par
for many companies. The importance of product recovery management to the
\par
profitability of the company depends on the ability to reduce the environmental
\par
impact of used products, the capability to recover as much economic value as
\par
possible out of the used products, the ability to use product recovery manage-
\par
ment as a marketing tool, and legislation. A limited number of pro-active manu-
\par
facturers have already set up systems for product recovery management and
\par
their experiences provide us with the following observations.
\par
CAUFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37. NO. 2 WINTER 1995 131Strategic Issues in Product RecoN<<ry Management
\par
First, many companies have problems acquiring accurate information on
\par
PRM issues in their business chain. This is not surprising, as many manufaaur-
\par
ers did not consider PRM to be a (core) business activity. Required data are often
\par
scattered throughout the company or the business chain, or are not available
\par
at all. Nevertheless, companies have been able to acquire essential information
\par
by collaborating with suppliers, repair centers, reprocessors, and waste man-
\par
agement companies. Basic information requirements deal with product charac-
\par
teristics, supply of used products, demand for reprocessed products, and the
\par
matching of demand and supply. Acquisition and processing of this information
\par
will require the development of new (company-wide or even business-wide)
\par
information systems.
\par
Second, there are a number of different product recovery options. These
\par
options are interrelated as demonstrated in the CopyMagic case study, where
\par
the company employed repair, cannibalization, recycling, and remanufacturing.
\par
BMW's PRM activities include remanufacturing and recycling. Selection of PRM
\par
options depends on technical feasibility of the PRM process, availability of suit-
\par
able used products, demand for reprocessed products, legislation, and available
\par
know-how in the company. Available know-how is one of the determinants in
\par
the PRM "make-or-buy" decision. An adequate solution could be to perform
\par
some PRM activities in-house and outsource the specialized PRM activities in
\par
which the company has little experience. Products and materials may go
\par
through a sequence of different phases in their life.
\par
Third, pro-active companies set specific reuse targets for their PRM opera-
\par
tions. BMW aims to reuse more than 80\% of all plastics. IBM UK aims to reach
\par
its recycling target of 95\% by 1995. The setting of measurable objectives is a
\par
necessary condition in establishing effective PRM systems.
\par
Fourth, in most cases PRM requires product redesign. Some general
\par
design for recycling and design for remanufacturing principles were discussed
\par
in the case study and the BMW example. These principles include design for
\par
disassembly, standardization of materials and components, coding of materials
\par
and components, recycled content, modular design, and the selection of durable
\par
and reusable materials. A by-product of design for PRM can be improved repair-
\par
ability. The benefits of redesigned produas will largely occur after the first
\par
redesigned products have been taken back.
\par
Fifth, PRM generally involves more cooperation for manufaaurers
\par
with other organizations in the business chain. Members of this business chain
\par
include repair centers, reprocessors, and waste management companies. Cooper-
\par
ation opportunities arise in the areas of exchange of information, involvement
\par
in product redesign, and joint PRM operations. The number of suppliers is likely
\par
to be reduced after product redesign has taken place due to the decrease in
\par
the number of different materials and components. Manufacturers active in
\par
remanufacturing have to deal with the supplier quality paradox: suppliers that
\par
make the highest quality components may lose the most business since their
\par
132 CALIFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37. NO. 2 WINTER 1995Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
components can be reused more often. The effect on\textbf{ the} components' prices is
\par
not always clear.
\par
Sixth, PRM issues could offer interesting cooperation opportunities
\par
between companies that operate in the same market. In the Netherlands,\textbf{ IBM}
\par
and DEC have set up a joint program, in collaboration with an equipment
\par
reprocessor, to recycle used personal computers. Other opportunities exist in the
\par
area of research. On June 8, 1992, General Motors, Ford, and Chrysler launched
\par
the United States Council for Automotive Research (USCAR) to coordinate their
\par
growing list of joint R\&D projects. Joint projects include recycling research."
\par
Additionally, companies can cooperate to establish materials coding standards.
\par
In-mold labeling of auto parts using standard coding is expected to become uni-
\par
versal by the late 1990s, and to dominate vehicles entering the retired-car
\par
stream a decade later."
\par
Seventh, the ability of companies to successfully integrate PRM in existing
\par
systems depends on their ability to
\par
{$\bullet$} acquire information,
\par
{$\bullet$} cooperate with other companies,
\par
{$\bullet$} redesign products and processes if necessary,
\par
{$\bullet$} accurately predict and control supply of used products,
\par
{$\bullet$} generate demand for reprocessed products, and
\par
{$\bullet$} control the PRM "production" process, e.g., ensure that remanufactured
\par
products are indeed as good as new.
\par
Companies that lease their products are generally in a more favorable
\par
position than companies that only sell products. Lease companies usually have
\par
more information on the quality and return of used products. We believe that
\par
companies with a good quality-image have a better chance of success. Convinc-
\par
ing the customer that reprocessed products fulfill all their quality requirements
\par
seems to be all-important.
\par
Eighth, PRM can have large influences on production, operations, and
\par
logistic management. Strategic changes may be required to deal with PRM.
\par
The case study illustrated the changes in network structure as the company
\par
progressed with PRM. Companies could be forced to acquire new skills, e.g.,
\par
to perform disassembly and repair operations, or to engage in new partnerships.
\par
New information systems must be established to monitor and control PRM activ-
\par
ities. Existing accounting systems may well be inadequate for PRM purposes.
\par
Transportation planning must take into account both forward and return flows.
\par
Warehouses must be designed to deal with two-way movements of products.
\par
Production control systems for remanufacturing must be able to deal with fun-
\par
damental uncertainties in quantities, timing, and quality of used products.
\par
Inventory control systems must be adapted. Employees must be con-
\par
vinced of the significance of PRM to the company and be rewarded accordingly.
\par
Although some research has been conducted in certain areas, specifically in
\par
CALIFORNIA MANAGEMENT REVIEW VOL 37. NO, 2 WINTER 1995 133Strategic Issues in Product Recovery Management
\par
models for repairable items, large research opportunities still exist. Current
\par
research by the present authors focuses on the design and analysis of produaion
\par
and inventory control systems, network design, and information systems.\textasciicircum '
\par
Should manufacturers also consider PRM when there are no legislative or
\par
regulatory changes that force them to? If so, which factors determine the success
\par
of PRM? We have identified several products and markets where PRM is being
\par
performed that are not subject to legislative or regulatory changes. For instance,
\par
remanufacturing is already being executed on a relatively large scale for: military
\par
products and components \textbraceleft e.g., aircraft and aircraft engines); commercial trans-
\par
portation-related products and components (e.g., locomotives and automotive
\par
engines); commercial heavy equipment (e.g., machine tools); and other com-
\par
mercial applications (e.g., medical diagnostic equipment and computers). It is
\par
remarkable that most remanufacturing operations are currently being performed
\par
by independent remanufacturers. The share of "original equipment manufactur-
\par
ers" in remanufacturing is relatively small.
\par
On-going research will hopefully provide us with a better insight in\textbf{ the}
\par
determinants of the economic attraaiveness of PRM for different products and
\par
industries.
