Abstract
\par
Various aspects of the role of uncertainty in greenhouse gas emission reduction policy are analyzed with the integrated assessment
\par
model FUND. FUND couples simple models of economy, climate, climate impacts, and emission abatement. Probability distribution
\par
functions are assumed for all major parameters in the model. Monte Carlo analyses are used to study the e!ects of parametric
\par
uncertainties. Uncertainties are found to be large and grow over time. Uncertainties about climate change impacts are more serious
\par
than uncertainties about emission reduction costs, so that welfare-maximizing policies are stricter under uncertainty than under
\par
certainty. This is more pronounced without than with international cooperation. Whether or not countries cooperate with one
\par
another is more important than whether or not uncertainty is considered. Meeting exogenously de"ned emission targets may be more
\par
or less di\$cult under uncertainty than under certainty, depending on the asymmetry in the uncertainty and the central estimate of
\par
interest. The major uncertainty in meeting emissions targets in each of a range of possible future is the timing of starting (serious)
\par
reduction policies. In a scenario aiming at a stable CO concentration of 550 ppm, the start date varies 20 years for Annex I countries,
\par
and much longer for non-Annex countries. Atmospheric stabilization at 550 ppm does not avoid serious risks with regard to climate
\par
change impacts. At the long term, it is possible to avoid such risks but only through very strict emission control at high economic
\par
costs. 1999 Elsevier Science Ltd. All rights reserved.
\par
Keywords: Climate change; Uncertainty; Cost-bene"t analysis; Tolerable windows
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Uncertainty abounds in climate change. Part of it
\par
relates to imperfect understanding of the mechanisms at
\par
work. Another part relates to the stochastic nature of
\par
important systems. A third part relates to the long lead-
\par
times of cause and e!ect. In time, we may be able to
\par
tackle, to some extent, the "rst source of uncertainty, but
\par
that time may well be a far future. We have to learn to
\par
live with the other two sources of uncertainty. This paper
\par
attempts to further that learning process.
\par
There are various ways of approaching climate change
\par
uncertainties, each with its merits. Below, I take four
\par
alternative approaches. These have been taken before,
\par
but never with one single model or, arguably, as rigorous
\par
as I do here. I leave other approaches aside. Stochasticity
\par
is omitted because it relates to variability at various
\par
time-scales, most of which are di!erent than the model's
\par
time-scale. The economic module, for instance, is
\par
a growth model, suitable for analyzing tendencies but not
\par
for recessions or adjustment processes. Dowlatabadi
\par
(1997) and Lempert et al. (1996) analyze stochasticity and
\par
adaptation. Learning is omitted partly because it is a very
\par
complex issue worthy of separate treatment (cf. Kolstad,
\par
1994,1996; Nordhaus and Popp, 1997; Peck and Teis-
\par
berg, 1993; Tol, 1998; Ulph and Maddison, 1997; Ulph
\par
and Ulph, 1996) and partly because its relevance for
\par
current decision-makers is not clear to me. It is uncertain
\par
what future decision-makers will learn or how they will
\par
react to it, so seen from the present the uncertainty is
\par
unresolved. Other approaches, such as fuzzy logic (Leim-
\par
bach, 1996,1998), cultural theory (Van Asselt et al., 1996),
\par
grey programming (Bass et al., 1997), ambiguity (Eismont
\par
and Welsch, 1996; Welsch, 1995) Dempster\textbraceright Shafer theory
\par
(Luo and Caselton, 1997), are omitted because these
\par
deviate too much from the Monte Carlo simulation
\par
techniques used here. The technique of representative
