emission reduction trajectories, comparing cooperative
\par
and non-cooperative policies under certainty and uncer-
\par
tainty. Section 5 looks at\&safe' policies, for various de"ni-
\par
tions of safety. Emission targets may be agreed upon, and
\par
these can be met assuming under certainty, in expecta-
\par
tion, or with a certain chance. Alternatively, one may
\par
seek a trajectory which avoids a\&high' probability of
\par
\&large' climate change impact, or\&high' emission reduc-
\par
tion costs. Section 5 concludes.
\par
2. The model
\par
Essentially, FUND consists of a set of exogenous scen-
\par
arios and endogenous perturbations, speci"ed for nine
\par
major world-regions, namely OECD-America, OECD-
\par
Europe, OECD-Paci"c, Central and Eastern Europe and
\par
the former Soviet Union, Middle East, Latin America,
\par
South and Southeast Asia, Centrally Planned Asia, and
\par
Africa.
\par
The model runs from 1950 to 2200, in time steps of
\par
a year. The prime reason for extending the simulation
\par
period into the past is the necessity to initialize the
\par
climate change impact module. In FUND, climate im-
\par
pacts are assumed to depend on the impact of the year
\par
before, to re\#ect the process of adjustment to climate
\par
change. Without a proper initialization, climate impacts
\par
are misrepresented in the "rst decades. Scenarios for the
\par
period 1950\textbraceright 1990 are based on the IMAGE 100-year
\par
database (Batjes and Goldewijk, 1994). Scenarios for the
\par
period 2010\textbraceright 2100 are based on the EMF Standardised
\par
Scenario, which lies somewhere in between IS92a and
\par
IS92f (Leggett et al., 1992). Note that the original EMF
\par
scenario had to be adjusted to "t FUND's nine regions
\par
and yearly time-step. The period 1990\textbraceright 2010 is a linear
\par
interpolation between observation and the EMF Scen-
\par
ario. The period 2100\textbraceright 2200 is based on extrapolation.
\par
The scenarios concern economic growth, population
\par
growth, urban population, autonomous energy e\$ciency
\par
improvements, decarbonization of the energy use, and
\par
methane and nitrous oxide emissions.
\par
The scenarios of economic and population growth are
\par
perturbed by the impact of climate change. Population
\par
falls with climate change deaths, resulting from changes
\par
in heat stress, cold stress, malaria, and tropical cyclones.
\par
Heat and cold stress are assumed to a!ect only the
\par
elderly, non-reproductive population; heat stress only
\par
a!ects urban population. Population also changes with
\par
climate-induced migration between the regions.
\par
The endogenous parts of FUND consist of the atmo-
\par
spheric concentrations of carbon dioxide, methane and
\par
nitrous oxide, the global mean temperature, and the
\par
impact of climate change on coastal zones, agriculture,
\par
extreme weather, natural ecosystems and malaria.
\par
Methane and nitrous oxide are taken up in the atmo-
\par
sphere, and then geometrically depleted. The carbon
\par
cycle is a "ve-box model, with geometric depletion in four
\par
boxes and no depletion in the "fth. The models are
\par
calibrated to Schimel et al. (1996). Radiative forcing for
\par
carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide are based on
\par
Shine et al. (1990). The global mean temperature is gov-
\par
erned by a geometric build-up to its equilibrium (deter-
\par
mined by radiative forcing), with a lifetime of 50 years. In
\par
the base case, global mean temperature rises in equilib-
\par
rium by 2.53C for a doubling of carbon dioxide equiva-
\par
lents. Global mean sea level is also geometric, with its
\par
equilibrium determined by the temperature and a lifetime
\par
of 50 years. These lifetimes result from a calibration to
\par
the best guess temperature and sea level for the IS92a
\par
scenario of Kattenberg et al. (1996). FUND also calculates
\par
hurricane activity, winter precipitation, and winter storm
\par
activity because these feed into the damage module.
\par
These factors depend linearly on the global mean temper-
\par
ature.
\par
The climate impact module is fully described in Tol
\par
(1996). The damage module has two units of measure-
\par
ment: people and money. People can die (heat stress,
\par
malaria, tropical cyclones), not die (cold stress), or
\par
migrate. These e!ects, like all impacts, are monetized.
\par
Damage can be due to either the rate of change
\par
(benchmarked at 0.043C/yr) or the level of change
\par
(benchmarked at 2.53C). Benchmark estimates can be
\par
found in Table 1. Damage in the rate of temperature
\par
change slowly fades at a speed indicated in Table 2.
\par
Damage is calculated through a second-order poly-
\par
nomial in climatic change. Damage is distinguished be-
\par
tween tangible (market) and intangible (non-market)
\par
e!ects. Tangible damages a!ect investment and con-
\par
sumption; through investment, economic growth is
\par
a!ected; through consumption, welfare is a!ected.
\par
Intangible damages a!ect welfare. Relative vulner-
\par
ability to climate change changes with economic devel-
\par
opment in many ways. The importance of agriculture fall
\par
with per capita income growth, and so do malaria inci-
\par
dence and the inclination to migrate. Heat stress in-
\par
creases with urbanization. The valuation of impacts on
\par
non-marketed goods and services increases with per
\par
capita income.
\par
The costs of emission reduction are calibrated to the
\par
survey results of Hourcade et al. (1996) supplemented
\par
with results of Rose and Stevens (1993) for developing
\par
countries. For the poorer regions, only a small number of
\par
cost estimates is available, leading to unrealistic regionalR.S.J. Tol / Global Environmental Change 9 (1999) 221\textbraceright 232 223
