3. Business as usual
\par
Emissions are not reduced in the business as a usual
\par
scenario. Fig. 1 shows a selection of the results for this
\par
scenario. The results are for a Monte Carlo simulation
\par
with 1000 replications, that is, FUND is run 1000 times
\par
with parameters randomly drawn from the distributions
\par
of Table 4. The results (1000 drawings, 250 years, nine
\par
regions, etc.) are summarized for the world as a whole for
\par
the years 2000, 2025, 2050, 2075, and 2100. Displayed are
\par
the 5-, 50-, and 95-percentiles, that is, the median and the
\par
bounds of the 90\%-con"dence interval. Although an
\par
integrated model, FUND omits some feedback mecha-
\par
nisms so that, for extreme parameter combinations,
\par
unrealistic results emerge. Outliers can therefore not
\par
be trusted, and the output is summarized with the robust
\par
median and 90\%-con"dence interval.
\par
The population in 2100 is projected to lie somewhere
\par
between 8 and 14 billion people, with a central estimate
\par
of 10.5 billion. This is the conventional wisdom to which
\par
FUND is calibrated (Bulatao et al., 1990; see also Lutz
\par
et al., 1997). World income lies somewhere between 140
\par
and 400 trillion dollar, with a central estimate of 220
\par
trillion dollar. This integrates population growth and per
\par
capita economic growth, as well as feedbacks from
\par
climate impact. Industrial emissions of carbon dioxide
\par
rise to between 21 and 76 billion tonnes of carbon per
\par
year in 2100, with a central estimate of 35 billion tonnes.
\par
FUND is pessimistic about the chance of carbon emis-
\par
