4. Optimal policies
\par
Various decision rules may guide emission reduction
\par
policies. One such rule is to optimize social welfare. This
\par
is the long-term, macro-economic version of (project-
\par
oriented) cost\textbraceright bene"t analysis. The negative impacts of
\par
climate change and greenhouse gas emission reduction
\par
are traded o! such that their sum is minimized, or, in this
\par
case, net present welfare is maximized. To account for
\par
uncertainty, the expected value of welfare is used.
\par
There are two complications. Firstly, decisions are
\par
taken by successive policy makers. It is here assumed that
\par
policy makers control a period of ten years, starting in
\par
1990, and know exactly what their predecessors have
\par
done, and their successors will do (cf. Tol, 1998, for
\par
alternatives). Welfare is discounted to the start of the
\par
decade under control, and ranges from then till 2200.
\par
Secondly, decisions are taken by policy makers of di!er-
\par
ent countries or, in the case of FUND, nine world-regions.
\par
It is here assumed that policy makers either do not
\par
cooperate but maximize the welfare of their regions while
\par
being perfectly knowledgeable of other policy makers
\par
actions, or cooperate so as to maximize the sum of
\par
regional welfares.
\par
Fig. 2 displays optimal trajectories of the atmospheric
\par
concentration of carbon dioxide for the cooperative and
\par
non-cooperative scenarios. For comparison, the business
\par
as usual scenario, and the cooperative and non-
\par
cooperative optima under certainty are also displayed.
\par
Optimal policies are calculated under uncertainty, and
\par
then evaluated with all parameters "xed to their best
\par
guesses (coinciding with the modes of the probability
\par
distributions of Table 4). Emission control is stricter
\par
under uncertainty than under certainty. The uncertainty
\par
about the impact of climate change dominates the
\par
uncertainty about the costs of greenhouse gas emission
\par
reduction. The same conclusion is drawn by, inter alia,
\par
Nordhaus (1994a) and Tol (1995). See Hope (1994) for
\par
a contrary view. This e!ect is less pronounced in case of
\par
international cooperation, because the base level of emis-
\par
sion reduction under certainty is higher in this case,
\par
taking away part of the risks of climate change and
\par
adding to the risks of emission reduction.
\par
Fig. 3 displays the median and 90\%-con"dence inter-
\par
vals of the atmospheric concentration of carbon dioxide,
\par
world income, climate impacts, and direct reduction
\par
costs for cooperative reduction under uncertainty, and
\par
compares these to the business as usual scenario. Even
\par
the most stringent of the optimal scenarios has little e!ect
\par
on CO concentrations and hence on impact. On the
\par
other hand, the policy costs little, either directly or in its
\par
consequences on income.
\par
