\par
6. Conclusions
\par
Uncertainty is one of the most important aspects of
\par
climate change. The analysis of the business as usual
\par
scenario illustrates this with ever-widening con"dence
\par
intervals around the central estimates of key variables.
\par
Note that only parameter uncertainty is taken into
\par
account. Structural changes or omitted feedbacks are not
\par
considered. Uncertainty about the uncertainty is ignored,
\par
as is the possibility of multiple baselines. Parameter
\par
uncertainty, however, serves to illustrate a number
\par
of points.
\par
In a welfare optimizing framework, uncertainty is
\par
a reason to increase emission control, and relatively more
\par
so in case regions do not cooperate with each other.
\par
However, optimizing net present welfare under
\par
uncertainty does not imply atmospheric stabilization.
\par
International cooperation has greater in\#uence on opti-
\par
mal emission control than does uncertainty.
\par
In a setting aimed at stabilizing atmospheric concen-
\par
trations, uncertainty has mixed e!ects. Meeting emis-
\par
sions targets in expectation, or with a 50\% chance can
\par
lead to lower or higher reduction than in case the future
\par
is known with certainty. The answer depends on the
\par
symmetry of the uncertainty about emissions, and is
\par
complicated by the fact that the best guess and modalR.S.J. Tol / Global Environmental Change 9 (1999) 221\textbraceright 232 231
\par
emissions do not coincide in a non-linear model. Further
\par
research is needed.
\par
If emission targets are to be met in each possible future,
\par
then the main uncertainty Annex I countries face is when
\par
the time for sharp emission reduction will come. In the
\par
scenario of Wigley et al. (1996) aiming at a stabilization
\par
at 550 ppm, hefty emission control may start a decade
\par
earlier or later under uncertainty than under certainty.
\par
For non-Annex countries, the main uncertainty is when
\par
\textbraceright{} if ever\textbraceright{} emission reduction will be required. The
\par
di!erence can amount to a number of decades.
\par
Emission reduction aimed at stabilization of atmo-
\par
spheric carbon dioxide at a level of 550 ppm does not
\par
prevent substantial chances on severe climate change
\par
impacts. The model shows that median monetized
\par
impact in 2100 falls from 3 to 2\% of world income, but
\par
the 95-percentile remains high at 8\%. Driving emission
\par
reduction to an (arbitrary) maximum of 5\% per year,
\par
impacts will stay below 4\% with 95\% probability. The
\par
economic cost, however, is substantial.
