\par
Sequential climate decisions under uncertainty:
\par
An integrated framework
\par
L. James Valverde A., Jr.a, Henry D. Jacoby b and Gordon M. Kaufman b
\par
\emph{a Department of Operational Research, London School of Economics, London WC2A 2AE, UK}
\par
\emph{b Sloan School of Management, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA}
\par
Received 9 June 1998; revised 5 February 1999
\par
We develop an integrated framework for evaluating sequential greenhouse gas abatement policies under uncertainty. The analysis
\par
integrates information concerning the magnitude, timing, and impacts of climate change with data on the likely effectiveness and cost of
\par
possible response options. Reduced-scale representations of the global climate system, drawn from the MIT Integrated Global System
\par
Model, form the empirical basis of the analysis. The method is illustrated in application to emissions control policies of the form
\par
considered under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change.
\par
\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
Efforts to negotiate a response to the threat of climate
\par
change have spawned research on methods for characteriz-
\par
ing this difficult issue and for analyzing emissions control
\par
options. One focus of this work has been a class of ap-
\par
plications commonly referred to as \emph{integrated assessment}
\par
models, which seek to pull together the many features of
\par
the problem into a coherent, consistent framework.1 As
\par
pointed out by the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate
\par
Change (IPCC), analysts working on the issue face two
\par
main challenges [2]:
\par
{$\bullet$}Integrating and managing a large and diverse array of
\par
data and models from many researchers and disciplines.
\par
{$\bullet$}Improving the relevance of the models to policy needs,
\par
as well as the presentation of model results to policy
\par
makers.
\par
The IPCC further observes that ``...treatments of decision
\par
making under uncertainty... are at present poorly devel-
\par
oped in international environmental economics, and espe-
\par
cially in the climate change literature'' [2, p. 7].
\par
The analytical approach explored here is a step towards
\par
satisfying these objectives. We present a policy-oriented,
\par
integrated decision analysis framework that focuses on the
\par
fact that decisions about greenhouse gas control are not
\par
made once and for all; current policy choices will be recon-
\par
sidered in future years in light of new data and improved
\par
understanding of the problem. Few assessments take ex-
\par
plicit account of the \emph{sequential }character of climate deci-
\par
sions, sometimes because of lack of familiarity with the
\par
range of analytic techniques that can be applied, but more
\par
often because computational support for these methods has
\par
1 For insightful discussions of climate-related integrated assessment mod-
\par
eling, see, e.g., [4,16,23].
\par
 Baltzer Science Publishers BV
\par
-- until recently -- been primitive, and the calculations pro-
\par
hibitive in cost. Of course, policy makers recognize that
\par
decisions will be reconsidered over time, particularly for
\par
a long-run issue like climate change. Failure to deal with
\par
this aspect of policy choice diminishes the value of much
\par
of available analysis.
\par
In developing our framework, we build on previous ap-
\par
plications of decision-oriented models in the climate area.2
\par
Two main features characterize our approach, each occa-
\par
sioned by the peculiar complexity and scope of the climate
\par
issue. First, we represent uncertainty about the response of
\par
the climate system to human emissions in terms of variables
\par
used by climate scientists in their modeling and analysis
\par
efforts. This formulation extends previous efforts, most of
\par
which apply a single, highly summarized indicator of cli-
\par
matic effect or social impact.3 Stating uncertainty in terms
\par
of key climate model parameters analysis models provides
\par
a more informative picture of uncertainty in the climate ma-
\par
chine, and it facilitates the elicitation of probabilistic expert
\par
judgements about climate-related uncertain quantities and
\par
their likely resolution over time.
\par
Second, we estimate key empirical relations using a sin-
\par
gle, integrated set of models of the global climate system.
\par
Because of its computational requirements, a sequential de-
\par
cision analysis must be based on relatively simple math-
\par
ematical representations of underlying processes. In the
\par
analysis presented below, there are six such components:
\par
(i) the trajectory of greenhouse emissions; (ii) the effect
\par
of these emissions on atmospheric concentrations; (iii) the
\par
translation of concentrations to radiative forcing; (iv) the
\par
response of climate to this forcing; (v) the impacts of any
\par
change in climate; and (vi) the economic valuation of these
\par
changes. Models used by specialists in each of these do-
\par
mains are large and complex, and to integrate them into a
\par
2 See, e.g., [5,7,9,10,15].
\par
3 See, e.g., [7,10].88 \emph{L.J. Valverde A. et al. / Sequential climate decisions under uncertainty}
\par
framework useful for policy analysis they must be recast in
\par
simpler forms, what we call \emph{reduced-scale }representations
\par
of the climate system. Estimating reduced-scale model pa-
\par
rameters from a single, more-detailed framework makes it
\par
possible to document the basis for the analysis. Also, it
\par
allows testing of the adequacy of the simpler models, and
\par
it allows review and analysis of key conclusions in full
\par
simulations of the integrated system.
\par
The basis for our reduced-scale modeling and estimation
\par
procedure is the Integrated Global System Model (IGSM),
\par
developed by the MIT Joint Program on the Science and
\par
Policy of Global Change [19]. The IGSM is comprised of
\par
the following components: a 12-region, multi-sector analy-
\par
sis of greenhouse emissions and the costs of emissions re-
\par
striction, an analysis of natural emissions of methane and
\par
nitrous oxide, a multi-gas model of atmospheric chem-
\par
istry and greenhouse gas concentrations, a 2D model of
\par
atmospheric dynamics with a coupled model of ocean cir-
\par
culations, and a model of the effect of climate on terrestrial
\par
ecosystems. The model is designed to simulate the human--
\par
climate--environment system, given sets of input parame-
\par
ters, and hypotheses about emissions control policies. In
\par
the analysis below, all but the summary measure of cli-
\par
mate change effects are drawn from this framework. The
\par
variables used here to represent climate uncertainty are the
\par
same ones chosen for a sensitivity test of the IGSM, as
\par
reported by Prinn et al. [19].
\par
Because climate model components can be so compu-
\par
tationally intensive, the task of estimating smaller versions
\par
needs to be based on a small number of simulations of
\par
the larger system. To deal with such limitations, we apply
\par
an iterative estimation procedure. Simulations of climate
\par
policy using the IGSM yield sets of system outputs; the
\par
reduced-scale models are, in turn, calibrated to mimic the
\par
integrated system behaviour. The resulting nimble models
\par
are then used to explore wider domains of policy choice
\par
than can be easily done with the computationally-expensive
\par
IGSM. This exploration of policy choice can then guide fu-
\par
ture decisions concerning which policies should be explored
\par
using the IGSM, and perhaps subsequent re-estimation of
\par
the reduced-scale versions.
\par
Figure 1 illustrates this interplay among models of dif-
\par
ferent scale. Starting at the lower left of the figure, an
\par
arrow represents the results of system experiments (e.g.,
\par
GHG emission scenario studies and sensitivity analysis)
\par
conducted on the IGSM. These results are used to cal-
\par
ibrate the reduced-scale models (shown in the right-hand
\par
box), inform the definition of policies to be studied, guide
\par
subsequent revisions of model structure, and estimate key
\par
model parameters. Proceeding around the top of the figure,
\par
the reduced-scale models can also be used to inform subse-
\par
quent analyses of climate policy, using the limited number
\par
of runs that are feasible within the larger integrated frame-
\par
work. The reduced-scale results also indicate which un-
\par
certainties are important to policy choice. This information
\par
can, in turn, lead to a judicious selection of cases to run us-
\par
ing the more computationally expensive parts of the IGSM.
\par
And so the analysis proceeds around the diagram, so long as
\par
the expected improvement from additional rounds is worth
\par
the cost of analysis. In the results below, we show the first
\par
pass of such a procedure, and we indicate how the results --
\par
including sensitivity analysis of model solutions -- provide
\par
inputs to the design of subsequent rounds of improvement.
\par
Our presentation is divided into three parts. Section 2 in-
\par
troduces the concepts that underlie the sequential decision
\par
modeling approach. Section 3 then summarizes the pro-
\par
cedure by which reduced-scale system response functions
\par
are drawn from the MIT IGSM, and explores the proper-
\par
ties of the sequential decision model. Section 4 presents
\par
a sensitivity test of key variables treated as certain in this
\par
application. We conclude, in section 5, with a discussion
\par
of the policy relevance of our results and the directions
\par
they suggest for follow-on efforts to assist climate change
\par
decision making.
\par
\textbf{2. Sequential climate change decision making}
\par
At any particular point in time, the preferred course of
\par
greenhouse gas abatement action will depend, in part, upon
\par
the choices available at subsequent decision points, and on
\par
what might be learned along the way. Also, both mid- and
\par
long-term abatement choices may depend on the observed
\par
outcomes of short-term policy actions. Such a decision con-
\par
text, where decisions and information unfold sequentially,
\par
is a dynamic programming problem, and various algorithms
\par
exist for determining the optimal policy associated with this
\par
type of problem formulation.
\par
Such problems are most commonly formulated as deci-
\par
sion trees, and are solved by backward induction. Here we
\par
apply an alternative formulation -- whose solution is also
\par
grounded in dynamic programming -- which uses \emph{temporal}
\par
\emph{influence diagrams }(TIDs).4 The influence diagram is both
\par
visually efficient and computationally powerful, and is a
\par
particularly useful tool when a decision tree representation
\par
may have so many nodes and branches that it is easy to
\par
get lost in the forest. To provide background for the dis-
\par
cussion below, we begin with a summary of the notation
\par
and concepts that underlie this way of laying out a decision
\par
problem under uncertainty.
\par
Influence diagrams are graphical networks designed to
\par
represent the important qualitative features of key elements
\par
of a decision problem. The flow of information, and its
\par
functional relation to choices of action, can be displayed to
\par
any desired level of aggregation. Most real-world decision
\par
problems are formulated in a domain consisting of deci-
\par
sion variables D= \textbraceleft D1, ..., Dm\textbraceright{} and uncertain quantities
\par
U= \textbraceleft X1, ..., Xn\textbraceright; the ID provides a convenient means
\par
for representing the relationships among these components.
\par
