\par
\textbf{3. A sequential decision model}
\par
\emph{3.1. Influence diagram representation}
\par
Our sequential model formulation begins with the
\par
TID shown in figure 5. We provide a brief summary of
\par
its structure before describing it in detail. The diagram
\par
depicts a two period model, covering the years 2000--2010
\par
and 2010--2050, with a no-forgetting arc to indicate the time
\par
ordering of the two abatement decisions. Decisions in each
\par
period lead to costs of emissions control in each period and
\par
to changes in global-mean surface temperature. Both abate-
\par
ment costs and temperature change are represented as value
\par
nodes. Temperature changes lead to impacts (a value node),
\par
which, when combined with control costs, yields the aggre-
\par
gate social loss in each period (also a value node). Note the
\par
parallel structure of decision and value nodes in periods 1
\par
and 2, with the graph rotated top-to-bottom in period 2, so
\par
as to highlight the uncertainties that apply to both periods
\par
of the model.
\par
In this formulation, global climate change is represented
\par
as a dynamic process that is functionally dependent on
\par
(i) the abatement decision in each period, and (ii) chance
\par
variables representing the uncertainty characterizing two
\par
important climate-related parameters: \emph{climate sensitivity}
\par
and \emph{ocean diffusivity}. Stated in this way, key uncertain-
\par
ties in the climate system are represented in terms that are
\par
familiar to scientists working in the field. Also, the im-
\par
pacts of climatic change are subject to uncertainty. In the
\par
example presented here, however, the costs of emissions
\par
abatement are treated as certain.7 The directed arcs from
\par
the uncertain quantities Climate Sensitivity and Ocean Dif-
\par
fusivity to Abatement Decision (2), but not to Abatement
\par
Decision (1), show that uncertainty concerning the response
\par
of the climate system is assumed to be resolved at the end
\par
of period 1, i.e., \emph{before }the period 2 decision is made. Sim-
\par
ilarly, we assume that uncertainty concerning the parame-
\par
terization of the damage function associated with period 1
\par
is resolved at the end of that period.
\par
One main advantage in framing the problem in TID
\par
form, we believe, is ease of communication (with experts
\par
and non-experts, alike). The principal alternative, deci-
\par
sion trees, can be very cumbersome for problems of this
