A conspicuous feature of the analysis presented above is
\par
the invariance of policy a11 as an optimal course of action
\par
in period 1, across a plausible range of cost values. This
\par
condition, however, does not hold for the bottom-most bar
\par
shown in figure 11. For this cost variable, although tra-
\par
versing the range 0.158--0.48\% has little influence on the
\par
expected value of the model, the procedure induces a shift
\par
in both the optimal policy in period 1 (from a11 to a12),
\par
and the probabilities associated with the period 2 options.
\par
In this way, uncertainty concerning the cost estimate for
\par
policy a12 gives rise to a situation where action in period 1
\par
may or may not optimal, depending on whether stabilizing
\par
emissions in the first period of the model is cheaper than the
\par
nominal baseline value assumed here. Subsequent analyses
\par
would treat the abatement costs shown in figure 11 as ex-
\par
plicit uncertainties in the decision model, i.e., they would
\par
be treated as stochastic variables. Alternatively, we can use
\par
the results of the sensitivity analysis as a guide to using the
\par
MIT IGSM to construct an intermediate policy, a+
\par
11, that is
\par
characterized by a level of stringency that lies somewhere
\par
between a11 and a12.
\par
Clearly, in the sample climate policies studied here, the
\par
overall analysis task is simplified by treating emissions con-
\par
trol costs as known with certainty. These sensitivity cal-
\par
culations illuminate the question whether the decisions that
\par
are the focus of the exercise are likely to change if cost un-
\par
certainty is introduced in subsequent rounds of the analysis.
\par
Of course, with a redefinition of the choice set to gain better
\par
discrimination at lower levels of control, cost uncertainty is
\par
likely to have a greater impact on the policy prescriptions
\par
of the model. In this way, sensitivity analysis plays an im-
\par
portant role not just in interpreting results, but also in the
\par
process formulation illustrated in figure 1.
\par
\textbf{5. Conclusions}
\par
The analytical framework presented here offers a nim-
\par
ble, computationally efficient vehicle for evaluating a broad
\par
range of possible GHG abatement policies under uncer-
\par
tainty. The sequential model formulation integrates a
\par
reduced-scale approach to climate modeling and predic-
\par
tion, with the analysis linked to a specific larger modeling
\par
framework, in this case the MIT Integrated Global System
\par
Model. The proposed approach facilitates the process of
\par
expert elicitation, which is needed in analyses of this kind.
\par
It also supports a clear, iterative approach to estimation and
\par
numerical specification of reduced-scale models, allowing
\par
efficient use of large-model computer runs, and it provides
\par
a clear basis for checking and documenting results. This
\par
approach of fitting to a specific larger system is, of course,
\par
generalizable, and can be applied to other integrated assess-
\par
ment efforts. Indeed, such analysis should be done more
\par
generally, because the results would reveal much about the
\par
differences among models values imposed in the type of
\par
decision context considered here.
\par
Further, several important aspects of sequential deci-
\par
sion problems, which may difficult to communicate other-
\par
wise, are made more transparent using the approach applied
\par
here. As with previous applications to climate policy over
\par
time, the analysis shows the ``best'' choice today, given the
\par
choices considered, and the underlying models. Further-
\par
more, it also makes clear that, even with a recommendation
\par
for today's choice, the preferred future choice (in period 2,
\par
in the example presented here) can only be computed with
\par
some probability. Since the optimality of the current choice
\par
only holds if it can be said \emph{with certainty }that the decision
\par
that ultimately becomes optimal in the later period will ac-\emph{L.J. Valverde A. et al. / Sequential climate decisions under uncertainty }101
\par
tually be taken, the notion of a probabilistic climate policy
\par
(table 4) is important information in judging whether this
\par
key assumption is supportable.
\par
As with other methods, our integrated framework sup-
\par
ports analysis of the sensitivity of the expected value of
\par
the sequential choice problem, and the specific decisions
\par
in each period, to uncertainties that are not treated explic-
\par
itly in the analysis (e.g., abatement costs, in the example
\par
used here). These sensitivity studies are an important com-
\par
ponent of the sequential estimation procedure described in
\par
section 1, for they can guide the design of subsequent runs
\par
of the parent framework to focus on regions of variables
\par
where there is the greatest influence on decisions. Without
\par
the iterative procedure shown in figure 1, the choice of un-
\par
derlying large-system runs is largely a matter of guesswork.
\par
Many lines of fruitful work lead from the formulation
\par
and example policies presented here. Important additional
\par
insights can be gained from formulations that take explicit
\par
account of uncertainty in emissions forecasts and abatement
\par
costs. More challenging extensions include the ability to
\par
consider less-than-complete resolution of key uncertainties,
\par
and less-than-complete certainty that the future choices im-
\par
plied by the optimal solution can and will be carried out.
\par
Even without these improvements, however, this form of
\par
analysis can help clarify the choices faced in the climate
\par
area and contribute to more coherent policy discussions.
