\par
Bayesian decision analysis for environmental
\par
and resource management
\par
Olli Varis
\par
Helsinki University of Technology, Laboratory of Water Resources, P.O. Box 5300, Helsinki, FIN-02015 HUT, Finland
\par
(Received 20 August 1996; accepted 15 November 1996)
\par
Abstract
\par
During the last two decades, much of the theoretical and practical advances in Bayesian decision analysis have been closely linked
\par
to the adaptation of emerging new computational - usually Artificial Intelligence - techniques and to progress in computer software,
\par
respectively. This paper documents and discusses experience on the use of two recent network model approaches, influence diagrams
\par
and belief networks, and relates those approaches to decision trees. They both allow probabilistic, Bayesian studies with classical
\par
decision analytic concepts such as risk attitude analysis, value of information and control, multi-attribute analysis, and various
\par
structural analyses. The theory of influence diagrams dates back to the early 1980s, and a variety of commercial software are on
\par
market. Belief network is a more recent concept that is under process of finding its way to applications. Illustration on environmental
\par
and resource management is provided with examples on freshwater and fisheries studies. \copyright{} 1997 Elsevier Science Ltd
\par
Keywords: Bayesian inference; belief networks; decision analysis; influence diagrams; risk; uncertainty; value of information
\par
