\par
1. I n t r o d u c t i o n
\par
Uncertainty is among the most discussed topics in
\par
resource and environmental management. Practical
\par
interest in probabilistic assessment, risk analysis, and
\par
related issues and techniques has grown rapidly in the
\par
recent years (WCED, 1987; De Jongh, 1988; USEPA,
\par
1989; ADB, 1990). Probabilistic and risk analysis types
\par
of approach are increasingly accepted in practical
\par
assessment work by international organizations and by
\par
national authorities in many countries. For instance, the
\par
Government of Finland has included the precautionary
\par
approach in its programme since 1995.
\par
Modern decision theory, together with various
\par
recently developed computational techniques for pro-
\par
cessing uncertain information, provide a wide base for
\par
novel, potential approaches to applications in the field.
\par
Presently, these opportunities are far from being prop-
\par
erly known and fully utilized.
\par
The concept of uncertainty has several facets in this
\par
context. From a decision-theoretic view, uncertainty
\par
can be grouped in three clusters (Howard, 1968; Varis
\par
et al., 1994):
\par
{$\bullet$} Acquisition, elicitation, presentation, and propagation
\par
of information available.
\par
{$\bullet$} Preferences and objectives of a given problem.
\par
{$\bullet$} Structural issues.
\par
177178 O. Varis/Bayesian decision analysis
\par
Pearl (1988) clusters the computational techniques
\par
used into two groups: logic-based approaches
\par
(monotonous logic and its applications; rule-based sys-
\par
tems, etc.) and probabilistic approaches (Bayes, Dem-
\par
pster-Shafer, fuzzy set theory, etc.). In this study, the
\par
focus is on Bayesian calculus due to its many favorable
\par
properties (Bunn, 1984; Morgan and Henrion, 1990;
\par
Howson and Urbach, 1991).
\par
This paper makes first an overview to the history
\par
of Bayesian decision analysis, with particular emphasis
\par
on recent evolution in computational methodology. A
\par
short review on application software is included, with
\par
the spreadsheet toolkit F. C. BeNe, developed by the
\par
author. Thereafter, three basic approaches to Bayesian
\par
decision analysis are presented: decision trees, influence
\par
diagrams, and belief networks. The rest of the paper
\par
concentrates on the experience of the author in apply-
\par
ing decision analysis in various resource management
\par
applications.
\par
The focal role has been taken by computational
\par
schemes for local updating of probabilistic information
\par
in conditioned networks. The problem has been
\par
approached both from the direction of the Dempster-
\par
Shafer Theory of Evidence (e.g. Gordon and Shortliffe,
\par
1985; Shenoy and Shafer, 1986), and from Bayesian
\par
statistics (e.g. Pearl, 1986a, b). Yet, in the resulted
\par
methodologies - known as belief networks, causal
\par
networks, Bayesian nets, qualitative Markov networks,
\par
or constraint networks - this division is difficult or
\par
impossible to distinguish.
\par
Characteristic of these techniques is the principle
\par
of networking nodes representing conditional, locally
\par
updated probabilities. The local-updating principle
\par
allows construction of large and densely coupled net-
\par
works without excessive growth in computation. Fur-
\par
thermore, networks can easily be constructed to operate
\par
interactively and on-line. As is usual in such tech-
\par
niques, the entire model - the hypothesis space - is
\par
subjected to Bayesian analysis, not only the parameter
\par
space. In recent years, they have spread quickly to
\par
many application areas, including fault diagnosis,
\par
reliability theory, medicine, pattern recognition, and
\par
decision analysis (e.g. Horwitz et al., 1988; Pearl,
\par
1988; Oliver and Smith, 1990; Szolovits and Pauker,
\par
1993; Russell and Norvig, 1995).
\par
According to Bobrow (1993), a particularly success-
\par
ful networking technique has been the belief network
\par
approach by Pearl (1986a, 1988). A review by Szolov-
\par
its and Pauker (1993) stated that '... Pearl's formulation
\par
has had a revolutionary impact on much o f AI'. Pearl
\par
pays particular emphasis on the simultaneous analysis
\par
of causal (expert) and evidential (empirical) infor-
\par
mation, an aspect which makes the approach and the
\par
philosophy attractive to resource and environmental
\par
studies.
\par
2. Bayesian decision analysis
\par
Within environmental and resource management, the
\par
applications of Bayesian analysis have been largely
\par
dominated by classical Bayesian inference (i.e. para-
\par
meter estimation), in which the Bayesian analysis is
\par
restricted to the parameter space. In decision theory,
\par
the idea of considering the entire model as a construct
\par
subject to uncertainty and subjectivity stem from the
\par
game theory of the 1930s and '40s (Shafer, 1990). At
\par
that time it became evident that costs and constraints
\par
must be explicit, due to improved estimates, used
\par
confidence interval requirements or statistical tests
\par
chosen (Shafer, 1990). Games evolved into sequential
\par
games against uncontrolled 'nature', and abstractions
\par
such as decision trees were developed. Bayesian
\par
decision theory gained increasing notice and emphasis
\par
(Wald, 1950). The basic theory was developed into
\par
more applicable level towards the late 1960s (Howard,
\par
1968; North, 1968; Raiffa, 1968).
\par
Important topics in contemporary decision analysis
\par
include risk attitude analysis (Pratt, 1964), value of
\par
information analysis (Merkhofer, 1977), various multi-
\par
attribute and multi-criteria issues (Keeney and Raiffa,
\par
1976), a diversity of behavioral and cognitive issues
\par
(Kahnemann et al., 1973; von Winterfeldt and
\par
Edwards, 1986), and computational techniques. I will
\par
concentrate on the last one of them, due to their recent
\par
progress and impacts within the field.
\par
Conventionally, one of the bottlenecks to practical
\par
applications of Bayesian approaches has been the high
\par
amount of computation required. Powerful numerical
\par
techniques have not been available until mid-1980s.
\par
According to Shafer and Pearl (1990), the recent devel-
\par
opments in decision analysis have been linked with
\par
advances in related computational mathematics. Arti-
\par
ficial Intelligence (AI) has had a rapidly growing
\par
impact within the last ten years.
\par
3. Dominant approaches
\par
In this section, three practical approaches to Baye-
\par
sian decision analysis; decision trees, influence dia-
\par
grams, and belief networks, are briefly reviewed. A
\par
conceptual introduction on each is given, with an
\par
illustrative example.
\par
3.1. Decision trees
\par
In historical perspective, the roots of decision trees
\par
are even older than those of modern Bayesian decision
\par
theory. They can be seen as a special case of von
\par
Neumann's and Morgenstern's (1944) (von Neumann
\par
and Morgenstern, 1944) game tree. The basic idea
\par
is that the events, both controllable (decision) and
\par
uncontrollable (chance) ones, are set up in procedural
\par
order. Each set of outcomes from a node, be they
\par
decision alternatives or possible outcomes of a chance,
\par
define a new branch in the tree. The following exampleO. Varis/Bayesian decision analysis 179
\par
should illustrate the formulation and evaluation of
\par
decision trees.
\par
The problem here is to define whether a hypothetical
\par
project requires a detailed or specific impact assess-
\par
ment. The results of a screening study are used to
\par
make this decision. Start from a chance node for
\par
Environmental Impacts (P(Impacts Significant) = 0.3,
\par
P(Impacts Specific) = 0.7). The next item is the
