3.2. Influence diagrams
\par
Influence diagrams have gained increasing popularity
\par
within decision analysis. They have been originally
\par
extended from decision trees, with the leading motiv-
\par
ation in developing a more compact and compu-
\par
tationally more efficient approach to decision analysis.
\par
The graph concept is efficient in both formulating and
\par
structuring problems, and in evaluation of desired parts
\par
or the whole of the model. They also share many of
\par
the features of belief networks, and often they are
\par
considered as their special case.
\par
An influence diagram is an acyclic Bayesian network
\par
of nodes connected with one-directional links (arcs).
\par
The nodes represent probabilistic variables (denoted
\par
as ovals), deterministic variables (including objective
\par
functions, rounded rectangles), and decisions
\par
(rectangles). Like a decision tree, the diagram describes
\par
causality or the flow of information and probabilistic
\par
dependencies in a system. Influence diagrams can be
