\par
evaluated in a couple of ways: The most used
\par
approaches include the node reduction technique by
\par
Shachter (1986), the approach by Lauritzen and Spieg-
\par
elhalter (1988), and the decision tree-based approach
\par
by ADA (1992).
\par
The use of influence diagrams is illustrated in some
\par
of the examples below, as well as with the hypothetical
\par
model used to illustrate decision trees. For further
\par
details, see, e.g. Howard and Matheson (1983),
\par
Shachter (1986), Oliver and Smith (1990) and Cle-
\par
men (1996).
\par
The influence diagram model for the example prob-
\par
lem has four nodes (Fig. 1 a). The uncontrollable prior -
\par
the Environmental Impacts, or more generally, the state
\par
of the nature - is a quantity which is not yet known
\par
accurately. The contents of this node as well as those
\par
of the other nodes are as in Section 3.1. The Screening
\par
node contains a conditional probability distribution,
\par
conditioned by the state of the nature. The screening
\par
results are known at the moment of decision on
\par
Detailed Assessment. The Satisfaction to the whole
\par
exercise depends on the state of the nature and on the
\par
decision. This model gives exactly the same result as
\par
the decision tree model, the only difference being in
\par
the notation used.
\par
The influence diagram notation provides the analyst
\par
with some attractive and useful properties. One of
\par
them is the straight way to perform the value of
\par
information analysis. It might be of interest to know
\par
the maximum additional amount of money that one
\par
should spend on a screening analysis. Assume now
\par
that at the moment of decision making the state of the
\par
nature is exactly known. In the influence diagram (Fig.
\par
ld), this would be denoted by drawing a new, direct
\par
link from Environmental Impacts to Detailed Assess-
\par
ment. Evaluation of the model without this new link
\par
yields the expected value of objective function to be
\par
73.5, and with the new link, it becomes 91 (Fig. le).
\par
This difference, 17.5 units, is the expected value of
\par
perfect information of the screening exercise. This is
\par
the maximum amount one should invest on additional
\par
screening. Similarly, one can study the value of imper-
\par
fect information and the value of imperfect and perfect
\par
control (cf Clemen, 1991; Lapin, 1991).
\par
3.3. Belief networks
\par
The background and the concept of belief networks
\par
was summarized in Section 2. Among the many exist-
\par
ing variations of the idea, I will concentrate on the
\par
generalized belief network approach by Varis (1994,
\par
1995a) which has been extended from Pearl's (1988)
\par
(Pearl, 1988) methodology.
\par
The network has n nodes that can arbitrarily be
\par
interconnected. The prior probabilities assigned to the
\par
outcomes are updated with the information linked from
\par
other parts of the network, yielding the posterior prob-
\par
ability distribution. The posteriors Bei i are calculated
\par
using two independent tree messages (denoted usually
\par
as 7r and A). The updated belief is obtained as the
\par
product of them and the prior.
\par
Some nodes may be understood as controllable,
\par
decision nodes. One or several nodes can act as a
\par
criterion or constraint for decision making, and consti-
\par
tute one or more objective functions.
\par
A link transfers information from a node to another
\par
node. Links are in two layers. An uncertainty link is
\par
defined as the link matrix Mi\textasciitilde{} between two nodes i
\par
and j. The link matrix can be direction specific:
\par
Mo@Mjl i. An outcome link presents a relation between
\par
outcomes yi and yj of i and j. Because the approach
\par
requires that each outcome has one value, the propa-
\par
gation of outcome values is unidirectional, and a func-
\par
tional relationship exists, yj = f ( y i ) . Technically, the
\par
approach divides the model into two layers in com-
\par
munication with one another. These deterministic equa-
\par
tions of the model constitute the outcome layer, and
\par
the belief network constitutes the probabilistic layer.
\par
Varis (1995a) developed the uncertainty balance
\par
approach to allow various optimization tasks using
\par
generalized belief networks. The key proposition is
\par
that the prior and posterior probability distributions
\par
of target variables (observations, management targets,
\par
constraints, etc.) should become equal. This implies
\par
that the joint distributions of the external information
\par
(prior) should be equal to their modeled distributions
\par
(posterior) and assures the proper utilization of the
\par
prior information. This occurs by finding optimal
\par
values for control variables (including parameters), and
\par
for link strengths by iteration (see also Varis and
\par
Kuikka, 1997a).
\par
Generalized belief networks can include models from
\par
many methodological families that have conventionally
\par
been considered as being distant, e.g. pragmatic,
\par
linguistic, mechanistic, and metric models can be used
\par
together in a hybrid model or in a meta-model (Varis,
\par
1995a; Varis and Kuikka, 1997a).
\par
The two-directional uncertainty scheme of belief net-
\par
works is illustrated with the following example. The
\par
model used contains only the probabilistic layer. For
\par
simplicity, the outcome layer and the use of decision
\par
variables have been left out. The example comes from
\par
fish stock assessment and fisheries restrictions. Data
\par
collection from nature is most often out of the question
\par
due to high costs, and indirect data are typically used.
