4.2.2. Type of structural analysis. (a) Decisions,
\par
objectives and constraints are pre-fixed, only their dif-
\par
ferent values are analysed; (b) detection of critical
\par
indicators and decisions; (c) detection of most appropri-
\par
ate nodes to be controlled; (d) the model is a network
\par
of conditional change variables, used primarily for
\par
finding the problem structure and for elicitation.
\par
4.2.3. Type of abstraction used in modeling. The
\par
emphasis in trying to model (a) the physical properties
\par
of the problem, (b) uncertain, competing hypotheses
\par
crucial to solving the studied problem or (c) probabilis-
\par
tic interdependencies within the system.
\par
4.2.4. Units used. The model may either use (a)
\par
measured (absolute) units or (b) relative units. The
\par
latter is particularly useful in the analysis of changes
\par
in the system.
\par
4.2. Descriptors and typologies
\par
In order to find typical features and typologies in
\par
the application of Bayesian decision analysis, I use the
\par
following attributes and descriptors. See Varis (1997)
\par
for more comprehensive analysis and more case stud-
\par
ies. Table 3 shows, what features of these are being
\par
used in the case studies.
\par
4.2.5. Dealing with objectives. (a) One can just be
\par
interested in the m i n i m u m or m a x i m u m of the expected
\par
values of the objective function. (b) Expected value -
\par
variance trade-off can, e.g. be done using the risk
\par
aversion by Pratt (1964). (c) For dealing with uncer-
\par
tainty, other approaches also exist (e.g. Hashimoto et
\par
al., (1982a, b)) (d) The value of information analysis
\par
is one possibility.
\par
4.2.1. Handling of decisions. This can take place
\par
by: (a) optimization and analysis o f impacts of given
\par
decisions in the various parts o f the network; (b) given
\par
the present state of the nature, how an action should
\par
change the status quo; (c) interdependencies of
\par
chained decisions.
\par
4.2.6. Meta-Modeling. The outcomes of more than
\par
one model can be utilized either by (a) including
\par
input-output relations and distributions of used models
\par
in the network (typical to influence diagrams), or by
\par
(b) embedding the entire models in the network (belief
\par
networks in particular).
