\par
Learning Bayesian decision analysis by doing: lessons from
\par
environmental and natural resources management
\par
Olli Varis a,*, Sakari Kuikka b
\par
a Helsinki UniTersity of Technology, Laboratory of Water Resources, 02015 HUT, Finland
\par
b Finnish Game and Fisheries Research Institute, 00721 Helsinki, Finland
\par
Received 25 February 1998; received in revised form 22 January 1999; accepted 8 February 1999
\par
Abstract
\par
The planet we are living on is getting small; each decade the number of people here grows by almost 1 billion. Due
\par
to the escalating pressure that mankind puts on natural resources and the environment, there is a pressing need to
\par
develop management schemes and approaches that acknowledge the pragmatic character of the problems: We
\par
scientists should not just passively obserTe and measure but also need to assist policy makers for better action. This
\par
requires the ability to combine, interconnect, link, and analyze jointly information, knowledge, and judgment across
\par
scientific disciplines. The methodological development is blooming and rich. However, the way to applications tends
\par
to be long. It is not enough that one has learned and applied a methodology; it has also to be comprehended and
\par
accepted by many others who often are not all that devoted to methodological challenges; and launched to
\par
responsible institutions. In this paper, we make an overview of lessons learned from studying, applying, and launching
\par
of Bayesian decision analysis---influence diagrams and belief networks in particular---in the field of resource and
\par
environmental management. A number of case studies from water resources and fisheries are used as an illustration.
\par
