\par
the first step. Getting other people to under-
\par
stand and accept the idea, and launch it to the
\par
practical level is often still more demanding. It
\par
is, though, only there where the new methodolo-
\par
gies pay back in terms of real benefits to the
\par
society, beyond just scientific adventure and
\par
merit. People who make real world decisions
\par
should be helped to make them in a better way.
\par
The objective of this paper is to summarize
\par
the experience gained, and lessons learned from
\par
trying to find, comprehend, and apply Bayesian
\par
decision analytic approaches---influence dia-
\par
grams and belief networks in particular, see Ap-
\par
pendix---and to transfer this experience to the
\par
practical level. This level has, in our case, con-
\par
sisted of a variety of national and international
\par
institutions working on the management of nat-
\par
ural resources and the environment. They in-
\par
clude regional river basin or watershed
\par
authorities, government organizations, interna-
\par
tional organizations such as UNU/WIDER
\par
(United Nations University/World Institute for
\par
Development Economics Research), ICES (Inter-
\par
national Council for the Exploration of the
\par
Sea), and IIASA (International Institute for Ap-
\par
plied Systems Analysis). A part of the experi-
\par
ence, but a majority of the consolidation of
\par
ideas has taken place after our previous
\par
overview paper (Varis et al., 1994) to Bayesian
\par
decision analysis.
\par
The basic idea of Bayesian network models
\par
(influence diagrams, belief networks) is that the
\par
uncertainty of the problems is described by the
\par
means of probabilities. These can be either un-
\par
conditioned ones (variables is not dependent on
\par
other variables), or conditional ones, in which
\par
case the value of a variable depends at least on
\par
one of the other model variables. Conditional
\par
probabilities enable the modelling of `level of
\par
determinism', i.e. a poor knowledge or poor
\par
control is modelled by weak conditional proba-
\par
bilities and vice versa.
\par
2. Experiences with influence diagrams
\par
Over a decade ago, we commenced to study
\par
the possibilities of using influence diagrams in
\par
our fields. At that point, we had quite a bit of
\par
experience on making process models for the
\par
analysis of biological and chemical systems (i.e.
\par
differential equation models with `physically
\par
based' parameterization). In addition, of course,
\par
we had used various statistics packages to the
\par
analysis of data from lakes, rivers, seas, on fish,
\par
plankton, chemical compounds and so on.
\par
It is definitely hard to imagine how many
\par
weeks it took us to arrive at any influence dia-
\par
gram model that could possibly be processed in
\par
a direction of a practically useful model. The
\par
greatest difficulty perhaps was to get rid of
\par
overly physical thinking we had learned in our
\par
training, and start to think in terms of informa-
\par
tion flows instead of material flows.
\par
We finally had success in finding an analogy
\par
with the classical textbook problems such as the
\par
Take Umbrella and Oil Wildcatter (Appendix),
\par
in a lake restoration case. From that analogy
\par
became the prototype for the first case study
\par
described below.
\par
2 .1 . Selected influence diagram studies
\par
With further elaboration, we prepared a thor-
\par
ough decision analysis of that problem (Case 1),
\par
and presented it to the municipal corporation in
\par
charge of the restoration task. The delay from
\par
the first case to an accepted practicable ap-
\par
proach was notable, and only a few years ago,
\par
the methodology has become commonplace in
\par
the discussions of ecosystem restoration analysts
\par
and practitioners in our country.
\par
We found the approach fascinating, and tried
\par
it in several other cases. Within an IIASA study
\par
on the management of the Zambezi river in
\par
southern Africa, we did quite a bit of experi-
\par
menting on different model structures. The task
\par
was to analyze the overexploitation risks of
\par
fisheries in Lake Kariba. It soon became very
\par
evident how important the thorough and exact
\par
definition of the problem at hand is. The very
\par
intuitive, graphical notation of influence dia-
\par
grams was highly useful in that work. Tens and
\par
tens of different models were produced, each
\par
reflecting a slightly or not that slightly modifiedO. Varis, S. Kuikka /Ecological Modelling 119 (1999) 177--195 179
\par
angle to the problem. Finally the structure was
\par
chosen (Case 2) that met best the targets set to
\par
the project.
\par
Much of the practical research effort and
\par
funds goes to various data collection activities
\par
such as realization of measurement campaigns
\par
and monitoring programs. We made an analysis
\par
of a real-time monitoring system for a river
\par
(Case 3). The data collection costs were con-
\par
trasted and traded off with the decreased uncer-
\par
tainty in the information that a river basin
\par
authority is using in giving recommendations to
\par
water user stakeholders including the public.
\par
Data collection was one of the leading elements
\par
in the case study of Case 1, but in the Case 3,
\par
the whole analysis was focused on that question.
\par
The literature knows influence diagrams which
\par
include no decision nodes, just probabilistic ones
\par
(e.g. Howard, 1990). We have come across with
\par
some cases in which such models, often called
\par
relevance maps, have been useful. One of our
\par
graduate students got a task to analyze various
\par
possibilities to rehabilitate fish stocks of a river,
\par
which has been subjected to various man-made
\par
disturbances over the past few centuries (Case
\par
4). She used a.o. such models.
\par
In a review on fisheries science, Kuikka (1998)
\par
discussed the use of Bayesian network models
\par
(both with and without decision and objective
\par
variables), especially when applying the precau-
\par
tionary approach to fisheries management. In
\par
this context the role of uncertainty is important:
\par
the higher it is, the lower the exploitation
\par
should be. An essential conclusion was, that
\par
structural uncertainty (which models to use)
\par
tends to dominate, even though most of the sci-
\par
entific work is focused on the parameter uncer-
\par
tainty. This is a hard job for scientist; adding of
\par
reasonable doubts (hypothesis, models) to the
\par
analysis increases easily uncertainty, which leads
\par
to lower allowable catches. The roads of scien-
\par
tists and managers should meet more pro-
\par
foundly. One key analysis that led to these ideas
\par
is presented as Case 5.
\par
According to our experience, influence dia-
\par
grams are well suited to the analysis of prob-
\par
lems in which the decisions include different
\par
options for reduction of uncertainty by im-
\par
proved information.
\par
2 .2 . Case 1: Restoration of a temperate lake
\par
The use of Lake Tuusulanja \textasciidieresis rvi; Finland, is
\par
remarkably harmed by eutrophication,
\par
cyanobacteria in particular. Among the various
\par
restoration measures taken during the last 3
\par
decades, we considered here the plan to divert
\par
water from a water transfer tunnel to the basin
\par
to dilute the lake (Varis et al., 1990).
\par
The influence diagram model consists of 10
\par
nodes (Fig. 1). Analogical to problem in Ap-
\par
pendix, on the top there is a node describing the
\par
uncontrolled state of the nature; the Prior Water
\par
Quality. It contains a probability distribution
\par
derived from monthly water quality data in
\par
1974--85. The Forecast node contains a distribu-
\par
tion describing the accuracies obtainable for
\par
forecast models, given different Monitoring
\par
Strategies (monthly, bi-weekly, and weekly). The
\par
information for this node was obtained from a
\par
study on a set of transfer function models
\par
(Sirvio \textasciidieresis, 1988). The same study served also as
\par
the basis of the information for node Posterior
\par
Water Quality, which includes the assessment
\par
for changes in Prior Water Quality, given 4 dilu-
\par
tion options (no extra inflow, flow of 0.1 and
\par
0.5 m3 s-1, and adaptive discharge).
\par
The rest of the model consists of costs and
\par
benefits associated with different elements of the
\par
project. The benefits of improved water quality
\par
were not fixed to any single monetary amount,
\par
but instead a risk-benefit sensitivity study was
\par
carried out (Fig. 1). Among other things, it
\par
shows an interesting phenomenon: a risk averse
\par
manager would act instead of measure. A risk
\par
prone one would measure instead of act. Such a
\par
feature is probably a more general property of
\par
environmental systems, and implications to, e.g.
\par
climatic change impacts are highly interesting.
\par
For the potential monetary value of further de-
\par
velopment of the forecasting models, the value-
\par
of-information study showed a value of 0.5
\par
money units, using the scale of horizontal axis
\par
of Fig. 1. This is remarkably low value not sug-
\par
gesting too much emphasis on refinement of
\par
forecasting models for this purpose.180
\par
Fig. 1. Influence diagram model for the restoration of Lake
\par
Tuusulanja \textasciidieresis rvi with two example risk-benefit plots. A risk
\par
averse manager would prefer action to measurements, while a
\par
risk neutral and risk prone one would measure and not act.
\par
O. Varis, S. Kuikka /Ecological Modelling 119 (1999) 177--195
\par
2 .3 . Case 2: Fisheries management in a tropical
\par
reserToir
\par
The second largest reservoir in Africa, Lake
\par
Kariba, is situated on the Zambezi River between
\par
Zimbabwe and Zambia. It was completed in 1960.
\par
The sardine, Limnothrissa miodon, was introduced
\par
into the lake in 1967--68 from Lake Tanganyika.
\par
The commercial fishery started in 1973. The sar-
\par
dine accounts for more than 90\% of the total
\par
commercial catch. The fishing effort has grown
\par
rapidly, but the catch per unit effort (CPUE) has
\par
undergone a remarkable decrease till 1980, but
\par
stagnated after that. This unexpected development
\par
of the CPUE might be due to (a) changes in the
\par
fish production, or (b) changes in the behavior of
\par
fishermen, or both.
\par
This analysis (Varis and Kuikka, 1990) was
\par
directed to find a sustainable level of fishing effort
\par
for the lake. The model (Fig. 2) consisted of the
\par
following nodes: Actual productiTity: seven pub-
\par
lished estimates on the maximal production of
\par
sardine in the lake plus the catch and effort data
\par
were used. ProductiTity or Exploitation rate?: The
\par
two competing hypotheses given above were con-
\par
trasted in this node. Fishing mortality: The annual
\par
survival rate was assumed to range between 10--
\par
40\% of the stock. Effort: The number of allowed
\par
fishing licenses. Values from 75 to 225\% of the
\par
1985 level were used. Catch: Product of realized
\par
production and exploitation rate. risk for collapse
\par
of stock: This unknown factor is an important
\par
basis of fisheries restrictions. A number of
\par
threshold values were used in sensitivity analyses.
\par
Market prices and elasticity of prices: Some pub-
\par
lished evidence was available for the modeling of
\par
the market mechanism part of the system. Objec-
\par
tiTe function: The income was maximized with
\par
constraints for the Risk for collapse of stock and
\par
the market valued CPUE.
\par
In the policy analysis phase, a number of sensi-
\par
tivity analyses were performed. As an example,
\par
Fig. 2 shows the optimal Effort as a function of
\par
risk threshold and risk attitude r (Pratt 1964). As
\par
an another example, the value-of-information
\par
analysis showed greatest value for the actual
\par
production.O. Varis, S. Kuikka /Ecological Modelling 119 (1999) 177--195 181
\par
Fig. 2. Left, influence diagram for sardine fisheries management of Lake Karibe. Right, optimal level of fishing effort as a function
\par
of risk attitude r and risk threshold for the collapse of sardine stock.
\par
2 .4 . Case 3: Real-time monitoring system for a
\par
riTer
\par
The major water quality problem in the Ker-
\par
ava River, Finland, is the occasional deteriora-
\par
tion of its hygienic state. Kl\o ve et al. (1993)
\par
studied the design of a real-time monitoring sys-
\par
tem to inform the authorities and the public on
\par
these problems. The system components were
\par
on-line water quality sensor system and adjacent
\par
forecast models.
\par
The influence diagram model (Fig. 3, Varis et
\par
al., 1993) had the following nodes: Monitoring
\par
system: The alternatives for making the forecasts
\par
were to use (a) river flow data, (b) water quality
\par
monitoring data, and (c) a laser-based Lidar
\par
monitoring system. Forecast and Measurement
\par
Uncertainty: Results from the study by Kl\o ve et
\par
al. (1993), expressed as Pearson's r2 for each
\par
alternative. Cost-Uncertainty EquiTalent: A
\par
parameter for scaling costs and uncertainties to
\par
units sensitive to the selection of the monitoring
\par
system. ObjectiTe Function: The difference be-
\par
tween System Uncertainty and Total Costs,
\par
which is maximized. Other nodes are self ex-
\par
planatory.
\par
Two types of sensitivity analyses were made.
\par
First, the risk attitude was traded-off with the
\par
Cost-Uncertainty EquiTalent parameter. Second,
\par
the risk attitude was studied together with the
\par
Discount Rate. Fig. 3 shows an example of the
\par
outcome of the second analysis.
\par
2 .5 . Case 4: Rehabilitation of fisheries in a
\par
temperate riTer.
\par
The Kyro \textasciidieresis njoki river, Finland has been heavily
\par
constructed for flood protection, hydropower,
\par
and other uses, over several centuries (Hilde \textasciiacute n
\par
and Rapport, 1993). The fish habitats have been
\par
impacted in many ways. The planning of the
\par
rehabilitation process is realized as an interactive
\par
procedure in which all stakeholders participate.
\par
Sistonen (1995) made a comprehensive survey of
\par
the priorities of the stakeholders, and structured
\par
the various components of the problem using
\par
influence diagrams. Remarkable part of the data
\par
was obtained through interviews.
\par
A number of models were constructed to form
\par
a two-level hierarchy. An upper level model was
\par
a summary of several lower level influence dia-
\par
grams. Due to the problem's character, the mod-
\par
els were very task specific, and therefore differed
\par
a lot from one another. As an example of a
\par
lower-level model, Fig. 4 presents an influence
\par
diagram for one of the reaches of the river.
\par
Each node contains a probability distribution,
\par
allowing a base for further analyses using the182
