3. Experiences with belief networks
\par
Gradually, when getting increasingly involved
\par
in Bayesian network models, we started to study
\par
computationally more advanced and philosophi-
\par
cally more general approaches. There is a number
\par
of Artificial Intelligence based modern techniques
\par
to Bayesian networks (see Horwitz et al., 1988;
\par
Pearl, 1988; Shafer, 1990; Szolovitz and Pauker,
\par
1993). We have worked mostly with the approach
\par
by Pearl (1988), but at the beginning, we had no
\par
real, clear idea how we could possibly benefit and
\par
apply that rather strange but fascinating
\par
methodology.
\par
It required again quite a bit of digestion and
\par
thinking (Varis et al., 1994), plus playing with the
\par
textbook examples until some ideas began to
\par
emerge and find their place. Now we have pro-
\par
duced software on our own using spreadsheets
\par
(Varis, 1997, 1998b).
\par
We first came up with a screening study on
\par
potential application possibilities of belief net-
\par
works (Varis, 1995). As it turned out, the many-
\par
sided properties of belief networks appear to
\par
allow their use in multiple ways in resource and
\par
environmental modeling (Fig. 5). We were also
\par
fascinated about the computational efficiency of
\par
uncertainty propagation with belief networks.
\par
We went on in parallel to the linguistic, prag-
\par
matic, and mechanistic directions. The case study
\par
finished first was on analytic models; one in which
\par
we made a meta-model that incorporated a couple
\par
of fish stock assessment models---both determin-
\par
istic and empirical ones---that are used for giving
\par
recommendations on quota decisions of salmon
\par
(Case 6) in the Baltic Sea.
\par
From the outset, we were mainly interested in
\par
structural uncertainties in assessment models and
\par
merging of information from several sources.
\par
That worked relatively well, although implemen-
\par
tation appeared fairly hard. Structural uncertain-
\par
ties did not matter much at a tactical level
\par
decision making---such as annual quota recom-
\par
mendations---but at the strategic level---such as
\par
in the analysis and selection between the applica-
\par
tion of various control options---their importance
\par
was much more pronounced.
\par
Based on the above idea of considering a fairly
\par
simple management oriented deterministic model
\par
as a structurally uncertain entity, the approach
\par
was further extended to include also estimation of
\par
parameters and the structural uncertainty of the
\par
model, and to optimization. That took place in a
\par
large study, again at IIASA, in which cost-effec-
\par
tive policies were designed to tackle the extensive
\par
river pollution problems in transitional economies
\par
of Europe (Case 7). The approach performed well
\par
in a river model context, which is computationally
\par
fascinating: a river has a tree structure, which is
\par
nice to model.
\par
The Case study 8, initiated as a mental game,
\par
turned out to produce a plausible methodology
\par
for expert knowledge acquisition in the analysis of
\par
complex problems. In the environmental sector
\par
such tasks, for instance, are frequent at the early
\par
phase of impact assessment. Our first application
\par
was on impact assessment of possible global cli-
\par
matic change on a lake, Tuusulanja \textasciidieresis rvi again
\par
(Varis and Kuikka, 1997a). The approach gained
\par
surprisingly quickly the acceptance of the national
\par
environmental authorities. It showed the impor-
\par
tance of being broad and holistic; the approach
\par
provided a tool to push and support experts to see
\par
beyond details. Hence, we got a chance to further
\par
develop and apply it in a much broader setting, atO. Varis, S. Kuikka /Ecological Modelling 119 (1999) 177--195 185
\par
the final stage of the nationwide climatic change
\par
research programme.
