3 .2 . Case 6: Salmon fisheries management
\par
The present state of wild Baltic salmon stocks is
\par
poor. From the whole stock, the share of the
\par
salmon with wild origin has decreased remarkably
\par
during the last decades to about 10\% due to the
\par
lack of spawners (i.e. too high fishing mortality).
\par
The International Baltic Sea Fishery Commission
\par
has stated as the goal of the Baltic salmon man-
\par
agement to safeguard wild salmon stocks. Total
\par
allowable catch (TAC) policy has been used to
\par
achieve this goal. The ICES produces the fish
\par
stock information.
\par
The uncertainties of the stock predictions and
\par
TAC based management are high. To reduce
\par
them, some variables additional to the standard
\par
virtual population analysis (VPA) approach can
\par
be used. They include water temperature, post-
\par
smolt growth, and the CPUE. Both the scientific
\par
discussions on the usefulness of different informa-
\par
tion sources, and the assessment work itself were
\par
found to require methodology that allows the
\par
consideration of all relevant information at hand.
\par
This type of problems are common in fisheries
\par
management (Schnute and Hilborn, 1993).
\par
To meet these requirements, a belief network
\par
was constructed, in which the VPA model and the
\par
regression models were embedded (Varis and
\par
Kuikka, 1997b). The state variables (age group
\par
specific stock sizes and post-smolt survivals), and
\par
the parameters to be estimated (fishing mortality
\par
rates) were included in the belief network as nodes
\par
(Fig. 6). The regression models were also in-
\par
cluded. The uncertainty balance iteration (Ap-
\par
pendix) was used in the calibration of the model
\par
parameters.
\par
The conventional VPA prediction can be used
\par
together with the meta-model prediction, giving
\par
also the probability distributions for the predic-
\par
tions (Fig. 6). The predictive roles of the regres-
\par
sion models can be analyzed by the link strengths
\par
between them and the VPA model. Various sensi-
\par
tivity analyses and causal hypothesis simulations
\par
can be performed, both for diagnosis and predic-
\par
tion. The link structure can also be used for
\par
knowledge acquisition. The belief network of the
\par
fishing mortality estimates is used to include the
\par
expert knowledge on various dependencies in the
\par
fishing mortality in the particular stock.
\par
The TAC recommendation can be given to the
\par
policy makers, not only as a single (expected)
\par
value that formerly was typically based on some
\par
of the regression models or on the VPA, but
\par
including the information of all the used models,
\par
as a joint probability distribution. This allows the
\par
consideration of different risk attitudes and per-
\par
ceptions in an analytic manner.
\par
