3 .3 . Case 7: Cost-effectiTe wastewater treatment
\par
for a riTer
\par
This Case (Varis, 1998a) deals with cost-effec-
\par
tive upgrading of wastewater treatment plants in a
\par
watershed on the basis of river water quality
\par
criteria. It represents a typical river basin manage-
\par
ment problem, particularly in conditions of for-
\par
merly socialist countries in Europe. There is a
\par
pressing need for improving water pollution con-
\par
trol. The scarcity of capital suggests gradual up-
\par
grading policy of wastewater treatment on a
\par
cost-effective basis (Somlyo \textasciiacute dy, 1994).
\par
A deterministic model with three state variables
\par
was used: DO (dissolved oxygen), BOD and NH4
\par
(carbonaceous and nitrogenous biological oxygen
\par
demand). Three rate parameters were estimated.
\par
The state equations constitute the state layer of
\par
the generalized belief network model used. The
\par
probabilistic layer was based on a series of paral-
\par
lel, coupled probability trees based on the river
\par
topology (Fig. 7), which describe the steady-state
\par
evolution of the state variables. State variables
\par
and parameters are represented as belief network
\par
nodes. Evidential information for states is ob-
\par
tained from historical data, that were included in
\par
the net as nodes.
\par
The analysis is divided into two subsequent
\par
phases, both of which use the uncertainty balance
\par
iteration approach. The same model including 2
\par
layers is used, but the targets, decision variables,
\par
and estimated link strengths were different. First,
\par
the parameter estimation is performed, in which
\par
the mean values at the state layer can be iterated
\par
to equal the posteriors. The strengths of the links
\par
shown in Fig. 7 are estimated; their values show
\par
the structural uncertainties of the state equations.
\par
The second phase consists of finding the most
\par
cost-effective solutions. Now, different treatment
\par
levels are used as decision variables (instead of
\par
parameters), link strengths are not estimated, and
\par
water quality targets with the target cost level are
\par
used as targets versus observations at the previous
\par
phase: things we want to observe in the future.
\par
The definition of variables can be changed in
\par
the course of the analysis due to the two-direc-
\par
tional uncertainty propagation in the probabilistic
\par
layer. At the diagnostic phase, both downstream
\par
(p) and upstream (l) messages are used. At the
\par
policy analysis phase, only the l message is used.
\par
A logical explanation exists: In diagnosis, all the
\par
data and model predictions are iterated to meet
\par
the balance, hence both propagation directions
\par
are used. In policy analysis, the targets influence
\par
only the treatment plants downstream of the
\par
point at which a target is set. When detecting a
\par
deviation between target and model prediction,
\par
the message induced is propagated upstream all
\par
the way to the posterior distributions of the treat-
\par
ment plant purification levels. This provides a
\par
basis for iteration similar to that in parameter
\par
estimation. The study produced in a series of
\par
optimized scenarios for the upgrading of the treat-
\par
ment plants, using a variety of economic and
\par
environmental criteria.O. Varis, S. Kuikka /Ecological Modelling 119 (1999) 177--195 3 .4 . Case 8: A nationwide climatic change impact
\par
assessment
\par
Climatic change impact studies are among the
\par
most complicated environmental assessments that
\par
scientists have ever faced. The risks and chances
\par
that policy-makers are facing are enormous. Al-
\par
though being dominated by uncertainties and in-
\par
terdisciplinary settings, the impact analyses are
\par
dominated by focused studies without a prior
\par
cross-sectoral scoping phase.
\par
Using belief networks, a probabilistic impact
\par
matrix approach was developed. Besides in cli-
\par
matic change research, the approach was designed
\par
to be applicable to conventional Environmental
\par
Impact Assessment, where various impact ma-
\par
trices are common. The starting point is a sheet
\par
with the most relevant attributes for the problem
\par
under study, and a set of outcomes describing
\par
their possible future evolution. For each attribute,
\par
a discrete probability distribution is assessed by
\par
the expert(s), describing its expected change. Us-
\par
187
\par
ing a cross-impact matrix, the expert(s) judge the
\par
interdependencies (link strengths) between each
\par
pair of attributes. The probability distributions
\par
and link strengths are connected to a belief net-
\par
work, which updates the prior distributions (Fig.
\par
8). These posterior distributions should not di-
\par
verge much from the priors. If they do, though, as
\par
it often appears, the assessment is inconsistent.
\par
An integrated assessment for the climatic
\par
change impacts on the watersheds of southern
\par
Finland was made (Kuikka and Varis, 1997) using
\par
a panel of 13 experts, who all were among the
\par
leading scientists in an extensive Finnish research
\par
programme on Climatic Change. The present case
\par
study was one of the final assessments of the
\par
programme. This study included 24 attributes
\par
(Fig. 8). Comprehensive sensitivity analyses were
\par
performed on the information obtained. Those
\par
included studies of the roles of the attributes'
\par
states (expressed as probability distributions), as
\par
well as the roles of the causalities between the
\par
attributes (expressed as links). Such analyses were
\par
Fig. 8. Summary results of climatic change impact assessment for watersheds in southern Finland.188
\par
O. Varis, S. Kuikka /Ecological Modelling 119 (1999) 177--195
\par
made separately for each four groups of attributes.
\par
A range of results were produced, by analyzing
\par
both the roles of different causalities in the models,
\par
as well as those of the states of the climatic,
\par
watershed, and socioeconomic variables. We
\par
found it very useful that both the state and the
\par
causality uncertainties could be analyzed at the
\par
same time. The study contributed to the compre-
\par
hension and acknowledgment of uncertainty
\par
analyses among the key scientists of the
\par
programme.
