As was explained in Introduction, we have been
\par
able to apply this new methodology in several
\par
institutes with different backgrounds. This gives us
\par
an opportunity to make some comments on the
\par
attitudes met. Probably the most positive attitudes
\par
have been among university students; the method-
\par
ological orientation is not that deep at that stage
\par
of a scientific career. In Finnish national institutes,
\par
the attitude has been fairly positive as well, and
\par
several scientists have started to apply Bayesian
\par
networks to their own studies. However, the atti-
\par
tudes in the international institutes have been more
\par
conservative. This is probably based on two facts:
\par
a very deep specialization on leads easily to a
\par
conservatives attitude, and on the other hand, a
\par
certain conservatism is justified in such cases,
\par
where the main task of the organization is an
\par
advisory one. Mistakes might have an effect on the
\par
credibility. However, it should be noted that even
\par
in such cases the future development is totally
\par
dependent on the willingness to learn and apply
\par
new methodological tools and the historical bur-
\par
den should not be too restrictive.
