4 .2 . Detecting typical features
\par
There is a set of typical, generic features of
\par
Bayesian network models (Varis, 1997). These
\par
features can be summarized in the following 6
\par
classes. The case studies described above fall in
\par
these categories as shown in Table 1.
\par
1. Meta-modeling: (a) input-output relations and
\par
distributions of used models are used in the
\par
network (typical to influence diagrams); (b)
\par
entire models are embedded in the network (in
\par
belief networks in particular).
\par
2. Handling of decisions: (a) given the present
\par
state of the nature, how an action should
\par
change the status quo; (b) optimization and
\par
analysis of impacts of unconditioned decisions
\par
in the various parts of the network; (c) interde-
\par
pendencies of chained decisions.
\par
3. Type of structural analysis: (a) decisions, ob-
\par
jectives and constraints are pre-fixed, only
\par
their different values are analyzed; (b) detec-
\par
tion of critical indicators and decisions; (c)
\par
detection of most appropriate nodes to be
\par
controlled; (d) the model is a network of con-
\par
ditional change variables, used primarily for
\par
finding the problem structure and for
\par
elicitation.
\par
4. Type of abstraction used in modeling: The em-
\par
phasis in (a) the physical properties of the
\par
problem; (b) uncertain, competing hypotheses
\par
crucial to solving the studied problem.O. Varis, S. Kuikka /Ecological Modelling 119 (1999) 177--195 189
\par
5. Units used: (a) measured (absolute) units; (b)
\par
relative units.
\par
6. Dealing with objectiTes: (a) minimize or
\par
maximize expected values; (b) expected
\par
value---variance trade-off; (c) other
\par
approaches to uncertainty; (d) value of
\par
information analysis.
\par
4 .3 . Methodological lessons
\par
As a final remark, we would again like to point
\par
out that the methodology used should be in the
\par
service of problem solving, and therefore, the
\par
method and the domain must be deeply compre-
\par
hended, not just one of them. This induces a
\par
challenge and incentive to move towards interdis-
\par
ciplinarity: domain experts, methodology people,
\par
and decision makers should understand each
\par
other and be able to work together.
\par
The most important lessons as presented in the
\par
text are:
\par
Starting with an unconventional approach re-
\par
quires time, imagination and ability to see
\par
analogies
\par
Getting other people to understand and accept
\par
the idea is often still more demanding
\par
Risk averse manager acts while risk prone one
\par
keeps studying
\par
Each modification of the model structure re-
\par
defines the problem
\par
Data collection is an investment to reduce
\par
uncertainty
\par
Sometimes just complex interconnections are of
\par
interest
\par
Structural uncertainty of a deterministic model
\par
can be modeled
\par
It is at the strategic level where the structural
\par
uncertainties really matter
\par
We can even do parameter estimation and opti-
\par
mization with belief nets
\par
Experts should be able and be supported to see
\par
beyond details
\par
Different experts have different and inconsis-
\par
tent judgments but a consensus is needed
\par
Analysis should be problem driven, not the
\par
other way round
\par
Bayesian decision analysis is not just parameter
\par
estimation
\par
We try to avoid giving the message that
\par
Bayesian decision analysis should be the only
\par
available tool to the problems we have described.
\par
Instead, in some other contexts, we have strongly
\par
advocated to the importance of a broad methodo-
\par
logical knowledge, and conscious choice of the
\par
used methodology (Varis, 1994, 1996). This is a
\par
very tough educational challenge.
