Appendix A. The Approaches
\par
Within environmental and resource manage-
\par
ment, the applications of Bayesian analysis have
\par
been dominated by classical Bayesian inference,
\par
i.e. parameter estimation, in which the Bayesian
\par
analysis is restricted to the parameter space. In
\par
decision theory, the idea of considering the entire
\par
model as a construct subject to uncertainty and
\par
subjectivity stem from the game theory of the
\par
1930s and `40s (Shafer, 1990). At that time it
\par
became evident that costs and constraints must be
\par
explicit, due to improved estimates, used confi-
\par
dence interval requirements or statistical tests
\par
chosen (Shafer, 1990). Games evolved into se-
\par
quential games against uncontrolled `nature', and
\par
abstractions such as decision trees were devel-
\par
oped. Bayesian decision theory gained increasing
\par
notice and emphasis (Wald, 1950). The basic the-
\par
ory was developed into more applicable level to-
\par
wards the late 1960s (Howard, 1968; North, 1968;
\par
Raiffa, 1968).
\par
Important topics in contemporary decision
\par
analysis include risk attitude analysis (Pratt,
\par
1964), value-of-information analysis (Merkhofer,
\par
1977), multiattribute and multicriteria issues
\par
(Keeney and Raiffa, 1976), several behavioral and190
\par
O. Varis, S. Kuikka /Ecological Modelling 119 (1999) 177--195
\par
cognitive issues (Kahnemann et al., 1973; von
\par
Winterfeldt and Edwards, 1986), and computa-
\par
tional techniques. We have tried to contribute to
\par
the diffusion of these all to applications in re-
\par
source and environmental management with vary-
\par
ing success. For more information on Bayesian
\par
decision analysis, see Raiffa (1968), Bunn (1984),
\par
Howard and Matheson (1984), Baird (1989),
\par
Morgan and Henrion (1990), Oliver and Smith
\par
(1990), Clemen (1991), Marshall and Oliver
\par
(1995), and Russell and Norvig (1995).
\par
Conventionally, one of the major bottlenecks to
\par
practical applications of Bayesian approaches has
\par
been the high amount of computation required.
\par
Powerful numerical techniques have not been
\par
available until mid-1980's. According to Shafer
\par
and Pearl (1990), the recent developments in deci-
\par
sion analysis have been linked with advances in
\par
related computational mathematics. Artificial In-
\par
telligence has had a rapidly growing influence
\par
within the last 10 years.
\par
The focal role has been taken by computational
\par
schemes for local updating of probabilistic infor-
\par
mation in conditioned networks. The problem has
\par
been approached both from the direction of the
\par
Dempster--Shafer Theory of Evidence (e.g. Gor-
\par
don and Shortliffe, 1985; Shenoy and Shafer,
\par
1986), and from Bayesian statistics (e.g. Pearl,
\par
1986a,b, 1988). Yet, in the resulted methodolo-
\par
gies---known as belief networks, causal networks,
\par
Bayesian nets, qualitative Markov networks, or
\par
constraint networks---this division is difficult or
\par
impossible to distinguish. We call these techniques
\par
belief networks, as appears to be more and more
\par
the convention.
\par
Below, 3 practical approaches to Bayesian deci-
\par
sion analysis; decision trees, influence diagrams,
\par
and belief networks, are briefly reviewed. A con-
\par
ceptual introduction on each is given, with an
\par
illustrative example.
\par
Decision trees have been the best known and
\par
used graphical abstraction used in Bayesian deci-
\par
sion theory over many decades. In historical per-
\par
spective, the roots of decision trees are even older
\par
than those of modern Bayesian decision theory.
\par
They can be seen as a special case of von Neu-
\par
mann and Morgenstern (1944) game tree. The
\par
basic idea is that the events, both controllable
\par
(decision) and uncontrollable (chance) ones, are
\par
set up in procedural order. Each set of outcomes
\par
from a node, be they decision alternatives or
\par
possible outcomes of a chance, define a new
\par
branch in the tree. The following example should
\par
illustrate decision trees.
\par
The problem here is to define whether a hypo-
\par
thetical project requires a detailed or specific im-
\par
pact assessment (this is de facto derived as a direct
\par
analogy from the much used Take Umbrella
\par
problem, Fig. 9f). The results of a screening study
\par
are used to make this decision. Start from a
\par
chance node for Environmental Impacts (P(Im-
\par
pacts Significant)=0.3, P(Impacts Specific)=
\par
0.7). Next comes the Detailed Assessment
\par
decision, and then the chance node for the screen-
\par
ing results (P(Result SignificantImpacts Signifi-
\par
cant)=0.7, P(Result SignificantImpacts
\par
Specific)=0.3, P(Result SpecificImpacts Signifi-
\par
cant)=0.2, P(Result SpecificImpacts Specific)=
\par
0.8), and the tree ends up with the objective
\par
function (Table 2). The decision tree (Fig. 9c),
\par
after evaluation (cf. Raiffa, 1968; Baird, 1989),
\par
shows the posterior probabilities and resulting
\par
satisfaction values, as they have been propagated
\par
from the right to the left.
\par
Influence diagrams have been originally ex-
\par
tended from decision trees, with the leading moti-
\par
vation in developing a more compact and efficient
\par
approach to decision analysis. They also share
\par
many of the features of belief networks, and often
\par
they are seen as their special case.
\par
An influence diagram is an acyclic Bayesian
\par
network of nodes connected with one-directional
\par
links (arcs). The nodes represent probabilistic
\par
variables (denoted as ovals), deterministic ones
\par
(including objective functions; rounded rectan-
\par
