\par
O. Varis, S. Kuikka /Ecological Modelling 119 (1999) 177--195
\par
gles), and decisions (rectangles). Like a decision
\par
tree, the diagram describes causality or the flow of
\par
information and probabilistic dependencies in a
\par
system. For details, see Howard and Matheson
\par
(1984), Shachter (1986), Oliver and Smith (1990)
\par
and Clemen (1991).
\par
The influence diagram model for the example
\par
problem has four nodes (Fig. 9a). The uncontrol-
\par
lable prior---the Environmental Impacts, or more
\par
generally, the state of the nature---is a quantity
\par
which is not yet known accurately. The Screening
\par
node contains a conditional probability distribu-
\par
tion, conditioned by the state of the nature. The
\par
screening results are known at the moment of
\par
decision on Detailed Assessment. The Satisfaction
\par
to the exercise depends on the state of the nature
\par
and on the decision. This model gives exactly the
\par
same result as the decision tree model, the only
\par
difference being in the notation used.
\par
The influence diagram notation provides the
\par
analyst with some attractive and useful properties.
\par
One of them is the way to perform the value of
\par
information analysis. It might be of interest to
\par
know the maximum additional amount of money
\par
that one should spend on a screening analysis.
\par
Assume now that at the moment of decision
\par
making the state of the nature is exactly known.
\par
In the influence diagram (Fig. 9d), this would be
\par
denoted by drawing a direct link from EnTiron-
\par
mental Impacts to Detailed Assessment. Evalua-
\par
tion of the model without this new link yields the
\par
expected value of objective function to be 73.5,
\par
and with the new link, it becomes 91 (Fig. 9e).
\par
This difference, 17.5 units, is the expected value of
\par
perfect information of screening, being the maxi-
\par
mum amount one should invest on it. Similarly,
\par
one can study the value of imperfect information
\par
and the value of imperfect and perfect control
\par
(Clemen, 1991; Lapin, 1991).
\par
Belief networks belong to the Bayesian family of
\par
modern computational techniques that have
\par
emerged from Artificial Intelligence research.
\par
Among the many existing variations of the idea,
\par
we will concentrate on the generalized belief net-
\par
work approach by Varis (1998a) which has been
\par
extended from Pearl (1988) methodology.
\par
The network has n nodes that can arbitrarily be
\par
interconnected. The prior probabilities assigned to
\par
the outcomes are updated with the information
\par
linked from the net's other parts, yielding the
\par
posterior distribution. The posteriors Belj are cal-
\par
culated using two independent messages (usually
\par
p and l). The updated belief is obtained as the
\par
product of them and the prior.
\par
Some nodes may be understood as controllable,
\par
decision nodes. One or several nodes can act as a
\par
criterion or constraint for decision making, and
\par
constitute one or more objective functions.
\par
A link transfers information from a node to
\par
another node. Links are in two layers. An uncer-
\par
tainty link is defined as the link matrix Mi ~j be-
\par
tween nodes i and j. The matrix can be direction
\par
specific: Mi ~j 4Mj i. A state link presents a rela-
\par
tion between outcomes yi and yj of i and j. Be-
\par
cause the approach requires that each outcome
\par
has one value, the propagation of outcome values
\par
is unidirectional, and a functional relationship
\par
exists, yi =f(yj). Technically, the approach divides
\par
the model into two layers in communication with
\par
one another. These deterministic equations consti-
\par
tute the state layer, and the belief network consti-
\par
tutes the probabilistic layer.
\par
Varis (1998a) developed the uncertainty balance
\par
approach to allow various optimization tasks us-
\par
ing belief networks. The key proposition is that
\par
the prior and posterior probability distributions
\par
of the target variables (observations, management
\par
goals, constraints, etc.) should become equal. This
\par
implies that the joint distributions of the external
\par
information (prior) should equal to the modeled
\par
distribution (posterior) and assures proper utiliza-
\par
tion of the prior information. This occurs by
\par
finding optimal values for control variables (in-
\par
cluding parameters), and for link strengths (ex-
\par
pressing the structural uncertainty of the model
\par
equations) by iteration (Fig. 10, see also Varis and
\par
Kuikka, 1997b). The approach has been designed
\par
to be as interactive as possible and to be operated
\par
on-line.
\par
The two-directional uncertainty scheme is illus-
\par
trated with the following example. The model
\par
used contains only the probabilistic layer. For
\par
simplicity, the state layer and the use of decision
\par
variables have been left out. The example comes
\par
from fish stock assessment and fisheries restric-
\par
