\textbf{Eliciting von Neumann-Morgenstern Utilities}
\par
\textbf{many years. This paper precisely suggests a new}
\par
\textbf{method of utility elicitation, the "gamble-tradeoff}
\par
\textbf{method," or "tradeoff method" for short. This method}
\par
\textbf{uses, in the context of uncertainty, a technique that has}
\par
\textbf{been used previously in multiattribute utility theory}
\par
\textbf{(the "saw-tooth" method in Fishburn 1967; Johnson}
\par
\textbf{1974; Keeney and Raiffa 1976, ?3.4.7; Kirkwood and}
\par
\textbf{Sarin 1980; von Winterfeldt and Edwards 1986), con-}
\par
\textbf{joint measurement theory (Krantz et al. 1971; Tversky}
\par
\textbf{et al. 1988), and in axiomatizations of several risk-}
\par
\textbf{theory forms (Wakker 1984, 1989, 1994; Tversky and}
\par
\textbf{Kahneman 1992; Wakker and Tversky 1993). The main}
\par
\textbf{advantage of the tradeoff method is that it minimizes}
\par
\textbf{the role of probabilities while preserving full validity}
\par
\textbf{when used in the expected utility criterion. Utilities can}
\par
\textbf{be generated when a subject distorts or misperceives}
\par
\textbf{probabilities, or when the subject does not understand}
\par
\textbf{probabilities. In the latter case, general events whose}
\par
\textbf{probabilities need not be known with precision can be}
\par
\textbf{used to elicit utilities.}
\par
\textbf{We would like to emphasize that we are in no way}
\par
\textbf{arguing that eliciting utilities with unknown probabil-}
\par
\textbf{ities is in general superior to eliciting utilities with}
\par
\textbf{known probabilities. Rather, we claim only that the}
\par
\textbf{tradeoff method enables one to elicit utilities without}
\par
\textbf{invoking known probabilities and that this can offer a}
\par
\textbf{number of advantages. The tradeoff method can per-}
\par
\textbf{fectly well be applied when probabilities are known.}
\par
\textbf{The method then still provides more robustness}
\par
\textbf{against deviations from expected utility than existing}
\par
\textbf{methods.}
\par
\textbf{Both under the rank-dependent deviations from ex-}
\par
\textbf{pected utility (Quiggin 1981; Schmeidler 1989) and un-}
\par
\textbf{der prospect theory (Kahneman and Tversky 1979),}
\par
\textbf{does the tradeoff method not only provide robustness}
\par
\textbf{against probability distortions, but it even completely}
\par
\textbf{eliminates the effects of probability distortions. Thus,}
\par
\textbf{under these theories, the method generates the correct}
\par
\textbf{utilities. In particular the method can be applied to}
\par
\textbf{gains and losses in "cumulative prospect theory" (Tver-}
\par
\textbf{sky and Kahneman 1992), which is the modern version}
\par
\textbf{of prospect theory. The desirability of developing elic-}
\par
\textbf{itation methods that remain valid for nonexpected util-}
\par
\textbf{ity models has been pointed out several times (Farquhar}
\par
\textbf{1984; Loomes and McKenzie 1989; Hogarth and Einhorn}
\par
\textbf{1990, p. 799; Birnbaum and Sutton 1992; Weber 1994).}
\par
\textbf{Tversky and Kahneman (1992, ?2.3) described the esti-}
\par
\textbf{mation of a complex choice model, such as cumulative}
\par
\textbf{prospect theory, as a problematic issue.}
\par
\textbf{Another feature of our method is that it can uncover}
\par
\textbf{deviations from expected utility through inconsistencies}
\par
\textbf{in the revealed tradeoff comparisons. Thus, the method}
\par
\textbf{is suited for axiomatizations of expected utility models}
\par
\textbf{and the rank-dependent generalizations thereof; see}
\par
\textbf{Wakker and Tversky (1993), Wakker (1994), and the}
\par
\textbf{references therein. Finally, the elicitation method can be}
\par
\textbf{used to elicit probabilities indirectly. The entire ex-}
\par
\textbf{pected utility model can then be elicited without prior}
\par
\textbf{knowledge of any probability or utility.}
\par
\textbf{We developed the tradeoff method primarily to be}
\par
\textbf{applied in prescriptive decision analysis. It is then used}
\par
\textbf{to construct a rational and truly representative utility,}
\par
\textbf{where inconsistencies are to be resolved rather than ac-}
\par
\textbf{cepted. The method is suited for prescriptive applica-}
\par
\textbf{tions because it allows for convenient cross-checkings,}
\par
\textbf{and because it appeals directly to a rational decision}
\par
\textbf{approach: the weighing of arguments.}
\par
\textbf{The paper is organized as follows. Section 2 contrasts}
\par
\textbf{the tradeoff method with existing methods of utility}
\par
\textbf{elicitation. Section 3 describes two experiments in which}
\par
\textbf{utilities are elicited by means of our method, one for}
\par
\textbf{monetary outcomes, the other for outcomes that de-}
\par
\textbf{scribe life-duration. It has often been found in the lit-}
\par
\textbf{erature that risk attitudes depend on the domain and}
\par
\textbf{the context. We find higher risk aversion for life dura-}
\par
\textbf{tions than for money. By the tradeoff method, curvature}
\par
\textbf{of utility can be disentangled from other aspects of risk}
\par
\textbf{attitude. Thus we find that the higher risk aversion for}
\par
\textbf{life duration is not due to curvature of utility but to}
\par
\textbf{more pronounced deviations from expected utility. Sec-}
\par
\textbf{tion 4 shows that the tradeoff method can elicit utility}
\par
\textbf{for a number of nonexpected utility theories. Sections 5}
\par
\textbf{and 6 discuss advantages and disadvantages of the}
\par
\textbf{method. Conclusions appear in ?7.}
\par
\textbf{2. Existing Methods of Utility}
\par
\textbf{Elicitation, and the New Method}
\par
\textbf{The most fruitful model for normative decision under}
\par
\textbf{uncertainty is the expected utility (EU) model. Its first}
\par
\textbf{1132 MANAGEMENT SCIENCE/Vol. 42, No. 8, August 1996WAKKER AND DENEFFE}
\par
\textbf{Eliciting von Neumann-Morgenstern Utilities}
\par
\textbf{axiomatic foundation has been laid down by Savage}
\par
\textbf{(1954). Normative objections were raised by Allais}
\par
\textbf{(1953), Machina (1982), and several others. The current}
\par
\textbf{interest in nonexpected utility models stems from the}
\par
\textbf{descriptive inadequacy of EU. Several non-EU models}
\par
\textbf{have been developed. Surveys are given by Machina}
\par
\textbf{(1987), Fishburn (1988), Karni and Schmeidler (1990),}
\par
\textbf{and Kischka and Puppe (1992). None of these models}
\par
\textbf{has as yet reached the stage of being operational for}
\par
\textbf{normative applications, let alone of being viewed as su-}
\par
\textbf{perior to EU. As a result, the dominant view still seems}
\par
\textbf{to be that EU is the proper normative model. This model}
\par
\textbf{is also the point of departure of our paper. It should,}
\par
\textbf{however, be recognized that eliciting utilities from cli-}
\par
\textbf{ents is a descriptive activity, so that prescriptive appli-}
\par
\textbf{cations still have to reckon with deviations from ex-}
\par
\textbf{pected utility. This consideration motivated the devel-}
\par
\textbf{opment of a robust method.}
\par
\textbf{In EU, probabilities measure uncertainties, and von}
\par
\textbf{Neumann-Morgenstern (vNM) utilities are used to}
\par
\textbf{evaluate outcomes. Decisions are subsequently made}
\par
\textbf{according to expectations of utility. The probabilities are}
\par
\textbf{often obtained from experts and utilities are elicited}
\par
\textbf{from clients.}
\par
\textbf{We concentrate on holistic utilities and do not discuss}
\par
\textbf{the decomposition of utility into separate dimensions of}
\par
\textbf{outcomes (price, fuel consumption, maximum speed,}
\par
\textbf{etc.); see Keeney and Raiffa (1976) and Dyer et al.}
\par
\textbf{(1992). For simplicity of the exposition, we assume that}
\par
\textbf{outcomes are real numbers and that monotonicity holds}
\par
\textbf{(more is preferred to less). We first discuss existing}
\par
\textbf{methods of utility elicitation and then describe the new}
\par
\textbf{method.}
\par
\textbf{The three most frequently used methods are direct}
\par
\textbf{scaling, the certainty-equivalent method, and the}
\par
\textbf{probability-equivalent method. A fourth method, the}
\par
\textbf{lottery-equivalent method, has not been applied as of-}
\par
\textbf{ten as the other methods, even though it possesses a}
\par
\textbf{number of advantages. Under direct scaling, the client is}
\par
\textbf{asked to evaluate outcomes directly on a numerical}
\par
\textbf{scale (or some categories), and, for instance, to assign a}
\par
\textbf{value of 0 to the worst outcome, a value of 100 to the}
\par
\textbf{best outcome, a value of 50 to an outcome that lies half-}
\par
\textbf{way between the best and the worst outcome, etc. Direct}
\par
\textbf{scaling is easy to use and thus has many practical ad-}
\par
\textbf{vantages. A fundamental problem, however, is that the}
\par
\textbf{use of direct scaling to elicit vNM utilities lacks a theo-}
\par
\textbf{retical justification. Even if a person follows the dictum}
\par
\textbf{of expected utility, there is no reason to expect that the}
\par
\textbf{values obtained from direct scaling can be used as vNM}
\par
\textbf{utilities. A priori, they might be any increasing trans-}
\par
\textbf{form thereof. In addition, direct scaling has sometimes}
\par
\textbf{been found to be problematic in applications (Torrance}
\par
\textbf{1976).}
\par
\textbf{The certainty-equivalent method, the probability-}
\par
\textbf{equivalent method, and the lottery-equivalent method}
\par
\textbf{have a sounder theoretical foundation. Specifically, if}
\par
\textbf{a person were truly to maximize EU and if no mea-}
\par
\textbf{surement errors were to occur, these methods would}
\par
\textbf{elicit vNM utilities exactly. We now discuss the}
\par
\textbf{certainty- and probability-equivalent methods in}
\par
\textbf{more detail and describe the resulting procedures for}
\par
\textbf{utility elicitation. These methods are used in the ex-}
\par
\textbf{periments described in ?3. The lottery-equivalent}
\par
\textbf{method, introduced by McCord and DeNeufville}
\par
\textbf{(1986), is discussed in ?4. We denote by (x, p; z) the}
\par
\textbf{two-outcome lottery that assigns probability p to out-}
\par
\textbf{come x and probability 1 - p to outcome z. In the}
\par
\textbf{description of the utility elicitation methods below,}
\par
\textbf{we first show how one equality of utilities can be de-}
\par
\textbf{rived from observed indifferences. Next we describe}
\par
\textbf{what we call the "basic procedure." This shows how}
\par
\textbf{the elicitation of some desired number of equalities of}
\par
\textbf{utilities can be implemented.}
\par
