\textbf{3. Experiments}
\par
\textbf{This section describes two experiments. They are pre-}
\par
\textbf{sented here first and foremost to clarify the applicability}
\par
\textbf{of the TO method. The results of these experiments also}
\par
\textbf{support the arguments presented in the following sec-}
\par
\textbf{tions. Section 4 demonstrates that the TO utility elici-}
\par
\textbf{tations reported here are also valid under rank-}
\par
\textbf{dependent utility and some other nonexpected utility}
\par
\textbf{theories.}
\par
\textbf{The subjects of the experiments were all acquainted}
\par
\textbf{with probabilities and expectations. Most of them had}
\par
\textbf{heard about expected utility at some point, but not}
\par
\textbf{shortly before the experiments. The subjects were asked}
\par
\textbf{to imagine that the outcomes of their decisions in the}
\par
\textbf{experiment affected their own life duration in the first}
\par
\textbf{experiment and their own monetary reward in the sec-}
\par
\textbf{ond one. It was emphasized that there were no right or}
\par
\textbf{wrong answers but rather that the subjects' answers}
\par
\textbf{should reflect their preferences. The experiments took}
\par
\textbf{about 20 minutes per subject.}
\par
\textbf{Procedure. The experiments were conducted in small}
\par
\textbf{groups (approximately ten subjects per group) in class-}
\par
\textbf{rooms. All items were administered with paper-and-}
\par
\textbf{pencil questionnaires. Because the TO answers served}
\par
\textbf{as input for the other questions, the TO elicitations were}
