\par
\textbf{Factors Inducing Violations of EU. Two important}
\par
\textbf{reasons for deviations from EU have been proposed in the}
\par
\textbf{literature: (1) probability distortion, and (2) response}
\par
\textbf{mode effects. Probability distortion is most pronounced}
\par
\textbf{for probabilities near 0 and 1, leading to the certainty effect}
\par
\textbf{(Kahneman and Tversky 1979, Tversky and Kahneman}
\par
\textbf{1992).5 Riskless gambles, yielding an outcome with prob-}
\par
\textbf{ability 1, occur both in the CE and the PE questions. We}
\par
\textbf{can thus expect significant certainty effects in our experi-}
\par
\textbf{ments. This explanation suggests that the utilities elicited}
\par
\textbf{by the CE and PE method are too risk averse.}
\par
\textbf{We now turn to the response mode effects. In the CE}
\par
\textbf{questions, subjects were asked to respond in terms of}
\par
\textbf{outcomes (money or life duration), in the PE questions}
\par
\textbf{they had to respond in terms of probabilities. The "scale}
\par
\textbf{compatability effect," an example of a response mode}
\par
\textbf{effect, suggests that subjects pay more attention to stim-}
\par
\textbf{ulus features that resemble the response scale. There-}
\par
\textbf{fore, they pay more attention to the probabilities in the}
\par
\textbf{PE elicitations and dislike more the uncertainty of the}
\par
\textbf{risky choice. This could explain why the PE elicitations}
\par
\textbf{suggest higher risk aversion.6}
\par
\textbf{5The certainty effect relates to the relative overvaluation of riskless}
\par
\textbf{gambles in comparison to risky gambles.}
\par
\textbf{6 Response mode effects have been used to explain preference rever-}
\par
\textbf{sals in other contexts. For extensive studies see Slovic et al. (1990),}
\par
\textbf{Fischer and Hawkins (1993), and Delquie (1993).}
\par
\textbf{Implications for the Elicitation Methods. The}
\par
\textbf{above explanations are in line with the findings of the}
\par
\textbf{experiments. Thus a possible interpretation of our find-}
\par
\textbf{ings is as follows: The TO method has elicited curvature}
\par
\textbf{of utility for life duration and for money. In the domains}
\par
\textbf{of our experiments, the two curvatures happen to be}
\par
\textbf{similar. The CE method is distorted by the certainty ef-}
\par
\textbf{fect, which is more pronounced in the life duration-}
\par
\textbf{experiment than in the monetary experiment. Therefore,}
\par
\textbf{the CE elicitations in the life duration-experiment deviate}
\par
\textbf{significantly from the TO elicitations and also from the CE}
\par
\textbf{elicitations in the monetary experiment. The PE method is}
\par
\textbf{subject to probability distortion, as is the CE method, and}
\par
\textbf{therefore exhibits similar characteristics. In addition, the}
\par
\textbf{PE method is distorted by response mode effects so that}
\par
\textbf{its results deviate more from EU than the CE results.}
\par
\textbf{Subjects are more risk averse for life years than for}
\par
\textbf{money. While the classical EU conclusion would be that}
\par
\textbf{utility for life duration is more curved than for money,}
\par
\textbf{our analysis concludes that curvature of utility is similar}
\par
\textbf{in the two experiments. The difference lies in the devi-}
\par
\textbf{ation from EU, which is more pronounced in the life-}
\par
\textbf{duration experiment.}
\par
\textbf{To conclude, deviations from EU seem to affect the}
\par
\textbf{CE and PE results while the TO method seems to be}
\par
\textbf{insensitive to such deviations. A theoretical foundation}
\par
\textbf{for this claim is derived in the following section.}
