\textbf{5. Discussion: Advantages of the}
\par
\textbf{Trade-off Method over Other}
\par
\textbf{Methods}
\par
\textbf{We already mentioned the validity problem of direct}
\par
\textbf{scaling. An additional problem is that the results of di-}
\par
\textbf{rect scaling seem to be more susceptible to domain and}
\par
\textbf{framing effects (Nord 1992, p. 560 and Fischer 1995).}
\par
\textbf{We shall not discuss direct scaling further.}
\par
\textbf{Instead, we turn to the PE, CE, and lottery-equivalent}
\par
\textbf{methods. As these methods assume EU, they are sus-}
\par
\textbf{ceptible to the many violations of EU that have been}
\par
\textbf{reported in the literature. In particular, they use prob-}
\par
\textbf{abilities in the calculations and require precise knowl-}
\par
\textbf{edge of those probabilities. This assumes that clients un-}
\par
\textbf{derstand and correctly perceive probabilities, which has}
\par
\textbf{been a central problem in utility elicitation. Conse-}
\par
\textbf{quently, these methods cannot be used for events with}
\par
\textbf{unknown or ambiguous probabilities. As pointed out}
\par
\textbf{above, the TO method can be used in such situations.}
\par
\textbf{To avoid the distortions due to the certainty effect,}
\par
\textbf{variations of the CE and PE method have been proposed}
\par
\textbf{in which a client compares two lotteries that both in-}
\par
\textbf{volve risk, and then substitutes one of the outcomes or}
\par
\textbf{one of the probabilities to obtain indifference (Farquhar}
\par
\textbf{1984). The most well-known variation is the lottery-}
\par
\textbf{equivalent method introduced by McCord and de Neuf-}
\par
\textbf{ville (1986). It uses indifferences (X, p; 0) (x, q; 0), for}
\par
\textbf{O * p * 1 and 0 * q * 1, to elicit utility. Thus it reduces}
\par
\textbf{the disturbing effects of probability distortion but does}
\par
\textbf{not entirely discard it, and still requires precise knowl-}
\par
\textbf{edge of probability.}
\par
\textbf{An earlier study that also avoided the certainty effect}
\par
\textbf{was Davidson et al. (1957), who formalized and applied}
\par
\textbf{the early ideas of Ramsey (193 1). They used events with}
\par
\textbf{probability 2 (elicited from preference-symmetry) and}
\par
\textbf{used indifferences (X, -; x) - (Y, -; y) to elicit equalities}
\par
\textbf{u(X) - u(Y) = u(x) - u(y). Six outcomes were elicited}
\par
\textbf{that are equally spaced in utility. For each subject, only}
\par
\textbf{one choice was between a certain outcome and a lottery,}
\par
\textbf{all other questions concerned two-lottery choices. Ex-}
\par
\textbf{tensive cross-checkings were done. Next, by means of}
\par
\textbf{the elicited utilities, subjective probabilities of events}
\par
\textbf{were elicited.}
\par
\textbf{Note that the probability - is crucial in the Ramsey}
\par
\textbf{method. Prospect theory suggests that the obtained util-}
\par
\textbf{ity values will still overestimate the degree of risk aver-}
\par
\textbf{sion because the probability - is transformed downward}
\par
\textbf{by the average subject.}
\par
\textbf{Like the methods described above, the TO method}
\par
\textbf{avoids the certainty effect by using choices between two}
\par
\textbf{gambles rather than choices between a gamble and a}
\par
\textbf{certain outcome. In addition, it does not only reduce,}
\par
\textbf{but completely circumvents errors due to probability}
\par
\textbf{distortion. A disadvantage of "two-gamble" ap-}
\par
\textbf{proaches is that processing two gambles is somewhat}
\par
\textbf{more complicated than processing a gamble and a cer-}
\par
\textbf{tain outcome. We think, however, that the TO questions}
\par
\textbf{appeal to a natural intuition as subjects make decisions}
\par
\textbf{by weighing positive and negative arguments: they are}
\par
\textbf{MANAGEMENT SCIENCE/Vol. 42, No. 8, August 1996 1145WAKKER AND DENEFFE}
\par
\textbf{Eliciting von Neumann-Morgenstern Utilities}
\par
\textbf{asked how much they need to gain in one event to}
\par
\textbf{match a loss in another event. As a result, we expect}
\par
\textbf{that the TO method will discourage the use of ad hoc}
\par
\textbf{decision heuristics (von Winterfeldt and Edwards 1986,}
\par
\textbf{?10.3; Tversky et al. 1988; Fischer 1995).}
\par
\textbf{Another advantage of the TO method is that gambles}
\par
\textbf{with specified probabilities need not be invoked. Such}
\par
\textbf{gambles may feel artificial and are problematic for sub-}
\par
\textbf{jects who are not familiar with probabilities. The elici-}
\par
\textbf{tation questions under the TO method can refer to}
\par
\textbf{events that are of interest to the client ("X if the market}
\par
\textbf{goes up, r if it does not"). Probabilities of such events}
\par
\textbf{are usually not known (contrary to the probabilities of}
\par
\textbf{lottery-wheel events). Dyckman and Salomon (1972)}
\par
\textbf{have suggested that artificial lotteries lead to distor-}
\par
\textbf{tions. They found that utility functions elicited by ran-}
\par
\textbf{dom devices such as colored chips in a box were rather}
\par
\textbf{different, usually displaying more risk-aversion than}
\par
\textbf{those based on simulations of actual decision situations.}
\par
\textbf{It had been known before that extraneous random}
\par
\textbf{devices with known probabilities are not needed to ax-}
\par
\textbf{iomatize EU. The first demonstration had been given by}
\par
\textbf{Savage in 1954.8 The TO method provides a similar re-}
\par
\textbf{sult for utility elicitation.}
\par
\textbf{The TO method is also well-suited for correcting mea-}
\par
\textbf{surement errors because cross-checkings are easily de-}
\par
\textbf{veloped. For example, for any reference outcomes r, R}
\par
\textbf{by means of which xo, . . . , xn are elicited, we can fix an}
\par
\textbf{outcome s that is different from r and ask the client for}
\par
\textbf{the outcome S {$>$} s such that the gambles (x1, A; s) and}
\par
\textbf{(xo, A; S) are indifferent. Then we can use s, S instead}
\par
\textbf{of the reference outcomes r, R and verify the indiffer-}
\par
\textbf{ences (xi,1, A; s) (xi, A; S). Under EU these indiffer-}
\par
\textbf{ences should hold for all i.}
\par
\textbf{Suppose for example that in the experiment in ?3 we}
\par
\textbf{find (x1, A; 0) (xo, A; 2) for a subject, where A de-}
\par
\textbf{scribes the event that the patient carries disease 1. Then}
\par
\textbf{8 The earlier attempt of Ramsey (1931) was incomplete, the approach}
\par
\textbf{of de Finetti (1937) had the drawback of assuming that utilities were}
\par
\textbf{known, and the approaches of von Neumann and Morgenstern (1944)}
\par
\textbf{and Anscombe and Aumann (1963) had the drawback of assuming}
\par
\textbf{given probabilities. Recent axiomatizations that avoid Savage's re-}
\par
\textbf{quirement of infinite state spaces are given by Wakker (1984, 1989,}
\par
\textbf{1993) and Gul (1992).}
\par
\textbf{we can take s = 0, S = 2, and verify the indifferences}
\par
\textbf{(x2, A; 0) - (x1, A; 2), (X3, A; 0) -(x2, A; 2), and (X4, A;}
\par
\textbf{0) - (X3, A; 2). If the client never deviates from EU then}
\par
\textbf{these indifferences should all hold. In practice, they can}
\par
\textbf{be used to independently measure the standard se-}
\par
\textbf{quence and can help to improve the accuracy of mea-}
\par
\textbf{surement.}
\par
\textbf{Note that the presence of inconsistencies in an indi-}
\par
\textbf{vidual's choices is a major reason for the existence of}
\par
\textbf{elicitation methods and decision analysis. Elicitation}
\par
\textbf{methods can help detect and remove inconsistencies}
\par
\textbf{that are due to human error. Prescriptive decision anal-}
\par
\textbf{ysis asks a client to resolve the inconsistencies detected}
\par
\textbf{by cross-checkings, rather than accept and study them}
\par
\textbf{through error theories as done in descriptive ap-}
\par
\textbf{proaches (Laskey and Fischer 1987, ?6.3). In addition,}
\par
\textbf{clients find utility elicitation more acceptable when they}
\par
\textbf{are informed of their inconsistencies and are encour-}
\par
\textbf{aged to modify their expressed preferences (Kimbrough}
\par
\textbf{and Weber 1994).}
\par
\textbf{Larger utility intervals can be used for cross-checking.}
\par
\textbf{If in the experiments of ?3 we were to find (x2, A; 0)}
\par
\textbf{- (xo, A; 5) for a subject, then under EU we should also}
\par
\textbf{find (X3, A; 0) - (x1, A; 5) and (X4, A; 0) - (x2, A; 5).}
\par
\textbf{These indifferences could then be used for a cross-}
\par
\textbf{checking. Note that we used zero years versus five}
\par
\textbf{years given not-A, instead of two years versus five}
\par
\textbf{years as in ?3. One can also check for standard-}
\par
\textbf{sequences conditional on other events or other verbal}
\par
\textbf{or numerical uncertainties. For instance, one can in-}
\par
\textbf{terchange the events A and not-A, and use indiffer-}
\par
\textbf{ences of the sort (t, A; xi+1) , (T, A; xi), for i = 1, . . .,}
\par
\textbf{n - 1, to elicit the standard sequence and for further}
\par
\textbf{cross-checkings. A "top down" elicitation can be}
\par
\textbf{used as an alternative. Then one starts with xn, and}
\par
\textbf{uses indifferences (xi, A; r) - (xj-1, A; R) to elicit xn1,}
\par
\textbf{... I xO.}
\par
\textbf{The TO method is also suited for theoretical work,}
\par
\textbf{and can be used to reformulate an axiomatic foundation}
\par
\textbf{of EU described by Wakker (1984, 1989) and Wakker}
\par
\textbf{and Tversky (1993). There EU was axiomatized by a}
\par
\textbf{"TO consistency" axiom. From that axiomatization it}
\par
\textbf{easily follows that a person violates EU if and only if}
\par
\textbf{inconsistencies can be produced by the TO elicitation}
\par
\textbf{procedure. In practice, EU will be discarded if the de-}
\par
\textbf{1146 MANAGEMENT SCIENCE/Vol. 42, No. 8, August 1996WAKKER AND DENEFFE}
\par
\textbf{Eliciting von Neumann-Morgenstern Utilities}
\par
\textbf{viations from expected utility, uncovered by cross-}
\par
\textbf{checkings, are too large.}
\par
\textbf{Another feature of the TO method is that it strongly}
\par
\textbf{appeals to the intuition of strength of preference. The}
\par
\textbf{indifferences (xo, A; 5) (x1, A; 2) and (xi, A; 5) (xi,}
\par
\textbf{A; 2) in the experiments, for i = 1, 2, 3, suggest that the}
\par
\textbf{strength of preference of xi+1 over xi is as large as the}
\par
\textbf{strength of preference of x1 over xo. These indifferences}
\par
\textbf{are used to elicit corresponding equalities of utility dif-}
\par
\textbf{ferences. Still, the elicited utilities have been derived}
\par
\textbf{solely from "ordinal" indifferences and are not suscep-}
\par
\textbf{tible to the methodological criticisms of approaches that}
\par
\textbf{take strength of preference as a directly observable}
\par
\textbf{primitive. The elicited utilities do have full validity as}
\par
\textbf{vNM utilities. Thus the method may provide a bridge}
\par
\textbf{between risky cardinal utility and "value functions"}
\par
\textbf{that are to represent strengths of preferences (Dyer and}
\par
\textbf{Sarin 1982, Barron et al. 1984, Wakker 1984).}
\par
\textbf{For simplicity, we presented the TO method for quan-}
\par
\textbf{titative outcomes. It can also be used when outcomes}
\par
\textbf{are not quantified. Whatever the outcomes R, r and X,}
\par
\textbf{x, Y, y are in Equation (2), the indifferences elicit the}
\par
\textbf{equality U(X) - U(x) = U(Y) - U(y). The outcomes}
\par
\textbf{need not be final and definite, but may involve uncer-}
\par
\textbf{tainty and risk. Some flexibility concerning the outcome}
\par
\textbf{space is, however, required. In axiomatic analyses, a}
\par
\textbf{topological "connectedness" condition (Wakker 1989)}
\par
\textbf{or an algebraic "solvability" condition (Wakker 1991)}
\par
\textbf{suffice. For the TO elicitation procedure in ?2 it is nec-}
\par
\textbf{essary that the outcomes xo, x1, ..., xn, satisfying the}
\par
\textbf{required indifferences are available. A similar richness}
\par
\textbf{condition is required for the CE method. The PE method}
\par
\textbf{does not require richness of outcomes, but instead re-}
\par
\textbf{quires richness of the conceivable probabilities, which}
\par
\textbf{seems to be more convenient.}
\par
\textbf{The TO method provides alternative means of elicit-}
\par
\textbf{ing qualitative properties of utility. For instance, dimin-}
\par
\textbf{ishing marginal utility ("risk aversion") can be inferred}
\par
\textbf{if and only if all elicited standard sequences xo, . . ., Xn}
\par
\textbf{have increasing step sizes xj+1 - xj. The utility is a}
\par
\textbf{linear/exponential function if and only if standard se-}
\par
\textbf{quences are invariant under addition of a constant}
\par
\textbf{("constant absolute risk aversion," Keeney and Raiffa}
\par
\textbf{1976). Utility is a log/power function if and only if elic-}
\par
\textbf{ited standard sequences are invariant under multipli-}
\par
\textbf{cation by positive constants ("constant relative risk}
\par
\textbf{aversion," Keeney and Raiffa 1976). These results also}
\par
\textbf{hold true for rank-dependent utility and cumulative}
\par
\textbf{prospect theory. Characterizations of parametric fami-}
\par
\textbf{lies of utility for nonexpected utility models have been}
\par
\textbf{studied by Miyamoto (1988), Wakker and Tversky}
\par
\textbf{(1993), Miyamoto and Wakker (1996), Dyckerhoff}
\par
\textbf{(1994), and Fishburn (1995).}
\par
\textbf{In a mathematical sense, the TO method can be con-}
\par
\textbf{sidered to be a special case of multi-attribute utility the-}
\par
\textbf{ory where dimensions now refer to different resolutions}
\par
\textbf{of uncertainty. A topic that has been discussed exten-}
\par
\textbf{sively in multiattribute utility is the issue of distortions}
\par
\textbf{of attribute weights (Weber et al. 1988, Tversky et al.}
\par
\textbf{1988, Fischer and Hawkins 1993, von Nitzsch and We-}
\par
\textbf{ber 1993). A recent survey has been given by Borcherd-}
\par
\textbf{ing et al. (1995). In our context the attribute weights are}
\par
\textbf{the probabilities. These distortions of attribute weights /}
\par
\textbf{probabilities do not by themselves constitute a problem}
\par
\textbf{for the TO method. Only when the distortion effects}
\par
\textbf{change during the elicitation process, can they affect the}
\par
\textbf{results of the TO method. As an example, in the exper-}
\par
\textbf{iments we used matching judgments. It has been found}
\par
\textbf{that these lead to an overweighting of the dimension}
\par
\textbf{where the matching is to be done, and to excessive at-}
\par
\textbf{tention to the scale to be matched. However, this dis-}
\par
\textbf{tortion is the same for all revealed values, and therefore}
\par
\textbf{does not affect the elicited utility values.}
\par
\textbf{6. Disadvantages of the Tradeoff}
\par
\textbf{Method}
\par
\textbf{A disadvantage of the TO method is that it is somewhat}
\par
\textbf{more laborious than existing methods. In each choice,}
\par
\textbf{clients are confronted with two gambles rather than}
\par
\textbf{with a gamble and a certain outcome. Therefore, sub-}
\par
\textbf{jects will initially find the TO method harder to under-}
\par
\textbf{stand. This was indeed found in the experiments. We}
\par
\textbf{expect, however, that after some practice subjects will}
\par
\textbf{find the TO method easier to deal with than the CE or}
\par
\textbf{PE methods. A first reason is that the new matching}
\par
\textbf{value that is elicited per choice question is always based}
\par
\textbf{on a comparison with the same "reference" outcomes}
\par
\textbf{under the opposite event. In the life-duration experi-}
\par
\textbf{ment, the reference outcomes were always two years}
\par
\textbf{MANAGEMENT SCIENCE/Vol. 42, No. 8, August 1996 1147WAKKER AND DENEFFE}
\par
\textbf{Eliciting von Neumann-Morgenstern Utilities}
\par
\textbf{and five years under disease 2, in the monetary exper-}
\par
\textbf{iment they were 10 and 80 under candidate 1. A second}
\par
\textbf{reason is that attention is focused on utility.}
\par
\textbf{Von Winterfeldt and Edwards (1986, ?10.3) pointed}
\par
\textbf{out that the CE method has the disadvantage of induc-}
\par
\textbf{ing subjects to anchor on expected value calculations,}
\par
\textbf{which can distort their responses. The TO method may}
\par
\textbf{have a similar disadvantage because an easy response}
\par
\textbf{strategy for subjects is to simply let the difference be-}
\par
\textbf{tween x; and xjFl be the same for all j. In our experi-}
\par
\textbf{ments, 16 of the 81 subjects used the mentioned re-}
\par
\textbf{sponse strategy in the TO questions, while 20 subjects}
\par
\textbf{resorted to expected value calculations in the CE ques-}
\par
\textbf{tions.}
\par
\textbf{The TO method is also more laborious because, to}
\par
\textbf{elicit n - 1 utility values,9 n indifferences must be ob-}
\par
\textbf{served. The CE and PE method need one observation}
\par
\textbf{less to elicit the same number of utilities. This is so be-}
\par
\textbf{cause the CE and PE methods assume one additional}
\par
\textbf{parameter given: the probability. Often a specific para-}
\par
\textbf{metric family has already been deemed appropriate for}
\par
\textbf{the utility, and utility elicitation is only used to deter-}
\par
\textbf{mine the parameter(s) of the parametric family. For in-}
\par
\textbf{stance, if a power family u(x) = xr is chosen, then only}
\par
\textbf{a few preference indifferences need to be observed for}
\par
\textbf{the assessment of the ("risk") parameter r (for multiat-}
\par
\textbf{tribute utility theory, compare Kirkwood and Sarin}
\par
\textbf{1980). The TO method can also be used to identify such}
\par
\textbf{parameters, again at the expense of additional questions}
\par
\textbf{but with more robustness.}
\par
\textbf{Another disadvantage of the TO method is that re-}
\par
\textbf{sponses are "chained": previously elicited values must}
\par
\textbf{be invoked to elicit new utility values. The elicitation of}
\par
\textbf{X3 requires the value x2 as an input, etc. Responses are}
\par
\textbf{also chained in the bisection version of the CE method.}
\par
\textbf{They are neither in the basic CE method nor in the PE}
\par
\textbf{method. The problem with chaining is that the elicita-}
\par
\textbf{tion procedure is more complicated and that errors}
\par
\textbf{propagate. The error in the elicitation of, say, the ninth}
\par
\textbf{value can be expected to be three times larger than the}
\par
\textbf{error in the elicitation of the first value if errors are in-}
\par
\textbf{dependent and identically distributed. Cross-checkings}
\par
\textbf{can, however, easily be developed for the TO method}
\par
\textbf{to reduce those errors.}
\par
\textbf{Finally, only one boundary value can be fixed for the}
\par
\textbf{TO method, such as the minimal starting level xo in the}
\par
\textbf{description of ?1, or a maximal starting level in the "top}
\par
\textbf{down" method described in ?5. The decision analyst}
\par
\textbf{cannot entirely control the location of the other bound-}
\par
\textbf{ary value that will be elicited.}
\par
\textbf{7. Conclusion}
\par
\textbf{This paper has discussed the tradeoff method for elic-}
\par
\textbf{iting von Neumann-Morgenstern utilities. The method}
\par
\textbf{is robust against distortions of probabilities and can be}
\par
\textbf{applied when probabilities are ambiguous or unknown.}
\par
\textbf{Probabilities can even be presented verbally. Also, the}
\par
\textbf{method can be adapted to rank-dependent nonexpected}
\par
\textbf{utility. Experiments suggest that the tradeoff method is}
\par
\textbf{less affected by deviations from EU than the CE and PE}
\par
\textbf{methods. The primary application of the tradeoff}
\par
\textbf{method lies in prescriptive decision analysis, where ra-}
\par
\textbf{tional and truly representative utilities are to be con-}
\par
\textbf{structed under guidance of a decision analyst, and}
\par
\textbf{where the method appeals to a rational decision pro-}
\par
\textbf{cess: the weighing of arguments.10}
