\par
\textbf{Judgemental and statistical time series forecasting:}
\par
\textbf{a review of the literature}
\par
\textbf{Richard Webby*, Marcus O'Connor}
\par
\emph{School of Information Systems, University of New South Wales, Kensington, N.S.W. 2052, Australia}
\par
\textbf{Abstract}
\par
This paper reviews the literature on the contributions of judgemental methods to the forecasting process. Using a
\par
contingent approach, it first reviews the empirical studies comparing the performance of judgemental and statistical
\par
methods and finds emphasis for the importance of judgement in providing contextual information for the final
\par
forecasts. It then examines four methods of integrating contextual information with the output of statistical models.
\par
Although judgemental adjustment of statistical forecasts is a viable alternative, simple combination of forecasts
\par
may offer superior benefits. Promising developments can also be gained from the use of decomposition principles in
\par
the integration process.
\par
\emph{Keywords: }Time series forecasting; Judgemental forecasting; Statistical forecasting; Forecast combination; Judgemental
\par
adjustment; Judgemental decomposition
\par
\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
In the past decade, opinion has been divided
\par
as to the role of judgement in time series
\par
forecasting. Some (e.g. Makridakis, 1988) sug-
\par
gested that judgement cannot be trusted. Others
\par
(e.g. Dalrymple, 1987) provided evidence that
\par
judgement was strongly preferred as the most
\par
important method of practical sales forecasting.
\par
In addition, studies of judgemental forecast
\par
accuracy have shown the credibility of simple
\par
eyeballing (e.g. Lawrence et al., 1985) and the
\par
need for judgement in situations involving con-
\par
textual information (e.g. Edmundson et al.,
\par
1988).
\par
This paper seeks to focus the debate by
\par
* Corresponding author.
\par
0169-2070/96/\$15.00 \copyright{} 1996 Elsevier Science B.V. All rights reserved
\par
\emph{SSD1 }0169-2070(95)00644-3
\par
reviewing the empirical evidence regarding the
\par
role of judgement in time series forecasting. We
\par
examine studies that compared judgemental and
\par
statistical forecast accuracy, as well as studies
\par
where judgement was integrated with statistical
\par
or structured forecasting methods. To do this in
\par
a unified manner, we develop a contingent
\par
framework within which studies can be com-
\par
pared and contrasted along a number of task,
\par
human and environmental dimensions.
\par
Section 2 presents the studies that have com-
\par
pared judgemental and statistical forecast ac-
\par
curacy. We begin by establishing the practical
\par
validity of this comparison (i.e. we ascertain that
\par
both judgement and statistical methods are used
\par
in practice) and then proceed with a factor-by-
\par
factor review of the literature along the contin-
\par
gent dimensions referred to above.92 \emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118}
\par
Section 3 then extends the results from Section
\par
2 by examining statistics and judgement as com-
\par
plementary approaches rather than competing
\par
approaches. We suggest that statistical (objec-
\par
tive) and judgemental (subjective) methods
\par
could perhaps be synthesised to gain the dual
\par
benefits of objective mechanical precision and
\par
subjective human interpretative abilities. Four
\par
main approaches to integrating subjective and
\par
objective forecasting are classified: model specifi-
\par
cation, forecast combination, judgemental ad-
\par
justment and judgemental decomposition. The
\par
literature is reviewed within those four
\par
categories and the pros and cons of the ap-
\par
proaches are debated with particular reference to
\par
their accuracy, efficiency, practicality and va-
\par
lidity.
\par
\textbf{2. Subjective versus objective forecasting}
\par
\textbf{methods}
\par
This section begins by briefly discussing the
\par
survey research documenting the practical use of
\par
subjective forecasting methods compared to ob-
\par
jective forecasting methods. The aim of this
\par
exercise is to confirm that both subjective and
\par
objective methods are prevalent in practice, and
\par
hence establish the validity of their empirical
\par
comparison. The section proceeds by using a
\par
contingent methodology to assess the accuracy of
\par
subjective versus objective time series forecast-
\par
ing in the empirical literature. This evaluation
\par
attempts to determine whether the effectiveness
\par
of subjective versus objective methods is depen-
\par
dent upon task, human and environmental dif-
\par
ferences (such as the availability or otherwise of
\par
contextual information).
\par
\emph{2.1. Practical use of subjective versus objective}
\par
\emph{methods}
\par
Many surveys have examined the relative use
\par
of subjective and objective methods in practice
\par
(Cerullo and Avila, 1975; Lawrence, 1983; Men-
\par
tzer and Cox, 1984; Sparkes and McHugh, 1984;
\par
Dalrymple, 1987; Taranto, 1989; Batchelor and
\par
Dua, 1990; Sanders and Manrodt, 1994). The
\par
evidence documented in those surveys indicates
\par
a remarkedly consistent finding-that subjective
\par
techniques generally represent about 40-50\% of
\par
the total techniques used in time series forecast-
\par
ing. When comparing early and recent surveys,
\par
there appears to have been little diminution in
\par
the role of judgement, contrary to what one
\par
might expect given the emergence of desk-top
\par
computing and forecasting software. In consider-
\par
ing this evidence, it is important to note that the
\par
majority of the respondents in those surveys
\par
were either forecasting experts or came from
\par
large organisations. Given that subjective meth-
\par
ods are used more frequently in small firms
\par
(Dalrymple, 1987; Taranto, 1989), it seems the
\par
general prevalence of judgement in forecasting
\par
may even be somewhat greater than indicated by
\par
the above surveys.
\par
It seems therefore that subjective and objec-
\par
tive methods are \emph{both }commonly used in prac-
\par
tice, and we can proceed with a comparison of
\par
the empirical accuracy of subjective and objec-
\par
tive forecasting methods, knowing that it is
\par
practically relevant as well as academically inter-
\par
esting.
\par
\emph{2.2. Contingent analysis methodology}
\par
The evaluation of the empirical studies com-
\par
paring the accuracy of subjective and objective
\par
methods of time series forecasting uses a contin-
\par
gent framework. The strategy adopted is to
\par
attempt to provide a comprehensive multi-factor
\par
review of the literature, rather than focus on a
\par
particular factor in isolation, since comparisons
\par
made across studies cannot control for multiple
\par
factors. Although the provision of contextual
\par
information is considered to be an important
\par
factor, it would be deficient to analyse its effects
\par
across a variety of empirical studies without
\par
considering the effects of other mitigating fac-
\par
tors.
\par
The factors considered are as follows. First,
\par
the effects of time series characteristics and
\par
presentation on the relative accuracy of the
\par
human judge are examined. Characteristics of
\par
the time \emph{series }include the \emph{periodicity }of the data,
\par
\emph{trend, seasonality, noise, instability, }number of\emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118 }93
\par
\emph{historicals }and number of \emph{forecasts }required.
\par
Aspects of the presentation of the time series
\par
include whether the series were presented in
\par
\emph{graphical }or tabular form and the nature of the
\par
\emph{feedback }provided to the judges. Second, the
\par
effects of subject/environment characteristics are
\par
considered. The situational characteristics of the
\par
\emph{judges }that are deemed important to the task
\par
include the \emph{experience }of the forecasters, the
\par
extent to which the judges made the forecasts
\par
with an understanding of the \emph{context }or nature of
\par
the series and an assessment of the \emph{motivation }of
\par
the judges in the task.
\par
The following sections discuss the empirical
\par
evidence for each of those contingent factors. To
\par
supplement and support this discussion, a set of
\par
reference tables is included in an Appendix to
\par
the paper, which provides further detail about
\par
the studies. Details of the empirical studies
\par
discussed in the following sections can be found
\par
in the reference tables.
\par
\emph{2.3. Empirical evidence}
\par
\emph{2.3.1. Series\textasciitilde task characteristics}
\par
\emph{2.3.1.1. Trend}
\par
In extrapolating trends, the human judge may
\par
revert to the mean, continue or enhance the
\par
trend, or apply an intermediate strategy and
\par
dampen the trend. The tendency to continue a
\par
trend may be enhanced by its length and consis-
\par
tency (Andreassen and Kraus, 1990) and by the
\par
presence of confirming news (De Bondt, 1990).
\par
Empirical work on line fitting by Mosteller et al.
\par
(1981) indicates that judgemental fitting is highly
\par
accurate with both upward and downward series,
\par
compared with linear regression. In that study,
\par
subjects may have tended to overstate slopes
\par
slightly, but this was not significant, and noise
\par
had no influence on trend identification.
\par
Judgemental modelling of trend in \emph{real }series is
\par
also highly accurate (Edmundson, 1987; Winton
\par
and Edmundson, 1993), and it seems that judges
\par
may dampen these trends. Studies of the ex-
\par
trapolation of exponential growth (Wagenaar
\par
and Sagaria, 1975; Timmers and Wagenaar,
\par
1977) have shown that people greatly underesti-
\par
mate exponential processes (although note the
\par
criticism of their normative model by Jones,
\par
1984), but exponential growth is rare in econ-
\par
omic series because many are affected by multi-
\par
ple causal forces (see Collopy and Armstrong,
\par
1993).
\par
Thus, based on the related literature, judge-
\par
ment can be expected to be reasonably accurate
\par
with trending series in comparison to statistical
\par
methods. However, the outcome of the com-
\par
parison will be dependent on the type of statisti-
\par
cal technique used to model trend-some tech-
\par
niques are highly accurate, others are likely to be
\par
particularly weak in handling trend.
\par
Studies of subjective versus objective time
\par
series forecasting, as opposed to line fitting,
\par
show that there is a distinct tendency for subjects
\par
to dampen upward and downward trends (Eg-
\par
gleton, 1982; Lawrence and Makridakis, 1989;
\par
Sanders, 1992). Lawrence and Makridakis (1989)
\par
found that forecasts of downward series were
\par
dampened significantly more than forecasts of
\par
upward series. O'Connor (1993) also observed
\par
the forecasting accuracy of downward sloping
\par
series was significantly inferior to upward sloping
\par
series. Sanders (1992) showed that judgemental
\par
forecasts of upward sloping series were relatively
\par
less accurate than forecasts of fiat series, al-
\par
though statistical forecasts of trending series
\par
were also worse.
\par
Thus, there may be a weak association be-
\par
tween the presence of trend and comparative
\par
judgemental inaccuracy, but all the above studies
\par
were conducted using artificial or manipulated
\par
data, and may not apply in real life situations
\par
which often require trends to be dampened
\par
(Gardner and McKenzie, 1985). The assumption
\par
of constancy made by researchers may not be
\par
valid, and the supposed 'bias' of trend dampen-
\par
ing may actually be appropriate behaviour.
\par
\emph{2.3.1.2. Seasonality}
\par
A substantial body of research suggests that
\par
humans possess good pattern recognition skills,
\par
in fields such as music, object perception and
\par
speech recognition (e.g. Simon and Sumner,
\par
1968). A time series pattern is arguably much
\par
less complex than the patterns in those tasks, but94 \emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118}
\par
nevertheless Edmundson (1987) found that
\par
humans could perform as well as automatic
\par
mechanisms in seasonal identification.
\par
The empirical evidence suggests that, in
\par
studies involving artificial series, judgemental
\par
performance deteriorates with high seasonality
\par
(Adam and Ebert, 1976; Sanders, 1992), and
\par
high signal (Eggleton, 1982; Lawrence and
\par
O'Connor, 1992). However, the accuracy of the
\par
statistical techniques also declines, and it is
\par
difficult to tell from these studies whether or not
\par
judgement is comparatively worse when the
\par
series contain high seasonality.
\par
Lawrence et al. (1985) showed that, with real
\par
time series, judgemental forecasts of \emph{seasonal}
\par
series were \emph{more }accurate than judgemental
\par
forecasts of non-seasonal series. However, there
\par
may have been differences other than seasonality
\par
involved, e.g. trend, noise and instability. The
\par
study compared judgemental forecasts with
\par
statistical forecasts, finding that the best statisti-
\par
cal method was generally more accurate than
\par
judgement, except in the case of tabular seasonal
\par
series forecasted by the authors.
\par
Edmundson (1987) conducted a discriminant
\par
analysis in order to determine the time series
\par
characteristics that allow us to differentiate be-
\par
tween judgemental and statistical forecasts in
\par
monthly series. He found that a purely seasonal
\par
metric was not statistically important, but a
\par
'signal to noise' metric was significant in dis-
\par
criminating between judgemental and de-
\par
seasonalised single exponential smoothing (DSE)
\par
forecasts over short (1-6 months) horizons.
\par
Edmundson concluded that judgement had rela-
\par
tively greater difficulty with a strong stable
\par
signal, counter to expectation. However, the
\par
'signal to noise' metric was not a good discrimin-
\par
ant between judgemental forecasts and Box-
\par
Jenkins forecasts. This implies that this metric
\par
may be associated more with DSE accuracy than
\par
judgemental inaccuracy. In other words, it may
\par
simply identify series where DSE performance
\par
differs, and hence those series where there is
\par
room for judgement to be better.
\par
Thus, the presence of seasonality is associated
\par
with higher forecast error for judgemental and
\par
statistical methods. It is relatively difficult to
\par
determine whether, with different levels of
\par
seasonality, subjective methods are comparative-
\par
ly better than objective methods.
\par
\emph{2.3.1.3. Noise}
\par
Noise refers to the random or white noise in a
\par
series, as distinct from instability which is related
\par
to the presence of discontinuities. The findings
\par
from studies in related fields suggest that high
\par
random noise leads to comparatively poor
\par
human performance (Beach and Peterson, 1967;
\par
Laestadius, 1970).
\par
The evidence in the time series forecasting
\par
literature tends to confirm that random noise has
\par
a decremental effect on the forecast accuracy of
\par
judgemental techniques (Adam and Ebert, 1976;
\par
Eggleton, 1982; Sanders, 1992; O'Connor et al.,
\par
1993). However, statistical methods also fare
\par
poorly. Determining which method is best for
\par
series with different noise levels is again not
\par
straightforward. Lawrence et al. (1985) showed
\par
that students with graphs were more accurate
\par
than statistical methods for 'macro' series, which
\par
would be expected to have relatively low noise,
\par
but not for 'micro' series, which would probably
\par
have high noise. However, the micro series may
\par
have also exhibited higher instability (see next
\par
section). The forecasts of the researchers them-
\par
selves in that study were more accurate with
\par
both types of series when they were presented in
\par
graphical form. Perhaps one reason for the
\par
problem that novices had with noisy series was
\par
because they may have read too much signal in
\par
the random perturbations (O'Connor et al.,
\par
1993).
\par
Thus, it is unclear whether subjective methods
\par
perform better or worse than objective methods
\par
when random noise is high.
\par
\emph{2.3.1.4. Instability}
\par
Instability refers to the presence of discon-
\par
tinuities or temporal disturbances in the series.
\par
The literature on 'bootstrapping' suggests that
\par
models outperform man, provided the data are
\par
relatively stable (Kleinmuntz, 1990). However,
\par
when 'broken-leg c u e s '1 a r e available (which are
\par
A broken-leg cue refers to an unusual important piece of
\par
information whose presence would dramatically alter judge-
\par
ment compared to a model of that judgement (Kleinmuntz,
\par
1990).\emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118 }95
\par
not able to be incorporated into the models),
\par
human judgement is most effective. This implies
\par
that judges will outperform models when they
\par
have contextual information to help them com-
\par
prehend discontinuities in series. In the absence
\par
of these explanatory cues, human pattern recog-
\par
nition skills may still allow the judge to notice
\par
discontinuities in the series, and hence discount
\par
them when making the forecast.
\par
In forecasting artificial series containing dis-
\par
continuities, judges generally perform \emph{worse}
\par
than statistical methods (Sanders, 1992; O'Con-
\par
nor et al., 1993). Only in one case was judge-
\par
ment superior-when, in Sanders (1992), subjects
\par
forecast a low noise series that contained an
\par
immediate level change ('step') mid-way through
\par
the series history. However, Sanders did not test
\par
judgement against the adaptive response rate
\par
(ARR), which was found to be consistently
\par
better than single exponential smoothing (SES),
\par
and judgement, in O'Connor et al. (1993).
\par
One problem with both these studies is that
\par
the forecasts were made in the absence of
\par
contextual information. In a realistic setting,
\par
such information should help judges identify,
\par
explain and anticipate discontinuities. For exam-
\par
ple, Sanders and Ritzman (1992) determined that
\par
expert \emph{contextual }judgement was most effective
\par
for the highly variable series-they found a sig-
\par
nificant interaction effect between contextual
\par
knowledge and data variability. Edmundson et
\par
al. (1988) also found that for forecasts for prod-
\par
ucts with sporadic promotions in supermarkets
\par
(high instability), contextual knowledge was vit-
\par
ally important.
\par
Thus, it appears that judgement generally does
\par
not deal well with instability, unless contextual
\par
information is available to explain discon-
\par
tinuities.
\par
\emph{2.3.1.5. Number of historical data points}
\par
Human information processing theory
\par
(Schroder et al., 1967) suggests that there is an
\par
inverted bell-shaped relationship between infor-
\par
mation load and decision quality. The empirical
\par
work (e.g. Miller, 1956; Phelps and Shanteau,
\par
1978) on information overload suggests that
\par
anywhere between three and ten pieces of in-
\par
formation (cues) represent the limits of human
\par
information processing capacities. The source of
\par
this variation lies not only in task and subject
\par
differences, but also in the different definitions
\par
of what constitutes a 'cue' (see Wood, 1986, for a
\par
definition). For example, a time series may be
\par
considered to be a single 'configural' cue (Bruner
\par
et al., 1956), a set of cues indicative of its
\par
observable characteristics, or it may contain cues
\par
for every historical data point.
\par
There is some indication that as little as 40
\par
data points may lead to overload (Lawrence and
\par
O'Connor, 1992), although this result was inter-
\par
preted more as a failure in the assumption of
\par
constancy and hence its implication is unclear. It
\par
has been suggested that complex statistical tech-
\par
niques such as Box-Jenkins may require as many
\par
as 50 observations in the modelling process (Box
\par
and Jenkins, 1970). Parsimonious techniques
\par
require significantly fewer observations 2 (e.g. the
\par
naive requires just one historical value), but it is
\par
possible that judgement (especially \emph{contextual}
\par
judgement) would be better than even these
\par
parsimonious methods when there is little his-
\par
torical data (e.g. new product forecasting, Wind
\par
et al., 1981).
\par
\emph{2.3.1.6. Length of forecast horizon}
\par
The empirical evidence suggests that short-
\par
term forecasts are generally more accurate than
\par
long-term forecasts, in line with expectations
\par
(Lawrence et al., 1985; Brown et al., 1987;
\par
Lawrence and Makridakis, 1989; Hopwood and
\par
McKeown, 1990). This is true for both subjective
\par
and objective methods, but again it seems un-
\par
clear which method has the greater advantage
\par
for different horizon lengths.
\par
However, recent research by Lawrence et al.
\par
(1994) suggests that judgemental forecast accura-
\par
cy sometimes deteriorates with shorter forecast
\par
horizons, despite the availability of more time
\par
series history and improved contextual informa-
\par
tion. This contrasts with the findings in the
\par
earnings forecasting literature that analyst timing
\par
advantage (i.e. proximity to the earnings
\par
announcement) plays an important role in the
\par
2 See Makridakis et al. (1983, p. 556) for guidelines as to
\par
the minimum data requirements of various objective meth-
\par
ods.96 \emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118}
\par
superiority of subjective forecasts over objective
\par
methods (e.g. Brown et al., 1987).
\par
Thus, the majority of the evidence indicates
\par
that judgement performs worse than objective
\par
methods over long forecast horizons.
\par
\emph{2.3.1.7. Feedback}
\par
In general, feedback has been found to have a
\par
beneficial effect on task performance in most
\par
estimation tasks (O'Connor, 1989). The type of
\par
feedback also appears to have an important
\par
influence on task performance (Balzer et al.,
\par
1992). Outcome feedback may be less effective
\par
(Fischer, 1982) than task performance feedback
\par
(Benson and 0nkal, 1992) in probability learning
\par
tasks (i.e. presenting an actual value may be less
\par
effective than presenting a summative error
\par
measure). In time series extrapolation, there has
\par
been relatively little work done on this issue.
\par
One study, by Mackinnon and Wearing (1991),
\par
suggested that simple outcome feedback may be
\par
just as effective. However, the deterministic
\par
nature of their series might have made outcome
\par
feedback appear more effective than it would be
\par
in a normal probabilistic forecasting task (Good-
\par
win and Wright, 1993).
\par
It is difficult to report on the effect of feed-
\par
back on judgemental time series forecasting.
\par
None of the studies we found systematically
\par
varied the feedback dimension, and it was also
\par
difficult to determine the quality of feedback in
\par
these studies. There is a clear need for research
\par
on the effect of different types of feedback on
\par
judgemental forecasting performance.
\par
\emph{2.3.1.8. Graphical versus tabular data presenta-}
\par
\emph{tion}
\par
There is mixed evidence in the related litera-
\par
ture about the relative effectiveness of graphical
\par
versus tabular presentation. Some representative
\par
studies are mentioned here (see also DeSanctis,
\par
1984). Benbasat and Dexter (1985) found no
\par
performance differences between graphical and
\par
tabular reports, while DeSanctis and Jarvenpaa
\par
(1989) concluded that the value of graphics only
\par
emerged after practice. Remus (1984, 1987)
\par
found that tables were superior to graphics in
\par
environments of low complexity, but graphics
\par
were superior with intermediate complexity
\par
(Remus, 1987). Dickson et al. (1986) also found
\par
a task-related effect. They concluded that "for a
\par
task activity that involves seeing time dependent
\par
patterns in a large amount of data, graphs are a
\par
good choice of format." (p. 46).
\par
It is difficult to tell whether a graphical or
\par
tabular presentation mode is best in time series
\par
forecasting. Lawrence et al. (1985) found no
\par
significant difference between the accuracy of
\par
judgemental forecasts made with graphical ver-
\par
sus tabular displays, although they suggested that
\par
forecasts made by the table method were more
\par
'robust' (i.e. had lower standard deviation).
\par
There was also some weak support for the
\par
hypothesis that graphs were better with short
\par
horizons and tables were better in the longer
\par
term.
\par
\emph{2.3.2. Judge\textasciitilde environmental characteristics}
\par
\emph{2.3.2.1. Experience}
\par
This factor involves an assessment of the
\par
experience possessed by the judges in the time
\par
series forecasting task. This is an attempt to
\par
codify the overall competence and knowledge of
\par
the human judge. There appear to be two major
\par
aspects to forecaster experience:
\par
\emph{{$\bullet$} Technical knowledge }(Sanders and Ritzman,
\par
1992), which is knowledge about data analysis
\par
and formal forecasting procedures, and
\par
\emph{{$\bullet$} Causal knowledge, }which pertains to an
\par
understanding of the cause-effect relationships
\par
involved (i.e. the structure, as opposed to the
\par
content), and is typically gained from general
\par
industry forecasting experience (Edmundson et
\par
al., 1988). This is one aspect of 'non-time series
\par
information', which also includes 'product
\par
knowledge', which is explained under the head-
\par
ing "Context", in the next section.
\par
The above two aspects are not analysed in-
\par
dividually in this review because it is generally
\par
impossible to separate them in the studies (cf.
\par
Sanders and Ritzman, 1992).
\par
Armstrong (1985) provides a review of the
\par
value of expertise (pp. 91-96). The evidence
\par
from a variety of fields (e.g. psychology, finance,
\par
medicine) generally indicates that expertise\emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118 }97
\par
beyond a certain minimal level has little in-
\par
cremental value (cf. the value of the expert
\par
weather forecaster-Murphy and Brown, 1984).
\par
Perhaps \textasciitilde is also holds true in time series fore-
\par
casting-maybe total novices (e.g. under-
\par
graduates) will be inept at the task, but subjects
\par
with a limited amount of training (e.g.
\par
graduates) will be just as good as experts, e.g.
\par
managers (Remus, 1990).
\par
The evidence from the time series forecasting
\par
literature is, unfortunately, rather difficult to
\par
interpret because of the confounding effects of
\par
different contextual and motivational levels in
\par
the studies. It is difficult to say whether ex-
\par
perience is related to the forecast accuracy of
\par
subjects compared with judges from this over-
\par
view table. At the intraostudy level, it appears
\par
that experience (industry or technical) has no
\par
effect on forecast accuracy (Edmundson et al.,
\par
1988; Sanders and Ritzman, 1992). However
\par
those two studies varied experience within a time
\par
series only task.
\par
\emph{2.3.2.2. Context}
\par
The 'context' factor represents an assessment
\par
of the 'contextual information' available to the
\par
forecaster. Several terms have been used in the
\par
literature that are similar to the meaning in-
\par
tended here by 'contextual information', e.g.
\par
contextual knowledge (Sanders and Ritzman,
\par
1992), product knowledge (Edmundson et al.,
\par
1988) and extra-model knowledge (Pankratz,
\par
1989). The existing definitions of forecasting
\par
'context' unfortunately do not distinguish clearly
\par
between contextual information and forecaster
\par
experience. Thus 'contextual information' is de-
\par
fined here as \emph{information, other than the time}
\par
\emph{series and general experience, which helps in the}
\par
\emph{explanation, interpretation and anticipation of}
\par
\emph{time series behaviour. }The exclusion of 'general
\par
experience' from this definition relates to the
\par
forecaster's knowledge of causal relationships
\par
among variables-this knowledge was defined as
\par
part of the experience dimension. Note that this
\par
definition of contextual information encompasses
\par
the labels used to describe series (cf. Goodwin
\par
and Wright's, 1993, definition), as labels provide
\par
some context within which to interpret time
\par
series behaviour.Whether or not this context is
\par
appropriate or valid is another issue.
\par
The definition of contextual information used
\par
in this analysis can be related to similar notions
\par
in literatures other than time series forecasting:
\par
\emph{{$\bullet$} Broken-leg cues }(Meehl, 1957). As men-
\par
tioned, this term is used in the psychology and
\par
bootstrapping literatures (Kleinmuntz, 1990) to
\par
describe any dramatic changes or events which
\par
cannot be captured in a formal model. The
\par
broken-leg analogy was made to emphasise the
\par
type of unusual event that invalidates the output
\par
of a clinical model.
\par
\emph{{$\bullet$} Soft information }(Mintzberg, 1975). In his
\par
studies of managerial work, Mintzberg observed
\par
that much of the information processed by mana-
\par
gers is of an informal, verbal, qualitative or 'soft'
\par
nature. Soft information is often in the form of
\par
rumour and hearsay and is particularly cherished
\par
by managers over the 'hard' data contained in
\par
MIS reports. Managers even strive to encourage
\par
the flow of soft information to the extent of
\par
leaving their door open to invite interruptions.
\par
Mintzberg's results have been replicated and
\par
confirmed by Kurke and Aldrich (1983).
\par
According to the definition given for 'con-
\par
textual information', not all of it will consist of
\par
'soft' information but an important portion of it
\par
will.
\par
Thus, on the basis of the prior related litera-
\par
ture, subjective methods are expected to per-
\par
form significantly better than objective methods
\par
when contextual information is available.
\par
Table 1, which examines when objective and
\par
subjective methods are best across different
\par
levels of contextual knowledge, provides strong
\par
support for the above expectation. It appears
\par
that subjective methods generally perform better
\par
in the presence of contextual information. How-
\par
ever, as Appendix reference Table Al(a)-(e)
\par
indicates, it is also possible that the improved
\par
performance is related to other factors, such as
\par
experience, motivation or differences in group
\par
versus individual forecasting.
\par
Edmundson et al. (1988), and later Sanders
\par
and Ritzman (1992), confirmed that contextual
\par
information is critical in sales forecasting, much
\par
m o r e s o than expertise. Edmundson et al.98 \emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118}
