\par
There have been a number of empirical studies
\par
that generally support a link between incentives
\par
and performance. A recent study (Henry and
\par
Sniezek, 1993) related to forecasting perform-
\par
ance found that judgements of future perform-
\par
ance in an 'almanac questions' task were the
\par
most accurate when individuals perceived high
\par
levels of internal control and when monetary
\par
rewards were present.
\par
High levels of motivation are expected to have
\par
a beneficial effect on human performance, par-
\par
ticularly when contextual information is avail-
\par
able. Unfortunately in the empirical research we
\par
reviewed, it is practically impossible to separate
\par
motivation from contextual information-the
\par
situations where contextual information is avail-
\par
able are also the situations where managers are
\par
making the forecasts as an important part of
\par
their job.
\par
Edmundson et al. (1988) compared the fore-
\par
casts made for key versus non-key products
\par
(managers would probably be more motivated
\par
for the important, key products) but again other
\par
factors interfere in the analysis such as greater
\par
instability and perhaps more contextual infor-
\par
mation with the key products, from the increased
\par
number of supermarket promotions.
\par
\emph{2.4. Summary}
\par
\emph{2.3.2.3. Motivation}
\par
Motivation refers to the environmental charac-
\par
teristics that induce rigour in the application of a
\par
forecaster's selected strategy. Beach et al.
\par
(1986a) described four motivating characteristics
\par
of the forecasting environment:
\par
{$\bullet$} the extrinsic \emph{benefit }of making an accurate
\par
forecast,
\par
{$\bullet$} whether the forecast will be \emph{revisable }in the
\par
light of further information before accuracy is
\par
assessed,
\par
{$\bullet$} whether the forecast is perceived to be
\par
within the judge's area of \emph{competence, }i.e.
\par
whether the judge's reputation is on the line, and
\par
{$\bullet$} the adequacy, reliability and understan-
\par
dability of the \emph{information-motivation }will be
\par
Table 2 summarises the findings made for each
\par
of the contingent factors. It shows:
\par
{$\bullet$} the expected relationships, based on a priori
\par
reasoning and the related literature, between
\par
high levels of each factor and judgemental ac-
\par
curacy in comparison to the accuracy of objec-
\par
tive forecasting methods;
\par
{$\bullet$} the overall findings made for each relation-
\par
ship, and whether they were in agreement with
\par
the hypotheses; and
\par
{$\bullet$} the effect of the interactions between factors
\par
that were investigated in the studies.
\par
In summary, therefore, contextual information
\par
appears to be the prime determinant of
\par
judgemental superiority over statistical models.
