\par
\emph{objectivity and precision. }In the next section, we
\par
explore the alternatives to integrating objective
\par
and subjective forecasting. We attempt to de-
\par
termine whether the effectiveness of the differ-
\par
ent approaches to integrating subjective and
\par
objective forecasting is dependent on contingent
\par
factors, such as contextual information. For
\par
example, judgemental adjustment may or may
\par
not be better than forecast combination when
\par
contextual information is high. We build upon
\par
the literature reviewed earlier in this paper by
\par
using the same contingent methodology to re-
\par
view the approaches to integrating subjective
\par
and objective forecasting.
\par
formation is particularly advantageous, pre-
\par
sumably because of the greater number of dis-
\par
continuities that can be explained by human
\par
judgement. However, contextual information is
\par
by no means the only factor to consider, as the
\par
above table shows. Factors such as trend, in-
\par
stability, historicals, the length of the forecast
\par
horizon and high seasonality in the presence of
\par
low instability may all have detrimental effects
\par
on judgemental accuracy, but perhaps only in
\par
the absence of contextual information. There is a
\par
need for research to investigate the effects of
\par
different time series characteristics when con-
\par
textual information is available.
\par
The next section examines the role of judge-
\par
ment \emph{in conjunction }with objective methods of
\par
forecasting, rather than \emph{in competition }with such
\par
methods. As we have seen, judgemental ap-
\par
proaches are useful in interpreting contextual
\par
information, yet lack the mechanical precision
\par
and rigour of statistical approaches. \emph{A composite}
\par
\emph{approach may enable the advantages of}
\par
\emph{judgemental interpretation of 'soft' contextual}
\par
\emph{information to be coupled with the benefits of}
\par
\emph{mechanical approaches, such as defensibility,}
\par
\textbf{3. Integrating objective and subjective}
\par
\textbf{forecasting}
\par
This section explores four approaches which
\par
may facilitate the interaction of judgement with
\par
structured forecasting methods: (a) model build-
\par
ing, (b) forecast combination, (c) judgemental
\par
adjustment and (d) judgemental decomposition.
\par
The literature in each category is reviewed, and
\par
then the paper concludes with a debate of the
\par
pros and cons of the four approaches.
\par
Fig. 1 presents a diagrammatic classification of
\par
the four approaches. It summarises how the
\par
subjective and objective processes may interact
\par
in each approach, and in so doing, provides a
\par
background for the discussion that follows. In
\par
the diagram, note that the input of \emph{soft }'con-
\par
textual factors' has been separated from the \emph{hard}
\par
quantitative information (represented by the
\par
time series 11, and the causal variables X 1 to Xn).
\par
\emph{Model building }is an approach in which judge-
\par
ment is used to select variables, specify model
\par
structure and set parameters based on contextual
\par
factors. From this, an econometric or statistical
\par
model is constructed which uses a time series,
\par
and perhaps other causal variables, to produce
\par
the forecast \emph{(Yt+k)"}
\par
In the \emph{combination }approach, an 'objective'
\par
forecast is combined with a 'subjective' forecast.
\par
The subjective forecast may have been produced
\par
from the time series only, or with the added
\par
information provided by contextual factors and100 \emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118}
\par
forecast, and the forecast is recomposed with any
\par
future-oriented information.
\par
Note also that the four approaches are by no
\par
means mutually exclusive-they can and do inter-
\par
act. For example, model building is precursory
\par
to forecast combination and judgemental adjust-
\par
ment.
\par
\textbf{\emph{2. Comblmf\textasciitilde n of ObleeU\textasciitilde{} \textasciitilde{} d S\textasciitilde Jer\textasciitilde hm Fon\textasciitilde ubl}}
\par
\emph{3.1. Model building}
\par
