\par
\emph{Judgemental adjustment }simply takes the ob-
\par
jective forecast and adjusts it for any contextual
\par
factors to produce the forecast.
\par
\emph{Judgemental decomposition }is a three-step
\par
process; judgement and statistical methods may
\par
operate at any of these steps. First, the time
\par
series is decomposed for any historical data (soft
\par
or hard), then the decomposed time series is
\par
The formulation of a statistical forecast re-
\par
quires the input of many aspects of judgement
\par
(Bunn and Wright, 1991). At the very least, a
\par
human judge is required in the first instance to
\par
select a forecasting model (or a set of models for
\par
combination, or from which the most accurate
\par
can be selected). The purpose of this section is to
\par
identify the areas where judgement is important
\par
in model building. It does not seek to describe a
\par
step-by-step approach to model building, nor
\par
does it provide a treatise on different forms of
\par
objective models.
\par
Bunn and Wright (1991) identified four areas
\par
where judgement may play a role in statistical
\par
model building: variable selection, model specifi-
\par
cation, parameter estimation and data analysis.
\par
These will be discussed with particular reference
\par
to the role of judgement in interpreting 'soft'
\par
contextual information.
\par
\emph{Variable selection and data analysis. }There are
\par
often many variables that may be causally re-
\par
lated to the forecast series. Statistical diagnostics
\par
(data analysis) can help the forecaster find im-
\par
portant causal variables, but, as Bunn and
\par
Wright state, "conventional wisdom is that vari-
\par
able selection should be essentially judgemental"
\par
(p. 509). The tasks of model specification and
\par
parameter estimation (described below) can be
\par
automated using techniques such as multiple
\par
regression or 'bootstrapping' (Dawes et al.,
\par
1989), but judgement is still required to identify
\par
the variables. Bootstrapping is successful when
\par
the relationships among variables are linear and
\par
when the variables are well-identified, but is
\par
vulnerable when there are unaccounted 'broken-
\par
leg' cues affecting the data (Kleinmuntz, 1990).
\par
Recent research by Lawrence and O'Connor
\par
(1992, 1993) has shown that bootstrapping the\emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118 }101
\par
time series forecaster was effective for stable
\par
artificial series, but not for real-world series.
\par
\emph{Model specification. }When causal information
\par
is \emph{quantitative, }it can be incorporated fairly easily
\par
into a formal model, either through the use of
\par
regression as indicated above, or through build-
\par
ing an econometric model. Causal variables can
\par
also be incorporated into time series analysis,
\par
using the technique of multivariate ARIMA. For
\par
example, the influence of advertising expendi-
\par
ture on a sales series could be accounted for by
\par
'prewhitening '3 the advertising series and apply-
\par
ing this transformation to the sales series before
\par
making the forecast (Makridakis et al., 1983,
\par
Chapter 10).
\par
Empirical research, summarised by Armstrong
\par
(1985, pp. 404-412), indicates that causal and
\par
extrapolative methods are roughly equivalent in
\par
accuracy, but that causal methods are more
\par
successful than extrapolative methods when the
\par
level of environmental change is high. This
\par
suggests that causal methods would work best for
\par
long-range forecasts. However, recent research
\par
by Clemen and Guerard (1989) indicates other-
\par
wise-the gains from econometric forecasts tend
\par
to decrease rapidly as the forecast horizon in-
\par
creased.
\par
Turner (1990) and Donihue (1993) indicate
\par
that, in practice, judgemental adjustments are
\par
frequently and successfully made to econometric
\par
models to incorporate extra-model information.
\par
However, instead of adjusting a model's \emph{output}
\par
to incorporate the extra-model information, the
\par
model itself may be changed. There appear to be
\par
two main ways of incorporating soft information
\par
into the model. First, the information may be
\par
subjectively encoded in the form of quantitative
\par
variables in a detailed econometric modelling
\par
approach (e.g. Abramson and Finizza's, 1991,
\par
application of belief networks in forecasting oil
\par
prices). However, this approach is costly, and
\par
may only be warranted for very important series.
\par
Moreover, it may still not accommodate all the
\par
'broken-leg' cues. The second approach, model
\par
intervention (see for example, West and Har-
\par
rison, 1989) allows the forecaster to \emph{identify}
\par
'broken-leg' cues, without needing to specify the
\par
\emph{influence }that the cues may have had on the time
\par
series. The influence is determined within the
\par
modelling process. Harvey and Durbin (1986)
\par
illustrated how model intervention could be used
\par
to incorporate the effect of a once-only event
\par
(e.g. the introduction of special legislation) into
\par
a structural time series model.
\par
\emph{Parameter estimation. }Harvey and Durbin
\par
(1986) also showed how the parameters associ-
\par
ated with a special event could be set so that the
\par
effect of the event diminished over time. How-
\par
ever, setting parameters may be a cognitively
\par
difficult task. Model intervention allows judges
\par
to identify special events and it automatically
\par
assesses the effects of these events on the series,
\par
but forecasters find it difficult to encode their
\par
beliefs as to the temporal effects of these events.
\par
Parameter estimation may be difficult because it
\par
requires understanding not only the influence of
\par
the event, but also the underlying model.
\par
The following sections examine alternative
\par
ways of introducing judgemental interpretation
\par
of contextual information into time series fore-
\par
casting. These approaches may not possess the
\par
objectivity and credibility of pure model-build-
\par
ing, yet they appear simpler and (arguably) more
\par
practical.
\par
\textbf{\emph{3.2. }}\emph{Combination of objective and subjective}
\par
\emph{forecasts}
\par
3 Prewhitening involves the use of A R I M A techniques to
\par
remove the signal from a series, so that only white noise
\par
remains.
\par
The combination of a judgemental forecast
\par
with a statistical forecast is a pragmatic approach
\par
to introducing subjective interpretation of con-
\par
textual factors. There is a great deal of evidence
\par
that combining two or more independent fore-
\par
casts leads to significant improvements in accura-
\par
cy (Clemen, 1989). The past literature has fo-
\par
cused predominantly on the combination of
\par
objective forecasts, whereas this section explicit-
\par
ly examines the empirical evidence on combina-
\par
tion of objective \emph{and }subjective forecasts.
\par
Only a few studies have investigated the in-
\par
fluence of situational differences on the effective-\emph{102 R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118}
\par
ness of combining objective and subjective fore-
\par
casts. Lawrence et al. (1986) examined the
\par
effectiveness of combination for series having
\par
different levels of forecasting difficulty and
\par
seasonality. Contrary to expectation, they found
\par
that combination was more effective for the low
\par
MAPE series. Seasonality, on the other hand,
\par
was found to have no influence on the benefit
\par
gained from combination. The effect of forecast
\par
horizon was also somewhat surprising, as the
\par
greatest improvement in accuracy was found in
\par
the short run (cf. Conroy and Harris, 1987).
\par
Sanders and Ritzman (1990) further investi-
\par
gated the effect of series difficulty, oper-
\par
ationalising it in terms of a coefficient of vari-
\par
ation rather than MAPE. They also found that
\par
combination was best for 'easier' series. For the
\par
'harder' series, where judgemental forecasts
\par
were significantly more accurate than statistical
\par
forecasts, the combined forecast was less accur-
\par
ate than the judgemental forecast. This finding is
\par
consistent with the argument that combination
\par
would be ineffective when one forecast is \emph{sub-}
\par
\emph{stantially }superior to another, since combining a
\par
very good forecast with a very poor forecast
\par
would be generally expected to reduce accuracy
\par
(in comparison to the very good forecast).
\par
The evidence presented in Appendix reference
\par
Table A2(a)-(c) confirms the result of Section 2
\par
that judgement was particularly accurate when
\par
contextual information is available. However it
\par
also indicates that combination was usually \emph{effec-}
\par
\emph{tive }in these circumstances (cf. Conroy and
\par
Harris, 1987; Bohara et al., 1987). Hence, with
\par
context, judgemental forecasts were superior to
\par
statistical forecasts, but the combination of the
\par
two improved accuracy even further.
\par
That finding can be related to the notion of
\par
'conditional efficiency' (Granger and Newbold,
\par
1973). A forecast is conditionally efficient if its
\par
error is not significantly greater than the com-
\par
posite forecast. In a study of the conditional
\par
efficiency of judgemental forecasts, Moriarty and
\par
Adams (1984) compared management forecasts
\par
with those based on the Box-Jenkins method.
\par
They found that the management forecasts were
\par
conditionally efficient with respect to the statisti-
\par
cal forecasts, even though the composite forecast
\par
was slightly (but not significantly) more accurate
\par
than the management forecast. The management
\par
forecasts could not be significantly improved by
\par
combining them with statistical forecasts.
\par
However, most of the studies show greater
\par
improvement from combination than Moriarty
\par
and Adams (1984). Although tests of conditional
\par
efficiency were not made in these studies, it
\par
appears that, in many cases, judgemental fore-
\par
casters may not use information efficiently.
\par
Judges have an information advantage, but they
\par
are not fully exploiting it.
\par
Several studies have focused on the influence
\par
of different combination schemes on accuracy.
\par
There is general support for the notion that the
\par
greater the number of forecasts, the better
\par
(Lawrence et al., 1986; Edmundson et al., 1988;
\par
Guerard and Beidleman, 1987). There is also
\par
some indication that mechanical combination is
\par
better than subjective combination (Angus-Lep-
\par
pan and Fatseas, 1986; Lawrence et al., 1986).
\par
There is recent evidence that a rule-based combi-
\par
nation strategy employing judgemental assess-
\par
ment of 'net causal forces' can aid in generating
\par
the weights for combining objective forecasts
\par
(Collopy and Armstrong, 1992b). This method is
\par
still mechanical, but uses judgemental input
\par
based on 'eyeballing' the series. Hence, it com-
\par
bines objectivity and subjectivity at the combina-
\par
tion weighting level, rather than at the level of
\par
the forecasts.
\par
There are mixed results regarding the best
\par
type of mechanical combination. The related
\par
literature on combining objective methods sug-
\par
gests that simple averaging is often just as good
\par
as weighted averages based on ex ante correla-
\par
tions (e.g., Newbold and Granger, 1974). Some
\par
studies of the combination of objective and
\par
subjective forecasts support this finding (Conroy
\par
and Harris, 1987; Bohara et al., 1987; Blattberg
\par
and Hoch, 1990), while others indicate that
\par
regression-based weighting is more accurate
\par
(Guerard and Beidleman, 1987; Newbold et al.,
\par
1987; Lobo, 1991). The use of the contingent
\par
methodology is needed to facilitate the interpre-
\par
tation of these sort of mixed results, but, un-
\par
fortunately, most papers do not report the neces-
\par
sary information about task differences. For\emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118 }103
\par
example, instability in series may cause problems
\par
for regression-based weighting 4. Also somewhat
\par
disappointing among the empirical research is
\par
the lack of testing for statistical significance (cf.
\par
Blattberg and Hoch, 1990). This makes it dif-
\par
ficult to make any conclusions with a great deal
\par
of confidence, apart from just generally stating
\par
that forecast combination improves accuracy.
\par
Clearly more contingency-based research is
\par
needed in this area, especially to determine the
\par
role of contextual information in the combina-
\par
tion of objective and subjective forecasts.
\par
\emph{3.3. Judgemental adjustment of objective}
\par
\emph{forecasts}
\par
Judgemental adjustment of an objective fore-
\par
cast appears to be quite a common practice (see
\par
Turner, 1990, for instance). In terms of use, it
\par
seems to be the major competing alternative to
\par
combination for integrating subjective and objec-
\par
tive forecasting. Yet, strangely, there appears to
\par
be a lack of studies comparing these two com-
\par
mon approaches (cf. Lim, 1993, who found
\par
adjustment to be less accurate than combination,
\par
albeit in an extrapolation task without contextual
\par
information).
\par
Based on the literature on anchoring and
\par
adjustment (Tversky and Kahneman, 1974),
\par
judgemental adjustment might be expected to
\par
have a detrimental effect on accuracy. However,
\par
the presence of contextual information, which
\par
we saw in Section 2 to be important in
\par
judgemental accuracy, may have a mediating
\par
role. With context, one would expect, a priori,
\par
that adjustment may be beneficial because the
\par
judge would bring information to the forecast
\par
that the model could not. However, without
\par
contextual information, judgemental adjustment
\par
might worsen accuracy because of the action of
\par
the anchoring and adjustment heuristic. The
\par
effectiveness of adjustment may also depend on
\par
the initial accuracy of the base statistical fore-
\par
4 Kang (1986) found, in combining objective forecasts, that
\par
simple averaging is best with unstable series, because in-
\par
stability tends to invalidate the ex ante weights derived from
\par
past series behaviour.
\par
cast; this may in turn be related to series charac-
\par
teristics (see also Section 2).
\par
\emph{Studies of non-contextual adjustment. }This
\par
form of adjustment may be attempted because
\par
the judge feels that the statistical forecast is
\par
inaccurate, even though the judge cannot as-
\par
sociate any contextual information with the
\par
series. There is mixed evidence regarding the
\par
effectiveness of non-contextual adjustment. It
\par
appears it may be beneficial when there is 'excess
\par
error', or room for improvement, in the statisti-
\par
cal forecast (Willemain, 1989, 1991; Lim and
\par
O'Connor, 1993), but not when the statistical
\par
forecast is accurate. Judgemental adjustment
\par
may also be more effective with seasonal series
\par
(Willemain, 1989), especially those with low
\par
noise (Sanders, 1992), but again this is related to
\par
the quality of the statistical forecast (Lim and
\par
O'Connor, 1993; Carbone et al., 1983).
\par
\emph{Contextual adjustment. }When contextual in-
\par
formation is available, the judge may wish to
\par
adjust the model-based forecast to incorporate
\par
the effects of this extra knowledge. This type of
\par
adjustment is generally effective (cf. Mathews
\par
and Diamantopolous, 1986). However, as in
\par
Section 2, it is again difficult to attribute the
\par
accuracy improvement solely to context-ex-
\par
perience and motivation may also play a part.
\par
There is a lack of studies which isolate these
\par
factors, as Edmundson et al. (1988) and Sanders
\par
and Ritzman (1992) did in comparing objective
\par
and subjective methods.
\par
\emph{Structured adjustment. }The practice of
\par
judgemental adjustment has been criticised
\par
because of its informal, ad hoc nature (Bunn and
\par
Wright, 1991). Four studies applied structured
\par
methods to generate judgemental adjustments.
\par
Reinmuth and Guerts (1972) used Bayesian
\par
techniques to incorporate customer survey in-
\par
formation into the adjustment made by the
\par
marketing staff. Cook et al. (1984) and Wolfe
\par
and Flores (1990) used the analytic hierarchy
\par
process (AHP) to generate adjustments. In
\par
Wolfe and Flores' study, subjects were given
\par
contextual information in the form of a brief
\par
economic commentary, accounting information,
\par
and historical EPS for one real undisguised
\par
company. The resultant adjustments significantly\emph{104 R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118}
\par
improved the accuracy of ARIMA-generated
\par
forecasts, especially when the ARIMA forecasts
\par
were initially inaccurate due to series volatility.
\par
Wolfe and Flores also examined the effect of
\par
expertise and forecast horizon, but neither af-
\par
fected accuracy. The external validity of this
\par
research may be limited because it appears that
\par
subjects did not see the base forecasts, or the
\par
resultant adjustments. The AHP is also quite an
\par
involved approach, although the complexity
\par
problem was partially addressed in a later study
\par
by Flores et al. (1992).
\par
Bunn and Wright (1991) point out that even
\par
the use of a structured adjustment process is still
\par
ad hoc, lacks interaction with the model and is
\par
"mostly qualitative". They proposed the need
\par
for an "interactive decomposition structure"-
\par
interactive in the sense that judgement should be
\par
part of an overall coherent modelling effort. The
\par
topic of judgemental decomposition is examined
\par
in the next section.
\par
\emph{3.4. Judgemental decomposition}
\par
The principle behind decomposition is the
\par
notion of 'divide and conquer' (Raiffa, 1968).
\par
Task decomposition encourages a person to
\par
concentrate selectively on one element at a time
\par
by breaking a task into its component parts.
\par
The basic difference between judgemental
\par
adjustment and judgemental decomposition of
\par
time series is that adjustment tries to account for
\par
past and future contextual factors and model
\par
misspecifications \emph{after }the forecast is made, while
\par
decomposition tries to remove the effects of past
\par
contextual factors from the series history \emph{before}
\par
extrapolating and accounting for future-oriented
\par
factors in the forecast. In essence, \emph{decomposition}
\par
\emph{considers the assessment of current status to be}
\par
\emph{separate from the assessment of change }(Arm-
\par
strong, 1985).
\par
This section considers the empirical studies
\par
that have investigated decomposition \emph{in general,}
\par
and then focuses on the use of decomposition in
\par
time series forecasting.
\par
Armstrong et al. (1975) asked subjects a set of
\par
general knowledge questions, and found that
\par
people made better judgements when using a
\par
decomposed approach. Decomposition was espe-
\par
cially useful when the subject knew relatively
\par
little about the topic. Phelps and Shanteau
\par
(1978) found that expert livestock judges consid-
\par
ered fewer than three pieces of information in
\par
picture form in comparison to 9-11 cues when
\par
the information was decomposed to a verbal
\par
form. They interpreted this result as implying
\par
that there are inter-correlations present in real
\par
stimuli that tend to reduce the number of dimen-
\par
sions processed, but it may also imply that
\par
decomposition enhanced the amount of infor-
\par
mation processed and hence decision quality (or
\par
that decomposition simply made hard-to-see cues
\par
obvious). In work related to decomposition,
\par
Fischhoff and Bar-Hillel (1984) found that focus-
\par
ing techniques such as isolation analysis can
\par
improve judgement in inferential problem solv-
\par
ing. Isolation analysis requires subjects to con-
\par
sider what judgement they would make if each
\par
cue was the only one available, prior to making a
\par
summary judgement based on all the informa-
\par
tion.
\par
Lyness and Cornelius (1982) compared holistic
\par
and decomposed judgement in a performance
\par
rating experiment. Students were required to
\par
evaluate hypothetical instructors based on writ-
\par
ten information. Lyness and Cornelius found
\par
that an algorithmic decomposition strategy
\par
(where the ratings were derived from dimension-
\par
al evaluations and importance weights) was gen-
\par
erally more effective than holistic judgement and
\par
clinical decomposition (where dimensions were
\par
considered separately, but the subjects then
\par
made overall evaluations). Unfortunately
\par
because of the nature of the task, their analysis
\par
considered only reliability, convergence and
\par
agreement measures; it did not compare the
\par
predicted values against actual or optimal values.
\par
Moreover, the result varied according to the
\par
measure used-with mean absolute deviation
\par
between raters, decomposition was superior;
\par
with correlations, holistic and decomposed
\par
judgement were equally effective. Lyness and
\par
Cornelius speculated that decomposition would
\par
be most advantageous in complex circumstances,\emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118 }105
\par
and tested the effect of information load (three,
\par
six or nine performance dimensions) on the
\par
effectiveness of decomposition. The hypothe-
\par
sised interaction between judgement strategy
\par
(holistic, decomposition) and information load
\par
was not found for the reliability and convergence
\par
scores. High inter-rater agreement was found
\par
with algorithmic decomposition at high infor-
\par
mation levels. However, the significance of this
\par
result was not statistically tested. Hence, the
\par
impact of decomposition on relationship between
\par
environmental complexity (information level)
\par
and decision quality has not been fully investi-
\par
gated.
\par
Three recent papers by MacGregor and as-
\par
sociates have further explored the role of de-
\par
composition in answering almanac-type ques-
\par
tions. MacGregor et al. (1988) showed that
\par
accuracy increased with greater structure in the
\par
decision aid. Subjects performed best when
\par
provided with the full algorithmic decomposition
\par
strategy-they were given the complete algo-
\par
rithm, asked to estimate its components, and
\par
then shown how to combine the parts.
\par
MacGregor and Lichtenstein (1991) showed
\par
that extending algorithmic decomposition to in-
\par
clude alternative problem solving approaches led
\par
to further improvement in performance. How-
\par
ever, they found that providing training on
\par
decomposition, so that people could create their
\par
own algorithms, was of little benefit. They also
\par
cautioned that "erroneous knowledge incorpo-
\par
rated into an estimation aid may lead one astray
\par
without a warning that it is doing so" (p. 115)-
\par
subjects with \emph{misinformation }tended to perform
\par
particularly badly.
\par
One issue raised in the MacGregor and Lich-
\par
tenstein paper was that decomposition seemed to
\par
work best with estimates of large numerical
\par
values. MacGregor and Armstrong (1993) con-
\par
firmed that decomposition improved accuracy for
\par
problems involving extreme and uncertain val-
\par
ues, but was \emph{less }accurate for problems with
\par
target values that were not extreme and uncer-
\par
tain. It was not clear whether the contingency of
\par
decomposition performance was due to the large
\par
magnitude of the target values, or their uncer-
\par
tainty, or both. However, these papers indicate
\par
that decomposition may be a risky strategy in
\par
certain circumstances and should be applied with
\par
caution. This issue will be discussed further.
\par
In the context of time series forecasting,
\par
Edmundson (1990) developed a decision aid,
\par
called GRAFFECT, that encouraged judgemen-
\par
tal forecasting along the lines of classical time
\par
series analysis. Users were able to judgementally
\par
extract the trend and seasonality from a time
\par
series, leaving a residual component. Edmun-
\par
dson found that the extrapolative accuracy of
\par
both novice and expert forecasters using GRAF-
\par
FECT were better than unaided forecasters and
\par
deseasonalised single exponential smoothing for
\par
the shorter forecast horizons. Considering that
\par
the subjects were not provided with a contextual
\par
information advantage, this seems to be a some-
\par
what surprising finding. Why should decomposed
\par
judgement be superior to one of the most accur-
\par
ate statistical techniques (see Makridakis et al.,
\par
1982) in a purely numerical task? One possible
\par
explanation is that the subjects were able to
\par
recognise and discount discontinuities in the
\par
series that were naively incorporated by the
\par
statistical model. The results of O'Connor et al.
\par
(1993) and Sanders and Ritzman (1992), to a
\par
lesser extent, suggest otherwise, but their series
\par
were artificially generated and their subjects
\par
were given different instructions and lacked
\par
Edmundson's decision aid. It is, of course, open
\par
to speculation as to how well the subjects in both
\par
those studies might have performed if the events
\par
causing the discontinuities had been identified.
\par
Collopy and Armstrong (1993) showed that
\par
decomposition of series by 'causal forces' can
\par
reduce forecast error. The effect of traffic vol-
\par
ume was removed from three series (highway
\par
deaths, injuries and accidents) to create com-
\par
ponent series that were forecast separately and
\par
then recomposed with the forecast for traffic
\par
volume. All forecasts were made using Holt's
\par
exponential smoothing. Collopy and Armstrong
\par
found that decomposition was effective for the
\par
deaths and injuries series, but not the accidents
\par
series. They cautioned that multiplicative re-
\par
composition of decomposed components can be
\par
risky because it has the potential for explosive
\par
errors, echoing the sentiments of MacGregor
\par
and Lichtenstein (1991) and MacGregor and
\par
Armstrong (1993). They also stressed that re-106 \emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-118}
\par
search attention should focus on the conditions
\par
where decomposition is effective.
\par
Goodwin and Wright (1993, p. 154) suggested
\par
that decomposition would be ineffective where:
\par
{$\bullet$} the decomposition is mechanical and/or the
\par
judge is sceptical about the decomposition tech-
\par
nique that is being employed;
\par
{$\bullet$} there is unfamiliarity with the technique
\par
used for decomposition and the type of judge-
\par
ments required by it;
\par
{$\bullet$} the judgements required by decomposition
\par
are actually more complex psychologically than
\par
holistic judgements;
\par
{$\bullet$} the judge experiences boredom or fatigue
\par
because the duration of the task is lengthened
\par
and the number of judgements increased.
\par
Goodwin and Wright (1993) also emphasised
\par
the need for decomposition research to focus on
\par
"analysing and integrating judgements based on
\par
time series information with contextual infor-
\par
mation" (p. 155). Very little work has examined
\par
judgemental decomposition of contextual factors
\par
from time series. There are software packages
\par
that facilitate additive decomposition of special
\par
events (e.g. FORSYS by Lewandowski, 1982)
\par
and multiplicative decomposition of events (e.g.
\par
FUTURCAST by Carbone and Makridakis5),
\par
but it seems that only anecdotal evidence (e.g.
\par
Gorr, 1986a,b) has been reported about the
\par
effect of event decomposition on forecast accura-
\par
cy. Lee et al. (1990) developed a prototype
\par
expert system for forecasting the demand of oil
\par
products for a single company. The system was
\par
designed to detect historical events, relate analo-
\par
gous events, and to decompose events from the
\par
time series. However, the system was not empiri-
\par
cally tested-the authors simply reported on its
\par
use in an oil company.
\par
\emph{3.5. Summary}
\par
Four approaches to integrating subjective and
\par
objective forecasting have been examined. All
\par
have shown the potential to reduce forecast
\par
error, but they may differ in ease-of-use, credi-
\par
bility, cost, and other factors identified as im-
\par
portant by practitioners (see Collopy and Arm-
\par
strong, 1992a).
\par
The incorporation of contextual factors in
\par
\emph{model building }involves either quantifying those
\par
factors in an extensive econometric model or
\par
using the technique of model intervention. Both
\par
approaches would produce credible forecasts,
\par
but they suffer from high set-up costs. Model
\par
intervention is a complex approach, requiring an
\par
understanding of the underlying model in order
\par
to codify beliefs about contextual factors by
\par
parameter setting.
\par
\emph{Forecast combination }is a relatively practical
\par
and simple approach which generally improves
\par
accuracy. However, it suffers from duplication of
\par
effort, redundancy of information (see Clemen
\par
and Winkler, 1985), loses resolution when the
\par
dependent variable (e.g. sales) is unstable (see
\par
Moriarty, 1990), and does not facilitate back-
\par
ward inference 6 (see Einhorn and Hogarth,
\par
1982). Some believe that combination should be
\par
replaced by an all 'encompassing' model
\par
(Diebold, 1989). The counter argument is that
\par
combining is more cost-effective than building a
\par
single comprehensive model (see Bunn, 1989).
\par
\emph{Judgemental adjustment }of a statistical forecast
\par
is perhaps the most easy-to-use and cost-effective
\par
of the four approaches, but it introduces the
\par
possibility of bias from the use of the anchor and
\par
adjustment heuristic. As Bunn and Wright (1991)
\par
emphasised, judgemental adjustment is ad hoc,
\par
informal and open to adversarial criticism.
\par
\emph{Judgemental decomposition }is arguably more
\par
complex than combination or adjustment, but
\par
brings structure, defensibility and backward in-
\par
ference to the task of forecasting with contextual
\par
information. It is possible that decomposition
\par
can reduce cognitive load, but it may also be
\par
risky and ineffective in certain circumstances
\par
(Goodwin and Wright, 1993). Research is
\par
needed on the accuracy of judgement decompo-
\par
