\par
\textbf{4. Conclusion}
\par
This paper has reviewed the literature on the
\par
importance of human-interaction factors in a
\par
comparison of judgemental and statistical ap-
\par
proaches to time series forecasting. It demon-
\par
strated the crucial role of the knowledge of the
\par
context of the time series to accuracy. The
\par
accuracy of judgemental eyeballing may be
\par
roughly equivalent to the statistical methods, but
\par
the major contribution of judgemental ap-
\par
proaches lies in the ability to integrate this non-
\par
time series information into the forecasts. Ac-
\par
cordingly, Section 3 examined four ways of
\par
integrating judgemental and statistical forecasts-
\par
model building, combination, judgemental ad-
\par
justment and judgemental decomposition. Sim-
\par
ple combination provides considerable advan-
\par
tages, while judgemental adjustment may be
\par
subject to bias. Promising advantages can be
\par
made from an improvement of the process of
\par
decomposition in any combination process.
\par
\textbf{Appendix. Reference tables}
\par
This appendix presents the tables that were
\par
constructed to provide the detailed support for
\par
the conclusions made in the main body of the
\par
paper. They are included here because we feel
\par
they are a useful reference source for future
\par
research in judgemental forecasting. It should be
\par
possible for a researcher doing work on the
\par
influence of seasonality, for example, to scan the
\par
table and quickly find the studies that examined
\par
seasonality.
\par
The tables do however require some interpre-
\par
tive effort on behalf of the reader. A graphical
\par
presentation format was chosen to display the
\par
results succinctly and attractively. Fig. A1 shows
\par
a key to the symbols used in the reference tables.
\par
It is hoped that future empirical studies would
\par
provide greater information about their data,
\par
methods and environmental characteristics
\par
because in many instances it was difficult to
\par
adequately classify past studies within this con-
\par
tingent framework. We also hope that future
\par
literature reviews could utilise the framework
\par
and build and improve upon this approach.
\par
A brief comment about the classification rules
\par
for certain factors:
\par
Trend-The trend factor reports the direction
\par
of slope in the series (up, fiat, down). Ideally, it
\par
would be useful to present the mean gradient (or
\par
equivalent) in the series, or at least the propor-
\par
tion of ups versus downs, but these measures
\par
were rarely reported in the studies.
\par
Seasonality-The seasonality factor indicates
\par
the presence of signal or pattern in the series.
\par
This measure should be interpreted in conjunc-
\par
tion with the data presented in the "Period"
\par
column to determine the type of seasonality (e.g.
\par
monthly versus quarterly).
\par
Historicals-This refers to the number of his-
\par
torical data points used. In the studies of 'real
\par
world' forecasting, it is often impossible to
\par
determine how many data points were used by
\par
the judges, so the table presents the number of
\par
data points used by the objective methods in
\par
those cases.
\par
Forecasts-This refers to the number of fore-
\par
casts made by the human judge. This measure
\par
should be interpreted in conjunction with the
\par
'feedback' dimension described in the next sec-108 \emph{R. Webby, M. O'Connor / International Journal of Forecasting 12 (1996) 91-I18}
\par
tion. If feedback was not provided, then the
\par
number represents the length of the forecast
\par
horizon, and the error measures have been
\par
calculated across the entire horizon. Where the
\par
forecast is indicated as a specific number (e.g.
\par
\#16) or a range (\#16-\#18) then the errors
\par
represent those specific step-ahead periods.
\par
Feedback-The assessment of feedback is a
\par
binary measure showing whether feedback was
\par
provided to subjects. Unfortunately, it was dif-
\par
ficult to determine whether outcome or perform-
\par
ance feedback was provided in most cases.
\par
Experience-The following decision rules were
\par
applied: O Undergraduate students with no
