\textbf{ABSTRACT}
\par
Growing recognition of the need for better management information system (MIS) theory
\par
and practice centers around problems with identifying the value of information and infor-
\par
mation systems in organizations. This paper addresses this issue by showing that existing
\par
theory in organizational economics and industrial organization directly relates to informa-
\par
tion problems in organizations (their nature, and their effects in terms of organization
\par
structure and performance). The paper categorizes these problems and associated theories
\par
into four distinct classes and shows that \textbf{MIS }research and practice can be mapped to
\par
information problems of each type. Because the economic theory pertinent to specific
\par
information problems includes the nature of the organizational response to information
\par
constraints, it also provides insight into the nature of the improvement to be realized if
\par
information constraints can be overcome. These improvements can then be interpreted as
\par
the value of the associated information systems which suggests using the resultant classi-
\par
fication scheme to map between information problems and system value. The paper shows
\par
that this methodology provides a more appropriate level of focus than either traditional
\par
normative or business value methods, especially because many improvements have struc-
\par
tural as well as performance consequences.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Subject Areas: Electronic Data Processing, Information Economics, Information Man-}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{agement, and Management Information Systems.}}
\par
One should hardly have to tell academicians that information is a valuable re-
\par
source: knowledge is power. And yet it occupies a slum dwelling in the town of
\par
economics. Mostly it is ignored: the best technology is assumed to be known;
\par
the relationship of commodities to consumer preferences is a datum. And one of
\par
the information-producing industries, advertising, \textbf{is }treated with a hostility that
\par
economists normally reserve for tariffs or monopolists.
\par
George J. Stigler \textbf{[63, }p. \textbf{2131}
