G. Anthony Gorry and Michael \textbf{S. }Scott-Morton \textbf{[20, }p. \textbf{551}
\par
\textbf{THE NEED FOR AN INFORMATION VALUE THEORY}
\par
The management of any organizational resource requires the ability to make
\par
choices from available options regarding the application of that resource. These
\par
choices should be made with an understanding of the expected return from each
\par
alternative and of the set of expected costs. Few would disagree that information
\par
systems are organizational resources within the meaning of this discussion. Unfor-
\par
tunately, the output of an information system is not usually a product with a value
\par
established in a market. Instead, information is a complementary good with respect
\par
to productive processes. It has no value except as realized through the manipulation
\par
of other resources. From this perspective, system designers require an under-
\par
standing of what benefit they expect to realize from the investment of resources in
\par
an information system. This need is fundamental for any investment in a productive
\par
resource but, as will be seen, is still problematic with respect to management
\par
information system (MIS) theory.
\par
\textbf{This }paper addresses the problem by linking investments in information technol-
\par
ogy (IT) to theories of the value of information in organizations. The linkage is
\par
accomplished through a framework derived from economic theory and results in a
\par
model with which to classify information systems and estimate their value to
\par
organizations. The resultant model can then serve as a basis for practitioner decision
\par
making and as a structuring mechanism and guide for academic research.
\par
The following section briefly summarizes MIS theory with respect to the value
\par
of information systems. It highlights the gap in existing research and cites devel-
\par
opments in the field that call for an expansion of the research focus. The remainder
\par
of the paper then begins this process by organizing the information problems in
\par
organizations into a simple framework which is consistent with established theories
\par
in both economics and MIS. These presentations are followed by a discussion of
\par
implications for both practitioners and researchers and concluding remarks.
\par
\textbf{MIS THEORIES OF INFORMATION VALUE}
\par
MIS research on the value of information and information systems has, in the main,
\par
progressed along two branches. The first branch is characterized by normative
\par
theories for the value of information (for a review see Ahituv \textbf{[3]), }while the other
\par
is characterized by econometric studies to estimate the business value of IS for
\par
firms, industries, or the economy (see Kauffman and Weill \textbf{[30]).}
\par
In normative theories, the value of information is defined to be the value of
\par
the outcome of action taken with the information less its value without the information.
\par
This approach has the advantage of considering the marginal contribution of infor-
\par
mation (and so of information systems) to the decision-making process.
\par
In business value research, a model for the impact of information systems on
\par
performance is constructed and then regression and/or time series techniques are
\par
used to estimate values for model parameters. Parameter values serve descriptive
\par
