Gurbaxani [21] used this approach.
\par
Both approaches, though, miss an important aspect of information and system
\par
value. Normative information value literature highlights the attributes of information
\par
and information systems which improve value given the underlying value of the
\par
decision. This value is typically assumed to be known to the designer and is rarely
\par
discussed in the literature.
\par
One example is Emery's 1969 model of information value [15] which relies
\par
on a known payoff matrix which maps states of nature and decision maker actions
\par
to payoffs. He worked backward to link actions to messages and message generation
\par
to observable states of nature. He explicitly recognized, however, that specification
\par
of the payoff matrix, the link between decision and value, is a deficiency in the
\par
theoretical model because of the difficulty in making this link in practice.
\par
Ahituv [2] strictly focused on attributes of information systems which may
\par
improve value but did not mention an underlying source of value. He mentioned
\par
that designers may have to trade-off attributes in the design process and implied
\par
through example that the weight of attributes may change from project to project.
\par
He did not, however, discuss how the nature of the project might affect attribute
\par
importance. Newman [44] showed that without a unifying link between user and
\par
designer perceptions of value, rational design processes result in different relative
\par
weights for system attributes (fineness in his example). Several other authors dis-
\par
cussed attributes of systems and implied that benefits accrue from improving system
\par
performance with respect to the attributes [3] \textbf{171 }[16] [32] [36] [SO], but none
\par
categorized these benefits and none provided a way of determining the underlying
\par
value of the decision.
\par
Business value research has gaps in this area which vary depending on the
\par
focus of the research. Research at the economy or industry level encompasses too
\par
many factors to be useful to individual decision makers. In discussing one class of
\par
studies, Kauffman and Weill wrote, ``the studies which reported more favorable
\par
results for the value of IT expenditures failed to explain how and why IT creates
\par
leverage on economic measures of firm and industry performance'' [30, p. 3791.
\par
At all levels of analysis (firm, industry, and economy), exogenous factors and
\par
measurement problems can lead to contradictory or vague results. In particular,
\par
many studies fail to indicate positive correlation between IT investment and organ-
\par
izational improvement, but do not (and cannot) address whether the organization,
\par
industry, or economy would have been worse off if the IT investment had not been
\par
made. On the other hand, there is too much investment in IT for all of it to be
\par
irrational, even for industries in which empirical research reports overall negative
\par
returns. Finally, the results of this research are fragile in a technologically turbulent
\par
environment.
\par
This paper addresses a gap common to both of the above research streams,
\par
namely that neither provides a useful link between theory and the circumstances
\par
faced by an individual decision maker. Normative theories can show that attributes
\par
such as fineness improve system performance, but do not provide insight to deter-
\par
mine whether the improved performance would be economically beneficial in the
\par
face of the decision maker's situation. Similarly, business value research indicates
