\par
The same gap in MIS theory was highlighted in discussions at both the 1989 ICIS2
\par
and Decision Sciences Institute3 meetings.
\par
\textbf{This }paper presents theory for the value of information and information \textbf{systems}
\par
which addresses the gap identified above. It draws on existing theory from industrial
\par
organization (10) economics which highlights relationships between uncertainty,
\par
information, and infomation costs and firm and industry behavior and structure.
\par
This approach has two benefits. First, the theory consists of a collection of research
\par
on different types of information constraints faced by individual firms and decision
\par
makers. A decision maker faced with an information problem can more easily
\par
match that problem with the relevant portion(s) of the theory. Second, the I 0 theory
\par
suggests the economic benefit to be realized from overcoming the information
\par
problem, presumably through the construction of an information system. Since the
\par
economic research typically indicates the nature of rational responses to informa-
\par
tion constraints, comparative statics analysis of the problem provides insight into
\par
the nature of the benefits to be realized as information constraints are overcome.
\par
The I0 theory also includes a very important class of rational responses to infor-
\par
mation constraints, namely the selection of alternate organizational forms. Analysis
\par
of these cases indicates not only how price or quantity decisions can be improved
\par
but also how IT leads to the restructuring of organizations and industries. By
\par
addressing these structural issues, \textbf{I0 }theory goes well beyond the insights offered
\par
by normative and business value research.
\par
The goal of presenting a theory of information value is accomplished in three
\par
steps. First, the next section uses theory from \textbf{I 0 }to suggest a view of firms that
\par
highlights the relationship between the firm, uncertainty, and organizational and
\par
industry structure and behavior. A simple economic model based on a profit function
