\textbf{INDUSTRIAL ORGANIZATION AND INFORMATION PROBLEMS}
\par
Modem I 0 traces its beginnings to work by Knight, Coase, and Commons in the
\par
\textbf{1920s }and \textbf{1930s [63], }including an important \textbf{1937 }paper by Ronald Coase titled
\par
``The Nature of the Firm'' [ll]. Coase pointed out that transactions can be carried
\par
out between individuals or firms in a market, within firms, and between \textbf{firms}
\par
and/or individuals under a contractual arrangement. He raised the fundamental
\par
question, ``Why is this variety of transaction organizing mechanisms observed?''
\par
and, in particular, ``Why are there firms?'' Coase pointed out that there must be
\par
valid reasons for rational individuals to choose one \textbf{form }for organizing a transaction
\par
over the others. Since Coase's paper, determining the factors affecting choice of
\par
transactional form has been an important focus of modem 10.
\par
One of the startling results of this work has been the prominence of information
\par
flows in determining transactional and organizational form. Spence summed up the
\par
importance of information in \textbf{I0 }theory when he wrote:
\par
There is an impressive and growing body of literature that suggests the firm, in
\par
large part, consists of nonmarket institutions whose function is to deal with
\par
resource allocation in the presence of informational constraints that markets
\par
handle poorly or do not handle at all. \textbf{\emph{[56, }}p. \textbf{1641}
\par
The nature of these information constraints, the response of entrepreneurs (owners) and
\par
managers to them, and the implications for MIS theory are major topics \textbf{of }this paper.
\par
\textbf{The Nature of the Firm}
\par
An inherent advantage of the firm is the return to specialization in production
\par
identified long ago by Adam Smith \textbf{\emph{[55]. }}It \textbf{is }important to note, however, that this
\par
advantage is not restricted to transactions organized within a \textbf{fm. }In any community,
\par
a multitude of individuals provide specialized \textbf{goods }and services outside the organ-
\par
izational umbrella of a firm. Others may work at the same tasks for wages within
\par
organizations. Two motivations have been identified for organizing specialists
\par
within a firm.
\par
The first stems from economies of physical production as when ore is smelted
\par
and turned directly into the finished product in one plant without the necessity of
\par
reheating. Though important, this aspect of organization is applicable in relatively
\par
few industries and is not a strictly limiting case since two firms could agree to
\par
build adjacent plants capable of achieving the same result.
\par
The second reason for organizing a firm is related to uncertainty and involves
\par
one of the key distinctions between \textbf{I 0 }and classical microeconomic theory, namely
\par
relaxing the assumption of instantaneous transactions (and, by implication, the
