\textbf{THE SEARCH FOR PRICES}
\par
A problem for managers is that the search for buyers and sellers is costly and,
\par
because of this cost, there is an incentive for each seller to display diverse prices
\par
\textbf{[46] [47] [58]. }Ideally, a buyer would like the information identifying the seller
\par
charging the lowest price for the item sought. Lacking this information, the buyer
\par
must obtain it by shopping, but shopping has monetary and effort costs, especially
\par
when finding and comparing prices is confused by quality differences. If the buyer
\par
undertakes the effort to determine prices, it is not known whether the expenditure
\par
of additional resources for more search will result in the location of a lower price.
\par
The reciprocal problem is faced by sellers. Given an offer, the seller would like to
\par
know if there is an interested buyer who is willing to pay more before committing
\par
to sell at the offered price.
\par
