\textbf{The Productivity of Factors}
\par
A distinguishing mark of organizations is that they display superadditivity. That is,
\par
the marginal product of the sum of the inputs is greater than the sum of the marginal
\par
products of the inputs taken separately. (If \textbf{this }were not \textbf{so, }the equivalent transactions
\par
would be carried out in the market.) For managers, this characteristic means that
\par
identifying each input's contribution to productivity, and therefore the appropriate
\par
level of investment, is more difficult than in market transactions [4].
\par
