\textbf{The Product of Labor}
\par
In contrast to the productivity of physical inputs, the information problems asso-
\par
ciated with labor as an input have received considerable attention in both economic
\par
theory and MIS research. In economic theory, problems of superadditivity with
\par
respect to labor are addressed in the theory of teams \textbf{[4] [41]. }While much of the
\par
discussion regarding physical inputs applies to labor, labor has three characteristics
\par
impossible to find in physical inputs: the ability to shirk, the ability to be creative,
\par
and the ability to quit. These characteristics complicate measurement of the marginal
\par
product of labor and, therefore, determination of appropriate compensation and
\par
levels of investment. The problem is further complicated by the fact that specialization,
\par
a hallmark of production within a firm, leads to private knowledge regarding the
\par
specialist's job.
\par
