\par
\textbf{As }with any input, owners prefer to pay labor \textbf{so }that the marginal cost of
\par
labor is equal to its marginal product. Unfortunately, the worker may be better
\par
informed about \textbf{the }actual product of that labor (though not the value of the product)
\par
than the owner. Also, in the absence of monitoring by ownership, workers have an
\par
incentive to shirk in their labor [4] [62]. Shirking can take a wide range of forms
\par
including outright nonperformance over a period, performing at less than actual
\par
capacity, and pilfering inventory and services (copies, postage, etc.) from the organi-
\par
zation. The problem of creativity or extra effort is the converse \textbf{\emph{of }}the shirking
\par
problem where difficulty in identifying and rewarding superior performance reduces
\par
the incentive for workers to exert exceptional effort. Finally, when the employee
\par
has a private opinion regarding his or her effort which is higher than that held by
\par
ownership, the employee has an incentive to leave the organization. This decision
\par
to leave must, of course, take into account the ability to duplicate the organization's
\par
inherent advantage in organizing capital and other resources. Gene Amdahl's departure
\par
from IBM is an example of this type of information differential problem \textbf{[51].}
\par
Owners have an incentive to monitor employee performance to detect and
\par
punish shirking and to reward creativity and effort, but monitoring is not costless.
\par
In the absence of costless monitoring, there is a \textbf{loss }of the efficiency associated
\par
with specialization from four sources:
\par
\textbf{1.}
\par
The cost of monitoring. Wages for managers who supervise workers and
\par
ensure performance are a cost that would not be incurred if workers always
\par
worked at peak performance or if effort could be costlessly monitored.
\par
\textbf{\emph{2.}}
\par
Worker inefficiency. Assume that output is a function of both quantity of
\par
labor and effort and that workers prefer to exert as little effort as possible
\par
to receive their wages. If they are caught shirking, however, they may be
\par
punished by being fired (denied wages) or docked (having wages reduced).
\par
Workers should exert effort to a level where the disutility of further effort
\par
is equal to the disutility of the consequences of shirking. These conse-
\par
quences are a function of, among other things, the probability of being
\par
caught and the penalty the owner can realistically impose \textbf{[4].}
\par
\textbf{\emph{3.}}
\par
Profit incentives. In some cases, monitoring costs lead to a situation
\par
where the best effort is induced with a compensation arrangement where
\par
labor's wage is dependent on some more easily observable value, com-
\par
monly profit \textbf{(so }that \textbf{\emph{p(x)=f(lc)) }}[4] [40]. Such schemes dilute the owner's
\par
risk premium and produce incentives to misrepresent costs between stages
\par
of production \textbf{\emph{[62].}}
\par
\textbf{4.}
\par
Market payments. There are situations, including some caused by moni-
\par
toring costs, where the cost of maintaining the transaction within the firm
\par
exceeds the production advantage the firm is assumed to enjoy. Then the
\par
transaction is best allowed to take place in the market \textbf{[24] \emph{[62]. }}The loss
\par
consists of monopoly rents paid to suppliers and contracting costs (trans-
\par
action costs), including contracting costs explicitly related to information
\par
problems \textbf{[24] [62] \emph{[63].}}
\par
