\textbf{Top Management as an Input}
\par
The control and compensation of top management in a publicly held corporation
\par
presents a special problem which has not, so far, been amenable to solution through
\par
information technology. This problem is of interest as a limiting case and so is
\par
briefly discussed here.
\par
The typical public corporation is marked by ownership dispersed through a
\par
multitude of stockholders and a management team appointed by a board of directors.
\par
The link between top management and ownership is distinguished by the fact that
\par
dispersed ownership does not share the internal communication economies held to
\par
be one of the principal advantages of the firm. Instead, top management may have
\par
an incentive to withhold information from ownership, a motivation behind require-
\par
ments for independent audits of public corporations.
\par
\textbf{As }in any principal/agent relationship, owners of such firms (stockholders)
\par
have an incentive to monitor the behavior of management. Unlike typical principal/
\par
agent relationships, however, the role of principal is not vested in a single individual
\par
but instead \textbf{is }dispersed among all of the owners of the corporation. When there
\par
are costs associated with monitoring the performance of agents (top management),
\par
a serious free-rider problem develops for monitors. Simply stated, if a stockholder
\par
owns some fraction, \textbf{\emph{01, }}of a corporation's stock with \textbf{O{$<$}a{$<$}l, }the improvement in
\par
top management performance resulting from monitoring, \textbf{\emph{I,,,, }}must be large enough
\par
that the monitoring stockholder's share of the improvement is greater than the cost
\par
of monitoring \textbf{\emph{(aZJC,) }[52].}
\par
Significant research on the role of large stockholders in firms has shown that
\par
the value of a firm increases with the ownership share of the largest stockholder
\par
\textbf{[20] [52]. }Implicit in these studies is the potential that changes in the cost of
