\textbf{PREDICTING STATES OF NATURE}
\par
Assuming perfect knowledge of the current values of the terms in (l), the problem
\par
of prediction revolves around the need to forecast the future values of these terms
\par
when acquisition of inputs, production, and realization of revenue do not occur
\par
simultaneously. Since simultaneous occurrence of these events is the exception
\par
rather than the rule, this problem is of considerable interest in most businesses. The
\par
problem is compounded when inputs have fixed asset characteristics (i.e., their
\par
useful life extends beyond the current production period). An additional difficulty
\par
arises from the effect of \textbf{risk }averse behavior on production decisions in an uncertain
\par
environment. In general, the problem is to reduce unexpected variation in the
\par
parameters \textbf{\emph{P(q), }pl(xl), }and \textbf{\emph{p2(x2) }}in (1) between determination and acquisition of
\par
optimal levels of inputs and the realization of revenue.
\par
\textbf{Uncertainty in Input Costs or Product Demand}
\par
The problems of uncertainty regarding the costs of variable inputs or demand for
