\textbf{Risk Aversion and Production Decisions}
\par
Most individuals are assumed to be risk averse with respect to wealth and income,
\par
and this assumption has been extended in theory to the behavior of firms, albeit
\par
with certain caveats [49]. The distinguishing characteristic of risk aversion is that,
\par
given some level of wealth, W, the utility increase from an increase in wealth by
\par
some amount, \textbf{a, }is less than the utility \textbf{loss }associated with a \textbf{loss }of the same
\par
amount (V( \textbf{W)-V( \emph{W-x){$>$} V( }}\emph{W+rr)- }\textbf{\emph{V( }}W)). This characteristic has an important
\par
theoretical consequence when applied to the production decisions of firms.
\par
\textbf{This }consequence was demonstrated in an important paper by Sandmo [49].
\par
He assumed that a firm makes a production decision regarding quantity of output
\par
in one period and then realizes the market price in a subsequent period. Price is
\par
assumed to be a random variable with a known density function and a known mean
\par
and the firm is assumed to be a price taker in a competitive market. By comparing
\par
production decisions made under certainty (price equal to the known mean of the
\par
uncertain case but no price variance) with those made under uncertainty, Sandmo
\par
shows that the firm faced with uncertainty in prices will always choose a level of
\par
production (and therefore profits) less than the optimal level of output under cer-
\par
tainty. Further, his results indicate that the difference between the optimal levels of
\par
output under certainty and uncertainty is increasing with the variance in prices.
\par
There are three implications of the preceding discussion.
