\par
Uncertainty leads to lower levels of production, lower levels of profit,
\par
and, by implication, a reduction in social welfare.
\par
In special cases, the reduction in production level due to uncertainty may
\par
be sufficient to produce negative profits. Uncertainty can then deter market
\par
entry or cause market failure.
\par
Systems that reduce uncertainty will similarly reduce the aforementioned
\par
problems. Profits, utility, output, and social welfare will improve and
\par
markets will be more efficient.
\par
\textbf{Prediction Problems in MIS Research and Practice}
\par
\textbf{As }with the determination of the productivity of variable assets, systems designed
\par
to forecast states of nature with respect to demand, prices, etc., have been an
\par
important subset of implemented information and decision support systems. Of
\par
interest with respect to IS design is the differentiation between search systems and
\par
the predictive systems discussed here. While both may have similar information
\par
outputs, say the price of the firm's products or inputs, forecasting systems typically
\par
rely on different information sources. Future values of a profit function term may
\par
be forecast from historical values of the same term, from historical values of
\par
different terms, or from the current values of some different observable phenomenon.
\par
Systems to support forecasting may include similar models regardless of the term
\par
being forecast. Data may be held internally or acquired from external sources.
\par
Predictive activities may be outsourced to industry experts, and forecasting in
\par
support of strategic decisions may rely on softer data sources (such as referential
\par
or full text databases) than forecasting in support of shorter term, more structured
\par
decisions.
\par
\textbf{FINDING IMPROVING TECHNOLOGIES}
\par
In the theory of the firm, a key function of the entrepreneur is the discovery of
\par
improving technologies where technology takes the widest possible meaning. An
\par
improving technology can be any invention, process, control technique, organization,
\par
inspiration, marketing approach, or equipment that improves the ratio of outputs
\par
to inputs. Since, by assumption, the firm exists only because it has improved on
\par
the prevailing market institutions (the ratio of outputs to inputs available through
\par
other methods of organizing the transactions), existence of a firm means that an
\par
entrepreneur has found and implemented an improving technology. \textbf{This }technological
\par
progress helps to define the boundaries of the firm.
\par
The benefits of technological improvements take two general forms. The most
\par
obvious benefit is any increase in profits due to reduced costs and/or increased
\par
production and sales accruing from the improvement. These benefits are consistent
\par
with traditional normative theories of information, but economic theory provides
\par
an important caveat. The stream of benefits must be discounted by the ability of
\par
competitors to imitate the technology. To the extent that imitators exist, contestable
\par
market theory \textbf{[8] }argues that any advantages of innovation will be eroded as the
\par
technology becomes common practice in the industry. For this reason, and because
\par
investment in the creation of new information (R\&D) is risky, Spence pointed out
\par
that there is a propensity to underinvest in the discovery of new technologies [57].
\par
