The second benefit from the acquisition of new technologies is structural in
\par
nature and also follows from contestable market theory. To the extent that new
\par
technologies improve the productivity of existing resources or that they enable an
\par
expansion in the scale of production, contestable market theory argues for a con-
\par
traction in the number of firms serving the market, with each surviving firm holding
\par
a larger market share. From this perspective, the early adoption of important tech-
\par
nologies may be the key to survival in a shrinking market, a conclusion that does
\par
not follow from traditional normative theories.
\par
\textbf{MIS }research and practice have addressed issues in the area of finding improving
\par
technologies. Model management is one example where, if models are considered
\par
an organizational resource \textbf{[39], }systems to locate and recommend relevant models
\par
that already exist within an organization are, in effect, locating technology which
\par
is new to the requiring user. CASE technologies and the Leonard0 Project under
\par
development at the Micro Computer Corporation (MCC) in Austin, Texas, are
\par
examples of software systems to manage existing technology in an organization.
\par
Other references to model management research with similar objectives are found
\par
in Dutta and Basu \textbf{[13] }and Stohr and Tanniru \textbf{[59].}
\par
While locating existing technology within an organization is important enough
\par
to induce significant research effort, the real promise of information technology in
\par
this area lies in lower costs and better effectiveness for boundary spanning search.
\par
Because of the scope of the universe to be surveyed in this process, finding and
\par
internalizing new technology can be an involved and expensive process. The
\par
growth in business consulting, corporate headhunting, and the salaries of newly
\par
minted M.S. and Ph.D. business and technical graduates are all evidence of a need
\par
for technology, but these and other boundary spanning activities are clumsy and
\par
slow. Because of the fundamental importance of technological innovation to the
\par
\textbf{fm, }current limitations on technology acquisition and transfer make support for
\par
this activity a high potential area for further MIS research. The commercial success
\par
of literally thousands of online referential databases, including the Chemical
\par
Abstracting Service, ABI Inform, and Dissertation Abstracts databases illustrate
\par
successful information systems aimed at technology acquisition, as well as the
\par
potential for information systems to support this type of information problem \textbf{[61].}
\par
\textbf{IMPLICATIONS FOR RESEARCH AND PRACTICE}
\par
A model such \textbf{as }the one developed here is only useful to the extent that it contributes
\par
to activities in the field. This section highlights certain of the contributions that this
\par
approach can make.
\par
The four types of information problems presented here represent a move
\par
toward a more appropriate level of focus for the valuation of information and
\par
information systems, whether in the project justification stage or after implementation.
\par
Mapping specific information problems to the relevant problem type, and then to
\par
the relevant economic literature, has several benefits. First, it enables the designer/
\par
evaluator to consider more of the organizational environment than a strict focus on
\par
system capabilities provides. Second, it limits the inclusion of irrelevant factors
\par
that may be incorporated in industry level studies relating productivity to IS investment.
\par
