\par
Third, the approach here suggests a consistent research methodology that can be
\par
applied across multiple problem \textbf{types. }Since information constraints are inherent in
\par
the relevant economic models, comparative statics techniques can be applied to the
\par
models to simulate the ability of IT to address these constraints.
\par
For researchers, this methodology could lead to the construction of testable
\par
hypotheses. These hypotheses could take many forms depending on the nature of
\par
the underlying economic theory, but would be expected to encompass familiar
\par
themes. If the model correctly predicts structural transformation from the application
\par
of IT, then insourcing or outsourcing, transformation between market and hierar-
\par
chical organization forms, or the inception of new or the cessation of old activities
\par
would be observed. If the model predicts organizational changes, then the impact
\par
should be observed in the addition or elimination of management layers, the reor-
\par
ganization of responsibility, or the substitution of capital for labor or vice versa. If
\par
the model predicts behavioral changes, then changes in prices, wages, or contractual
\par
arrangements might be observed and used to validate the model. The techniques
\par
can be applied to transaction, coordination, or production costs as appropriate.
\par
Extending the models to include concepts from contestable market theory
\par
enables them to incorporate competitive and strategic advantage issues. The com-
\par
petitive advantage and strategic system themes are also present if the models shift
\par
in focus to address a firm's ability to create tying arrangements by affecting the
\par
costs of its suppliers and customers. \textbf{As }an added benefit, these research methodologies
\par
can be applied to the acquisition and organization of IT resources themselves, as
\par
well as to the application of IT to other problems.
\par
For practitioners, the economic literature helps to structure the process of
\par
identifying information problems, the nature of expected benefits, and the types of
\par
systems needed to overcome information constraints. For example, an organization
\par
faced with fluctuating prices for inputs might realize that an electronic search
\par
system is appropriate. The economic theory and a knowledge of IT capabilities and
\par
the firm's circumstances may help the decision maker choose between a direct \textbf{ED1}
\par
system with one \textbf{or }more suppliers, participation in an electronic brokerage system,
\par
or even vertically integrating into the supply activity. The theory may highlight
\par
benefits such as permanent reductions in prices due to more efficient search and
\par
higher levels of output due to reduced price uncertainty. The theory also indicates
\par
that the ability to internalize system benefits has implications for the ownership of
\par
the intermediating systems. When the benefits can be internalized by the system
\par
owner, as when the seller price discriminates between a partner in an 10s and a
\par
traditional buyer, there might be incentive to adopt 10s. When electronic search
\par
systems lead to a general price reduction (no price discrimination), there would be
\par
a tendency toward third-party owned brokerage systems.
\par
Many components of the preceding analysis are not available in traditional IS
\par
value research. The ability to map between problem and expected benefit and the
\par
ability to identify potential structural transformations accruing from the relaxation
\par
of information constraints are major benefits of this approach. Further, the approach
\par
presented here is formed from an internally consistent collection of accepted theory
\par
from both economics and MIS research.
