\textbf{CONCLUSION}
\par
Firms face a multitude of information problems, and information flows and differ-
\par
entials between economic actors can have a major impact on the performance and
\par
organization of firms and industries. Information technology has the distinction of
\par
being able to fundamentally alter the cost of acquiring, storing, and transferring
\par
information. To the extent that firm organization and behavior is a response to
\par
information constraints, IT represents a major change to the constraint set and
\par
therefore to organizational form and behavior. This approach, viewing IT from a
\par
cost altering perspective, can represent a major center of focus for MIS research
\par
\textbf{1221.}
\par
Based on the consistency between the economic theories of industrial organization
\par
and research and practice in MIS, it is clear that there is a common ground between
\par
the two. From this point of view, it is natural to expect each field to contribute to
\par
the other. For professionals in MIS, insight into the information motivations of
\par
firms, managers, employees, and trading partners provided by \textbf{I 0 }theory provides
\par
an explanatory framework of the need for information systems in and between
\par
organizations. Increasing attention has been paid to this need and momentum in
\par
this area is growing steadily.
\par
For economists, the importance of information in industrial organizational
\par
theory makes an understanding of IT capabilities essential to an understanding of
\par
the dynamics of organizational \textbf{form. }These dynamics are, in part, expressed by
\par
Bonczek, Holsapple, and Whinston,
\par
\textbf{It may be going too fat to say that technological innovations in information}
\par
\textbf{processing caused the present-day prominence of organizations. However, since}
\par
\textbf{organizations are information processors, the technological innovations in infor-}
\par
\textbf{mation processing most certainly have had an enabling influence on the growth}
\par
\textbf{of organizations. At any rate, the }growth \textbf{in size and complexity of the information-}
\par
\textbf{processing aspect of organizations is an important characteristic of the informa-}
\par
\textbf{tion revolution. [lo, p. 51}
\par
Finally, a special class of strategic decisions is implicit in the economic per-
\par
spective on information problems. \textbf{As }discussed, information problems are seen to
\par
play a key role in determining organizational form, to include establishing the
\par
\textbf{firm's }boundaries, determining internal organization and external relationships, and
\par
in distributing the firm's profits. The fundamental association between information
\par
and organizational \textbf{form }implies a tremendous role for IT in redefining the organization.
\par
This idea is not new and has been stated many times. \textbf{A }contribution of this work
\par
is the categorization of information problems as a starting point for understanding
\par
specific opportunities and for explaining current trends. Recognizing the possibility
\par
for profitable reorganization as information constraints are overcome and acting on
\par
these opportunities should be one of the biggest challenges facing strategic man-
\par
agers in the information age. Further, the fundamental importance of information
\par
technology in this process makes the presence of MIS professionals in the upper
\par
levels of strategic management crucial for affected organizations \textbf{[33]. }From this
\par
perspective, an essential question for \textbf{MIS }researchers is not to determine the
\par
appropriate reference disciplines for MIS research but to build MIS into a position
